(window.LibraryShards = window.LibraryShards || {})["search-shard-guerric-of-igny.js"] = [{"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo I, De divinorum promissorum exspectatione, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 11\u201314.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Titus 2:13", "Matthew 6:21", "Philippians 3:20", "Lamentations 3:24\u201326", "Habakkuk 2:3", "Romans 8:12 (printed locator; see note)", "Micah 4:9 (printed locator; see note)"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This first Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric moves from the fulfilment of God's promises in Christ's first coming to patient hope for his return. The final prayer develops an image of remaining with Christ on the Cross. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "g-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DE ADVENTU DOMINI"}, {"anchor": "g-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE ADVENT OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "g-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De divinorum promissorum exspectatione."}, {"anchor": "g-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On Waiting for God's Promises", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Salvatorem exspectamus (Tit. ii, 13). Vere exspectatio justorum laetitia, exspectantium beatam spem, et adventum gloriae magni Dei et salvatoris nostri Jesu Christi. Et nunc quae est exspectatio mea, inquit justus, nonne Dominus? (Psal. xxxviii, 8.) Et conversus ad Dominum: Scio, inquit, quia non confundes me ab exspectatione mea (Psal. cxviii, 116); quia jam substantia mea apud te est (Psal. xxxviii, 8), quia natura nostra de nobis sumpta, et pro nobis oblata, apud te glorificata est: spem nobis tribuens, quia ad te omnis caro veniet (Psal. lxiv, 3); caputque membra sequentur, ut holocaustum compleatur. Pleniore tamen fiducia, quia tutiore conscientia, Dominum exspectare potest, cui dicere datum est: Substantia possessiunculae meae, o Domine, apud te est; quia facultates meas, vel tibi erogando, vel pro te contemnendo, in coelis thesaurizavi, et ad pedes tuos omnia mea deposui, sciens quia potens es depositum meum non modo servare, sed etiam in centuplum restituere, et vitam aeternam adjicere. Quam beati vos, pauperes spiritu, quia juxta consilium admirabilis Consiliarii thesaurizastis vobis thesauros in coelo; ne si in terra thesauri vestri remanerent, corda vestra pariter putrescerent! Ubi enim, inquit, thesaurus tuus, ibi et cor tuum (Matth. vi, 21). Eant igitur, eant corda post thesauros suos; sursum affixa sit cogitatio, et ad Dominum suspensa exspectatio, ut et vobis competat dicere cum Apostolo: Nostra autem conversatio in coelis est, unde etiam Salvatorem exspectamus (Philipp. iii, 20). O exspectatio gentium! universi qui te exspectant non confundentur. Te expectaverunt patres nostri, omnes justi ab origine mundi in te speraverunt, et non sunt confusi (Psal. xxi, 5, 6). Jam enim suscepta misericordia tua in medio templi tui, chorus laetantium et laudantium concinit: Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini (Matth. xxi, 9). Exspectans exspectavi Dominum, et intendit mihi (Psal. xxxix, 2). Et in humilitate carnis agnoscentes majestatem divinitatis: Ecce, inquiunt, Dominus noster iste; exspectavimus eum, et salvabit nos: iste Dominus, sustinuimus eum; exsultabimus et laetabimur in salutari ejus (Isa. xxv, 9)."}, {"anchor": "g-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. We await the Saviour (Tit. ii, 13). Truly, the waiting of the righteous is joy, as they await the blessed hope and the coming of the glory of our great God and Saviour Jesus Christ. \u2018And now, what am I waiting for,\u2019 says the righteous person, \u2018if not the Lord?\u2019 (Psal. xxxviii, 8.) Turning to the Lord, he says: \u2018I know that you will not disappoint my hope\u2019 (Psal. cxviii, 116), \u2018because my very being is already with you\u2019 (Psal. xxxviii, 8): for our nature, taken from us and offered for us, has been glorified with you. This gives us hope that all flesh will come to you (Psal. lxiv, 3), and that the members will follow the Head, so that the whole burnt offering may be complete. Yet the person who has been granted these words can await the Lord with fuller confidence, because his conscience is more secure: \u2018O Lord, the substance of my little property is with you. By spending my resources for you, or giving them up for your sake, I have stored them in heaven; I have laid all I possess at your feet, knowing that you can not only keep what I have entrusted to you, but return it a hundredfold and add eternal life.\u2019 How blessed you are, poor in spirit! Following the advice of the wonderful Counsellor, you have stored up treasures for yourselves in heaven, lest your hearts decay along with treasures left on earth. \u2018For where your treasure is,\u2019 he says, \u2018there is your heart also\u2019 (Matth. vi, 21). Let our hearts go, then, let them follow their treasures. Let our thoughts be fixed above and our expectation hang upon the Lord, so that you too may say with the Apostle: \u2018Our citizenship is in heaven, from which we also await the Saviour\u2019 (Philipp. iii, 20). O expectation of the nations! All who await you will not be put to shame. Our fathers awaited you; all the righteous from the beginning of the world hoped in you and were not put to shame (Psal. xxi, 5, 6). For now that your mercy has been received in the midst of your temple, the choir of those rejoicing and praising sings together: \u2018Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord\u2019 (Matth. xxi, 9). \u2018I waited and waited for the Lord, and he attended to me\u2019 (Psal. xxxix, 2). And, recognizing the majesty of divinity in the lowliness of flesh, they say: \u2018Look, this is our Lord; we have waited for him, and he will save us. This is the Lord; we have waited for him. We shall rejoice and be glad in his salvation\u2019 (Isa. xxv, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sicut autem Ecclesia in antiquis justis exspectavit adventum primum; sic in novis reexspectat secundum: sicut in primo certa de pretio redemptionis; sic in secundo, de praemio remunerationis: atque hujus exspectatione spei suspensa a terrenis, tam feliciter quam ardenter inhiat aeternis. Cum igitur alii festinantes praesentibus beari, nec sustinentes consilium Domini, currunt ad diripiendam praedam hujus mundi; beatus vir cujus est nomen Domini spes ejus, et non respexit in vanitates et insanias falsas (Psal. xxxix, 5). Qui abstinet se a vitiis eorum, tanquam ab immunditiis, sciens melius esse humiliari cum mitibus, quam dividere spolia cum superbis (Prov. xvi, 19). Loquensque secum consolatur se, et ait: Pars mea Dominus, dixit anima mea: propterea exspectabo eum. Bonus est Dominus sperantibus in eum, animae quaerenti illum. Bonum est praestolari cum silentio salutare Dei (Thren. iii, 24\u201326). Defecit quidem anima mea in salutare tuum, o Domine, sed in verbum tuum supersperavi (Psal. cxviii, 81). Spes quidem quae differtur, scriptum est, affligit animam (Prov. xiii, 12): sed, licet lassa sit ex desiderii dilatione, secura est tamen ex promissione. In ipsum solum sperans, sed et supersperans adjiciam spem: sicut assidue adjicitur tribulatio super tribulationem, dilatio super dilationem. Certus sum enim quia apparebit in finem, et non mentietur. Ideoque, et si moram fecerit, exspectabo eum, quia veniens veniet, et non tardabit (Habac. ii, 3), ultra praestitutum tempus vel opportunum. Quod opportunum? Cum impletus fuerit numerus fratrum nostrorum, et consummatum fuerit tempus misericordiae ad poenitentiam indultum. Audi Isaiam ad superna saepius admissum consilia, quo videlicet consilio Dominus judicium differat. Propterea, inquit, Dominus exspectat ut misereatur vestri; et ideo exaltabitur parcens vobis, quia Deus judicii Dominus. Beati omnes qui exspectant eum (Isai. xxx, 18)."}, {"anchor": "g-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Just as the Church awaited the first coming in the righteous of old, so she awaits the second in the righteous of today: as sure of the price of redemption at the first as of the reward to be bestowed at the second. Lifted away from earthly things by this hopeful expectation, she longs for eternal things as happily as she does eagerly. While others hurry to find happiness in present things and, refusing to wait for the Lord's counsel, rush to seize this world's spoils, blessed is the man whose hope is the name of the Lord, who has not turned to vanities and lying follies (Psal. xxxix, 5). He keeps away from their vices as from filth, knowing that it is better to be humbled with the meek than to divide spoils with the proud (Prov. xvi, 19). Speaking within himself, he takes comfort and says: \u2018The Lord is my portion, says my soul; therefore I shall wait for him. The Lord is good to those who hope in him, to the soul that seeks him. It is good to wait in silence for God's salvation\u2019 (Thren. iii, 24\u201326). \u2018My soul has indeed grown faint for your salvation, O Lord, but I have hoped all the more in your word\u2019 (Psal. cxviii, 81). It is written that hope deferred afflicts the soul (Prov. xiii, 12); yet, though wearied by the postponement of its desire, it remains secure because of the promise. Hoping in him alone, and hoping beyond hope, I shall add hope, just as tribulation is continually added to tribulation and delay to delay. For I am certain that he will appear at the end and will not lie. Therefore, even if he delays, I shall wait for him, because he who is coming will come and will not be late (Habac. ii, 3), beyond the appointed or fitting time. What is the fitting time? When the number of our brethren has been completed, and the time mercifully granted for repentance has run its course. Listen to Isaiah, so often admitted to heavenly counsels, explaining why the Lord postpones judgment: \u2018Therefore the Lord waits to have mercy on you; therefore he will be exalted in sparing you, because the Lord is a God of judgment. Blessed are all who await him\u2019 (Isai. xxx, 18).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Propterea, si sapis, prospicies tibi quomodo dilationis hujus induciis utaris: utique si peccator es, ad poenitentiam, non ad negligentiam; si sanctus, ut proficias in sanctitate, non ut deficias a fide. Si autem dixerit malus servus ille in corde suo: Moram facit Dominus meus venire; et coeperit percutere conservos suos, manducet autem et bibat cum ebriosis: veniet Dominus servi illius in die qua non sperat, et hora qua ignorat, et dividet eum, partemque ejus ponet cum hypocritis: illic erit fletus et stridor dentium (Matth. xxiv, 48\u201351). Hujus servi nequam et infidelis videtur esse vox illa taedii et desperationis, quae apud Isaiam legitur: quia videlicet moram faciente Domino, frequentibus nuntiis ejus increduli et illusores insultare videntur: Manda, remanda, manda, remanda; exspecta, reexspecta; modicum ibi, modicum ibi. Sed quae poena sequatur miseros, propheta non tacuit, cum post pauca subintulit: Et erit eis, inquit, verbum Domini, quod videlicet subsannant, dicentes prophetis: Manda, remanda; manda, remanda; exspecta, reexspecta; modicum ibi, modicum ibi: ut vadant et cadant retrorsum, et conterantur, et illaqueentur, et capiantur (Isai. xxviii, 13). Vadunt retrorsum per apostasiam, cadunt in criminalia, illaqueantur mortifera delectatione, vel quadam inextricabili peccandi necessitate, ut nec velint, nec possint poenitere: capiuntur improvisa morte, conteruntur aeterna damnatione. Nescit enim finem suum homo: sed sicut pisces capiuntur hamo, et aves laqueo; sic homines, inquit Salomon, capiuntur tempore malo, cum eis extemplo supervenerit (Eccle. ix, 12). Propterea videlicet, ne pro dilatione spei fides nostra tepescat, aut aestuet patientia, et incipiamus esse de illis qui ad tempus credunt, et in tempore tentationis recedunt (Luc. viii, 13): clamat nobis de coelo qui fidem donat, donatam probat, probatam coronat, inquiens: Quis crediderit, non festinet (Isai. xxviii, 16), scilicet videre quod credit. Si enim quod non videmus, speramus: per patientiam exspectamus. Sponsae quoque suae, quam sibi desponsavit in fide, mandat Deus per Osee, dicens: Dies multos exspectabis me: non fornicaberis, et non eris viro (Osee iii, 3). Hoc nempe est Dominum veraciter exspectare, si fidem ei servemus: ut licet careamus consolatione praesentiae ejus, adulterum tamen non sequamur; sed pendeamus ad reditum ejus. Sic namque Dominus ait in eodem propheta: Pendebit populus meus ad reditum meum (Id. xi, 7). Pulchre omnino et proprie, pendebit, tanquam inter coelum et terram: licet adhuc apprehendere coelestia non valeat, non velit tamen tangere terrena. Et si aliquando tangit, nonnisi summis articulis, id est, extremis partibus animae; propter necessitatem scilicet corruptionis nostrae, cui cogimur servire, quamdiu creatura vanitati subjecta est, non volens (Rom. viii, 12). Vulgo dicitur: Male exspectat, qui pendet. Ego autem dico: Feliciter exspectat, qui sic pendet. Quamobrem elegit suspendium anima mea, et in ipso suspendio mortem ossa mea: ut perseveranter in cruce ista pendere merear, donec immoriar."}, {"anchor": "g-p003", "label": "English", "text": "4. Therefore, if you are wise, consider how to use the respite of this delay: if you are a sinner, for repentance rather than negligence; if you are holy, to advance in holiness rather than fall away from faith. But if that wicked servant says in his heart, \u2018My Lord is slow in coming,\u2019 and begins to beat his fellow servants and eat and drink with drunkards, that servant's Lord will come on a day he does not expect and at an hour he does not know, and will cut him apart and assign him a place with the hypocrites: there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth (Matth. xxiv, 48\u201351). The voice of weariness and despair that we read in Isaiah seems to be that of this wicked and faithless servant. Because the Lord delays, unbelievers and scoffers seem to jeer at his frequent messengers: \u2018Command, command again, command, command again; wait, wait again; a little there, a little there.\u2019 But the prophet did not keep silent about the punishment that would follow these wretched people, for a little later he added: \u2018And the word of the Lord will be to them\u2019\u2014the word they mock when they say to the prophets, \u2018Command, command again; command, command again; wait, wait again; a little there, a little there\u2019\u2014\u2018so that they may go and fall backward, and be broken, and ensnared, and captured\u2019 (Isai. xxviii, 13). They go backward through apostasy, fall into grave sins, and are ensnared by deadly pleasure or by a kind of inescapable compulsion to sin, so that they neither wish nor are able to repent. They are captured by sudden death and broken by eternal condemnation. For man does not know his end: \u2018As fish are caught by a hook and birds by a snare, so men,\u2019 says Solomon, \u2018are caught in an evil time when it suddenly falls upon them\u2019 (Eccle. ix, 12). Therefore, lest the delay of hope make our faith grow lukewarm or our patience become agitated, and we begin to be among those \u2018who believe for a time and fall away in the time of temptation\u2019 (Luc. viii, 13), he who gives faith, tests the faith he has given, and crowns the faith he has tested, cries to us from heaven: \u2018Let the one who believes not hurry\u2019 (Isai. xxviii, 16)\u2014that is, to see what he believes. For if we hope for what we do not see, we await it patiently. God also commands his bride, whom he has betrothed to himself in faith, through Hosea: \u2018You will wait for me many days; you will not play the harlot or belong to a man\u2019 (Osee iii, 3). This is truly waiting for the Lord: keeping faith with him, so that although we lack the comfort of his presence we do not follow an adulterer, but hang upon his return. For the Lord says in the same prophet: \u2018My people will hang upon my return\u2019 (Id. xi, 7). How beautifully and aptly he says \u2018hang,\u2019 as though between heaven and earth: though they cannot yet grasp heavenly things, they are unwilling to touch earthly things. And if they sometimes touch them, it is only with their fingertips, that is, the outermost parts of the soul, because of the necessities of our corruptible condition, which we are compelled to serve as long as creation is subjected to vanity against its will (Rom. viii, 12). People commonly say, \u2018He who hangs waits badly.\u2019 But I say, \u2018He who hangs in this way waits happily.\u2019 Therefore my soul has chosen hanging, and my bones death in that hanging, so that I may be found worthy to hang perseveringly upon this cross until I die upon it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O Domine Jesu, cum animam potestate positurus esses, et quo genere mortis id facere velles, tuo subesset arbitrio; suspendium elegit anima tua, ut exaltatus a terra, nos quoque ad te traheres, et a terrenis suspenderes. Sed nec ante mortem deponi passus es, et nos usque ad mortem in cruce perseveremus, et de ipsa tanquam de sublimi gradu facilior sit in coelum conscensus. Gratias tibi, Domine Jesu! ibi sumus, ibi te exspectamus, non Eliam, qui ad deponendum nos veniat; sed Eli, id est, Dominum nostrum, qui nos suscipiat. Modicum ibi, modicum ibi, si mandas atque remandas. Ego quidem mandatis tuis semel credidi; sed adjuva incredulitatem meam, ut immobilis ibi te exspectem, reexspectem, donec quod credo videam: credo enim videre bona Domini in terra viventium. Credis et tu: igitur exspecta Dominum, viriliter age, confortetur cor tuum, et sustine Dominum! (Psal. xxvi, 13, 14). Vae his qui perdiderunt sustinentiam, et diverterunt in vias pravas. Et quid facient, cum inspicere coeperit Dominus? Qui tamen longanimiter se mandat exspectari, alibi se cito venturum esse promittit: in altero nimirum sustinentiam informans; in altero pusillanimes confortans, imparatos terrens, et pigros excitans. Ecce, inquit, venio cito, et merces mea mecum est, reddere unicuique secundum opera sua (Apoc. xxii, 12): et ad Jerusalem: Cito veniet salus tua: quare moerore consumeris? (Mich. iv, 9). Et revera tempus breve est, maxime uniuscujusque nostrum; licet longum videatur aestuandi, seu prae labore, seu prae amore. Ideoque utrumque necesse est: ut timeamus Judicem qui prope est, et forsitan mihi, forsitan tibi in januis est; et longanimiter exspectemus, si moram fecerit. Veniens veniet ille Dominus, timor et desiderium nostrum, requies et merces laborantium, dulcedo et amplexus amantium, beatitudo omnium, salvator noster Jesus Christus, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum."}, {"anchor": "g-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. O Lord Jesus, when you were about to lay down your life by your own power, and the kind of death you would choose lay within your own decision, your soul chose hanging, so that, lifted up from the earth, you might draw us too to yourself and suspend us above earthly things. You did not allow yourself to be taken down before death: may we too persevere upon the cross until death, and may ascent into heaven be easier from it, as from a lofty step. Thank you, Lord Jesus! There we are; there we await you\u2014not Elijah, coming to take us down, but Eli, that is, our Lord, to receive us. \u2018A little there, a little there,\u2019 if you command and command again. I have indeed believed your commands once for all; but help my unbelief, so that I may remain there unmoved, waiting and waiting again for you, until I see what I believe: for I believe that I shall see the Lord's goodness in the land of the living. You believe too: then wait for the Lord; act courageously, let your heart be strengthened, and wait for the Lord! (Psal. xxvi, 13, 14). Woe to those who have lost their endurance and turned aside into crooked ways. What will they do when the Lord begins his inspection? Yet he who commands us to await him with patience promises elsewhere that he will come quickly: in the one case teaching endurance, in the other comforting the fainthearted, terrifying the unprepared, and rousing the sluggish. \u2018Look,\u2019 he says, \u2018I am coming quickly, and my reward is with me, to repay each according to his works\u2019 (Apoc. xxii, 12); and to Jerusalem: \u2018Your salvation will come quickly; why are you consumed by grief?\u2019 (Mich. iv, 9). Truly the time is short, especially for each one of us, although it may seem long in our anguish, whether from toil or from love. Therefore both are necessary: to fear the Judge who is near, perhaps at my door, perhaps at yours, and to wait patiently if he delays. He who is coming will come: the Lord, our fear and our desire, the rest and reward of those who labour, the sweetness and embrace of those who love, the blessedness of all, our Saviour Jesus Christ, who lives and reigns for ever and ever.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "g-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Romans 8:12 beside words from Romans 8:20. The printed reference is retained."}, {"anchor": "g-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The hanging/death language is Guerric's sustained image of perseverance on Christ's cross, with a Scriptural allusion to Job 7:15. The proverbial contrast and bodily imagery are retained rather than paraphrased away."}, {"anchor": "g-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Micah 4:9, although the whole sentence follows liturgical wording rather than that verse verbatim. Eli and Elias are Guerric\u2019s wordplay on the Passion narratives."}, {"anchor": "g-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition numbers both final paragraphs 4; that numbering is retained. In the first paragraph, the reading ne si (\u201clest\u201d) corrects the printed ne se, and a missing opening parenthesis before Psalm 64:3 has been supplied."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon I: Waiting for God's Promises", "topics": ["Advent", "hope", "patience", "repentance", "Second Coming", "Cross"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo II, Quomodo Christo advenienti occurrendum, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 14-18.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Proverbs 25:25", "Isaiah 52:7", "Ezekiel 39:8", "Isaiah 39:3", "Luke 1:34, 43", "Sirach 22:31", "Psalm 117:25\u201326 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 55:11", "Psalm 1:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 107:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 3:9 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 17:14", "Psalm 79:4, 8, 20 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 33:2", "Luke 10:23\u201324", "1 Thessalonians 4:16", "Psalm 49:23 (historical numbering)", "John 14:18", "Psalm 83:12 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 24:16", "Job 9:11", "Psalm 34:10 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 146:6 (historical numbering)", "James 4:6", "Isaiah 53:2\u20133", "Malachi 3:2", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "2 Timothy 1:12"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This second Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric urges his hearers to run out with joy to meet the coming Christ, as the prophets and the just longed to see him. He then describes a hidden, spiritual coming between the first and the last, in which the soul is transformed into Christ's image. Until that is granted, faith and a pure conscience console the patient. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo Christo advenienti occurrendum"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How to Go Out to Meet the Coming Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ecce venit Rex, occurramus obviam Salvatori nostro. Salomon pulchre ait: Aqua frigida animae sitienti, et nuntius bonus de terra longinqua (Prov. XXV, 25). Bonus itaque nuntius, qui adventum Salvatoris nuntiat, reconciliationem mundi, bona superventuri saeculi. Quam pulchri pedes annuntiantium pacem, annuntiantium bona! (Isa, LII, 7.) Multi siquidem, non unus. Multi inquam, sed in uno spiritu longa serie ab initio saeculi nobis supervenere nuntii, et omnium vox una, simul una sententia: Venit ecce venit (Ezech. XXXIX, 8). Et unde, inquis, isti venere nuntii? Ut scriptum est, de terra longinqua (Isa. XXXIX, 3); quia de terra viventium, quae magno interstitio separata est ab hac terra morientium: nam inter nos et illos adhuc chaos magnum firmatum est. Inde tamen prophetae nobis, sicut et angeli missi sunt; quia si corpore hic versabantur, spiritu illuc assumebantur, quando mittendi erant; ut illic audirent et viderent, quod hic nuntiarent. Hujusmodi nuntii aqua refrigerii, potusque sapientiae sunt salutaris animae Deum sitienti: cui nimirum qui nuntiat adventum vel alia mysteria Salvatoris, haurit ei, et propinat aquas in gaudio de fontibus Salvatoris, ut etiam nuntianti sibi, sive Isaiae, sive cuilibet ex prophetis, eadem anima respondere videatur verbis Elizabeth, quia eodem potata spiritu quo Elizabeth: Et unde mihi hoc, ut veniat Dominus meus ad me? (Luc. I, 34, 43.) Ecce enim, ut facta est vox annuntiationis tuae in auribus meis, exsultavit in gaudio spiritus in corde meo, gestiens occurrere Deo Salvatori suo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Behold, the King is coming; let us go out to meet our Savior. Solomon says beautifully: \u201cCold water to a thirsty soul, and good news from a distant land\u201d (Prov. XXV, 25). Good, then, is the messenger who announces the Savior\u2019s coming, the reconciliation of the world, and the blessings of the age that is to come. \u201cHow beautiful are the feet of those who announce peace, who announce good things!\u201d (Isa, LII, 7.) There are many of them, not just one. Many, I say, but in one spirit, in a long succession from the beginning of the world, messengers have come to us, and the voice of them all is one, their judgment together one: \u201cBehold, he comes, yes, he comes\u201d (Ezech. XXXIX, 8). And where, you ask, did these messengers come from? As it is written, \u201cfrom a distant land\u201d (Isa. XXXIX, 3); for they came from the land of the living, which is separated by a great interval from this land of the dying\u2014for between us and them a great chasm is still fixed. Yet from there the prophets, like the angels, were sent to us; for although they were living here in body, in spirit they were taken there whenever they were to be sent, so that there they might hear and see what they were to announce here. Such messengers are water of refreshment and a drink of saving wisdom for the soul thirsting for God. Whoever announces to it the Savior\u2019s coming or any of his other mysteries draws and offers it water with joy from the fountains of the Savior, so that the same soul seems to answer the one announcing it\u2014whether Isaiah or any other of the prophets\u2014with Elizabeth\u2019s words, because it has been given to drink of the same Spirit as Elizabeth: \u201cAnd how has this happened to me, that my Lord should come to me?\u201d (Luc. I, 34, 43.) For behold, as the voice of your announcement sounded in my ears, the spirit in my heart leaped for joy, eager to go out to meet God its Savior.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Et revera, fratres, exsultatione spiritus Christo venienti occurrendum est. Et jam nunc a longe salutandus, vel certe resalutandus, qui mandat salutes Jacob. Amicum salutare non confundar (Eccli. XXII, 31), inquit Sapiens; quanto magis resalutare? O salutare vultus mei, et Deus meus! quanta dignatio fuit quod servos salutasti? sed quanto major, quod salvasti? Nec enim integra nobis salus esset, si salutes mandares, et non dares. Dedisti autem, non modo, quos salutaveras verbis pacificis, postmodum salutando in osculo pacis, videlicet per unionem carnis; sed etiam salutem operando per mortem crucis. Exsurgat igitur spiritus noster alacri gaudio, curratque obviam Salvatori suo, et jam a longe venientem adoret et salutet, acclamans ei et dicens: O Domine, salvum me fac; o Domine, bene prosperare! Benedictus qui venturus es in nomine Domini (Psal. CXVII, 25, 26). Salve, qui venis salvare nos; benedictus, qui venis benedicere nos. Igitur, o Domine, bene prosperare, qui tam prosper et salutaris advenis generi humano. Intende, prospere procede et regna. Prosperum iter faciet tibi Pater, Deus salutarium nostrorum. Prosperabitur, inquit Pater, in his ad quae misi illum (Isa. LV, 11): non pro votis carnalium, non pro voluntate Petri, qui abhorrebat illum pati. Et omnia quaecunque faciet prosperabuntur (Psal. I, 3): non ad praeproperam hominum voluntatem, sed ad veram eorum salutem. Vana quidem salus hominis (Psal. CVII, 13): sed Domini est salus (Psal. III, 9), qui operatus est salutem de sanguine suo, fundens illum in pretium, et propinans in potum. Veni ergo, o Domine, salvum me fac, et salvus ero (Jerem. XVII, 14). Veni et ostende faciem tuam, et salvi erimus (Psal. LXXIX. 4, 8, 20). Te enim exspectavimus: esto brachium nostrum, salus nostra in tempore tribulationis (Isai. XXXIII, 2). Sic Prophetae et justi desiderio et affectu tanto ante Christo venturo occurrebant, desiderantes, si fieri posset, oculis videre quod spiritu praevidebant. Unde Dominus discipulis dicebat: Beati oculi qui vident quae vos videtis. Dico enim vobis, quod multi prophetae, et reges voluerunt videre quae vos videtis, et non viderunt (Luc. X, 23, 24). Abraham quoque pater noster exsultavit, ut videret diem Christi: vidit, sed apud inferos, et gavisus est. In quo utique tepor et duritia cordis nostri suggillatur, si non videt eum gaudio spirituali."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. And truly, brothers, we must go out to meet the coming Christ with the rejoicing of the spirit. Even now, from afar, he must be greeted\u2014or certainly greeted in return\u2014he who sends greetings of salvation to Jacob. \u201cI will not be ashamed to greet a friend,\u201d says the Wise Man (Eccli. XXII, 31); how much more to greet him in return? O salvation of my face, and my God! How great was your condescension, that you greeted your servants; but how much greater was it that you saved them! For our salvation would not have been complete if you had sent greetings of salvation but had not given salvation. Yet you did give it\u2014not only by greeting those whom you had greeted with words of peace, later greeting them with the kiss of peace, that is, through the union of flesh, but also by accomplishing salvation through the death of the cross. Let our spirit, then, rise up in joyful gladness, and run out to meet its Savior; let it already adore and greet him as he comes from afar, acclaiming him and saying: \u201cO Lord, save me; O Lord, prosper!\u201d \u201cBlessed are you who are to come in the name of the Lord\u201d (Psal. CXVII, 25, 26). Hail, you who come to save us; blessed are you who come to bless us. Therefore, O Lord, prosper, since you come to the human race so prosperously and as its Savior. Attend, proceed prosperously, and reign. The Father, the God of our salvation, will make your journey prosperous. \u201cIt shall prosper,\u201d says the Father, \u201cin the things for which I sent him\u201d (Isa. LV, 11)\u2014not according to the desires of the carnal, nor according to the will of Peter, who shrank from his suffering. \u201cAnd all that he does shall prosper\u201d (Psal. I, 3)\u2014not according to the hasty will of human beings, but for their true salvation. \u201cVain indeed is the salvation of man\u201d (Psal. CVII, 13); \u201cbut salvation belongs to the Lord\u201d (Psal. III, 9), who accomplished salvation by his blood, pouring it out as the price and offering it as a drink. Come, then, O Lord, \u201csave me, and I shall be saved\u201d (Jerem. XVII, 14). \u201cCome and show your face, and we shall be saved\u201d (Psal. LXXIX. 4, 8, 20). For we have waited for you: \u201cbe our arm, our salvation in the time of tribulation\u201d (Isai. XXXIII, 2). In this way the prophets and the righteous, with such great desire and longing, went out so long beforehand to meet the Christ who was to come, wishing, if it were possible, to see with their eyes what they foresaw in spirit. Hence the Lord said to his disciples: \u201cBlessed are the eyes that see what you see. For I tell you that many prophets and kings wished to see what you see, and did not see it\u201d (Luc. X, 23, 24). Our father Abraham, too, rejoiced that he might see Christ\u2019s day; he saw it, but in the netherworld, and rejoiced. In this, surely, the lukewarmness and hardness of our heart are reproved, if it does not see him with spiritual joy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Christi nascentis diem anniversarium exspectamus; qui nobis in proximo videndus, Domino annuente, promittitur. Hoc sane gaudium nostrum tale videtur exigere Scriptura, ut spiritus noster levans se super se, Christo venienti quodammodo occurrere gestiat: et desiderio se extendens in anteriora, impatiensque morarum, jam videre contendat futura. Ego namque non solum ad secundum adventum, sed etiam ad primum arbitror pertinere, quod tot locis Scripturarum ei monemur occurrere. Quomodo, inquis? Quia videlicet sicut secundo adventui occurremus motu et exsultatione corporis: sic et primo occurrendum est affectu et exsultatione cordis. Scitis enim quia resumptis in resurrectione corporibus novis, secundum doctrinam Apostoli, rapiemur in nubibus obviam Christo in aera, et sic semper cum Domino erimus (I Thess. IV, 16). Sic nec modo desunt nubes, quae spiritus nostros, si nimis non fuerint pigri terraeque affixi, sublevabunt ad altiora; et sic cum Domino erimus, vel hora dimidia. Agnoscit, ni fallor, experientia vestra quod loquor, cum aliquando vocem dederunt nubes, id est, sonuerunt in Ecclesia prophetarum, vel apostolorum voces, ad quam sublimia sensus vestri, quasi vehiculo nubis, fuerint subvecti; et eo usque nonnunquam excesserint, ut gloriam Domini quantulumcunque mererentur speculari. Tunc, ni fallor, vobis innotuit veritas illius sermonis, quem Dominus de illa nube pluit, quam quotidie ponit ascensum vobis: Sacrificium laudis honorificabit me: et illic iter, quo ostendam illi salutare Dei (Psal. XLIX, 23). Ita ergo sit, ut ante adventum suum Dominus veniat ad vos, et antequam mundo generaliter adveniat, familiariter invisat vos. Non vos, inquit, relinquam orphanos: vado, et veniam ad vos (Joan. XIV, 18). Et quidem pro merito cujusque, vel studio, creber est ad unumquemque iste Domini adventus, hoc tempore medio inter adventum primum et novissimum, conformans nos adventui primo, et praeparans novissimo. Ad hoc nempe venit modo in nos, ne primo adventu frustra venerit ad nos, vel ne in novissimo veniat iratus adversus nos. Hoc siquidem adventu satagit reformare sensum superbiae nostrae, configuratum sensui humilitatis suae, quam primo veniens exhibuit; ut perinde reformet corpus humilitatis nostrae, configuratum corpori claritatis suae, quam denuo rediens exhibebit. Prorsus optandus omnibus votis, et expetendus studiis adventus iste familiaris, qui nobis gratiam impertiat adventus primi, et gloriam promittat novissimi. Quia enim misericordiam et veritatem diligit Deus, gratiam et gloriam dabit Dominus (Psal. LXXXIII, 12); per misericordiam praerogans gratiam, per veritatem reddens gloriam."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. We await the anniversary of Christ\u2019s birth, which, with the Lord\u2019s favor, is promised to us as something we shall soon see. Scripture certainly seems to require that our joy be such that our spirit, lifting itself above itself, eagerly desire in some way to go out to meet the coming Christ; stretching forward in its longing and impatient of delay, it strives already to see what is still to come. For I believe that it applies not only to the second coming but also to the first, that in so many places of Scripture we are urged to go out to meet him. \u201cHow so?\u201d you ask. Because, just as we shall go out to meet his second coming through the movement and exultation of the body, so we must go out to meet his first through the affection and exultation of the heart. You know, in fact, that when our bodies have been taken up again in the resurrection, renewed, then, according to the Apostle\u2019s teaching, \u201cwe shall be caught up in the clouds to meet Christ in the air, and so we shall always be with the Lord\u201d (I Thess. IV, 16). Even now, then, clouds are not lacking\u2014clouds that will lift our spirits to higher things, if they are not too sluggish or fixed to the earth\u2014and so we shall be with the Lord, even if only for half an hour. Your own experience, unless I am mistaken, recognizes what I am saying: sometimes the clouds have given voice\u2014that is, the voices of prophets or apostles have sounded in the Church\u2014and your understanding has been carried up to sublime heights, as though in a cloud\u2019s chariot; sometimes it has risen so far that it merited, however slightly, to contemplate the glory of the Lord. Then, unless I am mistaken, you came to know the truth of that saying which the Lord rained down upon you from that cloud, which he sets before you every day as a means of ascent: \u201cThe sacrifice of praise will honor me, and there is the way by which I will show him the salvation of God\u201d (Psal. XLIX, 23). Let it be so, then: before his coming, may the Lord come to you, and before he comes to the world as a whole, may he visit you personally. \u201cI will not leave you orphans,\u201d he says; \u201cI am going away, and I will come to you\u201d (Joan. XIV, 18). And indeed, according to each person\u2019s merit or devotion, this coming of the Lord to each one is frequent during this time between his first and final coming: it conforms us to the first coming and prepares us for the final one. For this is why he comes into us now: so that he may not have come to us in vain in his first coming, and so that at the last he may not come against us in anger. By this coming he earnestly strives to reform our proud mind, conforming it to the mind of his humility, which he displayed when he first came; and thereby to reform our body of humility, conforming it to the body of his glory, which he will display when he returns. This personal coming is wholly to be desired with every prayer and sought with every effort: it grants us the grace of his first coming and promises us the glory of the last. For because God loves mercy and truth, \u201cthe Lord will give grace and glory\u201d (Psal. LXXXIII, 12), granting grace beforehand through mercy and repaying glory through truth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Porro sicut dispositione temporis, sic etiam proportione similitudinis medius est adventus iste spiritualis inter adventum utrumque corporalem, et velut quidam mediator, participat cum utroque. Primus siquidem adventus, occultus et humilis; ultimus, manifestus et admirabilis: iste autem occultus quidem, sed mirabilis. Occultum sane dixerim, non quod ignoretur ab eo cui venit, sed quia secretus ei advenit. Unde glorians illa gloriosa anima secum cum dixit: Secretum meum mihi, secretum meum mihi (Isa. XXIV, 16). Sed nec ab ipso cui advenit, potest ante videri, quam teneri, juxta illud beati Job de se confitentis: Si venerit ad me, non videbo eum; si abierit, non intelligam (Job IX, 11). Nec veniens quippe videtur, nec recedens intelligitur, qui solummodo, dum praesens est, lumen est animae et intellectus, quo invisibilis videtur, et incogitabilis intelligitur. Caeterum quam admirabilis sit, licet occultus sit iste adventus Domini, quam suavi et felici stupore in admirationem sui suspendat et expendat animam contemplantis, quomodo omnia ossa interioris hominis acclament ei, Domine, quis similis tui (Psal. XXXIV, 10)? norunt quidem experti; sed utinam satis desiderent experiri non experti: ita duntaxat, ut non temeraria curiositas faciat scrutari majestatem opprimendos a gloria, sed pia charitas suspirare dilectum suscipiendos a gratia. Suscipiens enim mansuetos Dominus, humilians autem peccatores usque ad terram (Psal. CXLVI, 6), superbis resistit, humilibus autem dat gratiam (Jac. IV, 6). Cum igitur primus adventus sit gratiae; novissimus, gloriae: iste nimirum, gratiae pariter et gloriae; in quo videlicet per consolantem gratiam futuram nobis utcunque datur praelibare gloriam. Cum igitur in primo Deus majestatis visus sit contemptibilis, in novissimo autem videndus sit terribilis, in isto medio videtur mirabilis et amabilis: ut nec propter dignationem gratiae, qua se praebet amabilem, possit esse contemptui, sed admirationi; nec propter magnificentiam gloriae, qua mirabilis apparet, sit terrori, sed potius consolationi. De primo dicebat Videns: Vidimus eum, et non erat ei species, neque decor: unde nec reputavimus eum (Isa. LIII, 2, 3). De ultimo expavescit etiam justus, dicens: Et quis stabit ad videndum eum? (Malach. III, 2.) De isto autem dicit Apostolus: Gloriam Domini speculantes, in eamdem imaginem transformamur a gloria in gloriam, tanquam a Domini Spiritu (II Cor. III, 18). Mirabile prorsus, et amabile, cum Deus amor amantis sensibus illabitur: cum sponsam Sponsus in unitate Spiritus amplectitur, et in eamdem cum illa imaginem transformatur; per quam, velut per speculum, Domini gloriam speculatur. Quam beati, quorum ardens charitas hanc praerogativam jam meruit obtinere! sed et illi beati, quorum sancta simplicitas idem quandoque potest sperare! Et illi quidem jam de fructu amoris, sui capiunt solatium laboris: isti autem tanto fortasse majore merito, quanto interim minore solatio, portantes pondus diei et aestus, exspectant adventum remunerationis. Nos igitur, fratres, quos nondum consolatur tam sublimis experientia, ut patientes simus usque ad adventum Domini, consoletur interim certa fides et pura conscientia, quae tam feliciter quam fideliter cum Paulo dicat: Scio cui credidi, et certus sum quia potens est depositum meum servare in illum diem (II Tim. I, 12): in adventum scilicet gloriae magni Dei, et Salvatoris nostri Jesu Christi, cui est gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Moreover, just as it is intermediate in the order of time, so also in the likeness it bears, this spiritual advent stands between the two bodily advents and, as a kind of mediator, shares in both. The first advent was hidden and humble; the last will be manifest and admirable; this one, however, is hidden yet wondrous. I call it hidden\u2014not because it is unknown to the one to whom he comes, but because he comes to that person in secret. Hence that glorious soul, glorying within itself, said: \u201cMy secret is for me, my secret is for me\u201d (Isa. XXIV, 16). Yet even by the one to whom he comes, he cannot be seen before he is possessed, in accordance with the blessed Job\u2019s confession about himself: \u201cIf he comes to me, I shall not see him; if he goes away, I shall not understand\u201d (Job IX, 11). For he is neither seen as he comes nor understood as he departs; only while he is present is he the light of the soul and of the intellect, by which the invisible is seen and the unthinkable is understood. But how wondrous this advent of the Lord is, even though hidden; how, with what sweet and joyful astonishment, it suspends and absorbs the soul that contemplates him in wonder at himself, so that all the bones of the inner person cry out to him, \u201cLord, who is like you?\u201d (Psal. XXXIV, 10)\u2014those who have experienced it know; but would that those who have not experienced it desired enough to experience it. Let it be only in such a way that reckless curiosity does not lead one to scrutinize the majesty and be overwhelmed by the glory, but devout love makes one sigh for the Beloved and be received by grace. For the Lord receives the meek, but humbles sinners to the ground (Psal. CXLVI, 6); he resists the proud, but gives grace to the humble (Jac. IV, 6). Since, then, the first advent is one of grace and the last one of glory, this advent is plainly of both grace and glory: in it, through the grace that consoles us, we are in some measure given a foretaste of the glory that is to come. Since, then, in the first advent the God of majesty appeared contemptible, while in the last he will be seen as terrifying, in this middle advent he is seen as wondrous and lovable: so that, because of the condescension of grace by which he makes himself lovable, he cannot be despised but rather admired; and because of the magnificence of the glory in which he appears wondrous, he is not a cause of terror but rather of consolation. Of the first advent the Seer said: \u201cWe saw him, and there was in him neither appearance nor beauty; therefore we did not regard him\u201d (Isa. LIII, 2, 3). Of the last even the just man is afraid, saying: \u201cAnd who will stand to see him?\u201d (Malach. III, 2.) But of this advent the Apostle says: \u201cBeholding the glory of the Lord, we are transformed into the same image, from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord\u201d (II Cor. III, 18). Truly wondrous and lovable it is when God, who is love, glides into the senses of the one who loves; when the Bridegroom embraces the bride in the unity of the Spirit and is transformed with her into the same image, through which, as through a mirror, the glory of the Lord is beheld. How blessed are those whose ardent love has already merited this privilege! Yet blessed also are those whose holy simplicity may one day hope for the same. The former already draw comfort from the fruit of love for their labor; the latter, perhaps with all the greater merit because meanwhile they have less consolation, bear the burden of the day and the heat while awaiting the advent of their reward. Therefore, brothers, we whom so sublime an experience does not yet console: that we may be patient until the coming of the Lord, may certain faith and a pure conscience console us meanwhile, saying with Paul, as happily as faithfully: \u201cI know whom I have believed, and I am certain that he is able to guard my deposit until that day\u201d (II Tim. I, 12)\u2014that is, until the advent of the glory of the great God and our Savior Jesus Christ, to whom be glory forever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon II: How to Go Out to Meet the Coming Christ", "topics": ["Advent", "joy", "prophets", "messengers of good news", "Christ the King"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo III, Quod homo debeat semper esse paratus Domino advenienti, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 18-22.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Amos 4:12", "Luke 12:40", "Acts 1:7", "Psalm 17:24 (historical numbering)", "Wisdom 5:7 (printed locator; the words are Wisdom 4:7)", "Psalm 56:4\u20135 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 4:9 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 18:19", "Psalm 56:8\u20139 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:6 (historical numbering)", "2 Kings 15\u201318 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Samuel)", "Psalm 58:5\u20136 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 138:24 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 8:20", "Sirach 15:1\u20132", "Wisdom 6:17", "Song of Songs 5:2", "Psalm 118:131 (historical numbering)", "Luke 24:28\u201329", "Genesis 19:2\u20133", "Ecclesiastes 7:24", "Song of Songs 5:5\u20136", "Matthew 15:28", "Isaiah 58:2", "Psalm 88:15 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:14", "Proverbs 18:17", "Psalm 31:5 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 13:19", "Psalm 64:8 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 66:5 (printed locator; see note)", "Psalm 100:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 79:3 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This third Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric begins from the uncertainty of the hour of Christ's coming and of one's own death: the only security is never to be secure. David is the model of a soul kept ready through persecution, and justice and judgment prepare a dwelling for the Lord. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quod homo debeat semper esse paratus Domino advenienti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "That a man must always be ready for the coming Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Paratus est Israel in occursum Domini, quoniam venit (Amos IV, 12). Et vos, fratres, estote parati; quia, qua hora non putatis, Filius hominis veniet (Luc. XII, 40). Nihil certius quam quod veniat, sed nihil incertius quam quando veniat. Usque adeo siquidem non est nostrum scire tempora, vel momenta, quae Pater posuit in sua potestate (Act. I, 7), ut nec angelis qui assistunt ei, de die illa vel hora scire datum sit. Dies quoque noster ultimus, certissimus nobis quod veniat: incertissimus est quando, vel ubi, vel unde nobis adveniat; nisi quod, ut ante nos dictum est, senibus est in januis, qui juvenibus est in insidiis. Et utinam vel illi se observarent, qui mortem tam paratam intrare, imo jam intrantem vident. An non ex parte jam intravit, quae aliquas partes corporis jam permortuas occupavit? In pluribus tamen semimortuis videre est adhuc viventem concupiscentiam mundi: frigescunt membra, et fervet avaritia: vita finitur, et ambitio protrahitur. Quandoquidem nobis quoque quibus fortassis aetas, aut salus aliquid longius promittere videtur, quanto minus mors in conspectu est, tanto magis utique, si sapimus, suspecta est: ne videlicet dies illa, sicut fur in nocte, incautos et imparatos nos comprehendat; quae in insidiis posita, quominus videri aut caveri potest, eo magis timenda est. Una igitur securitas est, nunquam esse securum: quatenus timor se observans reddat semper paratum; donec securitas succedat timori, non timor securitati. Observabo me, inquit sapiens, ab iniquitate mea (Psal. XVII, 24), quandoquidem non possum a morte mea; sciens quia justus, si morte praeoccupatus fuerit, in refrigerio erit (Sap. V, 7), imo in morte triumphat, cui non dominatur iniquitas in vita. Quam pulchrum, fratres, quam beatum, in morte non modo securum, sed et gloriosum triumphare; ex auctoritate conscientiae, spiritu et voce Martini cruentam bestiam, si astare praesumpserit, increpare; Judici venienti et pulsanti gratanter aperire! Tum vero videas, heu, similes mei trepidare, inducias petere, nec accipere: oleum conscientiae lamentis poenitentiae velle emere, nec tempus sufficere: larvales illas facies declinare velle, nec posse: a facie irae tonantis velle in corpore delitescere, et cogi exire! Exibit spiritus ejus, et revertetur peccator in terram suam, unde sumptus est. In illa die peribunt omnes cogitationes eorum. Scio quidem, quia conditionis humanae est, sub discrimine exitus conturbari, cum etiam perfecti nolunt exspoliari, sed supervestiri: et qui nihil sibi sunt conscii, quia tamen non in hoc sunt justificati, necesse habent judicium, quod ignorant, vereri. Sed, sive conditionis affectu, sive sanctitatis defectu, sive judicii metu conturbata fuerit anima mea, inquit justus: Tu, Domine, misericordiae memor eris, mittesque misericordiam tuam et veritatem tuam, et eripies animam meam de medio catulorum leonum (Psal. LVI, 4, 5): et qui prius conturbatus eram, postmodum in pace in idipsum dormiam et requiescam (Psal. IV, 9). Vocavit Deus terram, et audivit eum in tremore. Cum enim auditum fecit de coelo judicium, auditu solo terra tremuit, et terrore purgata quievit. Beati qui nunc eatenus excoquunt in se omnem scoriam peccati, ut tunc solo sufficiat timore purgari. Mecum siquidem servo inutili et negligenti bene agitur, si vel per ignem salvus fiam; si, crematis lignis, feno, stipula, quae mihi coarcervavi, vel semiustus evadam. Et quidem comparatione mali, bonum vel per ignem salvari; sed indubitanter longe melius, sola purgatione metus consummari: optimum autem et felicissimum, nec metu turbari."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Israel is prepared to meet the Lord, for he comes\" (Amos IV, 12). \"And you, brothers, be ready; for the Son of Man will come at an hour you do not expect\" (Luc. XII, 40). Nothing is more certain than that he will come, but nothing is more uncertain than when he will come. Indeed, it is so far beyond our power to know the times or seasons, which the Father has placed in his own power (Act. I, 7), that it has not even been granted to the angels who stand by him to know of that day or hour. Our last day too is most certain to us in that it will come, but it is most uncertain when, or where, or from what quarter it will come upon us \u2014 except that, as has been said before our time, it stands at the door for the old, while it lies in ambush for the young. And would that even those would watch themselves who see death so ready to enter \u2014 indeed already entering! For has it not in part already entered, seeing that it has already taken possession of some parts of the body already wholly dead? Yet in many who are half-dead one may still see the desire of the world alive: the limbs grow cold, yet avarice burns hot; life comes to its end, yet ambition is drawn out still further. Since even for us, to whom perhaps our age or our health seems to promise a somewhat longer life, the less death is in view, the more, certainly, if we are wise, it ought to be suspected: lest that day, like a thief in the night, seize us unwary and unprepared; for being set in ambush, the less it can be seen or guarded against, the more it is to be feared. There is therefore but one security: never to be secure \u2014 so that fear, keeping watch over itself, may render us always ready, until security succeed to fear, and not fear to security. \"I will keep myself,\" says the wise man, \"from my iniquity\" (Psal. XVII, 24), since I cannot keep myself from my death; knowing that the just man, if he be overtaken by death, shall be in refreshment (Sap. V, 7) \u2014 indeed he triumphs in death, over whom iniquity has not had dominion in life. How beautiful, brothers, how blessed, to triumph in death not merely secure but even glorious; with the authority of conscience, in the spirit and voice of Martin, to rebuke the bloody beast should it presume to stand near; to open gladly to the Judge as he comes and knocks! Then indeed you would see, alas, those like me trembling, asking for a delay and not obtaining it; wishing to buy the oil of conscience with the laments of penitence, and time not sufficing; wishing to turn away from those ghastly faces, and not being able; wishing to hide in the body from the face of the thundering wrath, and being forced to go forth! \"His spirit shall go forth, and the sinner shall return to his own earth, from which he was taken. In that day all their thoughts shall perish.\" I know indeed that it belongs to the human condition to be troubled at the crisis of one's departure, since even the perfect do not wish to be stripped, but rather to be clothed over; and those who are conscious of nothing against themselves \u2014 because they are not thereby justified \u2014 must still fear the judgment which they do not know. But whether my soul be troubled by the feeling natural to this condition, or by a lack of holiness, or by fear of judgment, says the just man: \"You, Lord, will be mindful of your mercy, and you will send your mercy and your truth, and you will deliver my soul from the midst of the young lions\" (Psal. LVI, 4, 5); and I, who was troubled before, will afterward sleep and rest in peace, in the very same (Psal. IV, 9). \"God called the earth, and it heard him in trembling.\" For when he made judgment heard from heaven, the earth trembled at the mere hearing, and, purged by terror, grew quiet. Blessed are those who now so thoroughly burn away in themselves all the dross of sin, that then it may suffice for them to be purged by fear alone. For with me, a useless and negligent servant, it goes well if I am saved even by fire; if, when the wood, hay, and stubble which I have heaped up for myself are burned away, I escape even half-burned. And indeed, by comparison with evil, it is good to be saved even through fire; but without doubt it is far better to be made perfect by the purification of fear alone; but best and most blessed of all is not even to be troubled by fear.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Idcirco paratus esto, o verus Israel, in occursum Domini: non solum, ut cum venerit et pulsaverit, aperias ei; sed etiam adhuc illo longe agente, alacer et gratulabundus occurras ei; et tanquam fiduciam habens in die judicii, regnum ejus advenire pleno preceris affectu. Si ergo vis tunc paratus inveniri: Ante judicium para justitiam tibi (Eccli. XVIII, 19), juxta consilium Sapientis; paratus sis ad omne opus bonum faciendum; paratus sis nihilominus ad omne malum patiendum: ut sine reprehensione cordis cantet os tuum, Paratum cor meum, Deus, paratum cor meum. Paratum quidem, ut te cooperante faciam digna; paratum, ut patiar indigna. Et ideo paratum ad utrumque, quod cantabo et psallam in gloria mea: id est, laudabo et gloriabor pro utroque. Et statim in hoc ipsum se justus excitat, Exsurge, inquiens, psalterium et cithara (Psal. LVI, 8, 9); id est, cor meum et caro mea, ad exsultandum in Deum vivum: cor scilicet, propter speciales actiones; caro, propter suas passiones. Cor namque sapiens quae sursum sunt, psalterium est sonans de superiori: caro patiens quae deorsum sunt, cithara sonans ab inferiori. Sic et alibi devotionem suam David offerens Deo, Paratus sum, inquit, ut custodiam mandata tua: et tam paratus, quod non sum turbatus (Psal. CXVIII, 6), cum tentatio irrueret, et persecutio grassaretur. Cum persequeretur aemulus, malediceret servus, animam quaereret filius (II Reg. XV-XVIII); non sum tamen turbatus, quominus custodirem mandata perfectionis evangelicae: retribuentibus mala pro bonis, retribuens bona pro malis; sollicitus de persecutorum salute, moestus de morte; sustinens a servo exprobrari, non sustinens ab amico vindicari. Ecce ante Evangelium evangelica perfectio, charitas scilicet patiens et benigna, etiam erga malignos, quos tolerat. Quia igitur tam paratus erat, fiducialiter Domino nimirum occurrebat: Sine iniquitate, inquit, cucurri, et direxi distortos, quantum in me fuit. Tu exsurge igitur in occursum meum, occurrentis tibi: et quia nec sic pertingere possum ad celsitudinem tuam, nisi tu condescendens operi manuum tuarum porrigas dexteram: Exsurge in occursum meum (Psal. LVIII, 5, 6), et vide si via iniquitatis in me est: et si inveneris nescio quam viam iniquitatis, amove a me: et de lege tua misertus mei, deduc me in via aeterna (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24), id est, Christo, qui via est per quam itur, et aeternitas ad quam pervenitur; via immaculata, mansio beata."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Therefore be ready, O true Israel, to meet the Lord: not only that, when he comes and knocks, you may open to him; but also, while he is still far away, that you may run to meet him eagerly and with gladness; and, as one having confidence for the day of judgment, pray with full affection that his kingdom may come. If, then, you wish to be found ready at that time: Before judgment prepare justice for yourself (Eccli. XVIII, 19), according to the counsel of the Wise Man; be ready to do every good work; be ready likewise to suffer every evil: so that your mouth may sing, without reproach from your heart, My heart is ready, O God, my heart is ready. Ready, indeed, that with you cooperating I may do worthy deeds; ready, that I may suffer unworthy things. And therefore ready for both, so that I shall sing and make melody in my glory: that is, I shall praise and glory for both. And immediately for this very purpose the just man rouses himself, saying, Awake, psaltery and harp (Psal. LVI, 8, 9); that is, my heart and my flesh, to exult in the living God: the heart, namely, for its special actions; the flesh, for its sufferings. For the heart, wise toward the things that are above, is a psaltery sounding from above; the flesh, suffering the things that are below, is a harp sounding from below. So also elsewhere David, offering his devotion to God, says: I am ready to keep your commandments; and so ready that I was not troubled (Psal. CXVIII, 6), when temptation rushed in and persecution raged. When a rival persecuted him, a servant cursed him, a son sought his life (II Reg. XV-XVIII); yet I was not troubled, so as to fail to keep the commandments of evangelical perfection: to those repaying evil for good, repaying good for evil; anxious for the salvation of his persecutors, sorrowful over the death; enduring reproach from a servant, not enduring vengeance taken by a friend. Behold, before the Gospel, evangelical perfection\u2014namely, charity that is patient and kind, even toward the malicious, whom it bears with. Because, then, he was so ready, he indeed ran to meet the Lord with confidence: Without iniquity, he says, I have run, and I have set straight the crooked, as far as it was in me. Arise, therefore, to meet me as I run to meet you: and because even so I cannot attain to your height, unless you, condescending to the work of your hands, stretch forth your right hand: Arise to meet me (Psal. LVIII, 5, 6), and see whether there is a way of iniquity in me: and if you find some way of iniquity I know not of, remove it from me: and, having mercy on me according to your law, lead me in the way everlasting (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24), that is, in Christ, who is the way by which one travels, and the eternity to which one arrives; the way without stain, the blessed dwelling.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Puto tamen quia et ante mansionem illam beatam, si paraveris Domino viam immaculatam, ipse dignabitur saepius in via ponere gressus suos, dilatabitque subtus te gressus tuos, ut dilatato corde curras viam mandatorum; cujus angustum forte causabaris initium. Sapientia namque, ut ipsa perhibet, in viis justitiae ambulat (Prov. VIII, 20): et: Qui continens est justitiae apprehendet illam, et obviabit illi quasi mater honorificata. Dignos se ipsa circuit quaerens, et in viis suis ostendit se illis (Eccli. XV, 1, 2, et Sap. VI, 17). Si minus aut nunquam tibi eam occurrere conquereris, vide ne forte viam tuam corruperis. Sic enim scriptum habes: Insipientia viri corrumpit viam suam; Deus autem causabitur in corde suo. Ipse siquidem stat ad ostium et pulsat: ut si quis aperuerit ei, coenet cum eo coelestis mensae delicias. Loquitur sponsa: Vox dilecti mei pulsantis; Aperi mihi, soror mea sponsa (Cantic. V, 2). Aperi mihi cor tuum, et cibabo illud. Aperi mihi os tuum, et implebo illud. Os meum, inquit David, aperui, et attraxi spiritum (Psal. CXVIII, 131). Spiritus enim ante faciem nostram Christus Dominus, non modo invitandus, sed et attrahendus quadam violentia precis, vehementiaque fervoris in hospitium cordis: sicut de duobus discipulis in typum hujus rei historia refert Evangelii (Luc. XXIV, 28, 29). Et ipse siquidem non ob aliud, nisi ut probet devotionem charitatis tuae, aliquando se fingit longius ire: sicut et duo angeli, Loth adorante et obsecrante ut intrarent, dissimulabant tamen dicentes, Minime, sed manebimus in platea. Sed quid dicit Scriptura? Compulit illos oppido, ut diverterent ad eum (Gen. XIX, 2, 3). Pia violentia, qua regnum coeleste rapitur: laudabilis importunitas, quae Christum hospitem, aut angelos promeretur. Sed quid est, inquis, quod Jesus fingit se longius ire? Quid, nisi illud quod Ecclesiastes confitetur de se? Dixi, sapiens efficiar; et ipsa longius recessit a me (Eccle. VII, 24). Hoc autem sponsa manifestius exponit, nostram utique quotidianam exprimens querimoniam, ubi ait: Surrexi aperire dilecto meo: at ille declinaverat, atque transierat. Quaesivi illum, et non inveni (Cantic. V, 5, 6): vocavi, et non respondit mihi, sicut nec mulieri Chananeae. Spiritum sapientiae invocabas, spiritum gratiae quaerebas in oratione: si tibi videtur longius recedere, ne desperes, sed importunus insiste; donec respondeatur tibi, Magna est fides tua, fiat tibi sicut petisti (Matth. XV, 28)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. I think, however, that even before that blessed dwelling, if you prepare for the Lord an unstained way, he himself will deign often to set his steps upon the way, and will enlarge your steps beneath you, so that with enlarged heart you may run the way of the commandments, whose beginning you perhaps complained was narrow. For Wisdom, as she herself attests, walks in the ways of justice (Prov. VIII, 20): and: He who holds fast to justice shall lay hold of her, and she shall meet him like a mother held in honor. She goes about seeking out those worthy of herself, and shows herself to them in their ways (Eccli. XV, 1, 2, et Sap. VI, 17). If you complain that she meets you little or never, see whether perhaps you have corrupted your way. For thus it is written: The folly of a man corrupts his way, but he will accuse God in his own heart. For he himself stands at the door and knocks, so that if anyone opens to him, he may dine with him on the delights of the heavenly table. The bride speaks: The voice of my beloved knocking; Open to me, my sister, my spouse (Cantic. V, 2). Open to me your heart, and I will feed it. Open to me your mouth, and I will fill it. I opened my mouth, says David, and drew in the breath (Psal. CXVIII, 131). For the breath before our face is Christ the Lord, who is not only to be invited but also to be drawn in, by a certain violence of prayer and by the vehemence of fervor, into the lodging of the heart: just as the Gospel story relates of the two disciples, as a figure of this matter (Luc. XXIV, 28, 29). And he himself, indeed, for no other reason than to test the devotion of your charity, sometimes pretends to go farther: just as the two angels also, when Lot worshipped them and begged that they would come in, nevertheless feigned, saying, No, but we will stay in the street. But what does Scripture say? He pressed them greatly, so that they turned aside to him (Gen. XIX, 2, 3). A pious violence, by which the kingdom of heaven is seized; a praiseworthy importunity, which wins Christ as guest, or the angels. But what is it, you ask, that Jesus pretends to go farther? What, but that which Ecclesiastes confesses of himself? I said, I shall become wise; and she herself withdrew farther from me (Eccle. VII, 24). But the bride explains this more plainly, expressing indeed our daily complaint, where she says: I rose up to open to my beloved; but he had turned aside and passed on. I sought him, and I did not find him (Cantic. V, 5, 6): I called him, and he did not answer me, just as he did not answer the Canaanite woman either. You were calling upon the spirit of wisdom, you were seeking the spirit of grace in prayer: if he seems to you to withdraw farther off, do not despair, but press on importunately, until it is answered to you, Great is your faith; be it done to you as you have asked (Matth. XV, 28).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Cum tamen invitas Jesum, vide ne Deum majestatis ad sordidum et indignum invites hospitium; ubi nec te ipsum patiatur habitare quietum uxor litigiosa, seu fumus, aut stillicidium. Non enim nisi in pace fit locus ejus: nec aliud quam justitia et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus. Nunc autem me, inquit, de die in diem quaerunt, et scire vias meas volunt, quia gens quae justitiam fecerit, et judicium Dei sui non dereliquerit (Isa. LVIII, 2). Justitia, inquit, et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Noli causari quasi sumptuosum [fort. praesumptuosum] sit, viresque tuae excedat paupertatis, tam magnifico, tamque potenti praeparare domum hospiti: habes ad manum unde id possis. Humanum dico propter infirmitatem carnis tuae, vel magis angustiam mentis tuae. Confitere perfecte de praeterito: bonam voluntatem habe de caetero; pax enim hominibus bonae voluntatis (Luc. II, 14), et hoc judicio et justitia sedem Altissimo praeparasti. Vis manifestius audire de confessione, quod eam oporteat fieri in praeparatione adventus Domini? Justus, ait Scriptura, in initio sermonis accusator est sui. Et quid sequitur? Venit amicus ejus (Prov. XVIII, 17), qui modo ante confessionem inimicus assistebat longius. Cum enim dixit, Confitebor adversum me injustitiam meam Domino (Psal. XXXI, 5); et ipse remisit: Venit, inquam, et investigabit eum. Prorsus investigabit, tanquam potio valida, scrutans corda et renes; pertingens usque ad divisionem animae et spiritus, exhauriens noxia de medullis animae, mentisque visceribus, purgans affectus ut fructum plus afferat: de cujus primitivo fructu confessionis jam gaudet pater agricola. Ipse tamen qui post confessionem nonnunquam et antequam invocetur, adest, nonnunquam exspectat ut tu prior eum invites: et ut meritum tibi augeat, saepe diutius dissimulat; ut tu attentius psallendo, instantius orando, dulciter violentus intrare compellas: alioquin planget propheta quod civitates austri clausae sint, nullo introeunte (Jerem. XIII, 19). Cum ergo dicere potueris, Paratum cor meum, Deus, quia vacuatum a malis; paratum cor meum, quia sanctis plenum desideriis: tunc sedulus fac quod sequitur, Cantabo et psalmum dicam (Psal. LXIV, 8). Et quicunque sit sonus cantantis vel psallentis, haec sit intentio mentis: Exsurge, gloria mea, exsurge in occursum mei; quia, quantum potui, occurri tibi. O benigne Jesu, quam alacer et promptus, quam laetus et festivus occurris hujusmodi devotioni! quam hilarem te ostendis in viis istis! Occurristi, inquit Isaias, laetanti et facienti justitiam: in viis tuis recordabuntur tui (Isa. LXVI, 5). Si enim psallas sapienter, in via immaculata veniet; veniet qui et illuminabit abscondita tua, ut quae nescis intelligas mysteria Scripturarum, eritque ut dicas: Psallam et intelligam in via immaculata, quando veniet ad me (Psal. C, 1, 2). Excita, Domine, potentiam tuam, quae excitet pigritiam nostram in occursum tui: et veni, ut salvos nos facias (Psal. LXXIX, 3), Salvator mundi; qui vivis et regnas per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Yet when you invite Jesus, see that you do not invite the God of majesty into a sordid and unworthy lodging\u2014one in which not even you yourself could be permitted to dwell in peace, on account of a quarrelsome wife, or smoke, or a leaking roof. For his place is made only in peace, and the preparation of his throne is nothing other than justice and judgment. 'But now,' he says, 'they seek me day after day, and desire to know my ways, because they are a nation that has done righteousness and has not forsaken the judgment of its God' (Isa. LVIII, 2). 'Justice and judgment,' he says, 'are the preparation of his throne' (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Do not object, as though it were a costly thing [perhaps read: presumptuous], and beyond the strength of your poverty, to prepare a house for so magnificent and so powerful a guest: you have at hand the means by which you can do this. I speak in human terms, on account of the weakness of your flesh, or rather the narrowness of your mind. Make full confession concerning the past; have good will concerning what remains; for 'peace is to men of good will' (Luc. II, 14), and by this judgment and justice you will have prepared a seat for the Most High. Do you wish to hear more plainly about confession, that it must be made in preparing for the Lord's coming? 'The just man,' Scripture says, 'at the beginning of his speech is his own accuser. And what follows? His friend comes' (Prov. XVIII, 17)\u2014he who, just before, before the confession, stood far off as an enemy. For when he said, 'I will confess my injustice against myself to the Lord' (Psal. XXXI, 5), he himself forgave it; 'he comes,' I say, 'and will search him out.' He will indeed search him out thoroughly, like a strong medicine, examining hearts and loins, reaching even to the division of soul and spirit, draining out the harmful matter from the marrow of the soul and the inward parts of the mind, purging the affections so that it may bear more fruit; and in the first-fruit of this confession the Father, the husbandman, already rejoices. Yet he himself, who after confession is sometimes present even before he is invoked, at other times waits for you to invite him first; and in order to increase your merit, he often pretends for a longer time not to notice, so that you, by singing more attentively, by praying more insistently, may compel him, sweetly violent, to enter\u2014otherwise the prophet will lament that the cities of the south are shut up, with no one entering (Jerem. XIII, 19). Therefore, when you are able to say, 'My heart is ready, O God,' because it is emptied of evils; 'my heart is ready,' because it is filled with holy desires\u2014then diligently do what follows: 'I will sing and recite a psalm' (Psal. LXIV, 8). And whatever be the sound of the one singing or playing, let this be the intention of the mind: 'Arise, my glory, arise to meet me; for, as much as I was able, I have come to meet you.' O kind Jesus, how eager and ready, how joyful and festive you come to meet such devotion! How cheerful you show yourself along these paths! 'You met,' says Isaiah, 'the one who rejoices and does justice: in your ways they will remember you' (Isa. LXVI, 5). For if you sing wisely, he will come in the undefiled way; he will come who will also illumine your hidden things, so that you may understand the mysteries of the Scriptures which you did not know, and it will come to be that you say: 'I will sing and understand in the undefiled way, when he comes to me' (Psal. C, 1, 2). Stir up, O Lord, your power, that it may stir up our sluggishness to meet you; and come, that you may save us (Psal. LXXIX, 3), O Savior of the world, who live and reign through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints quia gens quae justitiam fecerit; the Vulgate of Isaiah 58:2 reads quasi gens. The edition's reading is kept. The following reference is printed Isa. LXVI, 5 (the last numeral is damaged in the scan); the words in viis tuis recordabuntur tui are Isaiah 64:5."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon III: That One Should Always Be Ready for the Coming Lord", "topics": ["Advent", "readiness", "confession", "psalmody", "hospitality to Christ"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo IV, De vita solitaria, et de via Domino paranda, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 21-26.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Isaiah 40:3", "Jeremiah 2:2", "Isaiah 35:2", "Psalm 64:13 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 5:17", "Psalm 106:8\u20139 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 4:10", "Ecclesiastes 9:17", "John 14:6", "Psalm 118:5 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 3:17", "Proverbs 16:7", "Psalm 80:14\u201315 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 13:3 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 17:11", "Psalm 36:7 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 21:11", "Job 21:13", "Psalm 17:31, 33 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 34:6 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 4:19", "Isaiah 40:4", "Luke 3:5", "Ezekiel 18:29", "Wisdom 1:4", "Psalm 141:4 (historical numbering)", "John 12:35", "Proverbs 6:23", "Proverbs 5:12, 14", "Psalm 118:110\u2013111, 94\u201395 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 36:31 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 11:9", "Numbers 22:27, 31", "Proverbs 24:16", "Psalm 145:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 93:18 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 16:5 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 138:24 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fourth Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Addressed to monks in the solitude of the desert, it reads John the Baptist's cry to prepare the way for the Lord as a call to correct one's life. Guerric commends the peaceful ways of wisdom, the spotless way of chastity, the baptism of penance and knowledge of God's law. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De vita solitaria, et de via Domino paranda."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the solitary life, and on preparing the way for the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Vox clamantis in deserto, Parate viam Domini (Isa. XL, 3). Primo omnium considerandam arbitror gratiam deserti, beatitudinem eremi; quae ab initio gratiae, quieti sanctorum meruit consecrari. Consecravit nobis plane habitationem deserti vox clamantis in deserto, Joannes praedicans et tribuens baptismum poenitentiae in deserto; quanquam et ante ipsum quibusque sanctissimis prophetarum, velut auditorium Spiritus, amica semper fuerit solitudo. Longe tamen excellentior atque divinior ipsi loco gratia sanctificationis accessit, cum Jesus Joanni successit: qui etiam, antequam praedicare poenitentibus inciperet, locum poenitentibus praeparandum putavit; atque per dies quadraginta, dum versatur in deserto, velut purificans locum, novumque novae vitae dedicans locum, tyrannum qui incubabat, omnemque malitiam et subtilitatem ejus, non tam sibi, quam illis qui accolae eremi futuri erant, superavit. Si ergo elongasti fugiens et manens in solitudine, ibi permane, ibi exspecta eum qui te salvum faciet a pusillanimitate spiritus, et tempestate. Quantalibet ingruat tibi tempestas bellorum, quantacunque patiaris in deserto, penuriam etiam victualium, noli pusillanimitate spiritus redire mente in Aegyptum. Felicius te pascet eremus manna, id est pane angelorum, quam Aegyptus ollis carnium. Ipse Jesus quidem in eremo jejunavit; sed multitudinem sequentium se in deserto, saepe et mirabiliter pavit. Saepius autem et mirabilius te satiabit, qui tanto gratiore merito, quanto sanctiore proposito secutus es eum in deserto. Cum enim diutius eum tui oblitum putaveris, ipse suae non immemor bonitatis, consolabitur te, et dicet tibi: Recordatus sum tui, miserans adolescentiam tuam, et charitatem desponsationis tuae, quando secuta es me in deserto (Jerem. II, 2). Tunc plane ponet desertum tuum quasi delicias paradisi: et ipse confiteberis quia gloria Libani data est ei, decor Carmeli et Saron (Isa. XXXV, 2). Sicut enim in multis hodie locis, etiam juxta litteram illud propheticum videmus impleri, Pinguescent speciosa deserti (Psal. LXIV, 13); et, Deserta in ubertatem versa advenae comedent (Isa. V, 17): sic quilibet Scripturae locus, qui ante tibi sterilis videbatur et aridus, repente ad benedictionem Dei mira replebitur ubertate, ac pinguedine spiritus; ut de saturitate mentis eructes hymnum laudis, Confiteantur, inquiens, Domino, misericordiae ejus, et mirabilia ejus filiis hominum: quia satiavit animam inanem, et animam esurientem satiavit bonis (Psal. CVI, 8, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. The voice of one crying in the desert: Prepare the way of the Lord (Isa. XL, 3). First of all, I think we must consider the grace of the desert, the blessedness of the wilderness; which from the very beginning of grace deserved to be consecrated to the rest of the saints. The voice crying in the desert clearly consecrated for us the dwelling-place of the desert\u2014John, preaching and bestowing the baptism of repentance in the desert; although even before him solitude had always been a friend to the holiest of the prophets, as a hearing-chamber of the Spirit. Yet a far more excellent and more divine grace of sanctification came upon that very place when Jesus succeeded John, who, even before he began to preach to penitents, thought it right that the place be prepared for penitents; and for forty days, while he dwelt in the desert, as though purifying the place and dedicating this new place to a new life, he overcame the tyrant who lay brooding there, and all his malice and cunning\u2014not so much for his own sake as for the sake of those who were to be future dwellers of the wilderness. If, then, you have fled far off and remain in solitude, stay there; wait there for him who will save you from faintness of spirit and from the storm. However great a storm of battles may rush upon you, whatever you may suffer in the desert, even a lack of provisions, do not, through faintness of spirit, turn back in your mind to Egypt. The wilderness will feed you more happily with manna, that is, with the bread of angels, than Egypt with its pots of meat. Jesus himself indeed fasted in the wilderness; but the multitude that followed him in the desert he often and wonderfully fed. But he will satisfy you still more often and still more wonderfully, since you have followed him into the desert with a purpose all the holier, and thus with a merit all the more pleasing. For when you have thought for a long time that he has forgotten you, he himself, not forgetful of his own goodness, will console you and say to you: I have remembered you, having pity on your youth and on the love of your betrothal, when you followed me into the desert (Jerem. II, 2). Then he will surely make your desert like the delights of paradise, and you yourself will confess that the glory of Lebanon has been given to it, the beauty of Carmel and Sharon (Isa. XXXV, 2). For just as today, in many places, we see that prophetic word fulfilled even according to the letter\u2014The beautiful places of the desert shall grow fat (Psal. LXIV, 13); and, The deserts turned into abundance shall strangers eat (Isa. V, 17)\u2014so too any passage of Scripture which before seemed to you barren and dry will suddenly, by God's blessing, be filled with wondrous abundance and with fatness of spirit, so that from the fullness of your mind you will belch forth a hymn of praise: Let them give praise to the Lord, he says, for his mercies, and his wonderful works to the children of men: for he has satisfied the empty soul, and filled the hungry soul with good things (Psal. CVI, 8, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Illud sane mirabili gratia provisum est divinae dispensationis, ut in his desertis nostris quietem habeamus solitudinis, nec tamen consolatione careamus gratiae et sanctae societatis. Licet cuique sedere solitarium, et tacere, eo quod neminem patiatur interpellantem: nec tamen vae soli, eo quod non habeat confoventem: aut, si ceciderit, sublevantem (Eccle. IV, 10). In frequentia hominum sumus, et sine turba sumus; velut in urbe versamur, nullum tamen tumultum patimur, quominus vox clamantis in deserto a nobis audiatur: si modo interius, sicut exterius silentium habeamus. Verba namque sapientium, ait Salomon, audiuntur in silentio, plus quam clamor principis inter stultos (Eccle. IX, 17). Et nunc quoque si medium silentium teneant omnia interiora tua, omnipotens sermo tibi secretus illabetur de sede paterna. Felix igitur, qui sic elongavit fugiens tumultum mundi: qui tam procul recessit in secretum et solitudinem quietae mentis, ut non modo vocem Verbi, sed et Verbum ipsum; non Joannem, sed Jesum mereatur audire! Interim tamen quid vox Verbi nobis clamet audiamus, ut quandoque de voce ad Verbum proficiamus. Parate, inquit, viam Domino: rectas facite semitas ejus (Isa. XL, 3). Parat viam, qui corrigit vitam: rectam facit semitam, qui per arctiorem se dirigit viam. Plane vita correcta, via est recta, per quam Dominus veniat ad nos, qui in hoc ipsum praevenit nos. A Domino siquidem gressus hominis dirigentur, et viam sic ejus volet, ut gratanter per eam veniens ad hominem, cum eo jugiter ambulet. Nisi enim suum ad nos adventum ipse praeveniat, qui est via, veritas et vita (Joan. XIV, 6); non potest via nostra corrigi secundum regulam veritatis, ac per hoc nec dirigi ad vitam aeternitatis. In quo vero corrigit adolescentior viam suam, nisi in custodiendo sermones ejus, nisi in sequendo vestigia ejus, qui viam fecit se ipsum, qua veniamus ad ipsum? Utinam dirigantur viae meae, ad custodiendas, o Domine, vias tuas (Psal. CXVIII, 5); ut propter verba labiorum tuorum custodiam etiam vias duras: quae etsi durae videantur carni, quia infirma est, suaves et pulchrae videbuntur spiritui, si promptus est! Viae, inquit, ejus viae pulchrae, et omnes semitae ejus pacificae (Prov. III, 17). Non solum pacatae, sed et pacificae sunt viae sapientiae: quia, cum placuerint Domino viae hominis, etiam inimicos ejus convertet ad pacem (Id. XVI, 7). Israel, si in viis meis, inquit, ambulasset, pro nihilo forsitan inimicos eorum humiliassem, et super tribulantes eos misissem manum meam (Psal. LXXX, 14, 15). Quare enim contritio et infelicitas in viis eorum, nisi quia viam pacis non cognoverunt (Psal. XIII, 3)? Eapropter, si quando vidi hominem inquietum, contumacem, litigiosum, gravissima tentatione vexari, vel tribulatione flagellari, recordabar illius proverbii: Semper jurgia quaerit malus: angelus autem crudelis mittetur contra eum (Prov. XVII, 11), cui traditur forsitan in afflictionem carnis, ut spiritus salvus sit in die Domini. Si tamen aliquando prosperatur via impiorum, noli aemulari in eo qui prosperatur in via sua, in homine faciente injustitias (Psal. XXXVI, 7): quia haec via eorum scandalum ipsis; ut tanto liberius in mortem currant, quanto minus sepitur eis via qua ambulant. De istis Sapiens loquitur: Via peccantium complanata lapidibus: sed in fine illorum, inferi, tenebrae et poenae (Eccli. XXI, 11): et, Ducunt in bonis dies suos, et in puncto descendunt ad inferos (Job XXI, 13). Beati igitur immaculati in via, qui ambulant in via Domini! quia, etsi de torrente in via bibunt, propterea exaltabunt caput; quia caput suum, quod sequuntur per viam passionis, consequentur in fine mansionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. It was surely provided by a wonderful grace of divine dispensation that in these deserts of ours we should have the quiet of solitude, and yet not lack the consolation of grace and holy fellowship. Each one may sit solitary and be silent, in that he suffers no one interrupting him; and yet it is not \"woe to him alone,\" in that he has no one to comfort him, or, if he should fall, to lift him up (Eccle. IV, 10). We are amid a throng of men, and yet we are without a crowd; we dwell as it were in a city, yet we suffer no tumult that would keep us from hearing the voice of one crying in the desert\u2014provided only that we keep interior silence as we keep exterior silence. For, as Solomon says, the words of the wise are heard in silence more than the shouting of a prince among fools (Eccle. IX, 17). And even now, if all your inward parts keep deep silence, the omnipotent Word will steal into you secretly from the Father's throne. Happy, then, is he who has so withdrawn, fleeing the tumult of the world, who has retreated so far into the secrecy and solitude of a quiet mind, that he deserves to hear not only the voice of the Word, but the Word itself\u2014not John, but Jesus! Meanwhile, however, let us listen to what the voice of the Word cries to us, so that one day we may advance from the voice to the Word. \"Prepare,\" he says, \"the way for the Lord; make straight his paths\" (Isa. XL, 3). He prepares the way who corrects his life; he makes the path straight who directs himself along the narrower way. Clearly a corrected life is a straight way, by which the Lord may come to us\u2014he who has gone before us for this very purpose. For indeed the steps of man are directed by the Lord, and he so wills his way that, gladly coming by it to man, he may walk continually with him. For unless he himself, who is the way, the truth, and the life (Joan. XIV, 6), should go before us in his own coming to us, our way cannot be corrected according to the rule of truth, and therefore cannot be directed toward the life of eternity. But in what does the young man correct his way, except in keeping his words, except in following the footsteps of him who made himself the way by which we come to him? \"Would that my ways were directed, O Lord, to the keeping of your ways\" (Psal. CXVIII, 5); that for the sake of the words of your lips I might keep even the hard ways\u2014which, though they seem hard to the flesh, because it is weak, will seem sweet and beautiful to the spirit, if it is willing! \"Her ways,\" it says, \"are beautiful ways, and all her paths are peaceful\" (Prov. III, 17). The ways of wisdom are not only peaceable but peace-making: because, when the ways of a man please the Lord, he will turn even his enemies to peace (Id. XVI, 7). \"If Israel,\" he says, \"had walked in my ways, I might perhaps have humbled their enemies with ease, and would have set my hand against those who troubled them\" (Psal. LXXX, 14, 15). For why is there destruction and unhappiness in their ways, except that they have not known the way of peace (Psal. XIII, 3)? For this reason, whenever I have seen a restless, stubborn, quarrelsome man vexed by some very grave temptation or scourged by tribulation, I would recall that proverb: \"The wicked man always seeks quarrels; but a cruel angel will be sent against him\" (Prov. XVII, 11)\u2014to whom perhaps he is handed over for the affliction of the flesh, that his spirit may be saved in the day of the Lord. Yet if sometimes the way of the impious prospers, do not envy the man who prospers in his way, the man who does unjust things (Psal. XXXVI, 7): for this very way of theirs is a snare to them; so that they run the more freely into death, the less their way is hedged in as they walk it. Of such the Wise Man speaks: \"The way of sinners is made smooth with stones; but at their end are hell, darkness, and torments\" (Eccli. XXI, 11); and, \"They spend their days in good things, and in a moment they go down to hell\" (Job XXI, 13). Blessed, then, are the undefiled in the way, who walk in the way of the Lord! For even if they drink of the torrent in the way, they shall therefore lift up the head; because the Head whom they follow through the way of suffering, they shall attain at the end of their dwelling.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Quidquid igitur occurrerit vobis in via Domini, laeto ac dilatato corde currite viam mandatorum Dei: quia etsi pusillanimis arcta videtur via, tamen recta; et si dura videtur, immaculata est. Plane beati immaculati in via! qui sic immaculato calle transeunt viam mundi, ut non inquinent vestimenta sua: aut certe, si inquinaverint, secundum beatitudinis non amittunt locum; sed lavant vestimenta sua baptismo poenitentiae, quo Joannes baptizat in eremo, viam parans Domino. Via utique immaculata, castitas; via grata, per quam Dominus gratiae dignanter veniat: cui propheta cantat, Deus meus, impolluta via ejus. Impolluta castitas Virginis, qua venit in uterum illius: impolluta necesse est sit castitas hominis, qua veniat in animam illius. Felix conscientia, cui convenit vox illa: Deus praecinxit me virtute continentiae, et posuit immaculatam viam meam (Psal. XVII, 31, 33)! Verumtamen, ne de castitate sine virtutibus aliis glorieris, quasi jam viam immaculatam Domino paraveris: scito quia nihilominus necesse est ut via haec etiam recta sit, plana sit, ut etiam tenebrosa vel lubrica non sit. Nam via impiorum tenebrae et lubricum; et angelus Domini persequens eos (Psal. XXXIV, 6). Et via impiorum tenebrosa: nesciunt ubi corruant (Prov. IV, 19). Putamusne, fratres, in aliquo nostrum inveniatur adhuc pravum aliquid in voluntatibus, asperum in moribus, tenebrosum per ignorantiam sensus, lubricum per inconstantiam actus? Quomodo autem scripturam de via paranda implebimus, nisi, sicut scriptum est, faciamus: Ut sint prava, in directa; et aspera, in vias planas (Isa. XL, 4, et Luc. III, 5)?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Therefore, whatever may meet you on the way of the Lord, run the way of God's commandments with a joyful and enlarged heart: for even if the way seems narrow to the faint-hearted, it is nevertheless straight; and if it seems hard, it is spotless. Truly blessed are the spotless in the way! who so pass through the way of the world by a spotless path that they do not stain their garments: or if indeed they have stained them, they do not lose the second place of blessedness; but they wash their garments in the baptism of penance, with which John baptizes in the desert, preparing the way for the Lord. Surely the spotless way is chastity; the pleasing way, by which the Lord of grace may deign to come: to whom the prophet sings, 'My God, his way is undefiled.' Undefiled was the chastity of the Virgin, by which he came into her womb; undefiled must necessarily be the chastity of man, by which he comes into his soul. Happy is the conscience to which that voice applies: 'God has girded me with the strength of continence, and has made my way spotless' (Psal. XVII, 31, 33)! Yet, lest you boast of chastity apart from the other virtues, as though you had already prepared a spotless way for the Lord, know that it is likewise necessary that this way be also straight, be level, and also not be dark or slippery. For the way of the wicked is darkness and slipperiness; and the angel of the Lord pursues them (Psal. XXXIV, 6). And the way of the wicked is dark: they do not know where they will fall (Prov. IV, 19). Do we suppose, brothers, that there may still be found in any of us something crooked in our wills, something rough in our conduct, something darkened through ignorance of understanding, something slippery through inconstancy of action? But how shall we fulfill the Scripture about preparing the way, unless, as it is written, we do this: that the crooked places be made straight, and the rough ways be made smooth (Isa. XL, 4, et Luc. III, 5)?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Primo itaque necesse est ut, si qua est in nobis prava et distorta voluntas, corrigatur, et ad regulam divinae voluntatis dirigatur. Aliter videbimur dicere, sicut impii dicunt: Non est recta via Domini. Sed statim refellit nos irrefragabilis veritas, et increpat terribilis severitas: Nunquid non viae meae rectae sunt, et non magis viae vestrae pravae sunt (Ezech. XVIII, 29)? Qui igitur viam Domino parare festinas primo omnium bona sit voluntas; quoniam in malevolam animam non introibit sapientia (Sap. I, 4). Cum autem hoc modo posueris prava in directa, scito quia non minus necesse est ut aspera sint in vias planas: ut scilicet omnem morum complanes asperitatem, et in quamdam vitae socialis sternas aequalitatem, ne mitis et suavis ille viator offensus asperitate viae resiliat. Quidni resiliret ille mitis et humilis corde, qui non nisi super mansuetum requiescit et humilem; cum etiam humanus omnis sensus refugiat et horreat hominem iracundum, et intractabilem? Sed, et si eatenus profecisti in via Domini, ut et propositum rectitudinis, et disciplinam mansuetudinis assecutus sis; profecisti quidem, sed non perfecisti, nisi lucerna sit pedibus tuis verbum Dei, et lumen semitis tuis. In via enim qua ambulabam, ait, absconderunt laqueum mihi (Psal. CXLI, 4). Et sunt viae quae videntur hominibus rectae, novissimum autem earum ducit in profundum inferni. Via enim impiorum tenebrosa, ut Salomon ait: nesciunt ubi corruant (Prov. IV, 19). Hoc et Dominus ait: Qui ambulat in tenebris, nescit quo vadat (Joan. XII, 35). At vero mandatum lucerna est, et lex lux, et via vitae increpatio disciplinae (Prov. VI, 23). Qui enim increpationes relinquit, errat. Si igitur sapis, in via qua nunquam ambulasti, non ipse tibi doctor aut ductor eris, sed aurem magistris inclinabis, et increpationibus eorum acquiesces, et consiliis; et operam scientiae et lectioni dabis, ne sero poenitens dicas: Cur detestatus sum disciplinam, et increpationibus non acquievit cor meum; nec audivi vocem docentium me, et magistris non inclinavi aurem meam? Pene fui in omni malo, in medio ecclesiae, et synagogae (Prov. V, 12-14). Quod enim scientia legis liberet a laqueis, audi confitentem David: Posuerunt peccatores laqueum mihi, sed de mandatis tuis non erravi. Quomodo id potuisti? Quia haereditate acquisivi testimonia tua. Et in via testimoniorum tuorum delectatus sum, sicut in omnibus divitiis. Me exspectaverunt peccatores ut perderent me: sed per hoc evasi, quia testimonia tua intellexi (Psal. CXVIII, 110, 111, 94, 95). Lex Dei, inquit, in corde justi, et per hoc non supplantabuntur gressus ejus (Psal. XXXVI, 31). Hinc et filius ejus Salomon ait: Simulator ore decipit amicum suum, justi autem liberabuntur scientia (Prov. XI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. First of all, then, it is necessary that, if there is in us any crooked and twisted will, it be corrected and directed to the rule of the divine will. Otherwise we shall seem to say, as the wicked say: 'The way of the Lord is not straight.' But immediately unanswerable truth refutes us, and terrible severity rebukes us: 'Are not my ways straight, and are not your ways rather crooked?' (Ezech. XVIII, 29). Therefore you who hasten to prepare the way for the Lord, let your will be good above all else; for wisdom will not enter into a malevolent soul (Sap. I, 4). But when you have in this way made the crooked straight, know that it is no less necessary that the rough places be made into smooth ways: namely, that you smooth away every roughness of conduct, and spread a certain evenness in your life together with others, lest that gentle and sweet traveler, offended by the roughness of the way, draw back. And why would he not draw back\u2014he who is gentle and humble of heart, who rests only upon the meek and the humble\u2014since even every human sense flees and shudders at an angry and intractable man? But even if you have progressed thus far on the way of the Lord, so as to have attained both the purpose of rectitude and the discipline of gentleness, you have indeed made progress, but you have not reached perfection, unless the word of God is a lamp to your feet and a light to your paths. For 'in the way in which I walked,' he says, 'they hid a snare for me' (Psal. CXLI, 4). And there are ways that seem right to men, but their end leads into the depths of hell. For the way of the wicked is dark, as Solomon says: they do not know where they will fall (Prov. IV, 19). The Lord also says this: 'He who walks in darkness does not know where he goes' (Joan. XII, 35). But indeed the commandment is a lamp, and the law is light, and the way of life is the rebuke of discipline (Prov. VI, 23). For whoever abandons rebukes goes astray. If, then, you are wise, on a way you have never walked, you will not be your own teacher or guide, but you will incline your ear to teachers, and you will acquiesce to their rebukes and counsels; and you will give diligence to knowledge and to reading, lest, repenting too late, you say: 'Why have I hated discipline, and my heart did not consent to rebukes; and I did not hear the voice of those who taught me, nor did I incline my ear to my teachers? I was almost in every evil, in the midst of the church and the assembly' (Prov. V, 12-14). That knowledge of the law frees from snares, hear David confessing: 'Sinners have laid a snare for me: but I have not strayed from your commandments.' How were you able to do that? 'Because I have acquired your testimonies as an inheritance. And in the way of your testimonies I have delighted, as in all riches. Sinners waited for me, to destroy me: but through this I escaped, because I understood your testimonies' (Psal. CXVIII, 110, 111, 94, 95). 'The law of God,' he says, 'is in the heart of the just, and therefore his steps shall not be overthrown' (Psal. XXXVI, 31). Hence also his son Solomon says: 'He who dissembles with his mouth deceives his friend, but the just shall be delivered by knowledge' (Prov. XI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed utinam haec via, qua salutem vel Salvatorem suscipere debemus, sicut jam non est tenebrosa per ignorantiam veri, sic nec lubrica sit per inconstantiam operis. Sed, sicut Balaam cadens apertos habebat oculos (Num. XXII, 27, 31), sic et nos: et videmus apertis oculis per scientiam, et cadimus per negligentiam. Volentes et scientes peccamus, et libentes lubricamus: nec tam lubricum viae causari possumus, quam propositum mentis, id est, pedem cui innitimur. Quis enim non ambulat in lubrico, dum in mundo, dum in corpore vivit luteo? Non igitur tam via quam pes in culpa est, qui in via Dei minus firmus est. Sed nunquid, qui cadit, non adjiciet ut resurgat? Septies cadit justus in die, et septies resurgit (Prov. XXIV, 16). Dominus enim erigit elisos, Dominus dirigit justos (Psal. CXLV, 8). Nam, si dicebam: Motus est pes meus; misericordia tua, Domine, adjuvabat me (Psal. XCIII, 18). Quando igitur aliter non potes cadens, surgens viam ambula, clamans tamen semper ei, quem sequi et consequi desideras, Perfice gressus meos in semitis tuis ut non moveantur vestigia mea (Psal. XVI, 5); et, si via iniquitatis in me est (ut est humana fragilitas), deduc me in via aeterna (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24); quatenus per te, qui es via et veritas, perveniam ad te, qui es veritas, et vita aeterna: tibi gloria per aeterna saecula. Amen"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But would that this way, by which we must receive salvation or the Savior, just as it is now no longer dark through ignorance of the truth, so also might not be slippery through inconstancy of action. But, just as Balaam, when falling, had his eyes open (Num. XXII, 27, 31), so also do we: we see with open eyes through knowledge, and yet we fall through negligence. We sin willingly and knowingly, and we slip gladly: nor can we blame the slipperiness of the way so much as the disposition of the mind, that is, the foot on which we lean. For who does not walk on slippery ground, while living in the world, while living in a body of clay? It is not, then, so much the way that is at fault as the foot, which is less firm on the way of God. But shall he who falls not add to his falling a rising up again? The just man falls seven times in a day, and seven times rises again (Prov. XXIV, 16). For the Lord raises up those who are cast down, the Lord sets the just upright (Psal. CXLV, 8). For, if I said, 'My foot is moved'; your mercy, O Lord, helped me (Psal. XCIII, 18). Therefore, when you cannot do otherwise, walk the way falling and rising, yet always crying out to him whom you desire to follow and to attain: 'Perfect my steps in your paths, that my footsteps be not moved' (Psal. XVI, 5); and, 'if the way of iniquity be in me' (as is human frailty), 'lead me in the way everlasting' (Psal. CXXXVIII, 24); so that through you, who are the way and the truth, I may come to you, who are the truth and life everlasting: to you be glory through everlasting ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon IV: On the Solitary Life and on Preparing the Way for the Lord", "topics": ["Advent", "solitude", "desert", "silence", "way of the Lord", "John the Baptist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-advent", "calendar_label": "Advent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-advent-05", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De adventu Domini, sermo V, Quomodo proficiendum in paranda via Domino, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 26-30.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Advent", "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "Isaiah 40:3", "Psalm 118:33 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 76:11 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 7:14", "Psalm 54:20 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 110:10 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:59 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 146:7 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 3:2", "Proverbs 16:5", "Psalm 84:14 (historical numbering)", "Genesis 3, Septuagint (printed locator; the words are Genesis 4:7 in the Septuagint)", "Matthew 3:3", "Isaiah 30:21", "Isaiah 35:8", "Isaiah 5:21", "1 Corinthians 3:18", "James 3:17", "Isaiah 35:9", "Proverbs 10:29", "Psalm 118:96 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 35:9\u201310"], "occasion": "De adventu Domini", "occasion_detail": "Advent; no particular Sunday is specified", "occasion_label": "Advent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fifth Advent sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. The way for the Lord is prepared by walking it and has no end, so progress must always begin again. Fear of the Lord leads to penance, which goes before grace. Through Isaiah's holy way and the serpent shedding its skin in a narrow passage, Guerric keeps his monks on the royal road toward its promised end. The source names Advent without assigning a particular Sunday."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO V."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON V.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo proficiendum in paranda via Domino."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How to Make Progress in Preparing the Way for the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Parate viam Domino (Isa. XL, 3). Via Domini, fratres, quam parare jubemur, ambulando paratur, parando ambulatur. Et licet multum profeceritis in ea, semper tamen vobis restat paranda: ut de his in quae pervenistis, tendatis et extendatis vos in ulteriora; quatenus per singulos profectus Dominus, cujus adventui via paratur, quasi novus, ac seipso semper major occurrat. Ideo bene justus orat: Legem pone mihi, Domine, viam justificationum tuarum, et exquiram eam semper (Psal. CXVIII, 33). Ideo forsitan via aeterna dicta est; quia licet providentia semitam cujusque investigaverit, terminumque constituerit quousque proficiat; terminum tamen non habet illius, in quam proficitis, bonitatis natura. Itaque sapiens impigerque viator cum consummatus fuerit, tunc incipiet: ita scilicet obliviscens quae retro sunt, ut quotidie dicat sibi, Nunc coepi (Psal. LXXVI, 11). Exsultat ut gigas, quem nil terreat ad currendam viam mandatorum Dei: Idithumque factus, pigros qui in via resident, facile transilit in impetu cursus sui. Et licet novissima diei hora venerit, consummatus in brevi explet tempora multa; ut repente de novissimo primus, inter primos coronam accipiat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Prepare the way for the Lord\" (Isa. XL, 3). The way of the Lord, brothers, which we are commanded to prepare, is prepared by walking, and is walked by preparing. And however much progress you have made in it, it always remains for you to prepare further: so that from the point you have reached, you may stretch and extend yourselves into what lies beyond; so that through each advance the Lord, for whose coming the way is prepared, may meet you as it were new, and ever greater than himself. For this reason the just man rightly prays: \"Set before me a law, O Lord, the way of your justifications, and I will always seek it\" (Psal. CXVIII, 33). For this reason, perhaps, it is called the eternal way; because although providence has traced out the path for each person and set a limit up to which he may progress, the nature of that goodness toward which you are progressing has no limit. And so the wise and tireless traveler, when he has been made complete, then begins; forgetting, that is, the things that are behind, so that he may say to himself every day, \"Now I have begun\" (Psal. LXXVI, 11). He exults like a giant, whom nothing may frighten from running the way of God's commandments: having become like Idithun, he easily leaps over those slothful ones who sit by the roadside, in the very impetus of his course. And even though he comes at the last hour of the day, being made complete, he fulfills much time in a short span; so that from being last he suddenly becomes first, and receives the crown among the first.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sed nos qui de profectu viae ejus loquimur, utinam vel initium ejus apprehenderimus! Meo namque sensu atque judicio non parum in ea proficit qui coepit; si tamen vere coepit, si viam civitatis habitaculi invenit. Quam pauci sunt, ait Veritas, qui inveniunt eam! (Matth. VII, 14.) Quam multi sunt qui in solitudine errant! prorsus omnes solitarii, id est omnes superbi, qui se solos reputant. Nullus eorum adhuc dicere potest: Nunc coepi, haec mutatio dexterae Excelsi (Psal. LXXVI, 11). Non enim est illis commutatio, ut desinant esse quod erant, quia non timuerunt Deum (Psal. LIV, 20), cum initium sapientiae sit timor Domini (Psal. CX, 10). Si initium sapientiae, nimirum et viae bonae. Ipse nempe timor Domini, ipse est qui statim illud sapiens consilium generat in corde viri, ut dicere possit: Cogitavi vias meas, et converti pedes meos in testimonia tua (Psal. CXVIII, 59). Ipse confessionem persuadet, quam Psalmista initium boni operis esse docet: Incipite, inquit, domino in confessione (Psal. CXLVI, 7). Ipse ad poenitentiam superbum inclinat, ut vocem clamantis in deserto viamque parari jubentis, et unde incipiendum sit ostendentis, audiat: Poenitentiam agite; appropinquabit enim regnum coelorum (Matth. III, 2). Huic sententia consonat Salomonis, qui manifesta definitione rudes instruit, dicens: Initium viae bonae, facere justa (Prov. XVI, 5). Quid est enim justa facere, nisi poenitentiam agere? nisi de nobisipsis quod Deo debemus exigere, et quod rapuimus exsolvere? Haec est justitia quae ante Dominum ambulat, viamque ei placitam parat, sicut scriptum est: Justitia ante eum ambulabit, et ponet in via gressus suos (Psal. LXXXIV, 14). Sicut enim Joannes Jesum, sic poenitentia gratiam praecedit; illam scilicet gratiam, qua post satisfactionem reconciliati, suscipimur in osculo pacis. In hac siquidem poenitentiae via, grato atque hilari occursu obviant sibi, seseque osculantur justitia et pax; justitia scilicet hominis se punientis, et pax Dei ignoscentis: laetumque ac jucundum celebrant in osculo sancto foedus reconciliationis. Denique ne parvi aestimetur meriti haec justitia hominis de semetipso facientis judicium et justitiam, neve oratiunculae, et vota pigrorum se laboribus poenitentium comparare praesumant; quid de ipsa Salomon sentiat; audiant: Initium, inquit, viae bonae, facere justa: accepta autem Deo magis, quam immolare hostias (Prov. XVI, 5). Si ergo videris hominem huic poenitentiae professum, regularibus ac sanctis laboribus, quorum debitor est, singulares oratiunculas praeferentem; ingere ei hanc Sapientis sententiam: Initium viae bonae, facere justa: accepta autem magis Deo quam immolare hostias. Similiter, si videris hominem, adversus quem frater suus aliquid habet, munus seu laudum, seu sacramentorum, ad altare offerentem; repete ei nihilominus eumdem sermonem: Initium viae bonae, facere justa, ut fratri reconcilieris: accepta autem Deo magis quam immolare hostias. Nam et illo etiam spectat haec sententia, ut daturus eleemosynam, prius, si quem defraudavit, vel simplum restituat. Sic et tu quoque voluntaria oris tui beneplacita nullatenus Deo esse credideris, illa scilicet quae in psalmis, vel in orationibus secretis ei obtuleris; si propterea canonem psalmorum ex Regula constitutum negligentius exsolveris. Nonne, si recte offeras, et non recte dividas peccasti? (Gen. III, sec. LXX.) Rectas, inquit, facite semitas ejus (Matth. III, 3, et Isa. XL, 3), vos qui paratis viam Domini."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But we who speak of progress on this way\u2014would that we had even grasped its beginning! For in my own sense and judgment, he who has begun makes no small progress on it; provided, however, that he has truly begun, if he has found the way to the city of habitation. \"How few are they,\" says the Truth, \"who find it!\" (Matth. VII, 14.) How many are there who wander in solitude! Indeed, all the solitary\u2014that is, all the proud, who consider themselves alone. None of them can yet say: \"Now I have begun; this is the change of the right hand of the Most High\" (Psal. LXXVI, 11). For there is no change for them, that they should cease to be what they were, because they have not feared God (Psal. LIV, 20), since the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom (Psal. CX, 10). If it is the beginning of wisdom, surely it is also the beginning of the good way. For it is indeed the fear of the Lord itself that immediately generates in a man's heart that wise counsel, so that he can say: \"I have thought on my ways, and turned my feet unto your testimonies\" (Psal. CXVIII, 59). It is this fear that persuades to confession, which the Psalmist teaches is the beginning of a good work: \"Begin,\" he says, \"to the Lord with confession\" (Psal. CXLVI, 7). It is this fear that inclines the proud man to penance, so that he hears the voice of the one crying in the desert, commanding that the way be prepared, and showing where one must begin: \"Do penance, for the kingdom of heaven will draw near\" (Matth. III, 2). With this sentence agrees that of Solomon, who instructs the unlearned with a plain definition, saying: \"The beginning of a good way is to do what is right\" (Prov. XVI, 5). For what is it to do what is right, except to do penance? Except to exact from ourselves what we owe to God, and to repay what we have stolen? This is the righteousness that walks before the Lord, and prepares for him a way pleasing to him, as it is written: \"Righteousness shall walk before him, and shall set her steps in the way\" (Psal. LXXXIV, 14). For just as John preceded Jesus, so penance precedes grace; that grace, namely, by which, after making satisfaction, reconciled, we are received with the kiss of peace. In this way of penance, indeed, righteousness and peace meet one another with a welcome and joyful encounter, and kiss one another; namely, the righteousness of the man punishing himself, and the peace of God forgiving; and they celebrate, in a holy kiss, the glad and joyous covenant of reconciliation. Finally, lest this righteousness of a man passing judgment and doing justice upon himself be reckoned of little merit, and lest little prayers and the vows of the slothful presume to compare themselves to the labors of the penitent, let them hear what Solomon thinks of it: \"The beginning,\" he says, \"of a good way is to do what is right; but more acceptable to God than to offer sacrifices\" (Prov. XVI, 5). If, therefore, you see a man professed to this penance, preferring his own private little prayers to the regular and holy labors of which he is a debtor, press upon him this sentence of the Wise Man: \"The beginning of a good way is to do what is right; but it is more acceptable to God than to offer sacrifices.\" Likewise, if you see a man, against whom his brother has some grievance, offering a gift at the altar, whether of praises or of the sacraments, repeat to him nevertheless the same word: \"The beginning of a good way is to do what is right, that you be reconciled to your brother; but it is more acceptable to God than to offer sacrifices.\" For this sentence also looks to this: that one who is about to give alms should first, if he has defrauded anyone, restore at least the simple amount. So also you yourself should by no means believe that the voluntary offerings of your mouth are pleasing to God\u2014those, namely, which you offer him in psalms or in private prayers\u2014if on that account you perform more negligently the canon of psalms established by the Rule. Have you not sinned, if you offer rightly but do not divide rightly? (Gen. III, sec. LXX.) \"Make straight,\" he says, \"his paths\" (Matth. III, 3, et Isa. XL, 3), you who prepare the way of the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O Domine, tu parasti directiones nobis, si modo nos recte ambulemus in illis. Tu legem posuisti nobis, viam justificationum tuarum, per illum scilicet, quem hujus sanctae institutionis Legislatorem dedisti: Haec via, inquiens, ambulate in ea, neque ad dexteram, neque ad sinistram (Isai. XXX, 21). Haec via plane, sicut propheta promiserat: Via directa nobis, ita ut stulti non errent per eam (Isa. XXXV, 8). Junior fui, etenim senui; et non vidi, si bene memini, stultum errantem per viam: cum vix aliquem sapientem viderim, qui rectitudinem ejus tenere potuerit. Vae, qui sapientes estis in oculis vestris, et coram vobismetipsis prudentes (Isa. V, 21)! quos sapientia vestra devios abducit a via salutis, nec sinit stultitiam sequi Salvatoris. Non ignorabat ille stultus propter Christum, quid haec sapientia terrena, animalis, diabolica mali faciat, qui dicebat: Si quis sapiens esse videtur in hoc saeculo, stultus fiat, ut sit sapiens apud Deum (I Cor. III, 18). Optabilis stultitia, quae divino judicio reputatur sapientia, quae hominem non sinit errare de via. Haec stultitia, nisi fallor, est illa desursum sapientia, pudica, pacifica, modesta, suadibilis, bonis consentiens (Jacob. III, 17): a quibus omnibus quam procul abhorreat sapiens in oculis suis, utinam nunquam experientia ipsa probasset in oculis nostris. Si contristat vos arrogantia, seu pervicacia hujusmodi sapientium, consolatur abundantius nostrorum pax atque modestia stultorum, qui quanto minus sibi credunt, tanto suadibiliores bonis consentiunt. Tales itaque stulti non errant facile de via Dei, sed juxta verbum prophetae semper audiunt vocem post tergum monentis: Haec via, ambulate in ea, neque ad dexteram, neque ad sinistram (Isai. XXX, 21): ne scilicet per fervorem excedentes modum, aut per teporem subsistentes citra modum, legem praescriptam, viamque deseratis regiam. Spiritus est ad dexteram, caro est ad sinistram; et haec sibi colluctantia semper sibi adversantur, ut non quaecunque vult, sive ista, sive ille, faciamus: ne, si plus justo sequimur impetum spiritus, declinemus ad dexteram; aut, si plus justo carni indulgemus, deviemus ad sinistram."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O Lord, you have prepared straight paths for us, if only we walk rightly in them. You have set before us a law, the way of your righteous judgments, through him whom you gave as the Lawgiver of this holy institution, saying: 'This is the way, walk in it, neither to the right hand nor to the left' (Isai. XXX, 21). This is indeed the way, as the prophet had promised: 'A way made straight for us, so that fools should not err therein' (Isa. XXXV, 8). I have been young, and now am old; and I have not seen, if I remember rightly, a fool going astray in the way \u2014 though I have scarcely seen any wise man who was able to keep to its straight course. Woe to you who are wise in your own eyes, and prudent in your own sight (Isa. V, 21)! whom your wisdom leads astray from the way of salvation, and does not allow to follow the foolishness of the Savior. That man, a fool for Christ's sake, was not ignorant of what evil this earthly, animal, diabolical wisdom works, who said: 'If any man seem to be wise in this world, let him become a fool, that he may be wise with God' (I Cor. III, 18). Desirable is that folly, which by divine judgment is reckoned wisdom, which does not allow a man to stray from the way. This folly, unless I am mistaken, is that wisdom from above, chaste, peaceable, modest, easy to be persuaded, agreeing with the good (Jacob. III, 17): from all of which how far the man who is wise in his own eyes recoils, would that experience itself had never proved it before our eyes! If the arrogance or stubbornness of such wise men grieves you, the peace and modesty of our fools consoles you all the more abundantly, who, the less they trust themselves, the more readily they agree with what is good. Such fools, then, do not easily stray from the way of God, but, according to the word of the prophet, always hear behind them the voice warning: 'This is the way, walk in it, neither to the right hand nor to the left' (Isai. XXX, 21): that is, lest, by exceeding the measure through fervor, or by falling short of the measure through lukewarmness, you abandon the prescribed law and the royal road. The spirit is on the right, the flesh is on the left; and these, warring against each other, are always opposed to one another, so that we do not do whatever either the one or the other wills: lest, if we follow the impulse of the spirit more than is right, we turn aside to the right; or, if we indulge the flesh more than is right, we deviate to the left.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hanc denique viam justificationum Domini, viam veritatis quam elegistis, quanta laude praedicet Isaias, delectat me cum charitate vestra recordari. Et erit, inquit, ibi, id est, in antiquis cubilibus draconum, in terra utique deserta et invia, semita et via, sicut hodie cernere est: quia in feris et incultis hominibus, qui sine lege vivebant, in ipsis invenitur ordo vitae, et disciplina. Et haec via, inquit, sancta vocabitur: quia nimirum sanctificatio est peccatorum, perditorumque salus. Quanta vero virtute ac reverentia sanctitatis praeemineat, inde probat propheta, quia non transibit, inquit, pollutus per eam (Isa. XXXV, 8). O mi Isaia, ibunt igitur qui polluti sunt per aliam viam? Imo potius huc omnes, huc veniant, hac incedant. Immundis enim praecipue Christus hanc viam stravit, qui venit quaerere et salvum facere quod perierat in viis saeculi. Quid igitur? transibit pollutus per viam sanctam? Absit! Veniat quantumlibet pollutus ad eam, non tamen transibit pollutus per eam: quia cum transierit, jam pollutus non erit. Admittit quidem via sancta pollutum, sed statim abluit admissum, quia diluit omne commissum, tanquam vere baptismus alter poenitentium. Hic plane, hic baptizat, non Joannes, sed Jesus baptismo poenitentiae: hic patet fons domus David in ablutionem peccatoris et menstruatae. Propterea namque via haec pollutum admittit, sed pollutum non transmittit: quia via arcta est, et quasi foramen illud angustum est, quo serpens innovandus cum exuviis suae vetustatis venire potest, sed cum ipsis transire non potest; sed novum suaque nuditate melius vestitum, transitus angustia trajicit, extricans omnem, quem attulerat, squalorem vetustatis. Bene ergo prudentiam serpentis imitari rogamur, qui nec aliter innovari possumus, nisi per angustum coarctemur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Finally, it delights me to recall, together with your charity, with what great praise Isaiah proclaims this way of the Lord's righteous judgments, this way of truth which you have chosen. 'And it shall be there,' he says \u2014 that is, in the ancient lairs of dragons, in a land indeed desert and trackless \u2014 'a path and a way,' as may be seen even today: because among wild and uncultivated men, who lived without law, there is found in them an order of life and discipline. 'And this way,' he says, 'shall be called holy': because it is, assuredly, the sanctification of sinners and the salvation of the lost. But how greatly it excels in the power and reverence of holiness, the prophet proves from this, that, he says, 'the unclean shall not pass through it' (Isa. XXXV, 8). O my Isaiah, shall those who are unclean, then, go by another way? Nay rather, let all come here, let them come here, let them walk by this road. For Christ laid this way especially for the unclean, he who came to seek and to save what had perished in the ways of the world. What then? Shall the unclean pass through the holy way? God forbid! Let him come to it however unclean he may be, yet he shall not pass through it unclean: because when he has passed through, he will no longer be unclean. The holy way indeed admits the unclean man, but immediately washes the one it has admitted, because it washes away every sin committed, being truly, as it were, a second baptism for penitents. Here plainly, here it is Jesus, not John, who baptizes with the baptism of penance: here the fountain of the house of David lies open for the washing of the sinner and of the menstruous woman. For this reason this way admits the unclean man, but does not let the unclean man pass through: because the way is narrow, and is like that narrow opening through which the serpent, needing to be renewed, can come with the worn skin of its old age, but cannot pass through still wearing it; rather, made new and better clothed in its own nakedness, it squeezes through the narrowness of the passage, freeing itself from all the squalor of the old age it had brought with it. Rightly, then, are we urged to imitate the prudence of the serpent, since we cannot be renewed in any other way unless we are compressed through a narrow strait.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sane quod illius antiqui serpentis insidias evadamus, si per hanc arctam viam hujus novi serpentis exemplum sequamur, Isaias promittit, qui de eadem via subdit: Non erit ibi leo, et mala bestia non ascendet per eam, nec invenietur ibi (Isa. XXXV, 9). Securi ergo simus, si de via non exorbitamus. Potest leo ille, qui circuit quaerens quem devoret, juxta iter scandala ponere, laqueos abscondere, viatores rugitu terrere: sed persistentes in via laedere non potest; quippe cui via ipsa terror et supplicium est. Fortitudo nempe simplicis, via Domini, et pavor his qui operantur malum (Prov. X, 29). Si ergo in via es, hoc solum time, ne devies, ne Dominum offendas, qui te per viam ducit, ut dimittat te vagum in via cordis tui: praeter eum ne timeas alienum. Quod si nimis arctam viam causaris, prospice finem, quo te via ducit. Si enim omnis consummationis videris finem, continuo dices: Latum mandatum tuum nimis (Psal. CXVIII, 96). Si minus videre potes, crede videnti Isaiae, qui oculus tui corporis est. Ipse utcunque videbat, cum id quod sequitur dicebat: Et ecce ambulabunt hac via qui liberati fuerint, et redempti a Domino: et venient in Sion cum laude, et laetitia sempiterna super capita eorum Gaudium quoque et laetitiam obtinebunt, fugiet dolor et gemitus (Isai. XXXV, 9, 10). Qui satis hunc finem cogitat, puto quia non solum viam dilatat sibi; sed etiam pennas, ut jam non ambulet, sed volet, assumit sibi. Semper ergo, fratres, cogitate finem praemiorum, et cum omni facultate et alacritate curretis viam mandatorum. Per ipsam vos deducat et perducat ipse, qui est via currentium, et bravium pervenientium, Christus Jesus; cui honor et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-advent-05-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Isaiah promises that we shall indeed escape the snares of that ancient serpent, if along this narrow way we follow the example of this new serpent; for he adds concerning this same way: \"There shall be no lion there, and the evil beast shall not go up by it, nor be found there\" (Isa. XXXV, 9). Let us therefore be secure, if we do not stray from the way. That lion, who goes about seeking whom he may devour, can set stumbling blocks along the road, hide snares, and terrify travelers with his roaring; but he cannot harm those who remain on the way, since the way itself is his terror and his torment. For \"the way of the Lord is strength to the simple, and fear to them that work evil\" (Prov. X, 29). If, then, you are on the way, fear only this: that you may turn aside, that you may offend the Lord who leads you along the way, lest he abandon you to wander in the way of your own heart; apart from him, fear no other. But if you complain that the way is too narrow, look to the end to which the way leads you. For if you see the end of all perfection, you will at once say: \"Your commandment is exceedingly broad\" (Psal. CXVIII, 96). If you cannot see this well enough, believe Isaiah who sees it, for he is the eye of your body. He himself saw it, after a manner, when he said what follows: \"And behold, they shall walk in this way who have been set free and redeemed by the Lord; and they shall come to Sion with praise, and everlasting joy shall be upon their heads. They shall obtain joy and gladness; sorrow and mourning shall flee away\" (Isai. XXXV, 9, 10). He who meditates enough on this end, I think, not only widens the way for himself, but also takes to himself wings, so that he no longer walks but flies. Therefore, brethren, always think on the end of the rewards, and with all ease and eagerness you will run the way of the commandments. May he himself lead and bring you through it, who is the way of those who run and the prize of those who arrive, Christ Jesus; to whom be honor and glory for ever and ever.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Advent Sermon V: How to Make Progress in Preparing the Way for the Lord", "topics": ["Advent", "way of the Lord", "penance", "spiritual progress", "humility"], "url": "sermons/guerric-advent-05.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:all-saints", "calendar_label": "All Saints", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-all-saints", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN SOLEMNITATE OMNIUM SANCTORUM,, SERMO, De pauperibus spiritu., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 205-210.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "All Saints", "Solemnity of All Saints", "Matthew 5:3", "Isaiah 61:1", "Ezekiel 40:37", "Sirach 44:6", "Song of Songs 1:5", "Psalm 119:7 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 17", "Proverbs 31:29", "Psalm 33:11 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 11:25", "Romans 14:17", "Psalm 15:5\u20136 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 48:21 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "In solemnitate omnium sanctorum", "occasion_detail": "Solemnity of All Saints", "occasion_label": "All Saints", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the first Beatitude, \"Blessed are the poor in spirit,\" as the opening of the Gospel and the first of eight steps that lead from renunciation of the world to the office of peacemaker and the merit of martyrdom. Those who possess God possess all things, while the miser is possessed by his money. Poverty of spirit lies more in humility than in want, and the poor already hold the kingdom, carrying God in their hearts. The sermon appears under the heading for the Solemnity of All Saints in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN SOLEMNITATE OMNIUM SANCTORUM,"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE SOLEMNITY OF ALL SAINTS,", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De pauperibus spiritu."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the poor in spirit.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beati pauperes spiritu (Matth. V, 3). Agnosco notum illud ac nobile prognosticum, quod in testimonium sui, necdum natus in carne Dei Filius protulit; jam vero natus, sed nondum notus, sibi competere docuit: Spiritus, inquit, Domini super me, evangelizare pauperibus misit me (Isai. LXI, 1). Ecce pauperes evangelizantur, ecce pauperibus Evangelium regni nuntiatur: Beati, inquit, pauperes spiritu, quoniam ipsorum est regnum coelorum (Matth. V, 3). Laetum prorsus ac novae plenum gratiae Novi Testamenti principium, etiam quantumlibet infidelem aut pigrum provocans ad audiendum, sed amplius ad faciendum, cum scilicet beatitudo miseris, exsulibus et egenis promittitur regnum coelorum. Gratum, inquam, et auspicatum novae legis initium, quando in ipso statim initio, tot beatitudinum benedictiones dat Legislator, quibus delectati eant de virtute in virtutem: per istos videlicet octo gradus ascensionis, quos in corde nostro disposuit structura Evangelii, secundum exemplar et imaginem coelestium, quod Ezechieli quoque ostensum est in monte visionum Dei. Est enim manifeste quidam ascensus cordium, et profectus meritorum iste per ordinem digestus octonarius virtutum, gradatim de imis ad summa perducens virum evangelicae perfectionis, donec ad videndum Deum deorum in Sion ingrediatur templum, de quo propheta dicit: Et in octo gradibus ascensus ejus (Ezech. XL, 37)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Blessed are the poor in spirit\" (Matth. V, 3). I recognize that well-known and noble sign which the Son of God, not yet born in the flesh, brought forth as testimony of himself; and indeed, now born, but not yet known, he taught that it applied to himself: \"The Spirit of the Lord is upon me,\" he says, \"he has sent me to preach the gospel to the poor\" (Isai. LXI, 1). Behold, the poor are evangelized; behold, to the poor the Gospel of the kingdom is announced: \"Blessed,\" he says, \"are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven\" (Matth. V, 3). A truly joyful beginning of the New Testament, full of new grace, which summons even the most unbelieving or slothful to listen, but still more to act \u2014 since blessedness is promised to the wretched, the exiles, and the needy, namely, the kingdom of heaven. A pleasing, I say, and auspicious beginning of the new law, since right at its very outset the Lawgiver gives so many blessings of beatitudes, by which men, delighted, may go from virtue to virtue: namely, through those eight steps of ascent which the structure of the Gospel has arranged in our heart, according to the pattern and image of heavenly things, which was also shown to Ezekiel on the mountain of the visions of God. For there is manifestly a certain ascent of hearts, and a progress of merits, this eightfold order of virtues arranged in sequence, leading step by step from the lowest things to the highest the man of evangelical perfection, until he enters to see the God of gods in Sion, into the temple of which the prophet says: \"And by eight steps was its ascent\" (Ezech. XL, 37).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Prima siquidem virtus inchoantium est renuntiatio saeculi, qua pauperes efficimur spiritu : secunda, mansuetudo, qua nos obedientiae subdimus, et assuescimus : deinde, luctus, quo peccata plorantur, vel virtutes implorantur. Ibi nimirum gustamus, unde amplius esuriamus, et sitiamus justitiam, tam in nobis quam in aliis, zeloque contra peccantes incipimus commoveri. Sed ne zelus immoderatior in vitium feratur, sequitur misericordia, qua temperetur. His ergo studiis, vel exercitiis, cum quis justus et misericors esse didicerit, idoneus erit fortassis contemplationi vacare, et mundando cordi, quo Deus videatur, operam dare. Exercitatus autem et probatus tam in actione, quam in contemplatione, tum demum dignus erit, qui nomen et officium Filii Dei gerens, pater et minister factus aliorum, tanquam mediator et sequester pacificet inter ipsos et Deum: pacificet ipsos ad invicem, vel etiam ad eos qui foris sunt, sicut scriptum est in laude sanctorum patrum, Pacificantes in domibus suis (Eccli. XLIV, 6). In hoc officio qui fidelis et constans fuerit, saepe virtutem et meritum sibi martyrii comparabit, persecutionem patiens propter justitiam; aliquando et ab his pro quibus pugnabit, ut sit ei dicere, Filii matris meae pugnaverunt contra me (Cantic. I, 5); et, Cum his qui oderunt pacem, eram pacificus; cum loquebar illis, impugnabant me gratis (Psal. CXIX, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. For the first virtue of beginners is renunciation of the world, by which we are made poor in spirit; the second, meekness, by which we submit ourselves to obedience and grow accustomed to it; then, mourning, by which sins are wept over, or virtues are implored. There, surely, we taste that from which we may hunger and thirst all the more for justice, both in ourselves and in others, and we begin to be moved with zeal against sinners. But lest this zeal, growing too immoderate, turn into vice, mercy follows, by which it is tempered. By these pursuits, or exercises, when someone has learned to be just and merciful, he will perhaps be fit to devote himself to contemplation, and to give effort to the cleansing of the heart by which God may be seen. But one trained and proved both in action and in contemplation will then at last be worthy \u2014 bearing the name and office of the Son of God, having become a father and minister to others \u2014 to make peace, as a mediator and go-between, between them and God: to make peace among themselves, or even with those who are outside, as it is written in praise of the holy fathers, \"Making peace in their own houses\" (Eccli. XLIV, 6). Whoever is faithful and steadfast in this office will often win for himself the virtue and merit of martyrdom, suffering persecution for the sake of justice \u2014 sometimes even at the hands of those for whom he fights, so that he may say, \"The sons of my mother have fought against me\" (Cantic. I, 5); and, \"With them that hated peace I was peaceable: when I spoke to them, they fought against me without cause\" (Psal. CXIX, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Quantae autem gloriae, quamque copiose mercedis in coelo sit culmen hujus perfectionis, inde utcumque licet aestimare, quod ipsa etiam abrenuntiantium saeculo primordia tantae Dominus praedicat felicitatis, ut dicat: Beati pauperes spiritu, quoniam ipsorum est regnum coelorum. Plane beati qui, projectis vilibus, sed gravibus sarcinulis hujus mundi, nolunt divites fieri, nisi de solo Creatore mundi, propter ipsum, tanquam nihil habentes, et per ipsum omnia possidentes. An non possident omnia qui possident continentem et disponentem omnia, quorum Deus portio est et haereditas; qui, ut nihil desit, timentibus eum, caetera prout expedire novit, dispensat ad utendum, et se ipsum servat ad fruendum? Cum haeres Dei, cohaeres autem Christi, adultus et emancipatus introducetur in plenariam possessionem exspectatae haereditatis, tunc profecto jus absolutum, ac liberum imperium habebit in creaturis: qui modo quanto tempore parvulus est, nihil differt a servo, cum sit Dominus omnium; sed sub tutoribus et actoribus est usque ad tempus praefinitum a Patre. Tunc mundus agnoscet justum ac legitimum haeredem Dominum suum, propter quem creatus est: agnoscet, inquam, quia jam non conformem sibi, sed reformatum in novitate sensus sui ad imaginem Dei, ad quam factus est. Sed et nunc quoque fideli homini totus mundus divitiarum est (Prov. XVII, sec. LXX) (12); non solum quia ad agnoscendum vel diligendum auctorem mundi, mundanis omnibus utitur tanquam ad hoc sibi datis; et in via testimoniorum Dei, quae videt facturam sicut et Scripturam perhibere factori, delectatur sicut in omnibus divitiis: sed etiam, quia ita sufficiens et gratus esse didicit, ut quasi totius mundi divitias computet illud prope nihil quod habet, sive hoc ipsum quod nihil habet. Ideo jure beatificat Ecclesiam sanctorum Salomon, quae pauper pro Christo, tam dives est in Christo. Multae, inquit, filiae congregaverunt divitias, tu supergressa es universas (Prov. XXXI, 29). Alii siquidem rapiunt non sua, et semper in egestate sunt: sancti dividunt propria, et ditiores fiunt. Divites enim eguerunt et esurierunt; inquirentes autem Dominum non minuentur omni bono (Psal. XXXIII, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. How great a glory, however, and how abundant a reward in heaven belongs to the summit of this perfection may in some measure be estimated from this: that the Lord proclaims even the very beginnings of those who renounce the world to be of such great blessedness that he says, \"Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.\" Truly blessed are they who, having cast off the cheap but heavy burdens of this world, refuse to become rich except from the Creator of the world alone, for his sake \u2014 as having nothing, and through him possessing all things. Do they not possess all things who possess him who contains and disposes all things, whose portion and inheritance is God himself; who, so that nothing may be lacking to those who fear him, dispenses the rest for their use as he knows to be fitting, and keeps himself for their enjoyment? When the heir of God and co-heir of Christ, having come of age and been emancipated, is brought into the full possession of the inheritance he has awaited, then indeed he will have absolute right and free dominion over creatures \u2014 he who, for as long as he is a little child, differs in nothing from a slave, though he is Lord of all, but is under guardians and stewards until the time appointed by the Father. Then the world will acknowledge him as its Lord, the just and lawful heir for whose sake it was created; it will acknowledge him, I say, because he is now no longer conformed to the world, but reformed in the newness of his mind to the image of God, to which he was made. But even now, too, to the faithful man the whole world is a world of riches (Prov. XVII, sec. LXX) (12); not only because he uses all worldly things as given to him for this purpose \u2014 to know and love the author of the world \u2014 and in the way of the testimonies of God, which he sees creation, like Scripture also, bearing witness to its Maker, he delights as in all riches; but also because he has learned to be so content and grateful that he reckons as the riches of the whole world that almost nothing which he has, or even this very fact \u2014 that he has nothing. Therefore Solomon rightly calls blessed the Church of the saints, which, poor for Christ's sake, is so rich in Christ. \"Many daughters,\" he says, \"have gathered together riches; you have surpassed them all\" (Prov. XXXI, 29). For others indeed seize what is not their own, and are always in want; the saints divide what is their own, and become richer. For \"the rich have wanted and suffered hunger; but they that seek the Lord shall not be deprived of any good thing\" (Psal. XXXIII, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Avarus quo plura possidet, pluribus eget. Quanquam nil minus habeat, quam quod habere se putat; cum habeatur, non habeat, servus pecuniae, mancipium avaritiae, cultorque crumenae, idololatra detestandus, cui nummus est deus. Pulchre autem jam nunc quoque vindicat in peccatores justitia divina, res ipsas quas amant convertens eis in tormenta, et vitia faciens esse supplicia. Pecunia enim quae justum amplius justificat, et verius ditat dispensata, vel semel data; avarum cruciat servata, prodigum polluit profligata. Prorsus beati pauperes Christi, quorum fides sic elusit sapientiam mundi, ut sola, quis sit optimus divitiarum usus, invenerit: quod videlicet divitiae, quae pauperem faciunt et miserum, si diliguntur; tunc demum faciunt divitem et beatum, si pro Christo contemnuntur. Confiteor tibi, Pater, Domine coeli et terrae, quia abscondisti haec a sapientibus et prudentibus, et revelasti ea parvulis (Matth. XI, 25), id est, humilibus; qui utique non sunt alii quam pauperes spiritu, quorum hic beatitudo praedicatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. The miser, the more he possesses, the more he lacks. Yet nothing is less his than what he thinks he has; since he is possessed rather than possessing, he is the slave of money, the chattel of avarice, the worshiper of his purse, a detestable idolater, for whom a coin is god. And now too divine justice fittingly takes vengeance upon sinners, turning the very things they love into torments for them, and making their vices into their punishments. For money, which more fully justifies and more truly enriches the just man when it is distributed or given away, torments the miser when it is kept, and defiles the prodigal when it is squandered. Truly blessed are the poor of Christ, whose faith has so outwitted the wisdom of the world that it alone has discovered what is the best use of riches: namely, that riches, which make a man poor and wretched if they are loved, then at last make him rich and blessed if they are despised for Christ's sake. I confess to you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that you have hidden these things from the wise and prudent, and have revealed them to little ones (Matth. XI, 25) \u2014 that is, to the humble, who are none other than the poor in spirit, whose blessedness is proclaimed here.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Hoc enim licet scientes tamen commonere vos velim, fratres, quod vera ac beata paupertas spiritus, plus est in humilitate cordis, quam in angustia rei familiaris: plus consistit in abdicatione superbiae, quam in contemptu substantiae. Substantia nonnunquam utiliter habetur; superbia nunquam nisi damnabiliter retinetur. Diabolus nihil in mundo possidet, aut possidere desiderat, quem sola, vel maxime superbia damnat. Parum igitur prodest renuntiare saeculi possessionibus, nisi renuntietur et moribus: imo stultum ac ridiculum est nudari divitiis, et divitum implicari vitiis; pauperem fieri rebus, nec ditari virtutibus; relinquere omnia, nec sequi Christum, sed magis forsitan in castris Christi juvare partes Antichristi. Plane Antichristi partes adjuvat, quisquis superbiae militat, nomenque sanctum, quod verbis vel habitu profitetur, moribus impugnat. Vexillum Christi humilitas; Antichristi, superbia, vel potius capitis ejus diaboli, qui super omnes filios superbiae regnat, et ab initio per superbiam peccat. Gloriemur igitur, fratres, quia pauperes sumus pro Christo; sed operam demus ut humiles simus cum Christo. Paupere superbo sicut nihil detestabilius, ita nihil miserabilius; cum eum et paupertas nunc affligat, et superbia in perpetuum abdicat. Pauper autem humilis, etsi uritur et purgatur in camino paupertatis, exsultat refrigerio conscientiae divitis: consolatur se promissione sanctae spei, sciens et sentiens quoniam ipsius est regnum Dei; quod jam intra se ipsum velut in semine vel radice gerit, primitias scilicet spiritus, et pignus aeternae haereditatis. Annon vestrum est, fratres, unde toties sicut vobis conscii estis, fructus suavissimos et beata gaudia decerpitis, quorum gustus amarescere vobis jam omnem fecit dulcedinem mundi? Gustastis enim, ni fallor, et vidistis quoniam bona est negotiatio vestra, qui pro re gratis contemnenda ac projicienda, summa comparastis bona. Denique non est regnum Dei esca et potus, sed justitia, et pax, et gaudium in Spiritu sancto (Rom. xiv, 17). Si haec ergo sentimus in nobis, cur non confidenter pronuntiemus intra nos esse regnum Dei? Quod autem intra nos est, illud vere nostrum est, quia nobis invitis eripi non potest."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. For though you know this, brothers, I would still like to remind you that true and blessed poverty of spirit consists more in humility of heart than in the straitness of one's household means; it consists more in the renunciation of pride than in the contempt of substance. Substance is sometimes held to good use; pride is never retained except to one's damnation. The devil possesses nothing in the world, nor desires to possess anything, and yet pride alone, or pride above all, condemns him. It profits little, then, to renounce the possessions of the world unless one also renounces its ways; indeed, it is foolish and ridiculous to be stripped of riches and yet entangled in the vices of the rich \u2014 to become poor in things but not enriched in virtues; to leave all things and yet not follow Christ, but rather, perhaps, while serving in the camp of Christ, to aid the cause of Antichrist. Clearly he aids the cause of Antichrist who serves in the army of pride, and who, while professing the holy name in words or in habit, fights against it by his conduct. The banner of Christ is humility; the banner of Antichrist is pride \u2014 or rather of his head, the devil, who reigns over all the sons of pride, and who from the beginning sins through pride. Let us glory, then, brothers, that we are poor for Christ's sake; but let us take care to be humble with Christ. As nothing is more detestable than a proud poor man, so nothing is more pitiable; for poverty afflicts him now, and pride casts him off forever. But the humble poor man, even though he is burned and purified in the furnace of poverty, exults in the refreshment of a rich conscience; he consoles himself with the promise of holy hope, knowing and feeling that the kingdom of God is his \u2014 which he already bears within himself as in a seed or a root, namely, the firstfruits of the Spirit, and the pledge of the eternal inheritance. Is it not yours, brothers, from which, as you yourselves are conscious, you so often pluck the sweetest fruits and blessed joys, the taste of which has already made all the sweetness of the world bitter to you? For you have tasted, unless I am mistaken, and have seen that your business dealing is good, you who for a thing to be freely despised and cast away have purchased the highest goods. For indeed the kingdom of God is not food and drink, but righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Spirit (Rom. xiv, 17). If, then, we feel these things within ourselves, why should we not confidently declare that the kingdom of God is within us? For what is within us is truly ours, because it cannot be snatched away from us against our will.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Recte itaque Dominus beatitudinem praedicans pauperum, non ait, ipsorum erit, sed, est regnum coelorum: non solum propter jus firmissimum, sed etiam propter pignus certissimum usumque felicissimum; non solum quia paratum est eis ab origine mundi, sed etiam quia jam in quamdam ipsius possessionem coeperunt introduci, jam habentes thesaurum coelestem in vasis fictilibus, jam portantes Deum in corporibus suis et cordibus. Quam beata gens, cujus est Dominus Deus ejus! quam vicini sunt Dei regno qui regem ipsum, cui servire regnare est, jam possident, et gestant in corde suo! Funes, inquit, ceciderunt mihi in praeclaris; etenim haereditas mea praeclara est mihi. Litigent alii de dividenda haereditate hujus mundi: Dominus pars haereditatis meae et calicis mei (Psal. xv, 6, 5). Pugnent inter se quis eorum fiat miserior, nihil omnium quae ambiunt, illis invideo: ego enim et anima mea delectabimur in Domino. O praeclara haereditas pauperum! o beata possessio nihil habentium! quoniam non solum omnem sufficientiam nobis subministras, sed etiam ad omnem gloriam abundas, ad omnem laetitiam redundas, tanquam in sinu reposita supereffluens mensura. Prorsus tecum sunt divitiae et gloria, opes superbae et justitia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Rightly, then, did the Lord, in proclaiming the blessedness of the poor, not say, 'theirs shall be,' but 'theirs is the kingdom of heaven' \u2014 not only because of the most firm right, but also because of the most certain pledge and the most blessed enjoyment; not only because it has been prepared for them from the foundation of the world, but also because they have already begun to be brought into a certain possession of it, already having the heavenly treasure in earthen vessels, already carrying God in their bodies and hearts. How blessed is the nation whose God is the Lord! How near to the kingdom of God are those who already possess the King himself \u2014 whom to serve is to reign \u2014 and bear him in their own heart! 'The lines,' he says, 'have fallen for me among goodly places; yes, my inheritance is goodly to me.' Let others quarrel over the dividing of the inheritance of this world: 'The Lord is the portion of my inheritance and of my cup' (Psal. xv, 6, 5). Let them fight among themselves over which of them shall become more wretched; I envy them nothing of all they covet, for I and my soul shall take delight in the Lord. O goodly inheritance of the poor! O blessed possession of those who have nothing! For you not only supply us with every sufficiency, but you also overflow for every glory, and run over for every gladness, like an overflowing measure laid up in the lap. Truly with you are riches and glory, glorious riches and righteousness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Superbiat, o pauperes, superbiat, o humiles, anima vestra, glorians in humilitate sua, omnemque altitudinem hujus mundi longe sub pedibus suis jacentem despiciat; indignumque judicet gloriae suae, ad concupiscentiam vilis praedae suam ulterius majestatem inclinare. Quid? jam jam tollenda es in coelum, et nunc te demum mergeres in coenum? parata tibi sunt aeterna, et praeeligeres transitoria, somnoque similia? te praestolatur curia sanctorum, et praeponeres societatem daemoniorum? Quam miser homo, qui cum in honore esset, non intellexit, ac perinde comparatus est jumentis insipientibus, et similis factus est illis! (Psal. xlviii, 21.) Annon manifeste hoc illis contingit, quos cum beata paupertas fecisset honorabiles coelo, mirabiles mundo, et, ut nihil praeteritum sit, etiam formidabiles inferno: postea arbitrantes miseriam, caecato sensu, paupertatem, humilitatem, ignaviam; voluerunt divites fieri, et inciderunt in tentationes et laqueos diaboli; cumque essent domini universorum, vendiderunt se ipsos sine pretio propter ea quae nihil sunt. Vae his qui perdiderunt sustinentiam, et diverterunt in vias pravas! Et quid facient cum inspicere coeperit Dominus? Ipsi viderint quid facturi sint. Vos enim quibus amica est paupertas, et grata spiritus humilitas, securos fecit de possidendo regno coelorum incommutabilis Veritas: vestrum illud esse asserens, vobis illud repositum fideliter custodiens; si tamen et vos ipsi spem istam in sinu vestro repositam firmiter custodiatis usque in finem, cooperante Domino nostro Jesu Christo, cui est honor et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Let your soul be proud, O poor; let it be proud, O humble, glorying in its own humility, and let it look down from far above upon all the loftiness of this world lying beneath its feet; let it judge it unworthy of its glory to bend its majesty any further toward the desire for vile prey. What? Are you now, even now, about to be lifted up into heaven, and would you at this very moment sink yourself into the mire? Eternal things are prepared for you, and would you choose instead transitory things, things like a passing dream? The court of the saints awaits you, and would you prefer the company of demons? How wretched is man, who, when he was in honor, did not understand, and so was compared to senseless beasts, and became like them! (Psal. xlviii, 21.) Does not this plainly happen to those whom blessed poverty had made honorable before heaven, admirable before the world, and\u2014that nothing be left unsaid\u2014even fearsome to hell: who afterward, their understanding blinded, judged poverty to be misery, and humility to be cowardice; who wished to become rich, and fell into the temptations and snares of the devil; and though they were lords of all things, sold themselves for no price for the sake of things that are nothing. Woe to those who have lost their endurance, and have turned aside into crooked ways! And what will they do when the Lord begins to examine them? Let them see for themselves what they are to do. But as for you, to whom poverty is a friend, and humility of spirit is dear, the unchangeable Truth has made you secure of possessing the kingdom of heaven: asserting that it is yours, faithfully keeping it stored up for you\u2014if only you yourselves also firmly guard this hope stored up in your bosom even to the end, with the cooperation of our Lord Jesus Christ, to whom be honor and glory through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-n012", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(12) Lectio haec Aug., Bernard. aliisque Patribus familiaris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-all-saints-n012", "label": "English", "text": "(12) This reading is familiar to Augustine, Bernard, and other Fathers.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "All Saints Sermon", "topics": ["All Saints", "Beatitudes", "poverty of spirit", "humility", "avarice", "kingdom of heaven"], "url": "sermons/guerric-all-saints.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:annunciation", "calendar_label": "The Annunciation of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-annunciation-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In annuntiatione dominica, sermo I, De gaudio hujus diei et de laudibus Virginis matris, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 115-119.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Annunciation", "Annunciation of the Lord", "Proverbs 12:25", "Psalm 118:49, 50 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 9:1 (printed doubled parenthesis retained; see note)", "Luke 1:70 (printed locator; quotation identified as Luke 1:32, see note)", "III Regum 10:18 (historical numbering; modern 1 Kings)", "Matthew 17:1, 2", "III Regum 10:18\u201320 (historical numbering; modern 1 Kings)", "Psalm 131:14 (historical numbering)", "Song of Songs 5:10", "III Regum 10:20 (historical numbering; modern 1 Kings)", "Mark 6:56", "Luke 6:49 (printed locator; quotation identified as Luke 6:19, see note)", "Luke 1:48 (printed Id.; see note)", "Song of Songs 5:14", "Psalm 44:9, 10 (historical numbering; stacte explained in note)", "1 Thessalonians 4:4, 5", "Genesis 27:27", "Song of Songs 4:11", "Song of Songs 3:6 (printed Id.)", "Song of Songs 4:6 (printed Id.)"], "occasion": "In annuntiatione dominica", "occasion_detail": "Annunciation of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Annunciation", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny presents the Annunciation as spiritual joy within Lenten observance: the angel's promise to Mary announces pardon, redemption and life to those humbled by repentance. He contrasts the fullness of salvation with human ingratitude, then contemplates Mary's royal ancestry and Christ's body through the images of Wisdom's house and Solomon's ivory throne. Mary's virginal chastity leads him to urge chastity joined to humility in his hearers. The fragrance of myrrh and incense becomes an image of bodily continence and prayerful devotion, returning at the close to the Word's descent into Mary's womb. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The source names the Annunciation of the Lord."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN ANNUNTIATIONE DOMINICA."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE ANNUNCIATION OF THE LORD.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De gaudio hujus diei et de laudibus Virginis matris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the joy of this day and the praises of the Virgin Mother.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Opportune nobis in his diebus Quadragesimalis observantiae solemnitas intervenit Dominicae Annuntiationis, ut qui fatigantur afflictione corporali, gaudio recreari valeant spirituali; et quos humiliavit poenitentiae luctus, annuntiatio illius qui tollit peccata mundi, consoletur. Sic quippe scriptum habes: Moeror in corde viri humiliabit eum; et sermone bono laetificabitur (Prov. xii, 25). Prorsus sermo bonus, sermo fidelis et omni acceptione dignus, Evangelium nostrae salutis; quod angelus missus a Deo, hodierna die Mariae evangelizavit, ac laetum de Incarnatione Verbi, dies diei, angelus Virgini verbum eructavit. Sermo ille, dum Filium promittit Virgini, veniam pollicetur reis, redemptionem captivis, adapertionem clausis, vitamque sepultis. Sermo ille, dum Filii regnum praedicat, justorum quoque gloriam annuntiat; terret inferos, laetificat coelos; et, sicut cognitione mysteriorum, sic etiam novitate gaudiorum perfectionem auxisse videtur Angelorum. Quem ergo non laetificet ille sermo bonus in afflictione sua, quem non consoletur verbum illud in humilitate sua? Memor esto, inquit David, verbi tui servo tuo, in quo mihi spem dedisti; haec enim me consolata est in humilitate mea (Psal. cxviii, 49, 50). Verbum tantum promissionis acceperat, sed nullo adhuc indicio sese effectus prodebat. Affligebat eum dilatio voti, sed consolabatur certitudo spei ex fide promittentis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. The solemnity of the Lord's Annunciation comes at a fitting time for us in these days of Lenten observance, so that those wearied by bodily affliction may be refreshed by spiritual joy, and those whom the sorrow of repentance has humbled may be comforted by the announcement of him who takes away the sins of the world. For you find it written: Sorrow in a man's heart will humble him; and by a good word he will be gladdened (Prov. xii, 25). A good word indeed, a faithful word and worthy of all acceptance, is the Gospel of our salvation, which the angel sent by God proclaimed to Mary on this day; and, as day speaks to day, the angel poured forth to the Virgin a joyful word concerning the Incarnation of the Word. That word, in promising a Son to the Virgin, promises pardon to the guilty, redemption to captives, an opening to those shut in, and life to the buried. That word, in proclaiming the Son's kingdom, announces also the glory of the just; it terrifies the underworld and gladdens the heavens; and it seems to have increased the perfection of the Angels by new joys as well as by knowledge of mysteries. Whom, then, should that good word not gladden in his affliction? Whom should that word not comfort in his lowliness? Remember, says David, your word to your servant, in which you gave me hope; for this has comforted me in my lowliness (Psal. cxviii, 49, 50). He had received only a word of promise, and its fulfillment had not yet shown itself by any sign. The delay of his desire afflicted him, but the certainty of hope, arising from the faithfulness of the one who promised, comforted him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si ergo David sola spe salutis hujus, quae nobis servabatur, animum pascebat, quod gaudium quae deliciae, rei ipsius exhibitio nobis esse debuerat? O felicitas temporum istorum! o infelicitas temporum istorum! An non felicitas temporum in quibus tanta plenitudo gratiae et omnium bonorum? an non infelicitas temporum in quibus tanta ingratitudo redemptorum? Ecce enim venit plenitudo temporis, in quo mittit Deus Filium suum, ut fiat Filius hominis hominumque Salvator; et ecce magnitudo temporis, ut Salvatorem suum fastidiat homo peccator. Annuntiatur salus perditis, et contemnunt; promittitur vita desperatis, et negligunt; venit Deus ad homines, et non assurgunt. Assurgit, qui aliqua devotione se erigit ut det gloriam gratiae Dei; assurgit, qui saltem cum gaudio suscipit verbum propriae salutis. Scio autem, scio quis est qui laetificatur illo sermone bono. Ille procul dubio quem prius humiliavit pius moeror in corde suo: moeror de peregrinatione et exsilio, moeror de vinculis mortis et periculis inferni; qui moerens quotidie plangit, quod praeoccupaverint eum laquei mortis, et pericula inferni invenerint. Huic nimirum jucundus advenit hodiernus ille nuntius de coelo, hic gratulabundus excipit sermonem de Dei Filio; huic, inquam, lugenti et moerenti quod tantis sit malis praeventus et circumventus, non sine gaudio liberator annuntiatur; qui oleum gaudii daturus est pro luctu, pallium laudis pro spiritu moeroris; qui videlicet finem dabit miseriis, et sine fine beatitudinem dabit miseris. Beati ergo qui lugent, quoniam ipsi consolabuntur; beati duos humiliavit pius moeror in corde suo, quoniam laetificabuntur sermone bono. Bonus plane sermo et consolatorius omnipotens Sermo tuus, Domine, qui hodie a regalibus sedibus venit in uterum Virginis, ubi etiam sedem sibi regalem exstruxit; in qua cum etiam nunc sedeat, quasi Rex circumstante exercitu Angelorum in coelis, est tamen moerentium consolator in terris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If, then, David nourished his soul on the mere hope of this salvation, which was being kept for us, what joy, what delight should the manifestation of the thing itself have been to us! O happiness of these times! O unhappiness of these times! Are they not happy times in which there is such fullness of grace and of every good thing? Are they not unhappy times in which there is such ingratitude among the redeemed? For see, the fullness of time has come, in which God sends his Son to become the Son of man and the Savior of men; and see the enormity of the time, that sinful man should disdain his Savior. Salvation is announced to the lost, and they despise it; life is promised to the despairing, and they neglect it; God comes to men, and they do not rise to greet him. He rises who lifts himself up with some devotion to give glory to God's grace; he rises who at least receives with joy the word of his own salvation. But I know, I know who is gladdened by that good word. It is undoubtedly the man whom devout sorrow has first humbled in his heart: sorrow over his pilgrimage and exile, sorrow over the bonds of death and the perils of hell; the man who mourns and laments daily that the snares of death have overtaken him and the perils of hell have found him. To him that welcome messenger from heaven comes today; he welcomes with rejoicing the word concerning the Son of God. To him, I say, grieving and sorrowing that he has been overtaken and surrounded by such great evils, a deliverer is announced, bringing no little joy: one who will give the oil of gladness in place of mourning, a mantle of praise in place of a spirit of sorrow; one who will put an end to miseries and give the miserable blessedness without end. Blessed, then, are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted; blessed are those whom devout sorrow has humbled in their hearts, for they shall be gladdened by a good word. Truly good and comforting is your almighty Word, Lord, who today came from the royal throne into the Virgin's womb, where he also built himself a royal seat. Although even now he sits upon it as King in heaven, surrounded by the host of Angels, he is nevertheless the comforter of the sorrowful on earth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Regali siquidem ex progenie Virgo electa est, generositatis quidem regiae nobilis proles, sed virtutis regiae nobilior indoles: ut aeterno Regi, Filio Regis, materna quoque nobilitas regium honorem deferret, et venientem a regali sede Patris, regalis etiam thronus in aula virginali Reginae susciperet Matris. In ipsa quippe et ex ipsa, Sapientia aedificavit sibi domum ((Prov. ix, 1), in ipsa et ex ipsa paravit sibi thronum, cum in ea et ex ea corpus aptavit sibi ita ad omnia perfectum et congruum, ut et domus ei sit ad quiescendum, et thronus ad judicandum, quod primo tabernaculum ei fuit ad pugnandum, et cathedra ad docendum. Vide autem ne forte ipsa sit sedes David patris ejus (Luc. i, 70), quam ei dandam promittit angelus; non quod David in ea sederit, sed quod ex semine David assumpta et fabricata sit. Et certe, si sedes David non est, tamen sedes tua, Deus, in saeculum saeculi ipsa est. Si solium David non est, certe solium excelsum et elevatum super omnem creaturam ipsa est. Si quis autem etiam thronum illum grandem, quem rex Salomon sibi fecit de ebore, corpus ipsum interpretetur, quod Pacificus noster hodie sumpsit ex Virgine, non multum a vero videbitur abhorrere; quandoquidem et illud quod sequitur, et vestivit eum auro fulvo nimis (III Reg. x, 18), eidem satis corpori congruit; quod jam Dominus decorem induit fulgoris intolerabilis. Vestivit, inquit, eum auro fulvo. Prorsus, si interroges oculos Apostolorum qui transfiguratum in monte viderunt (Matth. xvii, 1, 2), confitebuntur aurum nimis esse fulvum, id est, fulgorem corporis esse nimium, quem ferre nequiverunt, quamvis nimis pro Valde ponere consuetudinis sit Scripturarum. Caetera namque quae prosequitur Scriptura de illius throni magnificentia (III Reg. x, 18-20), accommodatius nisi fallor, si quis tractare voluerit, accipientur de corpore Christi, quod est Ecclesia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. For the Virgin was chosen from royal stock, a noble offspring of royal birth, but nobler still in the character of her royal virtue, so that to the eternal King, the Son of the King, his mother's nobility too might render royal honor, and a royal throne in the virginal court of his Queen Mother might receive him as he came from the Father's royal seat. For in her and from her Wisdom built herself a house ((Prov. ix, 1); in her and from her he prepared himself a throne, when in her and from her he fitted himself with a body so perfect and suitable in every respect that it might be both a house for his rest and a throne for his judgment, having first been a tent for his warfare and a chair for his teaching. Consider, however, whether this may be the seat of David his father (Luc. i, 70), which the angel promises will be given to him: not because David sat upon it, but because it was taken and fashioned from David's seed. And certainly, if it is not David's seat, it is nevertheless your seat, O God, for ever and ever. If it is not David's throne, it is certainly a throne high and lifted up above every creature. But if anyone also interprets that great throne which King Solomon made for himself out of ivory as the very body which our Peacemaker took today from the Virgin, he will seem to be not far from the truth. For the words which follow, and he covered it with exceedingly bright gold (III Reg. x, 18), fit that body well, since the Lord has now clothed it in the beauty of unbearable radiance. He covered it, it says, with bright gold. Indeed, if you question the eyes of the Apostles who saw him transfigured on the mountain (Matth. xvii, 1, 2), they will confess that the gold is exceedingly bright, that is, that the body's radiance is excessive, which they could not endure; although Scripture customarily uses 'excessively' to mean 'very.' For the other things Scripture goes on to say about the magnificence of that throne (III Reg. x, 18-20) will, unless I am mistaken, be more fittingly understood of Christ's body which is the Church, should anyone wish to expound them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Mihi sane magis mirari modo libet illud ebur tam pretiosum, imo et impretiabile, virginalis castimoniae, de quo scilicet sedem sibi facere delegit, qui sedet super Cherubim: Haec, inquiens, requies mea in saeculum saeculi; hic sedebo, quoniam elegi eam (Psal. cxxxi, 14). Quam nitidum ebur illud quod oculis tanti tamque divitis Regis placuit, in cujus diebus argentum nullius est pretii! quam frigidum, quod nec a conceptu incaluit, quam solidum, quod nec partus violavit! quam candidum simul et rubicundum, quod candor lucis aeternae ignisque Spiritus sancti, universa sui plenitudine replevit! Maria siquidem et ipsa candidior nive, rubicundior ebore antiquo; cui scilicet incomparabilem et castitas candorem, et charitas seu martyrium rutilantem prae antiquis electis noscitur contulisse ruborem. Nam et suam ipsius animam pertransivit gladius, ut mater summi Virginis et Martyris esset et virgo ipsa et martyr, candida et rubicunda, quemadmodum dilectus ejus candidus et rubicundus (Cantic. v, 10). Denique, sicut Salomon in omnibus thesauris suis et opibus tantis nihil habuit tam pretiosum, quod in opus illud, magnificum thronum scilicet gloriae suae judicaret ebori praeferendum, sic Maria singularem prae omnibus electis Angelorum et hominum invenit gratiam apud Deum, ut videlicet Dei conciperet et pareret Filium atque ex ebore corporis ejus thronum sibi virtus Altissimi sine manibus excideret gloriosum. Gloriosus prorsus ille thronus ac mirabilis, de quo Scriptura perhibet quia non est factum tale opus in universis regnis (III Reg. x, 20), quod facile testimoniis probaretur Angelorum, qui semper et insatiabiliter in gloriam et decorem Dominici corporis prospicere concupiscunt. Quia enim in universis regnis non est tale opus factum, quidquid factum est, flectat ei genu in omnibus regnis, coelestium, terrestrium, et infernorum. Quam beatus igitur ille venter eburneus, unde caro eburnea sumpta est Redemptoris, pretium animarum, miraculum Angelorum, solium summae majestatis, thronusque potestatis, cibus vitae immortalis, medicina peccati, restitutio sanitatis! Quotquot, inquit, tangebant eum, sanabantur a languoribus suis (Marc. vi, 56): nam virtus de illo exibat, et sanabat omnes (Luc. vi, 49)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. For my part, I would rather marvel now at that precious, indeed priceless, ivory of virginal chastity, from which he who sits above the Cherubim chose to make himself a seat, saying: This is my resting place for ever and ever; here I will sit, for I have chosen it (Psal. cxxxi, 14). How lustrous is that ivory which pleased the eyes of so great and wealthy a King, in whose days silver is of no value! How cool, since even conception did not heat it; how firm, since even childbirth did not violate it! How white and ruddy at once, filled with all the fullness of the brightness of eternal light and the fire of the Holy Spirit! For Mary herself is whiter than snow, ruddier than ancient ivory: chastity is known to have bestowed upon her an incomparable whiteness, and charity, or martyrdom, a glowing redness surpassing that of the elect of old. For a sword passed through her own soul too, so that the mother of the supreme Virgin and Martyr should herself be both virgin and martyr, white and ruddy, just as her beloved is white and ruddy (Cantic. v, 10). Finally, just as Solomon, among all his treasures and such great riches, had nothing so precious that he judged it preferable to ivory for that work, the magnificent throne of his glory, so Mary found singular grace before God above all the elect among Angels and men: namely, that she should conceive and bear the Son of God, and that the power of the Most High should carve for himself, without hands, a glorious throne from the ivory of her body. Glorious indeed and wonderful is that throne, of which Scripture declares that no such work was made in all the kingdoms (III Reg. x, 20). This could readily be proved by the testimony of the Angels, who always and insatiably long to look upon the glory and beauty of the Lord's body. For since no such work has been made in all the kingdoms, let everything that has been made bend the knee to him in all the kingdoms, in heaven, on earth and under the earth. How blessed, then, is that ivory womb, from which the Redeemer's ivory flesh was taken: the price of souls, the wonder of Angels, the seat of supreme majesty and throne of power, the food of immortal life, the medicine for sin, the restoration of health! As many as touched him, it says, were healed of their infirmities (Marc. vi, 56); for power went out from him and healed them all (Luc. vi, 49).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Beatus igitur venter, qui te, Domine Jesu, portavit! felix castitas uteri virginalis, quae huic operi materiam ministravit! Felix prorsus, fratres mei, nitor illius eboris, id est, candor castitatis: cui nec aurum mundanae sapientiae, nec argentum eloquentiae, nec gemmam alicujus excellentis gratiae, nostri praefert electio Salomonis; si modo tamen castitas humilitate commendetur, quia humilitatem ancillae suae respexit Dominus (Id. i, 48). Proinde namque Salomon, cum in carmine amoris castitatem praedicaret virginis, seu sponsae, videlicet seu sponsi (quia quorum spiritus unus et caro una, in plerisque etiam laus una), ut castitatem illius adornatam nostramque adornandam etiam aliis virtutibus doceret, ait Venter ejus eburneus distinctus sapphiris (Cantic. v, 14). Ut autem noveris quia castitas aliarum virtutum seu gratiarum sit receptaculum, apothecae aromaticae seu unguentariae nostri Salomonis non nisi domus eburneae sunt, sicut David in illo epithalamio cecinit, quod inscriptio tituli, Canticum pro dilecto, de Sponso Ecclesiae accipiendum ostendit. Myrrha, inquit, et gutta, et casia a vestimentis tuis, a domibus, eburneis ex quibus delectaverunt te filiae regum in honore tuo (Psal. xliv, 9, 10). Corpora siquidem eburnea sanctorum, domus Christi sunt, vestimentum Christi sunt: membra Christi sunt; templum Spiritus sancti sunt. Ab his vestimentis Christi, ab his domibus eburneis spirat ei omnis fragrantia virtutum et charismatum, ex quibus delectant et honorant Regem regum filiae Apostolorum et Prophetarum. Probastis et ipsi, fratres mei, ex quo coepistis possidere unusquisque vas suum in sanctificatione et honore, non passione desiderii, sicut et gentes quae ignorant Deum (I Thess. iv, 4, 5), quid unguentorum, quid odoramentorum divina largitate sit infusum; quo modo corpus, quod erat sentina sordium, nunc Spiritus sancti sit venerabile sacrarium; et quod erat vorago flagitiorum, nunc quaedam sit apotheca gratiarum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Blessed, then, is the womb that bore you, Lord Jesus! Happy the chastity of the virginal womb which supplied the material for this work! Happy indeed, my brothers, is the luster of that ivory, that is, the whiteness of chastity: our Solomon's choice prefers neither the gold of worldly wisdom nor the silver of eloquence nor the jewel of any excellent grace to it, provided, however, that chastity is commended by humility, for the Lord has looked upon the humility of his handmaid (Id. i, 48). Thus Solomon, when in the song of love he praised the chastity of the virgin, whether of the bride or of the bridegroom, since those who are one spirit and one flesh also share one praise in many things, wished to teach that the chastity of the one is adorned, and ours must be adorned, with other virtues too. He says: His belly is of ivory, set with sapphires (Cantic. v, 14). And so that you may know that chastity is a receptacle for other virtues or graces, our Solomon's storehouses of spices or ointments are nothing but houses of ivory. So David sang in that wedding song which the inscription of its title, A Song for the Beloved, showed was to be understood of the Bridegroom of the Church: Myrrh, he says, and stacte and cassia from your garments, from the ivory houses, from which the daughters of kings have delighted you in your honor (Psal. xliv, 9, 10). For the ivory bodies of the saints are Christ's houses, Christ's clothing, Christ's members and the temple of the Holy Spirit. From these garments of Christ, from these ivory houses, there breathes for him every fragrance of virtues and spiritual gifts, by which the daughters of Apostles and Prophets delight and honor the King of kings. You yourselves too have experienced, my brothers, since each of you began to possess his vessel in sanctification and honor, not in the passion of desire like the nations who do not know God (I Thess. iv, 4, 5), what ointments and perfumes have been poured in by divine bounty; how the body which was a cesspool of filth is now a venerable sanctuary of the Holy Spirit, and what was an abyss of shameful deeds is now a kind of storehouse of graces.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Myrrha, inquit, et gutta, et casia a vestimentis tuis, a domibus eburneis. Inhabitamus quidem domos luteas, sed quae materiae qualitate sunt luteae, continentiae virtute fiunt eburneae; ex quibus si myrrha prima spirare coeperit per mortificationem voluptatum, consequentur et aliae species aromaticae, quae multiformem virtutum gratiam spirabunt. Spirabunt autem Christo delectabiliter, tanquam de vestimentis suis; spirabunt etiam longe lateque proximis, tanquam bonus odor Christi in omni loco. Horum vestimentorum Christi fragrantiam statim ut sensit Isaac: Ecce, inquit, odor filii mei sicut odor agri cui benedixit Dominus (Gen. xxvii, 27). Sed et Sponsus ad sponsam, quae etiam corpus et vestimentum ipsius est, loquitur dicens: Odor vestimentorum tuorum, sicut odor thuris (Cant. iv, 11). Quem nimirum odorem thuris Sponso gratissimum, pietas, o fratres, vestrorum spirabit cordium, si ita fragrans et devota fuerit ut dirigatur oratio vestra sicut incensum in conspectu Altissimi, et ascendat coram eo sicut virgula fumi ex aromatibus myrrhae et thuris (Id. iii, 6). Et quidem myrrha fragrat ei de corporibus vestris; fragrat utique et mihi: utinam ita et thus fragret de cordibus vestris, quatenus Sponsus probans vos sicut castos, sic etiam devotos, frequentius vos sua visitatione dignetur, et dicat: Ibo mihi ad montem myrrhae, et ad collem thuris (Id. iv, 6). Videtur tamen sermo iste, prophetia hujus esse diei, per Salomonem olim praedicta, per Jesum hodie impleta. Hodie namque venit in illum montem altissimum montium, montem non modo myrrhae et thuris, sed et omnium aromatum; dico autem Virginem virginum, omnium plenam gratiarum, inter quas tamen praecipue, nisi fallor, redolebat sponso myrrha castitatis, et thus pietatis. Odor, iste, fratres, mei, super omnia aromata; odor iste Dominum majestatis de excelsis attrahit et invitat, ut inclinet coelos et descendat, sicut probat dies haec, cum Altissimus, angelo misso de coelis, ipse quoque descendit in uterum Matris, qui semper manet in sinu Patris, cum quo vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Myrrh, it says, and stacte and cassia from your garments, from the ivory houses. We do indeed inhabit houses of clay, but those which are clay by the quality of their material become ivory through the virtue of continence. If myrrh first begins to breathe from them through the mortification of pleasures, other kinds of spices will follow, breathing forth the manifold grace of virtues. They will breathe pleasantly for Christ, as from his own garments; they will also breathe far and wide for their neighbors, as the good fragrance of Christ in every place. As soon as Isaac perceived the fragrance of these garments of Christ, he said: Behold, the fragrance of my son is like the fragrance of a field which the Lord has blessed (Gen. xxvii, 27). The Bridegroom too speaks to the bride, who is also his body and his clothing, saying: The fragrance of your garments is like the fragrance of incense (Cant. iv, 11). That fragrance of incense, so pleasing to the Bridegroom, will surely be breathed forth, brothers, by the devotion of your hearts, if it is so fragrant and devout that your prayer is directed like incense before the Most High, and rises before him like a little column of smoke from spices of myrrh and incense (Id. iii, 6). Myrrh does indeed breathe forth for him from your bodies; its fragrance reaches me too. Would that incense might likewise breathe forth from your hearts, so that the Bridegroom, finding you devout as well as chaste, may more often deign to visit you and say: I will go to the mountain of myrrh and to the hill of incense (Id. iv, 6). Yet this saying seems to be a prophecy of this day, foretold long ago through Solomon and fulfilled today through Jesus. For today he came to that mountain, the highest of mountains, a mountain not only of myrrh and incense but of every spice: I mean the Virgin of virgins, full of every grace, among which, unless I am mistaken, the myrrh of chastity and the incense of devotion were especially fragrant to the Bridegroom. This fragrance, my brothers, surpasses every spice; this fragrance draws and invites the Lord of majesty from the heights to bow the heavens and descend, as this day proves, when the Most High, having sent an angel from heaven, himself also descended into his Mother's womb, he who always remains in the Father's bosom, with whom he lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The title and six numbered sections follow the printed edition. A second image of the same source resolves damage in the digitized pages."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Luke 1:70 for the promise of David\u2019s throne; the promise is in Luke 1:32. It also prints a doubled opening parenthesis before Proverbs 9:1. Both are retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Luke 6:49 for \u201cpower went out from him and healed them all\u201d; the saying is in Luke 6:19. The later Id. i, 48 means Luke 1:48."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Historical III Regum corresponds to modern 1 Kings, and Psalm numbering follows the Latin edition. \u201cStacte\u201d is the traditional name of an aromatic substance; no precise botanical identification is intended. The edition prints duos where the context calls for quos (\u201cthose whom\u201d); the English follows that sense. The imagery of ivory, virginity and martyrdom, bodily chastity and fragrance belongs to Guerric\u2019s argument. The repeated myrrh quotation has no new printed Psalm reference."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Annunciation Sermon I: The Joy of This Day and the Praises of the Virgin Mother", "topics": ["Annunciation", "Incarnation", "Virgin Mary", "joy", "humility", "chastity", "prayer"], "url": "sermons/guerric-annunciation-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:annunciation", "calendar_label": "The Annunciation of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-annunciation-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De verbi incarnatione in Maria et in anima fideli., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 120-124.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Annunciation", "Annunciation of the Lord", "Psalm 84:10 (historical numbering)", "Genesis 3:17\u201318", "Isaiah 16:1", "1 Corinthians 10:4", "Isaiah 45:8", "Ezekiel 44:2", "Isaiah 7:14", "Ezekiel 44:2\u20133", "Song of Songs 5:2", "Song of Songs 8:6", "Isaiah 50:5", "Luke 1:38", "1 Corinthians 6:20", "Romans 10:17", "Malachi 2:16\u201317 (printed locator; quotation from Malachi 2:7)", "Isaiah 26:17\u201318", "Matthew 12:50", "Galatians 4:19", "John 16:21", "Luke 1:45"], "occasion": "In Annuntiatione Dominica", "occasion_detail": "Annunciation of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Annunciation", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric considers the Incarnation in Mary and the formation of Christ in the faithful soul. He connects Mary's hearing and faith with love of God and neighbor, humility, chastity and care for the life of grace. The sermon appears under the Annunciation heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De verbi incarnatione in Maria et in anima fideli."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the incarnation of the Word in Mary and in the faithful soul.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Hodie Verbum caro factum est, et habitare coepit in nobis, secundum regulam scilicet sanae fidei, quam ecclesiasticorum dogmatum definitio tradidit nobis (7). Firmissime namque tenet, et nullatenus dubitat Ecclesia, carnem Christi non prius conceptam quam a Verbo susceptam; sed ipsum Verbum Dei carnis acceptione conceptum, carnemque Verbi incarnatione conceptam. Hodie itaque Sapientia aedificare coepit sibi domum corporis nostri in utero Virginis, et ad aedificandam unitatem Ecclesiae, angularem lapidem de monte sine manibus abscidit; dum sine opere humano de corpore virginali carnem sibi nostrae redemptionis separavit. Ab hoc ergo die Dominus virtutum nobiscum, susceptor noster Deus Jacob, quia hodie Domini est assumptio nostra, ut inhabitet gloria in terra nostra (Psal. LXXXIV, 10). Prorsus hodie benedixisti, Domine, terram tuam, illam benedictam in mulieribus. Hodie dedisti benignitatem Spiritus sancti, ut terra nostra daret benedictum fructum ventris sui, et rorantibus coelis desuper uterus virginalis Salvatorem germinaret. Maledicta terra in opere praevaricatoris, quae etiam exercitata spinas et tribulos germinat haeredibus maledictionis (Gen. III, 17, 18). At nunc benedicta terra in opere Redemptoris, quae remissionem peccatorum fructumque vitae parturit universis, et filiis Adae praejudicium originalis dissolvit maledicti. Prorsus benedicta illa terra, quae omnino intacta, nec fossa, nec seminata, de solo rore coeli Salvatorem germinat, et mortalibus panem Angelorum, alimoniam vitae aeternae ministrat. Haec itaque terra, quia inculta erat, videbatur esse deserta, sed erat opimo fructu referta; videbatur esse eremus solitudinis, sed erat paradisus beatitudinis. Plane hortus deliciarum Dei, eremus cujus campi germinaverunt germen odoris, plane desertum refertum, de quo Agnum dominatorem terrae Pater emisit. Emitte, inquit, Agnum, Domine, de petra deserti (Isai. XVI, 1), id est, abscinde Petram de Petra, Sanctum et inviolabilem sancta et inviolata proferat virginitas. Sane in hoc ipso satis decenter Christi occasus ortui, sepultura respondet conceptioni, dum videlicet emittitur Agnus de petra deserti, condendus in petra monumenti; et cujus corpori monumentum excidendum erat in petra, ipse ab initio conceptus sui, et corpus sibi excidit de petra, et corpori locum in petra; sic integritatem petrae, cum de ea emitteretur, non imminuens, sicut nec signatam sepulturae petram, cum de ea egrederetur, aperiens."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Today the Word was made flesh, and began to dwell in us, according, that is, to the rule of sound faith which the definition of the Church's teachings has handed down to us (7). For the Church holds most firmly, and in no way doubts, that the flesh of Christ was not conceived before it was taken up by the Word; but that the Word of God itself was conceived by the taking of flesh, and the flesh was conceived by the incarnation of the Word. Today, then, Wisdom began to build for herself the house of our body in the womb of the Virgin, and in order to build the unity of the Church, cut from the mountain the corner-stone without hands; while, without human labor, from the virginal body he separated for himself the flesh of our redemption. From this day, therefore, the Lord of hosts is with us, our protector, the God of Jacob, because today the assumption of our nature is the Lord's, so that glory may dwell in our land (Psal. LXXXIV, 10). Today, indeed, O Lord, you have blessed your land, that land blessed among women. Today you gave the kindness of the Holy Spirit, that our land might give forth the blessed fruit of its womb, and that, with the heavens raining down dew from above, the virginal womb might bring forth the Savior. Cursed was the earth in the deed of the transgressor, which even when tilled brings forth thorns and thistles for the heirs of the curse (Gen. III, 17, 18). But now the earth is blessed in the work of the Redeemer, which brings forth for all the remission of sins and the fruit of life, and dissolves for the sons of Adam the sentence of the original curse. Truly blessed is that land which, wholly untouched, neither dug nor sown, from the dew of heaven alone brings forth the Savior, and ministers to mortals the bread of angels, the nourishment of eternal life. This land, then, because it was untilled, seemed to be a desert, but it was filled with rich fruit; it seemed to be a wilderness of solitude, but it was a paradise of blessedness. Truly a garden of God's delights, a wilderness whose fields brought forth the bud of fragrance, truly a desert filled full, from which the Father sent forth the Lamb, ruler of the earth. \"Send forth,\" he says, \"O Lord, the Lamb from the rock of the desert\" (Isai. XVI, 1), that is, cut off the Rock from the Rock; let holy and inviolate virginity bring forth the Holy and Inviolable One. Indeed, in this very thing it is quite fitting that Christ's setting should correspond to his rising, his burial to his conception, since the Lamb is sent forth from the rock of the desert, to be laid in the rock of the tomb; and he whose body was to have a tomb hewn for it in rock, himself from the beginning of his conception both hewed for himself a body from a rock, and a place for his body in a rock; thus not diminishing the integrity of the rock when he was sent forth from it, just as he did not open the sealed rock of the tomb when he came forth from it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si ergo petra Christus (I Cor. X, 4), ut ait Apostolus, non degenerat a Matre Filius, quando et ipsa petrae nomine censetur. An non recte vocatur petra, quae et in amorem integritatis proposito firma, affectu solida, sensu quoque ipso adversus illecebram peccati tota insensibilis erat et lapidea? an non recte petra virginalis integritas, quae et nihil parit per naturam sui; et cum parit, roris virtute divini, nec admittens conceptum, nec emittens partum, novit aperiri? Sed aperietur terra, inquit Isaias, aperiatur, et germinet Salvatorem (Isai. XLV, 8). O sancte Isaia, qui dicis aperiatur, quid ergo est quod Dominus ad Ezechielem testatur: Porta haec clausa erit, et non aperietur (Ezech. XLIV, 2)? An tibi reseratum fuit tantummodo consilium fecundandae Matris, et clausum tibi fuit mysterium clausae perenniter integritatis? Absit! inquit; nemo magis quam ego conscius hujus secreti. Quomodo enim me latuisset perpetuae virginitatis mysterium, qui tanto ante praedixi: Ecce virgo concipiet, et pariet filium (Isai. VII, 14)? Quaeris igitur quomodo dicamus, ego, aperiatur; ille, non aperietur? Non aperietur viro, aperietur Domino; sicut ibidem ad ipsum dicitur Ezechielem: Vir non transibit per eam, quoniam Dominus Deus Israel ingressus est per eam. Aperietur autem Domino non virginalis integritas corporis, quia, sicut Ezechiel post praedicta subdidit: Haec clausa erit ipsi etiam principi (Ezech. XLIV, 2, 3), sed aperietur auris et porta cordis; quia videlicet intravit per aurem Virginis Verbum incarnandum, et exivit per clausam portam corporis incarnatum. Omnipotens namque Verbum Dei, etsi infirmitatem nostram suscepit, nihil tamen de sua sibi omnipotentia minuit, quominus contra naturam carnis, et supra intelligentiam sensus nostri, corpus suum palpabile per clausa posset et integra trajicere, sicut etiam [ad] discipulos ingrediens januis clausis, oculis probavit, quod ratio non capit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If then Christ is the Rock (I Cor. X, 4), as the Apostle says, the Son does not degenerate from his Mother, since she too is reckoned by the name of rock. Is she not rightly called rock, who was in the resolve of love for integrity firm, solid in affection, and even in sense itself wholly insensible and stony against the enticement of sin? Is not virginal integrity rightly called rock, which brings forth nothing by its own nature and, when it brings forth by the power of the divine dew, knows no opening either to receive conception or to bring forth the child? But Isaiah says, \"The earth shall be opened; let it be opened and bud forth the Savior\" (Isai. XLV, 8). O holy Isaiah, you who say \"let it be opened,\" what then is it that the Lord testifies to Ezekiel: \"This gate shall be shut, and shall not be opened\" (Ezech. XLIV, 2)? Was the plan for making the Mother fruitful revealed to you alone, while the mystery of her perpetually closed integrity was hidden from you? \"Far from it!\" he says; \"no one is more conscious of this secret than I. For how could the mystery of perpetual virginity have escaped me, who so long before foretold: 'Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son' (Isai. VII, 14)?\" You ask, then, how we can say, I, \"let it be opened\"; he, \"it shall not be opened\"? It shall not be opened to man, it shall be opened to the Lord; just as it is said there to Ezekiel himself: \"No man shall pass through it, because the Lord God of Israel has entered through it.\" But it is not the virginal integrity of the body that is opened to the Lord, because, as Ezekiel adds after the foregoing: \"This shall be shut even to the prince himself\" (Ezech. XLIV, 2, 3); rather, it is the ear and the gate of the heart that are opened; because, namely, the Word to be incarnated entered through the ear of the Virgin, and came forth incarnate through the closed gate of the body. For the almighty Word of God, although he took up our weakness, nevertheless diminished nothing of his omnipotence, so that, contrary to the nature of flesh and beyond the understanding of our sense, he could pass his palpable body through closed and intact things, just as also, entering [to] the disciples through closed doors, he proved by sight what reason cannot grasp.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Secura igitur, o Virgo intemerata, o porta sanctuarii perenniter clausa, secura aperi Domino Deo Israel, qui tibi jam dudum clamat: Aperi mihi, soror mea, amica mea (Cantic. V, 2). Non est quod timeas integritati tuae: Deus non novit integra violare, sed violata consolidare. Si Verbo Dei apta es, tum demum non modo clausa, sed et signata es. Pone me, inquit, ut signaculum super cor tuum, ut signaculum super brachium tuum (Id. VIII, 6). Jesus quippe impressus in corde, expressus in opere, sigillum plane et munimentum est inviolabile castitatis sponsae suae, et eo ipso quo se imprimit imitationis formam, imponit etiam incorruptionis custodiam. Sit ergo, o Virgo fidelis, auris tua aperta ad audiendum et mens tua ad credendum: aure percipe verbum angeli, et corde suscipe Verbum Altissimi, et corpore concipe Filium Dei. Dic et tu, o beata, tam humilis quam fidelis, dic Dominus aperuit mihi aurem; ego autem non contradico, retrorsum non abii (Isai. L, 5). Ecce ancilla Domini: praesto sum ejus voluntati; quinimo juvabo votis si possum, fiat mihi secundum verbum tuum (Luc. I, 38). Hoc dicere, sic suam devotionem offerre, istud plane est Domino pectus aperire, istud est etiam os aperire, et spiritum attrahere. Sic profecto aperiebatur terra, ut, quem rorabant coeli desuper, susciperet rorem, et germinaret Salvatorem. Nobile germen, germen justum, germen odoris, germen Domini, quod jam est in magnificentia et gloria, cum idem fructus terrae sub imis est super omnia, id est, exaltatus est super coelos Deus, et super omnem terram gloria ejus. Nihil enim interest sive germen, sive fructum aut florem dixeris, cum haec omnia, imo infinita alia unus Christus sit, res una, sed multiformis gratia, et virtutis unius operatio multifaria; cui si infinita nomina paupertas humani sensus et sermonis pro similitudine commodaverit, rei tamen implere significationem non poterit. Bene autem simul vocatur germen, et flos, et fructus, qui sine gradu profectus, ab initio conceptus sui omni virtute et gratia in se ipso exstitit perfectus. In nobis enim primum ipse germen est, cum fides in confessionem prorumpit vel opus manifestum: postmodum flos, cum super proficientem efflorescit sanctificatio Dei quodam spectabili decore virtutum; demum autem fructus, cum beatitudo satiat hominem consummatum. Pulcherrime sane ac vigilantissime non solum mysteria, sed et mysteriorum praesagia dispensatio praeordinavit divina, ut locus in quo terra germinavit Salvatorem germen justum, et flos de virga et radice Jesse ascendit, Nazareth, id est sanctitas, germen, flos, virgultum vocaretur; ut videlicet negotium loco, locusque negotio consona voce contestaretur, pariterque et nomen loci rem gerendam pronuntiaret, et res gesta causam nominis indicaret."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Therefore, without fear, O undefiled Virgin, O gate of the sanctuary closed forever, without fear open to the Lord God of Israel, who has long been crying out to you: 'Open to me, my sister, my love' (Cantic. V, 2). You have no reason to fear for your integrity: God does not know how to violate what is whole, but to make whole what is violated. If you are fitted to the Word of God, then you are not only closed, but also sealed. 'Set me,' he says, 'as a seal upon your heart, as a seal upon your arm' (Id. VIII, 6). For Jesus, impressed upon the heart, expressed in deed, is plainly the seal and the inviolable safeguard of the chastity of his bride; and by the very fact that he impresses himself as a pattern for imitation, he also imposes upon her the guardianship of incorruption. Let your ear, then, O faithful Virgin, be open to hear, and your mind to believe: with your ear perceive the word of the angel, with your heart receive the Word of the Most High, and in your body conceive the Son of God. Say, too, O blessed one, as humble as you are faithful, say: 'The Lord has opened my ear; but I do not contradict, I have not drawn back' (Isai. L, 5). 'Behold the handmaid of the Lord: I am ready for his will; indeed I will even assist his purposes if I can \u2014 be it done to me according to your word' (Luc. I, 38). To say this, to offer one's devotion in this way, is plainly to open one's breast to the Lord; it is also to open one's mouth and draw in the Spirit. Thus indeed was the earth opened, so that it might receive as dew him whom the heavens rained down from above, and bring forth the Savior as its sprout. A noble sprout, a righteous sprout, a sprout of fragrance, the sprout of the Lord, who is now in magnificence and glory, since that same fruit of the earth, though from the lowest depths, is above all things \u2014 that is, God has been exalted above the heavens, and his glory above all the earth. For it makes no difference whether you call him sprout, or fruit, or flower, since all these things \u2014 indeed, countless others besides \u2014 are one Christ, one reality, but grace of many forms, and the working of one power in many ways; and to him, even if the poverty of human understanding and speech lends countless names by way of likeness, it can never fulfill the full meaning of the reality. Rightly, however, is he called at once sprout, and flower, and fruit, who, without any gradual advance, existed perfect in himself in every virtue and grace from the very beginning of his conception. For in us, he is first a sprout, when faith breaks forth into confession or into a manifest work; afterward a flower, when the sanctification of God blossoms upon the one who is advancing, with a certain visible beauty of virtues; and finally a fruit, when beatitude satisfies the person brought to completion. Most beautifully indeed, and most watchfully, divine providence foreordained not only the mysteries themselves but also the foreshadowings of the mysteries, so that the place in which the earth brought forth the Savior, the righteous sprout, and where the flower arose from the rod and root of Jesse, should be called Nazareth \u2014 that is, 'holiness,' 'sprout,' 'flower,' 'shoot' \u2014 so that the event might bear witness to the place, and the place to the event, with a single harmonious voice, and that likewise the name of the place might proclaim the deed to be accomplished, and the deed accomplished might reveal the reason for the name.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Ignoscite mihi, fratres mei, quia, qui morum vestrorum servire debueram instructioni, plus forsitan quam necesse erat demoratus sum in illius ineffabilis admiratione et praeconio mysterii. Quid enim mirum si miror quod stupent Angeli, si praedicare gestio, quam coeli enarrant, gloriam Dei? quid mirum si me delectat quod beatos spiritus laetificat, quod peccatores justificat, justosque glorificat? Nescio tamen si ulla esse possit efficacior ac suavior morum aedificatio, quam hujus mysterii, id est Verbi incarnati, fidelis et pia consideratio. Quid enim sic hominem excitare potest ad amorem Dei, quam praeveniens hominem amor Dei tam vehemens ad hominem, ut homo propter hominem velit fieri? Quid autem ita nutrit amorem proximi, quam similitudo et natura proximi in humanitate Dei? Nam humilitatis exemplum nec cogitari posse majus aliquod existimo, quam exinanitionem Dei in formam servi, et servitutem plus quam servi. Castitatem porro quid aeque commendat, sicut quod ipsa castitas Salvatorem germinat? Aut fidei virtutem ac meritum quid evidentius ostendit, quam quod Virgo Deum fide concepit, fide omnia sibi promissa divinitus impleri promeruerit? Beata, inquit, quae credidit, quoniam perficientur quae dicta sunt ei a Domino (ibid. 45). Et ut plenius noveris conceptum Virginis non solum esse mysticum, sed et moralem; quod sacramentum est ad redemptionem, exemplum quoque tibi est ad imitationem; ut manifeste evacues in te gratiam sacramenti, si non imiteris virtutem exempli. Quae enim Deum fide concepit, si fidem habeas, idem tibi promittit, quod videlicet si verbum ex ore nuntii coelestis fideliter velis suscipere, Deum quem totus orbis non potest capere, possis et ipse concipere. Concipere autem corde, imo et corpore, licet non corporali opere aut specie, tamen plane corpore tuo; quandoquidem jubemur ab Apostolo glorificare et portare Deum in corpore nostro (I Cor. VI, 20). Attende itaque, ut scriptum est, diligenter auditui tuo, nam fides ex auditu, auditus autem per Verbum Dei (Rom. X, 17); quod tibi absque dubio evangelizat angelus Dei, cum de timore aut amore ejus tecum agit praedicator fidelis, quem angelum Domini exercituum dici et esse non est tibi fas ambigere (Malach. II, 16, 17). Quam beati qui dicere possunt: A timore tuo, Domine, concepimus et parturivimus spiritum salutis (Isa. XXVI, 17, 18)! qui nimirum non est alius quam spiritus Salvatoris, quam veritas Jesu Christi. Vide ineffabilem dignationem Dei, simulque virtutem incomprehensibilis mysterii: qui creavit te, creatur in te; et, quasi parum esset te ipsum habere patrem, vult etiam te sibi fieri matrem. Quicumque, inquit, fecerit voluntatem patris mei, ipse meus et frater, et soror, et mater est (Matth. XII, 50). O fidelis anima, expande sinus, dilata affectus; ne angustieris in visceribus tuis, concipe quem creatura non capit. Aperi Verbo Dei aurem audiendi: haec est ad uterum cordis via spiritus concipiendi, hac ratione compinguntur ossa Christi, id est, virtutes in ventre praegnantis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Forgive me, my brothers, because I, who ought to have served the instruction of your morals, have perhaps lingered longer than was necessary in admiration and praise of that ineffable mystery. For what wonder is it if I marvel at what makes the Angels stand amazed, if I am eager to proclaim the glory of God which the heavens declare? What wonder is it if I am delighted by what gladdens the blessed spirits, what justifies sinners, and glorifies the just? Yet I do not know whether there can be any more effective or sweeter edification of morals than the faithful and pious consideration of this mystery, that is, of the Word incarnate. For what can so arouse man to the love of God as the love of God going before man, so vehement toward man that he willed to become man for man's sake? And what so nourishes love of neighbor as the likeness and nature of a neighbor found in the humanity of God? For I judge that no greater example of humility can even be conceived than the emptying of God into the form of a servant, and a servitude greater than that of a servant. Furthermore, what commends chastity so much as the fact that chastity itself brings forth the Savior as its offspring? Or what shows the power and merit of faith more clearly than that the Virgin conceived God by faith, and by faith merited that all things divinely promised to her should be fulfilled? Blessed, he says, is she who believed, for the things that were spoken to her by the Lord shall be fulfilled (ibid. 45). And that you may know more fully that the Virgin's conception is not only mystical but also moral \u2014 what is a sacrament for redemption is also an example for you to imitate \u2014 so that you plainly render void in yourself the grace of the sacrament, if you fail to imitate the virtue of the example. For she who conceived God by faith promises you the same thing, if you have faith: namely, that if you are willing to receive faithfully the word from the mouth of the heavenly messenger, you too can conceive the God whom the whole world cannot contain. And to conceive \u2014 in heart, indeed also in body, though not by bodily act or bodily form, yet truly in your body \u2014 since we are commanded by the Apostle to glorify and to carry God in our body (I Cor. VI, 20). Attend, therefore, as it is written, diligently to your hearing, for faith comes from hearing, and hearing through the word of God (Rom. X, 17); which without doubt the angel of God announces to you as gospel, when a faithful preacher deals with you concerning fear or love of him \u2014 and you may not rightly doubt that he is rightly called, and truly is, the angel of the Lord of hosts (Malach. II, 16, 17). How blessed are they who can say: From fear of you, O Lord, we have conceived and brought forth the spirit of salvation (Isa. XXVI, 17, 18)! \u2014 which indeed is none other than the spirit of the Savior, the truth of Jesus Christ. Behold the ineffable condescension of God, and at the same time the power of this incomprehensible mystery: he who created you is created in you; and, as though it were not enough for him to have you yourself as a father, he wills also that you become a mother to him. Whoever, he says, does the will of my Father, he is my brother, and sister, and mother (Matth. XII, 50). O faithful soul, open wide your bosom, enlarge your affections; do not be straitened in your inmost being \u2014 conceive him whom no creature can contain. Open to the Word of God the ear of hearing: this is the way to the womb of the heart for conceiving the spirit; by this means the bones of Christ \u2014 that is, the virtues \u2014 are knit together in the womb of her who is pregnant.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Gratias tibi, sancte Spiritus, qui ubi vis spiras. Video munere tuo non unam, sed innumeras fidelium animas illo generoso germine gravidas; custodi opera tua, ne aliqua illarum abortiat, conceptumque divinae sobolis informem aut mortuum excutiat. Vos quoque, o matres beatae tam gloriosae prolis, attendite vobis ipsis, donec formetur Christus in vobis (Galat. IV, 19); attendite, ne qua foris offensa gravior fetum tenerum laedat, ne quid ingeratis ventri, id est menti, quod spiritum quem concepistis exstinguat. Parcite, si non vobis, certe Filio Dei in vobis; parcite, inquam, non solum ab operibus et sermonibus malis, sed etiam a cogitationibus noxiis et delectationibus mortiferis, quae plane suffocant semen Dei. Omni itaque custodia servate corda vestra, quia ex ipsis vita procedet, cum scilicet maturus se partus absolvet, et vita Christi, quae nunc abscondita est in cordibus vestris, manifestabitur in carne vestra mortali. Concepistis spiritum salutis, sed adhuc parturitis, nondum peperistis. Si labor in parturiendo, magna de spe partus consolatio. Mulier cum parit, tristitiam habet (Joan. XVI, 21) laboris; cum autem pepererit puerum, jam non memor erit pressurae propter gaudium, quia natus erit homo Christus in mundum exteriorem corporis nostri, quod et minor mundus solet appellari. Qui enim nunc conceptus est Deus in spiritibus nostris, configurans eos spiritui charitatis suae, tunc velut homo nascetur in corporibus, configurans ea corpori claritatis suae, in qua vivit et gloriatur Deus per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Thanks be to you, Holy Spirit, who breathe where you will. I see, by your gift, not one but countless souls of the faithful made heavy with that noble offspring; guard your works, lest any of them miscarry, and cast out the conceived offspring of divine progeny unformed or dead. You too, O blessed mothers of so glorious a child, attend to yourselves, until Christ be formed in you (Galat. IV, 19); take heed lest some graver offense from without injure the tender offspring, lest you introduce into the womb \u2014 that is, into the mind \u2014 anything that might extinguish the spirit you have conceived. Spare \u2014 if not yourselves, then certainly the Son of God within you; spare, I say, not only from evil deeds and words, but also from harmful thoughts and deadly pleasures, which utterly suffocate the seed of God. Keep your hearts, therefore, with all vigilance, for from them life shall proceed, when, that is, the mature birth is brought to completion, and the life of Christ, which is now hidden in your hearts, shall be made manifest in your mortal flesh. You have conceived the spirit of salvation, but you are still in labor; you have not yet given birth. If there is labor in giving birth, there is great consolation in the hope of the birth. A woman, when she gives birth, has sorrow (Joan. XVI, 21) of her labor; but when she has borne the child, she no longer remembers the anguish, for joy, because the man Christ shall have been born into the outer world of our body, which is also commonly called the lesser world. For he who is now conceived as God in our spirits, conforming them to the spirit of his charity, shall then, as it were, be born as man in our bodies, conforming them to the body of his glory, in which God lives and is glorified through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-n007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(7) Scilicet Gennadius."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-n007", "label": "English", "text": "(7) Namely, Gennadius.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The description of the priest as the messenger of the Lord of hosts comes from Malachi 2:7; the edition prints Malach. II, 16, 17."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition supplies ad in brackets before discipulos, in the comparison with Christ entering through closed doors."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Annunciation Sermon II", "topics": ["Annunciation", "Incarnation", "Virgin Mary", "faith", "charity", "humility", "chastity", "Holy Spirit", "spiritual growth"], "url": "sermons/guerric-annunciation-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:annunciation", "calendar_label": "The Annunciation of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-annunciation-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De hujus mysterii signis., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 124-128.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Annunciation", "Annunciation of the Lord", "Isaiah 7:13\u201314", "Jeremiah 31:22", "Psalm 66:6 (printed locator; quotation from Psalm 66:6-7, historical numbering)", "Isaiah 7:10\u201312", "Matthew 16:4", "Isaiah 1:4", "Psalm 85:17 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 7:14", "Luke 1:31", "1 Corinthians 1:18", "Ephesians 4:9\u201310", "Luke 1:30\u201331 (printed locator; quotation from Luke 1:37)", "Isaiah 30:15", "Isaiah 32:17", "Ecclesiastes 11:5", "Psalm 138:15 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 25:28", "Ezekiel 44:3", "Psalm 31:14 (printed locator; quotation from Psalm 131:14, historical numbering)", "3 Kings 10:18 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Revelation 3:20", "John 6:64", "Matthew 4:4", "John 5:26", "Luke 22:15, 19", "Genesis 25:22", "Luke 3:8"], "occasion": "In Annuntiatione Dominica", "occasion_detail": "Annunciation of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Annunciation", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads Isaiah's sign of the Virgin through the Incarnation, Passion and exaltation of Christ. His argument includes polemic against Jewish interpretations of the prophecy. He turns from Christ's hidden life in the womb to monastic silence, nourishment by the divine Word and patience within the Church. The sermon appears under the Annunciation heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De hujus mysterii signis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the signs of this mystery.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Audite, domus David: ecce dabit Dominus ipse vobis signum. Ecce virgo concipiet (Isai. VII, 13, 14). Hodie impleta est haec prophetia in auribus vestris. Hodie namque illud ineffabile conceptus virginalis miraculum, sicut audieratis promissum, sic audistis impletum: Virgo hodie concepit. Et hoc signum, prioribus retro saeculis inauditum, nostris saeculis Dominus dedit. Et sicut Jeremias eodem spiritu et eodem sensu praedixit: Creabit Dominus novum super terram, quia femina circumdabit virum (Jerem. XXXI, 22). Quid est enim, creabit Dominus novum super terram, femina circumdabit virum, quod ait Jeremias, nisi quod ait Isaias: Dabit Dominus signum, Virgo concipiet filium (Isai. VII, 13, 14)? Hoc nempe est, circumdabit. Non de consortio virili suscipiet, sed de se sola intra semetipsam concipiet, et solius materni corporis involucro vestiet. Alioquin dicant, si possunt Judaei, quod signum Dominus in hoc dedit, si non virgo, sed adolescentula, ut ipsi falso interpretantur, concepit; aut quid novum Dominus creavit, si femina masculum de masculo susceptum circumdedit. Potest quidem mentiri iniquitas sibi; sed in multitudine virtutis tuae, o Domine, mentientur tibi inimici tui, ut virtutem tuam dum negat pravitas et paucitas Judaeorum, gloriosius et copiosius confiteatur fides omnium populorum. Confiteantur tibi populi, Deus, confiteantur tibi populi omnes, quia terra dedit fructum suum, Virgo germinavit Jesum (Psal. LXVI, 6). Velint, nolint Judaei, creavit Dominus novitatem hujus miraculi, in signum eorum incredulitatis; utique in signum cui contradicunt usque hodie, plus, nisi fallor, pertinacia odibili, quam ignorantia miserabili."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Hear, O house of David: behold, the Lord himself will give you a sign. Behold, a virgin shall conceive (Isai. VII, 13, 14). Today this prophecy has been fulfilled in your hearing. For today that ineffable miracle of virginal conception, just as you had heard it promised, so you have heard it fulfilled: today a Virgin has conceived. And this sign, unheard of in former ages, the Lord has given to our age. And just as Jeremiah foretold in the same spirit and with the same meaning: The Lord will create something new upon the earth, for a woman shall encompass a man (Jerem. XXXI, 22). For what is it that Jeremiah says, \"The Lord will create something new upon the earth, a woman shall encompass a man,\" except what Isaiah says: \"The Lord will give a sign, a Virgin shall conceive a son\" (Isai. VII, 13, 14)? This indeed is what \"encompass\" means: she will not conceive through union with a man, but will conceive within herself alone, from herself alone, and will clothe him solely in the wrapping of a maternal body. Otherwise, let the Jews say, if they can, what sign the Lord gave in this, if it was not a virgin but a young woman who conceived, as they falsely interpret it; or what new thing the Lord created, if a woman merely enclosed within herself a male conceived from a male. Iniquity can indeed lie to itself; but in the multitude of your power, O Lord, your enemies will lie to you, so that while the wickedness and the small number of the Jews deny your power, the faith of all peoples may confess it more gloriously and abundantly. Let the peoples confess to you, O God, let all the peoples confess to you, for the earth has given its fruit, the Virgin has brought forth Jesus (Psal. LXVI, 6). Whether the Jews wish it or not, the Lord has created the novelty of this miracle as a sign of their unbelief; indeed as a sign which they contradict even to this day, more, unless I am mistaken, out of hateful obstinacy than out of pitiable ignorance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Non alienum tamen ab origine et consuetudine viperei germinis, si modo contradicunt huic signo, postquam factum est, qui ab initio in patre et rege suo impiissimo Achaz contradixerunt ne fieret. Locutus est enim Dominus ad Achaz, dicens: Pete tibi signum. Non petam, inquit, et non tentabo Dominum (Isai. VII, 10-12). O religio profana! o pietas exsecranda! o humilitas dolosa! Ne tentes, ut ais, Dominum, contemnis Dominum. Quomodo enim tentares, si fideliter obedires? Nunc autem quomodo non graviter tentas, quem manifesto contemptu irritas? Novimus utique, novimus dolum et invidiam Judaicae radicis, quae et antequam Christus nasceretur, invidere coepit gloriae illius. Nam et iste Achaz, quantum ex vita et moribus ipsius intelligi datur (nam cultor idolorum erat), non alia religione aut metu signum recusavit petere cum juberetur, nisi ideo scilicet ne Dominus glorificaretur. Sane mira et plane digna ira Dei et hominum, tam dira perversitas Judaeorum, qui, cum jubentur signa quaerere, renuunt, non, ut fingunt, ne tentent, sed ne glorificent Dominum (Matth. XVI, 4); cum non jubentur, tentant et quaerunt. Nam velut natura et usu Judaei signa quaerunt, et, si dantur, calumniantur et inficiari contendunt, ut manifeste confiteantur quia tentantes quaerebant, non ut crederent facienti, sed ut insultarent non facienti. O gens peccatrix; semen nequam, filii scelerati (Isai. I, 4)! ait Isaias, Numquid parum est vobis molestos esse hominibus, mihi et aliis prophetis, imo universo generi hominum, quia molesti estis et Deo meo? Propter hoc dabit Dominus ipse vobis signum (Isa. VII, 13, 14). Quia enim vos in perversum adversum eum inceditis, et ipse, ut ait, perversus incedet vobis. Non vultis signum dari, ne glorificetur auctor miraculi; et ipse propter hoc dabit, ut ipse glorificetur, et vos confundamini. Fac, Domine, fac mecum signum in bonum, ut videant qui me oderunt, et confundantur (Psal. LXXXV, 17), ait de Judaeis ad Patrem Filius."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Yet it is not foreign to the origin and custom of the viperous brood, if they now contradict this sign after it has been accomplished, seeing that from the beginning they contradicted in their father and most impious king Ahaz that it should ever come to pass. For the Lord spoke to Ahaz, saying: Ask for yourself a sign. I will not ask, he said, and I will not tempt the Lord (Isai. VII, 10-12). O profane religion! O execrable piety! O deceitful humility! When you say, \"Lest I tempt the Lord,\" you are despising the Lord. For how would you be tempting him, if you obeyed faithfully? But now, how are you not gravely tempting him, whom you provoke by manifest contempt? We know indeed, we know the deceit and envy of the Jewish root, which even before Christ was born began to envy his glory. For this Ahaz too, as far as can be understood from his life and character (for he was a worshiper of idols), refused to ask for a sign when commanded, not out of any religious scruple or fear, but precisely so that the Lord might not be glorified. Truly wondrous, and plainly deserving the wrath of God and of men, is such dire perversity of the Jews, who, when commanded to seek signs, refuse \u2014 not, as they pretend, lest they tempt God, but lest they glorify the Lord (Matth. XVI, 4); and when not commanded, they tempt and seek. For as if by nature and custom the Jews seek signs, and, if given, they slander them and strive to deny them, so that they plainly confess that in tempting they sought not in order to believe in the one who performed the sign, but in order to insult the one who did not perform it. O sinful nation, wicked seed, corrupt children (Isai. I, 4)! says Isaiah. Is it too little for you to be troublesome to men \u2014 to me and to other prophets, indeed to the whole human race \u2014 that you are troublesome also to my God? Therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign (Isa. VII, 13, 14). For because you walk perversely against him, he too, as he says, will walk perversely toward you. You do not wish a sign to be given, lest the author of the miracle be glorified; and for this very reason he will give it, that he himself may be glorified, and you confounded. Work, O Lord, work with me a sign for good, that those who hate me may see it and be confounded (Psal. LXXXV, 17), says the Son to the Father concerning the Jews.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Primum autem signum quod Pater et Filius operati sunt in confusionem infidelium et gloriam fidelium, in testimonium suae virtutis et opus nostrae salutis, arbitror hodiernum esse Virginis conceptum. Nam de ipso cum praemissum esset, dabit Dominus signum, quasi quaerentibus quod signum, intulit contextus Prophetae, verbis tamen Evangelistae, Ecce virgo concipiet et pariet filium (Isai. VII, 14, et Luc. I, 31); ut plane aut consequentia vel veritate contextus careat, aut (quod est probabilius) mendacium Judaeorum latebram non habeat. Merito proinde cum jam generatio prava et adultera signum quaerit, signum non datur ei, nisi signum Jonae (Matth. XVI, 4); ut videlicet qui ob perversitatem mentis non aedificantur signo virtutis, scandalizentur signo infirmitatis, triduanae scilicet sepulturae et mortis. Nam verbum crucis et mortis Judaeis, qui pereunt, scandalum est; his autem qui salvi fiunt, id est, nobis, virtus Dei est (I Cor. I, 18); nec minor aut infirmior nobis est Filius hominis in corde terrae, quam in consessu paternae dexterae. Signum quippe quod illi recusaverunt, sive in profundum inferni, sive in excelsum supra, nos fide plena ac veneratione devota suscipimus, agnoscentes Filium quem concipit Virgo, in profundo inferni signum nobis esse liberationis et veniae; in excelsum supra, signum et spem exsultationis et gloriae. Qui enim primum descendit in inferiores partes terrae, ut in sanguine testamenti sui extraheret vinctos de lacu in quo non est aqua, ipse est et qui ascendit super omnes coelos, ut adimpleret omnia (Ephes. IV, 9, 10). Jam levavit Dominus signum primum in patibulo crucis, postmodum in throno regni, et exaltavit signum ad populos nationum, quia contradictum est ei a populo Judaeorum, et quotidie a quatuor ventis congregat dispersos veri Israel ad hoc signum. O Radix Jesse, qui stas in signum populorum, super quem jam continent reges os suum, obstruatur etiam os loquentium iniqua, id est, Judaeorum blasphemantium, qui adhuc signo immaculato conceptus tui contradicunt, et nec angelo Gabrieli credunt dicenti quia non est impossibile apud te omne verbum (Luc. I, 30, 31). Beata quae credidit, cui haec ratio satisfecit, et, cum quaesisset quomodo Filium susciperet, quae non cognosceret virum, tam de integritate quam de prole securam deinceps effecit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. The first sign which the Father and the Son worked for the confusion of unbelievers and the glory of the faithful, in testimony of their power and as the work of our salvation, I judge to be the Son's conception in the Virgin today. For concerning it, when it had been foretold, \"The Lord shall give a sign,\" as though to those asking what that sign might be, the continuation of the Prophet added\u2014though in the words of the Evangelist\u2014\"Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son\" (Isai. VII, 14, et Luc. I, 31); so that plainly either the context lacks consequence or truth, or (which is more probable) the lie of the Jews finds no hiding place. Fittingly, then, since this crooked and adulterous generation now seeks a sign, no sign is given it except the sign of Jonah (Matth. XVI, 4); so that those who, on account of the perversity of their mind, are not built up by the sign of power, may be scandalized by the sign of weakness\u2014namely, of the three-day burial and death. For the word of the cross and of death is to the Jews who perish a stumbling block; but to us who are being saved it is the power of God (I Cor. I, 18); nor is the Son of Man any less or weaker for us in the heart of the earth than seated at the Father's right hand. For the sign which they refused, whether in the depth of hell or on high above, we receive with full faith and devout veneration, acknowledging that the Son whom the Virgin conceives is, in the depth of hell, a sign to us of liberation and pardon; and on high above, a sign and hope of exultation and glory. For he who first descended into the lower parts of the earth, that in the blood of his testament he might draw out the bound from the pit in which there is no water, is also he who ascended above all the heavens, that he might fill all things (Ephes. IV, 9, 10). The Lord has already raised up his first sign on the gibbet of the cross, afterward on the throne of the kingdom, and he has exalted the sign to the peoples of the nations, because it was contradicted by the people of the Jews, and daily he gathers from the four winds the scattered of the true Israel to this sign. O Root of Jesse, who stand as a sign of the peoples, before whom kings now keep their mouths shut, let the mouth of those who speak iniquity also be stopped\u2014that is, of the Jews who blaspheme, who still contradict the immaculate sign of your conception, and do not even believe the angel Gabriel saying that with you no word is impossible (Luc. I, 30, 31). Blessed is she who believed, for whom this reasoning was satisfying, and who, when she had asked how she should receive a Son, she who knew not man, was thereafter made secure both as to her integrity and as to her offspring.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quidquid ergo garriat impietas infidelium, nobis concipiat et pariat Virgo Filium; nos quippe signum habemus in bonum, etiam Matrem et Filium. Nobis plane Mater tota miraculum est, quae singulariter et sine exemplo mater est et virgo; nobis Filius totus miraculum, qui non solum singulariter, sed incomprehensibiliter Deus est et homo. Mater, virgo concipiens et pariens, signum nobis est quia Deus est homo iste qui concipitur et paritur; Filius, divina faciens, et humana patiens, signum nobis est quia in Deum transferet hominem, pro quo concipitur et paritur, etiam et patitur. Omnium tamen humanarum infirmitatum vel injuriarum quas pro nobis pertulit divina dignatio, sicut tempore primam, sic etiam humilitate fere maximam existimo, quod in utero concipi, in utero novem mensium tempore majestas illa incircumscripta passa est contineri. Ubi enim sic se exinanivit? aut quando ita penitus a semetipso defecisse visus fuit? Tanto tempore nihil illa sapientia loquitur, nihil illa virtus manifestum operatur, nullo signo visibili majestas, quae clausa latet, proditur. Non sic in cruce visus est infirmus, ubi quod infirmum ipsius fuit, statim apparuit fortius omnibus hominibus: quando et moriens glorificat latronem, et exspirans inspirat Centurionem; quando horarius dolor passionis ei non solum compati fecit elementa creaturarum, sed etiam contrarias fortitudines subigit aeternorum passioni dolorum. In utero autem sic est quasi non sit, sic omnipotens virtus vacat quasi nihil possit et Verbum aeternum sub silentio se premit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Whatever, then, the impiety of unbelievers may chatter, let the Virgin conceive and bear a Son for us; for we indeed have a sign for good, even the Mother and the Son. For us, the Mother is entirely a miracle, who singularly and without precedent is both mother and virgin; for us, the Son is entirely a miracle, who not only singularly but incomprehensibly is God and man. The Mother, a virgin conceiving and bearing, is a sign to us that this one who is conceived and born is God and man; the Son, doing divine things and suffering human things, is a sign to us that he will transfer into God the man for whom he is conceived and born, and also suffers. Yet of all the human infirmities or injuries which the divine condescension bore for us, I judge that, just as it was first in time, so also it was almost the greatest in humility, that his boundless majesty suffered to be conceived in the womb, and contained within the womb for the space of nine months. For where did he so empty himself? Or when did he seem so utterly to have ceased to be himself? For such a long time that wisdom speaks nothing, that power works nothing manifest, and majesty, lying hidden and enclosed, is betrayed by no visible sign. Not so did he appear weak on the cross, where what was weak in him immediately appeared stronger than all men: when, even while dying, he glorifies the thief, and, while breathing his last, he inspires the Centurion; when the brief pain of the Passion not only made the elements of creation suffer with him, but also subjects the opposing powers to the suffering of eternal pains. But in the womb he is as though he were not; thus the omnipotent power lies idle as though it could do nothing, and the eternal Word presses itself down into silence.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Vobis tamen, fratres, vobis illud silentium verbi loquitur, vobis clamat; vobis utique disciplinam silentii commendat. In silentio enim et spe erit fortitudo vestra (Isai. XXX, 15), sicut Isaias promittit, qui cultum justitiae silentium definivit (Id. XXXII, 17). Sicut namque puer ille conceptus in utero ad maturitatem partus sub alto et diuturno profecit silentio, sic et spiritum hominis disciplina silentii nutrit, format et roborat, et quanto occultius, tanto tutius et salubrius incrementum praestat. Nescit homo animalis, qui non percipit quae sunt spiritus Dei, quae sit via spiritus, et quomodo compingantur ossa in ventre praegnantis (Eccle. XI, 5); sed non occultatum os meum a te, ait sanctus Deo, quod fecisti mihi in occulto (Psal. CXXXVIII, 15) mentis, sub secreto silentii. Sed nec a vobis occultatum est hoc mysterium, fratres mei, quorum experientia et confessio testis mihi est quomodo quietus et modestus spiritus sub silentio roboretur, pinguescat et floreat; quomodo e contrario per verba, velut quadam paralysi, dissolvatur et defluat; quomodo macrescat, et marcescat, et aridus decidat. Denique si in silentio fortitudo non esset, Salomon nequaquam dixisset: Sicut urbs patens et absque murorum ambitu, ita vir qui non potest in loquendo cohibere spiritum suum (Prov. XXV, 28). Caeterum, si quaeris quo negotio sit occupanda mens in silentio, nihil tibi imponimus onerosum, comede panem tuum, sicut et ipse Dominus conceptus suo tibi commonstrat exemplo. Quid enim propheta de eo dixit, cum loqueretur de porta orientali semper clausa in domo Domini, quae tamen Deum Israel admisit et emisit? Princeps ipse, inquit, sedebit in ea, ut comedat panem coram Domino (Ezech. XLIV, 3). Sedebit, inquit, in ea, quoniam requiescet in ea, de qua et ipse loquitur: Haec requies mea (Psal. XXXI, 14); sedebit in ea tanquam in throno grandi, quem, sicut alias dixi, Salomon rex sibi de ebore fecit (III Reg. X, 18). Si attendas angustias uteri, locus prorsus angustus est, si latitudinem cordis, thronus grandis est, propter quam etiam uterus tantae majestatis capax factus est. In ea ergo princeps sedit et panem comedit; quia si quis, inquit, aperuerit mihi, intrabo ad eum, et coenabo cum eo, et ipse mecum (Apoc. III, 20)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Yet to you, brothers, to you that silence of the Word speaks, to you it cries out; to you indeed it commends the discipline of silence. For \"in silence and hope shall your strength be\" (Isai. XXX, 15), as Isaiah promises, who defined the practice of righteousness as silence (Id. XXXII, 17). For just as that child, conceived in the womb, advanced toward the maturity of birth under deep and prolonged silence, so too the discipline of silence nourishes, forms, and strengthens the spirit of man; and the more hidden it is, the safer and more wholesome growth it affords. The natural man, who does not perceive the things of the Spirit of God, does not know what is the way of the spirit, and how the bones are joined together in the womb of her that is with child (Eccle. XI, 5); but \"my bone is not hidden from thee,\" says the saint to God, \"which thou hast made for me in secret\" (Psal. CXXXVIII, 15) \u2014 in secret of mind, under the secrecy of silence. But neither is this mystery hidden from you, my brothers, whose experience and confession is witness to me how a quiet and modest spirit is strengthened, grows rich, and flourishes under silence; how, on the contrary, through words, as by a kind of paralysis, it is dissolved and wastes away; how it grows thin, and withers, and falls away dried up. Finally, if there were no strength in silence, Solomon would by no means have said: \"As a city that lies open and without the enclosure of walls, so is the man who cannot restrain his spirit in speaking\" (Prov. XXV, 28). But if you ask with what business the mind ought to be occupied in silence, we impose on you nothing burdensome: eat your bread, as the Lord himself, conceived in the womb, shows you by his own example. For what did the prophet say of him, when he spoke of the eastern gate, always shut, in the house of the Lord, which nevertheless admitted and sent forth the God of Israel? \"The prince himself,\" he says, \"shall sit in it, to eat bread before the Lord\" (Ezech. XLIV, 3). \"He shall sit,\" he says, \"in it,\" because he will rest in it, of which he himself also says: \"This is my rest\" (Psal. XXXI, 14); he shall sit in it as on a great throne, which, as I have said elsewhere, King Solomon made for himself of ivory (III Reg. X, 18). If you consider the narrowness of the womb, it is altogether a narrow place; if you consider the breadth of the heart, it is a great throne \u2014 on account of which the womb was made capable of so great a majesty. In it, therefore, the prince sat and ate bread; for \"if any man,\" he says, \"shall open to me, I will come in to him, and will sup with him, and he with me\" (Apoc. III, 20).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Non est haec coena sine pane, quando et ipse qui coenat panis est vitae, panis qui hodie descendit de coelo et dat vitam mundo. Sed res mira, si idem est qui coenat, et quod coenatur; et qui comedit, ipse est panis qui ab eo comeditur. Revera res mira, sed res vera; quia Christus non alio pane quam se ipso pascitur. Ipse namque totus est panis, Verbum propter se, caro propter unionem Verbi. Alioquin caro non prodest quidquam, cum spiritus sit qui vivificat (Joan. VI, 64), nec in solo pane vivat homo, sed in omni verbo quod procedit de ore Dei (Matth. IV, 4). Omne verbum procedens de ore Dei unum et unigenitum est Verbum Patris, quod, cum simplex sit, in se tamen continet rationem et formam omnis verbi divini. Verbum itaque pascitur de Verbo, Filius vivit de se ipso; quia sicut Pater habet vitam in semetipso, sic dedit et Filio vitam habere in semetipso (Joan. V, 26). Alia tamen ratione, sed tamen ineffabili beatitudine, Verbo pascebatur anima illa, Verbo ipsi in persona conjuncta; et incomparabili felicitate princeps ille sedens in porta virginalis uteri, panem verbi coram Domino comedebat. Id igitur operis tu quoque, si sapis, in silentio tuo actitabis, ut panem verbi divini comedas coram Domino, conservans, sicut Maria, quae de Christo dicuntur, et conferens in corde tuo. Hunc panem tecum Christus manducare delectabitur; et qui te pascit, ipse in te pascetur (Luc. XXII, 15, 19); et panis ipse quo plus editur, plus abundabit edendus: quia gratia non minuitur usu, sed augetur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. This supper is not without bread, since he himself who sups is the bread of life, the bread which today came down from heaven and gives life to the world. But it is a wondrous thing, if he who sups is the same as that which is supped upon; and he who eats is himself the bread which is eaten by him. Indeed it is a wondrous thing, but a true thing; for Christ is fed by no other bread than himself. For he himself is wholly bread: the Word on his own account, the flesh on account of its union with the Word. Otherwise the flesh profits nothing, since it is the spirit that gives life (Joan. VI, 64); nor does man live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God (Matth. IV, 4). Every word proceeding from the mouth of God is the one and only-begotten Word of the Father, which, although it is simple, nevertheless contains within itself the reason and form of every divine word. The Word, therefore, is fed from the Word; the Son lives from himself; for as the Father has life in himself, so has he given to the Son also to have life in himself (Joan. V, 26). Yet in another respect \u2014 though still by an ineffable blessedness \u2014 that soul was fed by the Word, being joined to the Word itself in person; and with incomparable felicity that prince, sitting in the gate of the virginal womb, ate the bread of the Word before the Lord. This work, therefore, you too, if you are wise, will carry on in your silence: that you may eat the bread of the divine Word before the Lord, keeping, as Mary did, the things that are said of Christ, and pondering them in your heart. Christ will delight to eat this bread with you; and he who feeds you will himself be fed in you (Luc. XXII, 15, 19); and this very bread, the more it is eaten, the more it will abound to be eaten: because grace is not diminished by use, but increased.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. In hoc propterea tibi sit exemplo Jesus conceptus et gestatus in utero, ut, sicut onus illud leve ac suave, etsi gravidavit, non tamen gravavit uterum Mariae, sic te non sentiat gravem aut molestum uterus Ecclesiae. Gravida est, o fratres, Ecclesia, non, sicut Maria, solo Jesu, sed, sicut Rebecca, Jacob et Esau, non solum scilicet bonis et modestis, sed etiam dyscolis et indisciplinatis; quos tamen et ipsos, propter nomen Jesu, vel forsitan aliquod initium substantiae ejus, Ecclesiae viscera suscipiunt et amplectuntur. Sed cum sese colliderent parvuli discordes in utero Rebeccae, quae prius oraverat ut conciperet, dolensque ventrem suum a tribulatione malorum et dolore, pene poenitebat quod concepisset: Si sic, inquit, futurum mihi erat, quid necesse erat concipere? (Gen. XXV, 22.). Si de aliquo nostrum, fratres, sic conqueri contigerit viscera matris nostrae, timeo ne melius fuisset si conceptus non fuisset homo ille; nisi quod nec de talibus sinit nos desperare, qui etiam de lapidibus suscitat filios Abrahae (Luc. III, 8). Ipse in eis, si qui tales sunt, emolliat cor lapideum, ne quatiant viscera matris; ipse viscera matris consoletur, ne fatigetur eos, qualescunque sint, portare, donec formetur in eis Christus, qui perfectus Deus, perfectus homo vivit et regnat, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Let Jesus, therefore, conceived and carried in the womb, be an example to you in this: that just as that burden, light and sweet, though it filled Mary's womb with pregnancy, did not weigh it down, so may the womb of the Church not feel you to be heavy or troublesome. The Church, O brothers, is pregnant \u2014 not, like Mary, with Jesus alone, but, like Rebecca, with Jacob and Esau \u2014 that is, not only with the good and well-behaved, but also with the unruly and undisciplined; yet even these, for the sake of the name of Jesus, or perhaps some small beginning of his substance in them, the womb of the Church receives and embraces. But when the quarreling little ones struggled against each other in the womb of Rebecca \u2014 she who had earlier prayed that she might conceive \u2014 grieving in her womb over the trouble and pain caused by these evil ones, she almost regretted that she had conceived, saying: 'If it was going to be like this for me, what need was there to conceive?' (Gen. XXV, 22.). If it should happen, brothers, that the womb of our mother thus complains concerning any one of us, I fear it would have been better if that man had not been conceived \u2014 except that he who raises up children of Abraham even from stones (Luc. III, 8) does not allow us to despair even over such as these. May he himself soften the stony heart in them, if any such there be, lest they shake the womb of their mother; may he himself console the womb of the mother, lest she grow weary of carrying them, whatever they may be, until Christ be formed in them \u2014 who, perfect God, perfect man, lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The words on the Lord choosing his resting place come from Psalm 131:14 in historical numbering; the edition prints Psal. XXXI, 14."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Gabriel's words that no word is impossible with God come from Luke 1:37; the edition prints Luc. I, 30, 31."}, {"anchor": "guerric-annunciation-03-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The call for the peoples to praise God and the words on the earth yielding its fruit come from Psalm 66:6-7 in historical numbering; the edition prints Psal. LXVI, 6."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Annunciation Sermon III", "topics": ["Annunciation", "Incarnation", "Virgin Mary", "prophecy", "Passion", "silence", "Scripture", "Church", "patience"], "url": "sermons/guerric-annunciation-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:viri-galilaei", "calendar_label": "The Ascension of the Lord", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-ascension", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In die ascensionis Domini, Sermo, De amore Christi in nos et de nostro in ipsum, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 153-156.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Ascension", "Ascension of the Lord", "John 17:11, 12", "John 13:1 (printed Id.)", "John 17:11-13, 15, 24 (printed scope)", "Psalm 32:9 (historical numbering)", "John 17:24, 20 (printed order)", "Philippians 1:29", "Matthew 24:28 and Luke 17:37 (combined printed locator; closing parenthesis absent)", "Deuteronomy 32:14 (printed locator retained)", "1 Thessalonians 4:16 (printed locator retained)", "Psalm 17:11 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 12:2", "Psalm 72:25 (historical numbering; closing parenthesis present)"], "occasion": "In die ascensionis Domini", "occasion_detail": "Ascension of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Ascension", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny applies Christ's prayer before the Passion to the Ascension, reading his departure through the love shown to his disciples. The Eucharist offers a new manner of remaining with and in them, while the washing of their feet teaches humility and forgiveness. Christ's prayer promises protection from evil, sanctification in truth and glory with him, without excusing negligence. Guerric urges his brothers to practise the daily struggle against vice: like young eagles learning to fly, they must lift their hearts after the ascended Christ. The sermon ends with contemplation, love and the liturgical exchange Lift up your hearts / We have them lifted to the Lord, directing desire toward Christ as heavenly treasure. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138-1157 and is read here from the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition. The source names the day of the Lord\u2019s Ascension."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN DIE ASCENSIONIS DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE DAY OF THE LORD'S ASCENSION.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De amore Christi in nos et de nostro in ipsum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On Christ's love for us and our love for him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Pater, cum essem cum eis, ego servabam eos in nomine tuo (Joan. xvii, 11, 12). Haec oratio Domini habita est ante diem passionis. Non absurde tamen aptatur diei Ascensionis, quando scilicet supremo discessurus erat a filiolis suis, quos Patri commendabat. Qui enim in coelis creavit, docet et regit multitudinem Angelorum, in terris aggregaverat sibi pusillum gregem discipulorum sub praesentia suae carnis erudiendum, quoadusque grandiusculi sensus effecti, disciplinae Spiritus fierent idonei. Magnus itaque parvulos magna dilectione diligebat, quippe quos ab more mundi abstraxerat, et relicta omni spe saeculi, de se solo pendere videbat. Quandiu tamen cum eis corporaliter conversari voluit, non facile aut multum hunc suum eis affectum prodidit; maturiorem se eis, quam teneriorem exhibens, sicut magistrum decebat et patrem. Cum autem tempus quo ab eis recessurus erat instaret, tunc veluti vinci tenero eorum affectu visus est, ut magnam multitudinem dulcedinis suae, quam eis absconderat, dissimulare non posset. Hinc illud est, quod cum dilexisset suos qui erant in mundo, in finem dilexit eos (Id. xiii, 1). Tunc enim propemodum omnem vim amoris effudit amicis, antequam etiam ipse sicut aqua effunderetur pro inimicis. Tunc eis sacramentum Corporis et Sanguinis sui tradidit, et celebrandum instituit, nescio virtute an charitate mirabiliori, hoc novum genus mansionis adinveniens in consolationem recessus sui, ut si discederet ab eis specie corporis, maneret non solum cum eis, sed etiam in eis virtute Sacramenti. Tunc veluti prorsus suae oblitus majestatis, et velut injuriam faciens sibi, nisi quia gloria est charitatis humiliare se pro amicis, dignatione ineffabili Dominus, et talis Dominus, pedes servorum lavit: uno opere condens eis et humilitatis exemplum, et remissionis sacramentum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Father, when I was with them, I kept them in your name (Joan. xvii, 11, 12). This prayer of the Lord was offered before the day of his Passion. Yet it is fittingly applied to the day of the Ascension, when he was about to leave his little children for the last time and was commending them to the Father. For he who in heaven created, teaches and rules the multitude of Angels had gathered for himself on earth a little flock of disciples, to be instructed in the presence of his flesh until their understanding had grown somewhat and they became fit for the teaching of the Spirit. Thus the great one loved the little ones with great love: he had drawn them away from the ways of the world and saw them depending on him alone, having abandoned every worldly hope. Yet as long as he chose to live bodily among them, he did not readily or fully reveal this affection of his to them, showing himself more reserved than tender, as befitted a teacher and father. But when the time was drawing near for him to depart from them, he seemed overcome by tender affection for them, so that he could no longer conceal the great abundance of his sweetness that he had hidden from them. Hence the saying that, having loved his own who were in the world, he loved them to the end (Id. xiii, 1). For then he poured out almost the whole force of his love upon his friends, before he himself was also poured out like water for his enemies. Then he handed on to them the sacrament of his Body and Blood and instituted its celebration, devising this new manner of remaining to console them for his departure, with power or charity more wonderful, I know not which. Thus, though he departed from them in bodily appearance, he would remain not only with them but also in them through the power of the Sacrament. Then, as though utterly forgetful of his own majesty and as though doing himself an indignity, except that it is the glory of charity to humble oneself for friends, the Lord, and such a Lord, with ineffable condescension washed his servants' feet, establishing for them in a single act both an example of humility and a sacrament of forgiveness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Tunc denique post exhortationem prolixioris sermonis Patri eos commendans, sublevatis in coelum oculis, inter caetera sic ait, Pater, cum essem cum eis, ego servabam eos in nomine tuo, et nemo ex his periit, nisi filius perditionis. Nunc autem ad te venio: serva eos in nomine tuo quos dedisti mihi. Non rogo ut tollas eos de mundo, sed ut serves eos a malo; et caetera, quae non est hujus temporis retexere, multo minus exponere. Summa tamen orationis, sicut indicat textus lectionis, est in his tribus, in quibus est et summa salutis, imo et perfectionis, ut nihil possit addi, scilicet, ut a malo serventur, ut in veritate sanctificentur, ut cum ipso glorificentur. Pater, inquit, quos dedisti mihi, volo ut ubi sum ego, et ipsi sint mecum, ut videant claritatem meam (Joan. xvii, 11-13, 15, 24). O felices, quorum advocatus ipse judex est; pro quibus orat qui pari honore cum eo quem orat adorandus est! Voluntate labiorum ejus non fraudabit eum Pater, cum quo est ei una voluntas et una potestas, quia unus Deus est. Omnino quod Christus orat, impleri necesse est: cujus sermo virtus est, et voluntas effectus; qui de omnibus quae sunt, dixit, et facta sunt, mandavit, et creata sunt (Psal. xxxii, 9). Volo, inquit, ut ubi ego sum, et ipsi sint mecum. O quanta securitas fidelium! quanta fiducia credentium! tantum non abjiciant gratiam quam acceperunt. Non enim solis Apostolis proposita est haec securitas, vel condiscipulis eorum, sed omnibus qui per eorum verbum credituri sunt in Dei verbum. Non pro eis, inquit, rogo tantum, sed pro eis qui credituri sunt per verbum eorum in me (Joan. xvii, 24, 20)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Then, finally, after the exhortation of a lengthy discourse, commending them to the Father and lifting his eyes to heaven, he said among other things: Father, when I was with them, I kept them in your name, and none of them perished except the son of perdition. But now I come to you: keep in your name those whom you have given me. I do not ask that you take them out of the world, but that you keep them from evil; and the rest, which this is not the time to repeat, much less explain. Yet the substance of the prayer, as the text of the reading shows, lies in these three things, in which also lies the fullness of salvation, indeed of perfection, so that nothing can be added: that they be kept from evil, that they be sanctified in truth, and that they be glorified with him. Father, he says, those whom you have given me, I will that where I am, they too may be with me, so that they may see my glory (Joan. xvii, 11-13, 15, 24). O happy people, whose advocate is himself their judge, for whom he prays who is to be adored with equal honor alongside him to whom he prays! The Father will not deny him the desire of his lips: with the Father he has one will and one power, because they are one God. What Christ prays for must assuredly be fulfilled: his word is power, and his will is accomplishment. Concerning all things that exist, he spoke and they were made; he commanded and they were created (Psal. xxxii, 9). I will, he says, that where I am, they too may be with me. O how great the security of the faithful! How great the confidence of believers! Only let them not cast away the grace they have received. For this security is offered not to the Apostles alone or to their fellow disciples, but to all who through their word will believe in the Word of God. I pray, he says, not for them alone, but for those who through their word will believe in me (Joan. xvii, 24, 20).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vobis autem fratres, donatum est, ut ait Apostolus, non solum ut in eum credatis, sed etiam ut patiamini pro eo (Philipp. i, 29): quos scilicet fides promissionis Christi, non securitate negligentiores, sed alacritate faciens ferventiores, et quotidiano vitiorum conflictu, martyrio coronat assiduos: assiduo, sed facili; facili, sed sublimi. Facile est, ubi supra virtutem nihil imperatur; sublime, ubi de omni virtute illius fortis armati triumphatur. An non facile suave Christi jugum portare? an non sublime in regno ejus excellere? Quid facilius, rogo, quam portare pennas quae portent portantem se? quid sublimius, quam supra omnem coelorum altitudinem, quo Christus ascendit, evolare? Plane sancti, quorum sicut aquilae juventus renovabitur, assument pennas sicut aquilae, et volabunt. Quo volabunt? Ubicunque, inquit, fuerit corpus, illuc congregabuntur et aquilae (Matth. xxiv, 28, et Luc. xvii, 37."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But to you, brothers, it has been granted, as the Apostle says, not only to believe in him but also to suffer for him (Philipp. i, 29). Faith in Christ's promise makes you more fervent through eagerness rather than more negligent through security, and crowns you, steadfast in the daily struggle against vices, with martyrdom: continual, yet easy; easy, yet sublime. It is easy where nothing beyond one's strength is commanded; sublime where the whole power of that strong armed one is overcome. Is it not easy to bear Christ's sweet yoke? Is it not sublime to excel in his kingdom? What is easier, I ask, than to carry wings that carry their bearer? What is more sublime than to fly above the whole height of the heavens, where Christ has ascended? Surely the saints, whose youth will be renewed like the eagle's, will take wings like eagles and fly. Where will they fly? Wherever the body is, he says, there the eagles too will gather (Matth. xxiv, 28, et Luc. xvii, 37.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Sed quid putamus, fratres, quomodo tunc repente de terris ad coelos evolare poterit, qui nunc exercitio et usu quotidiano volitare non didicerit? Si quaeris quo doctore, quo duce, nunquid non Christus sicut aquila, provocabat hodie ad volandum pullos suos, quando super eos volitabat, cum scilicet videntibus illis elevaretur, diuque sequerentur oculis euntem in coelum? Poterat utique repente in ictu oculi, ex oculis eorum rapi, et ubi vellet, constitui. Sed plane, sicut aquila provocans ad volandum pullos suos, et super eos volitans (Deut. xxxii, 14), et corda sursum levare post se nitebatur amore sui, et corpora similiter posse sublevari pollicebatur exemplo corporis sui: sicut Apostolus ait, aeterni conscius mysterii, quia et nos bajulis nubibus rapiemur obviam Christo (I Thess. iv, 16) redeunti. Ipse quidem ascendit super Cherubim, et volavit super pennas ventorum (Psal. xvii, 11), id est supergressus est virtutes Angelorum: tuae tamen condescendens infirmitati, expandet alas suas, et assumet te, atque portabit in humeris suis, si modo pullus degener non existas, ut a terra levari, et aura puriore perfrui non extimescas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But what do we think, brothers? How will someone suddenly be able then to fly from earth to heaven if he has not now learned to fly through daily practice and exercise? If you ask who is the teacher and who the guide, did not Christ, like an eagle, encourage his young to fly today when he flew above them, that is, when he was lifted up before their eyes and they followed him with their gaze for a long time as he went into heaven? He could certainly have been snatched from their sight suddenly, in the blink of an eye, and set wherever he wished. But indeed, like an eagle encouraging its young to fly and flying above them (Deut. xxxii, 14), he strove to lift their hearts up after him through love of himself and promised, by the example of his own body, that their bodies too could be lifted up. As the Apostle says, privy to the eternal mystery, we too, with clouds bearing us, shall be caught up to meet Christ (I Thess. iv, 16) as he returns. He himself indeed ascended upon Cherubim and flew upon the wings of the winds (Psal. xvii, 11), that is, he surpassed the powers of the Angels. Yet, stooping to your weakness, he will spread his wings, take you up and carry you on his shoulders, provided that you are not a degenerate fledgling, afraid to be lifted from the earth and enjoy a purer air.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Alii volant contemplando, tu saltem amando. Paulus mente excedit, et ad tertium usque pervolat coelum (II Cor. xii, 2); Joannes ad id quod id principio erat Verbum: tu saltem humi degenerem non trahas animum, nec patiaris obrutum inertia in terra putrescere cor tuum; sed clamanti, \u00ab Sursum corda, \u00bb Pontifici magno (qui hodie introivit in Sancta, aeterna redemptione inventa, ubi et assistit vultui Dei interpellans pro nobis) responde fideliter, \u00ab Habemus ad Dominum. \u00bb Sed et si aliquando quaesivisti non quae sursum sunt, sed quae super terram; jam te ipsum increpa, et dic Domino cum propheta: Quid enim mihi est in coelo? et a te quid volui super terram? (Psal. lxxii, 25) Heu, quam misere errabam! tam magnum est illud in coelo mihi repositum, quod contemnebam; tam nihil hoc super terram, quod tantopere expetebam! Christus itaque thesaurus tuus ascendit in coelum: ibi sit et cor tuum. Inde ducis originem, ibi habes Patrem et haereditatem, inde exspectas Salvatorem. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Others fly by contemplation; you, at least, by love. Paul passes beyond himself in mind and flies as far as the third heaven (II Cor. xii, 2); John to that Word who was in the beginning. You, at least, must not drag a debased spirit along the ground or let your heart, buried in sloth, rot in the earth. Rather, to the great High Priest crying, 'Lift up your hearts' (who today entered the holy place, having obtained eternal redemption, where he also stands before the face of God, interceding for us), answer faithfully, 'We have them lifted to the Lord.' And if at times you have sought not the things above but the things on earth, now rebuke yourself and say to the Lord with the prophet: For what have I in heaven? And apart from you, what have I desired upon earth? (Psal. lxxii, 25) Alas, how wretchedly I was mistaken! So great is that which was laid up for me in heaven and which I despised; so utterly nothing is this upon earth which I sought so eagerly! Christ, then, your treasure, has ascended into heaven: let your heart be there too. From there you draw your origin; there you have your Father and inheritance; from there you await the Savior. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition titles this simply \u201cSermon,\u201d without a number. Its ending is \u201cthe Savior. Amen.\u201d"}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition\u2019s Bible references and historical Psalm numbering are retained. The combined Matthew 24:28 / Luke 17:37 reference has no closing parenthesis in print."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Latin prints id principio where the context calls for in principio (\u201cin the beginning\u201d). The translation follows that sense. \u201cLift up your hearts\u201d and \u201cWe have them lifted to the Lord\u201d are the liturgical call and response."}, {"anchor": "guerric-ascension-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "\u201cFrom the ways of the world\u201d translates ab more mundi, not \u201cfrom love of the world.\u201d Christ\u2019s bodily departure is contrasted with his continuing sacramental presence in the disciples. The daily martyrdom described here is spiritual combat against vice, not bodily execution."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Ascension Sermon: Christ's Love for Us and Our Love for Him", "topics": ["Ascension", "love", "Eucharist", "humility", "prayer", "faith", "spiritual combat", "contemplation", "hope"], "url": "sermons/guerric-ascension.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-assumption-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In assumptione B. Mariae, Sermo I, Quod Maria duplici titulo sit mater, quodque recte dicatur thronus Dei, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 187-190.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Psalm 64:5", "Psalm 131:14", "Isaiah 62:4,5", "Genesis 3", "Galatians 4:19", "Isaiah 62:5", "Psalm 86:7", "Matthew 19:28", "Revelation 3:21", "Psalm 44:14,15", "Wisdom 4:1"], "occasion": "In assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This first sermon for the Assumption of Blessed Mary belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval liturgical collection, associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138-1157 and read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The individual delivery year is unknown. Beginning with an invitation from the Office of Virgins, Guerric presents Mary as mother both of Christ and, through him, of Christians, contrasting her with Eve and relating her to the Church. He develops the image of Mary as God's throne and ends with a prayer for her intercession. The Assumption is the source\u2019s historical occasion."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN ASSUMPTIONE B. MARIAE."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE ASSUMPTION OF BLESSED MARY.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quod Maria duplici titulo sit mater, quodque recte dicatur thronus Dei."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "That Mary is mother by a double title, and is rightly called the throne of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Veni, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum (11). Multi vocati, pauci vero electi. Beati quos elegisti, Domine; habitabunt in atriis tuis (Psal. lxiv, 5) : imo tu in eis habitabis, tu in eis regnabis, thronumque regni tui in eis collocabis. Sane omnium beatorum beatissima est Maria, quae de numero omnium electorum singulariter est electa et praeelecta : quoniam elegit eam Dominus, elegit eam in habitationem sibi, dicens, Hic requies mea in saeculum saeculi : hic habitabo quoniam elegi eam (Psal cxxxi, 14). Habitavit in ea novem mensibus, habitavit cum ea et sub ea annis pluribus. Habitans in ea replebat eam singulari gratia charismatum : habitans cum ea pascebat eam incomparabili piorum suavitate morum, et divinorum desiderabili sapientia sermonum. Nunc autem in ea et cum ea, sicut interminabili aevo, sic incomprehensibili modo habitans, satiat eam beatificantium gloria visionum; foris quidem glorificatae carnis formam exhibens, intus autem formam Verbi glorificantis imprimens. O Maria, non vocaberis, ait Dominus, ultra derelicta, et terra tua non vocabitur ultra desolata, ut quia virgo, ideo sis infecunda : sed vocaberis Voluntas mea, id est, dilectus Filius meus in ea, quia complacuit Domino in te, et terra tua inhabitabitur. Habitabit enim juvenis in virgine, et habitabit in te Filius tuus; imo, si magis placet, ut non recedamus a verbis Scripturae, habitabunt in te filii tui (Isa. lxii, 4, 5)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Come, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you (11). Many are called, but few are chosen. Blessed are those whom you have chosen, Lord; they will dwell in your courts (Psal. lxiv, 5) : rather, you will dwell in them, you will reign in them, and you will establish the throne of your kingdom in them. Certainly Mary is the most blessed of all the blessed, singled out and chosen before all among the whole number of the elect : for the Lord has chosen her, chosen her as a dwelling for himself, saying, This is my rest for ever and ever : here I will dwell, for I have chosen her (Psal cxxxi, 14). He dwelt in her for nine months; he dwelt with her and under her for many years. Dwelling in her, he filled her with a unique grace of spiritual gifts : dwelling with her, he nourished her with the incomparable sweetness of his holy ways and the desirable wisdom of his divine words. But now, dwelling in her and with her both for an endless age and in an incomprehensible manner, he satisfies her with the glory of visions that make her blessed; outwardly displaying the form of his glorified flesh, and inwardly impressing the form of the Word who glorifies her. O Mary, the Lord says, you will no longer be called forsaken, nor will your land any longer be called desolate, as though being a virgin meant that you must be barren : instead you will be called My Will, that is, my beloved Son is in her, because the Lord has taken pleasure in you, and your land will be inhabited. For the young man will dwell in the virgin, and your Son will dwell in you; or rather, if you prefer, so that we do not depart from the words of Scripture, your sons will dwell in you (Isa. lxii, 4, 5).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quid, haeretice, caput erigis? quid mysterium pietatis ad occasionem perfidiae rapis? Unum quidem genuit : qui sicut unicus est Patri in coelis, ita unicus est Matri in terris. Neque enim sicut tu impie blasphemas, alios postea filios genuit; sed intemeratum manet, sicut signaculum perpetuae virginitatis in matre, ita sacramentum catholicae unitatis in prole. Ipsa tamen unica virgo Mater, quae se Patris Unicum genuisse gloriatur, eumdem unicum suum in omnibus membris ejus amplectitur, omniumque in quibus Christum suum formatum agnoscit, vel formari cognoscit, matrem se vocari non confunditur. Eva vetus illa, non tam mater quam noverca, quae filiis ante propinavit praejudicium mortis, quam initium lucis. Dicta est quidem mater cunctorum viventium : sed inventa est verius interfectrix viventium, seu genitrix morientium; cum suum generare nihil aliud sit, quam mortem ingenerare (Gen. iii). Et quia illa non potuit fideliter interpretari nomen suum, ista implevit mysterium : quae et ipsa, sicut Ecclesia, cujus forma est, mater est omnium ad vitam renascentium. Mater siquidem est Vitae qua vivunt universi; quam dum ex se genuit, nimirum omnes ex ea victuri sunt, quodammodo regeneravit. Unus generabatur, sed nos omnes generabamur : quia videlicet secundum rationem seminis, quo regeneratio fit, jam tunc in illo omnes eramus. Sicut enim in Adam fuimus ab initio propter semen carnalis generationis; sic in Christo ante initium, propter semen spiritualis regenerationis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Why do you raise your head, heretic? Why do you seize the mystery of godliness as an occasion for unbelief? She bore one Son : who, just as he is the Father's only Son in heaven, is also his Mother's only Son on earth. For she did not afterwards bear other sons, as you impiously blaspheme; rather, just as the seal of perpetual virginity remains inviolate in the mother, so does the mystery of Catholic unity in her offspring. Yet this unique virgin Mother, who glories in having borne the Father's Only-begotten, embraces that same only Son of hers in all his members, and is not ashamed to be called the mother of all in whom she recognizes her Christ already formed or knows him to be taking form. That Eve of old was less a mother than a stepmother, who gave her children the sentence of death to drink before they first saw the light. She was indeed called the mother of all the living : but she proved more truly the slayer of the living, or the mother of the dying; for her giving birth means nothing other than bringing death into being (Gen. iii). And because she could not faithfully express the meaning of her name, this other mother fulfilled the mystery : she too, like the Church of which she is the figure, is mother of all who are born again to life. For she is the Mother of the Life by which all live; and in giving birth to that Life from herself, she in a certain manner gave rebirth to all who would live by it. One was being born, but we were all being born : for, in respect of the seed through which rebirth takes place, we were all already in him then. Just as we were in Adam from the beginning through the seed of bodily generation, so we were in Christ before the beginning through the seed of spiritual regeneration.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Porro beata Mater illa Christi, quia se matrem Christianorum cognoscit ratione mysterii; cura quoque se matrem eis praestat et affectu pietatis. Neque enim duratur ad filios, quasi non sint sui, cujus viscera semel quidem foeta, sed nunquam effoeta, nunquam desinunt fructum parturire pietatis. Benedictus siquidem fructus ventris tui, gravidam te, o pia Mater, inexhausta pietate reliquit; ex te quidem semel nascens, sed in te semper manens et affluens, et in horto concluso castitatis fontem signatum charitatis semper abundare faciens; qui licet signatus sit, foras tamen derivatur, et in plateis aquae ejus nobis dividuntur. Licet namque fons charitatis, proprius Ecclesiae, incommunicabilis sit alienis, beneficium tamen ejus impertire gaudet etiam inimicis. Denique si servus Christi filiolos suos iterum atque iterum parturit cura atque desiderio pietatis, donec formetur in eis Christus (Galat. iv, 19); quanto magis ipsa Mater Christi? Et Paulus quidem genuit eos, verbum veritatis quo regenerati sunt praedicando : Maria autem longe divinius atque sanctius, Verbum ipsum generando. Laudo quidem in Paulo praedicationis mysterium, sed plus miror ac veneror in Maria generationis mysterium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Moreover, because that blessed Mother of Christ knows that she is the mother of Christians by reason of the mystery, she also shows herself their mother by her care and loving devotion. She does not harden herself toward her children as though they were not her own : her womb, fruitful once but never exhausted, never ceases to bring forth the fruit of loving devotion. For the blessed fruit of your womb left you, O loving Mother, pregnant with inexhaustible devotion; born from you once, yet always remaining and overflowing in you, he makes the sealed fountain of charity abound continually within the enclosed garden of chastity. Although sealed, it is nevertheless channeled outward, and its waters are shared with us in the streets. For although the fountain of charity belongs to the Church and cannot be shared with those outside her, it gladly shares its benefit even with enemies. Indeed, if the servant of Christ labors in birth for his little children again and again, with devoted care and longing, until Christ is formed in them (Galat. iv, 19); how much more does the Mother of Christ herself? Paul indeed begot them by preaching the word of truth through which they were born again : but Mary did so in a far more divine and holy manner, by giving birth to the Word himself. I praise in Paul the mystery of preaching, but I admire and venerate still more in Mary the mystery of giving birth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Vide autem si non et filii Matrem videntur agnoscere, dictante utique ipsis veluti quadam naturali pietate fidei, ut ad invocationem nominis ejus, primo vel maxime refugiant in omnibus necessitatibus et periculis, tanquam parvuli ad sinum matris. Unde nimirum de his filiis puto non absurde intelligi, quod per prophetam promissum est ei. Habitabant in te filii tui (Isai. lxii, 5); salvo duntaxat intellectu, quo de Ecclesia haec prophetia principaliter accipitur. Et nunc siquidem habitamus in adjutorio Matris Altissimi, in protectione ipsius commoramur, tanquam sub umbra alarum ejus : et postmodum in consortio gloriae ipsius, tanquam in sinu ipsius confovebimur. Tunc erit vox una laetantium et aggratulantium Matri : Sicut laetantium omnium nostrum habitatio est in te (Psal. lxxxvi, 7), sancta Dei Genitrix. Nullatenus autem credideris majoris esse felicitatis et gloriae, habitare in sinu Abrahae, quam in sinu Mariae, cum thronum suum in ea posuerit Rex gloriae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. See, too, whether the children do not seem to recognize their Mother, prompted, as it were, by a certain natural devotion of faith to flee first or above all to the invocation of her name in every need and danger, like little children to their mother's bosom. Hence I think it is reasonable to understand of these children what was promised to her through the prophet. Your sons dwelt in you (Isai. lxii, 5); always preserving the interpretation according to which this prophecy is understood principally of the Church. Even now we dwell in the help of the Mother of the Most High; we remain under her protection, as though beneath the shadow of her wings : and afterwards, sharing her glory, we shall be cherished as though in her bosom. Then there will be one voice of those rejoicing with their Mother and congratulating her : The dwelling of all of us who rejoice is in you (Psal. lxxxvi, 7), holy Mother of God. Never believe that dwelling in Abraham's bosom is a greater happiness and glory than dwelling in Mary's bosom, since the King of glory has established his throne in her.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Veni, inquit, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum. Non poterat signantius aut elegantius describi praerogativa gloriae ejus, quam ut thronus Dei regnantis esse diceretur. Nulli siquidem animae tanta plenitudine aut familiaritate copiam sui divina majestas videtur indulgere, sicut illi in qua specialiter prae caeteris residere delegerit. Discipulis quidem Dominus loquebatur, qui pauperes facti pauperem sequebantur : In regeneratione, cum sederit Filius hominis in sede majestatis suae, sedebitis et vos super sedes (Matth. xix, 28). Alibi quoque promittit idem Agonotheta noster, de coelo spectans et incitans decertantes : Qui vicerit, inquit, dabo ei sedere mecum in throno meo, sicut et ego vici et sedi in throno Patris mei (Apoc. iii, 21). Matri vero, cujus longe differens est meritum, nihilominus differens promittit praemium. Veni, inquit, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum. Parum est, inquit, ut judicanti consedeas, nisi et ipsa mihi sedes fias : ut majestatem regnantis eo felicius quo familiarius in te contineas, et specialius prae caeteris incomprehensibilem comprehendas. Continuisti parvulum in gremio : continebis immensum in animo. Fuisti diversorium peregrinantis; eris palatium regnantis. Fuisti tabernaculum pugnaturi in mundo; eris solium triumphantis in coelo. Fuisti thalamus Sponsi incarnati; eris thronus Regis coronati."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Come, he says, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you. The prerogative of her glory could not be described more expressively or beautifully than by calling her the throne of God who reigns. For the divine majesty seems to grant no soul such full or intimate possession of himself as the soul in which he has chosen to dwell especially above all others. The Lord indeed spoke to the disciples who had become poor and followed him in his poverty : In the regeneration, when the Son of man sits on the seat of his majesty, you too will sit upon seats (Matth. xix, 28). Elsewhere our same Master of the contest, watching from heaven and urging on those who struggle, promises : To him who conquers, he says, I will grant to sit with me on my throne, just as I too conquered and sat on the throne of my Father (Apoc. iii, 21). But to his Mother, whose merit is far different, he promises a reward no less different. Come, he says, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you. It is little, he says, for you to sit beside me as I judge, unless you yourself become my seat : so that you may contain within yourself the majesty of him who reigns, the more blessedly for being the more intimately, and may embrace the incomprehensible more particularly than all others. You held the little child in your lap : you will hold the boundless one in your soul. You were the lodging of the traveler; you will be the palace of him who reigns. You were the tent of him who was to fight in the world; you will be the throne of him who triumphs in heaven. You were the bridal chamber of the incarnate Bridegroom; you will be the throne of the crowned King.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. O Fili Dei, nihil tibi, nihil in illo tuo displicuit hospitio quod tam libenter repetit, tamque affluenter remunerat tua dignatio. Nihil utique in illo sordidum offendisti, quia nulla erat libido, sed purissima castitas : nihil ruinosum, quia nulla erat superbia, sed fundatissima humilitas : nihil obscurum, quia exclusa infidelitas : nihil angustum, quia diffusa erat charitas. Adornaverat thalamum Virgo prudentissima, te regem Christum non solum hospitem susceptura; sed et sponsum habitura. Adornaverat, inquam, multiplici decore virtutum et gloria, tanto fortasse locupletius, quanto interius totum erat. Hunc ornatum mirabatur qui dicebat, Omnis gloria ejus filiae regis ab intus, in fimbriis aureis, circumamicta varietate (Psal. xliv, 14, 15) : et alius, O quam pulchra est casta generatio cum charitate ! (Sap. iv, 1.) Domum tuam decet Domine, hujusmodi sanctitudo et decor. Is decor te invitavit ut intrares : illexit, ut redires. Intrans benedictionis gratiam multiplicasti : sed rediens cumulasti. Cum intrasti, homo natus es in ea : cum redisti, Deus glorificatus es in ea. Tunc tibi posuisti in ea sacrarium gratiae; nunc autem, thronum gloriae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. O Son of God, nothing displeased you, nothing in that lodging of yours which your graciousness so gladly revisits and so abundantly rewards. Certainly you found nothing defiled there, for there was no lust, but the purest chastity : nothing ruinous, for there was no pride, but humility most firmly founded : nothing dark, for unbelief was excluded : nothing cramped, for charity was poured abroad. The most prudent Virgin had adorned the bridal chamber, ready to receive you, Christ the King, as her guest, and also to have you as her bridegroom. She had adorned it, I say, with the manifold beauty and glory of virtues, perhaps all the more richly because it was wholly inward. He admired this adornment who said, All the glory of her, the king's daughter, is within, in golden fringes, clothed about with variety (Psal. xliv, 14, 15) : and another said, O how beautiful is a chaste generation with charity ! (Sap. iv, 1.) Such holiness and beauty befit your house, Lord. That beauty invited you to enter : it drew you to return. By entering you multiplied the grace of blessing : but by returning you heaped it up. When you entered, you were born in her as man : when you returned, you were glorified in her as God. Then you established in her a sanctuary of grace for yourself; but now, a throne of glory.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Dicuntur quidem et sunt alii throni, quidam scilicet spiritus divini, prae aliis, ut putamus, subjectis ordinibus, Dei praesidentis majestate pleni : quorum quae sit praerogativa, Scriptura non exprimit, licet innuat ex vocabulo quod nonnulla sit. Sed et anima cujuslibet justi dicitur sedes sapientiae. Quae autem nunc es sedes sapientiae, profecto tunc erit et gloriae. Sit igitur illud coeli palatium plenum sedibus et thronis, sedeatque Deus in omnibus, accommodans se et aptans cuique pro meritis. Non immerito tamen creditur, sine injuria vel invidia caeterarum sedium quoddam esse speciale Regis solium excelsum et elevatum super gloriam omnium : Mariam dico exaltatam super choros Angelorum, ut nihil contempletur supra se Mater nisi Filium solum; nihil miretur supra se Regina nisi Regem solum; nihil veneretur supra se Mediatrix nostra nisi Mediatorem solum : cui nos ipsa precibus suis conciliet, commendet, et repraesentet unico Filio suo Jesu Christo, cui est honor et gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. There are indeed others who are called thrones and truly are thrones : certain divine spirits, filled with the majesty of God who presides, beyond the other subordinate orders, as we suppose. Scripture does not express what their prerogative is, although their name suggests that they have some prerogative. The soul of every just person is also called a seat of wisdom. But you who are now a seat of wisdom will assuredly then be a seat of glory as well. Let that heavenly palace, then, be full of seats and thrones, and let God sit in all, accommodating and adapting himself to each according to their merits. Yet it is reasonably believed, without wrong or envy toward the other seats, that there is one particular royal throne, lofty and raised above the glory of them all : I mean Mary exalted above the choirs of Angels, so that the Mother contemplates above herself only the Son; the Queen admires above herself only the King; our Mediatress venerates above herself only the Mediator. May she by her prayers reconcile us to him, commend us to him, and present us to her only Son Jesus Christ, to whom belong honor and glory for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-n011", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(11) In Offic. de Virginibus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-n011", "label": "English", "text": "(11) In the Office of Virgins.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The damaged source footnote number reads (11); it is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon follows historical Psalm numbering. In Isaiah 62:5 it first reads \u201cyour sons will dwell\u201d and later \u201cyour sons dwelt\u201d; both forms follow the edition. Genesis 3 is cited without a verse."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Mary\u2019s motherhood and throne, the contrast with Eve, and the distinction between Mediatress and Mediator are Guerric\u2019s own imagery. \u201cMystery of Catholic unity\u201d translates sacramentum catholicae unitatis without imposing a later sacramental classification. \u201cMaster of the contest\u201d renders agonotheta. In section 2 the translation follows the sense of a grammatically irregular Latin clause. In section 7 a damaged final letter may be the t of est (\u201cis\u201d); the Latin retains the visible es, while the English gives the contextual sense. The phrase about those rejoicing means \u201cone voice of those rejoicing.\u201d"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon I: Mary's Motherhood and the Throne of God", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "Church", "motherhood", "virginity", "charity", "glory", "contemplation", "hope"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-assumption-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De mutuo amore Jesu et Mariae., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 190-193.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Song of Songs 5:8", "Matthew 9:20\u201322", "Mark 5:25\u201334", "Isaiah 53:4", "Matthew 8:17", "Sirach 24:29", "John 6:35", "Luke 8:43\u201348"], "occasion": "In Assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the bride's words, \"tell my beloved that I languish with love,\" as Mary's on her deathbed. Using a licence he credits to Jerome, he imagines the angels visiting her: she has languished with fear and with sorrow, and now with love. She declines Gabriel's attendance, content with the disciple Jesus loved, and asks for the kiss of her Son's mouth, trusting that his glory has not changed his gentleness. The sermon appears under the heading for the Assumption of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mutuo amore Jesu et Mariae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the mutual love of Jesus and Mary.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Filiae Jerusalem, nuntiate dilecto quia amore langueo (Cantic. V, 8). Verba ista quae a nobis hac nocte cantata sunt, qualiter ad Assumptionem beatae Mariae possint videri pertinere, volumus, si placet, cum vestra charitate tractare. Id autem eo genere sermonis faciendum videtur, quo non modo saecularium, sed etiam ecclesiasticarum auctores litterarum nonnunquam usi sunt, maxime in tractandis Canticis, unde haec sunt sumpta. Hoc namque genus sermonis, salva veritate, quadam ampliori utitur libertate, cum videlicet sumpto themate causae, ut beatus Hieronymus ait, non tam loquitur quae revera facta vel dicta sint, quam ejusmodi fuisse negotium ostendit, ut ea quae dicuntur, etsi facta vel dicta non fuerint, fieri tamen et dici potuisse, aut etiam in affectu facientis, vel dicentis fuisse non absurde credi possint. Maria igitur migratura corpore decumbebat, ut est humana infirmitas. Porro filiae Jerusalem quae sursum est, id est angelicae Virtutes, scientes nimirum quia obsequiis Matris captanda est gratia Filii, visitabant satis officiose Dominam suam, Matrem Domini sui. Et fortasse aliquid hujusmodi primo post salutationis officia locuti sunt ei Angeli; sicut speciem humano vultui, ita sermonem humano nimirum conformantes affectui vel usui:"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Daughters of Jerusalem, tell my beloved that I languish with love\" (Cantic. V, 8). We wish, if it please you, to treat with your charity how these words, which have been sung by us this night, may be seen to pertain to the Assumption of blessed Mary. This must be done, it seems, in that kind of discourse which not only the authors of secular letters but also of ecclesiastical letters have sometimes employed, especially in treating the Canticle, from which these words are taken. For this kind of discourse, while preserving the truth, makes use of a certain ampler liberty, since, having taken up the theme of the matter, as blessed Jerome says, it does not so much speak of what was actually done or said, as show that the business was of such a kind that the things which are said, even if they were not done or said, could nevertheless have been done and said, or could even, not absurdly, be believed to have been in the affection of the one doing or speaking. Mary, therefore, about to depart in body, lay abed, as is human weakness. Moreover the daughters of the Jerusalem that is above, that is, the angelic Virtues, knowing well that favor with the Son is to be sought through attentions paid to the Mother, were visiting their Lady, the Mother of their Lord, with due dutifulness. And perhaps the Angels said something of this sort to her first, after the courtesies of greeting; conforming their appearance to a human countenance, and so their speech to human affection or custom:", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quid est, obsecro, mi Domina, quod sic aegra videris et languida? quid est quod praeter solitum tristis et pigra, ab heri et nudiustertius, sicut solebas, non sancta revisis loca, quorum contemplatione amorem tuum pascebas? Jam per aliquot dies non vidimus te, aut rupem Calvariae ascendentem, ut ibi locum crucis impleres lacrymis; aut ad sepulcrum Filii adorantem gloriam resurrectionis, aut in monte Oliveti deosculantem extrema vestigia ascendentis. --Ob hoc ipsum siquidem creditur in valle Josaphat commorata (ubi et sepulchrum ejus monstratur, ut ait Beatus Hieronymus, in ecclesia miro lapideo fabricata tabulatu), ne scilicet a sanctis locis longius recederet, sed saepius ea invisens, licet omnia memoria teneret, formam tamen gestorum, velut corporaliter in ipsis effigiatam locis, dulcius amplecteretur, ut vel sic suum aliquatenus amorem solaretur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. \"What is it, I beg, my Lady, that you seem thus sick and languishing? What is it that, contrary to your custom, you have been sad and sluggish since yesterday and the day before, and have not, as you were wont, revisited the holy places, by contemplation of which you used to nourish your love? For some days now we have not seen you either ascending the rock of Calvary, there to fill the place of the cross with your tears; or adoring the glory of the resurrection at the Son's tomb; or on the Mount of Olives kissing the last footprints of him who ascended.\" --For this very reason indeed she is believed to have dwelt in the valley of Josaphat (where also her tomb is shown, as blessed Jerome says, in a church wondrously built with a stone roof), namely so that she might not withdraw too far from the holy places, but by visiting them more often, although she held everything in memory, might the more sweetly embrace the form of the deeds, as it were bodily figured in those very places, so that even thus she might in some measure console her love.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Causam itaque percunctantibus illis, cur ab his modo vacaret, et ex toto decumberet: Langueo, inquit. Quare langues? Languor enim quem locum in corpore tuo reperit, in quo salus mundi tam diu habitavit? De corpore Filii tui virtus exibat et sanabat omnes, adeo ut etiam fimbria vestimenti haemorrhoissam sanaret (Matth. IX, 20-22; Marc V, 25-34 et Luc. VIII.43-48): et tu quae tamdiu ipsum in utero, in sinu, in gremio continuisti, ulli infirmitati, aut languori vel accessibilis esse potuisti? Non est, inquit, quod id miremini, si illud ipsum corpus Filii mei, quale aliquando fuerit recordemini. Et ipsum enim quam infirmum, quantisve defectibus subditum fuerit, voluntate tamen, ego novi quae ipsum in utero alui, uberibus lactavi, sinu fovi: nec solius infantiae, sed et sequentium defectus aetatum vidi, et eis, ut potui, ministravi: ad extremum non sine passione mea, ludibria et supplicia passionis et crucis aspexi, per singula discens quam vere de eo noster Isaias dixerit, Vere languores nostros ipse tulit, et dolores nostros ipse portavit (Isai. LIII, 4; Matth. VIII, 17). Cur ergo doleam non eum meo dedisse corpori, quod suo non dedit? Non sum ita delicata aut superba, ut quod ille dignatus est pati, ego quantulacunque ex parte pati non possim aut nolim. Et ille quidem misericordi voluntate, ego naturali necessitate. Sane aliud est sanitas, aliud est sanctitas. Sanctitatem dedit corpori meo, concepti sui corporis sacramento: sanitatem se daturum promisit, suscitati corporis exemplo. Denique, ut minus miremini languorem meum, amore langueo. Langueo plus impatientia amoris, quam passione doloris: plus vulnerata charitate, quam gravata infirmitate."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. When, therefore, they inquired the cause of why she was now free from these things, and lying abed altogether, she said: \"I languish.\" \"Why do you languish? For what place has languor found in your body, in which the salvation of the world dwelt for so long? Power went forth from the body of your Son and healed all, so much so that even the hem of his garment healed the woman with the flow of blood (Matth. IX, 20-22; Marc V, 25-34 et Luc. VIII.43-48): and you, who for so long held him in your womb, in your bosom, in your lap, could you be accessible to any infirmity or languor?\" \"There is no need,\" she said, \"for you to marvel at this, if you remember what that body of my Son was once like. For how weak it was, and to how many defects it was subject, by his own will, I know, who nourished him in the womb, suckled him at my breasts, cherished him in my lap: and I saw the defects not of infancy alone but of the ages that followed, and I ministered to him as I was able: and at the last, not without my own suffering, I beheld the mockeries and torments of the passion and the cross, learning from each thing how truly our Isaiah said of him, 'Surely he has borne our languors, and carried our sorrows' (Isai. LIII, 4; Matth. VIII, 17). Why then should I grieve that he did not give to my body what he did not give to his own? I am not so delicate or proud that I cannot or will not suffer, to whatever small degree, what he deigned to suffer. He indeed suffered by merciful will, I by natural necessity. Yet health is one thing, holiness another. He gave holiness to my body, by the sacrament of his own body conceived; health he promised he would give, by the example of his body raised up. In short, that you may marvel less at my languor, I languish with love. I languish more from the impatience of love than from the suffering of pain: more wounded by charity than weighed down by infirmity.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Heu! inquiunt illi, quam frequentes, imo continuae causae languoris! Bone Jesu, quomodo haec Mater tua postquam te genuit, nunquam fere nisi in languore fuit? Primo languit timore; postea, dolore; nunc, amore. Timore, a nativitate usque ad passionem, cum semper vitam Filii insidiis appeti videret: dolore, toto tempore passionis, donec redivivum reciperet: nunc amore et desiderio felicius, sed mirabilius cruciatur, quia sedentem in coelo non tenet. Quomodo, Jesu bone, qui summi fructus es gaudii, tam longi factus es illi causa martyrii, ut charissimam tibi ipsius animam, tot et tam acuti sine cessatione pertranseant gladii? Sed obsecramus te, Domina, quid vis faciamus tibi? Vis saltem ut resideat hic iste Gabriel symmystes tuus, ut assideat et ministret tibi, qui ab initio conscius et minister mysterii tui, custos quoque tui cubiculi meruit deputari? Non opus est, inquit Virgo. Sufficit mihi meus novus in carne angelus, discipulum dico quem diligebat Jesus, cujus me dilectionis haeredem reliquit, cum illum mihi, et me illi in cruce commendavit: cujus obsequio nihil mihi gratius, quia conversatione et affectu nihil castius, moribus nihil suavius, nihil sincerius fide, nihil sanctius sermone. Nos ergo, aiunt illi, in quo tibi esse poterimus officiosi? Filiae, inquit, Jerusalem, nuntiate dilecto quia amore langueo. Ipse novit quomodo meo medendum sit languori."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. \"Alas,\" they say, \"how frequent \u2014 nay, how continual \u2014 are the causes of your languishing! Good Jesus, how is it that this Mother of yours, after she bore you, has scarcely ever been free from languor? First she languished with fear; afterward, with sorrow; now, with love. With fear, from the nativity even to the passion, since she always saw the life of her Son beset by snares; with sorrow, throughout the whole time of the passion, until she received him alive again; now she is tormented more happily, but more wondrously, by love and longing, since she does not hold him as he sits in heaven. How is it, good Jesus, that you, who are the fruit of highest joy, became for her the cause of so long a martyrdom, that so many and so sharp swords should pass, without ceasing, through that soul of hers so dear to you?\" \"But we beseech you, Lady, what do you wish us to do for you? Do you wish at least that this Gabriel, your companion in the mystery, remain here, to sit by you and minister to you \u2014 he who from the beginning was made privy to, and minister of, your mystery, and deserved also to be appointed guardian of your chamber?\" \"There is no need,\" says the Virgin. \"My new angel in the flesh suffices me \u2014 I mean the disciple whom Jesus loved, whose heir in love he left me to be, when on the cross he commended him to me, and me to him: nothing is more pleasing to me than his service, since in conversation and affection nothing is more chaste, in manner nothing more gentle, in faith nothing more sincere, in speech nothing more holy.\" \"What, then,\" they say, \"can we do to be of service to you?\" \"Daughters of Jerusalem,\" she says, \"tell the beloved that I languish with love. He himself knows how my languor must be healed.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. At nosti, inquiunt, quia cum sciat omnia, de multis quasi nescius interrogat. Si ergo quaesierit, quid illud sit quod tuo vulneri velis adhiberi, quid respondebimus ei? Sodales, inquit, estis sponsi, Gabriel iste meus paranymphus: non puto vobis esse celandum amoris mysterium. Dicam igitur: tantum ne judicer temeraria, quod majora me quaerere videar. Osculetur me osculo oris sui. Si quid mihi conscia essem, contenta essem cum Maria Magdalene osculo pedum, ubi indulgentia sumitur delictorum. Sed quia in omni vita mea non me reprehendit cor meum, non puto me arroganter flagitare de osculo oris, gratiam gaudiorum. Cur autem arrogans videar, si illud os mihi reposco, quod de me sibi formavit ipse conditus et conditor? Cum parvulum tenebam inter brachia, quoties osculari speciosum forma prae filiis hominum mihi libebat, satis licebat: nunquam faciem avertebat, nunquam matrem repellebat. Et si fortassis impatientia desiderii eram nimia; ipse tamen more suo, morem matri gerebat. Gaudebat eam replere gratia, quae diffusa erat in labiis ejus, et dulcedine qua totus erat plenus, totus concupiscentia et desiderium, castarum scilicet animarum. Unde sicut ipse de se confitetur, Qui edunt me adhuc esurient, et qui bibunt me adhuc sitient (Eccli. XXIV, 29, et Joan. VI, 35): quanto gratiam oris illius gustavi suavius, tanto nimirum repeto nunc ardentius. Crevit quidem gloria et majestate: sed nihil ab illa ingenita mutatus est mansuetudine et bonitate. Nihil ad illum illud superbiae saecularis elogium: \u201cHonores mutant mores.\u201d Sublimior est, sed non superbior; gloriosior, sed non dedignantior. Non fastidiet matrem quam elegit, nec aeternam electionem suam novo judicio reprobabit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. \"But you know,\" they say, \"that although he knows all things, he asks about many things as though ignorant. If, then, he should ask what it is you wish applied to your wound, what shall we answer him?\" \"You are companions of the bridegroom,\" she says, \"and this Gabriel of mine is his paranymph: I do not think the mystery of love should be hidden from you. I will tell it, then \u2014 only let me not be judged presumptuous for seeming to seek things greater than myself. Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth. If I were conscious of any guilt in myself, I would be content, like Mary Magdalene, with the kiss of the feet, where pardon of sins is received. But because, throughout my whole life, my heart does not reproach me, I do not think that in demanding the kiss of the mouth I am arrogantly asking for the grace of joys. But why should I seem arrogant, if I ask back for myself that mouth which he, both the one fashioned and the fashioner, formed for himself out of me? When I held him as a little one between my arms, as often as it pleased me to kiss him who was beautiful in form above the sons of men, it was granted me freely enough: he never turned away his face, never repelled his mother. And if perhaps I was too impatient in my longing, he nevertheless, after his own manner, indulged his mother's way. He rejoiced to fill her with the grace that was poured out upon his lips, and with the sweetness of which he was wholly full \u2014 wholly the desire and longing, namely, of chaste souls. Whence, as he himself confesses of himself, 'They who eat me shall still hunger, and they who drink me shall still thirst' (Eccli. XXIV, 29, et Joan. VI, 35): the more sweetly I have tasted the grace of his mouth, the more ardently, surely, do I now seek it again. He has indeed grown in glory and majesty; but he has in no way been changed from that innate gentleness and goodness. To him applies nothing of that maxim of worldly pride: 'Honors change manners.' He is more exalted, but not more proud; more glorious, but not more disdainful. He will not disdain the mother whom he chose, nor by some new judgment reject his eternal election.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6.\n\nNe timeas, Maria, ait Gabriel more suo: invenisti enim gratiam apud Deum. Etiam in caeteris qui longe jacent tuis sub vestigiis, probare consuevit improbitatem hujus affectus et precis; qui nihil nimium, aut injuriosum sibi deputat, quod in eum vera duntaxat charitas praesumat. Et conversus ad sociam multitudinem: Eamus, inquit, eamus ne ipsi Filio facere videamur injuriam, si gloriam Matris differamus. Cum ergo reversi nuntiassent haec Domino suo, quid putamus Jesum nisi tale aliquid locutum? Ego sum qui patrem et matrem filiis honorandos commendavi; ego ut facerem quod docui, et exemplo essem aliis, ut Patrem honorarem, in terram descendi: nihilominus, ut Matrem honorarem, in coelum reascendi. Ascendi et praeparavi ei locum, thronum gloriae; ut a dextris Regis Regina coronata consedeat in vestitu deaurato, circumdata varietate. Neque hoc dico, quod in parte thronus ei collocetur: quin potius ipsa erit thronus meus. Veni igitur, electa mea, et ponam in te thronum meum. In te mihi quamdam regni sedem constituam, de te judicia decernam, per te preces exaudiam. Nullus mihi plus ministravit in humilitate mea. Communicasti mihi praeter alia quod homo sum: communicabo tibi quod Deus sum. Flagitabas osculum oris, quinimo tota de toto osculaberis. Non imprimam labia labiis, sed spiritum spiritui osculo perpetuo et indissolubili: quia concupivi speciem tuam, etiam desiderantius quam tu meam; nec satis glorificatus videbor mihi, donec tu conglorificeris. Gloria tibi, Domine Jesu, subintulit chorus Angelorum. Gloria tibi, Domine Jesu, ingeminet coetus fidelium. Glorificatio Matris tibi proficiat ad gloriam, nobis ad veniam; te praestante cui est honor, et gloria, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6.\n\n\"Do not fear, Mary,\" says Gabriel, in his accustomed way, \"for you have found grace with God.\" Even in others who lie far beneath your footsteps, he is accustomed to show that this boldness of affection and petition is no fault; for he counts nothing excessive or injurious to himself which true charity alone presumes to ask of him. And turning to the company gathered with him, he said, \"Let us go, let us go, lest we ourselves seem to do injury to the Son, if we delay the Mother's glory.\" When, then, they had returned and reported these things to their Lord, what do we suppose Jesus said but something like this? \"I am he who commended fathers and mothers to be honored by their children; I, in order to do what I taught, and to be an example to others, that I might honor my Father, descended to earth; nonetheless, that I might honor my Mother, I ascended again to heaven. I have ascended and prepared a place for her, a throne of glory, that at the right hand of the King the Queen, crowned, may sit in gilded raiment, surrounded with manifold adornment. Nor do I say this as though a throne were set apart for her in some portion: rather, she herself shall be my throne. Come, then, my chosen one, and I will set my throne in you. In you I will establish for myself a certain seat of my kingdom; from you I will decree judgments; through you I will grant hearing to prayers. No one has ministered more to me in my humility. You shared with me, besides other things, that I am man: I will share with you that I am God. You were begging for the kiss of the mouth; nay rather, you shall be kissed wholly, by the whole of me. I will not press lip to lip, but spirit to spirit, in a perpetual and indissoluble kiss; for I have desired your beauty, even more longingly than you have desired mine; nor shall I seem to myself sufficiently glorified, until you are glorified together with me.\" \"Glory to you, Lord Jesus,\" the choir of Angels added. \"Glory to you, Lord Jesus,\" let the assembly of the faithful repeat again and again. May the glorification of the Mother avail for your glory, and for our pardon; through you granting it, to whom is honor and glory, for all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon II", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "love of God", "Song of Songs", "angels", "longing for God"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-assumption-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De quiete spirituali., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 193-197.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 24:11", "Song of Songs 2:5", "Sirach 34:19", "Psalm 139:8 (historical numbering)", "Genesis 49:15", "Hebrews 4:3", "Psalm 94:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 11:9 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 13:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 26:4, 8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 114:7 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 14:8\u20139", "Isaiah 28:12", "Psalm 40:2 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 25:35", "Isaiah 66:2", "Psalm 1:4 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 57:20", "1 Thessalonians 4:11", "Proverbs 7:10\u201312", "1 Thessalonians 5:14", "2 Thessalonians 3:11\u201312, 14", "Isaiah 30:15"], "occasion": "In Assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Preaching on a feast that gives the brothers rest from the harvest, Guerric takes the words \"In all things I sought rest\" as the voice of Wisdom, of the Church and of Mary, who finds rest today. Christ still seeks rest among the humble and quiet, while the restless are driven like a wheel. Guerric urges quiet through humility and manual work, and recalls Paul's rebuke of those who walk unquietly. The sermon appears under the heading for the Assumption of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De quiete spirituali."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On spiritual rest.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1.\n\nIn omnibus requiem quaesivi (Eccli. XXIV, 11). Grata quies fessis. Grate igitur et opportune vobis, qui fessi estis, dies iste quietis et feriationis intervenit, ut dum sanctae Dei Genitricis requiem celebramus, non solum corpora recreentur hac diurna quiete a labore messis, sed etiam corda respirent in recordationem et amorem illius aeternae quietis. Et tibi tamen, o fratres, ibi etiam metetis, sed requiem metetis qui nunc laborem hujus messis seminatis. Fructus hujus laboris, illa requies erit: requies a labore, merces pro labore, cujus etiam recordatio fideles vires reparat in labore. Umbra est aestuantibus, cibus esurientibus. Sic enim ait illa, quae memoriam abundantiae suavitatis ejus eructabat: Sub umbra illius quem desideraveram sedi, et fructus ejus dulcis gutturi meo (Cantic. II, 5). O qui laboratis, o qui portatis pondus diei et aestus! sub umbra alarum Jesu invenietis requiem animabus vestris, firmamentum, ut scriptum est, virtutis, tegimen ardoris, umbraculum meridiani (Eccli. XXXIV, 19); ut ex sententia cordis sit illa confessio oris: Domine, Domine, virtus salutis meae, obumbrasti super caput meum in die belli (Psal. CXXXIX, 8), in die aestus et laboris, certaminis et tentationis. Cum enim meditatio aeternae requiei capitibus laborantium obumbrat, non solum ab aestu tentationum refrigerat, sed etiam ad laborem animos innovat, juxta illud quod scriptum est de asino forti Issachar, Vidit requiem quod esset bona, et terram quod optima, et supposuit humerum ad portandum (Gen. XLIX, 15): id est, desiderio illius quietis et haereditatis sponte humiliavit se ad laborandum. Et iste ergo dicere potuit: In omnibus requiem quaesivi, et in haereditate Domini morabor."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1.\n\n\"In all things I sought rest\" (Eccli. XXIV, 11). Rest is pleasant to the weary. Pleasantly, then, and opportunely for you who are weary, this day of rest and holiday has come, so that while we celebrate the rest of the holy Mother of God, not only may our bodies be refreshed by this day's rest from the labor of harvest, but our hearts too may breathe again in remembrance and love of that eternal rest. And yet for you, O brothers, there too you shall reap \u2014 but you who now sow the labor of this harvest shall reap rest. The fruit of this labor will be that rest: rest from labor, wages for labor \u2014 the very remembrance of which restores the faithful's strength in labor. It is shade for those who are scorched, food for those who hunger. For thus she speaks, who poured forth the memory of his abundant sweetness: \"Under the shadow of him whom I desired I sat, and his fruit was sweet to my throat\" (Cantic. II, 5). O you who labor, O you who bear the burden of the day and the heat! Under the shadow of Jesus' wings you will find rest for your souls \u2014 a stronghold of strength, as it is written, a covering from the heat, a shade from the noonday sun (Eccli. XXXIV, 19); so that from the conviction of the heart may come that confession of the mouth: \"Lord, Lord, the strength of my salvation, you have overshadowed my head in the day of battle\" (Psal. CXXXIX, 8), in the day of heat and labor, of struggle and temptation. For when meditation on eternal rest overshadows the heads of those who labor, it not only cools them from the heat of temptations but also renews their minds for labor, according to what is written of the strong ass Issachar: \"He saw rest, that it was good, and the land, that it was excellent, and he bowed his shoulder to bear burdens\" (Gen. XLIX, 15): that is, out of desire for that rest and inheritance, he willingly humbled himself to labor. And so he too could say: In all things I sought rest, and I will abide in the inheritance of the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2.\n\nFelix qui in omnibus laboribus et viis suis requiem quaerit beatam: semper festinans ingredi, ut Apostolus monet, in illam requiem (Hebr. IV, 3), ejus desiderio corpus affligens, animum autem jam illi requiei parans et componens: cum omnibus hominibus, quod ex ipso est, pacem habens; requiem et otium Mariae voluntate praeeligens, laborem autem et negotium Marthae necessitate quidem suscipiens; sed quanta praevalet pace et quiete spiritus adimplens, et semper ad illud unum necessarium de illa multiplici distractione sese recolligens. Homo hujusmodi etiam cum laborat, quiescit: sicut e diverso impius etiam cum quiescit, laborat. Quomodo enim quiescerent, quibus Deus immittit rotam malorum: quibus juravit in ira sua, Si introibunt in requiem meam? (Psal. XCIV, 12.) Extra illam non est nisi afflictio et miseria; et in circuitu ejus tempestas valida: sicut et in natura rerum fit, ut quidquid extra simplicitatem et unitatem puncti fuerit, in motu et agitatione sit; tantoque concitatiore quilibet circulus impetu rotetur, quanto ab immobilitate principii sui, id est, a medietate centri recesserit longius. Sane in circuitu impii ambulant (Psal. XI, 9), quibus Deus immittit rotam malorum: ideoque in illam internam et aeternam requiem intrare non possunt. Ideo contritio et infelicitas in viis eorum, quia viam pacis non cognoverunt (Psal. XIII, 3): quia nec quaesierunt, ut dicere possint: In omnibus requiem quaesivi; ut videlicet in multiplicitate actuum suorum, quibus turbantur, et turbant, unum quod est necessarium, cogitent aut requirant. Justorum potius est vox illa, qui et dicere possunt, Unam petii a Domino, hanc requiram. Vultum tuum, Domine, quaesivi, vultum tuum requiram (Psal. XXVI, 4, 8): qui nonnisi quietis illius amore laborant, qui omnino praeoptant ingredi putredinem in ossibus suis, ut requiescant in die tribulationis, quam in bonis ducere dies suos, et in puncto ad infernum descendere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2.\n\nHappy is he who in all his labors and ways seeks blessed rest: always hastening, as the Apostle counsels, to enter into that rest (Hebr. IV, 3), afflicting his body with desire for it, yet already preparing and composing his mind for that rest; having peace with all men, so far as it depends on him; preferring by choice the rest and leisure of Mary, yet undertaking, by necessity indeed, the labor and business of Martha \u2014 but carrying it out with as much peace and quiet of spirit as he can, and always gathering himself back, out of that manifold distraction, to that one thing which is necessary. Such a man, even when he labors, rests; just as, on the contrary, the wicked man, even when he rests, labors. For how could they rest, upon whom God sends the wheel of evils, to whom he swore in his wrath, \"If they shall enter into my rest\" (Psal. XCIV, 12.)? Outside that rest there is nothing but affliction and misery; and round about it a violent storm \u2014 just as happens also in the nature of things, that whatever is outside the simplicity and unity of the point is in motion and agitation; and the farther any circle recedes from the immobility of its origin, that is, from the center-point, the more violent is the impetus with which it is driven round. Indeed, the wicked walk round about (Psal. XI, 9), upon whom God sends the wheel of evils: and therefore they cannot enter into that inward and eternal rest. Therefore ruin and unhappiness are in their ways, because they have not known the way of peace (Psal. XIII, 3): because they have not even sought it, so that they might say, In all things I sought rest \u2014 that is, so that amid the multiplicity of their actions, by which they are disturbed and by which they disturb others, they might think of or seek that one thing which is necessary. Rather that voice belongs to the just, who can also say, \"One thing I have asked of the Lord, this will I seek. Your face, Lord, have I sought; your face will I seek\" (Psal. XXVI, 4, 8): who labor only out of love of that rest \u2014 who altogether prefer that rottenness enter into their bones, that they may rest in the day of tribulation, rather than spend their days in good things and in a moment go down to hell.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Si quis tamen curiosius inquirere velit, cujus potissimum vox illa sit, In omnibus requiem quaesivi: vox est utique Sapientiae, vox est Ecclesiae, vox est Mariae, vox est cujuslibet sapientis animae. Sapientia in omnibus requiem quaesivit, sed in solis humilibus invenit. Ecclesia in omnibus mundi nationibus requiem quaesivit, sed in solis credentibus invenit. Maria, sicut et quaelibet fidelis anima, in omnibus actionibus suis requiem quaesivit; sed hodie demum invenit, cum post persecutionem Herodis, et fugam Aegypti, post tot insidias et atrocitates Judaicae impietatis, post tot passiones suas, de passione et morte Filii; postquam tot et tam acerbi ipsius animam pertransierunt gladii, tandem hodie dicere datum est ei: Convertere, anima, in requiem tuam, quia Dominus benefecit tibi (Psal. CXIV, 7). Qui creavit me, creatus ex me, requievit in tabernaculo corporis mei, negare non poterit mihi requiem coeli sui. Qui enim gratiam ultro cumulat aliis, par pari quomodo non referet matri? Perge, Maria, perge secura in bonis Filii tui; fiducialiter age tanquam regina, mater Regis et sponsa. Requiem quaerebas, sed amplioris gloriae est, quod tibi debetur, regnum et potestas. Indivisum habere tecum cupit imperium, cui tecum in carne una, et uno spiritu indivisum fuit pietatis et unitatis mysterium: dum scilicet salvo honore naturae, geminato munere gratiae, juncta est mater in matrimonium. Requiesce igitur, o felix, inter brachia Sponsi. Replicabit tibi, ni fallor, inter amplexus et oscula, quam suaviter requieverit in tabernaculo corporis tui, quam suavis in cubiculo cordis tui. Non est injustus Deus, o fratres, ut obliviscatur operis boni: vivit semper apud eum memoria beneficii semel accepti. Beatus, apud quem Deus vel semel requiem invenerit, in cujus tabernaculo vel hora una requieverit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Yet if anyone wishes to inquire more carefully whose voice above all this is, \"In all things I sought rest\": it is indeed the voice of Wisdom, the voice of the Church, the voice of Mary, the voice of any wise soul. Wisdom sought rest in all things, but found it only in the humble. The Church sought rest among all the nations of the world, but found it only among believers. Mary, like any faithful soul, sought rest in all her actions; but only today has she finally found it, when, after the persecution of Herod, and the flight into Egypt, after so many plots and atrocities of Jewish impiety, after so many sufferings of her own concerning the passion and death of her Son\u2014after so many bitter swords had pierced her soul\u2014it was at last granted to her today to say: \"Return, O my soul, into thy rest, for the Lord hath been bountiful to thee\" (Psal. CXIV, 7). He who created me, having been created from me, rested in the tabernacle of my body; he cannot deny me the rest of his heaven. For he who freely heaps grace upon others\u2014how will he not repay his mother in like measure? Go forward, Mary, go forward secure in the goods of your Son; act with confidence as a queen, mother of the King and his spouse. You were seeking rest, but what is owed to you is of a greater glory\u2014kingdom and power. He desires to have undivided rule with you, he with whom you had, in one flesh and one spirit, an undivided mystery of devotion and unity: since, with the honor of nature preserved, and the gift of grace doubled, the mother was joined to him in marriage. Rest, then, O blessed one, between the arms of the Bridegroom. He will tell you, unless I am mistaken, amid embraces and kisses, how sweetly he rested in the tabernacle of your body, how sweet he was in the chamber of your heart. God is not unjust, O brothers, that he should forget a good work: the memory of a benefit once received lives always with him. Blessed is he with whom God has found rest even once, in whose tabernacle he has rested even for a single hour.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Ecce nunc quoque Sapientia clamitat in plateis: in omnibus requiem quaesivi. Pulsavi, nec fuit qui aperiret: vocavi, nec fuit qui responderet. Filius hominis, factus, secundum prophetam, velut vir vagus, et quasi viator declinans ad manendum (Jerem. XIV, 9, 8), non habet ubi reclinet caput suum: foris stat plenus caput rore, et cincinnos guttis noctium. Quis ille in nobis tam humanus atque hospitalis, ut surgat et aperiat ei, et inducat in cubiculum suum; aut certe ostendat ei coenaculum grande stratum, ubi cum discipulis pascha manducet novum? Hoc enim dico, fratres, nisi requiem quam quaerit, invenerit apud nos, nec nos optatam requiem inveniemus in illo. Ait quidem Dominus per prophetam: Haec est requies mea, reficite lassum, et hoc est meum refrigerium (Isa. XXVIII, 12). Beatus qui intelligit super egenum et pauperem: in die mala (Psal. XL, 2) parabit ei Dominus vicissitudine debita requiem et refrigerium. Si ergo humanitatem membris suis exhibitam Deus imputat sibi: quanto magis quod ipsius fit spiritui, cum gratiarum actione recolet, Hospes, inquiens, fui et suscepistis me? (Matth. XXV, 35.) Nunquid enim multorum paupertas sanctorum, quae vagos colligere, esurientes pascere non sufficit, Domino qui magis apud pauperes hospitari consuevit, inhumana et inhospitalis erit? Super quem autem requiescam, inquit, nisi super humilem et quietum, et trementem verba mea? (Isai. LXVI, 2.) O humilitas angusta tibi, ampla divinitati; pauper et insufficiens tibi, sufficiens ei quem non capit orbis: copiose ac deliciose reficiens illum, qui et Angelos pascit. Super quem, inquit, requiescam nisi super humilem? In omnibus requiem quaesivi: sed apud humilem ancillam inveni. Non est inventa similis illi in gratia humilitatis: ideo in plenitudine humilitatis requievit etiam corporaliter omnis plenitudo divinitatis. Quanquam aliter requieverit in Filio: quia etsi Mater humillima, longe tamen Filius humilior. Idcirco Spiritus septiformis non solum super ipsum requievit, sed etiam omnibus qui mites et humiles ab eo esse didicerunt, diversas in eo felicissimae quietis mansiones praeparavit; imo totum ipsum quoddam aureum fecit reclinatorium ad quiescendum. Hujus nimirum reclinatorii beatissimam requiem visus est quadam specie praelibasse, qui supra pectus ejus meruit in coena recumbere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Behold, even now Wisdom cries aloud in the streets: \"In all things I sought rest.\" I knocked, and there was none to open; I called, and there was none to answer. The Son of man, made, according to the prophet, like a wandering man, and like a traveler turning aside to lodge (Jerem. XIV, 9, 8), has nowhere to lay his head: he stands outside, his head full of dew, and his locks with the drops of the night. Who among us is so humane and hospitable as to rise and open to him, and bring him into his chamber; or at least show him a large furnished upper room, where he may eat the new Passover with his disciples? For I say this, brothers: unless he finds among us the rest he seeks, neither shall we find in him the rest we desire. The Lord indeed says through the prophet: \"This is my rest, refresh the weary, and this is my refreshment\" (Isa. XXVIII, 12). Blessed is he who understands concerning the needy and the poor: in the evil day (Psal. XL, 2) the Lord will prepare for him, in due return, rest and refreshment. If then God counts as done to himself the kindness shown to his members: how much more will he, giving thanks, recall what was done to his own spirit, saying, \"I was a stranger, and you took me in\" (Matth. XXV, 35.)? For shall the poverty of many saints, which does not suffice to gather in the wandering or feed the hungry, be inhospitable and inhumane to the Lord, who is accustomed rather to lodge among the poor? \"But upon whom shall I rest,\" he says, \"but upon him that is humble and quiet, and trembleth at my words?\" (Isai. LXVI, 2.) O humility, narrow to yourself, but spacious to divinity; poor and insufficient in yourself, yet sufficient for him whom the world cannot contain: abundantly and delightfully refreshing him who feeds even the Angels. \"Upon whom,\" he says, \"shall I rest but upon the humble?\" \"In all things I sought rest\": but I found it with a humble handmaid. None was found like her in the grace of humility: therefore in the fullness of her humility the whole fullness of divinity rested even bodily. Though it rested differently in the Son: for although the Mother was most humble, the Son was far more humble still. For this reason the sevenfold Spirit not only rested upon him, but also prepared various dwellings of most blessed rest in him for all who learned from him to be meek and humble; indeed, he made him wholly a kind of golden resting-place for repose. The most blessed rest of this resting-place he who deserved to recline upon his breast at the supper seemed, in some fashion, to have tasted beforehand.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed adhuc libet intueri, quam plenum rationis et congruentiae sit quod ait: Super quem requiescam nisi super humilem et quietum? Quomodo namque super inquietum aliquid quiesceret? quomodo super basem, quae nutaret aut vacillaret, columna immobilis staret? Quis autem, nisi humilis, quietus esse potest? quis, nisi humilis in pace quieti et modesti spiritus, seipsum possidere potest? Impium projicit ventus a facie terrae (Psal. I, 4), circumferturque omni vento doctrinae. Impius, inquit propheta, quasi mare fervens, quod quiescere non potest (Isai. LVII, 20). Fervet ira, aestuat avaritia, intumescit superbia, se ipsum jugiter pugna quatit intestina, ac seditione collidit domestica. Ut ergo requiescat in vobis, o fratres mei, amator ille largitorque quietis, date operam, juxta consilium Apostoli, ut quieti sitis. Quonam modo istud erit? Dico, inquit, vobis: Ut vestrum negotium agatis, et operemini manibus vestris (I Thess. IV, 11). Opus est onus, quo veluti pondus navibus, ita quies et gravitas inquietis additur cordibus, sed exterioris hominis fundatur atque componitur status. Magnorum initium malorum est, sicut legistis, mulier vaga, quietis impatiens, nec valens consistere in domo pedibus suis: nunc foris, nunc in plateis, nunc juxta angulos insidians (Prov. VII, 10-12). Nec sine causa est quod Magister gentium hoc inquietudinis malum sic habet suspectum, ut non solum correptione, sed etiam separatione censeat illud persequendum. Rogamus, inquit, vos, fratres, corripite inquietos (I Thess. V, 14). Et item in secunda ad eosdem Thessalonicenses, inter alia quae super inquietos velut e coelis tonabat: Audivimus, inquit, inter vos quosdam ambulantes inquiete, nihil operantes, sed curiose agentes. Hic qui ejusmodi sunt denuntiamus, ut cum silentio operantes, suum panem manducent. Quod si quis non obedit verbo nostro per epistolam, hunc notate: et ne commisceamini cum illo, ut confundatur (II Thess. III, 11, 12, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But it is still worth considering how full of reason and fittingness is what he says: Upon whom shall I rest, but upon him that is humble and quiet? For how could he rest upon something unquiet? How could an immovable column stand upon a base that trembled or wobbled? Who, indeed, except the humble, can be quiet? Who, except the humble man at peace, of a quiet and modest spirit, can possess himself? The wind casts away the wicked from the face of the earth (Psal. I, 4), and he is carried about by every wind of doctrine. The wicked man, says the prophet, is like the raging sea, which cannot rest (Isai. LVII, 20). He burns with anger, seethes with avarice, swells with pride, perpetually shakes himself with inward battle, and collides in domestic sedition. Therefore, my brothers, that he who loves and bestows rest may rest in you, strive, according to the counsel of the Apostle, to be quiet. How shall this be? I say to you, he says: that you do your own business, and work with your own hands (I Thess. IV, 11). Work is a burden, by which, just as weight is added to ships, so quiet and gravity are added to unquiet hearts, and the condition of the outer man is founded and composed. The beginning of great evils is, as you have read, a wandering woman, impatient of rest, unable to abide with her feet in her own house: now abroad, now in the streets, now lying in wait near the corners (Prov. VII, 10-12). It is not without cause that the Teacher of the Gentiles holds this evil of restlessness in such suspicion that he thinks it should be pursued not only by rebuke but even by separation. We beseech you, brothers, he says, rebuke the unquiet (I Thess. V, 14). And again in the second epistle to the same Thessalonians, among other things which he thundered down as from heaven against the unquiet: We have heard, he says, that there are some among you who walk unquietly, doing no work, but being busy about other men's business. Now them that are such, we charge, that working with silence, they eat their own bread. And if any man obey not our word by this epistle, note that man: and do not keep company with him, that he may be ashamed (II Thess. III, 11, 12, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Si quis forte (quod absit!) ejusmodi invenitur inter vos, pulchrius et honestius, antequam tam graviter et publice notetur, ultro confundetur, inde scilicet, quia notabilis est, et dissimulatur; reprehensibilis, et toleratur. Sic autem confundatur, ut confusione corrigatur, correptione laetetur, laetificetque non modo nos, sed Spiritum Dei: qui cum dicat, In omnibus requiem quaesivi, non invenit eam nisi in quietis, nec praestat nisi quietis. Unde et inquietos per prophetam revocat et compescit: Si revertamini, et quiescatis, salvi eritis (Isai. XXX, 15). Omnes ergo pariter sic operam demus, ut quieti simus, ut in quiete nostra semper in meditatione aeternae quietis occupemur, ac desiderio illius ad omnem parati laborem inveniamur. Hoc nobis impetret beata Dei Genitrix, cujus requiem celebramus, ab eo qui requievit in tabernaculo corporis et cordis ejus. Ipse est requies aeterna Christus Jesus; cui est honor, et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. If perhaps (which God forbid!) someone of this sort is found among you, it would be fairer and more decent that, before he is so gravely and publicly noted, he should be shamed of his own accord beforehand\u2014precisely because he is notable, and yet is overlooked; reprehensible, and yet is tolerated. But let him be so shamed that by shame he may be corrected, that he may rejoice in correction, and may give joy not only to us but to the Spirit of God: who, since he says, In all things I sought rest, finds it only in the quiet, and grants it only to the quiet. Hence through the prophet he recalls and restrains the unquiet: If you return and be quiet, you shall be saved (Isai. XXX, 15). Let us all, then, equally take care to be quiet, so that in our quiet we may always be occupied in meditation on eternal rest, and may be found ready, by desire of that rest, for every labor. May the blessed Mother of God, whose rest we celebrate, obtain this for us from him who rested in the tabernacle of her body and heart. He himself is eternal rest, Christ Jesus; to whom be honor and glory for all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon III", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "rest", "humility", "manual labor", "monastic life"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:assumption", "calendar_label": "The Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-assumption-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV, Quod beata Virgo Mariae et Marthae officia implevit., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 197-200.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Assumption", "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Luke 10:42", "Sirach 24:12", "Luke 10:39", "Sirach 2:19, 51 (printed locator; passage from Luke 2:19, 51)", "John 19:25", "John 2:4", "Matthew 12:47\u201348", "Luke 2:49", "Matthew 12:49", "Isaiah 62:4", "Luke 2:19, 51", "Isaiah 53:11", "Genesis 29", "Sirach 32:1\u20133", "Hosea 10:12", "2 Corinthians 9:6", "Psalm 111:2 (historical numbering)", "Luke 1:48", "Song of Songs 6:8"], "occasion": "In Assumptione B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Assumption of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Assumption", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric applies \"Mary has chosen the best part\" to the Mother of the Lord, who served Christ as Martha did and listened as her sister did. He explains why Christ seemed to set his mother aside, and why he entrusted her to the virgin disciple John. Contemplation is the reward of faithful labor, as Leah came before Rachel, and Mary now reaps what she sowed. The sermon appears under the heading for the Assumption of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quod beata Virgo Mariae et Marthae officia implevit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "That the Blessed Virgin fulfilled the offices of Mary and of Martha.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Maria optimam partem elegit (Luc. X, 42). Hoc de Maria sorore Marthae scriptum est: sed in Maria matre Domini plenius et sanctius impletum est. Hodie namque beata virgo Maria optimam partem elegit, imo quam dudum elegerat, hodie perpetuo possidendam accepit: ut scilicet inseparabiliter adhaereat Domino, perenniter fruatur Dei Verbo. Nec alienum aut incongruum, si quod de illa Maria dictum est, in istam transumatur; cum ad convenientiam transformationis, similitudo non solum nominis, sed etiam operis suffragetur. Illa siquidem Dominum hospitio tecti, haec thalamo suscepit uteri. Et qui creavit me, inquit, requievit in tabernaculo meo (Eccli. XXIV, 12). Illa sedens secus pedes Domini audiebat verbum illius (Luc. X, 39): haec sedula circa curam humanitatis ejus, conservabat omnia verba quae de illo erant, conferens in corde suo (Eccli. II, 19, 51). Sed et cum evangelizans circumiret Jesus civitates et castella, Maria comes individua vestigiis ejus adhaerebat, pendebatque ex ore docentis: adeo, ut nec procella persecutionis, nec horrore supplicii a consectatu Filii et Magistri potuerit absterreri, Stabat, inquit, juxta crucem Jesu Maria mater ejus (Joan. XIX, 25). Plane Mater, quae nec in terrore mortis filium deserebat. Quomodo enim morte terreri poterat, cujus charitas fortis ut mors, imo fortior quam mors erat? Plane juxta crucem Jesu stabat, cujus mentem dolor crucis simul cruci figebat, suamque ipsius animam tam multiplex pertransibat gladius, quantis confossum corpus Filii cernebat vulneribus. Merito igitur ibi mater agnita est, et cura ipsius idoneo tutori delegata est, ubi maxime probata est et Matris ad Filium sincera charitas, et Filii de Matre vera humanitas. Nam alias velut dissimulasse Matrem visus est: sive cum in nuptiis flagitanti miraculum ait, Quid mihi et tibi, mulier? (Joan. II, 4.) sive cum in mediis sermonibus Evangelii dicenti cuidam, Ecce mater tua et fratres tui stant quaerentes te: Quae est, inquit, mater mea? (Matth. XII, 47, 48.) Sed et Matri, cum miraculum posceret, sic respondendum erat, ut aliunde quam a Matre se miracula habere significaret: et illi qui sermonem Evangelii parentes annuntiando interrumpebat, melius respondere non poterat, ut videlicet spiritualia carnalibus praeferenda monstraret. Ac si more suo diceret parentibus, occupatum in negotio Evangelii quaerentibus: Quid est quod me quaeritis? Nescitis quia in his quae Patris mei sunt oportet me esse? (Luc. II, 49.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Mary has chosen the best part (Luc. X, 42). This was written of Mary the sister of Martha; but it was fulfilled more fully and more holily in Mary the Mother of the Lord. For today the blessed Virgin Mary has chosen the best part\u2014or rather, the part which she had long ago chosen, she today received to possess forever: namely, that she might cling inseparably to the Lord, and enjoy the Word of God perpetually. Nor is it foreign or unfitting that what was said of that other Mary should be transferred to this one; since, for the fittingness of the transfer, the likeness supports it not only in name but also in deed. For the one received the Lord under the hospitality of a roof; this one, in the bridal chamber of her womb. And 'He who created me,' she says, 'rested in my tabernacle' (Eccli. XXIV, 12). The one, sitting beside the Lord's feet, was listening to his word (Luc. X, 39); this one, diligently occupied with the care of his humanity, kept all the words concerning him, pondering them in her heart (Eccli. II, 19, 51). But also, when Jesus went about preaching the gospel through the cities and towns, Mary, an inseparable companion, clung to his footsteps, and hung upon the mouth of the teacher: so much so that neither the storm of persecution nor the horror of the punishment could frighten her away from following her Son and Master. 'There stood,' it says, 'by the cross of Jesus, Mary his mother' (Joan. XIX, 25). Truly a Mother, who did not abandon her son even in the terror of death. For how could she be terrified by death, whose love was strong as death\u2014indeed, stronger than death? Truly she stood beside the cross of Jesus, whose mind the sorrow of the cross fixed to the cross at the same time, and through her own soul there passed a sword as manifold as the wounds with which she saw the body of her Son pierced. Deservedly, then, she was there acknowledged as mother, and the care of her was entrusted to a fitting guardian, at the very place where both the Mother's sincere love for her Son and the Son's true humanity toward his Mother were most fully proven. For elsewhere he seemed, as it were, to disregard his Mother: whether when, at the wedding, as she urged him to a miracle, he said, 'Woman, what is that to me and to you?' (Joan. II, 4.); or when, in the midst of the discourses of the Gospel, to one who said, 'Behold, your mother and your brothers stand seeking you,' he said, 'Who is my mother?' (Matth. XII, 47, 48.). But to his Mother, when she was asking for a miracle, he had to answer in such a way as to show that he had his miracles from some source other than his Mother; and to the one who was interrupting the discourse of the Gospel by announcing his kinsfolk, he could not have answered better, in order to show that spiritual things are to be preferred to carnal ones. As if, according to his custom, he were saying to his parents, who sought him while he was occupied in the business of the Gospel: 'Why is it that you seek me? Did you not know that I must be about those things which are my Father's?' (Luc. II, 49.).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Absit enim ut Matrem aspernatus sit, qui tanta cura de parentibus honorandis legem dedit! Absit, inquam, ut in terra fastidierit Matrem, cujus e coelo concupivit decorem! Imo potius ordinabat in nobis charitatem, tam verbis quam exemplis nobiscum agens, ut carnalium affectibus necessitudinum, non solum Dei praeponamus dilectionem, sed etiam eorum qui Dei faciunt voluntatem. Ille siquidem affectus de cordibus exigitur omnium nostrum, quos summi Patris adoptavit dignatio, quo fideliter dicamus cum ipsius Unigenito: Quicunque fecerit voluntatem Patris mei qui in coelis est, ipse meus frater, et soror, et mater est (Matth. XII, 49). Prorsus filiis Dei competit vox ista, nec ullum fidelius testimonium reddit ipse Spiritus spiritui nostro quod simus filii Dei, quam ut sonet de cordibus nostris haec vox Unigeniti Dei. Itaque Mariam, quae secundum carnem mater erat, alia quoque ratione matrem sibi Jesus probat: quandoquidem et ipsa voluntatem Patris in tantum faciebat, ut de ipsa praediceret Pater, Vocaberis voluntas mea in ea (Isai. LXII, 4). Igitur ubi eam visus est ignorasse Filius, ibi amplius honorasse invenitur: cum ei scilicet materni nominis honor duplicatur, pro eo quod eumdem Filium, quem alvo gestaverat incarnatum, etiam nunc animo gestabat inspiratum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Far be it that he should have scorned his Mother, he who gave the law with such care concerning the honoring of parents! Far be it, I say, that he should have disdained on earth the Mother whose beauty he had desired from heaven! Rather, he was ordering love within us, acting with us both by words and by examples, so that we might set the love of God\u2014and also the love of those who do the will of God\u2014above the affections of carnal ties. For that affection is demanded of the hearts of all of us whom the condescension of the most high Father has adopted, so that we may faithfully say with his Only-Begotten: 'Whoever shall do the will of my Father who is in heaven, he is my brother, and sister, and mother' (Matth. XII, 49). This utterance indeed belongs to the children of God, nor does the Spirit himself give any more faithful testimony to our spirit that we are children of God, than when this utterance of the Only-Begotten of God resounds from our hearts. And so Jesus proves Mary, who was his mother according to the flesh, to be his mother also by another title: since she herself did the will of the Father to such a degree that the Father foretold concerning her, 'You shall be called my will in her' (Isai. LXII, 4). Therefore, where the Son seemed to have disregarded her, there he is found to have honored her the more: since for her the honor of the maternal name is doubled, in that the same Son whom she had carried incarnate in her womb, she even now carried in her soul by inspiration.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Caeterum cum dilexisset eam Jesus, in finem dilexit eam, ut non solum propter ipsam finem vivendi, verum et in ipsam finem pene faceret loquendi: dum velut inter ultima verba testamenti, curam Matris, cujus debitorem se cognoscebat, charissimo transcribit haeredi. Sic quippe inter Petrum qui plus diligebat, et Joannem qui plus diligebatur, haereditatem suam Christus divisit, ut Petrus sortiretur Ecclesiam, Joannes Mariam. Huic plane competebat haec portio non solum jure propinquitatis, sed etiam amoris privilegio testimonioque pudoris. Decebat namque ut nonnisi virgo virgini deserviret, quatenus et Virgo beata, divino languens prae amore, floribus fulciretur castitatis, et virginitas adolescentis id interim perciperet remunerationis, ut de contubernio proficeret tantae sanctitatis. Qui etiam, quia fidelis probatus est in obsequio incorruptae matris; mysteria quoque divinitatis, et arcana Verbi incorruptibilis sibi credi promeruit. Decebat, inquam, ut Matri Domini non alius obsequeretur quam dilectus Filii: quatenus et Mater semper suspirans Filium in dilectum Filii suavius respiraret, et discipulus Magistrum sibi conquerens nimis cito subtractum, Magistram totius veritatis se reperisse gauderet. Idque satis commode provisum, ut scripturus Evangelium familiarius de singulis conferret cum ea, quae conscia erat omnium; quippe quae ab initio omnia Filii sui diligentius observaverat, conservans omnia verba quae de illo erant, conferens in corde suo (Luc. II, 19, 51). Sic nempe se Martham exhibuit in cura pueri nutriendi; ut nihilominus Mariae partes exsecuta credatur in studio verbi cognoscendi. Maxime tamen postquam idem Filius ascendit ubi erat prius, Mater absoluta omni sollicitudine temporali, sanctoque Spiritu, quem praeter singulares illas conceptus sui primitias communiter cum Apostolis acceperat, plenius illuminata, gaudebat nimirum vacare, et videre quoniam Jesus est Deus. Visio prorsus ineffabilis gaudii, summaeque delectationis omnibus qui diligunt Jesum; sed prae omnibus illi quae genuit Jesum: cui sicut singulariter excepta est gratia Deum generandi; sic et praerogativa gloriandi in eo quem genuit. Omnino singularis atque incomparabilis gloria Virginis matris, videre Deum regem omnium in diademate carnis quo coronavit eum; ut et Deum agnoscat et adoret in corpore proprio, et corpus proprium glorificatum videat in Deo. His interim, ut opinor, contemplationibus pascebatur Maria, hanc optimam partem elegerat: quae non ablata, sed perfecta est hodie in ea. Quia enim negligens aut segnis non exstitit in opere Marthae, nequaquam vacua derelicta est a fructu Mariae. Labor in actione, fructus seu merces in contemplatione. Propterea, inquit, quod laboravit anima ejus, videbit et saturabitur (Isa. LIII, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Moreover, when Jesus had loved her, he loved her unto the end, so that for her sake he not only made an end of living, but in her regard almost made an end of speaking as well: since, as it were among the last words of his testament, he transferred the care of his Mother\u2014whose debtor he knew himself to be\u2014to his most beloved heir. For thus Christ divided his inheritance between Peter, who loved more, and John, who was loved more: so that Peter obtained the Church as his lot, and John, Mary. This portion plainly belonged to John not only by right of kinship, but also by the privilege of love and the testimony of chastity. For it was fitting that none but a virgin should serve a virgin: so that the Blessed Virgin, languishing for divine love, might be propped up by the flowers of chastity, and that the virginity of the young man might meanwhile receive this as its reward, that he might profit from the companionship of such great holiness. He also, because he was proved faithful in the service of the incorrupt Mother, deserved that the mysteries of the Godhead and the secrets of the incorruptible Word should be entrusted to him. It was fitting, I say, that none other than the beloved of the Son should serve the Mother of the Lord: so that the Mother, ever sighing for her Son, might breathe more sweetly in the beloved of the Son, and the disciple, lamenting that his Master had been taken from him too soon, might rejoice that he had found a Mistress of all truth. And this was quite fittingly provided, that he who was to write the Gospel might confer more intimately about particular matters with her who was privy to all things; since from the beginning she had more diligently observed all things concerning her Son, keeping all the words that were about him, and pondering them in her heart (Luc. II, 19, 51). Thus indeed she showed herself a Martha in the care of nursing the child; yet is nonetheless believed to have carried out the part of Mary in the pursuit of knowing the Word. But most of all, after that same Son ascended to where he was before, the Mother, freed from all temporal care, and more fully illumined by the Holy Spirit\u2014whom, besides those singular first-fruits of her conceiving, she had received in common with the Apostles\u2014rejoiced indeed to be at leisure, and to see that Jesus is God. A vision truly of ineffable joy and of the highest delight to all who love Jesus; but above all to her who bore Jesus: to whom, just as the grace of begetting God was singularly granted, so too was the prerogative of glorying in him whom she bore. Altogether singular and incomparable is the glory of the Virgin Mother, to see God, the King of all, in the diadem of flesh with which she crowned him; so that she both recognizes and adores God in her own body, and sees her own body glorified in God. With such contemplations, I suppose, Mary was being fed meanwhile; this best part she had chosen, which is not taken away but today is perfected in her. For because she was not negligent or slothful in the work of Martha, she was by no means left empty of the fruit of Mary. The labor is in action; the fruit, or reward, is in contemplation. Therefore, he says, because her soul labored, she shall see and be satisfied (Isa. LIII, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Vobis haec dicimus, fratres, ut si quis desiderio tangitur illius optimae partis quae in Maria laudatur, sciat quia haec praemium est illius quae in Martha non improbatur: nec aequum est ut praemium ante meritum requiratur. Jacob prius oportet jungi Liae, quam fruatur amplexibus Rachel (Gen. XXIX): sed et ipsum prius vocari et esse Jacob, quam Israel. Rectorem, inquit, te posuerunt? curam illorum habe, et sic considera, et omni cura tua explicita recumbe, ut laeteris propter illos, et ornamentum gratiae accipias coronam (Eccli. XXXII, 1-3). Labor siquidem operis, seu sollicitudo administrationis, semina sunt justitiae: pro quibus gaudia metenda sunt ex ore consolantis misericordiae. Sic enim ait propheta: Seminate vobis in justitia, metite in ore misericordiae (Osee X, 12). At enim qui parce seminat, parce et metet: et qui seminat in benedictione, de benedictionibus et metet (II Cor. IX, 6). Nullus sane tam profusa benedictione seminavit, sicut illa benedicta in mulieribus; quae benedictum semen de ventre profudit. Sed semen dicam, an fructum? Melius utrumque. Qui enim semen est operantibus justitiam, fructus erit metentibus gloriam. Semen, in passione: fructus, in resurrectione. Potens in terra semen istud, quod cadens in terram mox excitavit potentiam suam, ut multum fructum afferret, et in semine illo benedicerentur omnes gentes. Unde et ibi sequitur: Generatio rectorum benedicetur (Psal. CXI, 2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. We say these things to you, brethren, so that if anyone is touched by desire for that best part which is praised in Mary, he may know that this is the reward of that part which is not disapproved in Martha: nor is it right that the reward be sought before the merit. Jacob must first be joined to Leah, before he enjoys the embraces of Rachel (Gen. XXIX): and he himself must first be called and be Jacob, before he is Israel. Have they made you a ruler? he says; take care of them, and so consider, and when all your care has been discharged, take your rest, that you may rejoice because of them, and receive the crown as the ornament of grace (Eccli. XXXII, 1-3). For the labor of work, or the solicitude of administration, are the seeds of righteousness: for which joys are to be reaped from the mouth of consoling mercy. For thus says the prophet: Sow for yourselves in righteousness, reap in the mouth of mercy (Osee X, 12). But indeed he who sows sparingly shall also reap sparingly; and he who sows in blessing shall also reap of blessings (II Cor. IX, 6). No one indeed has sown with so abundant a blessing as she who is blessed among women, who poured forth the blessed seed from her womb. But shall I call it seed, or fruit? Better to call it both. For he who is seed to those laboring in righteousness will be fruit to those reaping glory. Seed, in the Passion; fruit, in the Resurrection. Mighty on earth was that seed, which, falling into the earth, soon roused its power, so that it might bring forth much fruit, and in that seed all nations might be blessed. Whence it follows there also: The generation of the upright shall be blessed (Psal. CXI, 2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. De benedictionibus suis itaque Maria metat: et quae benedictionem omnium gentium seminavit, benedictionem omnium gentium singulariter accipiat. Beatam me dicent, inquit, omnes generationes (Luc. I, 48). Parum est hoc. Beatam te dicent omnes beatorum spirituum ordines. Viderunt hodie ascendentem filiae Sion coelestis et beatam dixerunt, et reginae laudaverunt eam (Cantic. VI, 8). Prorsus de benedictionibus suis hodie Maria metit, quia refusa est spiritualiter in eam omnimoda benedictio illa quam de se profudit. Date ei, inquit Spiritus sanctus, de fructu ventris sui; et saturetur illo quem genuit. O Mater misericordiae, saturare gloria Filii tui; et dimitte reliquias tuas parvulis tuis. Tu jam ad mensam, Domina; nos sub mensa catelli. Sicut oculi ancillae in manibus dominae suae, ita familia haec famelica de te praestolatur alimoniam vitae. Per te fructum vitae communicavimus, in mensa praesentium sacramentorum: per te eumdem fructum vitae communicemus in mensa perennium gaudiorum, Jesum benedictum fructum ventris tui; cui est honor et gloria, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. So let Mary reap from her own blessings: and let her who sowed the blessing of all nations receive singularly the blessing of all nations. \"All generations shall call me blessed,\" she says (Luc. I, 48). This is too little. All the ranks of the blessed spirits shall call you blessed. Today the daughters of heavenly Sion saw her ascending, and called her blessed; and queens praised her (Cantic. VI, 8). Indeed, today Mary reaps from her own blessings, because that whole blessing which she poured forth from herself has been poured back upon her spiritually. \"Give her,\" says the Holy Spirit, \"of the fruit of her womb; and let her be satisfied with him whom she bore.\" O Mother of mercy, be satisfied with the glory of your Son; and leave your leftovers to your little ones. You are now at the table, O Lady; we are the little dogs beneath the table. As the eyes of the handmaid are on the hands of her mistress, so this famished household awaits from you the nourishment of life. Through you we have shared the fruit of life at the table of the sacraments now present: through you may we share that same fruit of life at the table of everlasting joys\u2014Jesus, the blessed fruit of your womb; to whom be honor and glory, forever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-assumption-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Mary keeping all these words in her heart is Luke 2:19 and 2:51. The edition prints Eccli. ii, 19, 51 here and Luc. ii, 19, 51 in paragraph 3."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Assumption Sermon IV", "topics": ["Assumption", "Mary", "Mary and Martha", "contemplation", "active life", "John the Apostle"], "url": "sermons/guerric-assumption-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN FESTO SANCTI BENEDICTI., SERMO I., De mansione in sapientia., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 99-103.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Sirach 14:22", "Proverbs 3:13", "3 Kings 11 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Romans 1:21\u201332", "Luke 8:13\u201314", "Isaiah 18:5", "Hosea 8:7", "Proverbs 27:8", "Sirach 6:22", "John 6:61", "John 6:69", "Psalm 93:20 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 30:20 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 10:4", "Psalm 64:9 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 13:12", "Isaiah 30:15", "Isaiah 32:17", "Sirach 1:33", "Joshua 5:13", "Wisdom 8:16", "Psalm 70:23 (historical numbering; opening parenthesis absent in print)", "Matthew 25:29"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric considers perseverance in wisdom through monastic stability, prayer, Scripture, work and silence. He connects obedience under difficult teaching with sacramental nourishment and the fear of God. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN FESTO SANCTI BENEDICTI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE FEAST OF SAINT BENEDICT.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mansione in sapientia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On abiding in wisdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beatus vir qui in sapientia morabitur, et qui in justitia meditabitur, et in sensu cogitabit circumspectionem Dei! (Eccli. XIV, 22.) Quam congrue verba haec in laudem beati Benedicti cantata sint, facile quivis vestrum intelligit, cui tamen vita et doctrina ipsius non ignota sit. Quam commode vero ad vitae nostrae doctrinam trahi possint, ipsa per se verborum facies ostendit, quae scilicet illa quibus nihil est utilius in vita, sapientiam, justitiam, timorem Dei, cum praemio etiam beatitudinis commendat. Beatus, inquit, vir qui in sapientia morabitur. Prorsus haec est beatitudo, haec sapientia, si moreris in sapientia, ut perseveranter eam teneas: non enim statim qui invenerit, sed qui tenuerit eam, beatus. Dicit quidem Scriptura: Beatus qui invenit sapientiam. Sed non ibi quievit, imo addidit, et qui affluit sapientia (Prov. III, 13); ne videlicet solum invenisse statim putaretur ad beatitudinem sufficere, nisi, cum inveneris, cum ea et in ea demoreris, et contubernio atque convictu ipsius delecteris; nec discedas a magisterio ejus, donec meditando justitiam, cogitando circumspectionem Dei, affluere prudentia possit. Nam et Salomon sapientiam invenit; sed, quia prudentia minus affluxit, nec satis prudenter sibi circumspexit, dum videlicet non caute sibi providit a mulieribus alienis, non modo sapientiam perdidit, sed usque ad ultimam idololatriae insipientiam proruit (III Reg. XI). Sapientes quoque hujus mundi, qui invisibilia Dei, a creatura mundi per ea quae facta sunt, intellecta conspexerunt, sapientiam invenisse visi sunt; sed, quia nec ipsi prudentia affluxerunt, et, cum cognovissent Deum, non sicut Deum glorificaverunt, stulti et ipsi facti sunt, et obscuratum est insipiens cor eorum usque ad reprobum sensum, et ignominiam turpitudinum (Rom. I, 21-32)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Blessed is the man who shall abide in wisdom, and who shall meditate in justice, and in understanding shall think upon the circumspection of God!\" (Eccli. XIV, 22.) How fittingly these words are sung in praise of blessed Benedict, anyone of you to whom his life and teaching are not unknown easily understands. But how conveniently they can be drawn into instruction for our own life, the very face of the words themselves shows, since they commend those things than which nothing is more useful in life\u2014wisdom, justice, the fear of God\u2014together with the reward of blessedness as well. Blessed, he says, is the man who shall abide in wisdom. This indeed is blessedness, this is wisdom: if you abide in wisdom, so that you hold it perseveringly; for blessed is not at once the one who has found it, but the one who has held fast to it. Scripture indeed says: \"Blessed is he who finds wisdom.\" But it did not stop there; rather it added, \"and who overflows with wisdom\" (Prov. III, 13); lest, namely, one should think that merely having found it suffices at once for blessedness, unless, when you have found it, you remain with it and in it, and delight in its company and converse; nor do you depart from its teaching, until, by meditating on justice, by thinking on the circumspection of God, you can overflow with prudence. For Solomon too found wisdom; but because he overflowed less with prudence, and did not look about himself prudently enough\u2014since, namely, he did not warily provide for himself against strange women\u2014he not only lost wisdom, but rushed forward even to the final folly of idolatry (III Reg. XI). The wise men of this world too, who gazed upon the invisible things of God, understood from the creation of the world through the things that were made, seemed to have found wisdom; but because they too did not overflow with prudence, and, when they had known God, did not glorify Him as God, they themselves also became fools, and their foolish heart was darkened even unto a reprobate sense, and the disgrace of base things (Rom. I, 21-32).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Alios itaque, sicut istorum exemplo liquet, repellit a sapientia quam invenerant, superbia cordis; alios, sicut in Salomone patet, abstrahit illecebra carnis; alii autem levitate et inconstantia mentis, levi perturbatione offensi eam relinquunt, qui scilicet ad tempus credunt, et in tempore tentationis recedunt. Quare autem recedunt? Quia radices non habent, quibus teneantur? (Luc. VIII, 13, 14.) Quomodo autem radicari poterunt, nisi morentur? Quae unquam planta radicatur, nisi loco cui affixa fuerit immoretur? Sic justus plantatus in domo Domini, nec radicari nec fundari in charitate poterit, nisi mora et stabilitate loci. Si autem radicatus non fuerit, nec florere, nec fructum qui maneat, afferre poterit; et, si florere visus fuerit alicujus spei initio, dicetur de illo: Ante messem totus floruit, et immatura perfectio germinavit (Isai. XVIII, 5); et secundum alium prophetam: Si fecerit fructum, alieni comedent eum (Osee, VIII, 7). Vis autem scire quam sit necessaria stabilitas loci ut in sapientia moreris, ut radicari et fructificare possis mora temporis? Interroga Patrem tuum Benedictum, et annuntiabit tibi quia claustrum monasterii, et stabilitas in congregatione, locus idoneus sit ad ferendum omnium fere fructus virtutum, quarum longum ibidem texit catalogum (Reg. S. Bened. cap. 4). Quid enim de instabili Salomon ait? Sicut avis transmigrans de nido suo, sic vir qui relinquit locum suum (Prov. XXVII, 8). Invenerat turtur nidum ubi poneret pullos suos, coeperat eum calefacere et ab eo calefieri, venerant usque ad partum pulli, et ecce avolat, opusque coeptum imperfectum relinquit. Quare, unde, vel quo avolet, ipse viderit, vel quando tot dispendia, quae interim patitur, compensabit, vel qua ratione primam fidem irritam factam excusabit. Ego enim non putarem esse consilii, pro spe incerta, certa subire dispendia; etsi profectus quorumdam me jubeat interim a praecipitatione cohibere sententiam."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Therefore, as is clear from the example of these men, pride of heart drives some away from the wisdom they had found; others, as is plain in the case of Solomon, the allurement of the flesh drags away; while others still, by lightness and inconstancy of mind, offended by some slight disturbance, abandon it\u2014namely, those who believe for a time, and in the time of temptation fall away. But why do they fall away? Is it not because they have no roots by which they might be held fast? (Luc. VIII, 13, 14.) But how will they be able to be rooted, unless they abide? What plant was ever rooted, unless it remained in the place to which it had been fixed? So the just man, planted in the house of the Lord, will not be able to be rooted or founded in charity, except through continued residence and stability of place. But if he has not been rooted, he will not be able either to flower, or to bring forth fruit that remains; and if he has seemed to flower with some beginning of hope, it will be said of him: \"Before the harvest it flowered entirely, and an unripe perfection sprouted\" (Isai. XVIII, 5); and according to another prophet: \"If it bears fruit, strangers shall eat it\" (Osee, VIII, 7). But do you wish to know how necessary stability of place is, so that you may abide in wisdom, so that you may be able to be rooted and to bear fruit through length of time? Ask your Father Benedict, and he will declare to you that the cloister of the monastery, and stability in the community, is a place fit for bearing almost all the fruits of the virtues, of which he weaves a long catalogue in that very place (Rule of St. Benedict, ch. 4). For what does Solomon say of the unstable man? \"As a bird that wanders from her nest, so is the man who abandons his place\" (Prov. XXVII, 8). The turtledove had found a nest where she might lay her young; she had begun to warm it and to be warmed by it; the young had come even to the point of birth, and behold, she flies away, and leaves the work she had begun unfinished. Why, whence, or whither she flies away, let her see to it herself; or when she will make up for the many losses which she meanwhile suffers; or by what reasoning she will excuse her broken first fidelity. For I would not think it a matter of good counsel, for an uncertain hope, to undergo certain losses; although the progress of certain persons bids me meanwhile restrain my judgment from precipitate haste.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Enimvero multum dissimilis est causa eorum qui amore sapientiae inquieti fiunt, et eorum de quibus loqui coeperam, qui levi et frivola permoti causa a sapientia resiliunt. Sicut enim per patientiam disciplinis sapientiae quis immoratur, ut discat sapientiam; sic qui facile perdunt sustinentiam, non demorabuntur, ut scriptum est, projicere illam. Quid autem eos offendat, praemisit Scriptura, cum ait: Quasi lapidis virtus, probatio erit in illis (Eccli. VI, 22). Offenderunt in lapidem offensionis et petram scandali, cujus probata virtus corripiebat et erudiebat insipientes, probabatque mentes; cum ipsi virtutem sapientiae duritiam lapidis interpretarentur, duraque omnia, disciplinam, vultum, sermonem causarentur. Durus est, inquiunt, hic sermo (Joan. VI, 61). Esto, sermo durus est; numquid non verus est? Lapis durus est; nunquid non pretiosus est? Quare autem veritas dura est tibi, nisi propter duritiam cordis tui? si cor tibi pietate mollesceret, plus tibi soliditas veritatis quam vanitas mendacii vel oleum adulationis placeret. Durus est, aiebant, hic sermo, quia quasi lapidis virtus erat illis probatio sapientiae; ideoque non sunt demorati eam projicere, et retro abire. Nec ob aliud pretiosum et electum a Deo lapidem reprobaverunt, nisi quia durum putaverunt. Et erat quidem petra Christus, sed virtute, non duritia. Erat petra, sed quae converti posset, imo et conversa est in stagna vel fontes aquarum, ubi mollia et humilia, quibus influeret, invenit corda fidelium. Nam et isti, qui tam cito offensi quadam specie duritiae retro abierunt, si permansissent cum Apostolis, bibissent forsitan et ipsi cum eis de consequenti eos Petra, bibissent flumina aquae vivae de petra percussa in cruce fluentis largissime, ita ut hodieque bibat populus et jumenta; imo suxissent etiam mel de petra, oleumque de saxo durissimo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Indeed, the case of those who become restless out of love of wisdom is very different from that of those I had begun to speak of, who, moved by some light and frivolous cause, spring back from wisdom. For just as a man dwells patiently under the discipline of wisdom in order to learn wisdom, so those who quickly lose their endurance will not delay, as it is written, to cast it away. And Scripture has already told us what offends them, saying: \"As the virtue of a stone, so shall the trial be in them\" (Eccli. VI, 22). They stumbled against the stone of stumbling and the rock of scandal, whose proven virtue was correcting and instructing the foolish, and testing minds; while they themselves interpreted the virtue of wisdom as the hardness of a stone, and complained that everything\u2014the discipline, the countenance, the word\u2014was hard. \"This is a hard saying,\" they said (Joan. VI, 61). Granted, the word is hard; but is it not true? A stone is hard; but is it not precious? Why then is the truth hard to you, unless because of the hardness of your heart? If your heart were softened by devotion, the solidity of truth would please you more than the vanity of falsehood or the oil of flattery. \"This is a hard saying,\" they said, because the trial of wisdom was to them as it were the virtue of a stone; and therefore they did not delay to cast it away and turn back. Nor for any other reason did they reject the precious and chosen stone of God, except that they thought it hard. And indeed Christ was the Rock\u2014but by virtue, not by hardness. He was a Rock, but one that could be turned\u2014indeed, was turned\u2014into pools or fountains of water, where it found the soft and humble hearts of the faithful into which it could flow. For these men too, who so quickly took offense at a certain appearance of hardness and turned back, if they had remained with the Apostles, would perhaps have drunk themselves, with them, of the Rock that followed them; they would have drunk most abundantly of the rivers of living water flowing from the Rock struck upon the Cross, so that even today the people and the cattle drink of it; indeed, they would even have sucked honey from the rock, and oil from the hardest stone.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Prorsus tu beatus es, Simon Bar-Jona, tibi jam revelabat Pater pietatem mysterii, latentem, ut videbatur, sub duritia sermonis, qui, interrogatis duodecim, an et ipsi vellent abire, constanter respondisti: Domine, verba vitae aeternae habes; ad quem ibimus? (Joan. VI, 69.) Plane beatus, qui cum sapientia morari, et cum domesticis ejus ad mensam ipsius pane interim Sacramenti nutriri delegisti; donec de fide proficientem ad intellectum pane vitae et intellectus te cibaret, et aqua sapientiae salutaris potaret. Beati et vos, fratres mei, qui in disciplinam sapientiae, et christianae scholam philosophiae nomina dedistis, sed, si perseveranter in sapientia moremini; ut cum durior sermo ipsius visus fuerit, duriora scilicet praecipientis aut durius corripientis, non sit in aliquo vestrum cor malum incredulitatis discedendi a Deo vivo, sed constanter dicatis cum Apostolo: Verba vitae aeternae habes; ad quem ibimus? Fingis quidem laborem in praecepto (Psal. XCIII, 20), duritiam in sermone; sed scimus quia magna est multitudo dulcedinis tuae, Domine, quam abscondisti timentibus te (Psal. XXX, 20), perficies sperantibus in te. Ego autem semper sperabo, etiamsi occideris me. Imo tunc amplius sperabo, cum flagellaveris, secueris, usseris, occideris quidquid vivebat in me; ut non ego, sed Christus vivat in me. Omnino non discedimus a te, quia vivificabis nos etiam occidens, sanabis percutiens. Vere beatus qui cum sapientia moratur, ac constantia et fide longanimiter patiens et fideliter obediens usque ad mortem, nec deserens locum suum, quotiescunque spiritus potestatem habentis ascenderit super eum, sciens quia curatio disciplinae faciet cessare maxima peccata (Eccle. X, 4)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Truly you are blessed, Simon Bar-Jona; to you already the Father was revealing the graciousness of the mystery hidden, as it seemed, beneath the hardness of the word\u2014you who, when the twelve were asked whether they too wished to go away, answered steadfastly: \"Lord, thou hast the words of eternal life; to whom shall we go?\" (Joan. VI, 69.). Truly blessed are you, who chose to dwell with wisdom, and to be nourished for a time at her table among her household with the bread of the Sacrament; until, as you advanced from faith to understanding, she fed you with the bread of life and understanding, and gave you to drink the water of saving wisdom. Blessed also are you, my brothers, who have given your names to the discipline of wisdom and to the Christian school of philosophy\u2014but only if you dwell perseveringly in wisdom; so that when her word seems harder\u2014that is, when her precepts are harder, or her correction more severe\u2014there may not be in any of you an evil heart of unbelief, departing from the living God, but that you may say steadfastly with the Apostle: \"Thou hast the words of eternal life; to whom shall we go?\" You indeed fashion labor in your precept (Psal. XCIII, 20), hardness in your word; but we know that great is the multitude of your sweetness, O Lord, which you have hidden for those who fear you (Psal. XXX, 20), and which you will perfect for those who hope in you. And I will always hope, even if you should slay me. Indeed, I will hope the more when you have scourged, cut, burned, and slain whatever was living in me; so that it is not I, but Christ who lives in me. We will in no way depart from you, for you will give us life even by killing us, and heal us even by striking us. Truly blessed is the one who dwells with wisdom, and who, with constancy and faith, patiently enduring and faithfully obeying even unto death, does not desert his place, as often as the spirit of the one having power rises up against him, knowing that \"the remedy of discipline shall make the greatest sins to cease\" (Eccle. X, 4).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sane ad hanc sapientiam morandi in sapientia, illud praecipue pertinere arbitror, ut inquietudo vel quaelibet levis molestia non facile quodcunque opus sapientiae nobis excutiat, verbi gratia, solemnem psalmodiam, orationem, lectionem divinam, pensum operis diurni, aut silentii disciplinam. Quia enim sapientia in exitu canitur: Gaudebunt, ait sanctus, labia mea, cum cantavero tibi Psal LXX, 23), dum etiam ad litteram, exitus matutini et vespere delectabis (Psal. LXIV, 9.) De oratione quoque certum et ipsi quotidie capitis experimentum, quod melior est finis orationis quam principium; ut fidele probetis dominicum illud toties inculcatum, tot exemplis commendatum de orationis perseverantia consilium. Jam legere et negligere, vel, antequam ceperis librum, de manibus projicere, quem fructum, putas, tibi poterit parere? Si Scripturae immoratus non fueris, ut assiduitate studii ei fias familiaris, quando, putas, se tibi revelabit? Qui habet, inquit, amorem verbi, dabitur ei intellectus, et abundabit; qui vero non habet, et quod habet per naturam, auferetur ab eo (Matth. XIII, 12; XXV, 29) propter negligentiam. De opere autem manuum, nonne et de isto satis comperistis quia sicut denarius operariis, sic consolatio saepius servatur in fine operis? De silentio quippe nota est promissio prophetica, quia in silentio et spe erit, inquit, fortitudo vestra (Isai. XXX, 15). Si enim colas justitiam in silentio, et, sicut Jeremias bonum esse ait, praestoleris cum silentio salutare Domini; in medio silentio secretus tibi illabetur a regalibus sedibus omnipotens sermo, et aquae Siloe, quae vadunt cum silentio, convallem quieti ac placidi pectoris grato rigabunt profluvio. Hoc non semel, sed saepius experieris, si modo silentium tuum cultus justitiae sit (Isai. XXXII, 17), id est, si in justitia mediteris, ut perseveres in Scriptura quam proposui, et in sensu cogites circumspectionem Dei."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Indeed, I think this belongs especially to that wisdom of abiding in wisdom: that restlessness, or any slight annoyance, should not easily shake us loose from whatever work of wisdom we are engaged in\u2014for example, the solemn chanting of the psalms, prayer, divine reading, the task of the day's work, or the discipline of silence. For wisdom is sung at its conclusion: 'My lips shall rejoice,' says the saint, 'when I shall sing to thee' Psal LXX, 23); as also, according to the letter, 'thou shalt make the outgoings of the morning and the evening to be joyful' (Psal. LXIV, 9.). Concerning prayer also, you yourselves receive daily a sure proof that the end of prayer is better than its beginning; so that you may prove faithful that counsel of the Lord, so often urged, commended by so many examples, concerning perseverance in prayer. Now what fruit, do you think, can it bear you, to read and then neglect what you have read, or to throw the book out of your hands almost before you have taken it up? If you do not linger over Scripture, so that by diligent study you become familiar with it, when, do you suppose, will it reveal itself to you? 'He who has,' it says, 'love of the word, to him shall understanding be given, and he shall have abundance; but he who has not, even what he has by nature shall be taken away from him' (Matth. XIII, 12; XXV, 29), on account of negligence. As for the work of the hands, have you not also learned well enough from this, that just as the wage is kept for the laborers, so consolation is more often reserved for the end of the work? Concerning silence, indeed, the prophetic promise is well known: 'In silence and in hope shall your strength be,' he says (Isai. XXX, 15). For if you cultivate justice in silence, and, as Jeremiah says it is good, wait with silence for the salvation of the Lord, then in the midst of silence the almighty Word will come down secretly to you from the royal throne, and the waters of Siloam, which flow with silence, will water the valley of a quiet and peaceful breast with their pleasant stream. This you will experience not once but often, provided only that your silence be the cultivation of justice (Isai. XXXII, 17); that is, if you meditate in justice, so that you persevere in the Scripture I have set before you, and in your understanding think upon the circumspection of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Haec meditare, in his esto, ut profectus tuus manifestus fiat. Si enim iniquitatem mediteris in cubili tuo, malitias scilicet quas malignus immittit, aut phantasias quas cor tuum parturit, aut inanes philosophias, seu fallaces theorias, quae sunt velut aegri somnia; nonne magis injustitiae quam justitiae cultus erit silentium tuum? Si ergo vis in sapientia morari, stude in justitia meditari. Concupisti, inquit, sapientiam? conserva justitiam, et Dominus praebebit illam tibi (Eccli. I, 33). Si autem quasi violente irruunt cogitationes noxiae, pone tibi virilem et fidelem custodiam, quae omni custodia servet cor tuum. Dico autem timorem Dei, qui nihil negligit, qui nullum indiscussum intrare permittit, saepius interrogans etiam angelum lucis: Noster es, an adversariorum? (Josue V, 13.) Undique enim sibi circumspicit, tanquam omni hora cogitans circumspectionem Dei, quem sine intermissione intueri ac judicare corda filiorum hominum attendit. Pulchre autem dictum est: In sensu cogitabit (Eccli. XIV, 22); quia prorsus sine sensu est et a corde excessit qui timorem Dei cogitare negligit, qui pondus tantae majestatis ac judicii sibi incumbentis non sentit. Signanter quoque circumspicere Deus dicitur, quia omnia tam praeterita quam futura habet praesentia, ut nec ista respiciat, nec illa prospiciat, sed similiter, quia simpliciter, cuncta circumspiciat; sitque illa aeternitas velut punctum, omnium media temporalium, cujus immobili simplicitati semper aequaliter praesens est circuitus et rota temporum. Hunc oculum aeternum, sine intermissione videntem et judicantem omnia, semper cogitat, suaque se cogitatione sollicitat timor Domini, qui revocat a malis non solum operibus, sed et cogitationibus; erudiens nos, ut justitiam potius meditemur; continens, ut cum sapientia moremur. Ita enim sensim fit ut qui primo coercetur timore judicii et poenae, postea pascatur amore ac meditatione justitiae, demumque requiescat ac delectetur in convictu et amplexu sapientiae. Haec non modo foras mittit timorem, diffundendo charitatem; sed etiam pellit ab animo taedium et langorem, infundendo suavitatem, sicut quidam, contubernium habens ipsius, de ea loquebatur: Intrans in domum meam conquiescam cum illa; non enim habet amaritudinem conversatio illius, nec taedium convictus illius, sed laetitiam et gaudium (Sap. VIII, 16). Horum participes nos faciat, qui particeps nostri fieri dignatus est Dei sapientia Christus Jesus, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Meditate on these things, be occupied in them, that your progress may be manifest. For if you meditate on iniquity in your bed\u2014that is, the malicious thoughts which the evil one injects, or the fancies which your own heart brings forth, or empty philosophies, or deceptive speculations, which are like the dreams of a sick man\u2014will not your silence then be the cultivation of injustice rather than of justice? If, therefore, you wish to abide in wisdom, strive to meditate in justice. 'Have you desired wisdom?' it says. 'Keep justice, and the Lord will give it to you' (Eccli. I, 33). But if harmful thoughts rush upon you as though by force, set for yourself a manly and faithful guard, which may keep your heart with all watchfulness. I mean the fear of God, which neglects nothing, which allows nothing to enter unexamined, often questioning even an angel of light: 'Art thou one of ours, or of the adversaries?' (Josue V, 13.). For it looks about on every side, as though at every hour thinking upon the circumspection of God, whom it attends as beholding without ceasing and judging the hearts of the children of men. It is beautifully said: 'He shall think with understanding' (Eccli. XIV, 22); for he is utterly without sense, and has gone out of his heart, who neglects to think upon the fear of God, who does not feel the weight of so great a majesty and of the judgment pressing upon him. God is also fittingly said to 'look around,' because he holds all things, past as well as future, as present to him, so that he neither looks back upon the one nor looks forward to the other, but looks around upon all things equally, because simply; and that eternity of his is, as it were, a point in the midst of all temporal things, to whose unmoving simplicity the circling wheel of the ages is ever equally present. Upon this eternal eye, seeing and judging all things without ceasing, the fear of the Lord continually thinks, and by its own thought disquiets itself\u2014that fear which calls us back from evils not only in deeds but also in thoughts; teaching us rather to meditate on justice, restraining us, that we may abide with wisdom. For thus it comes about by degrees that he who is first held in check by fear of judgment and punishment is afterward fed by love and meditation of justice, and at last rests and delights in the companionship and embrace of wisdom. This wisdom not only casts out fear, by pouring forth charity, but also drives weariness and listlessness from the soul, by infusing sweetness\u2014just as a certain man, who had companionship with her, spoke of her: 'Entering into my house, I will take my repose with her; for her company has no bitterness, nor her companionship any tediousness, but joy and gladness' (Sap. VIII, 16). May he make us partakers of these things, who deigned to become a partaker of us\u2014the Wisdom of God, Christ Jesus, who lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints violente in its description of harmful thoughts forcing their way in."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition omits the opening parenthesis before Psal LXX, 23, the quotation on rejoicing lips from Psalm 70:23 in historical numbering."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon I", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "wisdom", "perseverance", "monastic stability", "obedience", "prayer", "Scripture", "work", "silence", "fear of God", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De fiducia in Deum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 103-107.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Jeremiah 17:7", "Proverbs 10:6", "Genesis 27:28", "Hosea 14:6", "Isaiah 26:19", "Genesis 27:29", "Matthew 9:2, 22, 29", "Mark 11:22", "Matthew 8:26", "John 16:33", "1 John 5:4", "Psalm 124:1 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 2:11\u201312", "Matthew 27:46", "Psalm 36:25 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:31", "1 Peter 3:13", "Jeremiah 17:5", "Psalm 9:11 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 32:17\u201318", "Song of Songs 2:5 (printed locator; quotation from Song of Songs 2:3)", "Proverbs 1:33", "Job 5:17", "Revelation 3:19", "Proverbs 3:12", "Psalm 2:13 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 12:1", "Jeremiah 17:7\u20138", "Ezekiel 47:3\u201312", "Jeremiah 17:6, 8", "Joshua 15:19", "Sirach 24:41\u201342", "Isaiah 66:9", "Sirach 39:17"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Trust in God gives steadiness amid tribulation and the loss of spiritual consolation. Guerric develops the image of a tree rooted in living water through Scripture, compunction and the sacraments. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De fiducia in Deum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Trust in God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Benedictus vir qui confidit in Domino (Jerem. XVII, 7). Sanctus Pater noster gratia Benedictus et nomine, cujus memoria in benedictione est, ipse profecto fuit benedictus vir qui confisus est in Domino. Quem enim Dominus praevenit in benedictionibus dulcedinis, ut in Domino confideret, jam benedixit omni benedictione spirituali in coelestibus in Christo, quia in Domino confisus est. Nec solum in coelestibus benedictionem omnium angelorum, sed etiam in terrestribus benedictionem omnium gentium dedit illi Dominus. Ubi enim gentium non benedicetur hodie benedictus Domini Benedictus? Plane benedictio Domini super caput justi (Prov. X, 6), quem tot benedictionibus coeli et terrae gratia Dei cumulavit. Non tamen, sicut Esau, in pinguedine terrae et in rore coeli benedictio ejus (Gen. XXVII, 28); sed in pinguedine spiritus et in rore coeli, qui per prophetam loquitur: Ero quasi ros (Osee XIV, 6), et cui dicitur: Ros lucis, ros tuus (Isai. XXVI, 19). In Christo utique benedictio Patris, cui Pater imprecatus est: Qui benedixerit tibi, benedictionibus repleatur (Gen. XXVII, 29). Merito proinde benedictus in Domino vir qui confidit in Domino, quia qui in Domino fidit, in eo se ipsum figit. Ubi autem arbor radicem figit, inde succum vitae atque humorem pinguedinis bibit. Plane ad humorem radices misit, qui, ut verbis utar Benedicti magistri, spem suam Deo commisit, ac de ipso fonte summi boni aquas vitae totius benedictionis et gratiae bibit (Reg. S. Bened. cap. 4)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed is the man who trusts in the Lord (Jerem. XVII, 7). Our holy Father, Benedict both in grace and in name, whose memory is in benediction, was indeed himself a blessed man who trusted in the Lord. For him whom the Lord went before with the blessings of sweetness, so that he might trust in the Lord, the Lord had already blessed with every spiritual blessing in the heavenly places in Christ, because he trusted in the Lord. And the Lord gave him not only, in heavenly things, the blessing of all the angels, but also, in earthly things, the blessing of all nations. For in what nation today is Benedict, the Lord's blessed one, not blessed? Truly the blessing of the Lord is upon the head of the just man (Prov. X, 6), whom the grace of God has heaped with so many blessings of heaven and earth. Yet his blessing was not, like Esau's, in the fatness of the earth and the dew of heaven (Gen. XXVII, 28); but in the fatness of the spirit and the dew of heaven, who speaks through the prophet: I will be as the dew (Osee XIV, 6), and to whom it is said: The dew of light is your dew (Isai. XXVI, 19). In Christ indeed is the blessing of the Father, for whom the Father invoked this blessing: May he who blesses you be filled with blessings (Gen. XXVII, 29). Rightly, then, is the man blessed in the Lord who trusts in the Lord, because he who trusts in the Lord fixes himself in him. Now where a tree fixes its root, from there it drinks the sap of life and the moisture of richness. Clearly he sent his roots toward that moisture who, to use the words of our master Benedict, 'committed his hope to God,' and drank from that very fount of the highest good the waters of life of all blessing and grace (Rule of St. Benedict, ch. 4).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Per hanc nempe piam et fidelem fiduciam remittuntur peccata; sanitatum corporalium, sed magis spiritualium, obtinentur remedia; propulsantur pericula; terrores contemnuntur; vincitur mundus; omnia denique possibilia credenti redduntur. Illi quippe qui in peccatis erat: Confide, ait, fili, remittuntur tibi peccata tua. Illis quibus donabatur sanitas corporum vel animarum: Secundum fidem tuam fiat tibi; et: Fides tua te salvum fecit (Matth. IX, 2, 29, 22). Illis, qui periculo terrebantur naufragandi: Habete, inquit, fidem Dei (Marc. XI, 22); et: Quid timidi estis, modicae fidei? (Matth. VIII, 26.) Illis quos armabat adversus saevitiam mundi furoresque diaboli: Confidite, ait; ego vici mundum (Joan. XVI, 33). Et utique haec est victoria quae vincit mundum, fides nostra (I Joan. V, 4), si modo non sit tepida, non trepida; sed sit fiducia, id est fides non ficta, seu spes non infirma. Non solum autem mundus per eam vincitur, sed et coelum possidetur, super aeternitatem homo stabilitur, et in Deo ipso per charitatem radicatur et fundatur. Qui enim confidit in Domino, est sicut mons Sion; non commovebitur in aeternum, qui fundatus est super Aeternum (Psal. CXXIV, 1). Tam enim perire non potest, quam nec ille cui adhaerens unus cum ipso spiritus est. Quis enim speravit in Domino, et confusus est, permansit in mandatis illius, et derelictus est? (Eccli. II, 11, 12.) Si dixerit infidelis, Ille derelictus erat qui in cruce clamabat: Deus, Deus meus, utquid me dereliquisti? (Matth. XXVII, 46.) puto quia non usquequaque dereliquit, in quo erat mundum reconcilians sibi. O quanta consolatio, sic desolari; quanta dilectio, sic derelinqui, ut Unico Patris unice dilecto merearis vel in passionibus consociari!"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. By this devout and faithful trust, indeed, sins are forgiven; remedies are obtained for bodily health, but even more for spiritual health; dangers are driven off; terrors are held in contempt; the world is overcome; and finally, all things become possible to the one who believes. For to the one who was in sins, he said: Have confidence, son, your sins are forgiven you. To those to whom health of body or soul was granted: According to your faith be it done to you; and: Your faith has made you whole (Matth. IX, 2, 29, 22). To those who were terrified by the danger of shipwreck, he said: Have the faith of God (Marc. XI, 22); and: Why are you fearful, O you of little faith? (Matth. VIII, 26.) To those whom he armed against the savagery of the world and the ragings of the devil, he said: Have confidence; I have overcome the world (Joan. XVI, 33). And indeed this is the victory that overcomes the world, our faith (I Joan. V, 4), provided only that it be not lukewarm, not fearful; but be trust\u2014that is, faith not feigned, or hope not weak. Not only is the world overcome through it, but heaven is also possessed, man is made stable upon eternity, and he is rooted and grounded in God himself through charity. For he who trusts in the Lord is like Mount Sion; he shall not be moved forever, who is founded upon the Eternal One (Psal. CXXIV, 1). For he can no more perish than can the one to whom, clinging to him, he is made one spirit with him. For who has hoped in the Lord, and been confounded? Who has remained in his commandments, and been forsaken? (Eccli. II, 11, 12.) Should the unbeliever say: He was forsaken who cried out on the cross: My God, my God, why have you forsaken me? (Matth. XXVII, 46.) \u2014 I think that he did not altogether forsake him, in whom he was reconciling the world to himself. O how great a consolation, thus to be desolated; how great a love, thus to be forsaken, that you might merit to be associated even in his sufferings with the Only-begotten of the Father, the uniquely beloved!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Aperi, Domine, oculos hujus pueri, rudem loquor et novitium, cui, cum tribulatur, putat se derelictum. Novum hoc omnino et insolitum, si justum quempiam viderem derelictum, cum Ecclesia dicat: Junior fui, etenim senui, et non vidi justum derelictum (Psal. XXXVI, 25). Aperi, inquam, o Domine, oculos pueri tui, ut videat quia plures nobiscum sunt quam cum adversariis. Siquidem Dominus virtutum nobiscum, et cum eo omnis virtus et militia coeli, imo favor omnis creaturae, servientis nutui et arbitrio Creatoris sui. Si enim Deus pro nobis, quis contra nos? (Rom. VIII, 31.) et quis est qui nobis noceat, si boni aemulatores fuerimus? (I Petr. III, 13.) Poterit invidus ille saevire; sed hoc erit servire. Poterit urere; tundere, dissecare; sed hoc erit coronas fabricare. Diligam te, Domine, fortitudo mea, per quem mihi vermiculo succumbit omnis fortitudo contraria, et illuditur ab angelis tuis, in ministerium nostrum missis, antiqui draconis astutia, ut, volens nocere, prosit. Diligam te, Domine, propugnator potens, gubernator prudens, consolator clemens, remunerator affluens. Securus in eum projicio omnem sollicitudinem meam, cujus nec potentia vinci, nec sapientia falli, nec benevolentia potest fatigari quominus expleat omnem indigentiam meam. Quam satius, quam securius est ipsum esse sollicitum, quam me ipsum pro me! quam bonum est confidere in Domino, quam in homine! quam jure maledictus qui confidit in homine, carnem ponit brachium suum, et recedit a Domino cor ejus! (Jerem. XVII, 5.) et quidem egenus et pauper ego sum; sed si Dominus sollicitus est mei, dives sum, beatus sum; cui nimirum omnia cooperantur in bonum. Sperent igitur in te qui noverunt nomen tuum, quoniam non derelinquis sperantes in te, Domine (Psal. IX, 11); sedeatque populus tuus, sicut scriptum est, in pulchritudine pacis, in tabernaculis fiduciae, in requie opulenta, in securitate sempiterna (Isai. XXXII, 18, 17). Omnino pulchra pax et securitas sempiterna, habitare in adjutorio Altissimi, in protectione Dei coeli commorari. Prorsus requies opulenta, sedere feriatum sub vite vera, ficu et oliva; et post saturitatem variorum fructuum, se ipsum mulcere carmine amatorio, ac dicere: Sub umbra illius quam desiderabam [Vulg. quem desideraveram] sedi, et fructus illius dulcis gutturi meo? (Cant. II, 5.) Plane dulcis gutturi fructus de cujus gustu tam dulcis erumpit ructus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Open, Lord, the eyes of this child\u2014I speak of one unformed and a novice\u2014who, when he is afflicted, thinks himself abandoned. This would be altogether new and unheard of, were I to see any just man abandoned, since the Church says: \"I was young, and now I am old, and I have not seen the just man abandoned\" (Psal. XXXVI, 25). Open, I say, O Lord, the eyes of your servant, that he may see that there are more with us than with our adversaries. For the Lord of hosts is with us, and with him every power and the army of heaven, indeed the favor of every creature, serving the nod and will of its Creator. For if God is for us, who is against us? (Rom. VIII, 31.) And who is there to harm us, if we are zealous for what is good? (I Petr. III, 13.) That envious one may rage; but this will be to serve us. He may burn, beat, cut apart; but this will be to fashion crowns. I will love you, Lord, my strength, through whom every opposing strength yields to me, a mere worm, and the cunning of the ancient dragon is mocked by your angels, sent for our service, so that, wishing to do harm, it profits us. I will love you, Lord, mighty defender, prudent governor, gentle comforter, abundant rewarder. Secure, I cast all my anxiety upon him, whose power cannot be conquered, whose wisdom cannot be deceived, whose benevolence cannot be wearied from supplying all my need. How much better, how much more secure it is that he himself be anxious for me, than that I should be anxious for myself! How good it is to trust in the Lord, rather than in man! How justly cursed is he who trusts in man, and makes flesh his arm, and whose heart withdraws from the Lord! (Jerem. XVII, 5.) And indeed I am needy and poor; but if the Lord is anxious for me, I am rich, I am blessed; for to such a one, surely, all things work together for good. Let those, then, hope in you who know your name, for you do not abandon those who hope in you, O Lord (Psal. IX, 11); and let your people sit, as it is written, in the beauty of peace, in the tabernacles of confidence, in opulent rest, in everlasting security (Isai. XXXII, 18, 17). Truly it is a beautiful peace and an everlasting security, to dwell in the help of the Most High, to abide under the protection of the God of heaven. Truly it is an opulent rest, to sit at leisure beneath the true vine, the fig tree, and the olive; and, after being filled with manifold fruits, to soothe oneself with a love-song, and to say: \"Under his shadow, which I desired [Vulg.: whom I had desired], I sat, and his fruit was sweet to my throat\" (Cant. II, 5.). Truly sweet to the throat is that fruit, from the tasting of which so sweet an eructation breaks forth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Haec sunt ergo tabernacula fiduciae, in quibus verus Israel habitat confidenter, pascit et cubat, et non est qui exterreat; et, sicut Sapientia promittit, Requiescet absque terrore, abundantia perfruetur, malorum timore sublato (Prov. I, 33). Et bene timore malorum sublato, non timore Domini; ne fiducia ista et securitate, quam commendamus, negligentiam putes generari, cum non in alio quam in timore Domini sit fiducia fortitudinis. Timor quippe, dum cavet offensam, custodit gratiam; locumque servat fiduciae, quandiu tibi conscius non es Dominum offendisse. Inde fit ut nonnisi caste timeas eum; praeter eum nullatenus timeas alienum. Sola ergo conscientia bona fiduciam istam praesumit, suadet corda nostra esse in conspectu Judicis aeterni. Quis enim bene sibi de illo promittat, a quo se minime diligi intelligat? Qui autem diligit, nunquam diffidit, sciens quia is quem corda non fallunt, diligentes se diligit, etiam cum arguit et corripit. Siquidem eruditis corde in sapientia non aufert, sed auget fiduciam Patris disciplina, scientes scriptum, Beatus qui corripitur a Domino (Job V, 17), et: Ego quos amo arguo et castigo (Apoc. III, 19; Prov. III, 12). Cum igitur exarserit in brevi ira ejus, beati omnes qui confidunt in eo (Psal. II, 13), quos scilicet amoris proprii conscientia consolatur, quia, cum iratus fuerit, misericordiae recordabitur; et, cum dixerint: Confitebor tibi, Domine, quoniam iratus es mihi, statim subsequentur: Conversus est furor tuus, et consolatus es me (Isa. XII, 1), eo quod nonnisi in brevi exarserit ira ejus, quam tam cito placaverit confessio reatus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. These, then, are the tabernacles of confidence, in which the true Israel dwells securely, feeds and lies down, and there is none to terrify him; and, as Wisdom promises, \"He shall rest without terror, and shall enjoy abundance, the fear of evils being removed\" (Prov. I, 33). And rightly is it \"the fear of evils\" that is removed, not the fear of the Lord; lest you should think that this confidence and security which we commend breeds negligence, since the confidence of strength lies in nothing other than the fear of the Lord. For fear, while it guards against giving offense, preserves grace, and keeps a place open for confidence, so long as you are not conscious of having offended the Lord. Hence it follows that you should fear him alone chastely; and besides him you should in no way fear any other. It is a good conscience alone, then, that lays claim to this confidence, persuading our hearts that they stand in the sight of the eternal Judge. For who could rightly promise himself good from one by whom he understands himself to be in no way loved? But he who loves never distrusts, knowing that he whom hearts cannot deceive loves those who love him, even when he rebukes and corrects them. Indeed, for those whose hearts are instructed in wisdom, the Father's discipline does not take away but increases confidence, since they know it is written: \"Blessed is the man whom the Lord corrects\" (Job V, 17), and: \"Those whom I love, I rebuke and chastise\" (Apoc. III, 19; Prov. III, 12). When, therefore, his anger has been kindled but for a short while, blessed are all they who trust in him (Psal. II, 13), namely those whom the awareness of their own love consoles, because, when he has been angry, he will remember mercy; and when they shall have said: \"I will give thanks to you, O Lord, for you were angry with me,\" there shall at once follow: \"Your wrath is turned away, and you have comforted me\" (Isa. XII, 1), because his anger was kindled only for a short while, which the confession of guilt so quickly appeased.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Merito igitur benedictum virum qui confidit in Domino ligno comparat Jeremias quod transplantatur super aquas: quod quia ad humorem amoris mittit radices cordis, non timebit cum venerit aestus irae et tribulationis; sed et in tempore siccitatis, cum multo tempore coelum clausum fuerit, nec ros nec pluvia gratiae super eum descenderit, non erit tamen sollicitum, quasi Deus eum projecerit (Jerem. XVII, 7, 8). Sentit quippe se plantatum fide, radicatum charitate super aquas vitae, quae, secundum Ezechielem, de sanctuario egredientes vivificant omnia, et habent in utraque ripa omne lignum pomiferum, cujus folium non defluit, nec fructus deficit (Ezech. XLVII, 3-12). Cur igitur lignum illud benedictum aut timeret aestum, aut de siccitate esset sollicitum, cui aqua viva, id est Spiritus sancti gratia, vitalem in occulto succum spei et charitatis subministrare non desinit; unde et folium sit viride, id est Verbum plenum gratiae et veritatis; et ipsum non desinat aliquando facere fructum quodcunque opus pietatis? Et grata quidem illa verna temperies, pacis et laetitiae; desiderabilis illa pluvia voluntaria, quam segregavit Deus haereditati suae; sed, si ita necesse est, adurat omnia aestus tribulationis, veniant iterum quaecunque prophetavit Jeremias de sermonibus siccitatis spiritualis; non tamen timebit, quem fiducia Domini radicavit super aquam refectionis (Jerem. XVII, 6, 8), id est gratiam Spiritus sancti; quae etsi manifeste non pluat, ut sensibiliter influat, occulte tamen interius vivificat et fecundat; quandiu videlicet fidem propositi conservat, ad perseverandum confortat, verbumque irreprehensible et opus stabile subministrat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Rightly, then, does Jeremiah compare the blessed man who trusts in the Lord to a tree that is transplanted beside the waters: because it sends the roots of its heart into the moisture of love, it will not fear when the heat of wrath and tribulation comes; but even in time of drought, when the sky has long been shut up, and neither dew nor rain of grace has descended upon it, it will nevertheless not be anxious, as though God had cast it off (Jerem. XVII, 7, 8). For it feels itself planted by faith, rooted by charity, upon the waters of life, which, according to Ezekiel, going forth from the sanctuary, give life to all things, and have on either bank every fruit-bearing tree, whose leaf does not fall nor its fruit fail (Ezech. XLVII, 3-12). Why, then, should that blessed tree either fear the heat, or be anxious about drought, since the living water\u2014that is, the grace of the Holy Spirit\u2014does not cease to supply it, in secret, with the vital sap of hope and charity; whence its leaf also is green, that is, the Word full of grace and truth; and it itself never at any time ceases to bear fruit, whatever work of piety it may be? Pleasant indeed is that springtime mildness of peace and joy; desirable is that bountiful rain which God has set apart for his inheritance; but if it must needs be so, let the heat of tribulation scorch everything, let there come again whatever Jeremiah prophesied concerning words of spiritual drought; still he will not fear, whom trust in the Lord has rooted upon the water of refreshment (Jerem. XVII, 6, 8), that is, the grace of the Holy Spirit; which, even if it does not rain down openly so as to flow in perceptibly, nevertheless secretly gives life and fruitfulness within\u2014so long, that is, as it preserves the faith of one's purpose, strengthens him to persevere, and supplies a blameless word and a stable work.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. In hujusmodi vestra, fratres, sit consolatio, quoties alias consolationes non modo carnales, sed et spirituales subtraxerit vobis non negligentia vestra, sed dispensatio divina. Et fortasse quidem illa, quam diximus, occulta gratia Spiritus irriguum est inferius, quo nimirum Axa non vult esse contenta, nisi adjiciatur ei et irriguum superius (Josue XV, 19); ut scilicet de excelso effundatur Spiritus, rorent coeli desuper, et nubes angelicae pluant justum, justificans Dei verbum, loquens ad cor Jerusalem. Et bene illud irriguum inferius, quod quasi ad radices fluens nutrit humilitatem; hoc autem superius, quod sese desuper effundens, spe et exsultatione attollit mentem. Si ergo et vos, fratres, ad illud suspiratis superius irriguum, laudabile quidem desiderium; si tamen nondum attingitis, mittite radices interim ad humorem, salubre remedium. Qui igitur non praevalet capere laetitiam contemplativae, meditetur justitiam vitae activae. Inde radices affectionum impinguet, dulcoret mores, omnemque vitae statum informet, quatenus in ipso nec marcescat aut defluat folium, id est verbum leviter aut inutiliter prolatum, nec aliquando vita desinat facere fructum. Benedictum lignum, cujus folium ad medicinam, fructus ad vitam; id est, cujus sermo gratiam parit audienti, et opus vitam facienti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Let your consolation, brothers, be of this kind, whenever other consolations\u2014not only carnal but also spiritual\u2014are withdrawn from you, not through your own negligence, but by divine dispensation. And perhaps indeed that hidden grace of the Spirit of which we spoke is the lower watering, with which Axa surely does not wish to be content, unless the upper watering also be added to her (Josue XV, 19); so that, namely, the Spirit may be poured out from on high, the heavens may drop dew from above, and the clouds of angels may rain down the Just One, the justifying Word of God, speaking to the heart of Jerusalem. And rightly is that called the lower watering, which, flowing as it were to the roots, nourishes humility; but this the upper watering, which, pouring itself out from above, lifts up the mind with hope and exultation. If, then, you too, brothers, sigh after that upper watering, it is indeed a praiseworthy desire; yet if you do not yet attain it, in the meantime send your roots down to the moisture\u2014a wholesome remedy. Let him, then, who cannot grasp the joy of the contemplative life, meditate upon the righteousness of the active life. From this let him fatten the roots of his affections, sweeten his habits, and shape his whole manner of life, so that in him the leaf may neither wither nor fall away\u2014that is, no word spoken lightly or uselessly\u2014nor may his life ever cease to bear fruit. Blessed is the tree whose leaf is for healing and whose fruit is for life; that is, whose speech brings forth grace for the hearer, and whose work brings forth life for the one who does it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Ad hoc profecto vos, fratres mei, quos carnalis generatio vel consuetudo saecularis plantaverat in terra inaquosa, in terra salsuginis, divina generatio vel mutatio dexterae Excelsi transplantavit super aquas refectionis; ut quibus prius propter sterilitatem parabatur securis et ignis, nunc plantati in domo Domini, in atriis domus Dei nostri, floreatis, et fructum faciatis, et fructus vester maneat. An non aquae refectionis Scripturae Spiritus sancti, in quibus meditamur die ac nocte? an non aquae refectionis, lacrymae compunctionis, quae nobis factae sunt panes die ac nocte? an non aquae refectionis, Sacramenta et subsidia nostrae salutis, quibus pascimur et potamur in altari? In his siquidem omnibus, quasi quibusdam rivulis, fons sapientiae qui oritur in medio paradisi, derivatur foris, et in plateis aquas suas dividit. Ego, inquit Sapientia, quasi aquaeductus exivi de paradiso. Dixi: Rigabo hortum meum plantationum, inebriabo partus mei fructum (Eccli. XXIV, 41, 42). Ecce, ut colligere potestis ex ipsius ore Sapientiae parientis et plantantis, hortus plantationum est congregatio filiorum. Numquid enim ego, qui alios parere facio, ipse non pariam, dicit Dominus? (Isai. LXVI, 9.) Ipse parit, cum ingenerat voluntatem bonam; plantat, cum instituit vitam; rigat, cum sensibus infundit gratiam; colit, cum moribus adhibet disciplinam. Audite me, divini fructus, et quasi rosa plantata super rivos aquarum fructificate (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). Mittite radices ad humorem vitae, id est ad amorem terrae viventium, non hujus in qua omnia senescunt et putrescunt. Non potest arbor facere fructum qui permaneat, nisi sursum in coelestibus radicem figat, ut quae sursum sunt, non quae super terram, quaerat et sapiat. Quod enim physici dicunt hominem esse arborem inversam, eo quod nervi corporis radicem et initium in vertice habeant, ego illo potius sensu interpretor, quod radicem amoris et desiderii figere debeat in coelo, in summo vertice rerum capite nostro Jesu Christo. Qui illuc radices miserit, et de illo fonte aeterno succum vitae et gratiae jugiter biberit, non timebit cum venerit aestus judicii; sed afferens et offerens fructum quem fecit plurimum, id remunerationis accipiet ut floreat in aeternum ante Dominum, cui est honor et gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. For this very purpose, my brothers, you whom carnal generation or worldly custom had planted in a waterless land, in a land of saltiness, the divine generation or the change wrought by the right hand of the Most High has transplanted upon the waters of refreshment; so that you, for whom formerly, because of barrenness, the axe and the fire were being prepared, now, planted in the house of the Lord, in the courts of the house of our God, may flourish, and bear fruit, and your fruit may remain. Are not the waters of refreshment the Scriptures of the Holy Spirit, on which we meditate day and night? Are not the waters of refreshment the tears of compunction, which have become bread for us day and night? Are not the waters of refreshment the Sacraments and supports of our salvation, by which we are fed and given drink at the altar? In all these, as it were through certain little streams, the fountain of wisdom which rises in the midst of paradise is channeled abroad, and divides its waters in the streets. 'I,' says Wisdom, 'came forth from paradise like a watercourse. I said: I will water my garden of plantings, I will make drunk the fruit of my offspring' (Eccli. XXIV, 41, 42). See, as you can gather from the very mouth of Wisdom bringing forth and planting, the garden of plantings is the congregation of her sons. 'For shall not I, who make others to bring forth, myself also bring forth?' says the Lord (Isai. LXVI, 9.). He himself brings forth, when he begets a good will; he plants, when he establishes a life; he waters, when he pours grace into the senses; he cultivates, when he applies discipline to conduct. 'Hear me, divine fruits, and bear fruit like the rose planted beside streams of water' (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). Send down your roots to the moisture of life, that is, to the love of the land of the living, not of this land in which all things grow old and rot. A tree cannot bear fruit that will endure unless it fixes its root above, in the heavenly places, so that it may seek and savor the things that are above, not the things that are upon the earth. For as the natural philosophers say that man is an inverted tree, because the nerves of the body have their root and beginning in the crown of the head, I interpret this rather in this sense: that he ought to fix the root of love and desire in heaven, in the highest crown of all things, our head, Jesus Christ. Whoever sends his roots there, and continually drinks from that eternal fountain the sap of life and grace, will not fear when the heat of judgment comes; but bringing and offering the fruit that he has produced in abundance, he will receive this as his reward: that he may flourish forever before the Lord, to whom be honor and glory through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quotation on sitting under the beloved's shadow and tasting his fruit comes from Song of Songs 2:3; the edition prints Cant. II, 5."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon II", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "trust in God", "faith", "hope", "charity", "tribulation", "consolation", "Holy Spirit", "Scripture", "compunction", "sacraments"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De amicitia Dei prae omnibus quaerenda., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 107-111.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Sirach 45:1", "2 Corinthians 12:9", "Sirach 9:1\u20132 (printed locator; quotation from Ecclesiastes 9:1-2)", "1 Kings 2:6\u20137 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Samuel)", "Romans 8:38\u201339", "Galatians 2:20", "1 Corinthians 9:27", "2 Corinthians 12:7", "Isaiah 28:18", "John 15:14", "Psalm 88:23 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 52:6 (historical numbering)", "John 17:1", "Romans 12:17", "Luke 6:22", "Galatians 1:10", "Amos 5:10", "Micah 7:6", "Galatians 4:16", "Song of Songs 1:5", "Romans 12:21", "Wisdom 7:30", "2 Corinthians 7:21 (printed locator; quotation from 2 Corinthians 8:21)"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric places friendship with God before human favor. He considers humility, charity, reputation and fraternal correction, including the hostility that truthful correction can provoke. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De amicitia Dei prae omnibus quaerenda."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On seeking the friendship of God above all things.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Dilectus a Deo et hominibus (Eccli. XLV, 1). Paucis expressit bonum ac beatum virum, qualem nimirum constat dilectum Domini fuisse Benedictum. Paucis, inquam, comprehendit summam perfectionis, plenitudinem gratiae et virtutis, simulque beatitudinem vitae perennis, et consolationem praesentis. Quid enim deesse potest ad aeternam felicitatem illi qui a Deo diligitur? quid ad praesentem consolationem, illi qui ab hominibus diligitur? Illi nempe qui a Deo diligitur, etsi aliquid deesse videtur, non ob aliud deest, nisi ut nihil ei desit, et suo, ut putatur, defectu perfectior sit. Nam virtus in infirmitate perficitur. Paule, ait Dominus, sufficit tibi gratia mea (II Cor. XII, 9). Cui gratia Dei sufficit, sine damno gravi, imo cum lucro non mediocri aliquid gratiae deficit, quandoquidem ipse defectus et infirmitas virtutem perficit; et imminutio cujusdam gratiae, summam omnium gratiam Dei ampliorem ei et stabiliorem efficit. Longe fac, Domine, a servis tuis gratiam illam, quaecunque sit, quae adimere aut imminuere gratiam tui possit; unde videlicet aliquis, gloriosior in oculis suis, odiosior fiat in oculis tuis. Non est illa gratia, sed ira, illis plane quibus iratus es dari digna; quibus propter dolos posuisti talia, dejiciens eos dum allevantur, et elidens valide, dum super ventum ponuntur. Ut igitur illa sola gratia, sine qua nullus a te diligitur, salva nobis maneat, omnem aliam gratiam tua nobis gratia subtrahat, aut certe gratiam utendi simul conferat; quatenus habentes gratiam, per quam serviamus placentes cum metu et reverentia, de gratia muneris gratiam promereamur remuneratoris; et quanto quis fuerit gratiosior, tanto tibi fiat gratior."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Beloved of God and men\" (Eccli. XLV, 1). In few words he has expressed the good and blessed man, such as Benedict is known indeed to have been, the beloved of the Lord. In few words, I say, he has comprehended the sum of perfection, the fullness of grace and virtue, and at the same time the blessedness of life everlasting and the consolation of the present life. For what can be lacking to eternal felicity in him who is loved by God? What can be lacking to present consolation in him who is loved by men? Indeed, for him who is loved by God, even if something seems to be lacking, it is lacking for no other reason than that nothing may be lacking to him, and that by his seeming defect he may be made more perfect. For virtue is perfected in weakness. \"Paul,\" says the Lord, \"My grace is sufficient for thee\" (II Cor. XII, 9). To him for whom the grace of God suffices, some grace is lacking without grave loss\u2014indeed with no small gain\u2014since the very defect and weakness perfects virtue; and the diminishing of a certain grace makes God's grace, the highest of all graces, fuller and more stable for him. Keep far, O Lord, from Thy servants that grace, whatever it be, which could take away or diminish Thy grace; whereby, namely, someone, more glorious in his own eyes, becomes more hateful in Thine eyes. That is not grace but wrath, truly worthy to be given to those with whom Thou art angry; to whom, on account of deceits, Thou hast appointed such things, casting them down while they are being raised up, and dashing them violently while they are set upon the wind. Therefore, that that grace alone, without which none is loved by Thee, may remain safe to us, let Thy grace withdraw from us every other grace, or at least grant together with it the grace of using it rightly; so that, having the grace by which we may serve, pleasing with fear and reverence, we may merit from the grace of the gift the grace of the Rewarder; and the more gracious anyone is, the more may he become pleasing to Thee.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sane mirabili arte, sed mirabiliori charitate, prudens clementia clemensque prudentia Dei, saluti consuluit humanae, ut, cum cunctos diligat, non tamen facile de dilectione sua certos aut securos esse sinat; nec ob aliud magnam multitudinem dulcedinis suae timentibus se abscondat, nisi ut, dum semper custodit humiles, semper habeat amabiles. Sunt enim justi et sapientes, et opera eorum in manu Dei, et tamen nescit homo utrum amore an odio dignus sit; sed omnia in futurum servantur incerta (Eccli. IX, 1, 2) Moderator nempe omnium sic eis dispensat gratiam munerum et operum, ut ex quibusdam quae concedit, consolationem quod digni sint amore tribuat; ex quibusdam quae subtrahit, suspicionem ac metum quod odio digni sint injiciat. Consolatur, cum diluculo visitat; terret, cum subito probat. Nunc mortificat, nunc vivificat; nunc deducit ad inferos, nunc reducit. Nunc pauperem facit et ditat, nunc humiliat et sublevat (I Reg. II, 6, 7). Et in tanta vicissitudinum varietate tanto certius salutem nostram operatur, quanto nos incertiores de ea, cum majore metu ac tremore ei cooperamur. Ait quidem Paulus: Certus sum quia nihil me separabit a charitate Dei (Rom. VIII, 38, 39). Sed vere Paulus hic erat, non ego aut tu, quibus omnia in futurum servari expedit incerta. Paulus, inquam, hic erat qui hoc dicebat, cui nimirum et illud dicere competebat: Vivo jam non ego, vivit vero in me Christus (Galat. II, 20), qui scilicet ita totus in quemdam divinum conversus erat affectum, ut adhaerens Domino unus cum ipso esset spiritus. Vide tamen hunc ipsum Paulum, qui ita modo certus erat, alias trepidum ac sollicitum, et castigantem corpus suum, ne forte, cum aliis praedicaverit, ipse reprobus efficiatur (I Cor. IX, 27): vide etiam colaphizari a Satana (II Cor. XII, 7), ne forte extollatur. In ipso itaque Paulo jam tibi est videre quomodo omnis certitudo quae ad horam confortatur et convalescit de spiritus consolatione, item infirmatur et evanescit superveniente tentatione. Ego igitur miser et ipsa indignus vita, qua consolatione, qua fiducia me dignum esse amore praesumam, cui omnia et interiora et exteriora manifestum odium denuntiant; cujus vita, non tentatio, non militia est adversus peccatum, sed ultronea servitus peccati; qui tam mente quam carne servio legi peccati; qui cum prudentia carnis et amicitia mundi, quae ambae inimicae sunt Deo, foedus miserum pepigisse videor, plane foedus cum morte, pactum cum inferno? Scio tamen, scio qui dixit: Delebitur foedus vestrum cum morte, et pactum cum inferno non stabit (Isai. XXVIII, 18); quia nimirum inimicis Dei nil amicum esse poterit, et non mortem aut infernum, sed omnem quoque creaturam ad ultionem inimicorum zelus armabit. Quam beatus in die illo quicunque erit ille dilectus a Deo! quam laudabiles quibus tunc laus erit a Deo, quibus dicet evidenti rerum argumento: Vos amici mei estis, eo quod feceritis quae praecepi vobis! (Joan. XV, 14). Et haec quidem prima virtus, istud optimum gratiae munus, praecipuus vitae fructus: quia beatitudinis certissimum pignus, Dei scilicet amicitia promereri; quo propitio poterit homini aliquis inimicari, sed nihil proficiet inimicus in eo, et filius iniquitatis non apponet nocere ei (Psal. LXXXVIII, 23)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Indeed, by a wonderful art, but by a more wonderful charity, the prudent clemency and the clement prudence of God has taken counsel for human salvation, so that, although He loves all, He does not easily allow them to be certain or secure of His love; nor does He, for any other reason, hide from those who fear Him the great abundance of His sweetness, except that, while He always keeps them humble, He may always have them lovable. For there are just and wise men, and their works are in the hand of God, and yet man does not know whether he is worthy of love or of hatred; but all things are kept uncertain for the future (Eccli. IX, 1, 2). The Ruler of all things indeed so dispenses to them the grace of gifts and works, that from certain things which He grants, He gives the consolation that they are worthy of love; from certain things which He withdraws, He instills the suspicion and fear that they are worthy of hatred. He consoles when He visits at daybreak; He terrifies when He suddenly puts to the test. Now He brings death, now He brings life; now He leads down to the underworld, now He brings back. Now He makes poor and makes rich, now He humbles and lifts up (I Reg. II, 6, 7). And in so great a variety of changes, He works our salvation all the more surely, the more uncertain we are about it, since we cooperate with Him with greater fear and trembling. Paul indeed says: \"I am certain that nothing shall separate me from the charity of God\" (Rom. VIII, 38, 39). But truly this was Paul speaking, not I or you, for whom it is expedient that all things be kept uncertain for the future. Paul, I say, was the one who said this, to whom indeed it was also fitting to say: \"I live, now not I, but Christ liveth in me\" (Galat. II, 20)\u2014he who was so wholly turned into a certain divine affection that, clinging to the Lord, he was one spirit with Him. Yet see this very Paul, who was thus certain at one moment, at other times fearful and anxious, chastising his own body, lest perhaps, when he had preached to others, he himself should become reprobate (I Cor. IX, 27): see him also buffeted by Satan (II Cor. XII, 7), lest perhaps he be exalted. In Paul himself, then, you may now see how all certainty which is strengthened for an hour and grows strong from the consolation of the spirit, likewise grows weak and vanishes when temptation comes upon it. I, therefore, wretched and unworthy even of life itself\u2014with what consolation, with what confidence shall I presume myself worthy of love, I to whom all things, both inward and outward, proclaim manifest hatred; whose life is not a temptation, not a warfare against sin, but a willing servitude to sin; who serve the law of sin as much in mind as in flesh; who seem to have struck a wretched covenant with the prudence of the flesh and the friendship of the world, both of which are enemies to God\u2014plainly a covenant with death, a pact with hell? Yet I know, I know Him who said: \"Your covenant with death shall be done away, and your pact with hell shall not stand\" (Isai. XXVIII, 18); because surely nothing can be friendly to the enemies of God, and zeal will arm not only death and hell but every creature as well for the vengeance upon enemies. How blessed on that day will whoever is beloved of God be! How praiseworthy will those be to whom praise shall then come from God, to whom He will say, with manifest proof of the fact: \"You are my friends, because you have done what I have commanded you!\" (Joan. XV, 14). And this indeed is the first virtue, this the best gift of grace, the chief fruit of life: because it is the most certain pledge of blessedness, namely, to merit the friendship of God; with God favorable to him, someone may indeed be an enemy to a man, but the enemy will profit nothing against him, and the son of iniquity shall not have power to hurt him further (Psal. LXXXVIII, 23).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Jam vero si post hanc et per hanc summam omnium causamque bonorum, gratiam etiam illam promerearis, ut, dilectus a Deo, etiam dilectus sis ab hominibus, quae consolatio est illa in miseriis hujus vitae, quae requies, quod gaudium, quae deliciae; praesertim si didiceris favore gratiae humanae non evanescere, sed exardescere ad diligendum Deum amplius, propter quem diligaris ab hominibus? Alioquin nempe si aliunde oriatur, vel alio referatur, quid est favor humanus, nisi ventus urens, aura corrumpens vel pestilens, latrunculus spolians, homicida insidians, coluber in via, cerastes in semita, mordens ungulas equi, ut cadat ascensor ejus retro? Vir quippe vanus erigitur in superbiam, et vanitate sensus sui, velut equo praecipiti, fertur in ruinam. Hujusmodi nimirum serpens vulnerat et subvertit vestigia, dum, veluti blandiens capiti, tenera illius facit laudari primordia. Haud secus, nisi fallor, contingit homini qui, antequam diligere sciat, diligi nimie affectat, et, antequam vel sibi ipsi amicus esse didicerit, amicos sibi omnes conciliare quaerit; facit divitias non cum judicio, in medio dierum suorum derelinquet eas, et in novissimo suo erit insipiens, cum omnes amici ejus spreverint eum, pro quibus sprevit ipse Deum. Tunc fiet sermo qui scriptus est: Quoniam Deus dissipavit ossa eorum qui hominibus placent: confusi sunt, quoniam Deus sprevit eos (Psal. LII, 6)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Now if, after this and through this love, supreme above all and the cause of good things, you should also merit that grace, that, being loved by God, you might also be loved by men, what consolation is that in the miseries of this life, what rest, what joy, what delight\u2014especially if you have learned not to grow vain through human favor, but rather to be kindled to love God the more, on whose account you are loved by men? Otherwise, indeed, if it arises from elsewhere, or is referred to something else, what is human favor but a scorching wind, a corrupting or pestilent breeze, a thieving brigand, a lurking murderer, a serpent in the way, a horned snake in the path, biting the horse's heels so that its rider falls backward? For the vain man is lifted up into pride, and by the vanity of his own mind, as if by a headlong horse, is carried to ruin. This kind of serpent indeed wounds and overturns the footsteps, while, as if flattering the head, it makes his tender beginnings to be praised. Not otherwise, unless I am mistaken, does it happen to a man who, before he knows how to love, excessively desires to be loved, and, before he has even learned to be a friend to himself, seeks to win all men as friends to himself; he gathers riches without judgment, in the midst of his days he will abandon them, and in his last end he will be a fool, when all his friends have despised him, for whose sake he himself despised God. Then will be fulfilled the saying that is written: 'For God has scattered the bones of those who please men: they are confounded, because God has despised them' (Psal. LII, 6).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Primo itaque omnium illa, quae prima et finis est omnium, quaerenda est dilectio Dei, cujus merito digni simus etiam ab hominibus diligi; et cujus studio docti simus, dilectione hominum qualiter nos oporteat uti. Cum autem rem in tuto locaveris; ita scilicet firmato affectu mentis, ut nonnisi in Deo et propter Deum diligi velis: tunc omnino volo ut dulcedo morum tuorum, humilitas obsequiorum, honestas studiorum, ad omnem te commendet conscientiam hominum, ut ultro rapias affectus omnium, testimonio praediceris omnium; quatenus ipsa religio, quae te commendat, justa vicissitudine per te commendabilis fiat. Sic in te quoque filio adoptivo, Unigeniti complebitur oratio: Pater, clarifica Filium tuum, ut Filius tuus clarificet te (Joan. XVII, 1); dum videntes claritatem operum tuorum glorificabunt Patrem tuum. Neque id dicimus, quod inter dilectionem Dei et dilectionem proximi sit aliquis ordo temporis, quamvis sit ordo affectionis. Statim quippe ab initio et illam oportet observare, et istam non negligere; quando nec Deus sine proximo, nec proximus sine Deo vere potest diligi; quamvis ordinata affectio ignorare non possit quae de duabus dilectionibus praeponderet alteri, quae debeat alteri formam et modum praescribere, finemque praefigere. Sed hoc dicimus, quod aliud est amari, aliud amari studere. Quod profecto studium sicut periculose assumitur ab illis qui plus vanitate quam charitate blandi sunt, sic periculose negligitur ab his qui plus superbia quam sapientia austeri sunt. Quam ob rem beatum dixerunt, qui inter utrumque vitium, illius scilicet vanae amicabilitatis, et superbae severitatis, mediam tenere potuit regalem viam veritatis; id est ut, vera charitate plenus, nec per vanitatem hoc studium sectetur, nec per tumorem dedignetur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Therefore, before all things, that love of God must be sought which is the first and the end of all things, by whose merit we may be worthy to be loved by men as well; and by whose discipline we may be taught how we ought to make use of the love of men. But when you have placed the matter in a safe position\u2014that is, when the affection of your mind has been so fixed that you wish to be loved only in God and for God's sake\u2014then I wholly desire that the sweetness of your character, the humility of your service, the uprightness of your pursuits, should commend you to the conscience of all men, so that you may win the affections of all unsought, and be proclaimed by the testimony of all; so that religion itself, which commends you, may by a just reciprocity become commendable through you. Thus in you too, as an adopted son, the prayer of the Only-begotten will be fulfilled: 'Father, glorify your Son, that your Son may glorify you' (Joan. XVII, 1); while men, seeing the splendor of your works, will glorify your Father. Nor do we say this as though there were some order of time between the love of God and the love of neighbor, although there is an order of affection. For from the very beginning one must both observe the former and not neglect the latter, since neither can God truly be loved without neighbor, nor neighbor without God; although a well-ordered affection cannot fail to know which of the two loves outweighs the other, which ought to prescribe form and measure to the other, and set its end before it. But this we say: that it is one thing to be loved, another to strive to be loved. Which pursuit indeed is dangerously undertaken by those who are winsome more from vanity than from charity, and is just as dangerously neglected by those who are austere more from pride than from wisdom. For this reason they have been called blessed who, between both vices\u2014namely that of vain amiability and that of proud severity\u2014have been able to hold the middle royal road of truth; that is, so that, being full of true charity, they neither pursue this pursuit through vanity, nor disdain it through arrogance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Illa tamen est vis et natura veri amoris, ut, etiam cum non affectat, tamen efficiat se reamari: quoniam veritas, etiam sine alieno, suffragio facile se ad omnem conscientiam commendat, nisi dira malignae mentis obsistat nequitia, quae parata est sinistre interpretari omnia. Ad hunc tamen sanctum amorem conciliandum quidam proprium donum habent ex Deo; qui exhilarans facies eorum in oleo, perfudit eos quodam placore ac nitore gratioso, omnia tam facta quam dicta ipsorum gratificans in oculis omnium; cum multi qui forsitan non minus, imo et amplius amant, non facile eamdem gratiam inveniant. Ad omnes autem pertinet ut, providentes bona non solum coram Deo sed etiam coram hominibus (Rom. XII, 17 et II Cor. VII, 21), nec conscientiam negligant amore famae, nec famam fiducia conscientiae. Nam et de ipsa conscientia quomodo tibi blandiri potes, nisi sine querela sis inter fratres; imo, nisi te fratrem inter fratres in omni conversatione tua demonstres? Putas enim satis esse, si non scandalizas? Imo scandalizas, si non aedificas; si non, testimonium habens bonum intus et foris, quantum ad gradum tuum attinet, Deum ubique glorificas. Sane malis, quibus non placet nisi malum, quibus justus gravis est etiam ad videndum, si illis placere te non sinit, non tua negligentia (cum omnes etiam inimicos diligas), sed ipsorum nequitia, qui retribuunt mala pro bonis, odium pro dilectione tua; consolatur te qui ait. Beati eritis cum vos oderint homines (Luc. VI, 22), et ille qui ait quia, talibus si placeret, Christi servus non esset (Galat. I, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Yet it is the force and nature of true love that, even when it does not aim at it, it nevertheless brings it about that it is loved in return: because truth, even without another's support, easily commends itself to every conscience, unless the dire wickedness of a malignant mind stands in the way, which is ready to interpret everything in the worst sense. Yet for the winning of this holy love, some have a particular gift from God; who, gladdening their faces with oil, has suffused them with a certain graciousness and gracious brightness, making both their deeds and their words pleasing in the eyes of all; while many who perhaps love no less\u2014indeed even more\u2014do not easily find the same grace. But it belongs to all to take thought for what is good, not only before God but also before men (Rom. XII, 17 et II Cor. VII, 21), neither neglecting conscience out of love of reputation, nor neglecting reputation out of confidence in conscience. For how can you flatter yourself even about your conscience itself, unless you are without reproach among the brethren\u2014indeed, unless you show yourself a brother among brothers in all your conduct? Do you think it enough if you do not give scandal? Nay, you do give scandal if you do not build up; if you do not, having good testimony both within and without, as far as pertains to your rank, glorify God everywhere. Indeed, as for the wicked, whom nothing pleases but evil, to whom the just man is grievous even to look upon\u2014if they do not allow you to please them, it is not through your negligence (since you love even your enemies), but through their own wickedness, who repay evil for good, hatred for your love; he consoles you who says, 'Blessed shall you be when men hate you' (Luc. VI, 22), and he who says that if he pleased such men, he would not be a servant of Christ (Galat. I, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Verum illud miserabile est malum, omnique lacrymarum flumine plangendum, quod quidem saepius experiuntur non modo praelati, sed etiam subditi, qui fratres suos aemulantur Dei aemulatione; quod scilicet et boni, licet non ex ea parte qua sunt boni, bonos inveniuntur odisse. Oderunt corripientem in porta (Amos V, 10), et inimici hominis domestici ejus (Mich. VII, 6), ita ut etiam Paulus ingemiscat quod filiis et amicis inimicus factus sit, vera dicens (Galat. IV, 16). Ergo et ipse expertus est quod comicus ait, \u00ab Veritas odium parit (6); \u00bb imo et illud quod sponsa memorat: Filii matris meae pugnaverunt contra me (Cantic. I, 5), cum ego pugnarem pro eorum salute. O victrix regina, o triumphatrix magnifica, pugna, ne cedas, ne discedas, aut lassescas. Noli vinci a malo, sed vince in bono malum (Rom. XII, 21). Sapientia vincit malitiam (Sap. VII, 30); quanto magis infirmitatem aut imprudentiam? Neque contradictionem carnalium, malitiam interpretari potest charitas spiritualium; sed magis ignorantiam, aut infirmitatem qua praeventi sunt. Quanquam nec ipsis Apostolis contumelia visa sit quod mali a Domino sunt appellati. Quare autem haec ita loquor, fratres mei, dilecti Deo et mihi? Nunquid quia malum hujusmodi suspicer in aliquo vestrum, contra quod mihi vel fratribus meis sit pugnandum? Nunquid enim aliquis invenitur in vobis, non dico rebellis, sed vel durus et intractabilis? Loquor haec non quia ita in vobis sit, sed ne aliquando sit; ne forte aliquando cuipiam aliqua infirmitas subrepat, ut quamcunque correptionem charitatis, nisi cum charitate suscipiat. Sic fiet ut tam is qui corripit, quam ille qui corripitur, dilectus sit a Deo et hominibus, et memoria utriusque in benedictione sit; quod meritis beati Benedicti patris nostri praestare dignetur ille singulariter benedictus Dei Patris Jesus Christus, qui est Deus benedictus in saecula. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. But that is a wretched evil, to be mourned with every flood of tears, which indeed not only superiors but also subjects often experience, who are zealous for their brothers with a godly zeal; namely, that even good men\u2014though not in that respect in which they are good\u2014are found to hate good men. \"They hate him who reproves in the gate\" (Amos V, 10), and \"a man's enemies are those of his own household\" (Mich. VII, 6), so that even Paul groans that he has become an enemy to his sons and friends by telling them the truth (Galat. IV, 16). Therefore he too experienced what the comic poet says, \"Truth begets hatred\"; indeed, he experienced also that which the Bride recalls: \"The sons of my mother have fought against me\" (Cantic. I, 5), while I was fighting for their salvation. O victorious queen, O magnificent triumphant one, fight on; do not yield, do not withdraw, nor grow weary. \"Be not overcome by evil, but overcome evil with good\" (Rom. XII, 21). \"Wisdom overcomes malice\" (Sap. VII, 30); how much more, then, weakness or imprudence? Nor can the charity of spiritual men interpret the opposition of carnal men as malice; but rather as ignorance, or as a weakness by which they have been overtaken. Although it did not even seem an insult to the Apostles themselves that they were called evil by the Lord. But why do I speak this way, my brothers, beloved of God and of me? Is it because I suspect such an evil in any of you, against which I or my brothers must fight? Is anyone found among you who is\u2014I do not say rebellious\u2014but even hard and intractable? I say these things not because it is so among you, but lest it ever be so; lest perhaps at some time some weakness should creep upon someone, so that he would not receive any correction of charity except with charity. Thus it will come about that both he who corrects and he who is corrected will be loved by God and by men, and the memory of each will be in blessing; which may he who is singularly blessed of God the Father, Jesus Christ, who is God blessed forever, deign to grant through the merits of our blessed father Benedict. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-n006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(6) TERENT. Andr. I, 1, 41."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-n006", "label": "English", "text": "(6) TERENCE, Andria, I, 1, 41.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Ecclesiastes 9:1-2 describes the just and wise in God's hand; the edition prints Eccli. IX, 1, 2."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-03-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The words on providing good things before God and men come from Romans 12:17 and 2 Corinthians 8:21; the edition prints Rom. XII, 17 et II Cor. VII, 21."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon III", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "friendship with God", "grace", "humility", "charity", "human favor", "reputation", "fraternal correction", "perseverance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-benedict-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV., De lenitate et fide., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 111-116.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint Benedict", "Feast of Saint Benedict", "Sirach 45:4", "Numbers 11:24\u201327", "Hebrews 11:24\u201325", "Numbers 12:13 (printed locator; quotation from Numbers 12:3)", "Numbers 16:15", "1 Kings 2 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Samuel)", "Mark 9:49", "Hebrews 11:26\u201327", "Ephesians 2:6", "Hebrews 11:27", "Romans 8:24", "Psalm 15:8 (historical numbering)", "Habakkuk 2:4", "Romans 1:17", "Hebrews 11:6", "Psalm 52:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 35:2\u20133 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 7:28", "Luke 18:8", "James 2:19", "Hebrews 11:1, 6", "Psalm 33:17 (historical numbering)", "Luke 23:30", "1 Corinthians 16:13", "Jeremiah, verse 3 (printed locator; quotation from Jeremiah 5:3)", "Hebrews 11:1"], "occasion": "In festo sancti Benedicti", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Saint Benedict", "occasion_label": "Saint Benedict", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric compares Benedict with Moses through faith and gentleness. He distinguishes living faith from feigned belief and connects vigilance before God with hope of the promised reward. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina under the feast of Saint Benedict. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De lenitate et fide."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On meekness and faith.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. In fide et lenitate ipsius sanctum fecit illum (Eccli. XLV, 4). Haec quidem de Moyse scripta sunt, sed hodie beato Benedicto non incongrue, quantum arbitror, aptata sunt. Qui, cum omnino plenus fuerit Spiritu sanctorum, multo magis de spiritu Moysi credendus est habuisse non minimum. Si enim Dominus de spiritu Moysi tulit, et posuit super omnem illam suffraganeam turbam seniorum, qui in partem ministerii sunt asciti (Num. XI, 24-27), quanto magis super istum qui plenitudinem totius ministerii tanto verius quanto spiritualius implevit? Ille dux fuit exeuntium de Aegypto; iste, renuntiantium saeculo: legislator ille, legislator et iste: ille tantum minister litterae occidentis; iste, spiritus vivificantis: ille ad duritiam cordis Judaeorum dedit eis justificationes non bonas, praeter pauca moralia; iste solam puritatem evangelicam simplicemque morum tradidit disciplinam: ille tam multa scribit intellectu difficilia, factu impossibilia, vel inutilia; iste rectissimam vitae scribit Regulam, sermone luculentam, discretione praecipuam. Denique dux ille filiorum Israel, quos de Aegypto eduxit, in requiem promissam non induxit; dux noster recta via, orientis tramite, tanquam signifer exercitus monachorum hodie praecessit nos in regnum coelorum. Itaque non erit absurdum si credatur illius adaequasse meritum, cujus invenitur etiam superasse ministerium. Nec incongrue videbitur ad ipsum esse translatum quod de illo scriptum legitur: In fide et lenitate ipsius sanctum illum Dominus; praesertim cum harum praecipue virtutum, fidei scilicet et lenitatis, nobis ipse magister sit, qui nequaquam aliter vixit quam docuit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"In his faith and meekness he made him holy\" (Eccli. XLV, 4). These words indeed were written of Moses, but today, as far as I judge, they have not been unfittingly applied to blessed Benedict. For since he was altogether full of the Spirit of the saints, he is all the more to be believed to have had no small share of the spirit of Moses. For if the Lord took from the spirit of Moses and placed it upon that whole assisting crowd of elders who were taken into a part of his ministry (Num. XI, 24-27), how much more upon him who fulfilled the fullness of that whole ministry all the more truly as he fulfilled it more spiritually? That man was the leader of those going out of Egypt; this one, of those renouncing the world: that man was a lawgiver, and this one also a lawgiver: that man was only a minister of the letter that kills; this one, of the spirit that gives life: that man, on account of the hardness of the Jews' hearts, gave them statutes that were not good, apart from a few moral precepts; this one handed down only the purity of the Gospel and a simple discipline of morals: that man writes so many things difficult to understand, impossible or useless to do; this one writes a most upright Rule of life, brilliant in style, outstanding in discretion. Finally, that leader of the sons of Israel, whom he led out of Egypt, did not bring them into the promised rest; our leader, by the straight way, along the path of the rising sun, has today gone before us like a standard-bearer of an army of monks into the kingdom of heaven. Therefore it will not be absurd if it is believed that his merit equaled that of the one whose ministry he is even found to have surpassed. Nor will it seem unfitting that what is read as written of that man should be transferred to him: \"The Lord made him holy in his faith and meekness\"; especially since he himself is our master chiefly of these virtues, namely faith and meekness, he who never lived otherwise than he taught.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quid enim praeclarius illius fide, qui in aetate puerili mundum sibi arridentem irridens, tam mundi quam corporis sui florem quasi jam aridum calcavit, magis appetens pro Deo mala mundi perpeti, quam in eo temporaliter prosperari? Quid similius illi fidei Moysi, quam Apostolus commendans ait: Fide Moyses grandis factus negavit se filium filiae Pharaonis, magis eligens affligi cum populo Dei, quam temporalis peccati habere jucunditatem (Hebr. XI, 24, 25)? Quid autem sanctius lenitate Patris nostri, qui nec malitia falsorum fratrum vitae ipsius insidiantium: et pro vino venenum propinantium, potuit exasperari? Moysen quidem Scriptura praedicat mittissimum fuisse super omnes homines qui morabantur in terra (Num. XII, 13); nunquid tamen exacerbatum spiritum ejus negat? nunquid non iratum et valde iratum adversus aemulos memorat (Id. XVI, 15)? Hujus autem magistri nostri mansuetudinem, non solum erga maledicos, sed etiam erga maleficos, mirabilem me legisse memini, iracundiam non memini. Quanquam nec in ipso, sicut nec in Moyse, praeconio sanctae lenitatis praejudicet, si justus zelo contra peccantes fervet, sine quo lenitas tepor aut timiditas est. Quomodo namque lenitas illa sanctum quempiam faceret, quae in Heli, qui alius sanctus erat, damnata est? (I Reg. II). Fratres, habete pacem inter vos, mandat ille Magister pacificus et lenis; prius tamen praemittit: habete salem in vobis (Marc. IX, 49); sciens nimirum pacis lenitatem vitiorum esse nutricem, nisi prius asperitas zeli mordacem eis insperserit salem; sicut et carnes vermescere facit clementia temporis, nisi desiccaverit eas fervor salis. Habete itaque pacem inter vos, sed quae sale condita sit sapientiae; sectamini lenitatem, sed quae ferveat fide."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. For what is more splendid than the faith of him who, in his boyhood, mocking the world that smiled upon him, trampled underfoot the flower both of the world and of his own body as though already withered, desiring rather to suffer the evils of the world for God's sake than to prosper temporally in it? What is more like that faith of Moses which the Apostle commends, saying: \"By faith Moses, when he was grown a man, denied himself to be the son of Pharaoh's daughter, choosing rather to be afflicted with the people of God than to have the pleasure of temporal sin\" (Hebr. XI, 24, 25)? But what is holier than the meekness of our Father, who could not be provoked even by the malice of false brothers plotting against his very life, and offering him poison instead of wine? Scripture indeed proclaims that Moses was the meekest of all men who dwelt on the earth (Num. XII, 13); yet does it not also record his spirit as exasperated? Does it not relate him as angry, and very angry, against his rivals (Id. XVI, 15)? But I recall having read of the wonderful gentleness of this our master, not only toward those who spoke evil of him, but even toward those who did evil to him; I do not recall any anger in him. Although neither in him, nor in Moses, should this prejudice the praise of holy meekness, if a just man burns with zeal against sinners, without which meekness is mere lukewarmness or timidity. For how could that meekness which was condemned in Eli, who was otherwise a holy man, have made anyone holy? (I Reg. II). \"Brothers, have peace among yourselves,\" that peaceful and gentle Master commands; yet he first says beforehand: \"Have salt in yourselves\" (Marc. IX, 49); knowing surely that the meekness of peace is a nurse of vices, unless the sharpness of zeal first sprinkles them with biting salt; just as the mildness of the weather causes flesh to breed worms, unless the heat of salt dries it out. Have peace, then, among yourselves, but peace seasoned with the salt of wisdom; pursue meekness, but meekness that burns with faith.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. In fide et lenitate vos quoque sancti eritis; nec jam lenitas suspecta erit, si fides praecesserit: tantum fides non ficta, sed vera sit; fides non mortua, sed viva et vivida sit. Non modo viva vel vivida, sed et constans et intrepida fides erat Moysi, de quo Paulus scribit: Fide reliquit Aegyptum, non veritus animositatem regis. Animosi sunt reges, sed animosior est fides, quippe quae potentiam eorum nullam esse videt, ac per hoc omnem persequentium insaniam secura, quasi superior, irridet; promptior et fortior ad perferendum, quam illorum furor ad persequendum. Duo sane commendare mihi videtur Apostolus in illa memorabili fide Moysi, cui occasione lectionis hodiernae comparandam diximus fidem sancti patris nostri Benedicti: scilicet quod idem Moyses, qui fidei exemplum factus est, hujus mundi contempsit prospera, nec timuit adversa. Prospera contempsit, majores divitias aestimans thesauris Aegyptiorum improperium Christi: adversa non timuit, non veritus scilicet animositatem regis. Ad utrumque vero causam Apostolus subjunxit, unde scilicet id potuerit, ut discamus unde fides nostra infirmetur in nobis. Majores divitias reputabat thesauris Aegyptiorum improperium Christi: aspiciebat enim, inquit, in remunerationem. Non est veritus animositatem regis: invisibilem enim, inquit, tanquam videns sustinuit (Hebr. XI, 27, 26). Nimirum inde fit ut nihil reputentur temporalia, si prae oculis habeantur aeterna; inde fit ut facile potestas contemnatur humana, si tanquam semper imminens potestas timeatur divina. Utrumque vero sola fides agit, cujus tam vividi, tam perspicaces sunt oculi, ut et in ea quae futura sunt prospectum vivaciter porrigat, et in ea quae, licet praesentia sint, occulta tamen, intuitum perspicaciter figat. Fidei siquidem, quam aeternus Spiritus illuminat, nec mora temporis, nec moles corporis praejudicare potest quin et tempus praecedat, praesumendo quod futurum est, et corpus excedat, intuendo quod spiritus est. Gemina siquidem est fidei virtus, contemplantis non ea quae videntur, sed quae non videntur; sicut gemina est ratio eorum quae non videntur. Aut enim ideo non videntur quia praesentia non sunt, aut ideo quia, etsi praesentia sunt, spiritualia sunt. Praesentia non sunt bona futura quae promittuntur; praesens est, sed occultus est, quia spiritus est, Deus ipse qui promittit vel minatur. Porro fides, quia est substantia sperandarum rerum, bona futura, quae sperat, quasi praesentia praesumit, et jam quasi subsistere in corde credentis facit. Item quia est argumentum non apparentium (Ibid. 1), Deum, etsi non appareat, praesentem tamen esse sibi coarguit, probat et convincit. Illi qui dicebat: Qui conresuscitavit et consedere fecit in coelestibus in Christo (Ephes. II, 6), fides erat substantia rerum sperandarum; illi qui invisibilem tanquam videns sustinebat (Hebr. XI, 27), argumentum erat non apparentium. Qui enim dicebat: Spe salvi facti sumus (Rom. VIII, 24), nonne quod sperabat et per patientiam exspectabat, jam per fidem in corde suo subsistere demonstrabat? Et qui proponebat Dominum in conspectu suo semper, nonne persuasum tenebat, argumento fidei, eum praesentem esse, qui non apparebat? (Psal. XV, 8)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. In faith and gentleness you too will be holy; and gentleness will no longer be suspect, if faith has gone before it\u2014provided only that the faith be not feigned but true, not dead but living and vigorous. Not merely living or vigorous, but also steadfast and fearless was the faith of Moses, of whom Paul writes: \"By faith he left Egypt, not fearing the fierceness of the king.\" Kings are fierce, but faith is fiercer, since it sees that their power is nothing, and therefore, secure as one who is superior, it laughs at all the madness of persecutors; it is more ready and stronger to endure than their fury is to persecute. Two things, surely, the Apostle seems to me to commend in that memorable faith of Moses, which, on the occasion of today's reading, we said should be compared to the faith of our holy father Benedict: namely, that this same Moses, who became an example of faith, both despised the prosperities of this world and did not fear its adversities. He despised prosperity, esteeming the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of the Egyptians; he did not fear adversity, that is, he was not afraid of the fierceness of the king. For both the Apostle adds the cause, namely whence he was able to do this, so that we may learn whence our faith grows weak in us. He esteemed the reproach of Christ greater riches than the treasures of the Egyptians: \"for he looked,\" it says, \"unto the reward.\" He did not fear the fierceness of the king: \"for he endured, as seeing him who is invisible\" (Hebr. XI, 27, 26). Surely it comes about from this that temporal things are reckoned as nothing, if eternal things are kept before the eyes; it comes about from this that human power is easily held in contempt, if divine power is feared as always imminent. But faith alone does both of these things, whose eyes are so lively, so keen-sighted, that it both stretches its gaze vividly toward things that are future, and fixes its sight keenly upon things which, although present, are nonetheless hidden. For to faith, which the eternal Spirit illumines, neither the delay of time nor the bulk of the body can be an obstacle, so that it both goes before time, presuming what is to come, and goes beyond the body, beholding what is spirit. The power of faith is indeed twofold, contemplating not those things which are seen, but those which are not seen; just as the reason of things not seen is also twofold. For either they are not seen because they are not present, or they are not seen because, although present, they are spiritual. The future goods that are promised are not present; but he who promises or threatens, God himself, is present, yet hidden, because he is spirit. Furthermore, faith, because it is the substance of things to be hoped for, presumes the future goods which it hopes for as if they were present, and makes them, as it were, already subsist in the heart of the believer. Likewise, because it is the evidence of things not appearing (ibid. 1), it proves, demonstrates, and convinces that God, even though he does not appear, is nevertheless present to it. To him who said: \"Who raised us up together, and made us sit together in the heavenly places in Christ\" (Ephes. II, 6), faith was the substance of things to be hoped for; to him who endured, as seeing him who is invisible (Hebr. XI, 27), faith was the evidence of things not appearing. For did not he who said, \"We are saved by hope\" (Rom. VIII, 24), show that what he hoped for and awaited through patience already subsisted in his heart through faith? And did not he who set the Lord always in his sight hold it firmly persuaded, by the evidence of faith, that he who did not appear was present? (Psal. XV, 8).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Huic fidei prorsus aptissime congruit quod Scriptura dicit: Justus ex fide vivit (Habac. II, 4, et Rom. I, 17). Haec enim justum facit, justumque custodit, et, ut vivat in aeternum, interim spei gaudio pascit. Quid enim sic hominem a peccato revocat et servat sicut fides, quae invisibilem tanquam videns sustinet et observat? quid ita spe gaudentem facit sicut fides, quae semper in remunerationem aspicit? Nos enim, fratres, quare negligentes sumus, nisi quia praesentiam Judicis nostri minus vigilanter intendimus? aut quare tristitia pene absorbemur, nisi quia remunerationem promissam minus fideliter cogitamus? Haec siquidem duo mala sunt, negligentia scilicet et tristitia, quibus miserabiliter infidelitas nostra laborat; quia videlicet aut negligimus mandata, aut, si necessitate timoris a negligentia coercemur, nequaquam hilariter operamur, nec, sicut fides postulat, spe remunerationis in labore consolamur. Credere, inquit, oportet accedentes ad Deum quia est, et inquirentibus se remunerator est (Hebr. XI, 6). Si non dissimulemus quia est, habemus custodiam timoris; si non dissimulemus quia inquirentibus se remunerator est, habemus consolationem spei. Ubi custodia timoris, nullus locus negligentiae; ubi consolatio spei, nullus locus tristitiae. Sicut autem de fide, qua Deus esse creditur, timor salutis nascitur, ita cum minime creditur, aut dissimulatur, omnibus malis frena laxantur. Quare enim corrupti et abominabiles facti sunt in iniquitatibus, nisi quia dixit ille generalis insipiens in corde suo: Non est Deus (Psal. LII, 2, 1)? aut certe si nescire Deum non permittitur, non est timor Dei ante oculos ejus, sed dolose agit in conspectu ejus, dissimulans quod novit invitus, ut inveniatur iniquitas ejus ad odium (Psal. XXXV, 2, 3). Merito prorsus dolosus ad odium, qui ignorantiam Dei non habet, sed odium. Quomodo enim non odit Deum, cujus declinat aspectum, contemnit imperium, vellet non esse judicium? Ignorans quidem ignorabitur, odiens autem et odietur. Ille reprobabitur veritate judicii, in istum vindicabitur severitate odii."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. To this faith what Scripture says applies most fittingly: \"The just man lives by faith\" (Habac. II, 4, et Rom. I, 17). For faith makes a man just, and keeps him just, and meanwhile, that he may live forever, feeds him with the joy of hope. For what so recalls a man from sin and keeps him from it as faith, which, as though seeing the Invisible One, endures and keeps watch? What so fills a man with the joy of hope as faith, which always looks toward the reward? For why, brothers, are we negligent, except because we attend less watchfully to the presence of our Judge? Or why are we almost swallowed up by sadness, except because we think less faithfully upon the promised reward? These indeed are two evils\u2014negligence and sadness\u2014under which our unfaithfulness miserably labors; for either we neglect the commandments, or, if we are restrained from negligence by the necessity of fear, we by no means work cheerfully, nor, as faith requires, do we console ourselves in our labor with the hope of reward. \"It is necessary,\" he says, \"that he who comes to God believe that he exists, and that he is a rewarder to those who seek him\" (Hebr. XI, 6). If we do not pretend otherwise but believe that he exists, we have the guard of fear; if we do not pretend otherwise but believe that he is a rewarder to those who seek him, we have the consolation of hope. Where there is the guard of fear, there is no room for negligence; where there is the consolation of hope, there is no room for sadness. But just as from the faith by which God is believed to exist, a saving fear is born, so when he is not believed in at all, or is dissembled, the reins are loosed to every evil. For why are men become corrupt and abominable in their iniquities, except because that universal fool said in his heart, \"There is no God\" (Psal. LII, 2, 1)? Or certainly, if it is not permitted him not to know God, then \"the fear of God is not before his eyes,\" but he deals deceitfully in his sight, dissembling what he unwillingly knows, \"that his iniquity may be found unto hatred\" (Psal. XXXV, 2, 3). Rightly indeed is he deceitful unto hatred, who has not ignorance of God, but hatred. For how does he not hate God, whose gaze he avoids, whose command he despises, whose judgment he would wish not to exist? He who is ignorant will indeed be ignored; but he who hates will also be hated. The former will be condemned by the truth of judgment; upon the latter vengeance will be taken by the severity of hatred.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Adeo, fratres, periculosior est fides ficta, fide dolosa, quae se ipsam dissimulat, quam fides nulla. Fides nulla, per insipientiam dicit in corde suo: Non est Deus; fides ficta per nequitiam dolose agit in conspectu ejus, et se scire Deum vel sciri a Deo dissimulat; dum sciens et prudens, imo imprudens et impudens sub oculis ejus peccat. Nescio tamen quomodo possit dici fides, quae fictos et dolosos potius facit quam fideles. Scio quidem quia confessione fidei plena est terra, sed tamen audio prophetam plangentem, quia periit fides de terra (Jerem. VII, 28). Putas enim, Filius hominis si nunc veniret, inveniret fidem super terram (Luc. XVIII, 8)? Nunquid fidem reputaret hanc fidem fictam negligentium et contemnentium, qua levius damnat caecitatem infidelium, qua meliorem judicat credulitatem daemonum? Daemones credunt et contremiscunt (Jacob. II, 19); homines credunt nec contremiscunt. Daemones quem credunt verentur; homines dum quem credunt, nec timent nec verentur, gravius de contemptu judicantur. Non fallamur ergo, fratres, generali nomine fidei, quasi quaecunque fides ad justitiam debeat reputari; sed meminerimus Doctor gentium in fide et veritate, qualem definierit fidem, qua Deo est placendum: Fides, inquiens, est substantia rerum sperandarum argumentum non apparentium. Haec est fides quae per dilectionem operatur; ut, suadente conscientia meritorum, de fide spes nascatur, fidesque supponatur velut subjectum et fundamentum, cui superaedificentur bona aeterna, quae speranda sunt. Sine ista fide impossibile est placere Deo (Hebr. XI, 1, 6), et cum ista impossibile est displicere Deo. Oculi tui Domine, respiciunt fidem (Jerem. 3 v.), dixit ille qui jugiter in conspectu tuo stabat per fidem. Omnino juste et merito. Prorsus debita vicissitudo, ut oculi tui, Domine, respiciant fidem, quia oculi mei semper ad Dominum fidelem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. So much, brothers, is a feigned faith, a deceitful faith that dissembles itself, more dangerous than no faith at all. No faith at all, through folly, says in its heart, \"There is no God\"; feigned faith, through wickedness, deals deceitfully in his sight, and pretends either that it does not know God or that it is not known by God; while, knowingly and deliberately\u2014nay, imprudently and shamelessly\u2014it sins under his eyes. Yet I do not know how that can be called faith which makes men feigning and deceitful rather than faithful. I know indeed that the earth is full of the confession of faith, but yet I hear the prophet lamenting, because \"faith has perished from the earth\" (Jerem. VII, 28). Do you suppose that if the Son of Man came now, he would find faith upon the earth? (Luc. XVIII, 8). Would he reckon as faith this feigned faith of the negligent and the contemptuous, compared with which he condemns unbelievers' blindness more lightly and judges demons' belief better? \"The demons believe and tremble\" (Jacob. II, 19); men believe and do not tremble. The demons fear him whom they believe; men, while they believe him whom they fear not and do not revere, are more gravely judged for their contempt. Let us not, then, brothers, be deceived by the general name of faith, as though any faith whatsoever ought to be reckoned unto righteousness; but let us remember how the Doctor of the Gentiles defined, in faith and truth, the kind of faith by which God is to be pleased: \"Faith,\" he says, \"is the substance of things to be hoped for, the evidence of things not appearing.\" This is the faith that works through love; so that, with the consciousness of merits giving assurance, hope may be born from faith, and faith may be set beneath it as subject and foundation, upon which the eternal goods that are to be hoped for are built. \"Without this faith it is impossible to please God\" (Hebr. XI, 1, 6), and with it, it is impossible to displease God. \"Your eyes, Lord, look upon faith\" (Jerem. 3 v.), said he who stood continually in your sight through faith. Altogether justly and rightly. It is indeed a fitting exchange, that your eyes, Lord, should look upon faith, since my eyes are ever toward the faithful Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Nos autem, fratres, si tanquam sine fide simus, Deum post dorsum nostrum ponimus, ut, dissimulantes timorem ejus, in vanitates potius respiciamus, quo merito nos respiciendos putamus? Imo respiciemur, sed quo vultu? Vultus Domini super facientes mala (Psal. XXXIII, 17); sed quam iratus, quam terribilis, quam importabilis, tunc demum scietur, quando fugient qui oderunt eum a facie ejus. Et a facie tua, Domine, quo fugient, nisi in tenebras exteriores, in illud chaos et abyssum ignis et caliginis? Tunc enim dicent montibus, Cadite super nos; et collibus, Operite nos (Luc. XXIII, 30), levius aestimantes absorberi voragine inferni, quam sustinere faciem Dei irati. Enim vero tunc stabunt justi in magna constantia; tunc fides, quae modo sollicita stat in conspectu Domini ad videndam voluntatem ipsius, secura stabit ad videndam gloriam ejus. Vigilate, fratres, state in fide (I Cor. XVI, 13). Quem fides timore sollicitat, non potest dormitare per negligentiam; quem fides in spe radicat, non potest titubare per diffidentiam. Omnia autem vestra in charitate fiant, ut fidei lenitas jungatur, quatenus de unoquoque vestrum dicatur, In fide et lenitate ipsius sanctum fecit illum Dominus (Eccli. XLV, 4); quod ipse praestet Sanctus sanctorum, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. But we, brothers, if we are as though without faith, placing God behind our backs so that, disregarding fear of him, we look instead toward vanities\u2014with what justice do we think we deserve to be looked upon? Indeed we shall be looked upon, but with what face? \"The face of the Lord is against those who do evil\" (Psal. XXXIII, 17); but how angry, how terrible, how unbearable that face will be, will only then be known, when those who hated him flee from before his face. And from your face, Lord, where will they flee, except into outer darkness, into that chaos and abyss of fire and gloom? For then they will say to the mountains, \"Fall on us,\" and to the hills, \"Cover us\" (Luc. XXIII, 30), judging it lighter to be swallowed up in the chasm of hell than to endure the face of an angry God. But then indeed the just will stand in great steadfastness; then faith, which now stands anxiously in the sight of the Lord to see his will, will stand secure to see his glory. Watch, brothers, stand firm in faith (I Cor. XVI, 13). He whom faith troubles with fear cannot slumber through negligence; he whom faith roots in hope cannot waver through distrust. But let all your things be done in charity, so that gentleness may be joined to faith, so that it may be said of each one of you, \"In his faith and gentleness the Lord made him holy\" (Eccli. XLV, 4); which may he grant, the Holy One of holies, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints mittissimum, where mitissimum (most gentle) is expected."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The description of Moses as the meekest of men comes from Numbers 12:3; the edition prints Num. XII, 13."}, {"anchor": "guerric-benedict-04-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The words on the Lord's eyes looking upon faith come from Jeremiah 5:3; the edition prints Jerem. 3 v. without a chapter number."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saint Benedict Sermon IV", "topics": ["Saint Benedict", "Moses", "faith", "gentleness", "zeal", "hope", "fear of God", "judgment", "charity", "vigilance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-benedict-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-canticle", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO, In verba Cantici canticorum: \u00ab Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam. \u00bb\n(Cant. VIII, 13.), in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 210-214.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Song of Songs", "No liturgical occasion", "Song of Songs 8:13", "John 11:43\u201344", "Jeremiah 22:19", "Song of Songs 7:13", "Psalm 118:2, 129 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 24:27", "Psalm 118:103 (historical numbering)", "John 1:38\u201339", "Song of Songs 4:12", "Sirach 39:17", "Proverbs 16:23", "Sirach 40:21", "Psalm 103:31 (historical numbering; printed locator; passage from verse 34)", "Sirach 15:9", "Psalm 49:16 (historical numbering)", "Amos 5:25 (printed locator; passage from verse 23)", "Jeremiah 8:6", "John 3:29", "Song of Songs 2:14"], "occasion": "No historical liturgical occasion established", "occasion_detail": "No liturgical occasion", "occasion_label": "Song of Songs", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric takes the Bridegroom's words \"You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; let me hear your voice\" and declines them for himself, as one who dwells among the tombs of the flesh. The brothers dwell in gardens when they meditate on Scripture, and Christ, the true gardener, plants and waters the Church. Paul walked in the gardens of paradise and sang of Christ to the friends who listened. The words call the devout to psalmody and prayer, with the angels listening, and the preacher to speak, with the faithful listening. The sermon follows the All Saints sermon in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina and carries no liturgical heading. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "In verba Cantici canticorum: \u00ab Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam. \u00bb\n(Cant. VIII, 13.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Words of the Song of Songs: \"You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; let me hear your voice.\" (Cant. VIII, 13.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. In conventu sodalium et amicorum, id est, Ecclesia sanctorum, cujus vocem deceat audiri, sponsus ipse sponsae loquens, innuit cum ait: Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam. Non sum ego cui hoc dictum debeat videri: non sum qui habitem in hortis; magis mihi videor esse de illis qui habitant in sepulcris. Corpora enim peccatorum, quid sunt nisi sepulcra mortuorum? Qui ergo dediti sunt corporibus suis, non in hortis habitant, sed in sepulcris: Deumque exasperant, donec qui educit vinctos in fortitudine, forti voce inclamet, Lazari, veni foras; suisque discipulis super eo praecipiat, Solvite eum et sinite abire (Joan. XI, 43, 44). Magna sane differentia est sepulcrorum et hortorum. Haec plena omni spurcitia et ossibus mortuorum; illi, omni suavitate et gratia florum, aut fructuum. Quid enim si aliquando sepulcra videntur in hortis? Nam et Dominus in horto sepultus fuit. Etsi enim sepulcra in hortis, nunquid tamen horti in sepulcris? Ita fortassis, sed in sepulcris justorum. Ibi plane vernabit gratissima quaedam amoenitas hortorum, verno scilicet illo tempore resurrectionis eorum, cum reflorebit caro eorum, et non solum ossa justi sicut herba germinabunt, sed et totus justus germinabit sicut lilium, et florebit in aeternum ante Dominum. Non sic impii, non sic, qui sepultura asini sepeliuntur (Jerem. XXII, 19), et sine omni spe melioris resurrectionis subditi corruptioni, hac praesenti futuram auspicantur. De sepulcris eorum dicere coeperam, quod quantum a spurcitia illorum abhorret florentium species hortorum, tantum, imo incomparabiliter amplius distat oblectatio spiritualium a voluptate gaudiorum carnalium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. In the assembly of companions and friends, that is, the Church of the saints, whose voice it is fitting should be heard, the Bridegroom himself, speaking to the Bride, hints at this when he says: You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; let me hear your voice. I am not one to whom this should seem to be said: I am not one who dwells in gardens; I seem to myself rather to be among those who dwell in tombs. For what are the bodies of sinners but tombs of the dead? Those, then, who are given over to their bodies do not dwell in gardens but in tombs, and they provoke God to anger, until he who leads forth the bound in strength cries out with a mighty voice, \"Lazarus, come forth,\" and commands his disciples concerning him, \"Loose him and let him go\" (Joan. XI, 43, 44). There is indeed a great difference between tombs and gardens. The one is full of all filth and the bones of the dead; the other, of all the sweetness and grace of flowers or fruits. For what if tombs are sometimes seen in gardens? For even the Lord was buried in a garden. Though tombs may be in gardens, are gardens therefore in tombs? Perhaps so\u2014but in the tombs of the just. There, surely, a most pleasing charm of gardens will bloom, namely in that springtime of their resurrection, when their flesh will flower again, and not only will the bones of the just sprout like grass, but the whole just person will sprout like a lily, and will flourish forever before the Lord. Not so the wicked, not so those who are buried with the burial of an ass (Jerem. XXII, 19), and who, subject to corruption without any hope of a better resurrection, in this present corruption begin the corruption to come. I had begun to speak of their tombs, to say that as much as the appearance of flourishing gardens recoils from their filth, so much\u2014indeed incomparably more\u2014does the delight of spiritual things differ from the pleasure of carnal joys.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vos igitur, ni fallor, estis qui in hortis habitatis, qui scilicet in lege Domini meditamini die ac nocte, et quot libros legitis, tot hortos perambulatis; quot sententias eligitis, tot poma carpitis. Et beati quibus omnia poma nova et vetera servata sunt: id est, tam prophetarum, quam evangelistarum, vel apostolorum eloquia reposita sunt, ut et unicuique vestrum illud sponsae ad sponsum videatur dictum, Omnia poma, nova et vetera, dilecte mi, servavi tibi (Cantic. VII, 13). Scrutamini igitur Scripturas. Vos enim non falso vitam in ipsis putatis vos habere, qui nihil aliud in illis quaeritis quam Christum, cui testimonium perhibent Scripturae. Beati plane qui scrutantur testimonia ejus; in toto corde exquirunt eum. Mirabilia testimonia tua, Domine; ideo scrutata est ea anima mea (Psal. CXVIII, 2, 129). Scrutinio quidem opus est, non solum ut eruantur mystica, sed etiam ut sugantur moralia. Ideoque vos, qui perambulatis hortos Scripturarum, nolite negligenter et otiose transvolare; sed scrutantes singula, velut apes sedulae mel de floribus, spiritum de sermonibus colligite. Spiritus enim meus, inquit Jesus, super mel dulcis, et haereditas mea super mel et favum (Eccli. XXIV, 27). Ita probantes quid sapiat manna absconditum, eructabitis illud Davidicum: Quam dulcia faucibus meis eloquia tua! super mel et favum ori meo (Psal. CXVIII, 103)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. You, then, unless I am mistaken, are they who dwell in gardens, who meditate on the law of the Lord day and night, and for as many books as you read, so many gardens you walk through; for as many sentences as you choose, so many fruits you pluck. And blessed are those for whom all fruits, new and old, are kept in store: that is, the sayings of the prophets, as well as of the evangelists and apostles, are laid up, so that to each of you that word of the Bride to the Bridegroom may seem said: \"All fruits, new and old, my beloved, I have kept for you\" (Cantic. VII, 13). Search the Scriptures, then. For you do not falsely suppose that you have life in them, you who seek in them nothing other than Christ, to whom the Scriptures bear witness. Blessed indeed are they who search his testimonies; they seek him with their whole heart. \"Wonderful are your testimonies, O Lord; therefore has my soul searched them\" (Psal. CXVIII, 2, 129). There is indeed need for searching, not only that mystical meanings may be dug out, but also that moral lessons may be sucked out. Therefore you who walk through the gardens of the Scriptures, do not fly over them carelessly and idly; but searching out each thing, like busy bees gathering honey from flowers, gather spirit from the words. For \"my spirit,\" says Jesus, \"is sweeter than honey, and my inheritance above honey and the honeycomb\" (Eccli. XXIV, 27). Thus, proving what the hidden manna tastes like, you will belch forth that word of David: \"How sweet are your words to my palate! above honey and the honeycomb to my mouth\" (Psal. CXVIII, 103).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Ab his hortis in alios, ubi requies secretior et voluptas beatior, et species mirabilior, sponsus, ni fallor, vos introducit, cum intentos laudibus suis in voce exsultationis et confessionis, rapit in locum tabernaculi admirabilis usque ad domum Dei, lucem scilicet inaccessibilem, ubi habitat, ubi pascit, ubi cubat in meridie. Si enim devotio psallentium et orantium habet aliquid de illa pia curiositate quaerentium, Rabbi, ubi habitas? puto quia merentur audire, Venite et videte. Venerunt, inquit, et viderunt, et apud eum manserunt die illo (Joan. I, 38, 39). Quandiu apud Patrem luminum sumus, apud quem non est transmutatio, nec vicissitudinis obumbratio; noctem nescimus, tantummodo die beato fruimur. Cum inde labimur, in noctem nostram recidimus. Heu mihi! quam cito defecerunt dies mei, quam cito sicut fenum arui, qui quandiu fui in horto cum illo, sicut paradisus Dei virui et florui! Cum illo hortus voluptatis: sine illo locus horroris et vastae solitudinis. Existimo enim qui in hortum illius intrat, quod et ipse hortus fiat; sitque anima illius velut hortus irriguus, ut et sponsus in laudem ejus dicat: Hortus conclusus, soror mea sponsa (Cant. IV, 12). An non sunt hortus, in quibus fit quod ipse hortulanus loquitur plantationi, quam plantavit Pater suus? Audite me, inquit, divini fructus, et quasi rosa plantata super rivos aquarum fructificate (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). Quasi Libanus, odorem suavitatis habete. Florete flores quasi lilium, date odorem, et frondete in gratiam."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. From these gardens into others, where the rest is more secret and the pleasure more blessed, and the beauty more wonderful, the Bridegroom, unless I am mistaken, leads you in, when, as you are intent upon his praises in a voice of exultation and confession, he snatches you away into the place of the wonderful tabernacle, even to the house of God, that is, the inaccessible light, where he dwells, where he feeds, where he lies down at midday. For if the devotion of those singing psalms and praying has something of that holy curiosity of those who seek, \"Rabbi, where do you dwell?\" I think that they deserve to hear, \"Come and see.\" \"They came,\" it says, \"and saw, and remained with him that day\" (Joan. I, 38, 39). As long as we are with the Father of lights, with whom there is no change nor shadow of alteration, we know no night; we enjoy only the blessed day. When we slip away from there, we fall back into our own night. Woe to me! How quickly my days have failed, how quickly I have withered like grass\u2014I, who, as long as I was in the garden with him, flourished and blossomed like the paradise of God! With him, the garden is a place of delight; without him, a place of horror and vast solitude. For I think that whoever enters into his garden becomes himself a garden; and that his soul becomes like a watered garden, so that the Bridegroom too may say in praise of it: \"A garden enclosed, my sister, my spouse\" (Cant. IV, 12). Are they not a garden, in whom happens what the Gardener himself speaks to the planting which his Father planted? \"Listen to me,\" he says, \"you divine fruits, and bear fruit like the rose planted beside the streams of water\" (Eccli. XXXIX, 17). \"Have a sweet fragrance like frankincense. Bloom like the lily, give forth fragrance, and put forth leaves in grace.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O Domine Jesu, vere hortulane, operare in nobis quod exigis a nobis; nam sine te nihil possumus facere. Tu enim verus es hortulanus, idem creator qui cultor vel custos horti tui, qui verbo plantas, spiritu rigas, virtute incrementum das. Errabas, Maria, quae eum existimabas hortulanum illius horti pauperis et exigui, in quo sepultus fuit. Hortulanus est totius mundi, hortulanus est coeli, hortulanus est Ecclesiae, quam hic plantat et rigat, donec incremento consummato transplantet eam in terram viventium, secus decursus aquarum viventium, ubi non timebit cum venerit aestus, sed erit folium ejus viride, nec aliquando desinet facere fructum. Beati qui habitant in illis hortis tuis, Domine! in saecula saeculorum laudabunt te. Paulus habitabat in illis, cujus conversatio erat in coelis: qui mente saepius excedens perambulabat paradisum beatae voluptatis, hortos deliciarum Dei, ubi et pertransiens rosas Martyrum, et lilia Virginum, desinens etiam mirari altitudinem cedrorum Dei delectabatur potius carpere de fructu ligni vitae, quod est in medio paradisi, in ipso nimirum gustans plenius et felicius quam suavis est Dominus. Ideoque cum inde redibat, amicis auscultantibus memoriam abundantiae suavitatis ejus profusius eructabat, et ex abundantia cordis os redundabat. Quia enim sicut adipe et pinguedine repleta erat anima ejus, ideo labiis exsultationis laudabat os ejus. Cor quippe sapientis erudiet os ejus, et labiis illius addet gratiam (Prov. XVI, 23). Eructabat itaque cor ejus verbum bonum, ac de bono thesauro bonus bona proferebat, sponsumque ipsum qui in amicis auscultabat, velut jucundo carmine oblectabat. Prorsus carmen jucundum, suavisque melodia, ubi tam canorum Spiritus sancti organum,, tanta morum et sermonum consonantia, tanta dulcedine amoris, tanta gratia oris Christum Jesum jubilum cordis sui personabat. Si enim lingua suavis vim carminis non haberet, scriptum non esset: Tibiae et psalterium faciunt suavem melodiam, et super utraque lingua suavis (Eccli. XL, 21). Si carmine hujusmodi Psalmista Deum oblectari nesciret, nequaquam diceret: Jucundum sit ei eloquium meum (Psal. CIII, 31). Sed nec ipse sponsus diceret: Fac me audire vocem tuam; sonet vox tua in auribus meis. Vox enim tua suavis (Cantic. VIII, 13, et II, 14), quia exaltationes Dei in faucibus tuis, tanquam habitantis in hortis, in deliciis paradisi. E contra vero, Non est speciosa laus in ore peccatoris (Eccli. XV, 9), qui habitat in sepulcris. Cujus enim vita Deum exasperat, lingua non delectat, sed terribiliter increpatur voce divina: Quare tu enarras justitias meas; (Psal. XLIX, 16.) Cantica lyrae tuae non audiam (Amos V, 25)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. O Lord Jesus, true gardener, work in us what you require of us; for without you we can do nothing. For you are the true gardener, being yourself the creator who is also the tiller and keeper of your garden, who plant by the word, water by the Spirit, and give increase by power. You were mistaken, Mary, when you thought him the gardener of that poor and small garden in which he had been buried. He is the gardener of the whole world, the gardener of heaven, the gardener of the Church, which he plants and waters here, until, the growth being completed, he transplants her into the land of the living, beside the courses of living waters, where she shall not fear when the heat comes, but her leaf shall be green, and she shall never cease to bear fruit. Blessed are they who dwell in those gardens of yours, Lord! They shall praise you forever and ever. Paul dwelt in them, whose citizenship was in heaven: who, often going out of himself in mind, walked through the paradise of blessed delight, the gardens of God's delights, where, passing by the roses of the Martyrs and the lilies of the Virgins, and ceasing even to marvel at the height of the cedars of God, he delighted rather to pluck from the fruit of the tree of life, which is in the midst of paradise, in it indeed tasting. And so, when he returned from there, with his friends listening, he poured forth more abundantly the memory of the abundance of its sweetness, and out of the abundance of the heart his mouth overflowed. For because his soul was filled, as it were, with fat and fatness, therefore his mouth praised with lips of exultation. For the heart of the wise shall instruct his mouth, and shall add grace to his lips (Prov. XVI, 23). And so his heart poured forth a good word, and as a good man out of a good treasure brought forth good things, and he delighted, as it were with a pleasant song, the very Bridegroom who was listening in his friends. Truly a pleasant song, and a sweet melody, where so tuneful an instrument of the Holy Spirit, so great a harmony of conduct and of words, with so much sweetness of love, with so much grace of mouth, sounded forth Christ Jesus, the jubilation of his heart. For if a sweet tongue did not have the power of song, it would not be written: 'The pipe and the psaltery make sweet melody, but a pleasant tongue surpasses them both' (Eccli. XL, 21). If the Psalmist did not know that God is delighted by a song of this kind, he would by no means say: 'Let my speech be pleasing to him' (Psal. CIII, 31). Nor would the Bridegroom himself say: 'Make me hear your voice; let your voice sound in my ears. For your voice is sweet' (Cantic. VIII, 13, et II, 14), because 'the exaltations of God are in your throat,' as of one dwelling in the gardens, in the delights of paradise. But on the contrary, 'Praise is not seemly in the mouth of a sinner' (Eccli. XV, 9), who dwells among tombs. For whoever's life provokes God, his tongue does not delight him, but he is terribly rebuked by the divine voice: 'Why do you declare my justices?' (Psal. XLIX, 16.); 'I will not hear the songs of your lyre' (Amos V, 25).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed quoniam Scriptura quam assumpseram in excusationem sermonis, occasionem et materiam ministravit sermonis; si placet, finiamus quod de illa coeperamus. Dupliciter itaque potest intelligi, quod a sponso dicitur, Quae habitas in hortis, amici auscultant, fac me audire vocem tuam: vel quod devotum amatorem invitet ad psallendum vel orandum; vel quod sanctum praedicatorem excitet ad loquendum. Quod ut magis persuadeat, amicos auscultare memorat, angelos scilicet orantem vel psallentem; fideles praedicantem. Ubi primo intuemur, qua disciplina cordis et corporis nos oporteat in conspectu angelorum psallere vel orare, ne vacui dimissi vacuos nos dimittant, qui venerant ut vota deferrent, et dona referrent; imo recedant inimici qui venerant amici. Quinimo sponsus ipse, qui stat ad ostium et pulsat, si nulla ei digna devotio de intus respondens aperiat, recedet conquerens, et dicens: Attendi et auscultavi, nemo quod bonum est loquitur. Nullus est qui agat poenitentiam super peccato suo, dicens: Quid feci? omnes conversi sunt ad cursum suum, quasi equus impetu vadens in praelium (Jerem. VIII, 6). Si autem praedicatorem exhortatur, fiduciam ei loquendi tribuit de benevolentia et attentione auditorum, quia scilicet non increduli obloquuntur, aut litigant; non aemuli detrectant, aut subsannant; non tepidi dormitant, aut oscitant: sed amici attenti auscultant, quorum amor et meritum possit evangelizanti impetrare verbum et spiritum. Et bene amici auscultant. Est enim velut proprium amici, scilicet quod devotus audiat vocem sponsi, sicut Joannes ait: Amicus sponsi est qui stat, non vagans animo, aut prostratus somno, audit et gaudet gaudio propter vocem sponsi (Joan. III, 29), quam agnoscit etiam in servis. Et nos itaque inde probemus nos amicos, ut sive per os loquentis, sive legentis aut cantantis vox sponsi sonuerit, sic stemus ad audiendum, quatenus auditui nostro det gaudium et laetitiam; et non solum cum gaudio, suscipere verbum, sed et fructum afferre in patientia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But since the Scripture which I had taken up as an excuse for my sermon has furnished the occasion and material for the sermon, let us, if it please you, finish what we had begun concerning it. What is said by the Bridegroom\u2014'You who dwell in the gardens, the friends are listening; make me hear your voice'\u2014can therefore be understood in two ways: either as inviting the devout lover to sing psalms or to pray; or as rousing the holy preacher to speak. To make this more persuasive, he recalls that friends are listening\u2014namely, the angels listening to one praying or singing psalms, and the faithful listening to one preaching. Here we first consider with what discipline of heart and body we ought to sing psalms or pray in the sight of the angels, lest, being sent away empty, they in turn send us away empty\u2014they who had come to carry away our vows and bring back gifts; nay, lest those who had come as friends depart as enemies. Indeed, the Bridegroom himself, who stands at the door and knocks, if no devotion worthy of him answers from within and opens to him, will withdraw complaining, and say: 'I attended and listened; no one speaks what is good. There is none who does penance for his sin, saying, What have I done? They have all turned aside to their own course, like a horse rushing with impetuosity into battle' (Jerem. VIII, 6). But if he is exhorting the preacher, he grants him confidence to speak on account of the benevolence and attentiveness of his hearers\u2014namely, that the unbelieving do not speak against him or quarrel; that rivals do not disparage or mock; that the lukewarm do not doze or yawn: but that attentive friends listen, whose love and merit can obtain for the one preaching the gospel both the word and the spirit. And rightly do friends listen. For it is, as it were, proper to a friend that the devout one should hear the voice of the Bridegroom, just as John says: 'He that stands is the friend of the bridegroom'\u2014not wandering in mind, nor laid low in sleep\u2014'he hears him, and rejoices with joy because of the bridegroom's voice' (Joan. III, 29), which he recognizes even in his servants. Let us, then, likewise prove ourselves to be friends, so that whether through the mouth of one speaking, or reading, or singing, the voice of the Bridegroom sounds forth, we may so stand ready to listen that it gives to our hearing joy and gladness; and that we may not only receive the word with joy, but also bring forth fruit in patience.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The prayer asking God to make his speech pleasing comes from Psalm 103:34 in Vulgate numbering (104:34 in modern numbering); the edition prints Psal. CIII, 31."}, {"anchor": "guerric-canticle-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The words Cantica lyrae tuae non audiam come from Amos 5:23; the edition prints Amos V, 25."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "On the Words of the Song of Songs", "topics": ["Song of Songs", "Scripture", "Christ the gardener", "psalmody", "preaching", "Paul the Apostle"], "url": "sermons/guerric-canticle.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In epiphania Domini, sermo I, De donis Domino offerendis, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 46\u201350.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "Psalm 28:1\u20132 (source numbering)", "Proverbs 30:15", "Acts 3:6", "Proverbs 8:20\u201321, 18", "1 Corinthians 1:4\u20135, 7 (printed wording; see note)", "Apocalypse 3:17", "Ecclesiasticus 8:6 (printed locator)", "Matthew 13:44", "Jeremiah 41:8", "Job 10:2", "Song of Songs 1:12; 3:6; 4:6, 11 (source numbering)", "Matthew 27:48 (printed locator; see note)", "1 Timothy 4:5 (printed locator; see note)", "Proverbs 25:20; 21:26, 25 (printed locators)", "Isaiah 61:3, 10", "Psalm 29:12\u201313 (source numbering)", "2 Corinthians 6:12\u201313", "Job 37:17", "Lamentations 1:13", "Philippians 3:13, 12", "James 1:5"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric's first Epiphany sermon asks what gifts Christ's poor can offer the newborn King. Seven numbered sections interpret myrrh as penitential sorrow and toil, incense as devotion, and gold as wisdom. The address to brothers whose clothing scarcely suffices in winter places the argument in its monastic setting. This reading follows the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition; the approximate 1138\u20131157 composition range is distinct from that printing date. Editorial notes explain retained quotation variants and anomalous Scripture locators. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN EPIPHANIA DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE EPIPHANY OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De donis Domino offerendis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the gifts to be offered to the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Afferte Domino, filii Dei, afferte (Psal. xxviii, 1). Satius est ei afferre, quam sanguisugae, id est concupiscentiae, cujus duae filiae, voluptas et vanitas, non dant requiem die ac nocte, dicentes: Affer, affer (Prov. xxx, 15). Quid igitur afferemus Domino? Afferte Domino gloriam auri, et honorem thuris: afferte Domino gloriam nomini ejus, myrrham (Psal. xxviii, 1, 2) sepulturae ejus. Sed dicit mihi discipulus Christi, filius Petri: Argentum et aurum non est mihi (Act. iii, 6). Nec sacculi peregrinarum mercium myrrhae et thuris. Ergone vacuus apparebis in conspectu Domini, et cunabula novi Regis nullis honorabis xeniis? O dives paupertas, o locuples nuditas, si tamen Christiana et voluntaria! Quibus enim divitiis non abundas, non solum auri, sed etiam auri primi, auri igniti; non solum myrrhae et thuris, sed etiam universi pulveris pigmentarii? Imo qui sunt alii qui possint abundare divitiis hujusmodi, nisi pauperes Christi? In viis, inquit, justitiae ambulo, ut ditem diligentes me, et thesauros eorum repleam. Nec deest ei unde id possit: Mecum sunt, inquit, divitiae et gloria, opes superbae, et justitia (Prov. viii, 20, 21, 18). Bonae opes prorsus divitiae salutis, in quibus ita superbitur, ut justitia non deseratur. Fratres, superbia ista est gloria exsultantium in Domino, et insultantium mundo, quod nihil habeat tam pretiosum, ut paupertati valeat comparari sanctorum. O qui pusillo animo estis, cur non superbitis mecum in opibus istis? Superbiam istam non damnat, sed remunerat Magister humilitatis, si videlicet ita magnanimus sis in contemptu mundi, ut omnem gloriam ejus quasi de sublimi despicias, et nihili pendas prae amore et gloria tuae nuditatis. Omnino dives es, si in paupertate gloriaris, et de te quoque gratias aget congratulatio Apostoli: Gratias, inquit, ago Deo meo, quod in omnibus divites facti estis in illo, in omni verbo et sanctitate, ut nihil vobis desit in ulla gloria (I Cor. i, 4, 5, 7). Longe aliter illum alloquitur Dominus, qui sibi applaudebat de affluentia mundi hujus: Tu, inquit, dicis Quia dives sum, et nullius egeo; et tu miser es, et miserabilis, et pauper et caecus, et nudus (Apoc. iii, 17)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Bring to the Lord, children of God, bring (Psalm xxviii, 1). It is better to bring to him than to the leech, that is, concupiscence, whose two daughters, pleasure and vanity, give no rest day or night, saying: Bring, bring (Proverbs xxx, 15). What, then, shall we bring to the Lord? Bring to the Lord the glory of gold and the honour of incense; bring to the Lord the glory due to his name, myrrh (Psalm xxviii, 1, 2) for his burial. But a disciple of Christ, a son of Peter, says to me: Silver and gold I have none (Acts iii, 6). Nor have I bags of foreign wares, of myrrh and incense. Will you then appear empty-handed before the Lord, and honour the new King's cradle with no gifts? O rich poverty, O wealthy nakedness, provided that it is Christian and voluntary! For what riches do you not abound in: not only gold, but the finest gold, gold tried by fire; not only myrrh and incense, but every powder of the perfumer? Indeed, who else can abound in such riches except Christ's poor? I walk, he says, in the ways of justice, to enrich those who love me and fill their treasuries. Nor does he lack the means: With me, he says, are riches and glory, magnificent wealth and justice (Proverbs viii, 20, 21, 18). The riches of salvation are truly good wealth, in which one takes pride without abandoning justice. Brothers, this pride is the glory of those who exult in the Lord and mock the world because it has nothing so precious that it can compare with the poverty of the saints. O faint-hearted ones, why do you not take pride with me in these riches? The Master of humility does not condemn this pride but rewards it, provided that you are so great-hearted in despising the world that you look down, as from on high, upon all its glory and count it as nothing beside the love and glory of your nakedness. You are altogether rich if you glory in poverty, and the Apostle's rejoicing will give thanks for you too: I thank my God, he says, that in all things you have been made rich in him, in every word and in holiness, so that you lack nothing in any glory (1 Corinthians i, 4, 5, 7). Very differently does the Lord address the man who congratulated himself upon this world's abundance: You say, I am rich and need nothing; and you are wretched and miserable and poor and blind and naked (Apocalypse iii, 17).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sane inter divitias hujusmodi, pro quibus Paulus congratulabatur discipulis, non dubitaverim aurum, thus, et myrrham posse reperiri, quae digne et grate Christo nato queant offerri. At ego, inquies, non sum mihi conscius tale quid accepisse, nec aliquid tam pretiosum, ut est aurum, thus et myrrha, penes me posse inveniri. Ego enim, videns paupertatem meam, vix mendicabo stipem quotidianam, pauperem exigens vitam. Putas, inquit, te nihil tale accepisse? vide magis ne acceptam a Patre substantiam dissipaveris vivendo luxuriose. Sed omitto istud; non enim improperandum est, ut Sapiens ait, homini avertenti se a peccato (Eccli. viii, 6). Vellem magis ut non pigeret te tentare et quaerere si forte vel minimum aliquid paternae substantiae resedisset apud te, unde initium recuperandi posses capere. Vellem foderes intra te; solent namque pretiosi latere thesauri in abditis terrae. In agro absconditus erat thesaurus ille qui desiderio sui compulit hominem omnia sua vendere (Matth. xiii, 44). In agro se dicebant thesauros absconditos habere decem viri Israelitae, et per hoc gladium evaserunt homicidae (Jerem. xli, 8). O quanti thesauri bonorum operum, quantae divitiae piorum fructuum absconditi latent in agro humani corporis, et quanto plures in abdito cordis, si modo sit qui exerceat et fodiat! Neque illud Platonicum dico, quod anima ante corpus artes didicerit, quae oblivione et mole corporis obrutae, disciplina et industria refodiendae sint: sed quod ratio hominis et ingenium, juvante gratia, omnium sit virtutum seminarium. Si ergo ad cor redeas, et corpus exerceas, non diffidas te inventurum thesauros desiderabiles; et si non auri statim in initio, aut thuris, certe vel myrrhae non inutilis. Inutilem aut vilem tu ne dixeris, quam Christus acceptat in munere, qua sui corporis sepultura non solum praesignari, cum ei offerretur, sed etiam consummari voluit, cum ex ea perungeretur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Surely among those riches for which Paul congratulated his disciples, I would not doubt that gold, incense and myrrh can be found which may worthily and acceptably be offered to the newborn Christ. But I, you will say, am not aware of having received anything of the kind, nor can anything as precious as gold, incense and myrrh be found in my possession. Seeing my poverty, I can scarcely beg my daily pittance as I lead a poor life. Do you think, he says, that you have received nothing of the kind? Consider rather whether you have squandered the substance received from your Father by living in luxury. But I leave that aside; for, as the Wise Man says, one must not reproach a man who turns away from sin (Ecclesiasticus viii, 6). I would rather that you should not be reluctant to try and search whether perhaps some smallest part of your Father's substance remains with you, from which you might begin to recover. I would have you dig within yourself; for precious treasures often lie hidden in the secret places of the earth. Hidden in a field was that treasure which, through the desire for it, compelled a man to sell all he had (Matthew xiii, 44). Ten men of Israel said that they had treasures hidden in a field, and by this escaped the murderer's sword (Jeremiah xli, 8). O what treasures of good works, what riches of devout fruits lie concealed in the field of the human body, and how many more in the hidden heart, if only someone will cultivate and dig! I am not speaking of that Platonic notion that the soul learned the arts before entering the body, and that, buried by forgetfulness and the body's weight, they must be dug out again by instruction and effort; rather, that human reason and natural ability, with grace assisting, are a seedbed of all the virtues. If, then, you return to your heart and exercise your body, do not doubt that you will find desirable treasures; if not gold or incense immediately at the beginning, then certainly at least useful myrrh. Do not call useless or worthless what Christ accepts as a gift, by which he willed his body's burial not only to be foreshadowed when it was offered to him, but also to be accomplished when he was anointed with it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Si ergo manifestius vis audire, myrrha in corde tuo dolor est, myrrha in corpore tuo labor est, si tamen iste vel ille poenitentialis est. Myrrham siquidem utrumque esse non solum probat etymologia nominis, aut qualitas saporis, sed etiam medicinalis operatio virtutis. Myrrha namque amarissimum gustum sui etiam nomine prodit, effectu autem, praeter alias sui utilitates, corruptioni resistit. Et quid gustu amarius, quid effectu salubrius, quam dolor quo peccator ad poenitentiam contristatur? Recogitat annos suos in amaritudine animae suae, dicens Deo, Noli me condemnare (Job. x, 2); sed omnis haec amaritudo nihil aliud quam myrrha est, vindicans a corruptione tam luxuriarum, in quibus computruit, quam vermium immortalium, quos meruit. Quid vero labor corporis? Non tam myrrha est, quam fasciculus myrrhae, si credere volumus his qui de saeculo nuper venere; quibus regula jejuniorum et vigiliarum, quotidianum opus manuum, asperitas vestium, et omnia pene amara, quia insolita, velut in unum colligata fasciculum, imposita sunt ad ferendum. Et quidem amarissime portarent hunc myrrhae fasciculum, nisi quia, etsi nondum gaudent communicare passionibus Dilecti, ut dicant, Fasciculus myrrhae dilectus meus mihi (Cantic. i, 12), dulce tamen nimium habent amaritudinem cordis amaritudine mitigare exteriori, tanquam choleram stomachi potu absinthii. Sicut enim stomachus, immoderato usu dulcium corruptus, potione purgatur amara, sic eorum qui suaviter vixerunt in amaricata conscientia, nunquam melius quam contrario, id est, vita et consuetudine curantur austera. Maxime autem, si saepius detur eis bibere vinum myrrhatum Dominicae passionis, id est potentur vino compunctionis, quod quanto amarius est ex recordatione peccati, tanto salubrius est peccatori. Ideo Jesus noluit bibere (Matth. xxvii, 48), quia peccatum non fecerat, quod ei crucifixor imputabat, qui de amaritudine vitis alienae ei propinabat qui de genimine vitis verae, vinum novum cum amicis in regno Patris habebat bibere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. If, then, you wish to hear it more plainly, myrrh in your heart is sorrow; myrrh in your body is toil, provided that the one or the other is penitential. Both are myrrh, as is shown not only by the word's derivation or the nature of its taste, but also by the medicinal working of its power. For myrrh reveals its intensely bitter taste even by its name, while in its effects, besides its other uses, it resists corruption. And what is more bitter to taste, what more wholesome in its effect, than the sorrow by which a sinner is grieved into repentance? He recalls his years in the bitterness of his soul, saying to God: Do not condemn me (Job x, 2). Yet all this bitterness is nothing other than myrrh, rescuing him from the corruption both of the lusts in which he rotted and of the undying worms he has deserved. What, then, of bodily toil? It is not so much myrrh as a bundle of myrrh, if we believe those who have recently come from the world. The rule of fasts and vigils, daily manual work, rough clothing, and almost everything bitter because unfamiliar have been laid upon them to bear, as though bound into one bundle. They would indeed carry this bundle of myrrh with great bitterness, except that, even if they do not yet rejoice to share the sufferings of the Beloved and say, My beloved is a bundle of myrrh to me (Song of Songs i, 12), they nevertheless find it exceedingly sweet to soothe the bitterness of the heart with outward bitterness, like the stomach's bile with a drink of wormwood. For just as a stomach spoiled by excessive consumption of sweets is purged by a bitter draught, so those who have lived pleasantly are never better cured in their embittered conscience than by the opposite, that is, an austere life and habit. Especially is this so if they are often given the myrrhed wine of the Lord's Passion to drink, that is, the wine of compunction: the more bitter it is from remembering sin, the more wholesome it is for the sinner. For this reason Jesus would not drink (Matthew xxvii, 48), because he had committed no sin, which his crucifier imputed to him by offering him the bitterness of a foreign vine, whereas he was to drink new wine from the fruit of the true vine with his friends in his Father's kingdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Caeterum quanquam perfectis, qualis Timotheus erat, exercitatio corporalis ad modicum utilis sit comparatione pietatis (I Tim. iv, 5); quantum tamen rudibus et imperfectis, quales nos sumus, utilis sit, vos ipsi, fratres, vobis testes estis; quomodo scilicet redimit de corruptione vitam nostram amaritudo parcimoniae et laboris. Ipsi etenim scitis quomodo vermescerent corda, quomodo vermescerent corpora, si non quotidie de manibus laborantium distillaret myrrha. An non vermis luxuria? Nescio si alter nocentior. Ingreditur blandiendo, mordet ridendo, transforat delectando, perimit consensu voluntario. An non vermis acedia et tristitia? Sicut tinea, inquit, vestimento, et vermis ligno, ita tristitia viri nocet cordi (Prov. xxv, 20). Quid omnia desideria mala? an non vermes et ipsa? At in desideriis est omnis otiosus, et desideria occidunt pigrum (Id. xxi, 26, 25). Beatus igitur cui ad mortificandos vermes hujuscemodi, manus propriae distillant myrrham per universum sui, jamque praevenit ungere corpus suum in sepulturam. Sed suum dicam, an Jesu? Imo et Jesu. Corpus enim illius, membrum est Jesu. Quisquis ille est qui sic unctus est, non est quod timeat vermem qui non moritur; quia jam mortuus est ei vermis unde illi moritur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Yet although for the perfect, such as Timothy was, bodily exercise is useful for little in comparison with godliness (1 Timothy iv, 5), you yourselves, brothers, can testify how useful it is for beginners and the imperfect, such as we are: how the bitterness of frugality and toil redeems our life from corruption. For you yourselves know how hearts would breed worms, how bodies would breed worms, if myrrh did not drip daily from the hands of those who labour. Is lust not a worm? I do not know whether another is more harmful. It enters by flattering, bites with a laugh, pierces by giving pleasure, and kills through voluntary consent. Are sloth and sadness not a worm? As the moth, he says, harms clothing, and the worm wood, so a man's sadness harms his heart (Proverbs xxv, 20). What of all evil desires? Are they not worms too? But every idle man is among desires, and desires kill the sluggard (ibid. xxi, 26, 25). Blessed, then, is the man whose own hands drip myrrh over his whole being to mortify worms of this kind, and who thus anoints his body beforehand for burial. But should I say his body, or Jesus'? Indeed, Jesus' too. For his body is a member of Jesus. Whoever is thus anointed has no reason to fear the worm that does not die, because for him the worm through which he dies is already dead.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Myrrham itaque, et myrrham electam atque probatissimam Domino cum regibus rex et ipse obtulisti, si interim, cum non sufficit manus tua ad offerendum aurum sapientiae aut thus devotionis, saltem cor contritum corpusque afflictum cum amaritudine poenitentiae Domino sanctificasti. Existimo enim, sine praejudicio tamen melioris intellectus, myrrham esse primam oblationem incipientium, deinde thus proficientium, demumque aurum perfectorum; atque idcirco cum simul ab Evangelista nominata sunt, pretiosiora quaeque et digniora pro merito sui praelata esse in ordine nominum. Alias namque secundum ordinem profectuum myrrham thuri praepositam invenis, ut est illud, Sicut virgula fumi ex aromatibus myrrhae et thuris (Cantic. iii, 6). Et, Vadam ad montem myrrhae, et ad collem thuris (Id. iv, 6). Non parvus siquidem profectus est, cum de myrrha ad thus proficitur, cum de ista, quae ad usus est infirmitatis humanae, ad illud quod divinis et festivis deputatum est sacrificiis, pervenitur; ut qui sacrificium spiritus contribulati, corporisque humiliati offerebat non sine myrrha amaritudinis, jam sacrificium laudis offerat cum thure devotionis, et, juxta promissionem Domini, tanta consolatio immittatur lugentibus Sion, ut det eis coronam pro cinere, oleum gaudii pro luctu, pallium laudis pro spiritu moeroris (Isa. lxi, 3). Tunc cantat qui lugebat: Convertisti planctum meum in gaudium mihi, conscidisti saccum meum, spiritum moeroris; et circumdedisti me laetitia, pallio scilicet laudis, ut cantet tibi gloria mea, et non compungar (Psal. xxix, 12, 13). Gaudens enim gaudebo in Domino, et exsultabit anima mea in Deo meo, quia induit me vestimento salutis, et indumento laetitiae circumdedit me (Isa. lxi, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. You too, then, have offered myrrh to the Lord with the kings, yourself a king, and myrrh choice and most excellent, if meanwhile, when your hand cannot supply the gold of wisdom or the incense of devotion, you have at least consecrated to the Lord a contrite heart and an afflicted body with the bitterness of penance. For I think, without prejudice to a better understanding, that myrrh is the first offering of beginners, then incense that of those making progress, and finally gold that of the perfect; and that, when the Evangelist named them together, each more precious and worthy gift was therefore placed earlier in the order of names according to its worth. Elsewhere you find myrrh placed before incense according to the order of progress, as in the saying, Like a slender column of smoke from the spices of myrrh and incense (Song of Songs iii, 6); and, I will go to the mountain of myrrh and to the hill of incense (ibid. iv, 6). Indeed, it is no small progress to advance from myrrh to incense, from what serves human infirmity to what is assigned to divine and festal sacrifices; so that he who offered the sacrifice of a troubled spirit and a humbled body, not without the myrrh of bitterness, may now offer a sacrifice of praise with the incense of devotion. And, according to the Lord's promise, such consolation is sent to those mourning in Sion that he gives them a crown for ashes, the oil of joy for mourning, a mantle of praise for the spirit of sorrow (Isaiah lxi, 3). Then he who mourned sings: You have turned my lament into joy for me; you have torn my sackcloth, the spirit of sorrow; and surrounded me with gladness, that is, the mantle of praise, so that my glory may sing to you and I may not be pierced with sorrow (Psalm xxix, 12, 13). For rejoicing I will rejoice in the Lord, and my soul will exult in my God, because he has clothed me in the garment of salvation and surrounded me with the robe of gladness (Isaiah lxi, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. O fratres, quibus minus sufficiunt vestimenta vestrae paupertatis in hac asperitate hiemis, qui dicitis, Ante faciem frigoris hujus quis sustinebit? cur non induimini, quaeso, hoc vestimento laetitiae et salutis, hoc pallio laudis? Si Dominum laudatis, in Domino laudabitur anima vestra, et pallio laudis quo opertus est laudabilis nimis, vos quoque operiet et confovebit, quemadmodum gallina pullos suos sub alis, si modo velitis. Ah quoties voluit, et noluistis! Nunquid enim hoc pallium breve est, ut utrumque operire non possit? Non angustiamini, inquit, in nobis, angustiamini autem in visceribus vestris. Dilatamini et vos (II Cor. vi, 12, 13), et pallium dilatabit se usque ad vos. Non est abbreviata manus ejus, ut salvare nequeat: nec grande est ei ut consoletur nos, nisi pravitas nostra prohibeat. O qui algetis, pallium hoc calidum est; quia calor est, qui non modo interius, sed exterius calefacere potest. Nonne vestimenta tua calida sunt, cum terra tua perflata fuerit austro (Job xxxvii, 17); imo ignis missus in ossibus, ac vehementer accensus (Thren. i, 13)? Hoc igne thus illud adole, quod ubi cum regibus obtuleris, dirigatur oratio tua sicut incensum in conspectu Domini: et cum de sacrificio tuo odoratus fuerit odorem suavitatis, dicat tibi, Odor vestimentorum tuorum sicut odor thuris (Cantic. iv, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. O brothers, whose poor clothing does not suffice in the harshness of this winter, who say, Who can endure before the face of this cold? Why, I ask, do you not put on this garment of gladness and salvation, this mantle of praise? If you praise the Lord, your soul will be praised in the Lord, and with the mantle of praise in which he who is exceedingly praiseworthy is clothed, he will cover and warm you too, as a hen her chicks beneath her wings, if only you will. Ah, how often he has willed it, and you have not! Is this mantle so short that it cannot cover both? You are not straitened in us, he says, but in your own affections. Be enlarged yourselves too (2 Corinthians vi, 12, 13), and the mantle will widen to reach you. His hand is not shortened so that he cannot save; nor is it difficult for him to console us, unless our wickedness prevents it. O you who are cold, this mantle is warm; for it is a warmth that can heat not only within but without. Are not your garments warm when the south wind has blown over your land (Job xxxvii, 17); indeed, is it not fire sent into the bones and fiercely kindled (Lamentations i, 13)? With this fire burn that incense; when you have offered it with the kings, let your prayer be directed like incense before the Lord. And when he has smelled a sweet fragrance from your sacrifice, may he say to you: The fragrance of your garments is like the fragrance of incense (Song of Songs iv, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Jam vero de auro sapientiae (quae oblatio, ut diximus, perfectorum est) loquatur vobis qui illud assecutus est, quia nec novit illud nisi qui accipit. Qui ergo sapientiam didicit, sapientiam loquatur inter perfectos; et de auro quod possidet, murenulas aureas auribus vestris fabricet. Ego enim non arbitror comprehendisse me; sequor autem si quo modo comprehendam (Philipp. iii, 13, 12). Magnus prorsus ac beatus qui invenit sapientiam, et affluit prudentia: sapientiam, in contemplatione aeternorum; prudentiam, in administratione temporalium: vel, ut moderatius sapientem definiam, qui novit se ipsum regere et alium. Pretiosus est hoc aurum cunctis opibus, etiam thure et myrrha; et omnia quae desiderantur in virtutibus vel gratiis, huic non valent comparari. Denique sapiens, tanquam vir virtutum, dives in omnibus, et si praevalet aurum offerre in sublimi contemplatione vel prudenti administratione, non tamen negligit offere thus in devotione divini operis, myrrham in mortificatione sui. Nos igitur, fratres, quod habemus offeramus in gloriam novi Regis: quod minus habemus, ab ipso petamus cui offerre cupimus. Si quis indiget sapientia, postulet a Deo, qui dat omnibus affluenter (Jac. i, 5). Munera mea, data mea, ait Pater luminum, a quo omne donum perfectum, et omne datum optimum; ipsi gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now let one who has attained it speak to you of the gold of wisdom, which, as we have said, is the offering of the perfect; for no one knows it except the one who receives it. Let him, then, who has learned wisdom speak wisdom among the perfect, and from the gold he possesses fashion little golden ornaments for your ears. For I do not consider that I have attained; but I press on, if somehow I may attain (Philippians iii, 13, 12). Truly great and blessed is the man who finds wisdom and abounds in prudence: wisdom in contemplating eternal things, prudence in administering temporal things; or, to define the wise man more modestly, one who knows how to govern himself and another. This gold is more precious than all wealth, even incense and myrrh; and all that is desired in virtues or graces cannot compare with it. Finally, the wise man, as a man of virtues, rich in all things, even when able to offer gold in lofty contemplation or prudent administration, nevertheless does not neglect to offer incense in devotion to the divine office, and myrrh in the mortification of himself. Let us, then, brothers, offer what we have to the glory of the new King; what we lack, let us ask from him to whom we desire to offer it. If anyone lacks wisdom, let him ask of God, who gives abundantly to all (James i, 5). My gifts, my offerings, says the Father of lights, from whom comes every perfect gift and every bestowal that is best; to him be glory for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition\u2019s quotation of 1 Corinthians 1:5, 7 reads \u201choliness\u201d and \u201cglory,\u201d where the usual Vulgate reads \u201cknowledge\u201d and \u201cgrace.\u201d The translation follows the sermon\u2019s wording."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Latin reads Pretiosus est hoc aurum, an irregular adjective agreement meaning \u201cthis gold is precious.\u201d It also spells offerre as offere. Both forms are retained. The final phrase \u201cMy gifts, my offerings\u201d has no printed Bible reference."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Matthew 27:48 for the refusal of the drink, usually located at Matthew 27:34 (compare Mark 15:23). It cites 1 Timothy 4:5 for the bodily-exercise saying, usually located at 1 Timothy 4:8. The printed references are retained."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon I: Gifts to Offer the Lord", "topics": ["Epiphany", "poverty", "penance", "prayer", "wisdom", "monastic life"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In Epiphania Domini, sermo II, De variis modis quibus nos Deus illuminat, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 51-54.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "Isaiah 60:1", "Psalm 47:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 33:6 (historical numbering)", "Ephesians 5:8", "John 10:25", "Luke 3:16, 21\u201322", "Zechariah 6:12", "John 3:19", "1 Peter 2:9", "2 Corinthians 4:6", "John 17:11, 23", "Proverbs 4:18", "Daniel 12:3", "Philippians 2:15", "Matthew 5:14, 16", "Sirach 1:33", "Sirach 3:22", "Psalm 18:9 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 11:7", "Isaiah 26:7", "1 Timothy 4:7\u20139", "Job 36:32\u201333"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This second Epiphany sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. From Isaiah's \"Arise, be enlightened, Jerusalem\", Guerric gathers the day's three manifestations: the star to the Magi, the Jordan baptism, and the water made wine. The Magi's faith, which saw only weakness and still adored, shows how faith begins and grows. He bids his hearers look up to the lights of heaven, the saints, before they can bear the Sun itself. An editorial note records a printer's error. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De variis modis quibus nos Deus illuminat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Various Ways in Which God Enlightens Us", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum (Isa. LX, 1). Hodiernum diem luminum illuminavit nobis et consecravit lumen de lumine, quod hodie cum lateret incognitum, ad illuminationem omnium gentium sese dignatum est mundo revelare. Hodie namque se revelavit Chaldaeis novi sideris indicio, cum eorum primitiis omnium fidem gentium dedicavit. Hodie se revelavit Judaeis, non solum Joannis, sed et Patris et Spiritus sancti testimonio, ubi in Jordane baptizatus, baptisma omnium consecravit. Hodie manifestavit gloriam suam coram discipulis suis, quando mutatatione aquae in vinum, illud ineffabile praeostendit mysterium, in quo, verbo ipsius, substantiae mutantur rerum. His omnibus Dei apparitionibus praevidens Spiritus illuminandam Ecclesiae fidem, alloquitur eam sub typo et nomine Jerusalem, dicens: Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum. Venerat quidem lumen; in mundo erat, et mundus per ipsum factus erat; sed mundus ipsum non cognoscebat. Erat natus, sed non erat notus, donec declarare eum coepit luminosa dies ista. Ait ergo propheta: O Jerusalem nova, civitas magna novi regis, mons Sion, latera aquilonis (Psal. XLVII, 3), id est, de utroque aedificanda pariete circumcisionis et praeputii; Surge, illuminare, quia venit lumen tuum. Surgite, qui sedetis in tenebris; intendite lumini, quod exortum est in tenebris, sed non comprehenditur a tenebris. Accedite ad eum, et illuminamini (Psal. XXXIII, 6), et in lumine ejus videbitis lumen, diceturque vobis, Fuistis aliquando tenebrae, nunc autem lux in Domino (Ephes. V, 8). Intendite lumini aeterno quod se vestris aspectibus temperavit, ut, qui lucem habitat inaccessibilem, infirmis quoque et lippientibus oculis posset accedi. Advertite lumen in lucerna testae, solem in nube, Deum in homine, in luteo vasculo carnis nostrae splendorem gloriae, candoremque lucis aeternae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Arise, be enlightened, Jerusalem, for your light has come\" (Isa. LX, 1). The Light from Light has illumined and consecrated this day of lights for us: the Light which, though today it lay hidden and unknown, deigned to reveal itself to the world for the enlightenment of all nations. For today he revealed himself to the Chaldeans by the sign of a new star, when in their firstfruits he dedicated the faith of all the nations. Today he revealed himself to the Jews, not only by John's testimony, but by the testimony of the Father and the Holy Spirit, when, baptized in the Jordan, he consecrated the baptism of all. Today he manifested his glory before his disciples, when, by the changing of water into wine, he showed forth beforehand that ineffable mystery in which, by his own word, the substances of things are changed. Foreseeing that the faith of the Church would be enlightened by all these appearings of God, the Spirit addresses her under the type and name of Jerusalem, saying: Arise, be enlightened, Jerusalem, for your light has come. Indeed the light had come; it was in the world, and the world was made through it; but the world did not know it. He had been born, but was not yet known, until this day of light began to declare him. The prophet therefore says: O new Jerusalem, great city of the new King, Mount Sion, on the sides of the north (Psal. XLVII, 3), that is, to be built up from both walls, of circumcision and of the uncircumcision; Arise, be enlightened, for your light has come. Arise, you who sit in darkness; attend to the light which has risen in the darkness, but is not comprehended by the darkness. Come to him, and be enlightened (Psal. XXXIII, 6), and in his light you will see light, and it will be said to you: You were once darkness, but now you are light in the Lord (Ephes. V, 8). Attend to the eternal light which has tempered itself to your sight, so that he who dwells in unapproachable light might also be approached even by weak and bleary eyes. Observe the light in the lamp of clay, the sun in the cloud, God in man, in the little earthen vessel of our flesh the splendor of glory and the brightness of eternal light.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Latet quidem majestas in humanitate, virtus in humilitate; sed signa virtutum erumpentia, unde haec prodeant, non sinunt ambigere. Opera, inquit, quae ego facio, testimonium perhibent de me (Joan. X, 25). Magnum quidem testimonium Joannis, qui venit ut testimonium perhiberet, lucerna de lumine; at enim multo majus illud testimonium coeleste, quod Pater et Spiritus sanctus Filio perhibuere (Luc. III, 16, 21, 22), Pater, in voce, Spiritus, in specie, cum in ore duorum testium stet omne verbum. Sed et hoc ipsum si minus accipitur: Opera, inquit, innumerabilia et irrefragabilia, quae ego facio, testimonium perhibent de me (Joan. X, 25). Plane incorruptus et irrevincibilis testis, cum creatura insensibilis sensum quemdam accipit in confessionem Creatoris, voluntatem ejus exaudiens, ac nutui incunctanter obediens. Quid enim divinius poterat exhiberi sensibus mortalium, quam hodiernae novitatis signum, quod etiam ante signorum suorum praemisit Jesus initium? Vagit puer novus in terris, et sidus novum creat in supernis, ut lumen testetur de lumine, stella de sole, et reges in splendore ortus sui, ad exortum quoque aeternum splendorem, ab oriente ad verum Orientem, id est, virum cui nomen Oriens (Zach. VI, 12), stella perducat; non quasi stella, sed quasi rationale animal in via praecedens, in fine viae consistens, ac velut digito ipsum qui quaerebatur ostendens. Sed esto, calumnietur infidelis hanc stellam non esse opus Christi, dummodo confiteatur opus esse Patris in testimonium Filii. Attamen obruet eum multitudo infinita ac magnitudo mirabilium plane divinorum, quae absque contradictione Jesus noster operatus est in carne, sed non ex carne."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Majesty indeed lies hidden in humanity, power in humility; but the signs of power breaking forth do not allow us to doubt whence they proceed. \"The works,\" he says, \"that I do bear witness of me\" (Joan. X, 25). Great indeed is the testimony of John, who came to bear witness, a lamp testifying of the Light; but far greater is that heavenly testimony which the Father and the Holy Spirit bore to the Son (Luc. III, 16, 21, 22), the Father in the voice, the Spirit in the visible form, since every word stands firm in the mouth of two witnesses. But if even this be deemed insufficient: \"the works,\" he says, \"innumerable and irrefutable, which I do, bear witness of me\" (Joan. X, 25). Clearly an incorruptible and unconquerable witness, when insensible creation receives, as it were, a certain sense in confessing the Creator, hearkening to his will and obeying his command without delay. For what more divine sign could be shown to mortal senses than the sign of today's newness, which Jesus sent forth even before the beginning of his own [other] signs? A new child cries on earth, and creates a new star in the heavens above, so that light might testify of Light, the star of the Sun, and lead kings, by the splendor of its rising, to the eternal Splendor, who likewise has arisen\u2014from the east to the true East, that is, to the man whose name is the Orient (Zach. VI, 12)\u2014the star leading them not as a mere star, but as a rational creature going before on the way, standing still at the end of the way, and pointing out, as it were with a finger, the very one who was being sought. But suppose the unbeliever should slander this, saying that the star is not the work of Christ\u2014so long as he confesses it to be the work of the Father in testimony of the Son. Yet even so he will be overwhelmed by the infinite multitude and the magnitude of the plainly divine wonders which our Jesus worked in the flesh, but not from the flesh, without any possibility of contradiction.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed operiant tenebrae terram, et caligo populos, qui, cum lux venit in mundum, dilexerunt magis tenebras quam lucem (Joan. III, 19); dummodo tu illumineris, Jerusalem, civitas coelestis, quae in omni terra et populo, Deo paritura es filios lucis, quos de potestate tenebrarum transferet in regnum claritatis suae lumen verum. Gratias tibi, Pater luminum, qui de tenebris nos vocasti in admirabile lumen tuum (I Petr. II, 9). Gratias tibi, qui dixisti de tenebris lumen splendescere, et illuxisti in cordibus nostris ad illuminationem scientiae in facie Christi Jesu (II Cor. IV, 6). Haec siquidem est lux vera, imo vita aeterna, ut cognoscamus te unum Deum, et quem misisti Jesum Christum. Cognoscimus te, quia cognoscimus Jesum, quoniam Pater et Filius unum (Joan. XVII, 23, 11). Cognoscimus quidem per fidem, ipsam tenentes arrham fidelem, quia cognoscemus te per speciem. Interim tamen auge nobis fidem, deducens nos ex fide in fidem, a claritate in claritatem, tanquam a tuo Spiritu, ut de die in diem thesauros luminis profundius ingrediamur, quo fides amplior, scientia plenior, charitas ferventior fiat et diffusior, donec per fidem perducamur ad faciem, tanquam per stellam praeducem ad nostrum Bethlehemiticum ducem, qui egressus de Bethlehem regit Israelem, et regnat in Jerusalem; non ista quae occidit prophetas, ipsumque Dominum prophetarum, sed illa in qua coronat occisos idem Dominus, causa, virtus, et gloria martyrum"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But let darkness cover the earth, and gloom the peoples, who, when the light came into the world, loved darkness rather than the light (Joan. III, 19); provided only that you be enlightened, O Jerusalem, heavenly city, who in every land and people are to bear sons of light for God\u2014sons whom the true light will transfer from the power of darkness into the kingdom of its own brightness. Thanks be to you, Father of lights, who have called us out of darkness into your wonderful light (I Petr. II, 9). Thanks be to you, who said that light should shine out of darkness, and who have shone in our hearts for the illumination of the knowledge in the face of Christ Jesus (II Cor. IV, 6). For this indeed is the true light, nay rather eternal life, that we should know you the one God, and him whom you sent, Jesus Christ. We know you because we know Jesus, since the Father and the Son are one (Joan. XVII, 23, 11). We know you indeed through faith, holding fast that same faithful pledge, because we shall know you through vision. Meanwhile, however, increase our faith, leading us from faith to faith, from brightness to brightness, as it were by your own Spirit, so that from day to day we may enter more deeply into the treasures of light, whereby faith may become ampler, knowledge fuller, charity more ardent and more widely diffused, until through faith we be brought to the vision of your face, as it were through a guiding star to our Bethlehem-born Leader, who, having gone forth from Bethlehem, rules Israel, and reigns in Jerusalem\u2014not that Jerusalem which killed the prophets, and the very Lord of the prophets, but that one in which the same Lord crowns those who were slain\u2014he who is the cause, the strength, and the glory of the martyrs.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O quanto gaudio ibi tripudiat fides Magorum, cernentium in illa Jerusalem regnantem, quem in Bethlehem adoraverunt vagientem! Hic visus est in diversorio pauperum, ibi in palatio videtur Angelorum; hic in pannis parvulorum, ibi in splendoribus sanctorum; hic in gremio Matris, ibi in solio Patris. Plane digna beatorum fides Magorum, ut tam felici visione remuneretur; quae cum in eo nihil nisi infirmum et contemptibile videret, non tamen scandalizari potuit, quominus Deum in homine, et hominem in Deo veneraretur. Procul dubio stella ex Jacob illuxerat in cordibus eorum; stella, inquam, matutina, lucifer qui nescit occasum, qui etiam foris stellam, matutini ortus sui indicem, accenderat cenderat in cordibus eorum. De his omnino convenienter intelligi potest quod apud Salomonem scriptum est: Justorum semita, quasi lux splendescens procedit, et crescit usque ad perfectum diem (Prov. IV, 18). Primo namque semitam justitiae ingressi sunt ad lucem splendentis sideris, cujus ductu profecerunt ad videndum novum ortum matutinae, lucis sicque demum pervenerunt ad contemplandam faciem meridiani Solis, in die virtutis suae rutilantis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Oh, with what great joy does the faith of the Magi there leap for joy, beholding reigning in that Jerusalem the one whom they adored crying as an infant in Bethlehem! Here he was seen in the lodging-place of the poor, there he is seen in the palace of the Angels; here in the swaddling clothes of infants, there in the splendors of the saints; here in the lap of his Mother, there on the throne of the Father. Truly worthy was the faith of the blessed Magi, to be rewarded with so happy a vision\u2014they who, though they saw in him nothing but what was weak and contemptible, could not be scandalized so as to be kept from venerating God in man, and man in God. Without doubt the star out of Jacob had shone in their hearts; the star, I say, the morning star, the light-bearer who knows no setting, who had kindled also outwardly the star, the sign of his own morning rising, within their hearts. Of these it can altogether fittingly be understood what is written in Solomon: The path of the just, as a shining light, goes forward, and increases even to perfect day (Prov. IV, 18). For first they entered the path of righteousness by the light of the shining star, under whose guidance they advanced to the seeing of the new rising of the morning light, and so at last they arrived at the contemplation of the face of the noonday Sun, in the day of his glowing power.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Pulchre igitur signanterque in his Gentium primitiis, in his nascentis Ecclesiae primordiis, praeostensus est fidei profectus in singulis: unde scilicet incipiat, quomodo proficiat, et quo demum perveniat; ut facile ipsorum qui patres sunt, in filiis agnoscantur vestigia. Sicut illi coeperunt a visione sideris, profecerunt ad visionem pueri, pervenerunt ad visionem Dei, sic in nobis fides nascitur supernorum praedicatione luminarium; roboratur visione quarumdam imaginum, per speculum et in aenigmate Deum velut incarnatum nobis exhibentium; consummabitur, cum praesens et nuda videbitur veritas rerum facie ad faciem contemplantibus quod vix tenuiter et raptim in aenigmate nunc attingitur; cum et ipsa fides transibit in cognitionem, spes in possessionem, desiderium in fruitionem. Lucent namque et nobis stellae, non una, sed multae; nisi quia multae una, quarum scilicet cor unum est, et anima una, fides eadem, praedicatio consona, similisque vita. Quae sint hae stellae, si dubitas, Danielem interroga: Qui ad justitiam, inquit, erudiunt multos, quasi stellae in perpetuas aeternitates (Dan. XII, 3). Paulus etiam luminaria vocat eos, qui lucent in medio nationis pravae et perversae (Philipp. II, 15), continentque verbum vitae, tanquam splendorem mutuatum ab aeterna luce, quo et noctem hujus mundi jussi sunt illuminare. Ideo Dominus fons et origo lucis, cum ordinaret lunam et stellas in potestatem noctis, dicebat illis, Vos estis lux mundi: et iterum, Sic luceat lux vestra coram hominibus, ut videant opera vestra bona, et glorificent Patrem vestrum, qui in coelis est (Matth. V, 14, 16). Lucent itaque verbo, lucent exemplo, et utroque lucis radio ortum aeternae lucis annuntiant, dum coelestem, quem voce praedicant, etiam coelestis vitae similitudine commendant."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Beautifully then and significantly, in these firstfruits of the Gentiles, in these beginnings of the nascent Church, the progress of faith in each soul was shown beforehand: namely, whence it begins, how it advances, and to what at last it arrives; so that the footsteps of the fathers may easily be recognized in the children. Just as they began from the sight of the star, advanced to the sight of the child, and arrived at the sight of God, so in us faith is born from the preaching of the heavenly luminaries; it is strengthened by the vision of certain images, which, through a mirror and in a riddle, display God to us as it were incarnate; it will be consummated when the present and naked truth of things shall be seen, face to face, by those who contemplate it\u2014that truth which is now attained but faintly and fleetingly, in a riddle\u2014when faith itself shall pass into knowledge, hope into possession, desire into enjoyment. For stars shine for us too\u2014not one, but many; except that many are one, namely those whose heart is one, and soul one, faith the same, preaching harmonious, and life alike. If you doubt what these stars are, ask Daniel: Those who instruct many unto righteousness, he says, shall shine as stars for all eternity (Dan. XII, 3). Paul too calls them luminaries, those who shine in the midst of a crooked and perverse nation (Philipp. II, 15), and who hold fast the word of life, as a brightness borrowed from the eternal light, by which they are also commanded to illumine the night of this world. Therefore the Lord, the fountain and origin of light, when he ordained the moon and the stars for dominion over the night, said to them, You are the light of the world; and again, Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven (Matth. V, 14, 16). They shine therefore by word, they shine by example, and by both rays of light they announce the rising of the eternal light, while they commend, by the likeness of a heavenly life, that heavenly one whom they preach by voice.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Suspicite, fratres, in coelum; in coelo siquidem est conversatio eorum; suspicite, et intendite mentis oculos, si necdum potestis in rotam solis, saltem in fulgorem siderum; admiramini splendorem sanctorum, imitamini fidem, aemulamini sanctitatem. Stellae istae sicut flamma coruscant, et lumen luminum ortum demonstrant; ducunt ad cunabula novi Regis, ad cubiculum Virginis matris, quod est inviolabile fidei mysterium; imo perducent in templum Regis, in sanctuarium Dei Patris; quod est incogitabile fidei praemium. Interim autem, dum idonei non sumus, sapientiam Dei, quae in mysterio abscondita est, scrutari, vel majestatem quae in praemium reposita est, contemplari; contenti simus sanctorum claritatem mirari, sanctitatem imitari. Fili, inquit, concupisti sapientiam? serva mandata; et Dominus praebebit tibi illam (Eccli. I, 33). Altiora autem te ne quaesieris, et fortiora te ne scrutatus fueris: sed quae praecipit Dominus tibi, ipsa conserva semper (Eccli. III, 22). Praeceptum namque Domini lucidum, illuminans oculos (Psal. XVIII, 9), quibus postmodum ipsum corpus luminis valeas intueri, cum in luce radiorum, id est, in opere mandatorum, versari consueveris. Dulce quidem lumen, ut ait Salomon, et delectabile oculis videre solem (Eccle. XI, 7). Dulce prorsus et delectabile, sed illis quibus tolerabile. Sanos oculos fovet, infirmos torquet. Et quis est tam sano et acuto visu, ut in aspectu illius invisibilis Solis non caecutiat? quis ille scrutator majestatis, qui non opprimatur a gloria? Mirabilis facta est, o Domine, claritas tua, tum ex se, tum etiam ex me; quia confortata est adversum me, dum acies mea infirmata est in me, et jam non potero ad eam, sicut poteram in Adam. Potero forsitan ad claritatem siderum, qui non possum ad solem ipsum, et per hanc quandoque confortabor ad illum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Look up, brothers, to heaven; for their way of life is in heaven. Look up, and fix the eyes of your mind\u2014if you cannot yet gaze upon the wheel of the sun, at least upon the brightness of the stars. Admire the splendor of the saints, imitate their faith, strive to equal their holiness. These stars flash like flame, and show forth the rising of the light of lights; they lead to the cradle of the new King, to the chamber of the Virgin Mother\u2014which is the inviolable mystery of faith; indeed, they will lead us further, into the temple of the King, into the sanctuary of God the Father\u2014which is the inconceivable reward of faith. Meanwhile, however, since we are not fit to search out the wisdom of God which lies hidden in mystery, or to contemplate the majesty laid up as reward, let us be content to admire the brightness of the saints and to imitate their holiness. Son, it says, have you desired wisdom? Keep the commandments, and the Lord will give it to you (Eccli. I, 33). But do not seek things higher than yourself, and do not search into things stronger than yourself; but keep always what the Lord commands you (Eccli. III, 22). For the commandment of the Lord is lightsome, enlightening the eyes (Psal. XVIII, 9), with which you may afterward be able to gaze upon the very body of light itself, once you have grown accustomed to dwell in the light of its rays\u2014that is, in the doing of the commandments. Light is sweet indeed, as Solomon says, and it is delightful to the eyes to see the sun (Eccle. XI, 7). Sweet indeed and delightful, but only to those for whom it is bearable. It soothes sound eyes, it torments weak ones. And who has sight so sound and so keen that he would not be blinded at the sight of that invisible Sun? Who is that searcher of majesty who would not be overwhelmed by its glory? Wonderful, O Lord, has your brightness become, both in itself and also because of me; for it has grown strong against me, since my sight has grown weak within me, and now I can no longer attain to it as I could in Adam. Perhaps I shall be able to attain to the brightness of the stars, I who cannot attain to the sun itself, and through this I shall in time be strengthened toward that.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Eia, surge, illuminare, anima mea, quae sedebas in tenebris suspice luminaria coeli, leva oculos tuos in montes unde veniet auxilium tibi, si veneris ad illum qui habitat in coelis, id est, in montibus ipsis. A montibus, inquam, veniet auxilium tibi, quia lux tibi inaccessibilis illuminat mirabiliter a montibus aeternis. Susceperunt montes lucem populo, a quibus ad valles camposque descendat subjectos. Hoc itaque, dico, fratres, optimum est, nostraeque infirmitati congruum illuminationis initium, si intendamus in eos qui illuminati sunt. Rectissima via est inveniendi Jesum, si praecedentium patrum sequamur lumen praevium. Semita justi recta est, rectus callis justi ad ambulandum (Isa. XXVI, 7). Qui justum sequitur, non ambulat in tenebris; non modo videbit, sed habebit lumen vitae. Videbit ad consolationem praesentis vitae: habebit in possessionem haereditatis aeternae. Pietas nempe promissionem habet vitae quae nunc est, et futurae. Exerceamur ad pietatem, et neutro nos fraudabit qui se ultro nobis fecit debitorem (I Tim. IV, 8, 7, 9). Exerceamur in operibus lucis, et qui abscondit lucem in manibus suis, annuntians de ea amico suo, quod possessio ejus sit, et ad eam possit ascendere (Job XXXVI, 32, 33), nunc aliquando in solatium operis eam nobis demonstrabit, et postmodum in praemium dabit, ipse lux nostra Jesus Christus qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Come now, arise, be enlightened, my soul, you who were sitting in darkness; look up at the lights of heaven; lift up your eyes to the mountains, whence help will come to you, if you come to him who dwells in the heavens\u2014that is, in those very mountains. From the mountains, I say, will help come to you, because the light inaccessible to you shines wonderfully from the eternal mountains. The mountains have received light for the people, that from them it may descend to the valleys and plains lying below. This, then, I say, brothers, is the best and most fitting beginning of enlightenment for our weakness: that we should fix our gaze upon those who have been enlightened. It is the most direct way of finding Jesus, if we follow the guiding light of the fathers who went before us. The path of the just is straight; the way of the just is straight for walking (Isa. XXVI, 7). He who follows the just man does not walk in darkness; he will not only see but will possess the light of life. He will see it for the consolation of the present life; he will possess it as an eternal inheritance. For godliness holds the promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come. Let us train ourselves unto godliness, and he who has of his own accord made himself our debtor will cheat us of neither (I Tim. IV, 8, 7, 9). Let us exercise ourselves in the works of light; and he who hides light in his hands, declaring to his friend concerning it that it is his possession and that he can ascend to it (Job XXXVI, 32, 33), will now at times show it to us for the comfort of our labor, and afterward will give it as our reward\u2014he himself, our light, Jesus Christ, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints mutatatione, evidently a printer's doubling of mutatione (the changing of water into wine). The printed form is kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon II: On the Various Ways God Enlightens Us", "topics": ["Epiphany", "light", "Magi", "faith", "manifestation of Christ"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In Epiphania Domini, sermo III, Quibus gradibus ad sapientiae lumen perveniatur, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 54-59.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "Isaiah 60:1", "2 Corinthians 4:3", "Psalm 17:29 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 6:22, 34", "Romans 13:12", "Ephesians 5:8", "John 9:41", "Mark 8:22\u201324", "Psalm 39:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 18:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 35:4 (historical numbering)", "2 Kings 4:7 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Samuel)", "Isaiah 60:5", "Isaiah 2:3", "Proverbs 31:18", "John 3:20", "Sirach 23:26\u201327", "Hosea 10:12", "Colossians 1:10", "Psalm 118:66, 100 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:30", "Isaiah 11:9"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This third Epiphany sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Guerric sets out the steps by which the soul comes to the light of wisdom: faith first, then righteousness, then knowledge, and at last wisdom, the taste of eternal things. Along the way he asks for the light to see one's own sins, and he draws on the healing of the blind man in two stages and on David's understanding gained from the commandments. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quibus gradibus ad sapientiae lumen perveniatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "By What Steps One Comes to the Light of Wisdom", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum (Isa. LX, 1). Benedictum lumen quod venit in nomine Domini, Deus Dominus, et illuxit nobis; de cujus munere etiam dies iste, dies sanctificatus de illuminatione Ecclesiae illuxit nobis. Gratias tibi, lux vera, quae illuminas omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum, quae ad hoc ipsum venisti homo in hunc mundum. Illuminata est Jerusalem mater nostra, mater omnium qui illuminari meruerunt: adeo ut jam luceat omnibus qui in mundo sunt. Gratias tibi, lux vera, quae lucerna es facta, ut Jerusalem illuminares, et lucerna pedibus meis Verbum Dei fieret. Gratias, inquam, tibi, quoniam ipsa Jerusalem illuminata facta est lucerna, ut luceat omnibus qui sunt in domo magni patris familias. Non solum enim illuminata est, sed exaltata, et super candelabrum illud totum aureum sita est; civitas super montem montium, illa quondam derelicta et odio habita, posita est in superbiam saeculorum, ut tam longe lateque fulgeat ejus Evangelium, quam longe lateque mundi vigebat imperium. Et si opertum est ejus Evangelium, in his qui pereunt opertum est (II Cor. IV, 3), in quibus Deus hujus saeculi excaecavit mentes infidelium, ut non fulgeat eis gloria Ecclesiae Christi. Quibus tamen non fulget ut videant, fulget ut invideant, et Ecclesiae torquentur gloria, qui ipsius illuminari nolunt gratia. Verum Ecclesia nec propter gloriam qua floret, superbiens, nec gratiam qua pollet, invidens, etiam illis quos molestos tolerat, lumen quod accepit implorat, dicens: Quoniam tu illuminas lucernam meam, Domine; Deus meus, illumina tenebras meas (Psal. XVII, 29). Mei forsitan sunt, quia praedestinati; sed adhuc tenebrae sunt, quia nondum vocati aut justificati. Voca et ipsos in admirabile lumen tuum, et praedicabunt mecum admirabile nomen tuum. Quanquam justus quisque ac sanctus in Ecclesia ita se illuminatum gaudeat, ut adhuc se non minima ex parte tenebrosum videat et doleat; et ideo necessario, cum illuminatus sit, adhuc se illuminari petat. Quanto namque amplius lucerna ejus illuminata fuerit, tanto verius ex lucerna ipsa tenebras suas deprehendit. Nec statim contrarium putaveris, quod in Evangelio Veritas ait: Lucerna corporis tui est oculus tuus. Si oculus tuus simplex fuerit, totum corpus tuum lucidum erit (Matth. VI, 22 et Luc. XI, 34). Non enim, quia ex oculo sincerae intentionis totum opus nostrum lucidum fit, ideo consequens est ut statim omnes tenebrae ignorantiarum nostrarum illuminatae sint. Is nempe modus est adhuc illuminationis nostrae, ut in lumen veritatis multum existimetur profecisse, qui defectum suum potuerit nosse, et scire quid desit sibi. Inde est quod et apud sapientes hujus mundi, qui magis sobrie disputaverunt de scientia, primus, numeratur gradus scientiae, scire quod nescias."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Arise, be enlightened, O Jerusalem, for thy light has come (Isa. LX, 1). Blessed is the light that comes in the name of the Lord \u2014 God is the Lord, and he has shone upon us; by whose gift this day too, a day made holy by the enlightenment of the Church, has shone upon us. Thanks be to thee, true light, who dost enlighten every man coming into this world, who for this very purpose didst come as man into this world. Jerusalem our mother has been enlightened, the mother of all who have deserved to be enlightened, so that now she shines for all who are in the world. Thanks be to thee, true light, who hast become a lamp, that thou mightest enlighten Jerusalem, and that the Word of God might become a lamp to my feet. Thanks, I say, to thee, because Jerusalem herself, once enlightened, has become a lamp, so that she might shine for all who are in the house of the great father of the family. For she was not only enlightened but exalted, and set upon that lampstand all of gold; a city upon the mountain of mountains \u2014 she who was once forsaken and held in hatred \u2014 has been set up as the pride of the ages, that her Gospel might shine as far and wide as the empire of the world once flourished far and wide. And if her Gospel is veiled, it is veiled in those who are perishing (II Cor. IV, 3), in whom the god of this world has blinded the minds of unbelievers, so that the glory of the Church of Christ may not shine upon them. Yet for them it does not shine so that they may see, but shines so that they may envy, and they are tormented by the glory of the Church, they who refuse to be enlightened by her grace. But the Church, neither growing proud on account of the glory in which she flourishes, nor begrudging the grace by which she is strong, even for those whom she patiently endures as troublesome, begs for the light she has received, saying: For thou wilt light my candle, O Lord; O my God, enlighten my darkness (Psal. XVII, 29). Perhaps they are mine, because predestined; but they are still darkness, because not yet called or justified. Call them too into thy wonderful light, and they will proclaim thy wonderful name together with me. Although every just and holy person in the Church rejoices that he has been enlightened, yet he sees and grieves that he is still, in no small part, dark; and so, necessarily, even though he has been enlightened, he still asks to be enlightened further. For the more his lamp has been enlightened, the more truly does he discover his own darkness by that very lamp. And you should not at once think this contrary to what the Truth says in the Gospel: The lamp of thy body is thine eye. If thine eye be single, thy whole body shall be full of light (Matth. VI, 22 et Luc. XI, 34). For it is not the case that, because our whole work is made full of light from the eye of sincere intention, all the darkness of our ignorance is therefore at once illumined. For this indeed is still the measure of our enlightenment, that he is reckoned to have made great progress in the light of truth who has been able to know his own defect, and to know what he lacks. Hence it is that even among the wise men of this world, who have discussed knowledge more soberly, the first degree of knowledge is reckoned to be this: to know what you do not know.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Fratres, omnes vos filii lucis estis, et filii Dei. Non sumus noctis, neque tenebrarum. Nox enim praecessit, dies autem appropinquavit (Rom. XIII, 12). Et si fuimus aliquando tenebrae, nunc autem lux in Domino (Ephes. V, 8). Verumtamen si, quoniam non sumus tenebrae nec filii tenebrarum, dixerimus nos nihil pati tenebrarum, nos ipsos seducimus, et tenebras mortis, quae illuminari non mereantur, nobis inducimus. Quid enim, ait Lux mundi, quae in judicium venit in hunc mundum, ut qui non vident videant, et qui vident caeci fiant? Quia, inquit, dicitis, Videmus, peccatum vestrum manet (Joan. IX, 41). Video quantulumcunque, quoniam tu illuminas lucernam meam, o Domine, sed, quia parum est quod video, Deus meus, illumina tenebras meas (Psal. XVII, 29). Videmus ut multum per speculum et in aenigmate; sed multum longe est hoc ipsum a claritate vera claraque veritate, quae videbitur in facie. Ideo forsitan caecus, quem apud Marcum Dominus illuminare coeperat, homines quasi arbores ambulantes videbat (Marc. VIII, 22-24), quia videlicet non nisi per speculum et in aenigmate videbat; sed secundo imponens manum restituit eum, ita ut videret clare omnia; quod in nobis fiet, cum nos reficiet, et sanitatem perficiet impositione manuum secunda, quam coepit prima, restituens nos in claritate perfecta."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Brothers, you are all children of light, and children of God. We are not of the night, nor of darkness. For the night has passed, and the day is at hand (Rom. XIII, 12). And if we were once darkness, now we are light in the Lord (Ephes. V, 8). Yet if, because we are not darkness nor children of darkness, we should say that we suffer nothing of darkness, we deceive ourselves, and bring upon ourselves the darkness of death, which deserves not to be enlightened. For what does the Light of the world say, who came into this world for judgment, that those who do not see might see, and those who see might become blind? Because, he says, you say, We see: your sin remains (Joan. IX, 41). I see some little, however small, since thou, O Lord, dost light my candle; but because what I see is little, O my God, enlighten my darkness (Psal. XVII, 29). At most we see through a glass and in a dark manner; but this is very far indeed from the true clarity and clear truth which shall be seen face to face. Perhaps that is why the blind man, whom the Lord in Mark had begun to enlighten, saw men as though they were trees walking (Marc. VIII, 22-24), evidently because he saw only through a glass and in a dark manner; but by laying his hand on him a second time, he restored him so that he saw all things clearly \u2014 which shall happen in us, when he renews us and perfects our healing by a second laying on of hands, continuing what he began with the first, and restoring us to perfect clarity.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed hoc ipsum per speculum et in aenigmate, cui, aut quando, vel quandiu potest contingere? Quam misericorditer mecum ageretur, quam feliciter mihi viderer illuminatus, si peccata mea possem videre, cui quotidie necesse est plangere ac dicere: Circumdederunt me mala quorum non est numerus, et non potui ut viderem! (Psal. XXXIX, 13.) Nam delicta quis intelligit (Psal. XVIII, 13), quando etiam specie virtutis vitium decipiens interficit, quasi vere angelus tenebrarum transfigurans se in angelum lucis? Magnus utique, multumque illuminatus, qui delicta ad liquidum intelligere potuit et voluit; nam neque et dolosus potuit, et noluit intelligere, ut bene ageret (Psal. XXXV, 4). Deus illuminator omnium gentium, de te nobis cantabatur: Ecce veniet Dominus, et illuminabit oculos servorum suorum. Ecce venisti, o lumen meum, illumina oculos meos, ne unquam obdormiam in morte, ne peccatum ad mortem oculos nostros aliqua delectatione claudat, et ad consensum illiciat, aut, velut Isboseth, dormientem inveniens transfodiat (II Reg. IV, 7). Venisti, o lumen fidelium omnium, et ecce hodie de illuminatione verae fidei, id est, lucernae nostrae, dedisti nobis gaudere; da etiam de illuminatione tenebrarum nostrarum, quae residuae sunt, semper in Domino gaudere. Dedisti lumen verae fidei, da et lumen verae justitiae, da lumen divinae scientiae, da etiam et divinae sapientiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But this very seeing \"through a glass and in a dark manner\" \u2014 to whom, or when, or for how long can it happen? How mercifully would it be dealt with me, how happily would I seem to myself illumined, if I could see my sins \u2014 I for whom it is necessary daily to lament and say: \"Evils without number have surrounded me, and I was not able to see\" (Psal. XXXIX, 13.)! For \"who understands his faults\" (Psal. XVIII, 13), when even vice, deceiving under the appearance of virtue, kills \u2014 as if truly an angel of darkness transforming himself into an angel of light? Great indeed, and much illumined, is he who was able and willing to understand his sins clearly; for the deceitful man could not and would not understand, that he might do well (Psal. XXXV, 4). O God, enlightener of all nations, of you it was sung to us: \"Behold, the Lord will come, and he will enlighten the eyes of his servants.\" Behold, you have come, O my light; enlighten my eyes, lest I ever fall asleep in death, lest sin unto death close our eyes with some delight and entice us into consent, or, like Ishbosheth, find us sleeping and pierce us through (II Reg. IV, 7). You have come, O light of all the faithful, and behold, today you have given us to rejoice over the illumination of the true faith, that is, of our lamp; grant also that we may always rejoice in the Lord over the illumination of the darkness that still remains in us. You have given the light of true faith; give also the light of true righteousness, give the light of divine knowledge, give also the light of divine wisdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. His profecto gradibus proficiendum, hac via proficiscendum tibi esse puto, o anima fidelis, quo, fallacis mundi hujus tenebris exuta, pervenias ad patriam claritatis aeternae, ubi tenebrae tuae erunt sicut clara meridies, et nox sicut dies illuminabitur. Tunc prorsus, tunc videbis et afflues, et mirabitur, et dilatabitur cor tuum (Isa. LX, 5), quando replebitur divina majestate incircumscripti luminis omnis terra, et gloria ejus in te videbitur. Domus Jacob, venite, ambulemus in lumine Domini (Id. II, 3): ut veri filii lucis ambulemus de claritate in claritatem, tanquam praelucente Domini Spiritu, ut per singulos gradus virtutum regnum claritatis ulterius invadamus. Qui igitur jam per fidem sumus in lumine, ex ea et per eam in amplius et serenius lumen progrediamur, primo justitiae, dein scientiae, demumque sapientiae. Quod enim credimus per fidem, consequenter operandum vel promerendum est per justitiam, post intelligendum per scientiam, ac postremo contemplandum per sapientiam. Prima namque omnium accensa est lucerna fidei, ad quam operemur in nocte hujus saeculi. Inde scriptum est in laudem mulieris fortis, quae de nocte surrexit, et, de nocte ac die operans manibus suis, panem otiosa non comedit, Non exstinguetur, inquit, in nocte lucerna ejus (Prov. XXXI, 18), id est, non deficiet in tentatione fides ejus. Ad hanc lucernam non fiunt opera tenebrarum, sed exstinguitur cum fiunt. Qui enim male agit, odit lucem (Joan. III, 20). Ideoque exstinguit lucernam fidei, postponit memoriam Dei; non est timor Dei ante oculos ejus, et de tenebris quas sibi fecit, arbitrorumque absentia blanditur sibi, dicens: Quis me videt? Tenebrae circumdant me, et parietes cooperiunt me, et nemo circumspicit me. Quem vereor? Delictorum meorum non memorabitur Altissimus. Et non intelligit quoniam omnia videt oculus illius (Eccli. XXIII, 26, 27). Bona vero opera, etsi aliquando propter vanitatem declinandam in abscondito fiunt, ad lucem tamen internam et aeternam, id est sub judicio fidei et testimonio Dei fiunt, quia opera lucis sunt; lucernae utique ardentes in manibus operantium, et exspectantium adventum sponsi, qui educet quasi lumen justitiam tuam ad conspectum hominum et angelorum, et, sicut lux meridiana clara est, clarescere faciet opera tua, quia in Deo sunt facta, ut Deum in te, et te in Deo clarificent omnium judicia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. By these steps indeed, I think, O faithful soul, you must advance; by this road you must set out, so that, stripped of the darkness of this deceitful world, you may arrive at the homeland of eternal brightness, where your darkness shall be as the clear noonday, and night shall be lit up as day. Then truly, then you shall see and overflow, and your heart shall wonder and be enlarged (Isa. LX, 5), when the whole earth shall be filled with the divine majesty of uncircumscribed light, and his glory shall be seen in you. \"House of Jacob, come, let us walk in the light of the Lord\" (Id. II, 3): that as true children of light we may walk from brightness to brightness, as the Spirit of the Lord shines before us, so that through each degree of the virtues we may press on further into the kingdom of brightness. Therefore we who are already in the light through faith, from faith and through it let us advance into a fuller and more serene light \u2014 first of righteousness, then of knowledge, and finally of wisdom. For what we believe by faith must consequently be worked out, or earned, through righteousness; afterward it must be understood through knowledge; and finally it must be contemplated through wisdom. For of all lamps the first to be lit is the lamp of faith, by which we must work in the night of this present age. Hence it is written in praise of the valiant woman, who rose while it was yet night, and, working with her hands by night and by day, did not eat her bread in idleness: \"Her lamp shall not be put out in the night\" (Prov. XXXI, 18), that is, her faith shall not fail under temptation. By this lamp works of darkness are not done; rather, it is extinguished when such works are done. For \"he who does evil hates the light\" (Joan. III, 20). And so he extinguishes the lamp of faith, he puts away the memory of God; the fear of God is not before his eyes, and from the darkness he has made for himself, and the absence of witnesses, he flatters himself, saying: Who sees me? Darkness surrounds me, and walls cover me, and no one looks around at me. Whom do I fear? The Most High will not remember my sins. And he does not understand that the eye of the Most High sees all things (Eccli. XXIII, 26, 27). But good works, even when sometimes done in secret in order to avoid vanity, are nevertheless done before the inward and eternal light, that is, under the judgment of faith and the witness of God, because they are works of light \u2014 lamps indeed burning in the hands of those who work and who await the coming of the Bridegroom, who will bring forth your righteousness as light before the sight of men and angels, and, as the light of noonday is clear, he will make your works to shine clearly, because they were done in God, so that the judgment of all may glorify God in you, and you in God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Quanta vero claritate justitia resplendeat etiam in praesenti, quanta luce laetificet conscientiam justi, familiarius et suavius conscientiae vestrae testimonium vos docebit. Saepe autem et fides radiat, et justitia coruscat, et adhuc tamen intellectus caligat, ut mysterium fidei, quod velut involutum tenens veneratur, explicare nesciat; signatus sit ei liber Scripturae, quasi litteras nesciat; sed nec ad discretionem boni et mali, veri et falsi sensus exercitatos habeat. Et tales quidem multi in Ecclesia, quorum fides multa, abundans justitia, scientia fere nulla. Quod tamen ad lumen justitiae accedere oporteat lumen scientiae, docet sermo prophetae, qui, cum praemisisset: Seminate vobis ad justitiam, ut ostenderet quas interim primitias colligamus de hoc semine, subjunxit: Illuminate vobis lumen scientiae (Osee. X, 12). Sic et Apostolus ait: Fructificantes in opere bono, et crescentes in scientia Dei (Coloss. I, 10). Tu quoque, David, sedens in cathedra sapientissimus princeps inter tres, quomodo super senes intellexisti? Quia mandata, inquit, quaesivi. A mandatis prorsus intellexi, quia intellectus bonus omnibus facientibus timorem Domini. Nam et unctio docens de omnibus, sciens nimirum quo ordine quidque apud eam discendum sit, prius docet bonitatem et disciplinam, et postmodum scientiam: si tamen qui docendus est, primo omnium crediderit, id est, elementa exordii sermonum justitiae didicerit. Ideo ille discipulus unctionis tam prudenter precem ordinabat, atque allegabat: Bonitatem, inquit, et disciplinam et scientiam doce me, quia mandatis tuis credidi (Psal. CXVIII, 100, 66). Ac si diceret: Didici prima elementa, id est, fidem non fictam; jam, quod sequitur, doce justitiam, id est bonitatem et disciplinam, ut sic demum provehat ad sanctorum scientiam. Vocasti ad gratiam fidei, jam justifica per bonitatem amoris, et disciplinam casti timoris, ut post magnifices munere scientiae spiritualis. Quos autem vocavit, hos et justificavit; et quos justificavit illos et magnificavit (Rom. VIII, 30)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. With how great a brightness righteousness shines even in the present life, with how great a light it gladdens the conscience of the just, the testimony of your own conscience will teach you more intimately and more sweetly. But often it happens that faith shines, and righteousness glitters, and yet understanding remains dark, so that one who holds and venerates the mystery of faith as though wrapped up cannot unfold it; the book of Scripture is sealed to him as if he did not know letters; nor does he have senses trained to discern good from evil, true from false. And indeed there are many such in the Church, in whom faith is great, righteousness abundant, but knowledge almost none. Yet that the light of knowledge ought to be added to the light of righteousness is taught by the word of the prophet, who, having first said: 'Sow for yourselves unto righteousness,' in order to show what first-fruits we are meanwhile to gather from this seed, added: 'Light for yourselves the light of knowledge' (Osee. X, 12). So too the Apostle says: 'Being fruitful in good work, and increasing in the knowledge of God' (Coloss. I, 10). You too, David, sitting in the chair, the wisest prince among the three\u2014how did you understand above the elders? 'Because,' he says, 'I sought the commandments.' Entirely from the commandments I understood, for good understanding belongs to all who do the fear of the Lord. For the anointing which teaches concerning all things, knowing full well in what order each thing is to be learned from it, first teaches goodness and discipline, and afterward knowledge\u2014provided, however, that the one to be taught has first of all believed, that is, has learned the elementary first principles of the words of righteousness. Therefore that disciple of the anointing so prudently ordered his prayer, and pleaded: 'Teach me goodness,' he says, 'and discipline and knowledge, for I have believed your commandments' (Psal. CXVIII, 100, 66). As if he were saying: I have learned the first elements, that is, unfeigned faith; now, what follows, teach me righteousness, that is, goodness and discipline, so that it may at last advance me to the knowledge of the saints. You have called to the grace of faith; now justify through the goodness of love and the discipline of chaste fear, so that afterward you may magnify with the gift of spiritual knowledge. 'Whom he called, these he also justified; and whom he justified, these he also magnified' (Rom. VIII, 30).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Sciendum sane quod, cum sit haec scientia sanctorum, non tamen omnes sancti docentur eam, sed illi duntaxat in quibus nec industria gratiam, nec gratia destituit industriam; sed utrumque mutuo sibi suffragio cooperatur, ut et docibilis sit homo discipulus, et spiritus adsit doctor sedulus. Cum autem varia charismata contineat haec scientia, Spiritus tamen moderator non in unum facile confert omnia, sed dividit singulis prout vult, alii notitiam mysteriorum, alii intellectum Scripturarum, alii interpretationem sermonum, alii discretionem spirituum, alii illam, quae pernecessaria est, velut in gustu dignoscentiam, atque dijudicationem virtutum ac vitiorum, ne fallant vitia specie virtutum. Non nocebunt, inquit, et non occident in universo sancto monte meo; quia repleta est terra scientia Domini (Isai. XI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. It should indeed be known that, although this is the knowledge of the saints, not all saints are taught it, but only those in whom neither does industry fail grace, nor grace fail industry; rather, each cooperates with the other by mutual support, so that the man is a teachable disciple, and the Spirit is present as a diligent teacher. But since this knowledge contains various charisms, the Spirit who governs does not readily confer all of them on one person, but divides them to each as he wills: to one, knowledge of the mysteries; to another, understanding of the Scriptures; to another, interpretation of discourses; to another, discernment of spirits; to another, that most necessary gift, as it were a tasting discernment, and a judging apart of virtues and vices, lest vices deceive under the appearance of virtues. 'They shall not hurt,' it says, 'nor shall they kill in all my holy mountain; for the earth is filled with the knowledge of the Lord' (Isai. XI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Jam vero si quis post haec et per haec tria, fidem, justitiam atque scientiam, proficiat ad sapientiam, id est, saporem et gustum aeternorum, ut possit vacare et videre, videndoque gustare quam suavis Dominus; et quod oculus non vidit, et auris non audivit, et in cor hominis non ascendit, ei per Spiritum reveletur: hunc omnino dixerim illuminatum magnifice et gloriose, tanquam qui gloriam Domini revelata facie speculetur, et super quem gloria Domini saepius oriatur. Huic utique dicere habet non propheta, sed prophetarum Spiritus: Surge, illuminare, Jerusalem, quia venit lumen tuum, et gloria Domini super te orta est (Id. LX, 1). Fratres, non omnes capiunt hoc verbum, sed qui potest capere capiat. Non condemnatur qui non capit: sed qui non cupit, utique teporis arguitur. Qui autem cupit, noverit quia hoc lumen sapientiae fervens accendit oratio, sicut et lumen scientiae frequens lectio; si tamen cum legis, lucernam ardentem adhibueris, id est justitiam operum, piorumque experientiam sensuum. Tu autem, Domine, Pater luminum, qui lumen de te lumine unicum tuum, misisti sisti illuminare tenebras mortalium, da nobis per viam luminum pervenire ad lumen aeternum, ut placeamus coram te in lumine viventium, qui vivis et regnas per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-03-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now if anyone, after these and through these three\u2014faith, righteousness, and knowledge\u2014advances to wisdom, that is, to the savor and taste of eternal things, so that he may be free to see, and in seeing to taste how sweet the Lord is; and so that what eye has not seen, nor ear heard, nor has entered into the heart of man, may be revealed to him through the Spirit: this man I would call altogether magnificently and gloriously illumined, as one who beholds the glory of the Lord with unveiled face, and upon whom the glory of the Lord more often arises. To such a one, surely, it is not the prophet but the Spirit of the prophets who must say: 'Arise, be illumined, Jerusalem, for your light has come, and the glory of the Lord has arisen upon you' (Id. LX, 1). Brothers, not all grasp this word, but let him who can grasp it, grasp it. He who does not grasp it is not condemned; but he who does not desire it is surely convicted of lukewarmness. But let him who desires it know that this light of wisdom is kindled by fervent prayer, just as the light of knowledge is kindled by frequent reading\u2014provided, however, that when you read, you bring to it a burning lamp, that is, the righteousness of works and the experience of devout senses. But you, O Lord, Father of lights, who have sent your Only One, Light from your Light, to illumine the darkness of mortals, grant us to arrive by the way of lights at the eternal light, that we may be pleasing before you in the light of the living, you who live and reign through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon III: By What Steps One Comes to the Light of Wisdom", "topics": ["Epiphany", "light", "wisdom", "self-knowledge", "spiritual progress"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:ecce-advenit", "calendar_label": "Epiphany", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-epiphany-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In Epiphania Domini, sermo IV, De nova nostra nativitate et vita, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 59-64.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Epiphany", "Epiphany of the Lord", "1 Timothy 3:9", "Titus 1:16", "Ephesians 5:12", "2 Timothy 3:5", "Isaiah 61:9", "Hebrews 10:22", "John 13:10", "Sirach 34:30", "2 Peter 2:4", "4 Kings 5:10, 12, 14 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Job 40:18", "4 Kings 5:10, 13\u201314 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)"], "occasion": "In epiphania Domini", "occasion_detail": "Epiphany of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Epiphany", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fourth Epiphany sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Christ's baptism today brings a second birth for believers, but Guerric fears that monks keep the appearance of piety while denying its power. Naaman, washed seven times in the Jordan, becomes the figure of a sevenfold humility of Christ, in which those who lost the grace of their first Baptism may be cleansed again. The source explicitly names Epiphany."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De nova nostra nativitate et vita."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Our New Birth and Life", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Secunda nobis hodie, fratres, nativitas celebratur, quae de illa prima nativitate, tanquam de causa effectus, consequenter nata videtur. Illa siquidem, quam usque hodie celebravimus, nativitas Christi est; ista, quam hodie celebramus, nativitas nostra est. In illa namque Christus natus est, in ista Christianitas nata est. Cum enim tria sint quae nos faciunt christianos, fides, Baptismus, et altaris participatio, hodierna dies fidem nobis initiavit, Baptisma consecravit, ac mensae coelestis miracula praeformavit. Quomodo sane prima gentium illuminatio fidem nobis initiaverit, quomodo Christus baptizatus Baptisma nostrum consecraverit, qualiter illa conversio aquae in vinum, mutationem rerum in convivio mensae Dominicae praeostenderit, nequaquam jam opus est ut doceamus: sed illud arbitror operae pretium, ut nosmetipsos commoneamus, ne fides in nobis a suis degeneret initiis, ne vacuetur in nobis gratia Baptismatis, ne cedat nobis in judicium participatio calicis Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Today, brothers, we celebrate a second birth for ourselves, which seems to have been born as a consequence of that first birth, as an effect follows from its cause. For that birth which we have celebrated up until today is the birth of Christ; but this one, which we celebrate today, is our own birth. For in that birth Christ was born, but in this one Christianity was born. Since there are three things that make us Christians\u2014faith, Baptism, and participation at the altar\u2014today's feast has initiated faith in us, consecrated Baptism, and prefigured the miracles of the heavenly table. Indeed, there is no longer any need for us to teach how that first illumination of the Gentiles initiated faith for us, how Christ's being baptized consecrated our Baptism, how that turning of water into wine foreshadowed the change of things in the banquet of the Lord's table: but I think it worthwhile that we remind ourselves, lest faith in us should degenerate from its beginnings, lest the grace of Baptism be made void in us, lest our participation in the cup of Christ turn into judgment against us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Et ipsa quidem fides, qualis ab illis Magis, primis videlicet auctoribus suis, nobis hodie tradita sit; qualiter maximum fidei sacramentum mysticis insinuaverint munerum figuris, satis abundeque disseruere patres nostri, ut ex ea parte nullus jam alius labor quam in labores eorum introire nobis resederit. Sed neque magnopere postulat status, imo ruina temporis hujus, ut de mysterio fidei disputetur sed ut magis id modis omnibus elaboretur ut, sicut Apostolus ait, in conscientia pura idem fidei mysterium habeatur (I Tim. III, 9). Ecce enim hodie si de mysterio fidei interrogas, omnes fere invenies christianissimos; si conscientias discutias, paucos admodum invenies vere christianos. Totus fere mundus confitetur verbis se nosse Deum, factis autem negat (Tit. I, 16); adeo ut et qui speciem pietatis videntur habere, virtutem ejus saepius inveniantur propemodum abnegare. Quid putatis autem, fratres mei, quos in hoc significare voluerim, quos scilicet dicam pietatis habere speciem, sed abnegare virtutem? Si illos qui spatiosam viam saeculi ambulant, intelligitis, quid illi vel de sola praeferunt pietatis specie, qui nomine solo christiani, atque in vacuum assumentes nomen sanctum Christi, tota ac libera professione sectantur quae huic inimica sunt nomini; qui omni specie conversationis et modo vitae, se protestantur inimicos crucis Christi? Haeccine est species pietatis quam in eis videmus, habitus et incessus meretricius, sermo scurrilis, oculus impudicus, venter qui pro Deo colitur, et omnis illa vitae contumelia, quae publice et gloriabunde, velut in faciem pietati irrogatur? Quae enim in occulto ab ipsis fiunt, turpe est et dicere (Ephes. V, 12). Fodiat parietem eorum qui ad ipsos missus est prophetare, ut adhuc majores abominationes videat, quas non erubescunt aut verentur coram illa tremenda majestate perpetrare. Quid enim mihi, qui monachus videor, de his qui extra hoc monasterium sunt judicare? Utinam nec de his ipsis qui hic intus sunt, mihi esset judicare. Ad me ipsum namque conturbata est anima mea, dum scilicet vereor ne haec ipsa species pietatis, quam utcunque vobiscum gero, gravius me incipiat accusare, si pietatem ipsa me convicerit abnegasse."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. As for faith itself\u2014how it was handed down to us today by those Magi, who were indeed its first authors; how they expressed the greatest sacrament of faith through the mystical figures of their gifts\u2014our fathers have discussed this fully and more than abundantly, so that in that regard no other labor remains for us than to enter into their labors. But neither does the condition of the times, or rather the ruin of this present age, greatly demand that we dispute about the mystery of faith, but rather that we strive by every means to hold, as the Apostle says, the same mystery of faith in a pure conscience (I Tim. III, 9). For behold, today, if you ask about the mystery of faith, you will find almost everyone most Christian; but if you examine consciences, you will find very few who are truly Christian. Almost the whole world confesses in words that it knows God, but denies it by its deeds (Tit. I, 16); so much so that even those who seem to have the appearance of piety are often found to deny its power almost entirely. But what do you suppose, my brothers, I meant to signify by this\u2014whom indeed do I mean by those who have the appearance of piety, but deny its power? If you understand those who walk the broad way of the world, what mere appearance of piety do even they put forward, who are Christians in name only, and who, taking up the holy name of Christ in vain, pursue, by their whole and free profession, things that are hostile to this name; who, by every outward show and manner of life, declare themselves enemies of the cross of Christ? Is this the appearance of piety that we see in them\u2014harlot-like bearing and gait, scurrilous speech, shameless eye, the belly that is worshiped in place of God, and all that reproach of life which is publicly and boastfully inflicted, as it were, upon the very face of piety? For the things that are done by them in secret are shameful even to mention (Ephes. V, 12). Let him who was sent to them to prophesy dig through their wall, that he may see even greater abominations, which they do not blush or fear to commit in the presence of that tremendous majesty. But what right have I, who only appear to be a monk, to judge those who are outside this monastery? Would that I had no need to judge even those who are within it. For within myself my soul is troubled, since indeed I fear lest this very appearance of piety, which I somehow bear among you, may begin to accuse me the more gravely, if that appearance itself should convict me of having denied piety.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Omnino, fratres, verendum est mihi, an vero et vobis ipsis, vos videritis, ne ad nos praecipue spectet ille sermo qui per Apostolum prophetatus est de temporibus novissimis; ubi, cum malitiam hominum describeret tunc futurorum, qualis nunc manifeste legi potest in vita et moribus eorum, longum tandem sic conclusit malorum catalogum: Habentes, inquit, speciem pietatis, virtutem autem ejus abnegantes (II Tim. III, 5). Quid enim ita speciem pietatis habet, sicut haec quam praetendimus, humilitas tonsurae et habitus, parcimonia cibi et potus, labor fere continuus, et ordinatissimus per omnia vivendi modus? Caeterum de virtute ipsa pietatis, quam haec species exterior promittit, quam prope nihil in me meique similibus valeat inveniri, pudor mihi esset confiteri, si non omnibus essemus manifesti. Quae est enim virtus pietatis, nisi charitas non ficta, humilitas vera, patientia longanimis, obedientia non segnis? In his virtutibus earumque similibus, quantum vobis liceat gloriari, vestro, fratres, relinquimus judicio; de me siquidem ingenue confiteor, quia nomina quidem earum scio, saporem adhuc ignoro. Merito itaque pudorem mihi pariter et timorem incutit habitus quem gero religionis, cui fere nullum suffragatur testimonium virtutis. Numquid enim tuto mihi possum usurpare monachi nomen et honorem, cujus non habeo meritum et virtutem; cum, sicut ante nos dictum est, simulata sanctitas, duplicata sit iniquitas (AUG. in psal. LXIII); et lupus sub ovina pelle deprehensus graviore sit sententia percellendus? Propterea vos quoque, fratres, ut tuto gloriari possitis, in hac, qua corpora vestra decorata sunt, specie pietatis, ponite corda vestra in virtute ejus; ut species in corporibus, virtus in cordibus, probatos vos exhibeant tam angelis quam hominibus, sitque, sicut scriptum est: Omnes qui viderint eos, cognoscent illos quia isti sunt semen cui benedixit Dominus (Isa. LXI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Brothers, I must altogether fear\u2014whether you too should, you yourselves must judge\u2014that this saying which was prophesied by the Apostle concerning the last times applies especially to us; where, describing the wickedness of the men who were then to come, such as can now clearly be read in the life and conduct of men, he concluded his long catalogue of evils thus: \"Having,\" he says, \"the appearance of godliness, but denying the power thereof\" (II Tim. III, 5). For what has so much the appearance of godliness as this which we put forward\u2014the humility of the tonsure and habit, the sparingness of food and drink, the almost continuous labor, and the altogether well-ordered manner of life? But as for the very power of godliness which this outward appearance promises, how little it is to be found in me and those like me\u2014it would shame me to confess, were we not already manifest to all. For what is the power of godliness, but unfeigned charity, true humility, long-suffering patience, ready obedience? How far you may boast in these and similar virtues, brothers, I leave to your own judgment; as for myself, I confess frankly that I know the names of these virtues, but I do not yet know their taste. Rightly, then, the habit of religion which I wear strikes me with shame and fear alike, since it is supported by almost no testimony of virtue. For can I safely assume the name and honor of monk, when I do not have the merit and virtue of one? As it has been said before us, \"feigned holiness is doubled iniquity\" (AUG. in psal. LXIII); and a wolf caught under sheep's clothing is to be struck with a graver sentence. Therefore you too, brothers, so that you may boast safely in this outward appearance of godliness with which your bodies are adorned, place your hearts in its power; so that the appearance in your bodies and the power in your hearts may show you approved both to angels and to men, and so that it may be as it is written: \"All that see them shall know them, that these are the seed which the Lord hath blessed\" (Isa. LXI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Haec est, fratres, christianae fidei pietas vera, hoc est habere mysterium fidei in conscientia pura. Ut enim mysterium fidei, quod hodie nobis mystica commendaverunt Magorum munera, fideliter et digne conservaremus immaculatum in conscientia pura, hodie itidem Christus baptizatus, non sibi sed nobis, Aspersit, sicut Apostolus ait, corda a conscientia mala, et abluit corpora aqua munda (Hebr. X. 22). Quam felix, o fratres, anima illa, quae sic lota in salutem, mox e vestigio secuta est Salvatorem, ut deinceps adhaerens ei cum Apostolis, illam meruit audire vocem: Qui lotus est, non indiget nisi ut pedes lavet, sed est mundus totus! (Joan. XIII, 10.) Caeterum qui baptizatur a mortuo, et iterum tangit eum, quid proficit lavatio illius? (Eccli. XXXIV, 30.) Ego postquam Baptismo lotus fui, non unum mortuum, sed tot mortuos quot opera mortis, tetigi; et, juxta Petrum apostolum, contigit mihi illud veri proverbii, Canis reversus ad vomitum suum, et sus lota in volutabro luti (II Petr. II, 4). Quid igitur faciam? quibus ulterius aquis sordes tantas diluam? Audio quia sicut una fides, sic unum Baptisma est, nec secundo Baptismati locus est, quoniam quicunque baptizamur, in morte Christi baptizamur; qui sicut semel mortuus est, sic et semel baptizatus est, ut et nobis unici modum et formam Baptismatis praescriberet. Diffiderem forsitan ulterius posse mundari, nisi quia desperantem reducit in spem, inter alias Scripturae consolationes, etiam fidele consilium Elisei, etiamsi totus instar Naaman Syri lepra sim perfusus, ac veterno incurabili possessus. Vade, inquit, et lavare septies in Jordane, et mundaberis. Si dixisset, Lavare ter, mundaberis, continuo intellexissem trinam mersionem qua inundantur baptizati, diceremque totum illud miraculum gratiam prophetare Baptismatis, ut jam tunc Jordanis de virtute Dominici Baptismi coepisset ad salutem gentium operari. Nunc autem cum signanter dicat, lavare septies in Jordane, quid sibi vult septenarius iste, aut quis est Jordanis ille? Jordanis, ut aiunt, interpretatur Descensus eorum. Quorum? Illorum utique qui humiliantur ad poenitentiam, qui nimirum quanto profundius descendunt per gradus humilitatis, tanto se penitius mergunt et plenius abluunt spiritualis alveo Jordanis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. This, brothers, is the true godliness of the Christian faith; this is to hold the mystery of faith in a pure conscience. For in order that we might faithfully and worthily keep undefiled in a pure conscience the mystery of faith which the mystical gifts of the Magi commended to us today, Christ, baptized today, not for himself but for us, as the Apostle says, \"sprinkled our hearts from an evil conscience, and washed our bodies with clean water\" (Hebr. X. 22). How happy, O brothers, is that soul which, thus washed unto salvation, straightway followed the Savior in his very footsteps, so that thereafter, clinging to him with the apostles, it deserved to hear that voice: \"He that is washed needeth not but to wash his feet, but is clean wholly!\" (Joan. XIII, 10.). But he who is washed after touching a dead body, and touches it again, what does his washing profit him? (Eccli. XXXIV, 30.) I, after I was washed in Baptism, have touched not one dead body, but as many dead bodies as there are works of death; and, according to the apostle Peter, that true proverb has befallen me: \"The dog returned to his vomit,\" and \"the sow washed, to her wallowing in the mire\" (II Petr. II, 4). What, then, shall I do? With what further waters shall I wash away such great filth? I hear that, just as there is one faith, so there is one Baptism, and there is no place for a second Baptism, since whoever of us are baptized, are baptized into the death of Christ; who, just as he died once, so was baptized once, that he might prescribe for us too the manner and form of a single Baptism. I might perhaps despair of being able to be cleansed any further, were it not that, among the other consolations of Scripture, the faithful counsel of Elisha brings back the despairing soul to hope\u2014even if I were, like Naaman the Syrian, entirely covered with leprosy, and possessed by an incurable lethargy. \"Go,\" he said, \"and wash seven times in the Jordan, and you shall be made clean.\" If he had said, \"Wash three times, and you shall be made clean,\" I would at once have understood the threefold immersion by which the baptized are overwhelmed, and I would say that that whole miracle prophesied the grace of Baptism, so that the Jordan even then began, by the power of the Lord's future Baptism, to work for the salvation of the nations. But now, since he expressly says, \"wash seven times in the Jordan,\" what does this number seven mean, or what is that Jordan? Jordan, as they say, is interpreted \"Their Descent.\" Whose descent? Surely of those who are humbled unto repentance, who indeed, the more deeply they descend by the steps of humility, the more inwardly do they immerse themselves and the more fully do they wash in the channel of the spiritual Jordan.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ad meipsum, o Deus, conturbata est anima mea, memor peccatorum suorum; propterea memor ero tui de terra Jordanis, quomodo scilicet in descensu humiliationis mundaveris Naaman leprosum. Descendit, inquit, et lavit septies in Jordane, juxta sermonem viri Dei, et mundatus est. Descende et tu, o anima mea, de curru superbiae in salubres aquas Jordanis, qui de fonte domus David, toto jam profluit orbe in ablutionem peccatoris et menstruatae. Haec est nimirum humilitas poenitendi, quae, de domo pariter et exemplo profluens Christi, toto jam praedicatur orbe, totiusque purgat crimina mundi. O vos, Naaman Syri: non enim unus, sed innumeri: o, inquam, divites, sed leprosi; superbi, sed aerumnosi; quare tam vehementer abhorretis lavari his medicinalibus aquis? cur vobis ita viluit noster iste Jordanis prae fluviis Damasci? Dicitis enim, si forte quaerentibus aliquando consilium salutis imitanda praedicetur humilitas et paupertas Christi: Nunquid non meliores sunt fluvii Damasci omnibus aquis Israel, ut laver in eis, et munder? (IV Reg. V, 10, 14, 12.) Nunquid non est melius, et ad purgationem peccatorum efficacius, quotidie benefacere de affluentia mundi, quam semel omnia relinquendo pauperem fieri? Sed enim audi, Naaman. Fluvium Damascenum absorbebit Leviathan, et non mirabitur; nam de Jordane si habeat fiduciam quod influat in os ejus (Job XL, 18), spes eum frustrabitur. Neque enim grande reputat quantiscunque, praeter humilitatem christianam, virtutibus affluas: cum etiam pauperes Christi devorare se posse confidat, sed nihil in eis inimicus proficiat. Damascus secundum nomen suum, Civitas sanguinis est, et aquae ejus sanguine mistae sunt; quia opera etiam bona carnalium et saecularium, vix a quocunque peccato pura sunt. Et quomodo id quod infectum est sanguine, sanguinem mundabit? qua ratione lepram, id quod leprosum est curabit? At Jordanis noster ipse quidem purum profluit, nec te calumniari poterunt superbi, si totus in eo mersus fueris, ac velut consepultus humilitati Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Within myself, O God, my soul is troubled, mindful of its sins; therefore I will remember you from the land of the Jordan\u2014namely, how, in the descent of humiliation, you cleansed Naaman the leper. He went down, it says, and washed seven times in the Jordan, according to the word of the man of God, and was cleansed. Go down, you too, O my soul, from the chariot of pride into the healthful waters of the Jordan, which, flowing from the fountain of the house of David, now pours forth through the whole world for the washing of the sinner and of her who is unclean. This indeed is the humility of repentance, which, flowing alike from the household and the example of Christ, is now preached throughout the whole world, and purges the crimes of the whole world. O you Naamans of Syria\u2014for there is not one, but countless many: O, I say, rich, yet leprous; proud, yet wretched\u2014why do you so vehemently shrink from being washed in these healing waters? Why has our Jordan grown so cheap in your eyes compared to the rivers of Damascus? For you say, if perchance to those seeking the counsel of salvation the humility and poverty of Christ is preached as something to be imitated: \"Are not the rivers of Damascus better than all the waters of Israel, that I may wash in them, and be made clean?\" (IV Reg. V, 10, 14, 12.). Is it not better, and more effective for the cleansing of sins, to do good daily out of the abundance of the world, than, by forsaking all at once, to become poor? But hear this, Naaman. Leviathan will swallow up the river of Damascus, and not marvel at it; for if he should have confidence that the Jordan will flow into his mouth (Job XL, 18), his hope will be disappointed. For he does not reckon it a great thing, however much you may abound in virtues apart from Christian humility, since he is confident that he can devour even the poor of Christ\u2014but in them the enemy makes no progress. Damascus, according to its name, is the \"City of Blood,\" and its waters are mingled with blood; for even the good works of carnal and worldly men are scarcely pure from any sin whatsoever. And how shall that which is stained with blood cleanse blood? By what reasoning shall that which is itself leprous cure leprosy? But our Jordan flows pure indeed, nor will the proud be able to slander you, if you have been wholly immersed in it, and as it were buried together with the humility of Christ.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Audi saltem, o Naaman, domesticos tuos, humiles amicos, fideles consiliarios. Naaman recedebat indignans adversum virum Dei, quem consuluerat; at illi, Pater, inquiunt, etsi rem grandem dixisset tibi propheta, certe facere debueras. Fidele prorsus verbum, rationis et sapientiae plenum; quippe suggestio est rationalium hominis sensuum, quos sibi Deus testes retinuit adversus hominem ipsum. Pater, inquiunt, etsi rem grandem dixisset tibi Propheta, certe facere debueras. Non credere namque Prophetae, Dei est injuria: non tentare omnia pro salute, odium est sui ipsius et invidia. Quid enim grande esse potest in praecepto, ubi salus est in promisso? Quanto magis non debet videri grande vel difficile, discere ab Jesu quia mitis est et humilis corde, cum in eo pariter inveniatur et requies animae, et praesens medela spiritualis leprae, pignusque salutis aeternae? Tandem vix aegreque obtinetur a superbo peccatore ut descendat in Jordanem et lavet; si tamen septies lavat, totumque se plenius et saepius mergat, non moratur effectus salutis fidem prophetico facere sermoni. Sed, proh dolor! quanta est hujus nostri miseria temporis! quia non solum divites, sed etiam pauperes leprosi, ita inveniuntur delicati, ut vix usque ad talos salubribus intingi patiantur aquis: aut si semel laverint, mox sibi plenissime videantur mundati."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Listen at least, O Naaman, to your own household, your humble friends, your faithful counselors. Naaman was going away indignant against the man of God whom he had consulted; but they said, \"Father, even if the prophet had told you a great thing, surely you ought to have done it.\" A thoroughly faithful word, full of reason and wisdom; indeed it is the suggestion of a man's rational senses, which God has kept for himself as witnesses against the man himself. \"Father,\" they say, \"even if the Prophet had told you a great thing, surely you ought to have done it.\" For not to believe the Prophet is an injury to God; not to attempt everything for one's salvation is hatred and envy toward oneself. For what great thing can there be in a command, where salvation lies in the promise? How much less ought it to seem a great or difficult thing to learn from Jesus, because he is meek and humble of heart, since in him is found alike both rest for the soul, and a present remedy for spiritual leprosy, and the pledge of eternal salvation? At last, it is only with difficulty and reluctance that a proud sinner is brought to go down into the Jordan and wash; yet if he washes seven times, and plunges himself more fully and more often, the effect of salvation does not delay in proving the prophetic word true. But, alas, how great is the misery of this our present time! For not only the rich, but even the poor who are leprous, are found so delicate that they scarcely allow themselves to be dipped up to the ankles in these healthful waters; or if they wash once, they straightway seem to themselves most fully cleansed.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Et Elisaeus non ita sentit, sed signanter exprimit, Septies lavare, et mundaberis. Sciebat nimirum humilitatem Christi, quae nobis imitanda est, si perfecte volumus mundari, septemplicis esse virtutis. Prima virtus, quod, cum dives esset, pauper factus est. Secunda, quod paupertati extremitatem adjiciens, in praesepi positus est. Tertia, quod Matri subditus. Quarta, quod servi manibus hodie inclinavit caput. Quinta, quod discipulum furem tulit et proditorem. Sexta, quod sic fuit mansuetus ante iniquum judicem. Septima, quod crucifixoribus tam clemens intercessor fuit apud Patrem. His vestigiis, licet satis a longe, gigantem sequeris, si paupertatem diligis, si inter pauperes extremitatem eligis, si monasterii subditus es disciplinae, si minorem tibi pateris praeesse, si falsos fratres aequanimiter tuleris, si mansuetudine vincas cum judicaris, si charitatem rependas illis a quibus injuste pateris. Haec humilitas plane sine injuria unici Baptismi rebaptizat: quia mortem Christi non iterat, sed mortificationem et sepulturam criminum instaurat; et quod in illo Baptismo gestum est specie, hic rursus impletur veritate. Haec siquidem humilitas coelos aperit, spiritum adoptionis restituit: Pater agnoscit filium, quippe in innocentia et puritate pueri regenerati reformatum. Unde bene de Naaman Scriptura meminit quia restituta est caro ejus, sicut caro pueri parvuli (IV Reg. V, 13, 10 et 14): significans ut arbitror, quia eorum, qui sic se abluunt, non solum corda recuperant innocentiam parvulorum; sed etiam corpora secundum quemdam modum puerilem sibi vindicant puritatem mortificatione membrorum. Sic enim de quodam sanctorum legitur, quia in membris defuncti, septennis quodammodo pueri gratia videbatur (De S. Martino dictum apud Sulpicium). Ecce, fratres, qui primi gratiam Baptismatis tantis voluptatum sordibus amisimus, ecce verum Jordanem, descensum scilicet humilium, ubi die rebaptizari licet, invenimus. Hoc tantum est, ut non parcamus nobis de die in diem descendere profundius, et plenius mergi, Christoque penitus consepeliri. Ipsi gratias agamus, cujus humilitas et formam Baptismi hodie dedicavit credentibus, et gratiam non disparem reservavit poenitentibus. Ipsi gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-epiphany-04-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. And Elisha thinks no differently, but expressly states: 'Wash seven times, and you shall be clean.' For he knew that the humility of Christ, which we must imitate if we wish to be perfectly cleansed, is of a sevenfold virtue. The first virtue: that, though he was rich, he became poor. The second: that, adding the extremity of poverty, he was laid in a manger. The third: that he was subject to his Mother. The fourth: that today he bowed his head to the hands of a servant. The fifth: that he bore a disciple who was a thief and a betrayer. The sixth: that he was so meek before an unjust judge. The seventh: that he was so merciful an intercessor with the Father for those who crucified him. In these footprints, though at quite a distance, you follow the giant, if you love poverty, if among the poor you choose the lowest place, if you are subject to the discipline of the monastery, if you allow one lesser than yourself to be set over you, if you bear false brothers with equanimity, if you conquer by meekness when you are judged, if you repay with charity those at whose hands you suffer unjustly. This humility plainly rebaptizes without injury to the one Baptism, because it does not repeat the death of Christ, but renews the mortification and burial of sins; and what was accomplished in that Baptism as a figure is here fulfilled again in reality. Indeed, this humility opens the heavens, restores the spirit of adoption: the Father recognizes the son, inasmuch as he is reshaped into the innocence and purity of a regenerated child. Hence Scripture rightly records of Naaman that his flesh was restored like the flesh of a little child (IV Reg. V, 13, 10 et 14): signifying, as I judge, that the hearts of those who wash themselves in this way not only recover the innocence of little children, but their bodies too, in a certain childlike manner, claim for themselves a purity through the mortification of the members. For thus it is read of a certain one of the saints, that in the limbs of the deceased there seemed to appear, as it were, the grace of a seven-year-old child. (Said of St. Martin, in Sulpicius.) Behold, brothers, we who lost the grace of the first Baptism through such great filth of pleasures\u2014behold, we have found the true Jordan, namely the descent of the humble, where it is permitted to be rebaptized daily. This alone is needed: that we not spare ourselves from descending more deeply day by day, and being more fully submerged, and being wholly buried together with Christ. Let us give thanks to him, whose humility today both consecrated the form of Baptism for believers, and reserved a grace not unlike it for penitents. To him be glory forever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Epiphany Sermon IV: On Our New Birth and Life", "topics": ["Epiphany", "Baptism", "humility", "monastic life", "Naaman"], "url": "sermons/guerric-epiphany-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN NATIVITATE S. JOANNIS BAPTISTAE., SERMO I., De laudibus sancti., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 163-167.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Jeremiah 1:6", "Luke 16:16", "Matthew 3:8", "Romans 6:19", "Jeremiah 1:10", "Isaiah 9:10", "Isaiah 55:13"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric, pleading with Jeremiah that he is a child, praises John the Baptist, who prophesied before he could speak and made his parents prophets. Grace flowed principally into Mary, from her into John and from John into his parents. John stood nearer to Christ than any other mortal and prepared a perfect people for the Lord. Guerric urges the brothers to bring forth fruits worthy of repentance and seeks John's intercession. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN NATIVITATE S. JOANNIS BAPTISTAE."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE NATIVITY OF SAINT JOHN THE BAPTIST.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De laudibus sancti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the praises of the saint.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ah! Domine Deus, ecce nescio loqui, quia puer ego sum (Jerem. I, 6). Fratres, si Jeremias, sicut ex lectione audistis hesterna, ne praedicationis susciperet officium, verecundius quam justius minorem causabatur aetatem, quanto rectius ego sensum causarer minorem? Si sanctum ex utero, et quem manifeste divina mittebat electio, aetas deterrebat infirmior, mihi quae frons esse potest ad loquendum, cui nec ad sanctitatem suffragatur conscientia. nec ad sermonis facultatem suppetit scientia? Illi erat timori pueritia sua, mihi non erit puerilitas mea? Verumtamen tu, puer propheta Altissimi, de quo sermonem hodie nostri debet locus officii, puerilitatem nostram non pateris excusatam haberi, quin saltem gestiamus more puerorum de te quoquomodo balbutire; qui etiam in materia leviori, plena necdum didicimus verba formare. Tuum erit, o vox Verbi, vox sapientiae, tibi devotae solvere vinculum linguae, qui necdum loquens, usum loquendi muto potuisti patri reformare. Nec sine fenore magno rediit intercepta facultas loquendi, cui simul accessit gratia prophetandi, ut videlicet uterque parens aliquid sibi gauderet praerogatum de praerogativa filii."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Ah, Lord God, behold, I know not how to speak, for I am a child\" (Jerem. I, 6). Brethren, if Jeremiah, as you heard from yesterday's reading, pleaded his younger age\u2014more out of modesty than out of right\u2014so as not to take up the office of preaching, how much more rightly might I plead my lesser understanding? If a man holy from the womb, and one whom the divine election was manifestly sending, was deterred by his tender age, what boldness can there be for me to speak, for whom conscience lends no support toward holiness, nor knowledge supplies ability of speech? If his boyhood was a source of fear to him, shall not my childishness be one for me? Yet you, O boy-prophet of the Most High, of whom our office today must speak, do not allow our childishness to excuse us from being at least eager, in the manner of children, to stammer something about you however we can\u2014we who, even in lighter matters, have not yet learned to form complete words. It will be yours, O voice of the Word, voice of Wisdom, to loose the bond of the tongue devoted to you\u2014you who, before you could yet speak, were able to restore to your mute father the use of speech. Nor did the recovered faculty of speech return without great profit, since along with it came also the grace of prophesying, so that each parent might rejoice in having received something as a prerogative from the prerogative of the son.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Qui enim propheta, et plus quam propheta futurus erat, imo ante prophetare quam profari, prius Deum quam se ipsum sentire coeperat, parentes quoque suos prophetas faciebat; et in eos qui in se transfuderant carnis substantiam, spiritus sui et gratiae superabundantiam refundebat. Nam et Elisabeth, postquam exsultavit in gaudio infans in utero ejus, tunc et ipsa prophetavit repleta Spiritu sancto; quia necdum loquens filius, matri tamen occultam Domini praesentiam exsultatione manifesta revelavit. Salutabat Salvatorem motu, quo poterat, et in occursum Domini praecursor impiger erumpere gestiebat. Plane incomparabilis gratia, inaestimabilis divinae virtutis potentia. Vox Mariae dum auribus Elisabeth insonat, ad cor Joannis penetrat, qui abstrusus intra materna latebat viscera, spiritum ejus animat, gaudioque salutari vegetat: et cui virtus naturae vix adhuc totam infuderat animam, virtus vocis Mariae pleniorem infudit prophetiam, adeo ut etiam de plenitudine filii copiose videatur in matrem refundi. Vere gratia plena Maria: manifeste Deus totius gratiae in ea erat, de cujus munificentia tam copiose, tam magnifice, principaliter in Matrem, de Matre in Joannem, de Joanne in parentes gratiae largitas profluebat. Flumina prorsus de ventre Mariae fluebant aquae vivae, et fons vitae et gratiae oriebatur de medio paradisi ad irriganda ligna paradisi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. For he who was to be a prophet, and more than a prophet\u2014indeed, who began to perceive God before he perceived himself, and to prophesy before he could speak\u2014made his own parents prophets as well; and upon those who had transfused into him the substance of their flesh, he poured back the overflow of his spirit and grace. For Elizabeth too, after the infant leapt for joy in her womb, then herself prophesied, filled with the Holy Spirit; because the son, not yet able to speak, nevertheless revealed to his mother by manifest exultation the hidden presence of the Lord. He greeted the Savior by the movement he was able to make, and, as forerunner, longed eagerly to burst forth to meet the Lord. Truly incomparable grace, inestimable power of divine virtue! While the voice of Mary sounded in the ears of Elizabeth, it penetrated to the heart of John, who lay hidden within his mother's womb; it animated his spirit and quickened him with saving joy. And to him, into whom the power of nature had scarcely yet infused a complete soul, the power of Mary's voice infused a fuller prophecy, so much so that from the fullness of the son there seems to have overflowed abundantly even into the mother. Truly \"full of grace\" is Mary: manifestly the God of all grace was in her, from whose munificence the bounty of grace flowed so abundantly, so magnificently\u2014principally into the Mother, from the Mother into John, from John into the parents. Truly rivers of living water flowed from the womb of Mary, and the fountain of life and grace arose from the midst of paradise to water the trees of paradise.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Proxima erat fonti cedrus ista nobilis, Joannem loquor, consobrinum et amicum sponsi, praecursorem, Baptistam, et martyrem Domini: ideoque nimirum uberius irrigata in tantum excrevit, ut inter natos mulierum nihil illa sublimius posset inveniri. Omnino Salvatori proximus erat, qui non solum copula sanguinis conjunctus, aut amicitia intimus erat, sed etiam gloria annuntiationis, novitate nativitatis, sanctitate pene originali, praedicatione consimili, auctoritate baptizandi, virtute patiendi, ante mortales omnes ei propius accedebat. Denique si alia cessarent omnia, si cuncta de eo silerent prophetarum praeconia; solius gratia nominis ejus (9), quod vocatum est ab angelo, priusquam in utero conciperetur, satis abundeque singularem in eo futuram Dei gratiam testabatur. Dignum quippe erat ut gratiam Dei, quam gratia plena profudit, plenus gratia praedicaret, et gratia non mediocri emineret, qui gratiae tempus a tempore legis velut medius limes disterminaret. Usque ad Joannem enim Lex et Prophetae prophetaverunt (Luc. XVI, 16); qui videlicet primus ostendit praesentem, quem Lex et Prophetia promiserant venturum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Nearest to the fountain was this noble cedar\u2014I speak of John, cousin and friend of the Bridegroom, forerunner, Baptist, and martyr of the Lord: and therefore, watered more abundantly, he grew so great that among those born of women none more exalted could be found. He was altogether nearest to the Savior\u2014he who was joined not only by the bond of blood or intimate in friendship, but who also approached nearer to him than all other mortals, in the glory of the annunciation, the novelty of his birth, a holiness almost original, a preaching very similar, authority to baptize, and courage in suffering. In short, if all else were silent, if the proclamations of the prophets said nothing of him; the grace of his name alone (9)\u2014which was given by an angel before he was conceived in the womb\u2014would sufficiently and abundantly testify to the singular grace of God that was to be in him. For it was fitting that he who was full of grace should preach the grace of God which she who was full of grace poured forth, and that he who was to mark the dividing line, as it were, between the time of grace and the time of the law, should himself stand out with no small grace. For \"the Law and the Prophets prophesied until John\" (Luc. XVI, 16); he indeed was the first to show as present the one whom the Law and Prophecy had promised as coming.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Merito igitur nativitas hujus pueri multos gaudere tunc fecit, hodieque facit, qui in senectute parentum natus, mundo senescenti gratiam praedicaturus erat renascendi. Merito nativitatem istam, quam mirabiliter operatur gratia, miraturque natura, solemniter veneratur Ecclesia: praesertim cum singularis illius nativitatis, cujus gratia naturam reparavit, per hanc pignora fidelia sibi videat esse praemissa. Non ingratam se probat Ecclesia, non immemorem: fideliter agnoscit qua devotione, qua gratiarum actione suscipere debeat Praecursorem, per quem ipsum agnovit Salvatorem. Mihi plane novo nascitur gaudio lucerna mundi, cujus indicio lucem veram agnovi lucentem in tenebris, sed non comprehensam a tenebris. Mihi prorsus ineffabili nascitur gaudio, cum quo tot et tanta mundo nascuntur bona; qui videlicet Ecclesiam primus catechizat, per poenitentiam initiat, per baptismum praeparat, praeparatam Christo consignat et copulat; et quam ad vivendum temperanter informat, etiam ad moriendum fortiter propria morte confirmat, atque in his omnibus plebem perfectam Domino parat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Rightly, then, did the birth of this boy cause many to rejoice at that time, and it causes rejoicing today as well\u2014he who, born in the old age of his parents, was to preach to an aging world the grace of being born again. Rightly does the Church solemnly venerate this birth, which grace wrought so wondrously and nature marveled at: especially since she sees in it faithful pledges sent before her of that singular nativity whose grace repaired nature. The Church proves herself neither ungrateful nor forgetful: she faithfully recognizes with what devotion, with what thanksgiving, she ought to receive the Forerunner, through whom she came to know the Savior himself. For me indeed the lamp of the world is born with new joy, by whose sign I have recognized the true light shining in the darkness, yet not overcome by the darkness. For me indeed he is born with ineffable joy, with whom so many and such great goods are born to the world\u2014he who first catechizes the Church, initiates her through penance, prepares her through baptism, and, once prepared, consigns and joins her to Christ; and who forms her to live temperately, and also, by his own death, confirms her to die bravely, and in all these things prepares a perfect people for the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. O fratres, quorum propositum est (utinam et ita desiderium) ad perfectionem festinare; quam cito christianam assequeretur perfectionem, qui huic magistro animum accommodaret docibilem: cujus etiam prima elementa justitiae modum supergressa sunt perfectionis humanae; cujus rudimenta primaevae aetatis gravitatem sapientiae vicere senilis! Qui enim, antequam natus, sanctus fuit, quid mirum si in processu conversationis plus quam sanctus fuit? Mirari possumus, imitari non possumus sanctitatem tuam, sanctorum sanctissime: omnino necesse est ut, qui de publicanis et peccatoribus plebem perfectam Domino parare festinas, humanius eis loquaris quam vivas; et modum perfectionis non secundum vitae formulam tuae, sed secundum virtutem mediocritatis temperes humanae. Facite, inquit, dignos fructus poenitentiae (Matth. III, 8). Nos quidem, o fratres, gloriamur perfectius loqui, quam vivere: Joannes autem sublimius vivens quam possint homines vel intelligere, loquitur tamen eis prout possunt audire. Facite, inquit, fructus dignos poenitentiae Humanum, inquit, dico, propter infirmitatem carnis vestrae (Rom. VI, 19) Si nondum esse potest in vobis omnium plenitudo bonorum, sit saltem vera omnium poenitudo malorum. Si nondum facere praevaletis perfectae fructus justitiae, sit interim haec vestra perfectio, ut faciatis dignos fructus poenitentiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. O brothers, whose purpose it is (would that the desire were likewise!) to hasten toward perfection: how quickly would that man attain Christian perfection who made his mind teachable to this master\u2014whose very first elements of righteousness surpassed the measure of human perfection; whose rudiments, in his earliest age, outdid the gravity of old men's wisdom! For he who was holy before he was born\u2014what wonder if, in the course of his life, he was more than holy? We can marvel at your holiness, O holiest of the holy ones, but we cannot imitate it. It is altogether necessary that you, who hasten to prepare for the Lord a perfect people out of publicans and sinners, speak to them more mildly than you live, and temper the measure of perfection not according to the pattern of your own life, but according to the capacity of human mediocrity. 'Bring forth,' he says, 'fruits worthy of repentance' (Matth. III, 8). We indeed, O brothers, boast of speaking more perfectly than we live; but John, living more sublimely than men can even understand, nevertheless speaks to them as they are able to hear. 'Bring forth,' he says, 'fruits worthy of repentance. I speak in human terms,' he says, 'because of the weakness of your flesh' (Rom. VI, 19). If the fullness of all good things cannot yet be in you, let there at least be true repentance for all evil things. If you cannot yet manage to produce the fruits of perfect righteousness, let this be your perfection for the present: that you bring forth fruits worthy of repentance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Si recordari volumus, fratres, lectionis hesternae, ad dignos fructus poenitentiae pertinet quod Jeremiae, aut certe Joanni sub typo Jeremiae dictum est, Ut evellas, et destruas, et disperdas, et dissipes: ad fructus justitiae, ut aedifices et plantes (Jerem. I, 10, et XVIII, 7). Beatus qui manus hujus exstirpatoris adjuverit (nam ipse Dei adjutor est), qui videlicet cooperans verbo et gratiae Dei, in hoc studium et operam dederit ut evellat et disperdat de affectionibus et moribus suis omnem plantationem quam non plantavit Pater coelestis, destruat et dissipet omnem aedificationem Babylonicae superbiae et confusionis; ut postmodum melius aedificet et plantet, fiatque sicut scriptum est, Lateres ceciderunt, sed quadris lapidibus aedificabimus. Sycomoros succiderunt, sed cedros immutabimus (Isa. IX, 10). Ascendat abies pro saliuncula, et myrtus pro urtica (Id. LV, 13): omnis scilicet pulchritudo et gratia virtutum, pro situ et horrore vitiorum. Quis, putas, est ille tam perfectus in nobis, ut saltem hoc initium perfectionis habeat, qui scilicet ita digne perfecteque poenitens sit, ac tota severitate damnet mala sua quae fecit, prioribus usquequaque renuntians vitiis, ut omne germen maledictionis radicitus evellat et disperdat de agro cordis, ita ut de radice pessima, nullus amplius germinet fructus amaritudinis; quique semel ita destruat ac dissipet omnem altitudinem extollentem se adversus humilitatem Christi, ut iterum non reaedificet quae destruxit? Quam felix hodie esset Ecclesia sanctorum, quanta pace et gratia florerent congregationes beatorum pauperum, si fructus istos quos facere debuerat, rudium poenitentia, vel eorum qui perfecti et sancti videri volunt, afferret justitia! Denique, qui nec justitiam sanctorum, nec exactam habemus poenitentiam peccatorum, redimamus vel ex parte aliqua teporem nostrum devota veneratione sanctorum: praecipue beati Joannis, cujus tam magnifice sanctitas effloruit supra modum aliorum, ut crederetur Sanctus sanctorum. Magnificentiam gloriae sanctitatis ejus loquamur ac meditemur, fratres mei, ut propitium faciat peccatis nostris eum, cujus singulariter amicus esse meruit, Filium Dei, qui cum Patre et Spiritu sancto vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. If we wish to recall, brothers, yesterday's reading, it belongs to worthy fruits of repentance what was said to Jeremiah\u2014or indeed to John under the type of Jeremiah\u2014'that you may root out, and pull down, and destroy, and throw down'; and to the fruits of righteousness belongs, 'that you may build and plant' (Jerem. I, 10, et XVIII, 7). Blessed is he who helps the hands of this uprooter (for he himself is God's helper)\u2014namely, one who, cooperating with the word and grace of God, devotes study and effort to this: that he may root out and cast away from his affections and habits every planting which the heavenly Father did not plant, and may destroy and throw down every edifice of Babylonian pride and confusion; so that afterward he may build and plant the better, and it may come to pass as it is written: 'The bricks have fallen, but we will build with squared stones; the sycamores have been cut down, but we will change them for cedars' (Isa. IX, 10). 'Let the fir tree rise up in place of the brier, and the myrtle in place of the nettle' (Id. LV, 13)\u2014that is, let all the beauty and grace of the virtues rise up in place of the squalor and horror of the vices. Who, do you suppose, is so perfect among us that he has even this beginning of perfection\u2014namely, one who repents so worthily and so perfectly, and with total severity condemns the evils he has done, utterly renouncing his former vices, that he roots out and casts away, down to the root, every seed of cursing from the field of his heart, so that from that most evil root no fruit of bitterness may ever sprout again; and who, having once destroyed and thrown down every height that exalts itself against the humility of Christ, does not build up again what he has destroyed? How happy would the Church of the saints be today, with what peace and grace would the congregations of the blessed poor flourish, if the repentance of the untaught, or the righteousness of those who wish to appear perfect and holy, bore those fruits which it ought to have produced In short, since we have neither the righteousness of the saints nor the exact repentance of sinners, let us redeem our lukewarmness, at least in some part, by devout veneration of the saints\u2014especially of blessed John, whose holiness blossomed so magnificently beyond the measure of others that he was believed to be the Holy One of the holy ones. Let us speak of and meditate upon the magnificence of the glory of his holiness, my brothers, so that he may render favorable toward our sins the one whose singular friend he merited to be\u2014the Son of God, who with the Father and the Holy Spirit lives and reigns through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-n009", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(9) Joannes enim gratia Dei interpretatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01-n009", "label": "English", "text": "(9) For John is interpreted as \"the grace of God.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon I", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "Virgin Mary", "grace", "prophecy", "repentance", "perfection", "intercession"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De vi necessaria ad rapiendum regnum coelorum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 167-169.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Matthew 11:12", "Genesis 32:26, 29\u201330", "Hosea 12:4", "Wisdom 7:10", "2 Corinthians 12:3", "Genesis 32:25", "Ecclesiastes 7:24", "Isaiah 64:7", "Jeremiah 20:7", "Proverbs 16 (Septuagint; printed Prov. XVI, sec. LXX)", "Matthew 3:2"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the saying that from the days of John the Baptist the kingdom of heaven suffers violence. Repentance is the force by which sinners seize what was not granted to nature, and the brothers must wrestle without relenting, as Jacob wrestled with the angel. Christ, weakened even to death by love, cannot resist such force. Guerric calls the brothers to magnanimity and constancy under the example of John. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De vi necessaria ad rapiendum regnum coelorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the force necessary to seize the kingdom of heaven.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. A diebus Joannis Baptistae regnum coelorum vim patitur, et violenti diripiunt illud (Matth. XI, 12). Merito igitur laeta nobis est ejus nativitas, cujus tam fausta sunt tempora, ut regnum Dei, jam exinde nobis expositum sit ad diripiendum, quibus utique justitia non sufficiebat ad promerendum. Merito multi in nativitate ejus gaudent, sicut promittebat angelus; sub quo tanta felicitate mutatus est temporum status, ut regnum Dei quod ante nulla innocentium obtinebat justitia, nunc poenitentium invadat et possideat violentia. Quid enim aliud debuit dici poenitentia peccatorum, nisi violentia in regnum coelorum? An non violentia est, rapere virtute quod non erat concessum naturae; ut qui natura erant filii irae et gehennae, labore improbo qui omnia vincit, intrudant se in haereditatem sanctorum et consortium gloriae? An non plane violentus Deo strenuus ille luctator patriarcha Jacob, qui, ut scriptum est, contra Deum fortis fuit et invaluit, qui cum eo luctatus usque mane, constanter et obnixe tenebat rogantem dimitti, Non dimittam te, inquiens, nisi benedixeris mihi? Dico quia cum Deo luctatus est: Deus siquidem erat in angelo, cum quo luctatus est. Alioquin nec angelus diceret, Cur quaeris nomen meum, quod est mirabile? nec Jacob diceret, Vidi Dominum facie ad faciem (Gen. XXXII, 26, 29, 30): sed neque Dominus de Jacob diceret per Osee prophetam, in Bethel locutus est nobiscum (Osee XII, 4). Bona igitur violentia, quae benedictionem extorsit: felix lucta, qua Deus homini succubuit, victusque victorem gratia benedictionis et honore sanctioris nominis muneravit! Quid enim, si tetigit nervum femoris ejus, et emarcuit, ac de caetero claudicavit? Facilis jactura corporis damnumque consolabile, quod tanto compensatum est munere: praesertim illi qui potuit dicere, Super salutem et omnem pulchritudinem dilexi sapientiam (Sap. VII, 10). Utinam marcescat in me non solum nervus femoris, sed virtus totius corporis; dummodo vel unam merear benedictionem angeli. Utinam non solo claudicem cum Jacob, sed et moriar cum Paulo, ut gratiam et nomen Israelis obtineam munere perpetuo. Jacob quidem femur gerit marcidum, Paulus vero, corpus emortuum: quia mortificationem membrorum, quam coepit propheticae religionis initium, ad integrum consummavit Evangelium. Jacob claudicans, quia ex parte quae mundi sunt cogitans, alterum pedem a terra suspensum portat. Paulus cogitans solum quae Dei sunt, sive in corpore, sive extra corpus, nescit, Deus scit (II Cor. XII, 3), totus tamen in spiritu liber ad coelum volat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"From the days of John the Baptist the kingdom of heaven suffers violence, and the violent bear it away\" (Matth. XI, 12). Rightly, then, is his birth joyful for us, since its times are so blessed that the kingdom of God is thenceforth set before us to be seized by force\u2014by us, indeed, for whom righteousness alone would not have sufficed to merit it. Rightly do many rejoice at his nativity, as the angel promised; for under him the state of the times was changed to such happiness that the kingdom of God, which before no righteousness of the innocent could obtain, is now invaded and possessed by the violence of the penitent. For what else ought the repentance of sinners to be called, but violence against the kingdom of heaven? Is it not violence, to seize by force what was not granted to nature; so that those who were by nature children of wrath and of hell, by relentless labor that conquers all things, thrust themselves into the inheritance of the saints and the fellowship of glory? Was not that strenuous wrestler, the patriarch Jacob, plainly violent toward God, who, as it is written, was strong against God and prevailed, who, wrestling with him until morning, held fast, steadfastly and insistently, to him who asked to be let go, saying, \"I will not let thee go, except thou bless me\"? I say that he wrestled with God: for God indeed was in the angel with whom he wrestled. Otherwise the angel would not have said, \"Why dost thou ask my name, which is wonderful?\" nor would Jacob have said, \"I have seen the Lord face to face\" (Gen. XXXII, 26, 29, 30): nor would the Lord have said of Jacob through the prophet Hosea, \"In Bethel he spoke with us\" (Osee XII, 4). Good, then, was the violence that extorted a blessing: happy the struggle, in which God yielded to man, and, being conquered, rewarded the victor with the grace of a blessing and the honor of a holier name! For what matter is it, if he touched the sinew of his thigh, and it withered, and he limped thereafter? Easy is the loss of the body, and consolable the damage, which was compensated by so great a gift: especially for him who could say, \"Above health and all beauty have I loved wisdom\" (Sap. VII, 10). Would that not only the sinew of my thigh might wither in me, but the strength of my whole body, provided only I might merit even one blessing of the angel. Would that I might not only limp with Jacob, but also die with Paul, that I might obtain the grace and name of Israel by an everlasting gift. Jacob indeed bears a withered thigh, but Paul a body as it were dead: because the mortification of the members, which the beginning of prophetic religion first undertook, the Gospel brought to full consummation. Jacob limps, because, thinking in part on the things that are of the world, he carries one foot suspended from the earth. Paul, thinking only on the things that are of God, whether in the body or out of the body, he knows not, God knows (II Cor. XII, 3), yet wholly free in spirit flies to heaven.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vobis itaque dicimus, fratres, qui aggressi estis coelum rapere, qui congressi estis luctari cum angelo qui viam custodit ligni vitae: vobis, inquam, dicimus omnino necesse esse ut constanter et irremisse luctemini; non dico usque ad enervationem femoris, unde generatio propagatur carnis, sed etiam usque ad mortificationem corporis. Hoc tamen ipsum nec sic labor noster poterit assequi, nisi tactu et beneficio divinae virtutis, cum vestram videlicet constantiam invincibilem sibi probaverit. Sic enim scriptum est: Cumque videret quod eum superare non posset, tetigit nervum femoris ejus, et statim emarcuit! (Gen. XXXII, 25). An non tibi cum angelo, imo cum Deo ipso luctari videris, quando quotidie praeproperis tuis resistit votis? Lavaris velut aquis nivis, ut mundus sis corde et corpore, et ipse sordibus intingit te. Dicis, Sapiens efficiar (Eccle. VII, 24), et ipse longius recedit a te. Clamas ad eum, nec exaudit te: vis accedere ad eum, et repellit te. Decernis rem, et in contrarium tibi cedit, et per omnia fere in duritia manus suae adversatur tibi. O dissimulatrix clementia, quae duritiam te simulas; quanta pietate pugnas adversus eos pro quibus pugnas! Licet enim haec celes in corde tuo, scio tamen quia diligis diligentes te, et immensa multitudo dulcedinis tuae, quam abscondis timentibus te. Noli igitur desperare, constanter age, felix anima, quae cum Deo luctari coepisti: amat utique vim abs te pati, desiderat a te superari. Nam et cum iratus est, manumque ad feriendum extendit, quaerit, ut ipse confitetur, virum similem Moysi, qui resistat sibi; et si non invenerit, conqueritur et dicit: Non est qui consurgat, et teneat me (Isai. LXIV, 7). Nam si implacabilis sit ejus ira, inflexibilisque sententia, flebit et dicet Jeremias qui resistere tentaverat: Fortior fuisti, et invaluisti (Jerem. XX, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. To you, then, we speak, brothers, who have set out to seize heaven, who have come to grapple with the angel who guards the way of the tree of life: to you, I say, we declare that it is altogether necessary that you wrestle steadfastly and without relenting; I do not say to the withering of the thigh, whence the generation of flesh is propagated, but even to the mortification of the body. Yet even this our labor will not be able to attain, unless by the touch and favor of divine power, when he has proved your constancy invincible to himself. For thus it is written: \"And when he saw that he could not overcome him, he touched the sinew of his thigh, and immediately it withered\" (Gen. XXXII, 25). Do you not seem to yourself to wrestle with the angel, nay with God himself, when daily he resists your hasty desires? You wash yourself as it were with the waters of snow, that you may be clean in heart and body, and he dips you in filth. You say, \"I shall become wise\" (Eccle. VII, 24), and he withdraws further from you. You cry out to him, and he does not hear you: you wish to approach him, and he repels you. You determine upon a thing, and it turns out contrary to you, and in almost everything, by the hardness of his hand, he opposes you. O dissembling mercy, that feigns hardness; with how great pity do you fight against those for whom you fight! For although you conceal these things in your heart, I know nevertheless that you love those who love you, and the immense abundance of your sweetness, which you hide for those who fear you. Do not despair, then; act steadfastly, happy soul, who have begun to wrestle with God: he indeed loves to suffer violence from you, he desires to be overcome by you. For even when he is angry, and stretches out his hand to strike, he seeks, as he himself confesses, a man like Moses, who may resist him; and if he does not find one, he complains and says: \"There is none that ariseth, and taketh hold of me\" (Isai. LXIV, 7). For if his anger were implacable, and his sentence inflexible, Jeremiah, who had attempted to resist, will weep and say: \"Thou wast stronger than I, and didst prevail\" (Jerem. XX, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed absit, fratres, qui placita ei postulatis, absit ut fortis adversum vos sit, qui pro vobis infirmari usque ad mortem voluit! Tot vulneribus confossus est, toto corpore crucifixus est; unde, quaeso, virtus ei esse potest ad resistendum illi charitati, quae veluti victum et captum per omnia genera infirmitatum usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis perduxit? Jam non fortis ut mors, sed fortior quam mors dilectio: cum virtute dilectionis infirmata sit usque ad mortem Dei fortitudo; cujus tamen infirmitas fortior inventa est omni fortissimo; cujus mors probata est esse mors tua, o mors. Virtute igitur dilectionis armatus sis, quicunque es ille pius invasor, qui rapere contendis regnum coelorum: et securus esto, quia facile vinces regem ipsum coelorum. Si qua enim videtur tibi adversari difficultate, aut duritia; ne sis pusillanimis, sed intellige qua mente id faciat. Nempe ut contrarietate ipse tibi acuat animum, sicut solet esse natura magnanimorum ac fortium; ut vires exercitet, constantiam probet, multiplicet victorias, augeatque coronas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But far be it, brothers\u2014you who ask of him what is pleasing\u2014far be it that he should be strong against you, he who was willing to be weakened even unto death for you! He was pierced with so many wounds, he was crucified in his whole body; whence, I ask, can he have strength to resist that love which led him, as though conquered and captured, through every kind of weakness even unto death\u2014death, moreover, of the cross? Now love is not strong as death, but stronger than death: since by the strength of love the strength of God was weakened even unto death; yet his weakness was found stronger than every strongest thing; whose death has proved to be your death, O death. Be armed, therefore, with the strength of love, whoever you are, you devout invader who strive to seize the kingdom of heaven: and be assured that you will easily conquer the King of heaven himself. For if some difficulty or hardness seems to oppose you, do not be faint of heart, but understand with what mind he does this. Surely it is so that by this opposition he may himself sharpen your spirit, as is the nature of the magnanimous and strong; so that he may exercise your strength, test your constancy, multiply your victories, and increase your crowns.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quamobrem accingimini, filii Israel, et estote filii fortitudinis: non enim opus est nisi magnanimitate et constantia, quae nullis possit terreri adversis. Infirmus dicat, Fortis sum: et prae gaudio spei se nesciat infirmum, cui tam facile jam jamque capiendum est coelum. Plane coelum violenter rapit, qui imbecillitati suae vim facit, aut aetati. Imo, ut magis proprie loquar, plane vim facit propriae perditioni, qui intonante tuba mandati, nescit parcere sibi. Homo enim in laboribus, ut Scriptura ait, laborat sibi, et vim facit perditioni suae (Prov. XVI, sec. LXX). Accingimini, inquam, viri virtutis, et sequimini ducem ac magistrum felicis hujus militiae; Joannem Baptistam loquor, a diebus cujus coelum esse coepit expugnabile. Iste siquidem est, qui velut alter David princeps factus latronum, piorumque ductor praedonum, et victoriosum illum exercitum publicanorum et peccatorum per illam laudabilem ac religiosam violentiam post se induxit in regnum coelorum. Quis enim scelestus aut profanus tubam ejus audivit, Poenitentiam agite, appropinquavit enim regnum coelorum (Matth. III, 2); et non se continuo praeparavit ad bellum? Sequimini, inquam, ducem istum, cujus vexilla proprio rutilant sanguine, cujus hodie virtutes ac triumphos debita decantastis veneratione. Ipse, nisi fallor, quos post se traxerit exemplo, juvabit merito, commendabitque suffragio, cum inter natos mulierum nullus sit acceptior regi summo Jesu Christo Domino nostro, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Wherefore gird yourselves, sons of Israel, and be sons of fortitude: for nothing is needed but magnanimity and constancy, which can be terrified by no adversities. Let the weak man say, I am strong; and for the joy of hope let him not know himself weak, for whom heaven is now so easily, at any moment, to be seized. He plainly seizes heaven by violence who does violence to his own weakness, or to his age. Indeed, to speak more properly, he plainly does violence to his own ruin who, when the trumpet of the command is sounding, does not know how to spare himself. For man, as Scripture says, labors for himself in his labors, and does violence to his own ruin (Prov. XVI, sec. LXX). Gird yourselves, I say, men of valor, and follow the leader and master of this happy warfare\u2014I speak of John the Baptist, from whose days heaven began to be stormable. For he it is who, like another David made prince of robbers, and leader of devout plunderers, led after him into the kingdom of heaven that victorious army of publicans and sinners through that praiseworthy and religious violence. For what wicked or profane man heard his trumpet, 'Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand' (Matth. III, 2), and did not immediately prepare himself for war? Follow, I say, this leader, whose banners gleam red with his own blood, whose virtues and triumphs you have today sung with due veneration. He himself, unless I am mistaken, will aid by his merit and commend by his intercession those whom he has drawn after him by example, since among those born of women none is more acceptable to the highest King, Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints non solo rather than the expected non solum (not only)."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon II", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "kingdom of heaven", "repentance", "spiritual combat", "perseverance", "love of Christ", "constancy"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., Iterum de laudibus Sancti., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 169-173.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Matthew 11:11", "Proverbs 27:2", "1 Corinthians 4:4", "Ecclesiastes 9:1\u20132", "Genesis 7:1", "Genesis 17", "Job 1:8", "Numbers 12:6\u20138", "3 Kings 11:4 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Matthew 3:2", "Romans 13:12", "Ephesians 5:14", "Psalm 144:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 88:7 (historical numbering)", "Luke 3:16", "John 3:29\u201330", "2 Corinthians 4:5", "2 Corinthians 10:17\u201318", "Psalm 4:3 (historical numbering)", "Ecclesiastes 3:20", "Philippians 2:3"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric takes up Christ's praise that no one born of women is greater than John the Baptist, and sets it beside the praise God gave Noah, Moses and David. John's greatness lay in humility: thought to be the greatest of all, he declared himself unworthy to take off Christ's sandals, and so Christ glorified him. Guerric warns that the proud imitate the devil by envy and detraction, while John lessened himself so that another might appear. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Iterum de laudibus Sancti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "Again, on the praises of the Saint.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Inter natos mulierum non surrexit major Joanne Baptista (Matth. XI, 11). Salomon ait: Laudent te labia proximi tui (Prov. XXVII, 2). Sed quam felicius et gloriosius, cum laudant quempiam labia Dei sui! Deus quippe nec falli potest, nec adulari. Deus neminem facile laudat, quem laude posse inflari videat, vel quem ex fine debere reprobari praevideat. Homini quidem recte dicitur, ne laudet hominem in vita sua; cujus sicut scire non potest intima, si nec praescire novissima; cum de seipso quoque confiteri habeat: Nihil mihi conscius sum, sed non in hoc justificatus sum (I Cor. IV, 4). Sunt enim justi et sapientes, et opera eorum in manu Dei sunt et tamen nemo scit utrum sit dignus amore an odio, sed omnia in futurum servantur incerta (Eccle. IX, 1, 2). Felix igitur, qui testimonio ipsius Judicis dignum se amore scire meruerit; nisi quod testimonium justitiae praesentis, mutabilitati humanae suspicionem ac metum non adimit de futuris. Indubitabile tamen eximiae virtutis et magnae perfectionis est indicium, quoties hominem adhuc corruptioni subditum, suae suffragio laudis illud irrefragabile Dei dignatur judicium. Magnum profecto justitiae praeconium quod summa justitia dicit ad Noe: Te vidi justum coram me (Gen. VII, 1). Magni quidem insigne meriti, quod Abrahae testificatur Deus, propter ipsum implendas esse promissiones, quae ad ipsum factae sunt (Gen. XVII, et XXII, 17, 18). Quantae autem illud est gloriae, quod de beato Job Dominus gloriatur adversus invidum: Numquid considerasti servum meum Job, quod non sit ei similis in terra: homo simplex et rectus, et timens Deum, ac recedens a malo? (Job. I, 8.) Quantae etiam illud erat gratiae, quod pro Moyse aemulabatur et confundebat aemulos ejus? Si quis, inquit, fuerit inter vos propheta Domini, in visione apparebo ei, vel per somnium loquar ad illum. At non talis servus meus Moyses, qui in omni domo mea fidelissimus est. Ore enim ad os loquor ei, et palam, non per aenigmata videt Deum. Quare ergo non timuistis detrahere servo meo Moysi? (Num. XII, 6-8.) Quis vero in omnibus sicut David, de quo gratulatur Dominus, quia invenit virum secundum cor suum? (III Reg. XI, 4.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Among those born of women there has not arisen a greater than John the Baptist (Matth. XI, 11). Solomon says: Let the lips of your neighbor praise you (Prov. XXVII, 2). But how much more blessed and glorious it is when the lips of his God praise someone! For God can neither be deceived nor flattered. God does not easily praise anyone whom he sees could be puffed up by praise, or whom he foresees must in the end be rejected. To a man indeed it is rightly said that he should not praise a man in his lifetime; for just as he cannot know his inmost parts, so he cannot foreknow his final end; since he must also confess of himself: I know nothing against myself, yet I am not thereby justified (I Cor. IV, 4). For there are just and wise men, and their works are in the hand of God, and yet no one knows whether he is worthy of love or of hatred, but all things are kept uncertain for the future (Eccle. IX, 1, 2). Happy, therefore, is he who by the testimony of the Judge himself has deserved to know himself worthy of love; except that the testimony of present righteousness does not remove from human mutability the suspicion and fear concerning things to come. Nevertheless it is an undoubted sign of surpassing virtue and great perfection whenever that unshakable judgment of God deigns to support with its praise a man still subject to corruption. Great indeed is the proclamation of righteousness when supreme Righteousness says to Noah: I have seen you just before me (Gen. VII, 1). Great indeed is the mark of merit when God testifies concerning Abraham, that for his sake the promises made to him would be fulfilled (Gen. XVII, et XXII, 17, 18). But how great is that glory, when the Lord boasts of blessed Job against the envious one: Have you considered my servant Job, that there is none like him on the earth: a simple and upright man, and fearing God, and avoiding evil? (Job. I, 8.) How great also was that grace, that he was jealous on Moses' behalf and confounded his rivals? If there be, he says, a prophet of the Lord among you, I will appear to him in a vision, or speak to him in a dream. But not so my servant Moses, who is most faithful in all my house. For I speak to him mouth to mouth, and openly, not in riddles does he see God. Why then did you not fear to speak against my servant Moses? (Num. XII, 6-8.) But who among all men was like David, of whom the Lord rejoices, because he found a man after his own heart? (III Reg. XI, 4.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Verumtamen quantumlibet magni fuerint, sive isti, sive alii; nec inter istos, nec inter alios natos mulierum, teste Nato Virginis, surrexit major Joanne Baptista. Nam etsi stella differat a stella in claritate, et in choro sanctorum siderum, quae ante exortum veri Solis illustrarunt noctem hujus saeculi, quadam effulserunt claritate mirabili; nullum tamen in omnibus majus aut splendidius lucifero isto, lucerna scilicet ardenti et lucenti, quam Pater Christo suo paravit: lucifero, inquam, praelucanae lucis, qui praecursor Solis diem instantem mortalibus nuntiavit; dormientibus in tenebris et umbra mortis clamitans et dicens: Poenitentiam agite; appropinquavit enim regnum coelorum (Matth. III, 2). Ac si diceret: Nox praecessit, dies autem appropinquavit: abjicite opera tenebrarum (Rom. XIII, 12). Surge, qui dormis, et exsurge a mortuis, et illuminabit te Christus (Ephes. V, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Nevertheless, however great these men were, whether these or others; neither among these nor among others born of women, by the testimony of the Son born of the Virgin, has there arisen a greater than John the Baptist. For although star differs from star in brightness, and in the choir of holy stars which before the rising of the true Sun illumined the night of this age, shining with a certain wondrous brightness; yet none among them all was greater or more splendid than this day-star, that is, the lamp burning and shining, which the Father prepared for his Christ: the day-star, I say, of the light before dawn, who as forerunner of the Sun announced to mortals the day now at hand; crying out and saying to those sleeping in darkness and the shadow of death: Repent; for the kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matth. III, 2). As if he said: The night has passed, but the day has drawn near: cast off the works of darkness (Rom. XIII, 12). Awake, you who sleep, and rise from the dead, and Christ shall enlighten you (Ephes. V, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Non est autem negligenter praetereundum, sed etiam atque etiam considerandum, quam sublimis meriti, quam excellentis gratiae et virtutis, oculus ille quem nil fallit, eum perviderit, qui post tanta praecedentium praeconia, hanc de illo sententiam tulit: quod inter natos mulierum, nullus illo major surrexit; nec nisi de ordine Angelorum, vel qui pervenisset ad aequalitatem Angelorum, aliquem ei praeferendum judicavit. Nec semel quidem, aut perfunctorie vel breviter laudem ejus attigit, sed quotiescunque se offerebat occasio sermonis, laudibus ejus delectabatur immorari: quod satis probat historia Evangelii. Propterea Marcus quoque, Lucas et Joannes initia librorum praeconiis ejus dedicaverunt: ut videlicet auctoritas tanti nominis prima fronte praelati, totum deinceps commendabilius redderet Evangelium; et lucerna in ipso ingressu ac vestibulo ardens et lucens perduceret ad lucem, quae lucebat in tenebris, sed a tenebris nequibat comprehendi. Matthaeus enim, quia eum nativitas Domini in initiis occupatum tenuit, mox ut puerum nutriendum Nazareth commendavit, ad Praecursorem et Baptistam ejus stylum convertit, arbitrans imperfectum fore quidquid de Sponso diceret, si de individuo amico Sponsi reticeret. Haec namque cura non minima fuit, non solum Evangelistis narrantibus, sed etiam Prophetis et Angelis praenuntiantibus, ut lucerna Christi, testis Domini talis induceretur, cujus splendore et auctoritate inimici facile confunderentur, et ex cujus magnitudine incomparabilis Altissimi magnitudo manifeste monstraretur. Cum enim maximus inter natos mulierum Natum Virginis testabatur tam longe magnitudinem suam vincere, ut indignum se confiteretur ejus calceamenta portare: quid aliud dabatur intelligi, nisi quia ille erat magnus Dominus, cujus magnitudinis non est finis (Psal. CXLIV, 3); et de quo dicitur: Quoniam quis in nubibus aequabitur Domino, similis erit Deo in filiis Dei? (Psal. LXXXVIII, 7.) Et haec utique illa magnitudo Joannis fuit, qua inter magnos tam magnus excrevit, quia videlicet virtutes suas magnas et innumeras, quibus nulli mortalium secundus fuit, maxima omnium virtutum humilitate cumulavit sibi; cum putaretur summus omnium, ultro ac devotissime praeferens humillimum omnium et in tantum praeferens, ut calceamentis ejus detrahendis se testaretur indignum (Luc. III, 16)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But this must not be passed over carelessly; rather it must be weighed again and again, how sublime the merit, how excellent the grace and virtue, which that eye that nothing deceives beheld in him of whom, after such great praises of those who went before, he pronounced this sentence: that among those born of women none greater than he has arisen; nor did he judge anyone fit to be set above him, except one from the order of Angels, or one who had attained equality with the Angels. Nor did he touch on his praise only once, or in passing, or briefly; but as often as an occasion for speaking presented itself, he delighted to linger over his praises \u2014 as the history of the Gospel sufficiently proves. For this reason Mark also, and Luke, and John dedicated the openings of their books to his praises: so that the authority of so great a name, set forth at the very front, might thereafter render the whole Gospel more commendable; and the lamp, burning and shining right at the entrance and in the vestibule, might lead on to the light which was shining in the darkness, but which the darkness could not grasp. For Matthew, since the nativity of the Lord kept him occupied at the outset, as soon as he had committed the child to Nazareth to be raised, turned his pen to the Forerunner and Baptist of the Lord, judging that whatever he said about the Bridegroom would be incomplete if he kept silent about the Bridegroom's inseparable friend. For this concern was no small one, not only for the Evangelists in their narrating, but also for the Prophets and the Angels in their foretelling: that the lamp of Christ, such a witness of the Lord, should be set forth, by whose splendor and authority enemies might easily be confounded, and from whose greatness the incomparable greatness of the Most High might be plainly displayed. For when the greatest among those born of women testified that the Born of the Virgin so far surpassed his own greatness that he confessed himself unworthy to carry his sandals, what else was one given to understand, except that he was that great Lord, of whose greatness there is no end (Psal. CXLIV, 3); and of whom it is said: For who among the clouds shall be equal to the Lord? who shall be like to God among the sons of God? (Psal. LXXXVIII, 7.). And this indeed was that greatness of John, by which he grew so great among the great: namely, that he crowned his great and countless virtues \u2014 in which he was second to no mortal \u2014 with the greatest of all virtues, humility; so that, though he was thought the highest of all, he freely and most devoutly set before himself the lowliest of all, and preferred him so greatly that he testified himself take off his sandals (Luc. III, 16).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Mirentur igitur alii, quod a Prophetis praenuntiatus; quod ab archangelo promissus, et eodem quo Christus; cum tamen Christus in cubiculo, Joannes in oraculo; quod de tam sanctis ac nobilibus parentibus; quod de senibus et sterilibus, contra debitum naturae, dono gratiae; quod ante sanctus quam natus; ante propheta, quam prophetaret; quod plus quam propheta, quia angelus angelicum officium vitamque gerens in terris, in carne praeter carnem vivens; et licet innocentissimus, poenitentiae tamen formam plus exemplo quam verbo tradens; quod in spiritu et virtute Eliae praecurrens adventum Redemptoris, et viam ei parans in eremo; quod corda patrum ad filios, et filiorum ad patres convertit; quod Filium baptizare, Patrem audire, Spiritum sanctum videre meruit; quod denique usque ad mortem pro veritate certavit: ut etiam ad inferos Christum praecurreret; ante passionem Christi, martyr Christi fuit. Mirentur, inquam, haec alii, si tamen est qui digne possit mirari: nobis, fratres, non solum miranda, sed et imitanda proponitur virtus humilitatis ejus, qua major se credi, cum potuit, noluit; imo credentes ne errarent, quantum valuit compescuit. Fidelis enim amicus Sponsi, magis amator Domini sui, quam sui; se minui, ut ille cresceret optabat, et de minoratione sui gloriam illius augere satagebat (Joan. III, 29, 30), ante Apostolum opere et veritate dicens illud apostolicum: Non nosmetipsos praedicamus, sed Dominum Jesum Christum (II Cor. IV, 5). Ideo nimirum magna est gloria ejus in salutari tuo, in Jesu tuo, Domine, cujus justitiae et bonitatis est diligentes se diligere, est glorificantes se glorificare. Magna prorsus gloria ejus in Jesu, magna a Jesu: qui et apud se glorificavit eum consortio gloriae suae, et apud homines testimonio vocis suae. Persuasum sibi habebat Joannes illud fidele Sapientis consilium: Qui gloriatur, in Domino glorietur: non enim qui se ipsum commendat, ille probatus est, sed quem Deus commendat (II Cor. X, 17, 18). Maluit itaque in Domino sane, quam in seipso vane gloriari; quia et maluit veraciter a Domino, quam fallaciter a seipso commendari. Ideo probatus est Deo et hominibus; et gloria ejus veritas apud homines, felicitas apud Deum: qui si seipsum glorificasset, gloria sua nihil esset."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let others, then, marvel that he was foretold by the Prophets; that he was promised by an archangel, and by the same archangel as Christ, though Christ was announced in a chamber, John in the sanctuary; that he was born of such holy and noble parents; that he was born of parents old and barren, against the due course of nature, by the gift of grace; that he was holy before he was born; a prophet before he prophesied; that he was more than a prophet, because he was an angel, carrying on an angelic office and life on earth, living in the flesh beyond the flesh; and although most innocent, yet delivering the pattern of penance more by example than by word; that, going before the coming of the Redeemer in the spirit and power of Elijah, he prepared his way in the wilderness; that he turned the hearts of the fathers to the children, and of the children to the fathers; that he merited to baptize the Son, to hear the Father, to see the Holy Spirit; and finally, that he strove even unto death for the truth, so that he went before Christ even into the underworld \u2014 a martyr of Christ before the passion of Christ. Let others, I say, marvel at these things, if indeed there is anyone who can worthily marvel at them: to us, brothers, there is set forth not only to be marveled at, but also to be imitated, the virtue of his humility, by which \u2014 though he could have let himself be believed greater \u2014 he was unwilling; indeed, he restrained, as far as he was able, those who so believed, lest they err. For as the faithful friend of the Bridegroom, loving his Lord more than himself, he desired to be made small that the Bridegroom might grow great, and he strove, by his own diminishment, to increase the Bridegroom's glory (Joan. III, 29, 30), saying by deed and in truth, before the Apostle, that apostolic word: We preach not ourselves, but the Lord Jesus Christ (II Cor. IV, 5). Therefore, no wonder, great is his glory in your salvation, in your Jesus, O Lord, whose justice and goodness it is to love those who love him, to glorify those who glorify him. Great indeed is his glory in Jesus, great from Jesus: who glorified him both with himself, by the fellowship of his own glory, and before men, by the testimony of his own voice. John had taken to heart that faithful counsel of the Wise One: He who glories, let him glory in the Lord; for it is not he who commends himself who is approved, but he whom God commends (II Cor. X, 17, 18). He therefore preferred to glory soundly in the Lord, rather than vainly in himself; for he preferred also to be commended truly by the Lord, rather than falsely by himself. Therefore he was approved by God and by men; and his glory is truth before men, happiness before God \u2014 for if he had glorified himself, his glory would have been nothing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. O filii hominum, usquequo gravi corde? utquid diligitis vanitatem, et quaeritis mendacium? (Psal. IV, 3.) Utquid vanam et mendacem gloriam diligitis, et gloriam quae a Deo solo est, non vultis? Sed quomodo quaeritis? Utinam agendo fortia, non loquendo sublimia! utinam saltem vera loquendo, licet vane, et non mentiendo plane! utinam denique mentiendo tantum de vobis, non etiam detrahendo de aliis! Hoc jam non est gloriam accipere vel quaerere, sed magis furari et rapere. Quaerere siquidem gloriam, non sua, sed contraria via; non illa scilicet cui debetur gloria, sed contumelia, id est, non virtutis titulo, sed mendacii et detractionis latrocinio: quid aliud est hoc, nisi rapere, vel furari nequiter, quod concupieris turpiter? Certe si gloriam ab homine non quaereres, sed oblatam acciperes, jam ex hoc secundum veritatem non ambulares. Nunc autem quid, cum non solum accipis si offertur, sed et quaeris cum non offertur? quid denique, cum alium velut trucidas venenosa et insidiosa lingua, ut gloriam ejus tibi rapias; ut de malo ejus tu melior, de contemptu ejus tu gloriosior appareas? Sit ergo illud, gloriam scilicet accipere vel quaerere ab homine, insipientium vanitas: quid istud, nisi saevientium crudelitas? Sit certe illud, ut mitius loquar, tentatio humana: quid istud, nisi imitatio diabolica? Plane imitantur diabolum qui sunt ex parte illius, id est, omnes superbi. Ipse est enim rex super omnes filios superbiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. O sons of men, how long will you be dull of heart? Why do you love vanity and seek after a lie? (Psal. IV, 3.) Why do you love vain and lying glory, and refuse the glory that comes from God alone? But how do you seek it? Would that it were by doing brave deeds, not by speaking lofty words! Would that it were at least by speaking the truth, even if vainly, and not by plainly lying! Would that, finally, it were only by lying about yourselves, and not also by detracting from others! This is no longer to receive or seek glory, but rather to steal and to plunder. For to seek glory by a way not its own but contrary to it \u2014 that is, not by the way to which glory is properly due, but by disgrace; not, that is, by the title of virtue, but by the robbery of lying and detraction \u2014 what else is this but wickedly to seize or steal what you have shamefully coveted? Certainly, if you did not seek glory from man but accepted it when offered, even in this you would not be walking according to truth. But what of it, when you not only accept it if offered, but seek it when it is not offered? What, finally, when you all but murder another with a venomous and treacherous tongue, so as to snatch his glory for yourself \u2014 so that from his disgrace you may appear better, from his contempt more glorious? Let it be, then, that to receive or seek glory from man is the vanity of fools: but what is this other thing, if not the cruelty of the savage? Let the former, to speak more gently, be a human temptation: but what is this latter, if not an imitation of the devil? Plainly those who belong to his party \u2014 that is, all the proud \u2014 imitate the devil. For he is king over all the children of pride.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Quomodo, inquis, imitantur? Diabolus quia superbivit, meliori se, id est, Deo invidit: quia invidit, detraxit. Sic filii superbiae ejus, mox ut infecti sunt vitio ejus, id est amore propriae excellentiae, invident alienae. Ut coeperint invidere, incipient, si possunt, detrahere, ut scilicet accrescat eis quod derogaverint aliis. Quanto salubrius imitarentur humilitatem Joannis, qui detrahebat sibi, ut adderet alii: qui contendebat minor credi quam credebatur, ut alius inciperet apparere quod non putabatur? Denique si non placet humilitas ex sui honestate et aequitate, placeat saltem ex utilitate: quia nulla via rectior, aut facilior ad gloriam divinam; sed nec ad gratiam humanam ulla pulchrior aut justior, et saepe nulla compendiosior: Quanto, inquit, magnus es, humilia te in omnibus, et invenies gratiam coram Deo et hominibus (Eccle. III, 20, et Philipp. II, 3). Haec beatum Joannem glorificavit apud Deum et homines, sicut est dies haec, quae de nativitate ejus gloriosa consolationem praestat mundo, gaudium coelo, gloriam Deo: cui honor et gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. How, you ask, do they imitate him? The devil, because he was proud, envied one better than himself, that is, God; and because he envied, he detracted. So the children of his pride, as soon as they are infected with his vice \u2014 that is, the love of their own excellence \u2014 envy another's excellence. Once they have begun to envy, they will begin, if they can, to detract, so that what they have taken away from others may grow to their own credit. How much more wholesomely would they imitate the humility of John, who detracted from himself in order to add to another; who strove to be thought less than he was thought to be, so that another might begin to appear what he was not thought to be! Finally, if humility does not please by reason of its own honesty and fairness, let it at least please by reason of its usefulness: for there is no path straighter or easier to divine glory; nor is there any way more beautiful or more just \u2014 and often none shorter \u2014 to human favor either. As it is said: However great you are, humble yourself in all things, and you will find favor before God and men (Eccle. III, 20, et Philipp. II, 3). This is what glorified blessed John before God and men, as this day does, which by his glorious nativity grants consolation to the world, joy to heaven, and glory to God \u2014 to whom be honor and glory for ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon III", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "humility", "glory", "praise", "envy", "pride"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:john-baptist", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of St. John the Baptist", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV., De fortitudine et mortificatione., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 173-176.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saint John the Baptist", "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "Matthew 11:7", "Song of Songs 2:16", "Proverbs 8:17", "Isaiah 35:2", "John 1:19\u201320", "John 3:26, 28", "Job 31:18", "Acts 14:21", "1 Timothy 5:5\u20136", "Matthew 11:8", "Revelation 18:7", "4 Kings 6:30 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Proverbs 30:20", "Sirach 5:11\u201312", "Job 1:19", "Job 40:10, 16", "Isaiah 36:7", "Isaiah 24:20", "Psalm 67:31 (historical numbering)", "Wisdom 3:7", "Wisdom 2:2"], "occasion": "In Nativitate S. Joannis Baptistae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist", "occasion_label": "Saint John the Baptist", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads Christ's questions to the crowds about John: no reed shaken by the wind, no man in soft garments, but a prophet. John's firmness before Herod, his contempt of bodily comfort and his prophetic grace are set against those who seek ease. Those who cannot match John's rough clothing and food should at least not be reeds in the wind. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Saint John the Baptist in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De fortitudine et mortificatione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On fortitude and mortification.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Coepit Jesus dicere ad turbas de Joanne: Quid existis videre in deserto? (Matth. XI, 7.) Ecce illud quod sponsa de sponso gloriatur in Cantico amoris sui, Ego dilecto meo, et dilectus meus mihi (Cantic. II, 16): Joannes Jesu, et Jesus Joanni. Joannes Jesum praedicat, Joannem Jesus commendat. Par pari redditur, et tam amica quam justa vicissitudine charitas in invicem provocatur, aut remuneratur. Ego enim, inquit Jesus, diligentes me diligo, et glorificantes me glorificabo (Prov. VIII, 17). Habet pietas promissionem vitae quae nunc est, et futurae; et Dominus dilectum sibi quem glorificaturus est in futuro redditione praemii, etiam nunc incipit glorificare redditione [Al. exhibitione] praeconii, testemque suum etiam apud homines proprii sublimat honore testimonii. Quid existis, inquit, videre in deserto? Vos hominem miramini commorantem in deserto: sed per istum pinguescent speciosa deserti, et florebit solitudo, cum ubique novi cultores eremi de Joannis succrescent exemplo. Tunc erit desertum quasi deliciae paradisi, et solitudo quasi hortus Domini. Tunc gloria Libani dabitur ei, decor Carmeli et Saron (Isai. XXXV, 2). Sed quid, inquam, existis videre in deserto? arundinem vento agitatam? (Matth. XI, 7.) Et si moretur in desertis, non est tamen arundo deserti, sed cedrus paradisi, columna coeli, gloria generis humani, miraculum mundi, virtute et merito ultra mensuram hominum, conditione citra naturam angelorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Jesus began to say to the crowds concerning John: What did you go out into the desert to see? (Matth. XI, 7.) Behold that which the bride boasts of concerning the bridegroom in the Song of her love: I am my beloved's, and my beloved is mine (Cantic. II, 16): John is Jesus', and Jesus is John's. John preaches Jesus, Jesus commends John. Like is repaid with like, and charity, by so friendly as well as just a reciprocity, is either provoked or rewarded in turn. For Jesus says, I love those who love me, and I will glorify those who glorify me (Prov. VIII, 17). Godliness has the promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come; and the one dear to him, whom the Lord will glorify in the future by the giving of a reward, he even now begins to glorify by the giving [Other reading: showing] of a proclamation, and he exalts his own witness, even among men, by the honor of his testimony. What, he says, did you go out to see in the desert? You marvel at a man dwelling in the desert: but through him the fair places of the desert shall grow fat, and the solitude shall flourish, when everywhere new cultivators of the wilderness shall spring up after John's example. Then the desert shall be as the delights of paradise, and the solitude as the garden of the Lord. Then the glory of Lebanon shall be given to it, the beauty of Carmel and Sharon (Isai. XXXV, 2). But what, I say, did you go out to see in the desert? A reed shaken by the wind? (Matth. XI, 7.) And though he dwells in the deserts, yet he is not a reed of the desert, but a cedar of paradise, a pillar of heaven, the glory of the human race, the wonder of the world, beyond the measure of men in virtue and merit, yet short of the nature of angels in his condition.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Non est arundo vento agitata, sed palma fortior omni procella, quam nullus turbo quatiat: aut certe cypressus in monte Sion aeternae plantata, altius scilicet quam ut ventorum rabiem timeat. Non est obnoxius tempestatibus hujus aeris, quia superior est cunctis cupiditatibus mundi. In coelo radicem fixit, quo nullus spiritus procellarum aspirat, unde minas et bella ventorum, et cuncta in saeculo adversantia securus irrideat. Irascatur Herodes, insidietur Herodias, de interioribus eorum ebulliat tempestas, ac nequam spiritus procellarum, qui in abyssis cordium eorum cubat, omnes vires suas et argumenta malitiae commoveat: nihil illum terrebit, nihil a sententia movebit, quominus libere nuptias eorum incestas arguat. Quomodo enim flecterent adversa, quem mollire non potuerunt prospera? Flaverunt siquidem aliquando et molles, atque blandientes venti, aurae scilicet favoris popularis: sed nec illis a rectitudine sua potuit inclinari. Vos misistis ad Joannem et testimonium perhibuit veritati. Miserunt enim Judaei sacerdotes et levitas, ut interrogarent eum, Tu quis es? Et confessus est, et non negavit: Quia non sum ego Christus (Joan. I, 19, 20): cum tamen ei flaret favor popularis, et blandiretur existimatio communis, non ut Christum praedicaret se esse, sed ut credentibus tantum non contradiceret. Sed et alias cum discipuli ejus dicerent ei, Rabbi, cui tu testimonium perhibuisti, ecce hic baptizat, et omnes veniunt ad eum: numquid hoc vento potuit agitari, aut de sua desertione, et alterius frequentia et gloria, ad dolorem vel invidiam moveri? Imo constantia immobili ventum repellens et frangens, respondit: Ipsi vos mihi testimonium perhibetis, quia dixerim, Non sum ego Christus, sed quia missus sum ante illum (Joan. III, 26, 28)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. He is not a reed shaken by the wind, but a palm stronger than any storm, which no whirlwind can shake: or rather a cypress planted on the eternal Mount Sion, that is, planted too high to fear the raging of the winds. He is not subject to the storms of this air, because he is above all the lusts of the world. He has fixed his root in heaven, where no spirit of storms breathes, whence he may securely mock the threats and wars of the winds, and all things in the world that are opposed to him. Let Herod rage, let Herodias lay snares, let the storm boil up from their inward parts, and let the wicked spirit of the tempests, which lies in the depths of their hearts, stir up all his powers and the devices of his malice: nothing will terrify him, nothing will move him from his resolve to freely rebuke their incestuous marriage. For how could adversities bend him whom prosperity could not soften? For once gentle and flattering winds also blew\u2014namely the breezes of popular favor: but not even by these could he be bent from his uprightness. You sent to John, and he bore witness to the truth. For the Jews sent priests and Levites to ask him, Who are you? And he confessed, and did not deny: I am not the Christ (Joan. I, 19, 20): although the favor of the people was blowing upon him, and common opinion was flattering him, not that he should preach himself to be the Christ, but that he should merely not contradict those who believed it. But also at another time, when his disciples said to him, Rabbi, he to whom you bore witness, behold, he baptizes, and all come to him: could he be shaken by this wind, or be moved to sorrow or envy at his own forsaking and another's increase and glory? Rather, with unshaken constancy repelling and breaking the wind, he answered: You yourselves bear me witness that I said, I am not the Christ, but that I am sent before him (Joan. III, 26, 28).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed quid existis videre? hominem mollibus vestitum? Laudaverat eum de constantia animi, et nunc laudat de contemptu corporis sui, ac postremo de superexcellentia gratiae prophetalis, atque dignitate angelici officii et nominis. His etenim duobus gradibus ad spiritualia ac divina nititur humilitas humana; si videlicet adversus irruentium tentationum ventos immobilis persistat, et desiderio spiritualium bonorum corpus affligat. Sicut enim in initiis, ut vincamus tentationes, corpus est castigandum, ne regnet in eo peccatum: sic etiam victis tentationibus in eodem tamen est persistendum, non solum metu recidendi, sed etiam desiderio proficiendi; ut scilicet de mortificatione carnis spiritus vegetior fiat, et quanto levius ac subtilius vinculum gestat, tanto ad spiritualia liberior assurgat. Joannes qui antequam exiret de ventre, sanctificatus erat, cui illud dicere competebat: Ab infantia mea mecum crevit sanctitas, et de utero matris meae egressa est mecum (Job XXXI, 18); quem nullus tentationis ventus agitare poterat: nec mollibus tamen vestiebatur, nec suavibus vescebatur; sciens utique, quia sicut peccatoribus, ut sancti fiant, sic sanctis, ut sanctiores fiant, necessaria est severior vivendi disciplina. Nunc autem Deo gratias, qui dedit nobis, si tamen dedit, sine pugna victoriam, sine poenitentia veniam, sine opere justitiam, sanctimoniam sine labore, deliciis carnalibus simul et spiritualibus affluere. Induimur, si non purpura et bysso, certe mollioribus et calidioribus quam sint purpura et byssus; et epulamur quotidie splendide: et nihilominus distenti et ructantes crapulam, dormiemus cum Lazaro quondam paupere in sinu Abrahae, vel potius in sinu Christi cum Joanne? Plane si ita est, melius nobiscum actum est, quam cum illis, qui tantis emere laboribus quod nos delicati gratuito possidemus. Plane si ita est, ridenda est magis quam praedicanda vita Joannis: et omnem nationem filiorum Dei, qui dura et arcta via secuti sunt Unicum Patris, reprobare magis expedit, quam imitari."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But what did you go out to see? A man clothed in soft garments? He had praised him for steadfastness of soul, and now he praises him for contempt of his own body, and finally for the surpassing excellence of his prophetic grace, and the dignity of his angelic office and name. For by these two steps human humility strains toward spiritual and divine things: namely, if it stands unmoved against the winds of assailing temptations, and afflicts the body out of desire for spiritual goods. For just as at the beginning, in order to conquer temptations, the body must be chastised, lest sin reign in it, so also, once temptations have been conquered, one must nevertheless persist in the same discipline, not only from fear of falling back but also from desire of advancing\u2014so that, by the mortification of the flesh, the spirit may become more vigorous, and the lighter and finer the bond it bears, the freer it may rise toward spiritual things. John, who was sanctified before he came forth from the womb, to whom it was fitting to say, 'From my infancy holiness grew up with me, and it went forth with me from my mother's womb' (Job XXXI, 18); whom no wind of temptation could stir\u2014yet he was not clothed in soft garments, nor did he feed on delicacies; knowing well that, just as for sinners in order to become holy, so also for the holy in order to become holier, a stricter discipline of life is necessary. But now, thanks be to God, who has given to us\u2014if indeed he has given it\u2014victory without struggle, pardon without penance, righteousness without labor, holiness without toil, so that we may abound in carnal and spiritual delights at once! We are clothed, if not in purple and fine linen, certainly in garments softer and warmer than purple and fine linen; and we feast splendidly every day; and nevertheless, bloated and belching from our surfeit, shall we sleep with Lazarus, once a poor man, in the bosom of Abraham\u2014or rather, in the bosom of Christ with John? Clearly, if this is so, it has gone better for us than for those who had to purchase with such great labors what we, in our softness, possess as a free gift. Clearly, if this is so, the life of John is more to be laughed at than preached, and it is more fitting to reject than to imitate the whole nation of the sons of God who followed the Only-begotten of the Father along a hard and narrow way.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Verisimilius autem videtur quod non ita est; sed magis sicut Veritas dicit, quia per multas tribulationes oportet nos intrare in regnum Dei (Act. XIV, 21): et sicut discipulus Veritatis perhibet, quia vidua in deliciis vivens mortua est (I Tim. V, 5, 6). Ideo Dominus cum de asperitate vestis laudasset amicum in omni domo sua fidelissimum, Ecce, inquit, qui mollibus vestiuntur, in domibus regum sunt (Matth. XI, 8): volens nimirum intelligi, quia qui sectantur delicias carnis, regno hujus saeculi militant, non regno Dei. Heu! quam terribilem sententiam coelitus audivi contra mollitiem illius magnae et purpuratae meretricis! Quantum, inquit, glorificavit se et in deliciis fuit, tantum date illi tormentum et luctum (Apoc. XVIII, 7). Utinam tamen his deliciis, tantum mollitie vestium, et suavitate epularum contenta esset tenuitudo delicatorum, nec alias spinas vitiorum germinaret in eis rhamnus voluptatum, ut hoc esset omne peccatum eorum, deliciari, non luxuriari! Ipsi viderint, si ita exstinctus est in eis ingenitus ille pestifer ignis, ut tot fomentis circumpositis nequeat reaccendi; praesertim ventis ex adverso flantibus, id est, verbis et risibus lascivis tentantibus. Soleo tamen audire quia saepe vivit etiam in moriente, viget in sene: suscitatur et exsurgit etiam sine fomite, furit nullo irritante. Ipsi ergo viderint sibi. Nihil enim mihi de aliorum conscientiis judicare, cum sciam multos in omni copia et gloria mundi temperanter et pudice vixisse: et e diverso nonnullos in habitu asperiore et victu parciore nequiter egisse. Sed et scio regem Israel sub purpura sua cilicio ad carnem fuisse indutum (IV Reg. VI, 30); et panem solum nonnunquam regibus appositum: cum hodie delicatissima quaeque ac mollia tegat pulla vestis poenitentium, vel magis poenitentiam profitentium; et fastidiis eorum non satis sint opes mundi, non satisfaciant artes et tormenta coquorum. Verum quia in hujusmodi causis facile quisque sibi invenit patrocinium; alius, infirmitatis, alius, societatis cui morem gerit; alius, honoris quem gerit: esto, vivant ut volunt, comedant ut volunt; tantum ne excedat ulterius hoc malum, ad id scilicet, quod qui perpetrare volunt, confiteri nolunt, quia tueri non possunt; et incipiant notari proverbio mulieris nequam, quae comedit, et tergens os suum dicit: Non sum operata malum (Prov. XXX, 20). Nam et de magna illa Babylone, de cujus medio delicati poenitentes isti videbantur exisse, audeam dicere: Quantum vult purpurata sit, quantum vult delicata sit; tantum meretrix non sit, nec divaricet pedes suos omni transeunti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But it seems more likely that this is not so; rather, as the Truth says, that we must enter the kingdom of God through many tribulations (Act. XIV, 21), and as the disciple of the Truth testifies, that the widow who lives in delights is dead (I Tim. V, 5, 6). Therefore, when the Lord had praised his friend\u2014most faithful in all his house\u2014for the roughness of his garment, he said, 'Behold, those who are clothed in soft garments are in the houses of kings' (Matth. XI, 8): wishing it to be understood, no doubt, that those who pursue the delights of the flesh serve the kingdom of this world, not the kingdom of God. Alas! What a terrible sentence I have heard from heaven against the softness of that great harlot clothed in purple! 'As much,' it says, 'as she glorified herself and lived in delights, so much torment and mourning give to her' (Apoc. XVIII, 7). Would that the frailty of the delicate were content with these delights alone\u2014the softness of garments and the sweetness of feasts\u2014and that the bramble of pleasures did not also germinate other thorns of vice in them, so that this alone might be their whole sin: to take delight, not to indulge in lust! Let them see to it themselves, whether that inborn pestilent fire is so extinguished in them that it cannot be rekindled though so much kindling is heaped around it\u2014especially when winds blow against it from the other side, that is, when lascivious words and laughter tempt it. Yet I am accustomed to hear that it often lives even in the dying man, and thrives in the old man; it is stirred up and rises even without kindling, it rages though nothing incites it. Let them, then, see to it themselves. For it is nothing to me to judge the consciences of others, since I know that many have lived temperately and chastely amid all the abundance and glory of the world, while, on the other hand, some have acted wickedly under a rougher habit and a more frugal diet. But I also know that the king of Israel wore, under his purple, a hairshirt next to his flesh (IV Reg. VI, 30), and that sometimes bread alone was set before kings\u2014whereas today the dark garment of penitents, or rather of those who merely profess penitence, covers the most delicate and soft of things, and the riches of the world are not enough to satisfy their fastidiousness, nor do the arts and torments of cooks suffice. But since in such matters everyone easily finds himself an excuse\u2014one, the excuse of infirmity; another, of the company whose custom he follows; another, of the rank he bears\u2014so be it: let them live as they wish, let them eat as they wish; only let this evil go no further, namely, to the point where those who wish to commit it are unwilling to confess it, because they cannot defend it, and begin to be marked by the proverb of the wicked woman, who eats, and wiping her mouth says, 'I have done no evil' (Prov. XXX, 20). For indeed, concerning that great Babylon, out of whose midst these delicate penitents seem to have come forth, I would dare to say: Let her be clothed in purple as much as she wishes, let her be as delicate as she wishes; only let her not be a harlot, nor spread her legs for every passerby.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Tu ergo si non potes imitari Joannem in asperitate vestitus, vel parcimonia victus: vel in hoc imitari contende, ut non sis arundo vento agitata; et, ut Sapiens ait, Non te ventiles in omnem ventum, et non eas in omnem viam. Sic enim peccator probatur in duplici lingua. Sed esto firmus, inquit, in via Domini (Eccli. V, 11, 12), ne te projiciat ventus a facie terrae seu loci, cui professus es; seu regni, cui praeparandus es. Solet repente ventus vehemens irruere a regione deserti, et concutere quatuor angulos domus filiorum Job (Job I, 19), et fit ruina ejus magna, si tamen super arenam fundata sit, non supra petram; si arundinea sit, non lapidea: imo si is qui aedificat eam, et inhabitat, arundo sit; foris per hypocrisim nitidus, jactantia vel promissione cassa sonorus; intus veritate vacuus. In secreto hujus arundinis Behemoth dormire amat, quae et in locis humentibus (Job XL, 10, 16), ubi nulla fluxarum rerum penuria, sed ad luxum superfluunt omnia, saepius et uberius crescit atque luxuriat (Isai. XXXVI, 7). Huic arundini si quis innitatur, manum ejus perforabit: quia si quis in adjutorium operis eum asciverit, vel alicujus ei muneris functionem imposuerit, sperantem in se gravi scandalo vulnerabit. Patientia tamen Domini hujusmodi arundinem etiam quassatam non conterit, sed sustinet, ut in melius convertatur (Isa. XXIV, 20). Nonnunquam etiam increpat feras arundinis (Psal. LXVII, 31), quae cum principe suo in secreto ejus dormiunt: nonnunquam justi tanquam scintillae aeris candentis in arundineto discurrunt (Sap. III, 7), qui tanquam carbones desolatorii, quidquid in eo steriliter silvescit incendunt, novisque fructibus purum praeparant locum. Hoc agebat Joannes, vox clamantis in deserto, non arundo, sed scintilla in arundineto: cujus sermo, ut scriptum est, sermo scintillae ad commovendum cor (Sap. II, 2) pigrum et stolidum, ut pararet viam Domino. Hoc etiam nunc in nobis agat eadem vox, cooperante Verbo ipso, cujus erat vox, Jesu Christo Domino nostro, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. You, then, if you cannot imitate John in roughness of clothing or frugality of food, strive at least to imitate him in this: that you not be a reed shaken by the wind; and, as the Wise man says, \"Do not winnow yourself to every wind, and do not go in every way.\" For in this way a sinner is proved to have a double tongue. But \"be firm,\" he says, \"in the way of the Lord\" (Eccli. V, 11, 12), lest the wind cast you down from the face of the land or place to which you have professed yourself, or of the kingdom for which you are to be made ready. A violent wind is accustomed to rush in suddenly from the region of the desert and strike the four corners of the house of the sons of Job (Job I, 19), and its ruin is great \u2014 if, that is, it was founded upon sand and not upon rock; if it was made of reed and not of stone; or rather, if the one who builds it and dwells in it is himself a reed \u2014 outwardly sleek through hypocrisy, resounding with boasting or with empty promise, but within empty of truth. In the hiding-place of such a reed Behemoth loves to sleep, a reed that also in moist places (Job XL, 10, 16) \u2014 where there is no lack of transient things, but all things overflow unto luxury \u2014 grows and flourishes more often and more abundantly (Isai. XXXVI, 7). If anyone leans upon this reed, it will pierce his hand: for if anyone takes him on to help in some work, or lays upon him the carrying out of some office, he will wound with grievous scandal the one who trusted in him. Yet the patience of the Lord does not crush even a reed of this kind when it is bruised, but sustains it, that it may be turned to something better (Isa. XXIV, 20). Sometimes, too, he rebukes the beasts of the reed (Psal. LXVII, 31), who sleep with their prince in its hiding-place: and sometimes the just run to and fro in the reed-bed like sparks of glowing bronze (Sap. III, 7), who, like desolating coals, burn up whatever grows there wild and barren, and make ready a clean place for new fruits. This is what John did, the voice of one crying in the desert \u2014 not a reed, but a spark within the reed-bed: whose speech, as it is written, was \"the speech of a spark\" (Sap. II, 2) to stir the sluggish and foolish heart, that he might prepare the way of the Lord. May that same voice now also work this in us, with the cooperation of the Word himself, whose voice it was \u2014 Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of Saint John the Baptist Sermon IV", "topics": ["John the Baptist", "fortitude", "mortification", "steadfastness", "prophecy", "poverty"], "url": "sermons/guerric-john-baptist-nativity-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-lent-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN QUADRAGESIMA., SERMO I., De tribulatione et consolatione., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 91-96.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Lent", "Lent", "Psalm 33:20 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 2:14", "Luke 6:24", "Hebrews 12:5", "2 Timothy 2:12", "Psalm 93:17\u201318 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 14:29\u201331", "Psalm 117:13 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 36:24 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 18:17", "Isaiah 43", "Psalm 10:8 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 64:5", "Psalm 138:16 (historical numbering)", "Joel 3:21", "Isaiah 3:4 (printed locator; quotation from Isaiah 4:4)", "1 Corinthians 3:13, 15", "Daniel 3:49\u201350", "Isaiah 9:5", "Luke 7:47", "Habakkuk 3:16", "Job 6:9\u201312", "Psalm 6:2 (historical numbering)", "Revelation 3:19", "Job 5:17", "Jeremiah 10:24", "Psalm 88:45 (historical numbering)", "Ezekiel 24:12\u201313", "Malachi 3:2\u20133", "Micah 7:8\u201310", "Isaiah 12:1", "Ecclesiastes 9:10", "Galatians 6:10", "Hebrews 4:3", "Isaiah 3:12", "2 Corinthians 1:3\u20134"], "occasion": "In Quadragesima", "occasion_detail": "Lent", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric distinguishes sorrow over adversity from sorrow over sin. Consolation, confession and penance lead into his account of purification through love, suffering and the judgment to come. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN QUADRAGESIMA."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "FOR LENT.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De tribulatione et consolatione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On Tribulation and Consolation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Benedictus Deus, Pater misericordiarum et Deus totius consolationis, qui consolatur nos in omni tribulatione nostra (II Cor. 1, 3, 4). Multae tribulationes justorum, et de omnibus his liberabit eos Dominus (Psal. XXXIII, 20). Duplex autem genus tribulationum patimur, quia non solum duplici natura subsistimus, carne scilicet ac spiritu, sed et duplici qualitate vivimus; partim scilicet carnaliter, partim spiritualiter. In mundo eramus toti carnales; in coelo erimus toti spirituales: nunc autem ex parte carnales, ex parte spirituales. Prout autem proficimus in spiritu, aut magis efficimur spirituales et minus carnales, aut magis remanemus carnales et minus spirituales. Propterea duplex modo consurgit tribulatio; quia, quod in nobis carnale est, contristat adversitas; quod spirituale, iniquitas. Si nihil in nobis esset carnale, nulla nos contristaret, imo nulla esset adversitas: si nihil spirituale, non nos nostra contristaret, sed magis delectaret iniquitas, sicut de quibusdam dicitur: Laetantur cum male fecerint, et exsultant in rebus pessimis (Prov. II, 14). Illa ergo nostra de adversitate tristitia, carnalis et secundum saeculum est: haec autem de iniquitate, spiritualis et secundum Deum. Illa mortem operatur, si desit consolatio: haec salutem operatur, si non desit consolatio. Multum tamen interest in adversitate exteriori, cujusmodi sit consolatio tristium; quia consolationi divitum comminatio divina vae intentat aeternum (Luc. VI, 24). Quia nimirum plerumque qui humiliari poterant ex adversitate, gravius superbiunt de prosperitate, proditque ex adipe iniquitas eorum: imo et audacter provocant Deum, cum ipse dederit omnia in manibus eorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed be God, the Father of mercies and the God of all comfort, who comforts us in all our tribulation (II Cor. 1, 3, 4). Many are the tribulations of the just, and out of all these the Lord will deliver them (Psal. XXXIII, 20). Now we suffer a twofold kind of tribulation, because we not only subsist in a twofold nature\u2014namely flesh and spirit\u2014but also live by a twofold quality; that is, partly carnally, partly spiritually. In the world we were wholly carnal; in heaven we shall be wholly spiritual: but now we are partly carnal, partly spiritual. But insofar as we advance in the spirit, either we become more spiritual and less carnal, or we remain more carnal and less spiritual. Therefore a twofold tribulation now arises; because adversity grieves what is carnal in us, while iniquity grieves what is spiritual. If there were nothing carnal in us, no adversity would grieve us\u2014indeed, there would be no adversity at all; if there were nothing spiritual, our own iniquity would not grieve us, but would rather delight us, as is said of certain men: 'They are glad when they have done evil, and rejoice in most wicked things' (Prov. II, 14). That sorrow of ours, then, which comes from adversity, is carnal and according to the world; but this sorrow, which comes from iniquity, is spiritual and according to God. The former works death, if consolation is lacking; the latter works salvation, if consolation is not lacking. Yet in outward adversity it matters greatly what kind of consolation the sorrowful have; for upon the consolation of the rich the divine threat holds out eternal woe (Luc. VI, 24). For indeed it often happens that those who could have been humbled by adversity grow more gravely proud through prosperity, and their iniquity comes forth from their fatness: nay, they even boldly provoke God, though he himself has given all things into their hands.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Nos autem et cum abundat illa consolatio temporalis, oportet esse gratiores et humiliores: et cum deficit, de aeterna redemptione certiores et laetiores. Cum tamen suppetit sive salus corporum, sive tranquillitas temporum aut copia rerum; ea moderatione utenda ac dispensanda est, ut sit non occasio peccati, sed adjutorium virtutis, ac potius de bonis bona faciamus quam de bonis mali fiamus. Illa namque consolatione, qua se diffundit voluptas, vel extollit vanitas, renuat consolari anima mea (Hebr. XII, 5); magisque eligat cum Moyse et viris Israeliticis affligi, quam temporalis peccati habere jucunditatem. Sive autem consolemur exterius, sive tribulemur, benedictus Deus, qui consolationem internam et aeternam dedit in cordibus nostris, id est, gaudium spei, quae suadet nos etiam gloriari in tribulationibus nostris, pollicens, quia si compatimur, et conregnabimus (II Tim. II, 12). Illam sane tribulationem, quam interius de iniquitate nostra patimur, tanto molestiorem portamus, quanto periculosiorem experimur; quia nimirum quotidie de vita periclitamur, ut veraciter confiteamur: Nisi quia Dominus adjuvit me, paulo minus habitasset in inferno anima mea. Gratias tibi, Domine Jesu, quia si gravia sunt pericula, facilia sunt remedia; quia, si dicebam, Motus est pes meus; misericordia tua, Domine, adjuvabat me (Psal. XCIII, 17, 18). Et quod semel in Petro figurasti corporaliter (Matth. XIV, 29-31), in filiis Petri quotidie adimples spiritualiter. Jussisti nos venire ad te super aquas, id est, hoc mare magnum et spatiosum manibus: ecce virtute tua ambulamus, sed pondere nostro gravamur, et plerumque irruente spiritu procellarum impellimur, ut etiam mergi incipiamus, id est, pene jam jamque tentatori consentiamus. Sed, si celeriter confiteamur pedem motum, id est, titubasse animum, et fideliter invocemus auxilium, tu misericorditer porrigis manum, firmasque et dirigis gressum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But we, when that temporal consolation abounds, ought to be the more grateful and the more humble; and when it fails, the more certain and the more joyful concerning the eternal redemption. Yet when there is at hand either health of body, or tranquility of the times, or abundance of goods, it must be used and administered with such moderation, that it may be not an occasion of sin but a help to virtue, and that we may rather make good things out of goods than become evil men because of goods. For let my soul refuse to be consoled by that consolation in which pleasure spreads itself abroad, or vanity exalts itself (Hebr. XII, 5); and let it rather choose to be afflicted with Moses and the men of Israel, than to have the temporal delight of sin. But whether we be consoled outwardly or be afflicted, blessed be God, who has given an inward and eternal consolation in our hearts, that is, the joy of hope, which persuades us even to glory in our tribulations, promising that if we suffer with him, we shall also reign with him (II Tim. II, 12). That tribulation indeed, which we suffer inwardly from our own iniquity, we bear it as all the more troublesome, the more dangerous we find it to be; for indeed daily we are in peril of our life, so that we may truly confess: 'Unless the Lord had helped me, my soul had almost dwelt in hell.' Thanks be to thee, Lord Jesus, for though the dangers are grievous, the remedies are easy; for, 'if I said: My foot is moved; thy mercy, O Lord, assisted me' (Psal. XCIII, 17, 18). And that which thou didst once figure bodily in Peter (Matth. XIV, 29-31), thou dost daily fulfill spiritually in the sons of Peter. Thou hast bidden us come to thee upon the waters, that is, upon this great and wide sea: behold, by thy power we walk, but we are weighed down by our own weight, and often, when the spirit of storms rushes in, we are driven on so that we even begin to sink\u2014that is, we are almost on the very point of consenting to the tempter. But if we quickly confess that our foot is moved, that is, that our soul has wavered, and faithfully call upon thy help, thou dost mercifully stretch forth thy hand, and dost steady and direct our step.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Super hoc quotidie justus decantat: Impulsus sum, ut caderem, sed non cecidi, quia Dominus suscepit me (Psal. CXVII, 13). Sed, cum ceciderit non collidetur, quia Dominus supponit manum suam (Psal. XXXVI, 24): id est, cum peccaverit, non condemnabitur, quia advocatum habet Jesum, eo quod etsi septies in die cadit, septies resurgit. Non delectatur jacere ubi cadit, et in luto volutari; sed ocius exsurgens excutit se de pulvere, et sordes diluit satisfactione. Per hoc namque justus fit, quod semper in initio sermonis accusator est sui (Prov. XVIII, 17), memor consilii divini, quo dictum est ei: Dic iniquitates tuas, ut justificeris (Isai. XLIII, sec. LXX). Ita fit ut, dum seipsum judicat, et Deum de se vindicat, illum quem judicem metuebat justum, reus advocatum habeat. Justus enim Dominus, et justitias dilexit (Psal. X, 8); nec potest injustas causas suscipere aut tueri, qui, cum acceperit tempus, etiam justitias judicabit. Et tamen qui judicem se minatur futurum superbe de justitia praesumentium, advocatum se pollicetur humiliter peccata confitentium; quoniam apud illum, in cujus conspectu non justificabitur omnis homo, nullatenus causam testificari nostram melius possumus, quam si nosmetipsos accusantes et punientes, justitiam ipsius in nos transferamus, et partes judicis adversus reos exsequamur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Concerning this the just man daily sings: 'I was thrust, that I might fall, but I did not fall, because the Lord upheld me' (Psal. CXVII, 13). 'But when he shall fall, he shall not be bruised, because the Lord puts his hand under him' (Psal. XXXVI, 24): that is, when he has sinned, he will not be condemned, because he has Jesus as his advocate, for although he falls seven times a day, he rises seven times. He does not delight to lie where he falls, and to wallow in the mire; but rising up quickly, he shakes the dust from himself, and washes away his filth by satisfaction. For by this a man becomes just, because at the very outset of his speech he is always his own accuser (Prov. XVIII, 17), remembering the divine counsel by which it was said to him: 'Declare thy iniquities, that thou mayest be justified' (Isai. XLIII, sec. LXX). Thus it comes about that, while he judges himself and vindicates God's justice against himself, the guilty man has as his advocate him whom, as judge, he feared to be just. For 'the Lord is just, and he has loved justice' (Psal. X, 8); nor can he who, when he shall have received the appointed time, will judge even deeds of justice, take up or defend unjust causes. And yet he who threatens that he will be a judge to those who presume proudly upon their own justice, promises that he will be an advocate to those who humbly confess their sins; since before him, in whose sight no man shall be justified, we can in no way better bear witness to our cause than if, accusing and punishing ourselves, we transfer his justice onto ourselves, and carry out the part of a judge against the guilty.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Sed etsi in aliquo veniali vel per ignorantiam fallimur, vel per infirmitatem vincimur, ut Jebusaeum nostrum nequaquam exterminare praevaleamus, sed semper sub oculis nostris in terra nobiscum habitare patiamur; in hac quoque imperfectione nostra consolatur nos sermo propheticus, qui nos in se transfigurans ait: In ipsis fuimus semper et salvabimur (Isai. LXIV, 5). Imperfectum meum, inquit corpus Christi, viderunt misericordes oculi tui, et in libro tuo omnes scribentur (Psal. CXXXVIII, 16); id est, scripti invenientur tam perfecti quam imperfecti. Pollicetur siquidem Dominus se perfecturum imperfectionem nostram, loquens per Joelem prophetam: Et mundabo, inquit, sanguinem eorum quem non mundaverunt (Joel III, 21). Hinc Isaias, cum praemisisset de perfectione futurae coelestis Jerusalem: Et erit, omnis qui relictus fuerit in Sion, et residuus in Jerusalem, sanctus vocabitur omnis qui scriptus est in vita in Jerusalem: quod in infirmis nonnisi purgatione divina perficienda sit sanctitas illa, subjunxit, dicens: Si abluerit Dominus sordem filiarum Sion, et sanguinem laverit de medio Jerusalem. Consolatur quidem me lavatio quae promittitur; sed terret modus qui subditur: Spiritu, inquit, judicii, et spiritu ardoris (Isai. III, 4). Ignis enim opus cujusque probabit. Si cujus opus arserit, detrimentum patietur: ipse autem salvus fiet merito fundamenti; sic tamen quasi per ignem (I Cor. III, 13, 15)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But even if in some venial matter we are deceived through ignorance, or overcome through weakness, so that we are by no means able to exterminate our Jebusite, but always allow him to dwell with us in the land before our eyes; in this imperfection of ours too the prophetic word consoles us, which, transfiguring us into itself, says: \"In these we have always been, and we shall be saved\" (Isai. LXIV, 5). \"My imperfection,\" says the body of Christ, \"your merciful eyes have seen, and in your book all shall be written\" (Psal. CXXXVIII, 16); that is, both the perfect and the imperfect will be found written there. For the Lord promises that he will perfect our imperfection, speaking through the prophet Joel: \"And I will cleanse,\" he says, \"their blood which they have not cleansed\" (Joel III, 21). Hence Isaiah, having first said concerning the perfection of the future heavenly Jerusalem: \"And it shall come to pass, that everyone who is left in Sion, and remains in Jerusalem, shall be called holy, everyone who is written among the living in Jerusalem\" \u2014 because in the weak that holiness cannot be perfected except by divine purification, he added, saying: \"If the Lord shall wash away the filth of the daughters of Sion, and shall wash the blood from the midst of Jerusalem.\" The washing that is promised indeed consoles me; but the manner that is added terrifies me: \"By the spirit,\" he says, \"of judgment, and the spirit of burning\" (Isai. III, 4). For fire will test the work of each one. If anyone's work shall burn, he shall suffer loss; but he himself shall be saved on account of the foundation, yet so as by fire (I Cor. III, 13, 15).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Quanto melius nunc arderemus delectabili amore, quam tunc illo poenali ardore? quanto suavius nunc igne amoris purgaremur, ut mundanum in nobis non relinqueretur quod mundi incendio obnoxium inveniretur, sed, sicut tribus pueris ventus roris fieret nobis fornax Babylonis? (Dan. III, 49, 50.) Alioquin, si tepide et negligenter viventes, ligna, fenum, stipulam nobis coacervamus, et tantam ignium materiam hinc nobiscum portamus; cum examen illius purgatorii tales intraverimus, quis poterit de nobis habitare cum igne devorante? aut quis habitabit ex nobis cum ardoribus sempiternis, quando omne etiam vestimentum mistum sanguine erit in combustionem et cibus ignis? (Isai. IX, 5.) Scio quidem quia, si satis arderem igne charitatis, de me quoque diceretur: Dimittuntur ei peccata multa, quoniam dilexit multum (Luc. VII, 47). Nunc autem, si purgari non mereor igne dilectionis, utinam vel purgarer alicujus igne passionis, fieretque mihi quod propheta imprecatur sibi: Ingrediatur putredo in ossibus meis, et subter me scateat, ut requiescam in die tribulationis (Habac. III, 16). Utinam qui coepit, ipse me conterat; solvat manum suam, et succidat me: et haec mihi sit consolatio, ut affligens me dolore non parcat. Verumtamen nosti, Domine, infirmitatem figmenti mei. Quae est enim fortitudo mea, ut sustineam? aut quis finis meus, ut patienter agam? Nec fortitudo lapidum fortitudo mea, nec caro mea aenea est (Job VI, 9-12). Hoc primum quidem peto, Domine, ne in furore tuo arguas me, neque in ira tua corripias me (Psal. VI, 2). Sed, quoniam audio abs te: Ego quos amo, arguo et castigo (Apoc. III, 19); et: Beatus homo qui corripitur a Domino (Job V, 17): corripe me, Domine, in misericordia, et non in furore tuo, ne forte ad nihilum redigas me (Jerem. X, 24), et dicatur de me: Detraxisti eum ab emundatione (Psal. LXXXVIII, 45). Intuere infirmitatem meam, et secundum illam manum tuam moderare, ne vehementia verberis frangat patientiam cordis; sed magis tribulatio patientiam operetur, patientia probationem, probatio spem. Sed vae mihi, si quotidie uror, et non emundor, ut et de me dici contingat: Multo labore sudatum est, et non exivit de anima rubigo ejus, neque per ignem. Immunditia tua exsecrabilis, quia mundare te volui, et non es mundata a sordibus tuis; sed nec mundaberis prius, donec quiescere faciam indignationem meam in te (Ezech. XXIV, 12, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. How much better would it be for us to burn now with delightful love, than then with that punishing fire? How much more sweetly would we be purged now by the fire of love, so that nothing worldly would be left in us found liable to the world's burning, but, as for the three youths, the furnace of Babylon would become for us a wind of dew? (Dan. III, 49, 50.) Otherwise, if living tepidly and negligently we heap up for ourselves wood, hay, and straw, and carry with us from here so much material for fires; when we enter that purgatorial trial being such as this, who among us will be able to dwell with the devouring fire? Or who of us will dwell with the everlasting burnings, when every garment too, mingled with blood, shall be for burning and food for the fire? (Isai. IX, 5.) I know indeed that, if I burned sufficiently with the fire of charity, it would also be said of me: \"Many sins are forgiven her, because she loved much\" (Luc. VII, 47). But now, if I do not merit to be purged by the fire of love, would that I might at least be purged by the fire of some suffering, and that there might happen to me what the prophet calls down upon himself: \"Let rottenness enter into my bones, and swarm beneath me, that I may rest in the day of tribulation\" (Habac. III, 16). Would that he who has begun would himself crush me; would that he would loosen his hand and cut me off; and let this be my consolation, that in afflicting me with sorrow he would not spare me. Yet you know, Lord, the weakness of my frame. For what is my strength, that I should endure? Or what is my end, that I should act patiently? Neither is my strength the strength of stones, nor is my flesh of bronze (Job VI, 9-12). This first indeed I ask, Lord: rebuke me not in your fury, neither chastise me in your wrath (Psal. VI, 2). But since I hear from you: \"Those whom I love, I rebuke and chastise\" (Apoc. III, 19); and: \"Blessed is the man whom the Lord corrects\" (Job V, 17): correct me, Lord, in mercy, and not in your fury, lest perhaps you reduce me to nothing (Jerem. X, 24), and it be said of me: \"You have cut him off from cleansing\" (Psal. LXXXVIII, 45). Look upon my weakness, and moderate your hand according to it, lest the violence of the blow break the patience of my heart; but rather let tribulation work patience, patience proof, and proof hope. But woe to me, if I am burned daily and am not cleansed, so that it should happen to be said of me too: \"Much labor has been expended, and its rust has not gone out of the soul, not even by fire. Your filthiness is execrable, because I wished to cleanse you, and you have not been cleansed from your filth; nor shall you be cleansed, until I have caused my indignation to rest upon you\" (Ezech. XXIV, 12, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Quam vereor ne id cadat in nos, qui districtione vitae, velut quodam purgatorio igne, quotidie coquimur, et nunquam ab iniquitate nostra, sicut oportet, mundamur! et forsitan non mundabimur, donec sedeat Judex ipse quasi ignis conflans et emundans argentum, et purgabit filios Levi (Malach. III, 2, 3). Verumtamen, quantulumcunque proficiat nunc iste ignis, malo tamen interim mihi hunc adhiberi, quam ad illum penitus differri. Nam, si ad invicem comparentur, iste quidem medicina est misericordis, ille ultio irati. Ita sentiebat, qui dicebat: Domine, ne in furore tuo arguas me, neque in ira tua corripias me (Psal. VI, 2). Scio quidem quia tunc etiam, cum conturbata fuerit anima mea, Domine, in ira misericordiae memor eris. Scio quia etiam in luctu et poenis consolarer me spe meliori: et inimicae potestati, cui emendandus ad tempus traditus essem, verbis prophetae responderem, dicens: Ne laeteris, inimica mea, super me, quia cecidi: consurgam, cum sedero in tenebris; Dominus lux mea est. Iram Domini portabo, quia peccavi ei, donec judicet causam meam. Educet me in lucem, videbo justitiam ejus: et aspiciet me inimica mea, et operietur confusione (Mich. VII, 8-10). Et dicam in illa die: Confitebor tibi, Domine, quoniam iratus es mihi: conversus est furor tuus, et consolatus es me (Isai. XII, 1). Verumtamen cur timebo in die mala? Cur non magis in die bona bonis fruor, et diem malam praecaveo? Nonne ecce nunc tempus acceptabile, ecce nunc dies salutis? Cur non audiam modo consilium Sapientis: Quodcunque potest manus tua instanter operare: quia non est consilium, nec ratio, nec sapientia apud inferos quo tu properas (Eccle. IX, 10); et illud Apostoli: Dum tempus habemus, operemur bonum ad omnes? (Galat. VI, 10.) Coeperamus quidem a consolationibus, sed ad terrores devenimus; quia tunc nimirum consolatio utilis erit, si terrori mista fuerit; tunc ingrediemur in veritate tua (Hebr. IV, 3), Domine, si ita laetatur cor nostrum, ut timeat nomen tuum. Alioquin, si omnino vos securos faceremus, diceretur et de nobis: Popule meus, qui beatum te dicunt, ipsi te decipiunt, et vestigia gressuum tuorum dissipant (Isai. III, 12). Tunc autem vere beati erimus, si miseriam nostram recte agnoscamus et pie doleamus: tunc securi in morte erimus, si semper pavidi suspectique vivamus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. How I fear that this may fall upon us, who are daily cooked, as it were by a kind of purgatorial fire, through the strictness of our life, and yet are never cleansed from our iniquity as we ought to be! And perhaps we shall not be cleansed until the Judge himself sits like a refiner's fire, purifying silver, and shall purge the sons of Levi (Malach. III, 2, 3). Nevertheless, however little this fire now profits, I would rather in the meantime have this fire applied to me than be put off entirely until that other one. For if the two be compared with one another, this one is the medicine of a merciful God, that one the vengeance of an angry God. So thought he who said: Lord, rebuke me not in thy fury, nor chastise me in thy wrath (Psal. VI, 2). Indeed I know that even then, when my soul shall be troubled, Lord, in wrath thou wilt remember mercy. I know that even in mourning and punishments I would console myself with better hope, and to the hostile power, to which I would be handed over for a time to be corrected, I would answer in the words of the prophet, saying: Rejoice not, my enemy, over me, because I have fallen; I shall rise again when I sit in darkness; the Lord is my light. I will bear the wrath of the Lord, because I have sinned against him, until he judge my cause. He will bring me forth into the light, I shall see his justice; and my enemy shall look upon me, and shall be covered with confusion (Mich. VII, 8-10). And I will say in that day: I will give thanks to thee, O Lord, for thou wast angry with me: thy wrath is turned away, and thou hast comforted me (Isai. XII, 1). But why should I fear in the evil day? Why should I not rather enjoy good things in the good day, and take precaution against the evil day? Is it not now the acceptable time, is it not now the day of salvation? Why should I not now heed the counsel of the Wise Man: Whatsoever thy hand is able to do, do it earnestly: for neither counsel, nor reason, nor wisdom shall be in hell, whither thou art hastening (Eccle. IX, 10); and that saying of the Apostle: While we have time, let us do good to all men (Galat. VI, 10.). We began indeed with consolations, but we have come round to terrors; because surely consolation will be profitable only if it be mingled with terror; then shall we enter into thy truth (Hebr. IV, 3), O Lord, if our heart so rejoices that it fears thy name. Otherwise, if we were to make you altogether secure, it would be said of us too: O my people, they that call thee blessed, the same deceive thee, and destroy the way of thy steps (Isai. III, 12). But then shall we truly be blessed, if we rightly acknowledge our wretchedness and devoutly grieve over it; then shall we be secure in death, if we live always fearful and watchful.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quoted spirit of judgment and burning comes from Isaiah 4:4; the edition prints Isai. III, 4."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Lent Sermon I", "topics": ["Lent", "tribulation", "consolation", "repentance", "confession", "penance", "purgation", "purgatory", "hope", "judgment"], "url": "sermons/guerric-lent-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-lent", "calendar_label": "Lent", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-lent-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., SABBATO HEBDOMADAE QUADRAGESIMAE. De parabola filii prodigi. (Luc. XV, 11-32.), in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 96-100.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Lent", "Saturday in Lent; week unspecified", "Luke 15:11\u201332", "Psalm 31:5 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 5:5", "Isaiah 44:6 (printed locator; quotation from Isaiah 64:6)"], "occasion": "Sabbato hebdomadae Quadragesimae", "occasion_detail": "Saturday in Lent; week unspecified", "occasion_label": "Lent", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "The father in the parable of the prodigal son receives the penitent before his confession is complete. Guerric considers the abundance of mercy, union with God and the humility that sustains the virtues. The 1879 edition places the sermon on a Saturday in Lent without specifying the week. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SABBATO HEBDOMADAE QUADRAGESIMAE. De parabola filii prodigi. (Luc. XV, 11-32.)"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "ON SATURDAY OF THE WEEK OF LENT. On the parable of the prodigal son. (Luc. XV, 11-32.)", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. O Felix humilitas poenitentium! o beata spes confitentium! quam potens es apud omnipotentem, quam facile vincis invincibilem, quam cito tremendum judicem convertis in piissimum patrem! Prodigus iste filius, cujus hodie in magnam aedificationem nostram audivimus peregrinationem aerumnosam, poenitentiam lacrymosam, susceptionem gloriosam: prodigus, inquam, iste tam graviter reus, nondum confessus erat, sed tantum confiteri deliberaverat; nondum satisfecerat, sed tantum ad satisfaciendum animum inclinaverat: et de solo fere proposito conceptae humilitatis veniam statim obtinuit, quae tanto tempore tantis expetitur votis, imploratur lacrymis, ambitur obsequiis. Latronem in cruce sola confessio absolvit; istum, sola voluntas confitendi. Dixi, inquit, Confitebor adversum me injustitiam meam Domino: et tu remisisti impietatem peccati mei (Psal. XXXI, 5): ubique misericordia praevenit. Praevenerat voluntatem confessionis, ipsam inspirando: praevenit et vocem confessionis, quod confitendum erat indulgendo. Cum adhuc, inquit, longe esset, vidit illum pater ipsius, et misericordia motus est; et accurrens cecidit super collum ejus, et osculatus est eum. Quantum haec verba sonare videntur, tardius videbatur patri filio veniam dedisse, quam illi accepisse. Sic festinabat absolvere reum a tormento conscientiae suae, quasi plus cruciaret misericordem compassio miseri, quam ipsum miserum passio sui. Neque hoc dicimus quo in natura incommutabili humanos ponamus affectus, sed ut in amorem illius summae bonitatis affectus noster dulcescat; dum ipsam plus nos amare, quam ipsi nos amemus, ex similitudine discimus humana."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. O happy humility of penitents! O blessed hope of those who confess! How powerful you are before the Almighty, how easily you conquer the unconquerable, how swiftly you turn the dreadful Judge into the most loving Father! That prodigal son, whose grievous wandering, tearful repentance, and glorious welcome we heard today to our great edification: that prodigal, I say, so gravely guilty, had not yet confessed, but had only resolved to confess; he had not yet made satisfaction, but had only inclined his mind toward making satisfaction\u2014and from the mere purpose of a humility now conceived, he at once obtained the pardon which is sought for so long with such great prayers, implored with tears, and courted with services. The thief on the cross was absolved by confession alone; this man, by the mere will to confess. \"I said,\" he says, \"I will confess against myself my injustice to the Lord: and thou hast forgiven the wickedness of my sin\" (Psal. XXXI, 5): everywhere mercy goes before. It had gone before the will to confess, by inspiring it itself; it went before the voice of confession too, by pardoning what was to be confessed. \"When he was yet a great way off,\" it says, \"his father saw him, and was moved with compassion; and running to him, fell on his neck, and kissed him.\" As far as these words seem to suggest, it seemed the father was slower to give pardon to the son than the son was to receive it! So he hastened to absolve the guilty one from the torment of his conscience, as though the compassion of the merciful one tormented him more than the suffering of his own wretchedness tormented the wretched one himself. We do not say this so as to place human feelings within the unchangeable nature of God, but so that our affection may grow sweet toward the love of that highest goodness, while from this human likeness we learn that he loves us more than we love ourselves.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vide autem quomodo ubi abundavit delictum, superabundet gratia. Reus vix poterat sperare veniam; Judex, imo non jam judex, sed advocatus, accumulat gratiam. Cito, inquit, proferte stolam primam, et induite illum, et date annulum in manu ejus, et calceamenta in pedes; et adducite vitulum saginatum, et occidite, et manducemus et epulemur: quia hic filius meus mortuus erat, et revixit. Verum ut haec omnia praetereamus, scilicet stolam primam, id est sanctificationem spiritus, qua baptizatus induitur, et poenitens reinduitur; annulum fidei, quo subarrhatur; calceamenta, quibus ad calcanda serpentum venena munitur, vel ad evangelizandum praeparatur; vitulum saginatum, qui ei in altari immolatur; festiva illa gaudia, quae, pro recepto indicta filio, toto celebrantur coelo: ut haec, inquam, omnia taceamus, et doctioribus tractanda servemus; amplexus ille et osculum paternae pietatis, quid non habet gratiae et dulcedinis? quid non felicissimi gaudii? quid non sanctissimae voluptatis? Cecidit inquit, super collum ejus, et osculatus est eum. Cum sic super eum affectabat, quid agebat amplectens et osculans, nisi illum sibi vel se ipsum illi insinuabat, se illi inspirabat, ut, adhaerendo sibi unus, secum fieret spiritus, sicut adhaerendo meretricibus unum cum eis corpus fuerat affectus? Parum erat illi summae misericordiae viscera suae miserationis non claudere miseris; in ipsa sua eos viscera trahit, suisque inserit membris. Non poterat nos sibi arctius astringere, non poterat non magis intimos habere, quam ut incorporaret sibi, et, sicut charitate, sic virtute ineffabili, non solum assumpto corpori, sed etiam ipsi conniteretur spiritui. Quod si tanta est gratia poenitentium, quae erit gloria regnantium? si ista sunt solatia miserorum, quae erunt gaudia beatorum? et qui hoc praerogat in via, quid servat in patria? Utique quod in cor hominis non ascendit; ut ei similes simus, et sit Deus omnia in omnibus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But see how, where sin abounded, grace abounds the more. The guilty one could scarcely hope for pardon; the Judge\u2014no, no longer a judge, but an advocate\u2014heaps up grace. \"Quickly,\" he says, \"bring forth the first robe, and clothe him, and put a ring on his hand, and shoes on his feet; and bring the fatted calf, and kill it, and let us eat and feast: for this my son was dead, and is come to life again.\" But to pass over all these things\u2014namely, the first robe, that is, the sanctification of the spirit with which the baptized is clothed and the penitent reclothed; the ring of faith, by which he is betrothed; the shoes, with which he is armed to tread upon the venom of serpents, or prepared to preach the Gospel; the fatted calf, which is sacrificed for him upon the altar; those festive joys which, proclaimed for the recovered son, are celebrated throughout all heaven\u2014to pass over all these, I say, and leave them for more learned men to treat: that embrace and kiss of fatherly love\u2014what grace and sweetness does it not contain? What most blessed joy? What most holy delight does it not hold? \"He fell,\" it says, \"on his neck, and kissed him.\" When he showed such feeling toward him, what was he doing in embracing and kissing him, but insinuating him to himself, or himself to him, breathing himself into him, so that, by clinging to him, the two might become one spirit together, just as, by clinging to harlots, he had been made one body with them? It was too little for that highest mercy not to close the inward depths of its compassion to the wretched; it draws them into its very depths, and grafts them into its own members. It could not bind us more closely to itself or hold us in greater intimacy than by incorporating us into itself and, by ineffable power as by charity, uniting us not only to the body it had assumed but also to its very spirit. But if such is the grace of penitents, what will be the glory of those who reign? If these are the comforts of the wretched, what will be the joys of the blessed? And he who gives this in advance on the way, what does he keep in store in the homeland? Surely that which has not entered into the heart of man: that we may be like him, and God may be all in all.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Jam vero tu, beate peccator (sed licet non ideo beatus quia peccator, sed quia poenitens de peccato), quid tibi, rogo, animi erat inter amplexus patris et oscula, cum prope desperatum refovebat, cum cor mundum tibi innovans, laetitiam salutaris sui tibi refundebat? Et quomodo, inquit, sermo explicabit quod mens non capit? Inenarrabiles gemitus, et inexplicabiles sunt affectus, quos de incomprehensibili velut impraegnatus parturit animus. Angustum est eis cor humanum: unde et scissum effundit se ipsum; et ardorem quem concipit, sed non capit, quibus potest modis, lacrymis, gemitibus, suspiriis, evaporat ac digerit. Norunt haec melius, qui gustaverunt saepius et uberius. Nunc quoque, inquam, cum dimissus post illos amplexus et oscula, te et te ipsum recogitas, cum causam tuam qualis fuerit, qualiterve ab eo judicata sit retractas, et illinc abundantiam delicti tui, hinc superabundantiam gratiae pensas: quid tibi, obsecro, parit cogitatio tua? Quidni, inquit, intolerabilis in meditatione mea exardescat ignis, illinc dolore et pudore, hinc gaudio et amore? Non me hominem putarem, sed lapidem, si ita duri cordis existerem, ut me ipsum non dolerem vel erubescerem, vel ita nequam et ingrati, ut non totus in gaudium vel amorem illius Patris liquefierem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Now you, blessed sinner (though not blessed because a sinner, but because repentant of sin), what, I ask, was in your heart amid the embraces and kisses of the Father, when he revived you who were nearly in despair, when, renewing a clean heart in you, he poured back into you the joy of his salvation? And how, he says, shall speech unfold what the mind cannot grasp? Unspeakable are the groans, and inexplicable the feelings, which the soul brings forth in labor, as if made pregnant by something incomprehensible. The human heart is too narrow for them; hence, being torn, it pours itself forth, and the fervor it conceives but cannot contain, it releases and works off by whatever means it can\u2014tears, groans, sighs. Those who have tasted this more often and more abundantly know it better. Now too, I say, when, released after those embraces and kisses, you reconsider yourself and yourself again, when you turn over in your mind what your case was, and how it was judged by him, and weigh on the one hand the abundance of your sin, on the other the overabundance of grace: what, I ask, does your reflection bring forth in you? Why, he says, would not an intolerable fire blaze up in my meditation, on the one side from grief and shame, on the other from joy and love? I would not think myself a man but a stone, if I were so hard of heart as not to grieve or blush for myself, or so wicked and ungrateful as not to melt entirely into joy and love of that Father.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Custodi igitur, o felix peccator, custodi sollicite ac vigilanter hunc spiritum tuum, hunc justissimum humilitatis ac pietatis affectum; quo semper ita de te sentias in humilitate, de Domino in bonitate. Nihil illo majus in donis Spiritus sancti, nihil pretiosius in thesauris Dei, nihil sanctius inter omnia charismata, nihil salubrius inter omnia sacramenta. Custodi, inquam, si vis ipse custodiri, humilitatem illius sensus et vocis, qua Patri confiteris, et dicis: Pater, jam non sum dignus vocari filius tuus; fac me sicut unum de mercenariis tuis. Nihil ita Patrem promeretur, sicut hujus vocis affectus; nec melius te filium facies dignum, quam si semper confitearis indignum. Haec humilitas non solum peccatores justificat, sed etiam justos consummat, justitiamque eis accumulat, si videlicet se servos fateantur inutiles, etiam cum quae praecepta sunt, fecerint omnia. Peccatum tuum coram te sit semper, et, juxta consilium Sapientis, etiam de propitiato peccato noli esse absque metu (Eccli. V, 5). Incerta et occulta sunt judicia Dei; non temere praesumendum est de illis, cum nil inde certius teneamus, quam quod in conspectu Dei non justificabitur omnis vivens, nisi in quantum se judicat peccatorem. Alioquin quasi pannus menstruatae universae justitiae nostrae (Isai. XLIV, 6). Blande te misericordia suscepit, pie refovit; time judicium, ne gratia, quae data est humili, auferatur superbienti. Elegisti abjectus esse in domo patris tui; contentus eras fieri quasi unus ex mercenariis; sta in sententia, ut, etiamsi promotus es, adhuc ad majora proveharis. Extremum locum semper occupa, aut certe desidera; servitutem tibi mercenarii, non libertatem filii tibi vindica. Devotione quidem filii Patrem cole, conscius quid ipse de te meruerit; sed humilitate et labore mercenarii contentus esto, conscius quid ipse merueris. Nunquam tibi displiceat humilitas, per quam placere coepisti, et sine qua displicere incipies, quantiscunque pollere virtutibus, quantumcunque patri officiosus deservire videaris. Humilitas siquidem omnium virtutum est maxima, cum tamen virtutem se esse nesciat. Ipsa fere omnium radix, et seminarium, et fomes, et incentivum, et ipsa cumulus et fastigium, custodia et disciplina. Ab ipsa incipiunt, per ipsam proficiunt, in ipsa consummantur, per ipsam conservantur. Cumque omnibus ut virtutes sint tribuat, si qua earum defuerit, vel minus perfecta fuerit, ipsa alterius defectu proficiens, dispendium illius de se compensat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Keep, then, O happy sinner, keep carefully and watchfully this spirit of yours, this most just affection of humility and devotion; by which you should always feel thus about yourself\u2014in humility\u2014and about the Lord\u2014in goodness. Nothing is greater than this among the gifts of the Holy Spirit, nothing more precious among the treasures of God, nothing holier among all the charisms, nothing more wholesome among all the sacraments. Keep, I say, if you wish yourself to be kept, the humility of that feeling and of that voice by which you confess to the Father and say: \"Father, I am no longer worthy to be called your son; make me as one of your hired servants.\" Nothing so wins the Father's favor as the affection of this voice; nor will you make yourself more worthy to be a son than if you always confess yourself unworthy. This humility not only justifies sinners, but also perfects the just, and increases righteousness for them, namely if they confess themselves unprofitable servants even when they have done all that was commanded. Let your sin always be before you, and, according to the counsel of the Wise Man, even concerning sin that has been forgiven, do not be without fear (Eccli. V, 5). The judgments of God are uncertain and hidden; one must not presume rashly about them, since we hold nothing more certain from this than that in the sight of God no living person will be justified, except insofar as he judges himself a sinner. Otherwise, all our righteousness is like a menstrual cloth (Isai. XLIV, 6). Mercy has received you gently, has tenderly nursed you back; fear judgment, lest the grace that was given to the humble be taken away from the one who grows proud. You chose to be an outcast in your father's house; you were content to become as one of the hired servants; stand firm in that resolve, so that even if you are promoted, you may still be advanced to greater things. Always occupy the lowest place, or at least desire it; claim for yourself the servitude of a hired servant, not the freedom of a son. Indeed, worship the Father with the devotion of a son, being conscious of what he himself has deserved from you; but be content with the humility and labor of a hired servant, conscious of what you yourself have merited. Never let humility displease you, through which you began to be pleasing, and without which you will begin to be displeasing, however great the virtues you may possess, however dutifully you may seem to serve the Father. For humility is indeed the greatest of all virtues, even though it does not know itself to be a virtue. It is almost the root, and seedbed, and kindling, and spark of all virtues, and it is itself their crown and summit, their guard and discipline. From it they begin, through it they advance, in it they are perfected, through it they are preserved. And although it grants to all of them that they be virtues, if any of them should be lacking, or less than perfect, humility itself, profiting by the defect of another, compensates by itself for that one's loss.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The 1879 edition prints non where the 1576 edition has nos (us), and conniteretur where it has couniret (unite). The English follows the earlier readings. The comparison is Sermones D. Guarrici abbatis Igniacensis, edited by Ioannes Costerius (Antwerp: Philippus Nutius, 1576), signature K v (Google Books PA112)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-lent-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The menstrual-cloth quotation comes from Isaiah 64:6; the edition prints Isai. XLIV, 6."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Lent Sermon II", "topics": ["Lent", "repentance", "confession", "humility", "mercy", "grace", "hope", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-lent-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:nativity-mary", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-mary-nativity-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN NATIVITATE B. MARIAE, SERMO I., Comparatio partus Virginis cum vite quae incorrupta dat odorem suum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 199-203.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Nativity of Mary", "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 24:23", "2 Peter 1:19", "Genesis 27:27", "Song of Songs 1:13", "Psalm 33:9 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:30", "Song of Songs 1:15", "Song of Songs 2:5"], "occasion": "In Nativitate B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Nativity of Mary", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "For Mary's birthday Guerric takes the words \"Like the vine I brought forth the sweetness of fragrance.\" As the vine's flower gives its scent without losing its bloom, the Virgin brought forth the Savior without losing her virginity. Christ is the sweetness that draws, the honorableness that sanctifies and the honor that glorifies, and Guerric asks the brothers for a chastity that flowers in gaze, speech and dress. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN NATIVITATE B. MARIAE"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE NATIVITY OF BLESSED MARY", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Comparatio partus Virginis cum vite quae incorrupta dat odorem suum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "A comparison of the Virgin's childbearing with a vine that, uncorrupted, gives its fragrance.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris (Eccli. XXIV, 23). Natalem beatissimae Virginis matris celebramus, de qua Vita omnium accepit natalem. Nata est hodie Virgo, de qua Salus omnium voluit nasci, ut natis ad mortem, daret ad vitam posse renasci. Nata est hodie Mater nova, quae primae matris maledictionem dissolvit; ut per istam benedictionem haereditate possideant, qui per illam sub praejudicio maledicti aeterni fuerant nati. Prorsus nova mater, quae novitatem attulit filiis inveteratis, vitiumque sanavit tam ingenitae, quam superadditae vetustatis. Prorsus nova mater, quae tam novo miraculo parit, ut pariat, et virgo sit; ipsumque pariat, qui omnia matremque ipsam inter omnia creavit. Mirabilis quidem novitas fecundae virginitatis, sed longe mirabilior novitas editae prolis. Nulli enim incredibile jam erit Virginem permansisse quae peperit, qui Deum agnoverit esse qui natus fuit. Nullatenus siquidem cum injuria maternae nasceretur integritatis, qui etiam corrupta redintegrare consuevit: nec veritas assumpti corporis potentiae praejudicavit Creatoris, quominus servaret sibi, quod pluribus dedit creaturis. Multas nempe creaturas invenies nasci sine corruptione gignentium, et quadam voce sua Creatori super inviolabili partu suo perhibere testimonium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"I, like the vine, have brought forth the sweetness of fragrance\" (Eccli. XXIV, 23). We celebrate the birthday of the most blessed Virgin Mother, from whom the Life of all received his birth. Today was born the Virgin from whom the Salvation of all willed to be born, so that to those born unto death he might give the power to be reborn unto life. Today was born the new Mother who dissolved the curse of the first mother, so that through this blessing those might possess as an inheritance who, through that first mother, had been born under the judgment of the eternal curse. Truly a new mother, who brought newness to aged children, and healed the fault both of inherited and of added old age. Truly a new mother, who gives birth by so new a miracle that she both gives birth and remains a virgin; and brings forth him who created all things, and the mother herself among all things. Marvelous indeed is the novelty of fruitful virginity, but far more marvelous is the novelty of the offspring brought forth. For to no one will it now be incredible that she who bore remained a virgin, once he has acknowledged that he who was born is God. For in no way would he be born to the injury of his mother's integrity, he who was accustomed even to restore wholeness to corrupted things; nor did the truth of the body he assumed prejudice the power of the Creator, so that he could not keep for himself what he gave to many creatures. For indeed you will find many creatures born without the corruption of those who bear them, and bearing witness, as it were, by their own voice to the Creator concerning their inviolate birth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sed loquatur Mater ipsa, quae sui conscia est mysterii, et nos edoceat quomodo vel quid ipsa genuerit. Loquatur autem non novae assertionis argumento, sed antiquo prophetiae oraculo; quia, ut ait Petrus apostolus, etiam miraculis firmior testis est propheticus sermo (II Petr. I, 19). Quid enim minus patet calumniae, aut suspicionem admittit falsitatis, quam testimonium divinitus de nondum natis prolatum? Longe igitur antequam nasceretur Maria, vocem ipsius assumebat Spiritus, qui in ea futurus erat, et tam divinitatem Filii, quam integritatem Matris, opus videlicet suum, contra blasphemias impiorum defendebat, et si persona ipsius, in modo communem intellectum sequimur, quod nunc audistis dicebat: Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris (Eccli. XXIV, 23). Quod enim personae Sapientiae, id est ipsius Filii, tribuit haec verba contextus lectionis, sicut ipsi scitis, qui regulas nostis Scripturarum, nihil huic praejudicat intellectui, quominus, sicut alia multa, possint aptari personae quoque Virginis matris. Sed neque illud ignoratis quod satis superque suppetant alia testimonia, familiarius ac manifestius huic negotio servientia: sed non erit fraudanda exspectatio vestra de his quae lectio proposuit hodierna."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But let the Mother herself speak, who is conscious of her own mystery, and let her teach us how, or what, she herself brought forth. Let her speak, however, not by the argument of some new assertion, but by the ancient oracle of prophecy; for, as the apostle Peter says, even more firm than miracles is the prophetic word as witness (II Petr. I, 19). For what is less open to calumny, or admits less suspicion of falsehood, than a testimony uttered by God concerning those not yet born? Long, then, before Mary was born, the Spirit who was to be in her took up her voice, and defended both the divinity of the Son and the integrity of the Mother\u2014his own work\u2014against the blasphemies of the impious; and, speaking in her person, if we follow the common understanding of the manner, he was saying what you have just heard: \"I, like the vine, have brought forth the sweetness of fragrance\" (Eccli. XXIV, 23). For the fact that the context of the reading attributes these words to the person of Wisdom, that is, to the Son himself, as you yourselves know, who know the rules of Scripture, in no way prejudices this understanding, so that, like many other things, they may also be fitted to the person of the Virgin Mother. But you are also not unaware that there are more than enough other testimonies, more closely and manifestly serving this matter; yet your expectation shall not be cheated as regards what today's reading has set before us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Respondeat ergo Maria blasphemis tam pro se, quam pro Filio; cunctasque haereses solo interimat verbo, et dicat: Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris. Ac si aperte dicat: Partus quidem meus non habet exemplum in sexu mulierum, sed habet similitudinem in naturis rerum. Quaeris quomodo virginitas genuit Salvatorem? Sicut flos vitis odorem. Si corruptum inveneris florem, pro eo quod dedit odorem, violatum crede pudorem, quia edidit Salvatorem. Quid potes calumniari in proprietate similitudinis? Quid enim aliud est virginitas, quam flos inviolati corporis? Quid aliud est filius virginitatis, quam suavitas odoris? Tu tamen cave ne bono moriaris odore. Bonus enim aliis odor vitae in vitam, his qui scilicet salvi fiunt: aliis odor mortis in mortem, his scilicet qui pereunt, nimirum tanquam odor vineae florentis animalibus venenatis. Sane suavitate hujus odoris refocillabatur spiritus patriarchae senis, qui filium tangens, et fragrantiam Christi sentiens, de memoria abundantiae suavitatis illius eructabat, dicens: Ecce odor filii mei, sicut odor agri pleni, cui benedixit Dominus (Gen. XXVII, 27). Suavitatem hujus odoris odoratus est Deus Pater, et delectatus repropitiatus est humano generi, cum Filius offerret semetipsum oblationem et hostiam Deo in odorem suavitatis. Hoc suavi odore trahimur, cum per conversionem currimus ad ipsum: hoc trahuntur adolescentulae, cum per devotionem currunt post ipsum. Quanquam alius sit odor ille qui de fama praedicationis percipitur, alius odor iste qui de vestimentis vel unguentis, vel de ipso fortassis corpore ejus quodammodo crassior aspergitur. Qui utique non est aliud quam virtus quae de illo exit, quae pigros excitat, et fervorem amoris innovat, ut ad currendam viam mandatorum exsultare faciat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Let Mary, then, answer the blasphemers both on her own behalf and on behalf of her Son, and let her destroy every heresy with a single word, saying: \"I, like the vine, have brought forth the sweetness of fragrance.\" As if she were saying openly: My childbearing indeed has no example among the sex of women, but it has a likeness among the natures of things. You ask how virginity brought forth the Savior? As the flower of the vine brings forth fragrance. If you find the flower corrupted because it gave forth fragrance, then believe that modesty was violated because it brought forth the Savior. What fault can you find with the fitness of the comparison? For what else is virginity but the flower of an inviolate body? What else is the son of virginity but the sweetness of fragrance? Yet take care that you do not die by a good fragrance. For to some it is a good fragrance of life unto life\u2014namely, to those who are saved; to others it is an odor of death unto death\u2014namely, to those who perish\u2014just as the fragrance of a flowering vineyard is to venomous animals. Indeed, by the sweetness of this fragrance was the spirit of the aged patriarch refreshed, who, touching his son and perceiving the fragrance of Christ, breathed out from the memory of that abundant sweetness, saying: \"Behold, the smell of my son is as the smell of a plentiful field, which the Lord hath blessed\" (Gen. XXVII, 27). The sweetness of this fragrance God the Father smelled, and, being delighted, became favorable again to the human race, when the Son offered himself as an offering and a sacrifice to God in an odor of sweetness. By this sweet fragrance we are drawn, when through conversion we run toward him; by this the young maidens are drawn, when through devotion they run after him. Although the fragrance that is perceived from the report of his preaching is one thing, and that fragrance, in a way more tangible, which is shed from his garments or ointments, or perhaps from his very body, is another. Yet this is nothing other than the power that goes forth from him, which rouses the slothful and renews the fervor of love, so that it makes them exult in running the way of the commandments.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quia ergo tam odoriferum fructum edidit, glorietur Maria et dicat: Ego quasi vitis fructificavi suavitatem odoris. Pulchre quasi vitis: nam botrus Cypri dilectus suus illi (Cantic. I, 13), de quo non solum torcular passionis rubens mustum expressit pretiosi sanguinis, quo calix praeclarus inebriat; sed et quotidie devotio sancta sibi vinum exprimit, quod laetificat cor hominis, et inebriat voluptate gaudii et amoris. Verum non prius inebriat gustus saporis, quam trahat suavitas odoris; nec laetificat gaudium visionis, nisi prius alliciat pietas opinionis: quia nisi crediderimus non intelligemus, nec gustabimus quoniam suavis est Dominus (Psal. XXXIII, 9). Fides siquidem est quae odoratur, experientia quae gustat et fruitur. Ideo fortassis Maria Jesum suum describens per virtutes ipsius et efficientias, primo suavitatem odoris eum nominat; quia videlicet hoc initio Jesus in nobis habet subsistere, si redolentia sanctae opinionis suae ad se nos trahat. Quis sit autem fructus unde iste odor prodit, et quo nos trahat, aperit cum subdit: Et flores mei fructus honoris et honestatis (Eccli. XXIV, 23). Prorsus iste est Jesus suavitas odoris invitans, honestas sanctificans, honor glorificans. Suavitas odoris, qua velut ad viam adducimur: honestas, per quam deducimur: honor, ad quem perducimur. Pulchre autem honestas dicitur, quasi honoris status. Neque enim honor ille summae dignitatis et gloriae statum tunc in nobis habere posset, nisi nunc honestas vitae et morum sedem ei praepararet. Nullus tunc, sicut nunc, passim cernitur honoratus sine honestate, sicut nec ullus honestus sine honore. Est ergo Jesus primo suavitas odoris his quos vocat: deinde, honestas his quos justificat: demum, honor illis quos magnificat. Nam quos praedestinavit, hos et vocavit: et quos vocavit, hos et justificavit: quos autem justificavit, illos et magnificavit (Rom. VIII, 30)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Since, then, she brought forth so fragrant a fruit, let Mary glory and say: I, like a vine, have borne the sweetness of fragrance. Beautifully does she say \"like a vine\": for her Beloved is to her a cluster of henna (Cantic. I, 13), from which not only did the winepress of the passion squeeze forth the reddening must of precious blood, by which the splendid cup inebriates; but also daily holy devotion presses out for itself a wine which gladdens the heart of man, and inebriates with the delight of joy and love. Yet the taste of flavor does not inebriate before the sweetness of fragrance draws us; nor does the joy of vision gladden unless the devotion of belief first entices us: for unless we have believed we shall not understand, nor shall we taste that the Lord is sweet (Psal. XXXIII, 9). Faith indeed is what smells the fragrance; experience is what tastes and enjoys it. Perhaps for this reason Mary, in describing her Jesus by his virtues and effects, first names him the sweetness of fragrance; because indeed this is the beginning by which Jesus must take root in us, if the fragrance of his holy reputation draws us to himself. But what the fruit is from which this fragrance proceeds, and whither it draws us, she reveals when she adds: And my flowers are the fruit of honor and honorableness (Eccli. XXIV, 23). This indeed is Jesus: the sweetness of fragrance inviting, the honorableness sanctifying, the honor glorifying. The sweetness of fragrance, by which we are drawn, as it were, onto the way; the honorableness, through which we are led along; the honor, to which we are brought. And \"honorableness\" is beautifully so named, as though it were the settled state (status) of honor. For that honor of highest dignity and glory could not then have its state in us, unless now the honorableness of life and conduct prepares a seat for it. No one then is seen honored without honorableness, as many are everywhere now, just as no one will be honorable without honor. Jesus, therefore, is first the sweetness of fragrance to those whom he calls; then, honorableness to those whom he justifies; finally, honor to those whom he magnifies. For whom he predestined, these he also called; and whom he called, these he also justified; and whom he justified, these he also magnified (Rom. VIII, 30).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Talis est igitur Dilectus meus, ait Maria: et iste est filius meus, o filiae Jerusalem. Iste est benedictus fructus ventris mei, hunc fructificaverunt flores mei. Non ait. Flos; sed, flores: quia cum virgo sancta est, flos virginitatis in ea multiformis est. In Maria tamen gratia singulari prae omnibus multipliciter floruit, quae tota pulchra intus et foris, tota vernabat quadam florulentia et venustate pudoris. Nam et in te quoque si castimonia perfecta sit, non solum reflorebit caro tua, sed et super te totum efflorebit sanctificatio quaedam divina. Non erit aspectus petulans, aut vagus, sed pudicitia floridus: non sermo lascivus aut ineptus, sed verecundia gratus, aut sapientia conditus. Non prurient aures libidine audiendi nova vel turpia, nec palatum desiderio sumendi dulcia. Non erit incessus inordinatus, sed modestus: non ipse habitus non dicam meretricius, sed neque superstitiosus, imo religiosus; totusque hominis tui status sanctimoniae gratia florulentus, ut recte sponso dicere possis, cum eum ad secretum tuum invitaveris, Lectulus noster floridus (Cantic. I, 15). Quinimo ipse totus eris quidam flos pulcherrimus, qualibus sponsa languorem amoris sui fulciri ac recreari desiderat, cum ait: Fulcite me floribus, stipate me malis, quia amore langueo (Cantic. II, 5). Hujusmodi justus, etiam cum senuerit in terra radix ejus, et in pulvere emortuus fuerit truncus illius, ad odorem aquae vivae in resurrectione, id est in refloritione justorum, germinabit sicut lilium: et florebit in aeternum ante Dominum, florem de flore, natum virginem, virginis filium, Sponsum et coronam virginum; florem, inquam, quo coronanda est non solum integritas virginum, sed et castitas continentium: ipsi gloria per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Such, then, is my Beloved, says Mary: and this is my son, O daughters of Jerusalem. This is the blessed fruit of my womb, which my flowers have borne as fruit. She does not say \"flower,\" but \"flowers\": because, since she is a holy virgin, the flower of virginity in her is manifold. Yet in Mary, by singular grace above all others, it flowered in manifold ways, for she, wholly beautiful within and without, blossomed entirely with a certain flowering and loveliness of modesty. For in you too, if chastity be perfect, not only will your flesh reflower, but also upon you a certain divine sanctification will flower forth entirely. Your gaze will not be wanton or wandering, but blooming with modesty; your speech not lascivious or foolish, but gracious with reserve, or seasoned with wisdom. Your ears will not itch with lust to hear new or shameful things, nor your palate with desire to taste sweet things. Your walk will not be disorderly, but modest; your very dress will be not, I will not say meretricious, but not even superstitious, but rather religious; and the whole condition of your person will be flowering with the grace of holiness, so that you may rightly say to the Bridegroom, when you have invited him into your secret chamber: Our bed is flowery (Cantic. I, 15). Indeed, you yourself will be wholly a most beautiful flower, of the kind with which the Bride desires the languor of her love to be propped up and refreshed, when she says: Prop me up with flowers, surround me with apples, for I languish with love (Cantic. II, 5). Such a just one, even when his root has grown old in the earth, and his trunk has died away into dust, will sprout like a lily at the scent of living water in the resurrection, that is, in the reflowering of the just: and he will flourish forever before the Lord \u2014 a flower born of a flower, a virgin born, the son of a virgin, the Bridegroom and crown of virgins; a flower, I say, by which is to be crowned not only the integrity of virgins, but also the chastity of the continent: to him be glory through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary Sermon I", "topics": ["Nativity of Mary", "Mary", "virgin birth", "virginity", "chastity", "resurrection"], "url": "sermons/guerric-mary-nativity-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:nativity-mary", "calendar_label": "The Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-mary-nativity-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De pulchritudine sanctae dilectionis., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 203-206.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Nativity of Mary", "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 24:24", "2 Corinthians 5:16", "Isaiah 53:2", "Wisdom 7:26", "James 1:17", "Proverbs 25:27", "Psalm 44:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 44:5 (historical numbering)", "Micah 4:2", "Isaiah 66:7", "Sirach 2:10", "Psalm 9:11 (historical numbering)", "1 John 3:3"], "occasion": "In Nativitate B. Mariae", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Nativity of Mary", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Returning to the feast's reading from a year before, Guerric takes Mary's words \"I am the mother of fair love.\" Christ has a bodily, a moral and a divine form, and Christians love him now in the beauty of his life and teaching. Mary labors to form her Son in all the adopted children, and fair love must rule over base, natural and worldly loves. The sermon appears under the heading for the Nativity of Blessed Mary in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De pulchritudine sanctae dilectionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the beauty of holy love.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, et timoris, et agnitionis, et sanctae spei (Eccli. XXIV, 24). Meminisse potestis cum praeterito anno loqueremur de principio lectionis hodiernae, non inconvenienter, ut arbitror, beatae Dei Genitrici nos illud assignasse, salvo tamen intellectu illo, quo tota lectio Filio ejusdem proprie competit, id est, Dei sapientiae. Multo autem manifestius in eo quod modo de eadem lectione proposuimus, ejusdem Virginis se demonstrat vox et persona, si tamen advertamus, in eo quod ait, Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, etc., quam pulchre et commode Filium his virtutum nominibus describat. Notus erat Matri Filius, nec minus utique quam illi qui dicebat: Etsi cognovimus Christum secundum carnem, sed nunc jam non novimus (II Cor. V, 16). Primo novit eum secundum formam carnis, in qua ipsa eum genuit: sed longe hoc a cognitione illius formae, in qua Pater eum genuit. In illa visus est aliquando, et non erat ei species neque decor (Isa. LIII, 2): in ista splendor est gloriae et candor lucis aeternae (Sap. VII, 26), apud quem non est transmutatio, nec vicissitudinis obumbratio (Jac. I, 17). Visio illius formae incredulis auxit peccatum; visio istius justis servatur in praemium. Igitur inter formam carnis et formam Verbi, quasi medius de ista ad illam gradus est, quaedam alia forma Christi, spiritualis quidem, sed quam in carne palam exhibuit; forma scilicet vitae quam in corpore gessit ad informationem eorum qui erant credituri. Si enim secundum exemplar vitae et morum, quod in eo monstratum est, formatus fuerit in nobis Christus; tunc demum idonei erimus videre non formam solum, quae formata est propter nos, sed etiam illam quae formavit nos."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"I am the mother of fair love, and of fear, and of knowledge, and of holy hope\" (Eccli. XXIV, 24). You may remember that last year, when we were speaking about the beginning of today's reading, we assigned it, not unfittingly I think, to the Blessed Mother of God\u2014while still preserving that understanding by which the whole reading properly belongs to her Son, that is, to the Wisdom of God. But much more clearly, in what we have now proposed from this same reading, the voice and person of this same Virgin shows itself\u2014if indeed we notice how beautifully and fittingly, in saying, \"I am the mother of fair love,\" etc., she describes her Son by these names of virtues. The Son was known to the Mother, and indeed no less than to him who said: \"Though we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him so no longer\" (II Cor. V, 16). First she knew him according to the form of flesh, in which she herself bore him; but this is far removed from the knowledge of that form in which the Father begot him. In the former he was once seen, and \"he had no beauty nor comeliness\" (Isa. LIII, 2); in the latter he is \"the brightness of glory and the whiteness of eternal light\" (Sap. VII, 26), with whom there is \"no change, nor shadow of alteration\" (Jac. I, 17). The sight of that first form increased the sin of unbelievers; the sight of this second form is kept as a reward for the just. Therefore, between the form of flesh and the form of the Word, as a kind of middle step from the one to the other, there is a certain other form of Christ\u2014spiritual indeed, but one which he openly displayed in the flesh: namely, the form of life which he led in the body for the instruction of those who were to believe. For if Christ shall have been formed in us according to the pattern of life and conduct which was shown in him, then at last we shall be fit to see not only the form which was formed for our sake, but also that form which formed us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Est utique in Christo alia forma corporalis, alia moralis, alia intellectualis. In corporali frater est; in morali magister est; in intellectuali Deus noster est. Corporalem suscepit, ut impleret sacramentum: moralem exhibuit, ut praeberet exemplum: intellectualem seu divinam revelabit ad praemium. Quanquam etiam corporalem tunc cernere, in quam angeli concupiscunt prospicere, non minima sit portio gloriae. Beatus autem qui nunc amator factus est formae hujus, quae in exemplum est proposita: nam qui scrutator est illius quae in praemium est reposita, opprimetur a gloria (Prov. XXV, 27). Hujus formae mirator et amator factus erat, qui dicebat: Speciosus forma prae filiis hominum (Psal. XLIV, 3). Vis scire quia non formam corporis, sed cordis, non pulchritudinem membrorum, sed morum praedicabat? audi sequentia: Specie tua, inquit, et pulchritudine tua, intende, prospere procede, et regna. Adhuc fortassis dubium est, nisi addat: Propter veritatem, inquit, et mansuetudinem et justitiam (Psal. XLIV, 5). Prorsus haec est species tua et pulchritudo tua, qua regnum acquisivisti, pulcherrime regum; veritas utique sermonum, mansuetudo morum, justitia judiciorum. Hac pulchritudine facile illexisti et subjecisti tibi etiam corda inimicorum, quippe totus concupiscentia et desiderium. Mirabilis triumphus gratiae, omnino novum ac pulcherrimum genus victoriae, hostem non perdere ad mortem fortitudine, sed convertere ad amorem pulchritudine. Ecce mundus totus post eum abiit concupiscens speciem decoris ejus, non quia faciem ejus viderit, sed quia tam multa amabilia de mansuetudine, veritate et justitia ejus audivit. Ex Sion species decoris ejus, quia de Sion exivit lex, et verbum Domini de Jerusalem (Mich. IV, 2): unde scilicet Evangelium ad nos missum est, in quo quaedam pulchrior Christi facies ostensa est; forma scilicet vitae et doctrinae, quam et tradidit verbo, et in se expressit exemplo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. There is indeed in Christ one form that is bodily, another moral, another intellectual. In the bodily form he is our brother; in the moral form he is our teacher; in the intellectual form he is our God. He took on the bodily form in order to fulfill the sacrament; he displayed the moral form in order to provide an example; he will reveal the intellectual or divine form as a reward. Although even to behold the bodily form then, into which the angels desire to look, is no small portion of glory. But blessed is he who has now become a lover of this form which has been set forth as an example; for he who is a searcher into that form which is reserved as a reward \"shall be overwhelmed by its glory\" (Prov. XXV, 27). A marveler and lover of this form was he who said: \"Thou art beautiful in form above the sons of men\" (Psal. XLIV, 3). Do you wish to know that he was praising not the form of the body but of the heart, not the beauty of the limbs but of conduct? Listen to what follows: \"With thy comeliness,\" he says, \"and thy beauty, set out, proceed prosperously, and reign.\" Perhaps this is still doubtful, unless he adds: \"Because of truth,\" he says, \"and meekness, and justice\" (Psal. XLIV, 5). This indeed is your comeliness and your beauty, by which you acquired your kingdom, O most beautiful of kings: truth of speech, meekness of conduct, justice of judgments. By this beauty you easily enticed and subjected to yourself even the hearts of your enemies, being wholly desire and longing. A wondrous triumph of grace, altogether a new and most beautiful kind of victory\u2014not to destroy the enemy unto death by strength, but to convert him to love by beauty. Behold, the whole world has gone after him, desiring the beauty of his comeliness, not because it saw his face, but because it heard so many lovable things about his meekness, truth, and justice. \"Out of Sion is the loveliness of his beauty\", because \"out of Sion went forth the law, and the word of the Lord out of Jerusalem\" (Mich. IV, 2)\u2014whence, namely, the Gospel was sent to us, in which a certain more beautiful face of Christ was shown: namely, the form of life and doctrine, which he both delivered in word and expressed in himself by example.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. In hac forma nosse Christum, interim pietas est Christianorum; cum nosse in forma carnis, scandalum fuerit Judaeorum: nam nosse in forma divinitatis felicitas est, et gaudium angelorum. Ideo et Paulus sciens quia caro non prodest quidquam, sine spiritu videlicet qui vivificat, nosse jam Christum secundum carnem repudiat (II Cor. V, 16): nimirum ut spiritui vivificanti studiosius totus intendat. Hoc ipsum et Maria sapere videtur, quae dilectum uteri sui, dilectum votorum suorum insinuare cupiens affectibus omnium, describit eum non secundum carnem, sed secundum spiritum: quasi diceret et ipsa: Etsi cognovi Christum secundum carnem, sed nunc jam non novi. Cupit namque et ipsa formare Unigenitum suum in omnibus filiis adoptionis, qui etsi geniti sunt verbo veritatis, nihilominus tamen parturit eos quotidie desiderio et cura pietatis, donec occurrant in virum perfectum, in mensuram plenitudinis aetatis Filii sui, quem semel parturivit et peperit; imo, ut Isaias ait, Antequam parturiret peperit (Isai. LXVI, 7): quia peperit sine dolore, nec experta est difficultatem et molestiam parturitionis, cum pareret gaudii fructum aeterni."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. To know Christ in this form is, for the time being, the piety of Christians; whereas to know him in the form of flesh was the stumbling-block of the Jews; for to know him in the form of divinity is the happiness and joy of angels. Therefore Paul also, knowing that the flesh profits nothing without the spirit which gives life, refuses now to know Christ according to the flesh (II Cor. V, 16)\u2014surely so that he might more earnestly direct his whole attention to the life-giving spirit. This same wisdom Mary too seems to show, who, desiring to instill into the affections of all the beloved of her womb, the beloved of her prayers, describes him not according to the flesh but according to the spirit\u2014as if she too were saying: \"Though I have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now I know him so no longer.\" For she too desires to form her Only-begotten in all the sons of adoption, who, although they have been begotten by the word of truth, nevertheless she labors to bring them forth daily by the desire and care of piety, until they attain to a perfect man, to the measure of the fulness of the age of her Son, whom once she labored with and brought forth\u2014or rather, as Isaiah says, \"Before she was in labor, she brought forth\" (Isai. LXVI, 7); because she brought forth without pain, nor did she experience the difficulty and distress of labor, since she was bringing forth the fruit of eternal joy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hunc igitur commendans nobis sic ait: Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, et timoris, et agnitionis, et sanctae spei (Eccli. XXIV, 24). Ergone est iste Filius tuus, o Virgo virginum? ergone talis est dilectus tuus, o pulcherrima mulierum? Plane talis est dilectus meus, et ipse est Filius meus, o filiae Jerusalem. Dilectus meus est pulchra dilectio in se ipso: dilectus meus est pulchra dilectio, timor, et spes, et agnitio in illo qui natus est ex ipso. Ipse enim est non solum quem diligimus, timemus, agnoscimus, et in quem speramus; sed haec etiam omnia in nobis operatur, atque his virtutibus, veluti quibusdam membris et partibus, in nobis perficitur atque formatur. Tunc enim perfecte, pro modulo hujus vitae, Christus in te formatus est, tunc veritas ipsius in te expressa est, si veritatem quae ipse est agnovisti, et agnitam glorificasti, timore videlicet et spe; et ne spes confundat, charitas diffusa sit in corde. Si huic sensui judicas incongruum, quod alius sit istarum ordo virtutum vel perfectuum, et alius ordo positionis nominum: non enim ait: Ego mater agnitionis, et timoris, et spei, et dilectionis; sed ait: Ego mater pulchrae dilectionis, et timoris, et agnitionis, et sanctae spei: fortassis et in hoc ordine ipso quem ponit, inveniri poterit aliquid congruentiae et rationis. Sicut enim de agnitione timor nascitur, quem, ne in desperationem concidat, spes adjuncta consolatur, ipsique spei, ne confundat, dilectio suffragatur: ita versa vice dilectio castum parit timorem, timorque cum dilectione illuminat agnitionem, secundum illud Sapientis, Qui timetis Dominum, diligite illum, et illuminabuntur corda vestra (Eccli. II, 10). Quanto autem verius illuminato corde Deus agnoscitur, tanto fiducialius in eum speratur. Unde est illud: Sperent in te omnes qui noverunt nomen tuum, Domine (Psal. IX, 11). Spes vero sancta est, sicut Joannes de spe videndi Deum loquens ait: Omnis qui habet hanc spem in illum sanctificat se ipsum, sicut et ille sanctus est (I Joan. III, 3)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Commending him to us, therefore, she speaks thus: I am the mother of fair love, and of fear, and of knowledge, and of holy hope (Eccli. XXIV, 24). Is this, then, your Son, O Virgin of virgins? Is this, then, your beloved, O most beautiful of women? Truly such is my beloved, and he is my Son, O daughters of Jerusalem. My beloved is fair love in himself: my beloved is fair love, fear, and hope, and knowledge in him who was born of him. For he is not only the one whom we love, fear, acknowledge, and in whom we hope; but he also works all these things in us, and by these virtues, as by certain members and parts, is perfected and formed in us. For then, perfectly\u2014so far as this life allows\u2014is Christ formed in you, then is his truth expressed in you, if you have acknowledged the truth which he himself is, and having acknowledged it, have glorified it, namely with fear and hope; and lest hope confound, let charity be poured out in the heart. If you judge it inconsistent with this meaning that the order of these virtues or perfections should be one thing, and the order of the naming of them another\u2014for she does not say: I am the mother of knowledge, and of fear, and of hope, and of love; but she says: I am the mother of fair love, and of fear, and of knowledge, and of holy hope\u2014perhaps even in this very order which she sets forth, something of fittingness and reason may be found. For just as fear is born of knowledge, which, lest it fall into despair, is consoled by hope joined to it, and hope itself, lest it confound, is supported by love: so, conversely, love brings forth chaste fear, and fear together with love illumines knowledge, according to that word of the Wise Man: You who fear the Lord, love him, and your hearts shall be enlightened (Eccli. II, 10). But the more truly God is known by an enlightened heart, the more confidently is he hoped for. Hence that word: Let all who know your name, O Lord, hope in you (Psal. IX, 11). And holy is that hope, as John says, speaking of the hope of seeing God: Every one that has this hope in him sanctifies himself, even as he is holy (I Joan. III, 3).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Pulchre autem et proprie dicta est pulchra dilectio, cum charitas Deus sit, ac per hoc summa pulchritudo; sitque virtus ista fere tota pulchritudo Ecclesiae, quam ipse Sponsus ejus in Cantico amoris invenitur tantum ac toties in ea mirari et extollere. Prorsus dilectio pulchra, quae de corde puro est, et conscientia bona, et fide non ficta. Ubi enim cor purum est, nulla est ruga; ubi conscientia bona, nulla est macula: ubi fides non ficta, non est aliquid ejusmodi quod oculis Sponsi displiceat, quominus sibi Ecclesiam nunc gratiosam, tunc gloriosam exhibeat. Distinguenda etiam erat hoc nomine dilectio sanctorum ab amore hominum, sive turpi, qui nec nominandus est inter fideles; sive naturali, quo amantur parentes; sive saeculari, quo se invicem amant propter saeculi necessitates vel cupiditates. Procul ergo, procul; imo nusquam prorsus sub oculis Dei amor turpitudinis: seorsum autem interim cedat amor necessitudinis, solusque regnet amor internae et aeternae pulchritudinis, quo solum Deum, vel propter Deum amant vere pulchri. Tu vero, bone Jesu, pulchritudo Sanctorum, speciosus forma, non modo prae filiis hominum, sed et prae virtutibus coelorum; specie tua et pulchritudine tua, intende, prospere procede, et regna: tamque late regnet pulchra dilectio tua, ut turpem de finibus rerum perturbet et eliminet, saecularem ad se convertat et inclinet, naturalem sub se regat et ordinet; ut illa pulchra et vera dilectione te diligat mundus, qua dilexisti mundum, Salvator mundi, qui vivis et regnas Deus, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-mary-nativity-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Beautifully and fittingly is it called fair love, since charity is God, and by this is the highest beauty; and this virtue is almost the whole beauty of the Church, which her very Bridegroom in the Song of love is found so greatly and so often to admire and extol in her. Truly it is fair love, which comes from a pure heart, and a good conscience, and unfeigned faith. For where the heart is pure, there is no wrinkle; where the conscience is good, there is no stain; where faith is unfeigned, there is nothing of the sort that could displease the eyes of the Bridegroom, so as to prevent him from presenting the Church to himself now as gracious, and then as glorious. It was also necessary that the love of the saints be distinguished by this name from the love of men\u2014whether the base love, which must not even be named among the faithful; or the natural love, by which parents are loved; or the worldly love, by which people love one another on account of the necessities or desires of this world. Far, then, far away\u2014nay, altogether absent from the eyes of God\u2014be the love of baseness: let the love of natural ties withdraw aside for the time being, and let the love of inward and eternal beauty alone reign, by which the truly beautiful love God alone, or love others for God's sake. But you, good Jesus, beauty of the Saints, fair in form not only above the sons of men, but even above the virtues of the heavens; with your beauty and your comeliness, set out, proceed prosperously, and reign; and may your fair love reign so widely that it may disturb and banish base love from the bounds of all things, turn worldly love toward itself and incline it, and rule and order natural love beneath itself; so that the world may love you with that fair and true love with which you loved the world, O Savior of the world, who live and reign, God, through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity of the Blessed Virgin Mary Sermon II", "topics": ["Nativity of Mary", "Mary", "charity", "beauty", "Christ", "spiritual motherhood"], "url": "sermons/guerric-mary-nativity-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo I, Qualia nos doceat Deus puer, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 29\u201332.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Isaiah 9:6", "Psalm 84:8 (source numbering)", "Matthew 18:4 (printed locator; see note)", "John 10:9", "Psalm 111:6 (printed locator; see note)", "Galatians 6:3", "Ecclesiasticus 3:20 (source numbering)", "James 4:6", "1 Peter 5:5"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric's first Nativity sermon contemplates the eternal Word as a vulnerable child. Four numbered paragraphs move from the renewal brought by Christ's birth to humility, confidence in mercy, and the gentleness that should shape Christian conduct. This reading uses the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition; the approximate 1138\u20131157 composition range is distinct from that publication date. The Nativity heading does not specify a Christmas Mass. Two editorial notes explain inaccurate Scripture locators printed in the edition without changing the sermon."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DE NATIVITATE DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE NATIVITY OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Qualia nos doceat Deus puer."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "What God as a child teaches us.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Puer natus est nobis (Isai. ix, 6). Puer antiquus dierum. Puer forma corporis et aetate; antiquus dierum, incomprehensibili Verbi aeternitate. Quanquam et in ipsa sua dierum antiquitate et si puer non sit, novus tamen semper sit; imo non tam novus sit, quam ipsa novitas in se semper manens et innovans omnia, a quo quaeque res prout recedit, inveteratur; prout reaccedit, renovatur. Et novo quodam modo unde antiquus, inde novus: quoniam quidem aeternitas ipsius sine initio nascendi, sine defectu veterascendi, ipsa est et antiqua novitas, et nova antiquitas ipsius. Alia tamen est novitas hujus temporalis nativitatis, quia puer natus est nobis innovandis, qui ab aeterno Deus natus est angelis beatificandis. Illa quidem nativitas amplioris gloriae, sed ista profusioris misericordiae. Ista siquidem propter me, qui misericordia indigebam, quoniam miseria circumdatus eram, et miseria quam expiare non poteram. Ostende nobis misericordiam tuam (Psal. lxxxiv, 8), qui nondum idonei sumus videre gloriam tuam: appareat benignitas et humanitas Salvatoris nostri Dei, ut per hanc digni efficiamur et idonei, quibus appareat majestas et divinitas Creatoris nostri Dei. Ostende nobis, Domine, misericordiam tuam, nostra indutam miseria, et novo miserendi genere de ipsa miseria miserorum operantem remedia. Ad hoc siquidem ars misericordiae beatitudinem Dei, et miseriam hominis in unitate Mediatoris temperavit; ut operante sacramento unitatis, per virtutem resurrectionis beatitudo miseriam absorbeat, mortemque vita deglutiat; et totus homo glorificatus in divinae consortium naturae transeat. Usque adeo autem universitas [sic] infirmitates carnis, quibus ob culpam humana subjacet natura, absque culpa videlicet, dignatio suscepit divina; ut nec infantiae recusaret injurias, nec alia, quam communis habet conditio, sortiri vellet exordia: excepto sane, quod per Spiritum sanctum immaculatus nascens de immaculata, vitia nostrae originis emundabat, atque secundae nativitatis nobis sacramenta dedicabat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. A child is born to us (Isaiah ix, 6). A child, the Ancient of Days. A child in bodily form and age; the Ancient of Days in the incomprehensible eternity of the Word. Yet even in that ancientness of his days, although he is not a child, he is always new; indeed, he is not so much new as newness itself, always abiding in itself and making all things new. Every thing grows old as it withdraws from him, and is renewed as it draws near again. And in a certain new way, the very reason he is ancient is the reason he is new: for his eternity, without a beginning through birth or a decline through growing old, is itself both his ancient newness and his new ancientness. Yet the newness of this birth in time is different: he who from eternity was born as God to give the angels blessedness has been born as a child to make us new. That birth indeed has greater glory, but this one has more abundant mercy. This birth is for me, who needed mercy because I was surrounded by misery, and by misery which I could not expiate. Show us your mercy (Psalm lxxxiv, 8), for we are not yet fit to see your glory. Let the kindness and humanity of God our Saviour appear, so that through this we may become worthy and fit for the majesty and divinity of God our Creator to appear to us. Show us, Lord, your mercy, clothed in our misery and, by a new manner of showing mercy, working remedies from the very misery of the miserable. For this purpose the art of mercy has blended God's blessedness and man's misery in the unity of the Mediator, so that, as the sacrament of unity works, blessedness may through the power of the resurrection swallow up misery and life devour death, and the whole man, glorified, may pass into fellowship with the divine nature. Indeed, the divine condescension took upon itself, without sin, all the infirmities of the flesh to which human nature is subject because of sin, to such an extent that it did not refuse the hardships of infancy, nor did it wish to have a beginning different from that of our common condition; except, of course, that, born immaculate of the immaculate Virgin through the Holy Spirit, he cleansed the faults of our origin and consecrated for us the sacraments of a second birth.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Itaque parvulus natus est nobis (Isa. ix, 6), et Deus majestatis exinaniens semetipsum, non solum terreno mortalium corpori, sed etiam infirmae ac pusillae infantium se configuravit aetati. O beata infantia, cujus infirmum et stultum fortius et sapientius est omnibus hominibus; quia nimirum Dei virtus, et Dei sapientia sua in nostris, divina operatur in humanis! Hujus siquidem infantis infirmitas principem mundi triumphat, fortem armatum ligat, crudelem tyrannum captivat, et captivitatem nostram absolvit et liberat. Hujus infantis muta, ut videtur, et elinguis simplicitas linguas infantium facit disertas; linguis hominum loqui facit et angelorum, linguas dividens igneas: inscius iste, ut videtur, ipse est qui docet hominem scientiam et angelum, tanquam vere Deus scientiarum, Sapientia Dei, et Verbum. O sacra et dulcis infantia, quae veram humanis innocentiam refudisti, per quam omnis aetas in beatam redeat infantiam; tibique conformis fiat, non pusillitate membrorum, sed humilitate sensus, et pietate morum. Prorsus vos filii Adae, qui nimis grandes estis in oculis vestris, et in giganteam enormitatem per superbiam excrevistis; nisi conversi fueritis, et effecti sicut parvulus iste, non intrabitis in regnum coelorum (Matth. xviii, 4). Ego sum ostium (Joan. x, 9) regni, ait parvulus iste: nisi incurvetur altitudo virorum, non eos admittet humilitatis janua. Ideo nimirum conquassabit capita in terra multorum (Psal. cxi, 6); et qui elati accedunt, repulsi capite illiso cadent retrorsum. Quid enim? adhuc tu superbis, terra et cinis, postquam Deus factus est humilis? adhuc magnus es in oculis tuis, postquam Deus parvulus factus est sub oculis tuis? Ille semetipsum exinanivit ut pene videretur esse nihil, sine quo factum est nihil: et tu in immensum inflaris et extolleris, existimans te aliquid esse, cum vere sis nihil! ipse te seducis (Galat. vi, 3), clamat tibi Apostolus; cum, etsi aliquid esses, si magnum aliquid esses, tanto magis humiliari te oporteret. Quanto, inquit Sapiens, magnus es, humilia te in omnibus: et invenies gratiam coram Deo (Eccli. iii, 20), qui superbis resistit, humilibus autem dat gratiam (Jacobi iv, 6 et I Petr. v, 5); qui et ipse, ut tibi fieret exemplum, cum esset maximus omnium, factus est humillimus omnium et minimus. Parum erat ei, ut fieret minor angelis conditione mortalis naturae, nisi et hominibus minor fieret aetate imbecillis infantiae. Videat igitur pius et humilis, et glorietur, videat impius et superbus, et confundatur. Videat, inquam, Deum immensum, parvulum factum, et infantulum adorandum, stupendum mysterium, redemptionem piorum, gloriam humilium, judicium impiorum, ruinam superborum. O venerandum ac tremendum mysterium! Quam sanctum et terribile est nomen tuum, fons misericordiarum, et abyssus judiciorum! Quis de hoc fonte bibit, et non amavit? quis hanc abyssum consideravit, et non expavit? Qui non amavit, nequam et impius est: sicut qui non expavit, vecors et insensatus est. Nihil tamen est cur judicium expavescas, si non adversus misericordiam insolescas. Et ipse, siquidem mallet amari, quam serviliter timeri: magisque probat quod liber ac voluntarius offert affectus filiorum, quam quod coactitius extorquet metus servulorum. Ideo et nunc cum primo se manifestavit mortalibus, puerum se maluit exhibere, magisque amabilis quam terribilis apparere: ut, quia salvare veniebat, et non judicare; praeferret interim unde provocaret amorem, differret unde incutere posset timorem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Thus a little child is born to us (Isaiah ix, 6), and the God of majesty, emptying himself, conformed himself not only to the earthly body of mortals, but also to the weak and tiny age of infants. O blessed infancy, whose weakness and foolishness are stronger and wiser than all men, because God's power and God's wisdom work what is their own in what is ours, divine things in human things! For this infant's weakness triumphs over the prince of the world, binds the strong armed man, takes the cruel tyrant captive, and releases and frees our captivity. This infant's simplicity, seemingly mute and without speech, makes the tongues of infants eloquent; dividing fiery tongues, it makes them speak with the tongues of men and angels. This seemingly unknowing child is himself the one who teaches knowledge to man and angel, as truly the God of knowledge, the Wisdom of God, and the Word. O holy and sweet infancy, which has poured true innocence back into mankind, through which every age may return to blessed infancy and be conformed to you, not in smallness of limbs, but in humility of mind and holiness of conduct. Truly, you children of Adam, who are too great in your own eyes and have grown through pride to a monstrous gigantic size: unless you are converted and become like this little child, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven (Matthew xviii, 4). I am the door (John x, 9) of the kingdom, says this little child: unless the tall stature of men bends low, the gate of humility will not admit them. For this reason he will crush the heads of many upon the earth (Psalm cxi, 6); and those who approach with heads raised will be repulsed, their heads struck, and fall backwards. What then? Do you still boast, dust and ashes, after God has become humble? Are you still great in your own eyes, after God has become little before your eyes? He emptied himself so as almost to seem nothing, he without whom nothing was made; and you swell and exalt yourself beyond measure, thinking yourself something when you truly are nothing! You deceive yourself (Galatians vi, 3), the Apostle cries to you; whereas, even if you were something, even something great, you ought all the more to humble yourself. The greater you are, says the Wise Man, humble yourself in all things, and you will find grace before God (Ecclesiasticus iii, 20), who resists the proud but gives grace to the humble (James iv, 6 and I Peter v, 5). He himself, to become your example, though greatest of all, became humblest and least of all. It was not enough for him to become less than the angels through the condition of mortal nature, unless he also became less than men through the age of helpless infancy. Let the devout and humble see and rejoice; let the impious and proud see and be confounded. Let them see, I say, the immeasurable God made little, an infant to be adored: an astounding mystery, the redemption of the devout, the glory of the humble, the judgment of the impious, the downfall of the proud. O mystery worthy of reverence and trembling! How holy and terrible is your name, fountain of mercies and abyss of judgments! Who has drunk from this fountain and not loved? Who has contemplated this abyss and not trembled? Whoever has not loved is wicked and impious; just as whoever has not trembled is senseless and foolish. Yet there is no reason to dread judgment if you do not rise insolently against mercy. He himself would rather be loved than feared as a slave fears; and he approves more highly what the free and willing affection of children offers than what the forced terror of servants wrings out. Therefore, when he first manifested himself to mortals, he chose to show himself as a child and to appear lovable rather than terrible: since he was coming to save and not to judge, he would for the time display what would awaken love, and defer what could strike fear.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Cum fiducia igitur accedamus ad thronum gratiae ejus, qui nec cogitare sine tremore poteramus de throno gloriae ejus. Nihil hic terroris, nihil severitatis quam metuas; sed omnimoda benignitas atque mansuetudo, de qua praesumas. Et si potestas et terror apud ipsum est, totum tamen interim dissimulat, donec poenitenti parcat, confitentem recipiat. Ne sit tibi scrupulo, quod graviter deliquisti: nescit puer laesus irasci; aut, si irascitur, facile potest placari. Vere nihil placabilius animo hujus pueri, qui in hoc ipsum pacem tuam satisfactionemque praevenit, priorque legatos de pace mittit: ut qui reus es, velis reconciliari. Tantummodo velis, et vere perfecteque velis: non modo veniam indulgebit, sed et gratiam cumulabit: quinimo lucrum non mediocre deputans, ovem recuperasse perditam, diem festum aget cum angelis. Congruebat ergo cum divina mansuetudine aetas innocentis ac facilis infantiae, satisque convenienter et commode salutem peccatorum ab hac exorditus est aetate; ut quos reatus propriae terrebat conscientiae, spes consolaretur non difficilis indulgentiae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Let us therefore approach with confidence the throne of his grace, we who could not even think of the throne of his glory without trembling. Here there is no terror, no severity for you to fear, but every kind of goodness and gentleness in which to place your trust. Even if power and terror are with him, for the time he conceals it all, until he spares the penitent and receives the one who confesses. Do not let the fact that you have sinned grievously trouble you: a child who has been hurt does not know how to be angry; or, if he is angry, he is easily appeased. Truly nothing is more ready to be appeased than the heart of this child, who anticipates your peace-making and satisfaction for this very purpose, and sends envoys of peace first, so that you, the guilty one, may be willing to be reconciled. Only be willing, truly and fully willing: he will not merely grant pardon, but heap grace upon you; indeed, reckoning the recovery of the lost sheep no small gain, he will celebrate a feast with the angels. The innocent and yielding age of infancy therefore accorded with divine gentleness, and it was fitting and advantageous that he began the salvation of sinners at this age, so that those whom the guilt of their own conscience terrified might be comforted by the hope of pardon readily obtained.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. O puer dulcissime, Jesu bone, quam magna est multitudo dulcedinis tuae, quam abscondisti timentibus te, et perficies sperantibus in te, qui tantam exhibuisti necdum scientibus te! Prorsus dulcedo incomparabilis, et pietas ineffabilis; ut Deum qui creavit me, videam puerum creatum propter me: Deusque majestatis et gloriae non solum similis mihi fiat corporis veritate, sed etiam miserabilis, et velut humanae indigens opis appareat aetatis infirmitate. Vere tu puer Deus, salutare vultus mei, et Deus meus, cum totus sis dulcedo, et desiderium, dulciorem tamen te mihi facit teneritudo membrorum. Haec nimirum te capabilem facit sensibus et affectibus parvulorum, qui nondum sunt te solidum idonei capere cibum. Dulce interim, dulce prorsus et sapidum, cogitare puerum Deum: quin etiam efficax et operatorium ad medendum, ac dulcorandum, si quis in nobis est, rancorem animorum, sermonum amaritudinem, morum austeritatem. Neque enim crediderim, ubi sensus et memoria fuerit hujus divinae dulcedinis, locum irae vel tristitiae inveniri: sed omnis indignatio, et amaritudo cum omni malitia tolletur a nobis. Ita fiet ut quasi modo geniti infantes, modo genitum infantem Dominum digne laudemus, et in consonantia morum et vocum, ex ore infantium et lactentium perficiatur laus infantis, et lactentis Domini Jesu Christi, cui cum Patre et Spiritu sancto laus et jubilatio, per aeterna saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. O sweetest child, good Jesus, how great is the abundance of your sweetness, which you have hidden for those who fear you and will bring to fulfilment for those who hope in you, you who have shown so much to those who do not yet know you! Truly, incomparable sweetness and ineffable loving-kindness: that I should see God who created me as a child created for me; and that the God of majesty and glory should not only become like me in the reality of his body, but even appear pitiable and as if needing human assistance through the weakness of his age. Truly, you, the child who is God, the salvation of my countenance and my God, although you are wholly sweetness and desire, the tenderness of your limbs makes you sweeter still to me. For this makes you comprehensible to the senses and affections of little children, who are not yet fit to receive you as solid food. Meanwhile it is sweet, altogether sweet and savoury, to contemplate God as a child; indeed it is effective and powerful to heal and sweeten whatever rancour of spirit, bitterness of speech, or harshness of conduct is in us. For I could not believe that where there is a sense and remembrance of this divine sweetness, room could be found for anger or sadness; rather, all indignation and bitterness, with all malice, will be taken away from us. Thus, like newborn infants, we shall worthily praise the infant Lord newly born, and in the harmony of our conduct and our voices, from the mouths of infants and nursing children shall be perfected the praise of the infant and nursing Lord Jesus Christ, to whom with the Father and the Holy Spirit be praise and jubilation, through the eternal ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-n001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Matth. xviii, 4. The sentence beginning nisi conversi fueritis corresponds to Matthew 18:3; verse 4 continues the teaching on becoming humble like a child. The historical locator remains unchanged in the sermon."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-01-n002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Psal. cxi, 6. The quotation conquassabit capita in terra multorum corresponds to Psalm 109:6 in Vulgate numbering (Psalm 110:6 in common modern numbering). The apparent reference error is retained and explained rather than silently corrected."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon I: What God as a Child Teaches Us", "topics": ["Nativity", "Incarnation", "humility", "mercy", "repentance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo II, Quale donum, contemnente synagoga, suscepit Ecclesia, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 32-34.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Isaiah 9:6", "John 1:11", "Isaiah 1:2", "Acts 13:46", "Isaiah 54:1", "Isaiah 60:5", "Isaiah 49:21", "Isaiah 54:2\u20133", "Psalm 2:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 33:11 (historical numbering)", "Romans 8:32", "Wisdom 7:11"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This second Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Taking Isaiah's \"a child is born to us, a son is given to us\", Guerric reads the gift as the barren Church's joy and the rejected Synagogue's loss: the dowry of the old covenant passes to the new Bride. He then counts what is given with the Son, and asks for right faith, a pure conscience and ready devotion to receive it. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quale donum, contemnente synagoga, suscepit Ecclesia."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "What Gift the Church Received When the Synagogue Spurned It", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Puer natus est nobis, et filius datus est nobis (Isai. IX, 6). Inde probatur quod natus est nobis, quia scilicet datus est nobis. Illis enim recte dicitur natus, quibus videtur datus. Et omnibus quidem gratia nativitatis hujus oblata est, sed non ab omnibus suscepta est. Non enim omnium est fides. In propria venit, et sui eum non receperunt (Joan. I, 11). Inutiliter autem natus esset nisi et datus esset: frustraque factus esset filius hominis, si non recepissent eum filii hominum, quibus daret potestatem filios Dei fieri. Vae igitur, Judaea incredula, ingrata et impia: ablatum est a te regnum Dei, et datum est genti facienti fructus ejus. Ejectus Jesus est de Synagoga, sed susceptus est ab Ecclesia. Ejectus est de contradictionibus Judaeorum, constitutus est in caput Gentium. Ecce populus, quem non cognovit, servivit illi, auditu auris obedivit ei; cum de vobis filiis alienis, filiis sceleratis et parricidis, sit pii patris illa querela terribilis: Filios enutrivi et exaltavi, ipsi autem spreverunt me (Isai. I, 2). Sed, qui spernit, nunquid non et ipse spernetur? Quia repulistis verbum Dei, inquiunt apostoli, et indignos vos judicastis vitae aeternae, ecce convertimur ad gentes (Act. XIII, 46). O felix gentilitas! ecce affertur tibi Jesus: curre obviis manibus, extende manus, expande sinus, devotionem tuam probet tam actus quam effectus; filium qui tibi offertur, suscipe fide, amplexare affectu; semperque inter ubera tua commoretur. Et ecce videor mihi audire vocem exsultationis et confessionis de cunctis partibus orbis; a finibus terrae laudes audio, gloriam justi. Est autem sonus ut multitudinis, ut sonus castrorum Dei, vox una laudantium et dicentium: Puer natus est nobis, et filius datus est nobis (Isa. IX, 6). Ipsa est, ni fallor, Ecclesia laetabunda, et laudans, toto orbe terrarum; pro eo quod sibi natum, sibi datum amplexatur filium, quem oblatum, sed ablatum merito videt perfidiae Judaeorum. Lauda, sterilis, quae non pariebas, decanta laudem et jubila, quae non parturiebas: quia per hunc filium qui datus est tibi, multi erunt filii desertae, magis quam ejus, quae vinculo legis Deo maritata (Isa. LIV, 1), ei videbatur adhaerere. Tunc mirabitur et dilatabitur cor tuum (Isa. LX, 5). Tunc dices in corde tuo: Quis genuit mihi istos? Ego sterilis et non pariens, transmigrata et captiva: et istos quis enutrivit? Ego destituta et sola, et isti ubi hic erant? (Isa. XLIX, 21.) O mater incorrupta, virgo fecunda, filius, qui datus est tibi, hos tibi dedit. Est enim Filius Altissimi, cui Pater istos adoptavit, ut sint omnes conformes imaginis ejus, et sit ipse primogenitus in multis fratribus. Dilata, inquit, itaque locum tentorii tui ab ortu ad occasum, et a mari ad mare, quia ad dexteram et ad laevam penetrabis, et semen tuum gentes haereditabit (Isa. LIV, 2, 3). Iste namque unigenitus Mariae, primogenitus est omni creaturae, cui dicitur a Patre: Postula a me, et dabo tibi gentes haereditatem tuam, et possessionem tuam terminos terrae (Psal. II, 8)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. A child is born to us, and a son is given to us (Isai. IX, 6). From this it is proved that he was born to us, namely because he was given to us. For he is rightly said to be born to those to whom he appears to be given. And indeed the grace of this birth was offered to all, but it was not received by all. For not all have faith. He came into his own, and his own did not receive him (Joan. I, 11). But he would have been born in vain unless he had also been given; and the Son of man would have been made in vain, if the sons of men had not received him, to whom he might give the power to become sons of God. Woe, then, unbelieving Judea, ungrateful and impious: the kingdom of God has been taken away from you, and given to a nation that produces its fruits. Jesus was cast out of the Synagogue, but received by the Church. He was cast out amid the contradictions of the Jews; he was set up as the head of the Gentiles. Behold, a people whom he did not know served him; at the hearing of the ear they obeyed him; while concerning you, strange children, wicked and parricidal children, there stands that terrible complaint of the loving Father: 'I have nourished and raised up children, but they have despised me' (Isai. I, 2). But he who despises\u2014shall he not also himself be despised? Because you have rejected the word of God, say the apostles, and have judged yourselves unworthy of eternal life, behold, we turn to the Gentiles (Act. XIII, 46). O happy Gentile people! Behold, Jesus is brought to you: run to meet him with open hands, stretch forth your hands, open wide your bosom; let both deed and effect prove your devotion; the son who is offered to you, receive with faith, embrace with affection; and let him abide always between your breasts. And behold, I seem to hear a voice of exultation and confession from all parts of the world; from the ends of the earth I hear praises, the glory of the just one. And the sound is as of a multitude, as the sound of the camps of God, one voice of those praising and saying: 'A child is born to us, and a son is given to us' (Isa. IX, 6). This is, unless I am mistaken, the Church, rejoicing and praising throughout the whole world; because she embraces as born to her, given to her, the son whom she sees offered to the faithlessness of the Jews, but deservedly taken away from it. Praise, O barren one who did not bear; sing out praise and shout for joy, you who did not travail: because through this son who is given to you, there will be more children of the deserted one than of her who, bound by the law in marriage to God (Isa. LIV, 1), seemed to cling to him. Then your heart shall marvel and be enlarged (Isa. LX, 5). Then you will say in your heart: Who has borne these for me? I was barren and not bearing, exiled and captive: and who has reared these? I was destitute and alone, and where were these? (Isa. XLIX, 21.) O undefiled mother, fruitful virgin, the son who was given to you has given these to you. For he is the Son of the Most High, for whom the Father has adopted these, that all might be conformed to his image, and that he himself might be the firstborn among many brethren. Enlarge, he says, therefore, the place of your tent from the rising to the setting, and from sea to sea, for you shall spread abroad to the right hand and to the left, and your seed shall possess the nations as an inheritance (Isa. LIV, 2, 3). For this only-begotten of Mary is the firstborn of all creation, to whom it is said by the Father: Ask of me, and I will give you the nations for your inheritance, and the ends of the earth for your possession (Psal. II, 8).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sicut autem, suscepto Dei Filio, Ecclesia sterilis peperit plurimos, et roborata est: ita ipso ejecto, aemula ejus Synagoga, quae multos filios habebat, infirmata est. Ideo enim hodie Ecclesia gratulabunda de dato sibi filio gratiarum actione et voce laudis replet coelos: illa muta sedet in tenebris, aut certe ejulatu fatigat inferos. Misera et caeca, cur non advertit quam manifeste Deus ejus ad nos transierit; quam evidenter ipsa repudiata, solumque retinente libellum repudii, litteram dico legis, quae adulteria ipsius, coarguit, universam dotem ipsius ad novam Sponsam nuptam transtulerit? Ecce in manibus nostris tabulae matrimonii, in duobus Testamentis; ecce sacramenta unctionum, dignitas regum, ordo sacerdotum, ministeria levitarum, cultus templi et vasorum, sacrificiorum veritas, omnis denique ipsorum gloria, translata ab ipsis, collata est nobis, quia causa et veritas omnium, Filius datus est nobis. Ipse calicem legis, quae in manu est Mediatoris, ex hoc Judaeo in hunc Christianum inclinavit. Et omne quod in ea merum ac verum fuit, eliquatum nobis transfudit: faex sola, quae non est exinanita, apud Judaeos remansit; ipsam bibunt, ipsa pars calicis eorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But just as, having received the Son of God, the barren Church has borne very many children and has been strengthened, so, with him cast out, her rival the Synagogue, who had many children, has been weakened. For this reason, today the rejoicing Church fills the heavens with thanksgiving and the voice of praise for the son given to her; that other one sits mute in darkness, or indeed wearies the depths below with wailing. Wretched and blind one, why does she not notice how plainly her God has passed over to us; how evidently, having repudiated her and left her only the bill of divorce\u2014I mean the letter of the law, which convicts her of her adulteries\u2014he has transferred her whole dowry to the new Bride, now wedded? Behold, in our hands are the marriage-tablets, in the two Testaments; behold the sacraments of anointings, the dignity of kings, the order of priests, the ministries of Levites, the service of the temple and its vessels, the truth of the sacrifices, and in short, all their glory, taken away from them, has been bestowed upon us, because the cause and the truth of all these things, the Son, has been given to us. He himself has tilted the cup of the law\u2014the law which is in the hand of the Mediator\u2014away from this Jew and toward this Christian. And all that was unmixed and true in the law, he has strained out and poured over to us: only the dregs, which were not emptied, remained among the Jews; that is what they drink, that is their portion of the cup.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Sed quid verbis moror? Numquid enim potest enarrari, quae et quanta sunt data nobis, cum iste Filius datus est nobis? Nonne omne datum optimum, et omne donum perfectum ab ipso, et cum ipso, pariter ad nos descendit? nonne universas opes coeli, omnes thesauros Dei, secum nobis advexit? qui licet adhuc in ipso contineantur absconditi, multa tamen jam et magna, et quasi donativum pugnaturis erogavit; sed nihil omnia ad ea quae victoribus reservavit. Quis enumeret tot munera charismatum, tot genera virtutum, nostrorum scilicet varietatem armorum, subsidia Sacramentorum, fercula Scripturarum, gradus et ordines ministrorum; in omni aetate, sexu, et conditione, natione et lingua, tot martyrum trophaea, confessorum insignia, virginum coronas? si miraris, inquit Ecclesia, unde repente mihi pauperculae haec multitudo divitiarum, et magnitudo gloriae: inde nimirum, quia Filius Dei datus est nobis, Deus gratiae, Dominus virtutum, et Rex gloriae. Imo magis mirum esset, si non sequerentur Dominum bona sua, cum etiam ipse sit omnia bona. Inquirentes, inquit, Dominum, non minuentur omni bono (Psal. XXXIII, 11); quanto magis suscipientes? Si enim miraris ista nobis per Filium data, ego (ut plus mireris) adjiciam omnia omnino nobis cum illo esse donata. Cum enim Filium, per quem et in quo omnia subsistunt, Pater nobis donavit, quomodo non omnia nobis cum illo donavit? (Rom. VIII, 32.) Omnia nostra sunt, ait Apostolus, quia noster est auctor omnium Dominus; ut, etsi videamur tanquam nihil habentes, inveniamur tamen omnia possidentes. Vides igitur quantum, et quale datum sit Filius; quamque probabile sit quod de Sapientia, quae ipse est, legitur: Venerunt mihi omnia bona cum illa, et innumerabilis honestas per manus illius (Sap. VII, 11). Quare autem hoc ita inculco, fratres mei? Quare, nisi ut discatis gloriari, quia filius datus est nobis; et quidquid mundus potest dare, aut promittere vobis, non modo vile et indignum ad illum, sed etiam detrimentum propter illum deputetis? Non quomodo mundus dat, dat ille vobis: tantum grati estote, quia datus est vobis, et satagite ut amplius et perfectius detur vobis. Qui enim semel datus est mundo in forma carnis, et certis diebus aut horis datur fidelibus in specie panis, scilicet in esu Sacramenti sui, saepius et incertis horis datur devotis, in gustu spiritus sui. Primum, ad redemptionem; secundum, ad sanctificationem; tertium etiam ad consolationem. Primum exigit ut fides sit recta; secundum, ut conscientia pura; tertium, ut devotio prompta. Haec mentem elevat, ut gratiae occurrat; cor aperit, ut excipiat; affectum dilatat, ut plurimum ipsius capiat. Non solum autem istam, sed et omnem gratiam abundare faciat in vobis, Deus omnis gratiae, Filius, qui datus est vobis, qui est Deus benedictus in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But why do I linger over words? For can it be told, how many and how great are the things given to us, when this Son was given to us? Does not every best gift and every perfect gift come down to us from him, and with him equally? Did he not bring with him to us all the riches of heaven, all the treasures of God? For although these still remain hidden, contained within him, yet already he has paid out many and great things, as it were a donative to those about to fight; but all these are nothing compared to what he has reserved for the victors. Who could count so many gifts of graces, so many kinds of virtues\u2014that is, the variety of our weapons, the aids of the Sacraments, the courses of the Scriptures, the ranks and orders of ministers; in every age, sex, and condition, nation and tongue, so many trophies of martyrs, insignia of confessors, crowns of virgins? If you wonder, says the Church, whence suddenly came to me, poor little one, this multitude of riches and this greatness of glory: it is, of course, because the Son of God was given to us, the God of grace, the Lord of powers, and the King of glory. Indeed it would be far more wonderful if his own goods did not follow the Lord, since he himself is all good things. Those who seek the Lord, it says, shall not be diminished in any good thing (Psal. XXXIII, 11); how much more those who receive him? For if you wonder at these things given to us through the Son, I will add (that you may wonder the more) that absolutely all things have been given to us together with him. For when the Father gave us the Son, through whom and in whom all things subsist, how did he not give us all things together with him? (Rom. VIII, 32.) All things are ours, says the Apostle, because the Lord, the author of all things, is ours; so that, although we seem as having nothing, yet we are found possessing all things. You see, then, how great and of what kind a gift the Son is; and how fitting it is that what is read of Wisdom, which he himself is, applies here: All good things came to me together with her, and innumerable riches through her hands (Sap. VII, 11). Why, then, do I press this point so insistently, my brothers? Why, except that you may learn to glory in this, that the Son has been given to us; and that whatever the world can give or promise you, you should reckon not only as vile and unworthy compared to him, but even as a loss on account of him. He does not give to you as the world gives: only be grateful, because he has been given to you, and strive that he may be given to you more fully and more perfectly. For he who was given once to the world in the form of flesh, and is given to the faithful on fixed days or hours under the appearance of bread\u2014that is, in the eating of his Sacrament\u2014is given more often, and at uncertain hours, to the devout, in the tasting of his Spirit. The first was for redemption; the second, for sanctification; the third, also for consolation. The first requires right faith; the second, a pure conscience; the third, ready devotion. This last lifts up the mind, that it may go out to meet grace; opens the heart, that it may receive it; enlarges the affection, that it may grasp as much of it as possible. May God of all grace, the Son who has been given to you, who is God blessed forever and ever, cause not only this grace but every grace to abound in you. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon II: The Gift the Church Received When the Synagogue Spurned It", "topics": ["Nativity", "Incarnation", "Church and Synagogue", "faith", "grace"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo III, De admirabili Dei cum homine commercio, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 35-38.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Isaiah 9:6", "Hebrews 2:16", "John 1:12", "Romans 5:2", "Psalm 84:11 (historical numbering)", "Song of Songs 1:2", "Philippians 2:7", "Isaiah 25:9", "Jeremiah 23:24", "Baruch 3:36", "Psalm 47:15 (historical numbering)", "Judges 6:39\u201340", "Jonah 3\u20134", "Isaiah 40:17", "Isaiah 45:14\u201315", "Isaiah 53:4\u20135", "Psalm 97:1\u20133 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 40:6", "Psalm 97:2 (historical numbering)", "3 Kings 3:26\u201327 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)", "Matthew 11:50 (printed locator; the words are Matthew 12:50)", "Luke 1:38", "3 Kings 3:26 (Vulgate numbering; 1 Kings)"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This third Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. The child is born for us, not for himself or the angels, and his birth annuls the ancient condemnation of ours. Guerric dwells on God poured out in the manger, the scandal of hidden strength, and Solomon's judgment between the two mothers. Every soul that does the Lord's will is called to be a mother of Christ in others. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De admirabili Dei cum homine commercio."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Wonderful Exchange Between God and Man", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Puer natus nobis (Isa. IX, 6). Nobis prorsus; non enim sibi, non angelis. Sibi, inquam, natus non est, ut scilicet hac nativitate esse, aut melius esse inciperet, qui antequam temporaliter nasceretur, aeternaliter erat, et perfecta beatitudo sibi erat, quia de Deo perfecto perfectus Deus natus erat. Angelis natus non est; quia nec angelus ille qui in veritate stabat, reparatione indigebat; nec illius qui ceciderat, casus reparabilis erat. Ideo nusquam angelos apprehendit, sed semen apprehendit Abrahae (Hebr. II, 16); et qui Deus natus erat sibi, puer natus est nobis: se quodammodo relinquens, et angelos transiliens, ut ad nos usque deveniens unus fieret e nobis; et exinanitus a semetipso, minoratur ab angelis, ut aequalis fieret nobis. Qui igitur natus aeternaliter sibi et angelis erat beatitudo, natus temporaliter nobis, factus est nobis redemptio; quia nos solos laborare videbat antiquo nostrae nativitatis praejudicio. Quam felix, quam amabilis nativitas tua, puer Jesu, quae nativitatem omnium emendat, conditionem reformat, praejudicium solvit, chirographum damnatae naturae rescindit: ut si quem piget damnabiliter esse natum, possit felicissime renasci! Quotquot autem receperunt eum, dedisti eis potestatem filios Dei fieri (Joan. I, 12). Gratias agimus gratuitae atque gratiosae nativitati tuae, Deus filii hominis, per quem accessum habemus in gratiam istam in qua stamus, et gloriamur in spe gloriae filiorum Dei (Rom. V, 2). Prorsus admirabile commercium, ut sumens carnem, largiaris deitatem! commercium, inquam, charitate, non cupiditate contractum; indulgentiae quidem tuae gloriosum, sed indigentiae meae omnino lucrosum. Vere tu puer misericors, quem sola misericordia puerum fecit: licet pariter misericordia et veritas in te obviaverunt sibi (Psal. LXXXIV, 11). Vere, inquam, tu misericors puer, natus es nobis, non tibi: lucra nostra, non augmenta tua nascens de nobis quaesisti, quia ad hoc solum nasci dignatus es, ut nos minoratione tui proveheres, humiliatione glorificares. Tu exinanitus nos replesti: tu enim omnem tuae plenitudinem divinitatis in hominem transfudisti. Transfudisti, sed non confudisti. Transfusum tamen Deum in hominem non dicerem, si ad mensuram datum huic homini spiritum audirem: si de omni sua plenitudine penes Deum aliquid recedisset, quod non in hominem, cui unitus est, effudisset."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"A child is born to us\" (Isa. IX, 6). To us indeed; for he was not born for himself, nor for the angels. He was not born, I say, for himself, as though by this birth he should begin to exist, or begin to exist better\u2014he who, before he was born in time, existed eternally, and was perfect blessedness to himself, because he who was born was perfect God from perfect God. Nor was he born for the angels; for neither did that angel who stood firm in truth need restoration, nor was the fall of the one who had fallen reparable. Therefore he nowhere took hold of angels, but took hold of the seed of Abraham (Hebr. II, 16); and he who was born God for himself was born a child for us: in a certain manner leaving himself behind, and passing over the angels, so that, coming all the way to us, he might become one of us; and having emptied himself of himself, he was made lower than the angels, that he might become equal to us. He, therefore, who born eternally was blessedness to himself and to the angels, born in time for us, was made redemption for us; because he saw that we alone were laboring under the ancient condemnation attached to our birth. How happy, how lovable is your birth, child Jesus, which amends the birth of all, reforms their condition, annuls the sentence, cancels the bond of condemned nature: so that if anyone is grieved to have been born under condemnation, he may be reborn most happily! \"But as many as received him, to them you gave power to become sons of God\" (Joan. I, 12). We give thanks to your free and gracious birth, God, Son of man, through whom we have access into this grace in which we stand, and we glory in the hope of the glory of the sons of God (Rom. V, 2). Truly a wonderful exchange, that taking on flesh you should bestow deity! An exchange, I say, contracted by charity, not by greed; glorious indeed for your generosity, but wholly profitable for my need. Truly you are a merciful child, whom mercy alone made a child: although in you mercy and truth have met together equally (Psal. LXXXIV, 11). Truly, I say, you, merciful child, were born for us, not for yourself: being born of us, you sought our gain, not any increase for yourself, because you deigned to be born for this alone, that by your diminishment you might advance us, by your humiliation you might glorify us. You, emptied, filled us: for you poured out the whole fullness of your divinity into man. You poured it out, but you did not confound it. Yet I would not say that God was poured into man, if I heard that the Spirit was given to this man by measure: as if something had remained with God from his whole fullness which he had not poured out into the man to whom he was united.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Merito igitur oleum effusum nomen (Cantic. I, 2) Dei hominis, vel certe unguentum exinanitum; cum ita funditus Dei in hominem effusus sit, ut eum semetipsum exinanisse (Phil. II, 7) confiteatur fides Apostoli. Quamquam sic utique se exinanivit, ut in se ipso nihil prorsus minuerit, aut mutaverit; nec alia esse potuit naturae immutabilis exinanitio, quam naturae nostrae, quae terra inanis et vacua est, assumptio. Magna prorsus exinanitio ista in oculis videntium, ut splendor gloriae et figura paternae substantiae, nonnisi formam praeferat servilem, et in ipsa non habens speciem neque decorem. Quasi enim parum exinanitus esset, si homo solum fieret, etiam humanae carnis gloriam ita funditus in se vacuavit, sapientiam infatuavit, infirmavit virtutem, minuit magnitudinem; ut et in nativitate minimum, et in passione se exhiberet novissimum virorum: unde nec reputaverunt eum. Vis autem videre Deum exinanitum a semetipso? vide jacentem in praesepio. Ecce Deus noster (Isa. XXV, 9), Isaias ait, qui de tam longinquo vidit, et cognovit praesepe Domini sui, imo Deum in praesepi. Ecce, inquit, Deus noster. Ubi, quaeso? In praesepio, inquit. Infantulum quidem ibi invenio. Hunccine tu dicis illum esse, qui dicit, Coelum et terram ego impleo (Jerem. XXIII, 24): imo cujus majestati angusta est omnis coeli latitudo? Pannis involutum video. Hunccine tu dicis illum esse, qui gloriam et decorem induit lucis inaccessibilis, amictus incircumscripto lumine sicut vestimento? Vagientem audio. Hiccine est qui in coelis tonat, sub cujus vocis tonitruo potestates angelicae submittunt alas suas? Sic est, inquit alius propheta satisfaciens pro Isaia, omnino sic est: Hic est Deus noster, sed exinanitus est, ut te repleat, et a se quodammodo deficere voluit, ut te reficiat. Conclamat omnis chorus prophetarum uno spiritu, eademque sententia, etsi aliqui voce diversa, sic est: Hic est Deus noster, et non aestimabitur alius praeter eum (Baruch. III, 36); neque enim est alius praeter eum. Sed scio, inquit David, scio, quia non credet signo huic, sed contradicet generatio prava et adultera Judaeorum; enarrate vos, apostoli, in progenie altera Gentium. Quid? Quoniam hic quem Judaeus jacentem in praesepi nec respicit, pendentem in cruce despicit; cum miro modo nascitur, non videt; cum mira operatur, invidet; cum dira patitur, irridet: Hic, inquam, hic est Deus noster in aeternum et in saeculum saeculi, ipse reget nos in saecula (Psal. XLVII, 15)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Rightly, then, is the name of God-made-man called \"oil poured out\" (Cantic. I, 2), or indeed \"ointment emptied out\"; since God is so utterly poured out into man that the faith of the Apostle confesses that he \"emptied himself\" (Phil. II, 7). Although he indeed so emptied himself that he diminished or changed nothing at all in himself; nor could the emptying of an unchangeable nature be anything other than the assumption of our nature, which is earth empty and void. Truly this is a great emptying in the eyes of those who behold it, that the splendor of glory and the figure of the Father's substance should show forth nothing but the form of a servant, and in it have neither comeliness nor beauty. For as though it were too little an emptying to become man only, he so utterly emptied in himself even the glory of human flesh, made foolish wisdom, weakened power, diminished greatness; that both in his birth he showed himself the least, and in his passion the last of men: wherefore they did not even esteem him. Do you wish to see God emptied of himself? See him lying in the manger. \"Behold our God\" (Isa. XXV, 9), says Isaiah, who from so far off saw and recognized the manger of his Lord, indeed God in the manger. \"Behold,\" he says, \"our God.\" Where, I ask? \"In the manger,\" he says. I find there indeed a little infant. Do you say that this is he who says, \"I fill heaven and earth\" (Jerem. XXIII, 24); indeed, for whose majesty the whole breadth of heaven is too narrow? I see him wrapped in swaddling cloths. Do you say that this is he who is clothed with glory and beauty of inaccessible light, wrapped in unbounded light as with a garment? I hear him crying. Is this he who thunders in the heavens, under the thunder of whose voice the angelic powers lower their wings? \"So it is,\" says another prophet, answering for Isaiah, \"entirely so it is: This is our God, but he has emptied himself, that he might fill you, and he willed in a certain manner to fail from himself, that he might restore you.\" The whole choir of the prophets cries out together with one spirit and the same judgment, though with different voices: \"So it is: This is our God, and none other shall be accounted beside him\" (Baruch. III, 36); for indeed there is no other besides him. \"But I know,\" says David, \"I know that this perverse and adulterous generation of the Jews will not believe this sign, but will contradict it; you, apostles, declare it among another people, the Gentiles.\" What? That he whom the Jew does not even look upon lying in the manger, and despises hanging on the cross; when he is born in a wondrous manner, the Jew does not see it; when he performs wondrous works, he envies; when he suffers dreadful things, he mocks: This, I say, this is our God forever and world without end; he himself shall rule us forever (Psal. XLVII, 15).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Et ecce jam praedicantibus apostolis, testificantibus prophetis, etsi vellus siccatum est, sed areae latitudo infusa est (Judic. VI, 39, 40): etsi cucurbita aruit, Ninive salvata est (Jonae III et IV); et amissio Judaeorum, divitiae facta est mundi, et salus gentium. Adorant omnes reges terrae parvulum qui natus est nobis, omnes gentes serviunt ei: quia si qui sunt qui non serviant, vel non sint servituri, quasi non sint, sic sunt coram eo (Isa. XL, 17); gens etiam et regnum, quod non servierit ei, peribit. Jam tamen videmus et gaudemus impletum, quod per Isaiam fuerat a Patre promissum: Viri sublimes ad te transibunt, tui erunt, post te ambulabunt, te adorabunt, teque deprecabuntur. Et merito, inquit, quia in te tantum est Deus, et non est absque te Deus. Vere tu es Deus absconditus, Deus Israel Salvator (Isa. XLV, 14, 15). Si unquam manifestius, si expressius potuit dici Jesus Deus, nunquam credat Judaeus: aut certe si derogat haec esse dicta de Jesu, ostendat alium in quo sit Deus, et qui sit Deus, nec praeter eum Deus; quando quidem nec personarum Trinitatem fatetur, ut assignare possit Deum, in quo sit Deus, ita ut alius Deus praeter eum non sit. Sed te, perfide, scandalizat, quod ad pietatem aedificare debuerat; quod scilicet Deus absconditus, et homo oculis adhibitus est, tanquam lutum ex sputo, quod ad videndum Deum caecum illuminaret. Hoc, inquam, scandalo tibi est, quod in infirmitate carnis abscondita est fortitudo Dei; et in infirmitate crucis abscondita est fortitudo hominis Dei; quod inglorius inter viros aspectus ejus, et forma ejus inter filios hominum: unde nec reputasti eum, sed putasti quasi leprosum, et percussum a Deo, et humiliatum; cum Deus posuerit in eo iniquitates omnium nostrum (Isa. LIII, 4, 5). Nam et tuam quoque suscepisset, si tu eam vel ad extremum deponere voluisses. At tu, miser, non deponis, sed apponis iniquitatem super iniquitatem; super sanguinem prophetarum, sanguinem Filii Dei et Apostolorum. Impone igitur, quandoquidem ita vis, impone jugum iniquitatum tuarum, tibi et filiis tuis: mihi enim regnum Christi portare magis decet, ut meas ipse portet iniquitates. Tu porta colligationes impietatis, fasciculos deprimentes: ego portem fasciculum myrrhae, quem Maria mihi colligavit, pannisque involutum in praesepi reposuit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. And behold, now that the apostles are preaching and the prophets bearing witness, even though the fleece was dried up, yet the breadth of the threshing floor was soaked (Judic. VI, 39, 40); even though the gourd withered, Nineveh was saved (Jonae III et IV); and the loss of the Jews has become the riches of the world, and the salvation of the Gentiles. All the kings of the earth adore the little child who is born for us, all nations serve him; for if there are any who do not serve him, or who will not serve him, they are before him as though they were not (Isa. XL, 17); indeed, the nation and the kingdom that does not serve him shall perish. Now, however, we see and rejoice that what was promised by the Father through Isaiah has been fulfilled: 'Lofty men shall pass over to you, they shall be yours, they shall walk after you, they shall worship you, and shall make supplication to you.' And rightly so, he says, because God is in you alone, and there is no God apart from you. Truly you are the hidden God, the God of Israel, the Savior (Isa. XLV, 14, 15). If Jesus could ever have been called God more plainly, more expressly \u2014 let the Jew never believe it. Or indeed, if he denies that these things were said of Jesus, let him show another in whom God dwells, and who is God, and besides whom there is no other God \u2014 since he does not even confess the Trinity of Persons, so that he might be able to point out some God in whom God is, such that there is no other God besides him. But you, faithless one, are scandalized by what ought to have built up your piety: namely, that God was hidden, and the man was applied to the eyes like the clay made from spittle, to enlighten the blind so that he might see God. This, I say, is a scandal to you: that in the weakness of the flesh the strength of God was hidden; and in the weakness of the cross was hidden the strength of the God-man; that his appearance was without glory among men, and his form among the sons of men \u2014 hence you did not esteem him, but thought him as it were a leper, struck by God, and humbled; whereas God had placed upon him the iniquities of us all (Isa. LIII, 4, 5). For he would have taken up your iniquity too, if you had only been willing at last to lay it down. But you, wretched man, do not lay it down, but heap iniquity upon iniquity \u2014 upon the blood of the prophets, the blood of the Son of God, and of the Apostles. Impose, then, since you so wish, impose the yoke of your iniquities upon yourself and upon your children: for it is more fitting for me to bear the kingdom of Christ, so that he himself may bear my iniquities. You carry the bundles of impiety bound together, weights that press down; I will carry the bundle of myrrh, which Mary bound up for me, and wrapped in swaddling clothes and laid in the manger.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Vos igitur, fratres mei, qui cognovistis praesepe Domini vestri, Dominumque in praesepi, quem Israel non cognovit: vos, inquam, quibus Salvator ideo non viluit, quia misericors esse voluit; sed eo vobis tenerior in affectu, quo minor est in aspectu: cantate, et exsultate et psallite, Puer natus est nobis, filius datus est nobis. De Judaeis quidem est natus, sed nobis est natus; quia illis ablatus, nobis est datus. Cantate Domino canticum novum, quia mirabilia fecit. Notum fecit Dominus salutare suum (Psal. XCVII, 1-3): adeo ut et asinus de Gentibus cognoscat in praesepi Dominum suum, fenum sibi factum, quia omnis caro fenum (Isa. XL, 6). In conspectu namque Gentium revelavit justitiam suam (Psal. XCVII, 2): quam Judaeus ignorat, velum adhuc habens super faciem suam. Velum habet, quia zelum tenet: unde nec velum videt, sed invidet quod puer natus est nobis, filius datus est nobis. Nec invidet, quod cupiat habere sibi, sed quod cupiat perire et mihi et sibi. Mallet aemula et mala meretrix infantem necari, quam vivum mihi dari. Sed judicium nostri Salomonis, cujus sermo penetrabilior omni gladio ancipiti, scrutans corda et renes in invenienda matre non erravit: Date, inquit, Ecclesiae infantem vivum: haec est enim mater ejus (III Reg. III, 26, 27). Quicunque enim fecerit voluntatem ejus, ipse mater, et frater, et soror ejus est (Matth. XI, 50). O Domine Salomon, tu matrem dicis, ego ancillam profiteor. Ancilla Christi sum: fiat mihi secundum verbum tuum (Luc. I, 38). Et quidem matrem amore et sollicitudine me praestabo, quantum potero; sed conditionis meae semper memor ero."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. You therefore, my brothers, who have known the manger of your Lord, and the Lord in the manger, whom Israel did not know: you, I say, to whom the Savior has not thereby become worthless, because he wished to be merciful; but is all the dearer to your affection, the less he is in outward appearance \u2014 sing, and exult, and make music: 'A child is born to us, a son is given to us.' He was indeed born of the Jews, but he was born for us; because, taken away from them, he was given to us. Sing to the Lord a new song, for he has done wonderful things. The Lord has made known his salvation (Psal. XCVII, 1-3): so much so that even the ass from among the Gentiles recognizes in the manger his Lord, who has been made hay for him, since all flesh is hay (Isa. XL, 6). For in the sight of the Gentiles he has revealed his righteousness (Psal. XCVII, 2), which the Jew does not know, still having a veil over his face. He has a veil, because he clings to jealousy; hence he does not even see the veil, but envies that a child is born to us, a son is given to us. Nor does he envy in the sense of wishing to have him for himself, but in that he wishes him to perish both for me and for himself. The rival and wicked harlot would rather the infant be killed than be given alive to me. But the judgment of our Solomon, whose word is sharper than any two-edged sword, searching hearts and inmost parts, did not err in finding the mother: 'Give,' he said, 'the living child to the Church, for she is his mother' (III Reg. III, 26, 27). For whoever does his will, he is his brother, and sister, and mother (Matth. XI, 50). O Lord Solomon, you call me mother; I profess myself a handmaid. I am the handmaid of Christ: be it done to me according to your word (Luc. I, 38). And indeed I will show myself a mother in love and care, as far as I am able; but I will always remain mindful of my condition.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. O fratres, hoc nomen matris non est singulare praelatis, quamvis specialis eis incumbat cura maternae sollicitudinis et pietatis: commune est et vobis, qui facitis voluntatem Domini. Utique et vos matres estis pueri qui datus est in vobis, et in vobis, ex quo videlicet a timore Domini concepistis, et parturistis spiritum salutis. Vigila igitur, o mater sancta, vigila super curam recens nati, donec formetur in te Christus, qui natus est tibi: quia quo tenerior est, eo facilius potest perire tibi, qui nunquam perit sibi. Spiritus siquidem qui in te est, si tibi exstinctus fuerit, redit ad Deum qui dedit illum. Vigila, inquam, super curam recens nati, memor quoniam aemula tua dormiens oppressit filium, qui datus erat ei. Quae est enim illa, nisi carnalis anima, quae spiritum exstinguit negligentia et inertia. Hujusmodi homines, cum religionis amiserint fervorem, ipsius tamen sibi arrogant gloriam et nomen. Inde contentiones carnalium adversus spirituales, etiam in capitulis, ubi verus Salomon invisibiliter judex praesidet. Filius meus, inquiunt carnales, vivit, et tuus mortuus est: ego spiritum Dei habeo, tu non habes; in me vivit amor Dei, in te mortuus est: dum scilicet aemulantur sibi religionis auctoritatem, cujus spirituales habent veritatem; ut subducta eis auctoritate, consuetudines inducant pro sui sensus libidine. Et revera quidem mater vult aemulae dari vivum et integrum infantem, non invidens ei gloriam, dummodo habeat et virtutem, sed illa, Nec mihi, inquit, nec tibi sit, sed dividatur (III Reg. III, 26); quia nimirum sanctitatis sibi cupit retinere honorem, et aliis relinquere laborem. Sed non errat qui judicat, etsi aliquandiu dissimulat. Gladius Salomonis matrem invenit, cui adjudicat indivisum, sicut affectum charitatis, sic effectum potestatis; sicut fervorem operandi, sic favorem imperandi. Vos itaque, fratres, in quibus fides per dilectionem operans nata est de Spiritu sancto, custodite eam, pascite, nutrite, tanquam parvulum Jesum, donec formetur in vobis puer qui natus est nobis; qui non solum nascendo, sed vivendo et moriendo, formam cui conformemur tradidit nobis: memor quippe semper, quod non nisi nobis natus fuit, nec nisi nobis vivere voluit, nec mori nisi pro nobis, qui pro se non habuit; ut et nos per ipsum renasceremur, secundum ipsum viveremus; in ipso moreremur, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. O brothers, this name of mother is not reserved to prelates alone, although a special care of maternal solicitude and devotion falls upon them: it is common also to you, who do the will of the Lord. Indeed you too are mothers of the child who is given in you, and in you, from the moment, that is, when you conceived from the fear of the Lord and brought forth the spirit of salvation. Watch, therefore, O holy mother, watch over the care of the newly born, until Christ, who was born for you, be formed in you: for the more tender he is, the more easily he can perish for you, though he never perishes for himself. For the Spirit who is in you, if he be extinguished in you, returns to God who gave him. Watch, I say, over the care of the newly born, remembering that your rival, while sleeping, overlay the son who had been given to her. For who is that rival but the carnal soul, which extinguishes the spirit through negligence and sloth? Such men, when they have lost the fervor of religion, nonetheless claim for themselves its glory and name. Hence arise contentions of carnal men against spiritual men, even in chapters, where the true Solomon invisibly presides as judge. 'My son,' say the carnal, 'lives, and yours is dead: I have the Spirit of God, you do not; in me the love of God lives, in you it is dead'\u2014while, that is, they jealously claim for themselves the authority of religion, of which the spiritual possess the truth; so that, authority having been wrested from them, they may introduce customs according to the lust of their own judgment. And indeed the true mother wishes the living and whole infant to be given to her rival, not envying her the glory, provided she also have the virtue; but that other says, 'Let it be neither mine nor yours, but let it be divided' (III Reg. III, 26); because, of course, she desires to keep for herself the honor of holiness, and leave to others the labor. But he who judges does not err, even though for a while he dissembles. The sword of Solomon finds out the mother, to whom he awards the child undivided, both the affection of charity and the effect of power; both the fervor of working and the favor of commanding. You therefore, brothers, in whom faith working through love has been born of the Holy Spirit, guard it, feed it, nourish it, as the little Jesus, until the child who is born to us be formed in you; who delivered to us not only by being born, but by living and dying, a form to which we should be conformed: ever mindful that he was born for us alone, and willed to live for us alone, and to die for none but us\u2014he who had no need to die for himself\u2014so that through him we might be reborn, according to him we might live, and in him we might die, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon III: On the Wonderful Exchange Between God and Man", "topics": ["Nativity", "Incarnation", "self-emptying", "humility", "spiritual motherhood"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo IV, De plenitudine temporis a Christo allata, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 38-43.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Galatians 4:4", "Colossians 2:9", "Psalm 84:2, 13 (historical numbering)", "Romans 14:17", "Psalm 118:64 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 64:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 35:7\u20138 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 106:15 (historical numbering)", "Job 6:5", "Proverbs 20:14", "Romans 8:18", "Psalm 70:15\u201316 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 142:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 70:16 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 12:10", "2 Corinthians 6:2", "Ephesians 5:16", "Song of Songs 8:7", "John 3:17", "Galatians 6:1\u20139 (printed locator; see note)", "Wisdom 4:13", "Ecclesiastes 7:11", "Sirach 14:14", "Isaiah 9:6", "Sirach 14:8, 10", "Psalm 22:1\u20132 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fourth Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. Beginning from Paul's fullness of time, Guerric reads it as an abundance of grace, the fulfillment of prophecy and the mature age of faith. Salvation is bought at a trifling price, yet the present age meets it with complaint and ingratitude. He closes with thanks for the Word made hay in the manger for beasts as well as men. An editorial note explains an inaccurate printed locator. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De plenitudine temporis a Christo allata."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Fullness of Time Brought by Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ecce jam plenitudo temporis (Galat. IV, 4). Plenitudo ista temporis apud Paulum accipitur, seu propter abundantiam gratiae, seu propter adimpletionem praecedentis prophetiae, seu propter pleniorem aetatem fidei adultae. De singulis videamus. Omnium plenitudo bonorum Christus Dominus est: quippe qui omnibus thesauris sapientiae et scientiae Dei, totiusque gratiae plenus est; imo in quo omnis plenitudo divinitatis habitat corporaliter (Coloss. II, 9). Viderunt apostoli plenum gratiae et veritatis, et de plenitudine ejus omnes acceperunt: adeo ut novissimus eorum et abortivus Paulus, vas electionis, de plenitudine quam acceperat, disseminaret ubique plenitudinem gratiae et veritatis, clamans et dicens: Ecce jam venit plenitudo temporis (Galat. IV, 4). Cum enim plenus omnibus bonis natus esset temporaliter auctor temporis, quidni plenitudinem attulisset temporis? Cum coeli rorarent desuper, et nubes pluerent Justum, Salvatorem terra germinaret, tanta supernae benedictionis ubertas quidni fecunditatem terris omnibus praestaret? Benedixisti, Domine, terram tuam, dedisti benignitatem, et terra nostra dedit fructum suum (Psal. LXXXIV, 2, 13); videlicet de uno grano frumenti, quod germinavit alvus Virginis, ubique terrarum pullulante copiosa messe fidelium. Nolo enim hanc plenitudinem temporis requiras in copia bonorum temporalium, sed aeternorum; non in proventu agrorum, sed coelorum. Si coeli rorant desuper, et nubes pluunt Justum; si terra germinat Salvatorem, et justitia oritur simul; si denique oritur in diebus Domini non solum justitia, sed et abundantia pacis: feliciora tempora ne quaesieris, quando et regnum Dei nihil aliud est quam justitia, et pax, et gaudium in Spiritu sancto (Rom. XIV, 17), quod de illis procedit. Nam et nostrorum temporum ille status judicatur optimus, atque pulcherrimus, cum et justitia moribus imponit disciplinam, et abundantia cum pace quietem vitae praestat et laetitiam. Denique misericordia Domini plena est terra (Psal. CXVIII, 64): benedixit Dominus coronae anni benignitatis suae, et campi ejus repleti sunt ubertate (Psal. LXIV, 12) totius gratiae spiritualis. Et quis, nisi ingratus, plenitudinem neget esse temporis? Quae unquam aurea aetas simile aliquid habuit hujus plenitudini temporis, quando panis angelorum, omnem habens saporem suavitatis et omne delectamentum, apponitur etiam jumentis; et non solum homines, sed et jumenta, cibo pascuntur coelesti? Homines enim et jumenta salvabis, Domine, quemadmodum multiplicasti misericordiam tuam, Deus (Psal. XXXV, 7, 8). Infinite multiplex haec tua, Deus, misericordia, ut qui panis angelorum, non solum hominum dites ac beatifices mensas, sed etiam fenum factus, jumentorum repleas praesepia. Confiteris, o Domine sapientia misericors, quia sapientibus et insipientibus debitor es, ut qui utrosque fecisti, utrisque necessariam alimoniam procures; et tam homines quam jumenta, tam spirituales quam animales, suo quemque gradu et ordine salves. Confiteantur igitur Domino misericordiae ejus, et mirabilia ejus filiis hominum (Psal. CVI, 15): quia misit Verbum suum carnem factum, medicinam et escam omnium; ut etiam incapaces Verbi, sanentur tamen et satientur carne Verbi. Edant itaque pauperes et saturentur, et confiteantur venisse beatam temporis plenitudinem; quando et in praesepibus suis paratum sine sudore vultus sui panem inveniunt coelestem. Numquid ergo mugiet bos, cum ante praesepe sic plenum steterit? (Job VI, 5.) Mugiat, sed prae gaudio spiritus in jubilo cordis; quia praesepe Domini sui, imo Dominum in praesepi suo cognovit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Behold, now is the fullness of time (Galat. IV, 4). This \"fullness of time\" is taken by Paul in reference either to the abundance of grace, or to the fulfillment of earlier prophecy, or to the fuller age of mature faith. Let us look at each in turn. Christ the Lord is the fullness of all good things: for he is full of all the treasures of the wisdom and knowledge of God, and of all grace; indeed, in him dwells all the fullness of the Godhead bodily (Coloss. II, 9). The apostles saw him full of grace and truth, and from his fullness all received: so much so that the last of them and the one born as it were out of due time, Paul, the chosen vessel, from the fullness he had received, scattered everywhere the fullness of grace and truth, crying out and saying: Behold, now the fullness of time has come (Galat. IV, 4). For since the author of time, full of all good things, was born in time, why should he not have brought the fullness of time? When the heavens dropped dew from above, and the clouds rained down the Just One, and the earth brought forth the Savior as its growth, why should such abundance of heavenly blessing not bring fruitfulness to all the earth? You have blessed, O Lord, your land; you have given your bounty, and our land has yielded its fruit (Psal. LXXXIV, 2, 13); namely, from the one grain of wheat which sprouted from the Virgin's womb, a plentiful harvest of the faithful has sprung up throughout all the earth. For I do not want you to seek this fullness of time in an abundance of temporal goods, but of eternal ones; not in the yield of fields, but of heavens. If the heavens drop dew from above, and the clouds rain down the Just One; if the earth brings forth the Savior as its sprout, and righteousness arises at the same time; and if, finally, in the days of the Lord there arises not only righteousness but also abundance of peace: do not seek for happier times, since the kingdom of God is nothing other than righteousness, and peace, and joy in the Holy Spirit (Rom. XIV, 17), which proceeds from these things. For indeed that condition of our times is judged best and most beautiful, when righteousness imposes discipline on morals, and abundance together with peace provides quiet of life and gladness. In short, the earth is full of the mercy of the Lord (Psal. CXVIII, 64): the Lord has blessed the crown of the year with his bounty, and his fields are filled with the abundance (Psal. LXIV, 12) of all spiritual grace. And who, unless he is ungrateful, would deny that this is the fullness of time? What golden age ever possessed anything like this fullness of time, when the bread of angels, having every taste of sweetness and every delight, is set before even the beasts of burden; and not only men, but even beasts of burden, are fed with heavenly food? For you will save men and beasts, O Lord; O how you have multiplied your mercy, O God (Psal. XXXV, 7, 8). Infinitely manifold is this your mercy, O God, that you who are the bread of angels not only enrich and make blessed the tables of men, but, having become hay, fill the mangers of beasts of burden. You confess, O Lord, merciful Wisdom, that you are debtor both to the wise and to the foolish, since, having made both, you provide necessary nourishment for both; and you save men as well as beasts, the spiritual as well as the animal, each in its own degree and order. Let his mercies therefore give praise to the Lord, and his wonders to the children of men (Psal. CVI, 15): for he sent his Word made flesh, the medicine and food of all; so that even those incapable of the Word might nevertheless be healed and satisfied by the flesh of the Word. Let the poor therefore eat and be filled, and confess that the blessed fullness of time has come; since even in their mangers they find heavenly bread prepared without the sweat of their brow. Will the ox then bellow, when he has stood before a manger so full? (Job VI, 5.) Let him bellow\u2014but let it be for the joy of spirit, in the jubilation of the heart; because he has recognized the manger of his Lord, indeed, the Lord himself in his manger.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Libet autem adhuc manifestius videre, Christus veniens e coelis quantam plenitudinem temporis advexerit, quam facili et exiguo pretio omne pretiosum comparari posse decreverit. Duobus minutis, seu calice aquae frigidae, aut certe sola bona voluntate, jam a diebus ejus emitur regnum coelorum, quanquam etiam nunc vix inveniat emptorem in tanta multitudine divitum. Proh pudor! etiam nos ipsi, qui jam emere coepimus, jam tabulas emptionis conscripsimus, jam pignus haereditatis accepimus; etiam nos, inquam, toties a pacto resilimus, ac pene complaciti poenitemus, querimur, murmuramus, quasi in negotio simus circumventi; quasi magnum sit illud prope nihil, quod exigitur a nobis. Bene de nobis prophetavit Scriptura: Malum est; malum est, dicit omnis emptor, et cum recesserit, gloriabitur (Prov. XX, 14). An non merito poterit gloriari, qui immensum illud et aeternum pondus gloriae, hoc levi et momentaneo tribulationis suae comparaverit, qui sic pro nihilo salvus factus fuerit? At nunc quotidianas hinc inde querelas et molesta murmura audias: Malum hoc, malum illud; grave hoc, importabile illud; quis tanta ac talia ferat? Ita nemo fere est qui digne pro merito sui rem aestimans dicat: Non sunt condignae passiones hujus temporis ad futuram gloriam quae revelabitur in nobis (Rom. VIII, 18), nisi tunc demum cum coeperit revelari. Tunc plane gloriabitur, qui modo detrahit, ac de pretio se gravari gemit; cum scilicet de foro, id est, de mundo hoc, ubi contractus celebratur hujus commercii, recesserit et in domum aeternitatis suae, secum ferens rem tantam tantillo emptam, redierit. Homo plane secundum cor Dei erat ille, homo simplex sine querela, qui ab hac arte, imo inertia et infidelitate emptorum alienus erat; Davidem dico, qui dicebat: Quoniam non cognovi litteraturam, Domine, memorabor justitiae tuae solius (Psal. LXX, 15, 16). Justitiae meae nullatenus memorabor, ut labores meos exaggerem, merita magnificem; potius memorabor justitiae tuae solius, qui gratis obligasti te mihi promissorem. In veritate tua exaudi me, in justitia tua, et non intres in judicium cum servo tuo (Psal. CXLII, 1, 2): qui, si voluero me justificare, os meum condemnabit me. Per hoc enim, inquit, introibo in potentias Domini (Psal. LXX, 16); quia scilicet justitias meas non statuam: per hoc potentem me faciet, et nunc in praelio, et tunc in regno; quia semper infirmitates meas confitebor. Cum enim infirmor, tunc potens sum (II Cor. XII, 10). Sicut ergo prudenter apud Deum causam agit, qui justitiae suae immemor totum misericordiae committit; sic prudenter emit, qui omnem fictionem, atque avaritiam negotiationis scire renuit; qui inventa illa pretiosa margarita, non solum omnia sua, sed et seipsum ultro ac desideranter inpendit. At nos tepidi, ficti, ingrati, indevoti, voluptatum amatores magis quam Dei, vix ab illa voce infidelis et nequissimi murmuris, Malum est, malum est (Prov. XX, 14), valemus compesci; etiam postquam gustavimus, et vidimus, sicut de muliere forti scriptum est, quoniam bona est negotiatio nostra."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But I wish to see still more clearly how great a fullness of time Christ brought when he came from the heavens, and with how easy and small a price he determined that everything precious could be purchased. With two mites, or a cup of cold water, or indeed with good will alone, the kingdom of heaven has been bought ever since his days \u2014 though even now it scarcely finds a buyer amid so great a multitude of the rich. For shame! Even we ourselves, who have already begun to buy, who have already drawn up the tablets of purchase, who have already received the pledge of inheritance \u2014 even we, I say, so often draw back from the bargain, and, having almost agreed, repent, complain, murmur, as though we had been cheated in the transaction; as though that were a great thing \u2014 that next-to-nothing which is required of us. Scripture prophesied well concerning us: \"It is bad, it is bad,\" says every buyer, and when he has gone away, he will boast (Prov. XX, 14). And will he not rightly be able to boast, who has purchased that immense and eternal weight of glory with this light and momentary tribulation of his, who has thus been saved for next to nothing? But now you may hear on every side daily complaints and troublesome murmurs: \"This is bad, that is bad; this is heavy, that is unbearable; who can bear such great and such things?\" Thus there is scarcely anyone who, rightly valuing the matter according to its worth, says: \"The sufferings of this time are not worthy to be compared with the future glory that shall be revealed in us\" (Rom. VIII, 18) \u2014 except only then, when it has begun to be revealed. Then clearly he will boast, who now disparages it and groans that he is burdened by the price; that is, when he has departed from the marketplace \u2014 that is, from this world, where the contract of this trade is transacted \u2014 and has returned to the house of his eternity, carrying with him so great a thing bought for so small a price. That man was truly a man after God's own heart, a simple man without complaint, who was a stranger to this art \u2014 or rather, to this sluggishness and faithlessness of buyers; I mean David, who said: \"Because I have not known letters, O Lord, I will be mindful of your justice alone\" (Psal. LXX, 15, 16). \"I will by no means be mindful of my own justice, so as to exaggerate my labors or magnify my merits; rather I will be mindful of your justice alone, who freely bound yourself to me as one who promises.\" \"Hear me in your truth, in your justice, and do not enter into judgment with your servant\" (Psal. CXLII, 1, 2): for if I wished to justify myself, my own mouth would condemn me. \"For by this,\" he says, \"I will enter into the powers of the Lord\" (Psal. LXX, 16); that is, because I will not establish my own righteousness: by this he will make me powerful, both now in the battle and then in the kingdom; because I will always confess my weaknesses. \"For when I am weak, then I am powerful\" (II Cor. XII, 10). Just as, therefore, he pleads his case wisely before God who, forgetful of his own righteousness, commits everything to mercy, so he buys wisely who refuses to know any pretense or greed in the trading; who, having found that precious pearl, spends not only all that he has, but also himself, freely and eagerly. But we, lukewarm, false, ungrateful, undevout, lovers of pleasures more than of God, can scarcely restrain ourselves from that voice of faithless and most wicked murmuring, \"It is bad, it is bad\" (Prov. XX, 14) \u2014 even after we have tasted and seen, as it is written of the valiant woman, that our trading is good.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Adhuc ferebat me quidam implacabilis zelus adversus ingratitudinem et infidelitatem hujus temporis nostri; sed revocat me, de qua sermonem institui, sancta ac beata plenitudo temporis Christi, qua etiam hodie plenius pascendi estis. Conjunctim tamen currunt, pariterque feruntur haec duo tempora tam diversa, tamque adversa, tempus scilicet gratiae, et tempus malitiae; cum tamen nonnisi unum tempus inveniatur utriusque. Si enim modo non esset tempus gratiae, non diceret Apostolus: Ecce nunc tempus acceptabile, ecce nunc dies salutis (II Cor. VI, 2). Et iterum, si modo non esset tempus malitiae, non diceret idem apostolus: Redimentes tempus, quoniam dies mali sunt (Ephes. V, 16). Confligunt igitur in tempore uno gratia et ingratitudo, velut in stadio uno. Jam olim siquidem sapientia pugnat adversus malitiam, propter quod et nunc in hujus mundi descendit aream. Pugnat, inquam, nolens vinci a malo, sed volens vincere in bono malum. Abundabat iniquitas, et licet humana congelata esset, divina tamen non frigescebat charitas. Magnus ille ardor charitatis, et revera, sicut scriptum est, aquis multis inexstinguibilis (Cantic. VIII, 7); quod cum extremum jam judicium postularet multitudo peccatorum, Deus tamen Filium suum misit, non ut judicaret mundum, sed ut salvaretur per ipsum (Joan. III, 17). Cum igitur jam pene consummata mundi malitia finem indicaret temporis, Redemptoris adventus novam et inopinatam plenitudinem temporis rebus infudit humanis. Cum enim mundus consenuisset, atque prope interitum esset temporis aetate, subito ad adventum Conditoris sui, nova et insperata renovatus est virtutis juventute, fideique quodam juvenili calore. Fides siquidem, cujus primaevum tempus, velut quaedam pueritia, fuit in Patriarchis, qui exorti sunt primo mane Ecclesiae nascentis; adolescentia vero in Prophetis; ad plenitudinem demum juvenilis excrevit roboris in Apostolis, quando et calorem suae virtutis mundo spectabilem praebuit in tam praeclaris, atque fortissimis innumerabilium martyrum triumphis. Hanc adultam ac plenam aetatem fidei vocat Apostolus plenitudinem temporis, quando scilicet hi qui sub paedagogo legis erant, nihilque a servo differebant, dum erant parvuli, jam adulti libertatem filiorum receperunt per Unigenitum Patris (Galat. VI, 1-9). Qui nimirum, ut nulla plenitudo suo deesset tempori, plenus tam gratia quam veritate venit, quatenus et per gratiam impleri faceret legis mandata, et per veritatem impleret ipse promissa; adeo ut quidquid etiam universis retro saeculis mystice gestum aut dictum fuit, totum verius ac plenius impletum sit in hac plenitudine temporis. Nam et hic ipse dies tot sacramenta, tot oracula, quasi recapitulata in brevi hodie exhibuit, ut de eo vere dici possit: Consummatus in brevi, explevit tempora multa (Sap. IV, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. A certain relentless zeal against the ingratitude and unfaithfulness of our present time was still carrying me along; but the holy and blessed fullness of the time of Christ, on which I began this sermon, and with which you are to be fed more fully even today, calls me back. Yet these two times, so different and so opposed to each other\u2014namely, the time of grace and the time of malice\u2014run together and are carried along jointly, even though there is found to be but one single time for both. For if the present were not the time of grace, the Apostle would not say: 'Behold, now is the acceptable time; behold, now is the day of salvation' (II Cor. VI, 2). And again, if the present were not the time of malice, the same Apostle would not say: 'Redeeming the time, because the days are evil' (Ephes. V, 16). Grace and ingratitude, therefore, clash together in one single time, as if in one single arena. For wisdom has long since been fighting against malice, and for this reason it has now also come down into the arena of this world. It fights, I say, unwilling to be conquered by evil, but willing to conquer evil with good. Iniquity was abounding, and although human charity had grown cold, divine charity nevertheless did not grow cold. That great ardor of charity is indeed, as it is written, unquenchable by many waters (Cantic. VIII, 7); for though the multitude of sins now demanded the final judgment, yet God sent his Son, not to judge the world, but that the world might be saved through him (Joan. III, 17). Therefore, when the malice of the world, now almost complete, was signaling the end of time, the coming of the Redeemer poured out upon human affairs a new and unexpected fullness of time. For when the world had grown old, and was near destruction through the age of time, suddenly, at the coming of its Founder, it was renewed with a new and unhoped-for youthfulness of virtue, and with a certain youthful warmth of faith. For faith, whose earliest age\u2014a kind of childhood, as it were\u2014was in the Patriarchs, who arose in the first morning of the Church then being born; whose adolescence was in the Prophets; at last grew up into the fullness of youthful strength in the Apostles, when it also displayed to the world the warmth of its vigor, made visible in the splendid and most valiant triumphs of countless martyrs. This mature and full age of faith the Apostle calls the fullness of time\u2014namely, when those who were under the schoolmaster of the law, and who, while they were children, differed in nothing from a slave, now grown up, received the freedom of sons through the Only-begotten of the Father (Galat. VI, 1-9). He indeed, so that no fullness might be lacking to his own time, came full both of grace and of truth, so that through grace he might cause the commands of the law to be fulfilled, and through truth he himself might fulfill the promises; to such a degree that whatever had been done or said mystically throughout all the ages gone before was fulfilled more truly and more fully in this fullness of time. For even this very day has displayed, as it were recapitulated in brief, so many mysteries, so many oracles, that of it may truly be said: 'Being perfected in a short time, he fulfilled many years' (Sap. IV, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Cum ergo tot modis constet quia venit beata plenitudo temporis, recte querelas insipientium Salomon compescuit, dicens: Ne dicas, Quid, putas, causae est quod meliora fuere tempora priora quam nunc sunt? Stulta est, inquit, hujusmodi interrogatio (Eccle. VII, 11), cum manifeste felicissima tempora fecerit hominibus gratia Dei; ingratitudo autem hominum pessima fecerit ea sibi. O tempus acceptabile, cui tam beatum, tam auspicatum initium dedit dies iste, quo natus conditor aeternus temporis, aeternitatem mortalibus nuntiavit! O vere dies salutis dies iste, quo nata salus mundi se ipsam poculum salutare miscuit aegrotis! O fratres, de die isto loquebatur, ni fallor, Sapiens filio suo: Non defrauderis a die bono. Et exponens quid sit non defraudari: Particula, inquit, boni doni non te praetereat (Eccli. XIV, 14). Diem quippe bonum pro eo dicit, quia datum est hodie donum bonum, prorsus datum optimum, donumque perfectum, descendens a Patre luminum; quod quale sit nemo scit, nisi qui accepit. At vos, fratres, spiritum qui ex Deo est accepistis, ut sciatis quae a Deo donata sunt vobis, qui ex sententia cordium cantatis: Filius datus est nobis (Isai. IX, 6). Filius iste, panis est filiorum, qui de seipso hodie beatum ac solemne universae familiae Patris exhibet epulum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Since, then, it is established in so many ways that the blessed fullness of time has come, Solomon rightly checked the complaints of fools, saying: 'Do not say, What do you suppose is the reason that former times were better than now are?' 'Such a question,' he says, 'is foolish' (Eccle. VII, 11), since plainly the grace of God has made the times most happy for mankind, but the ingratitude of men has made those same times most wretched for themselves. O acceptable time, to which this day gave so blessed, so auspicious a beginning\u2014the day on which the eternal Founder of time was born and announced eternity to mortals! O truly this day is the day of salvation, on which the salvation of the world, being born, mixed itself as a saving cup for the sick! O brothers, it was of this day, unless I am mistaken, that the Wise Man spoke to his son: 'Do not defraud yourself of a good day.' And explaining what it means not to be defrauded, he says: 'Let not the portion of a good gift pass you by' (Eccli. XIV, 14). He calls it a good day precisely because today a good gift was given\u2014indeed the very best gift given, and a perfect gift, descending from the Father of lights\u2014a gift of such a kind that no one knows it except the one who has received it. But you, brothers, have received the Spirit who is from God, that you may know the things that have been given to you by God, you who sing from the conviction of your hearts: 'A Son is given to us' (Isai. IX, 6). This Son is the bread of sons, who today, from himself, provides a blessed and solemn banquet for the whole family of the Father.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Gratias tibi, Pater misericordiarum, qui panem nostrum quotidianum das nobis hodie, tantaque largitate aperuisti manum tuam ad implendum omne animal benedictione, ut ipsa etiam benedictione plenum inveniatur eorum praesepe. Quam miserum, quam brutum ac stolidum, imo quam sibi nequam et invidum illud animal, quod sese isto die bono defraudat, ut eum particula doni boni praetereat, ut scilicet expers gratiae coelestis, quae ei proposita est, fiat, diemque totius refectionis et gaudii tristi ac jejuno corde praetereat; quasi necdum venerit copiosissima plenitudo temporis, nec praesepia simplicium et humilium panis impleverit coelestis! Hujusmodi hominem sibi nequam et invidum, Deo ingratum et injuriosum, notat Sapientia, cum ait: Oculus malus ad mala opera, et non saturabitur pane, indigens et in tristitia erit super mensam suam. Ideo, inquit, non saturabitur bonis anima ipsius, quia oculus ejus ad mala, nec revertetur oculus ejus, ut videat bona, ut cum pietate et fide consideret quae ei apposita sunt in magna divitis mensa. Nequam est enim, inquit, oculus lividi, avertens faciem suam et despiciens animam suam (Eccli. XIV, 10, 8). Procul dubio, fratres, si faciem non avertimus a consideratione illius qui jacet in praesepi, ipso intuitu possumus felicissime pasci, dicemusque: Dominus pascit me, et nihil mihi deerit; in loco pascuae, ibi me collocavit (Psal. XXII, 1, 2). Tunc plane sciemus quia venit desideratissima plenitudo temporis, in quo misit Deus Filium suum, per quem tanta jam replemur plenitudine bonorum: ipsi benedictio et gratiarum actio, et nunc, et per infinita saecula saeculorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Thanks be to you, Father of mercies, who give us our daily bread today, and who with such great generosity have opened your hand to fill every living creature with blessing, that even their manger is found full of that same blessing. How wretched, how brutish and foolish\u2014indeed how wicked and grudging toward itself\u2014is that creature which cheats itself on this good day, so that the portion of the good gift passes it by; so that, lacking the heavenly grace set before it, it lets the day of all refreshment and joy pass by with a sad and fasting heart, as though the most abundant fullness of time had not yet come, and the heavenly bread had not filled the mangers of the simple and the humble! Wisdom marks out such a man, wicked and grudging toward himself, ungrateful and offensive toward God, when it says: 'An evil eye is bent toward evil deeds, and he shall not be satisfied with bread; he shall be needy and sorrowful at his own table.' 'Therefore,' it says, 'his soul shall not be filled with good things, because his eye is bent toward evil things, nor will his eye turn back to see good things,' so that he might with devotion and faith consider what has been set before him at the great table of the rich host. 'For wicked,' it says, 'is the eye of the envious man, turning away his face and despising his own soul' (Eccli. XIV, 10, 8). Without doubt, brothers, if we do not turn our face away from gazing on him who lies in the manger, by that very gaze we can be most happily fed, and we shall say: 'The Lord feeds me, and I shall want nothing; in a place of pasture, there he has set me' (Psal. XXII, 1, 2). Then we shall clearly know that the most longed-for fullness of time has come, in which God sent his Son, through whom we are now filled with so great a fullness of good things: to him be blessing and thanksgiving, both now and through the endless ages of ages.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints the reference as Galat. VI, 1-9; the passage on the heir under tutors and the fullness of time is Galatians 4:1-9. The printed reference is kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon IV: On the Fullness of Time Brought by Christ", "topics": ["Nativity", "fullness of time", "grace", "ingratitude", "Christmas joy"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "seasonKey:season-christmas", "calendar_label": "Christmastide", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-nativity-05", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De nativitate Domini, sermo V, De mira Dei providentia in nativitate Christi, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 43-46.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Christmas", "Nativity of the Lord", "Luke 2:15", "John 20:29", "John 1:1 (printed locator; the words are 1 John 1:1)", "John 1:14", "Psalm 47:9 (historical numbering)", "Wisdom 18:15", "Psalm 11:5 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 10:23", "Matthew 11:25\u201326", "Isaiah 9:6", "Isaiah 5:18", "Matthew 19:14", "Psalm 83:11 (historical numbering)", "1 Peter 3:15", "James 1:21"], "occasion": "De nativitate Domini", "occasion_detail": "Nativity of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Christmas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "This fifth Nativity sermon belongs to Guerric of Igny's medieval collection of sermons for the liturgical year, here read from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its individual date is unknown; the collection is associated approximately with his abbacy in 1138\u20131157. The brothers came to hear the Word of God, but at Bethlehem they may also see it. Guerric draws lessons from the infant Word's silence, poverty and humility, and from the shepherds' simple faith. He ends at the altar, where the same body lies hidden under humble forms as the infant was wrapped in cloths. The heading names the Nativity without a particular Christmas Mass."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO V."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON V.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mira Dei providentia in nativitate Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On God's Wonderful Providence in the Birth of Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Convenistis, fratres, ad audiendum verbum Dei. Deus autem aliquid melius providit nobis, ut non solum audire, sed etiam videre datum nobis hodie sit Verbum Dei, si modo transeamus usque Bethlehem, et videamus hoc verbum quod fecit Dominus, et ostendit nobis (Luc. II, 15). Noverat hominum sensus invisibilium incapaces, coelestium indocibiles, ad fidem quoque difficiles, nisi res ipsa de qua fides suadetur, visibiliter ipsis sensibus convincendis ingeratur. Nam etsi fides est ex auditu, multo tamen proclivius ac promptius ex visu: sicut illius exemplo docemur, cui dicitur, Quia vidisti me, credidisti (Joan. XX, 29); qui scilicet dum audiebas incredulus fuisti. Quia enim difficilius audita, quam visa creduntur, ideo merito fides illorum qui non viderunt, a Domino beata praedicatur; quippe qui plus detulerint auctoritati sermonis, quam experientiae proprii sensus, aut rationis. Deus tamen per omnia volens satisfacere tarditati nostrae, Verbum suum quod prius fecerat audibile, hodie fecit nobis etiam visibile, imo et tractabile; ut et quidam ex nobis potuerint dicere: Quod fuit ab initio, quod audivimus, quod vidimus oculis nostris, quod perspeximus, et manus nostrae tractaverunt de Verbo vitae (Joan. I, 1). Fuit ab initio illius, quae sine initio est, aeternitatis; audivimus promissum ab initio temporis; vidimus et contrectavimus exhibitum in fine temporis. Alias autem invenies quod non solum visibile et tractabile, sed etiam gustabile et odorabile nobis Verbum Dei factum sit, quippe quod per omnes vias sensuum, aditum sibi ad animam quaesierit, quatenus sicut per sensus mors intraverat, sic per eosdem rediret et vita. Quod ergo Verbum caro factum est (Joan. I, 14), nobis qui toti sumus caro, factum est; ut qui antea Verbum Dei tantum poteramus audire, jam carnem factum et videre possimus et gustare, omnesque sensus in testimonium auditus convocare; quatenus omnes nostri sensus consensu uno, et voce una confiteantur, Sicut audivimus, sic vidimus (Psal. XLVII, 9). Incomparabiliter tamen amplius tunc concessum est visui, quam unquam fuerat auditui, cum modo videatur Verbum, quod Deus est, et antea magnum haberetur audire verbum quodcunque ex Deo. Et verbum quidem quod ex Deo est, vidi aliquando, fratres, cum taedio audiri; sed Verbum quod Deus est, nunquid poterit nisi cum gaudio videri? In me primum sententiam feram: quia Verbum, quod Deus est, cum se hodie mihi videndum praebeat in eo quod sum, si me non laetificat, impius sum; si non aedificat, reprobus sum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. You have come together, brothers, to hear the word of God. But God has provided something better for us, so that today it is given to us not only to hear but also to see the Word of God \u2014 if only we go over as far as Bethlehem, and see this word which the Lord has made, and shown to us (Luc. II, 15). He knew that the senses of men are incapable of invisible things, unteachable in heavenly things, and also slow to believe, unless the very thing of which faith is being persuaded is impressed visibly upon the senses themselves so as to convince them. For although faith is from hearing, yet it comes much more readily and easily from sight: as we are taught by the example of him to whom it was said, \"Because you have seen me, you have believed\" (Joan. XX, 29) \u2014 that is, you who, while you were only hearing, were unbelieving. For since things heard are believed with more difficulty than things seen, therefore the faith of those who have not seen is rightly proclaimed blessed by the Lord; since they gave more credit to the authority of the word than to the experience of their own sense or reason. Yet God, wishing in every way to satisfy our slowness, has today made for us even visible \u2014 indeed even touchable \u2014 his Word, which before he had made only audible; so that some of us have even been able to say: \"That which was from the beginning, which we have heard, which we have seen with our eyes, which we have looked upon, and our hands have handled, concerning the Word of life\" (Joan. I, 1). He was from the beginning of that eternity which has no beginning; we heard him promised from the beginning of time; we saw and touched him shown forth at the end of time. Elsewhere, too, you will find that the Word of God was made for us not only visible and touchable, but even tastable and smellable, since through every avenue of the senses it sought an entrance to the soul, so that just as death had entered through the senses, so also life might return through the same senses. Therefore that \"the Word was made flesh\" (Joan. I, 14) was done for us, who are wholly flesh; so that we, who before could only hear the Word of God, might now both see and taste it made flesh, and might summon all our senses as witness to our hearing; so that all our senses, with one consent and one voice, might confess, \"As we have heard, so have we seen\" (Psal. XLVII, 9). Yet incomparably more was then granted to sight than had ever been granted to hearing, since now the Word which is God is seen, whereas before it was reckoned a great thing merely to hear any word whatsoever from God. And indeed, brothers, I have sometimes seen a word which is from God heard with weariness; but can the Word which is God possibly be seen except with joy? Let me pass judgment first upon myself: since the Word, which is God, offers itself today to be seen by me in that which I am \u2014 if it does not make me glad, I am impious; if it does not build me up, I am reprobate.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si quis ergo, fratres, acediosus invenitur inter nos, nolo diutius ut fatigentur aures ejus sermone nostro contemptibili; transeat usque Bethlehem, et illud in quod desiderant Angeli prospicere, prospiciat ibi, Verbum scilicet Dei, quod Dominus ostendit nobis; imaginetur animo, Sermo Dei vivus et efficax qualis ibi jaceat [ al. qualiter ibi taceat] in praesepi. Si tantum pietas oculum illuminet intuentis, quid tam delectabiliter potest videri, tam salubriter cogitari? Quid aeque mores aedificat, spem roborat, charitatem inflammat? Omnino fidelis sermo, et omni acceptione dignus, omnipotens Sermo tuus, Domine; qui tam alto silentio a regalibus Patris sedibus animalium illapsus praesepibus (Sap. XVIII, 15), melius interim suo nobis silentio sermocinatur. Qui habet aures audiendi, audiat quid nobis hoc pium et mysticum aeterni Verbi loquatur silentium; quoniam, nisi me fallit auditus, inter alia quae loquitur, loquitur pacem in plebem sanctorum, quibus reverentia ipsius et exemplum religiosum imposuit silentium, et rectissime quidem imposuit. Quid enim tanto pondere, tantaque auctoritate disciplinam commendat silentii, quid tanto terrore compescit inquietum malum linguarum procellasque verborum, sicut Sermo Dei silens in medio hominum? Non est sermo in lingua mea, confiteri videtur omnipotens Sermo, dum Matri subditus est; et nos qua dementia dicemus, Linguam nostram magnificabimus, labia nostra a nobis sunt; quis noster Dominus est? (Psal. XI, 5.) Liberet mihi, si liceret, obmutescere et humiliari, et silere etiam a bonis, ut attentius et diligentius aurem possem adhibere vocibus arcanis, sacrisque sensibus hujus divini silentii, et vel tanto tempore sub silentio discere in schola Verbi, quanto Verbum ipsum sub disciplina siluit Matris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If then, brothers, anyone among us is found weighed down with listlessness, I do not wish his ears to be wearied any longer by our contemptible discourse; let him go over to Bethlehem, and there look upon that into which the angels desire to look, namely the Word of God, which the Lord has shown us; let him picture in his mind how the living and effective Word of God lies there [alternate reading: how it keeps silence there] in the manger. If only piety would illumine the eye of the one beholding, what could be seen so delightfully, thought upon so wholesomely? What equally builds up conduct, strengthens hope, inflames love? Altogether faithful is the saying, and worthy of all acceptance \u2014 your almighty Word, O Lord; who, gliding down in so deep a silence from the Father's royal throne into the mangers of animals (Sap. XVIII, 15), meanwhile speaks to us the better by his very silence. Let him who has ears to hear, hear what this pious and mystical silence of the eternal Word speaks to us; for, unless my hearing deceives me, among the other things it speaks, it speaks peace to the people of the saints, upon whom reverence for it and its devout example have imposed silence \u2014 and most rightly has it imposed it. For what commends the discipline of silence with such weight and such authority, what restrains with such terror the restless evil of tongues and the storms of words, as the Word of God, silent in the midst of men? \"There is no word in my tongue\" \u2014 the almighty Word seems to confess this, while he is subject to his Mother; and with what madness shall we say, \"We will magnify our tongue, our lips are our own; who is our Lord?\" (Psal. XI, 5.). It would please me, if it were permitted, to become mute and be humbled, and to keep silence even from good things, so that I might the more attentively and diligently apply my ear to the secret voices and the sacred meanings of this divine silence, and might learn in silence in the school of the Word for at least as long a time as the Word himself kept silence under the discipline of his Mother.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O fratres, si pie ac diligenter intendamus huic Verbo quod Dominus fecit hodie, et ostendit nobis, quanta, quamque facile possemus ab ipso perdoceri? Verbum siquidem breviatum est, ita tamen ut in eo consummatum sit omne verbum quod ad salutem est, quia nimirum verbum consummans et abbrevians in aequitate ipsum est. Et haec consummatio abbreviata inundavit justitiam (Isa. X, 23), sicut promissum meministis per Isaiam; quia de sua plenitudine in participes suos redundavit justitiam, ut abundet justitia ipsorum, plus quam et Pharisaeorum et Scribarum; licet illi per tam multiplices se exercitent justificationes legis, isti autem brevi ac simplici contenti sint verbo fidei. Sed quid mirum si omnia verba sua nobis Verbum Dei breviavit, quando et se ipsum breviari, et quodammodo minui voluit, adeo ut de incomprehensibili immensitate sui ad augustias uteri quodammodo se contraxerit, et, continens mundum, in praesepi se passum sit contineri? In coelo Verbum istud horribili altitudine sua virtutes angelicas stupefacit: in praesepi simplices et hebetes pascit: ibi subtilissimis Angelorum intellectibus inscrutabile, hic etiam obtusis hominum sensibus palpabile. Quia enim Deus non poterat loqui nobis quasi spiritualibus, sed quasi carnalibus, Verbum ejus factum est caro, ut non solum audire, sed et videre posset omnis caro quod os Domini locutum est. Et quia in sapientia sua non cognovit mundus sapientiam Dei, dignatione ineffabili eadem Sapientia Dei se stultitiam fecit, ut quamlibet idiotis vel hebetibus se discibilem praeberet, et per stultitiam praedicationis salvos faceret credentes. Confiteor tibi, Pater, Domine coeli et terrae, quia sapientiam tuam abscondisti a sapientibus et prudentibus, et revelasti eam parvulis. Ita, Pater, sic fuit placitum ante te (Matth. XI, 25, 26), ut parvulis daretur parvulus qui natus est nobis, quandoquidem altitudo superborum nimis abhorret ab hujus humilitate parvuli, et quod altum est apud homines, abominabile est apud Deum, qui, cum veraciter altus sit, parvulus factus est pro nobis. Cum solis nimirum parvulis concordat iste parvulus, in solis requiescit humilibus et quietis. Sicut igitur parvuli gloriantes de eo, cantant, Parvulus natus est nobis (Isa. IX, 6), sic et ipse gloriatur de eis: Ecce ego, inquiens, et pueri mei, quos mihi donavit Dominus (Id. V, 18). Ut enim parvulo Filio suo Pater sodales daret de coaetaneis suis, statim primo omnium ab innocentia parvulorum coepit gloria martyrii; hoc significante Spiritu sancto, quia nonnisi talium esset regnum coelorum (Matth. XIX, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O brothers, if we attend to this Word which the Lord has made today, and shown to us, piously and diligently, how much\u2014and how easily\u2014might we be thoroughly taught by it? For indeed the Word has been shortened, yet in such a way that in it every word that pertains to salvation is fulfilled, because it is indeed the word that completes and cuts short in equity. And this shortened completion has overflowed with righteousness (Isa. X, 23), as you recall was promised through Isaiah; for from his own fullness he has poured forth righteousness upon those who share in him, so that their righteousness abounds more than that of the Pharisees and Scribes\u2014though those exercise themselves through so many manifold justifications of the law, while these are content with the brief and simple word of faith. But what wonder is it if the Word of God has shortened all his words for us, when he himself willed to be shortened, and in a certain manner diminished, so much so that from the incomprehensible immensity of himself he contracted himself, as it were, to the narrow confines of a womb, and he who contains the world suffered himself to be contained in a manger? In heaven this Word, by its awesome height, strikes the angelic powers with astonishment; in the manger it feeds the simple and the dull: there inscrutable even to the most subtle intellects of the Angels, here even palpable to the dull senses of men. For since God could not speak to us as to spiritual beings, but as to carnal ones, his Word was made flesh, so that all flesh might not only hear, but also see, what the mouth of the Lord has spoken. And because the world, in its own wisdom, did not know the wisdom of God, by ineffable condescension this same Wisdom of God made itself foolishness, so that it might offer itself as teachable even to the unlearned and the dull, and through the foolishness of preaching might save those who believe. I confess to you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that you have hidden your wisdom from the wise and prudent, and have revealed it to little ones. Thus, Father, so it was pleasing before you (Matth. XI, 25, 26), that the little one who was born for us should be given to little ones, since the haughtiness of the proud shrinks utterly from the humility of this little one, and what is high among men is abominable before God, who, though he is truly high, was made a little one for us. For this little one agrees indeed only with little ones; he rests only in the humble and the quiet. Therefore, just as little ones, glorying in him, sing, 'A little one is born to us' (Isa. IX, 6), so also he himself glories in them, saying, 'Behold, I and my children, whom the Lord has given me' (Id. V, 18). For in order that the Father might give companions to his little Son from among his own contemporaries, the glory of martyrdom began first of all from the innocence of little children; the Holy Spirit signifying by this that the kingdom of heaven belongs only to such as these (Matth. XIX, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Si tales effici volumus, fratres, iterum atque iterum transeamus usque Bethlehem, et diligentius intueamur hoc Verbum quod caro factum est per Deum immensum, qui parvulus factus est, ut in hoc visibili et breviato Verbo discamus sapientiam Dei, quae tota humilitas facta est. In hanc quippe virtutem illa omnimoda virtus se interim totam contulit. Nihil aliud illa summa Sapientia interim scire voluit, nisi illam scilicet humilitatem, cujus se postmodum magistrum voluit profiteri. Et iste quidem, quod nimirum in suggillationem mei ipsius loquor, iste, inquam, digne et juste magister docendae humilitatis effectus est; qui, licet eam sibi non ignoraret et originalem a Matre, et naturalem a Patre, nihilominus tamen ab ipso Matris utero didicit eam ex his quae passus est. In diversorio viatorum natus est, ut hospites et peregrinos super terram, ipsius exemplo edocti, nos esse confiteremur. Ibi quoque novissimum eligens locum, in praesepio positus est, ut et nos illud Davidicum: Elegi abjectus esse in domo Dei, magis quam habitare in tabernaculis peccatorum (Psal. LXXXIII, 11), opere disceremus. Pannis involutus est, ut habentes tantum quibus tegamur, his contenti simus; per omnia paupertate materna contentus, Matrique per omnia subditus, ut totius religionis forma jam in ejus nativitate nata videretur. Beata sane simplicium fides pastorum, quae, cum invenisset infantem pannis involutum, nequaquam in illis scandalizata est ad incredulitatem, ut minora de eo sentiret, sed aedificata potius ad pietatem, ut tantae dignationi gratior fieret! Quanto namque se pro ipsis profundius humiliatam ac funditus exinanitam illa exhibuit majestas, tanto facilius et plenius, si digne de eis sentire volumus, totos eorum affectus sibi illius rapuit et vindicavit charitas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. If we wish to become such as these, brothers, let us again and again go over to Bethlehem, and look more diligently upon this Word made flesh through the immense God, who became a little one, so that in this visible and shortened Word we may learn the wisdom of God, which has become wholly humility. For indeed that all-encompassing power has for the time being wholly given itself over to this one virtue. For the time being that highest Wisdom willed to know nothing else but that very humility, of which it afterward willed to profess itself the teacher. And he indeed\u2014which I say to my own reproach\u2014he, I say, became worthily and justly the teacher appointed to teach humility; who, although he was not ignorant of it, having it as original from his Mother and natural from his Father, nevertheless learned it even from his Mother's womb, from the things which he suffered. He was born in a wayfarers' inn, so that, taught by his example, we might confess ourselves to be guests and pilgrims upon the earth. There also, choosing the lowest place, he was laid in a manger, so that we too might learn by deed that word of David: 'I have chosen to be an outcast in the house of God, rather than to dwell in the tabernacles of sinners' (Psal. LXXXIII, 11). He was wrapped in swaddling clothes, so that we, having only enough to cover us, might be content with that; content in all things with his Mother's poverty, and subject to his Mother in all things, so that the whole pattern of religion might be seen to be already born in his very birth. Blessed indeed was the simple faith of the shepherds, which, when it found the infant wrapped in swaddling clothes, was by no means scandalized by them into unbelief, so as to think less of him, but was rather built up into piety, so as to become more pleasing to so great a condescension! For the more deeply that majesty showed itself humbled and utterly emptied out for their sake, the more easily and fully\u2014if we wish to think worthily of them\u2014did his love seize and claim for itself the whole of their affections.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Fratres, et vos invenietis hodie infantem pannis involutum, et positum in praesepio altaris; videte vilitas tegminis ne offendat aut turbet obtutum vestrae fidei, veritatem reverendi corporis sub aliis rerum formis intuentis. Sicut enim mater Maria quibusdam assumentis pannorum involvit infantem, sic mater gratia dispensatoriis speciebus rerum ejusdem sacri corporis nobis obtegit veritatem; sic etiam mater sapientia aenigmatibus et figuris arcanam divini Verbi contegit majestatem; quatenus et ibi simplicitas fidei, et hic exercitium studii, meritum sibi cumulet ad salutem. Nam ego, fratres, cum vobis veritatem, quae Christus est, verbis meis enuntio, quid aliud quam Christum pannis satis vilibus involvo? Beatus tamen, cui nec in pannis istis Christus est vilior, sicut nec merces pretiosae prudenti vilescunt propter veteres quibus amiciuntur sacculos. Christus plane est, quem vobis tradere cupio, qualibuscunque sermonibus nostris, ut, juxta sermonem Petri apostoli, sanctificetis eum in cordibus vestris (I Petr. III, 15.) Suscipite in mansuetudine insitum verbum, quod potest salvare animas vestras (Jacobi I, 21). Et verbum Christi abundanter habitet in vobis, amor scilicet et memoria Verbi incarnati, quatenus tam feliciter quam fideliter decantetis: Verbum caro factum est, et habitavit in nobis (Joan. I, 14). Tota igitur pietate cogitemus Christum in pannis, quibus Mater eum operuit, ut aeterna felicitate videamus gloriam et decorem quem ei Pater induit, gloriam quasi Unigeniti a Patre, cum quo et cum Spiritu sancto honor et gloria, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-nativity-05-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Brothers, you too will find today an infant wrapped in swaddling clothes, and laid in the manger of the altar; see that the cheapness of the covering does not offend or disturb the gaze of your faith, which beholds the truth of that venerable body under other forms of things. For just as Mother Mary wrapped the infant in some scraps of cloth, so Mother Grace covers for us the truth of that same sacred body with outward forms of things appointed for the dispensation; and so too Mother Wisdom covers the hidden majesty of the divine Word with enigmas and figures; so that in the one case the simplicity of faith, and in the other the exercise of study, may each heap up its own merit unto salvation. For I, brothers, when with my words I announce to you the truth which is Christ, what else am I doing but wrapping Christ in rather cheap swaddling-cloths? Yet blessed is he to whom Christ is not thereby made cheaper, even in such cloths \u2014 just as precious wares do not become worthless in the eyes of a prudent man because of the old sacks in which they are wrapped. It is truly Christ whom I desire to hand over to you, by whatever kind of words of ours, so that, according to the saying of the apostle Peter, you may sanctify him in your hearts (I Petr. III, 15.). Receive with meekness the implanted word, which is able to save your souls (Jacobi I, 21). And let the word of Christ dwell abundantly in you \u2014 that is, the love and remembrance of the incarnate Word \u2014 so that you may sing, as happily as faithfully: The Word was made flesh, and dwelt among us (Joan. I, 14). Let us therefore, with all devotion, think upon Christ in the swaddling-clothes with which his Mother covered him, so that in eternal blessedness we may see the glory and beauty with which the Father has clothed him \u2014 the glory as it were of the Only-Begotten from the Father \u2014 with whom, and with the Holy Spirit, be honor and glory, through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Nativity Sermon V: On God's Wonderful Providence in the Birth of Christ", "topics": ["Nativity", "Word made visible", "silence", "humility", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-nativity-05.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, Dominica in ramis palmarum, sermo I, Quomodo Christus omnia homini factus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 127\u2013130.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "Philippians 2:5\u20136, 8", "Galatians 6:2", "Psalm 23:6 (printed locator; see note)", "Luke 12:47\u201348 (printed locator; see note)", "Mark 7:37", "Isaiah 43:23\u201324", "Luke 22:44", "Isaiah 63:3", "Philippians 2:5"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny\u2019s first Palm Sunday sermon presents Christ\u2019s service as the remedy for human pride. Christ is imagined addressing the disobedient servant, offering to bear his burdens, feed him and redeem him; the meditation then turns to the Passion and a prayer for grace to imitate humility, patience and obedience in the common life. The collection belongs approximately to Guerric\u2019s abbacy in 1138\u20131157, but no individual delivery date is established. This reading follows the 1879 Patrologia Latina edition. The source explicitly names Palm Sunday. Historical polemic is retained and identified in an editorial note."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DOMINICA IN RAMIS PALMARUM."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "PALM SUNDAY", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo Christus omnia homini factus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "How Christ Became All Things for Man", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Hoc sentite in vobis, quod et in Christo Jesu, qui, cum in forma Dei esset (Philipp. ii, 5, 6), etc. Audiat servus nequam et fugitivus, hominem dico, qui, cum esset in natura et conditione servili et necessitate serviendi, servire detrectans, arripere sibi conatus est libertatem et aequalitatem Domini sui. Christus cum in forma Dei esset, non rapina, sed natura aequalis, quia coomnipotens, coaeternus et consubstantialis, exinaniens semetipsum, non solum formam servi accepit, in similitudinem hominum factus; sed etiam ministerium servi implevit, humilians semetipsum, et obediens factus Patri usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis (Ibid. 8). Sed parum tibi videatur quod, cum esset Filius et coaequalis, tanquam servus servivit Patri, si non et servo suo plus quam servus servivit. Factus quidem erat homo, ut serviret Creatori. Et quid justius quam ut servias illi a quo creatus es, sine quo nec esse potes? et quid beatius aut sublimius quam servire illi cui servire regnare est? Non serviam, inquit homo Creatori. Ego igitur serviam tibi, inquit Creator, homini. Tu recumbe; ego ministrabo, ego tibi pedes lavabo. Tu quiesce; ego languores tuos feram, infirmitates portabo. Utere me, ut libet, in omni necessitate tua, non modo ut servo tuo, sed etiam ut jumento tuo, ut peculio tuo. Si fatigatus aut oneratus es, ego te et onus tuum feram, ut legem meam primus impleam. Alter, inquit, alterius onera portate, et sic adimplebitis legem Christi (Galat. vi, 2). Si esuris, aut sitis, et ad manum forte nihil melius habes, nec alter tibi vitulus aeque saginatus praesto est, ecce ego paratus immolari, ut carnes meas comedas, et sanguinem bibas. Nec verearis ex morte servi damna servitii ejus pati; etiam comestus et bibitus, manebo tibi integer et vivus, serviamque ut prius. Si in captivitatem ductus aut venumdatus es, ecce vende me, teque pretio mei, vel me ipso pretio redime. Vile quidem mancipium videor; sed etsi noctu et clanculo distrahar quasi res furtiva, etsi ab avarissimis Judaeorum sacerdotibus emar; attamen saltem triginta argenteos appretiari potero; hoc pretio mei poterit emi sepultura peregrinorum, me pretio vita sepultorum. Si infirmaris, et mori times, ego moriar pro te, ut de sanguine meo tibi conficias medicamenta vitae."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Have this mind in you which was also in Christ Jesus, who, being in the form of God (Philipp. ii, 5, 6), etc. Let the wicked runaway servant hear this\u2014I mean man, who, although by nature and condition a servant and under the necessity of serving, refused to serve and tried to seize for himself the freedom and equality of his Lord. Christ, being in the form of God, equal by nature rather than by robbery, because he is equally almighty, coeternal and consubstantial, emptied himself. He not only took the form of a servant, being made in the likeness of men, but also fulfilled a servant\u2019s ministry, humbling himself and becoming obedient to the Father unto death, even death on a cross (Ibid. 8). Yet think it little that, although the Son and coequal, he served the Father as a servant, unless he also served his own servant more than a servant would. Man had indeed been made to serve the Creator. What could be more just than to serve him by whom you were created, without whom you cannot even exist? And what could be happier or more exalted than to serve him whom to serve is to reign? \u201cI will not serve,\u201d man says to the Creator. \u201cThen I will serve you,\u201d the Creator says to man. \u201cRecline at table; I will minister to you, I will wash your feet. Rest; I will bear your ailments and carry your infirmities. Use me as you please in every need, not only as your servant but also as your beast of burden, as your property. If you are tired or burdened, I will carry you and your burden, so that I may be the first to fulfil my own law. Bear one another\u2019s burdens, he says, and so you will fulfil the law of Christ (Galat. vi, 2). If you are hungry or thirsty and happen to have nothing better at hand, and no other calf as well fattened for you is at hand, here I am, ready to be sacrificed, so that you may eat my flesh and drink my blood. Do not fear that the servant\u2019s death will cost you his service: even when eaten and drunk, I shall remain whole and alive for you, and I shall serve as before. If you have been taken captive or sold, sell me, and redeem yourself with my price, or with myself as the price. I seem a worthless slave; yet even if I am sold by night and in secret as stolen property, even if I am bought by the most avaricious priests of the Jews, I can still be valued at least at thirty pieces of silver. With this price paid for me a burial place for strangers can be bought; with me as the price, life for the buried. If you are sick and afraid to die, I shall die for you, so that from my blood you may prepare medicines of life for yourself.\u201d", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Euge, serve bone et fidelis, servisti revera, servisti in omni fide et veritate, servisti in omni patientia et longanimitate. Non tepide, qui exsultasti ut gigas ad currendam viam (Psal. xxiii, 6) obedientiae non ficte, qui etiam animam post tot et tantos labores superimpendisti; non murmurose, qui, innocens flagellatus, nec os aperuisti. Scriptum est, et justum est: Servus sciens voluntatem domini sui, et non faciens digna, plagis vapulabit multis (Luc. xii, 47, 48). Sed iste servus, obsecro, quid non dignum fecit? quid debuit facere et non fecit? Bene omnia fecit, clamant qui facta ejus observabant, et surdos fecit audire et mutos loqui (Marc. vii, 37). Omnia fecit digna, et quomodo sic omnia passus est indigna? Dorsum posuit ad flagella, plagis vapulavit non paucis nec parvis; indicio sunt sanguinis rivuli qui de tot partibus profluunt corporis. Contumelia et tormento interrogatus fuit, veluti servus aut latro subditus quaestioni, qua confessio extorqueatur criminis. O detestabilem superbiam hominis servire contemnentis, quae alio exemplo non poterat humiliari, nisi servitute et tali servitute Domini sui! et utinam vel sic possit! utinam vel nunc habeat et referat gratiam tantae humilitati ac bonitati! Sed, ut mihi videtur, adhuc audio eumdem Dominum apud Isaiam conquerentem de ingratitudine nequam servi, ubi scilicet ait: Non te servire feci in oblatione, nec laborem tibi praebui in thure. Verum tamen me servire fecisti in peccatis tuis, laborem praebuisti mihi in iniquitatibus tuis (Isai. xliii, 23, 24). Et quem laborem? Usque ad defatigationem, esuriem et sitim; sed usque ad sudorem, sudorem autem sanguinis decurrentis in terram (Luc. xxii, 44); sed usque ad mortem, mortem autem crucis: ne omnia modo replicem, quod alapis caesus, sputis illitus, coronatus spinis, confixus clavis, lancea perforatus, aceto et felle potatus. Hoc torcular, inquit, calcavi solus, et de gentibus non est vir mecum (Isai. lxiii, 3). Vos ergo, qui statis tota die otiosi, attendite et videte si est labor sicut labor meus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Well done, good and faithful servant! You have truly served; you have served with all faithfulness and truth, with all patience and forbearance. Not lukewarmly, for you rejoiced like a giant to run the course (Psal. xxiii, 6) of obedience; not insincerely, for after so many and such great labours you spent your very life besides; not with murmuring, for, though innocent and scourged, you did not even open your mouth. It is written, and it is just: \u201cThe servant who knows his lord\u2019s will and does not act worthily will be beaten with many blows\u201d (Luc. xii, 47, 48). But this servant, I ask you, what did he do that was unworthy? What ought he to have done that he did not do? \u201cHe has done all things well,\u201d cry those who observed his deeds, \u201cand has made the deaf hear and the mute speak\u201d (Marc. vii, 37). He did everything worthily; how, then, did he suffer all these unworthy things? He offered his back to the scourges; he was beaten with blows neither few nor small, as the streams of blood flowing from so many parts of his body bear witness. He was questioned with insult and torture, like a slave or robber subjected to torment to wring out a confession of crime. O detestable pride of man, who disdains to serve! No other example could humble it except the service, and such service, of his own Lord. If only even this could do so! If only even now he would feel and show gratitude for such humility and goodness! Yet I seem still to hear the same Lord complaining in Isaiah about the wicked servant\u2019s ingratitude, where he says: \u201cI did not make you serve me with offerings, nor impose labour on you with incense. Yet you have made me serve with your sins; you have imposed labour on me with your iniquities\u201d (Isai. xliii, 23, 24). And what labour? To the point of exhaustion, hunger and thirst; even to sweat, sweat of blood running down to the ground (Luc. xxii, 44); even to death, death on a cross. I need not now recount everything: how he was struck with blows, smeared with spittle, crowned with thorns, fastened with nails, pierced with a lance, given vinegar and gall to drink. \u201cThis winepress,\u201d he says, \u201cI have trodden alone, and of the nations there is no man with me\u201d (Isai. lxiii, 3). You, then, who stand idle all day, attend and see whether there is any labour like my labour.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Utique valde laborasti, Domine mi, in serviendo mihi; justum profecto et aequum esset ut saltem de caetero tu quiesceres, tuusque tibi servus vel ordine vicis suae, quia ad ipsum ventum est, serviret. Quam magno, Domine mi, inutile tibi meum redemisti servitium, qui nec ministeriis indiges Angelorum! Quam suavi et benigna arte pietatis recuperasti tibi et subdidisti contumacem servum, vincens in bono malum, humilitate confundens superbum, beneficiis obruens ingratum! Sic nimirum, sic sapientia vincit malitiam; sic carbones ignis congessisti super caput contumacis, quibus accenderetur ad poenitentiam. Vicisti ergo, Domine, vicisti rebellem: ecce do manus in vincula tua, jugoque tuo suppono cervicem. Dignare tantum ut tibi serviam, patere ut tibi laborem. Accipe me servum sempiternum, licet inutilem, nisi nunc quoque mecum sit et mecum laboret gratia tua, semper et praeveniens et subsequens. Praeveniat nos, prior ostendens humilitatis et patientiae exempla, subsequatur nos, adjuvans ut imitemur ostensa. Quam felices nos, o fratres mei, si super hoc audiamus consilium Apostoli: Hoc sentite, inquit, in vobis quod et in Christo Jesu (Philipp. ii, 5) praecessisse cognoscitis! hoc est, nullus extollatur supra se, sed magis humilietur infra se; qui major est, aliis serviat; si quis laeditur, prior satisfaciat; in commune usque ad mortem quisque obediat. His vestigiis, fratres, sequemur Christum in forma Dei, in qua vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. You have indeed laboured greatly, my Lord, in serving me. Surely it would be just and right that at least from now on you should rest, and that your servant should serve you, if only because his turn has now come. At what a price, my Lord, you have redeemed my service, useless to you who do not even need the ministries of angels! With what a gentle and kindly art of loving mercy you have won back and subjected your stubborn servant, overcoming evil with good, confounding the proud by humility, overwhelming the ungrateful with benefits! In this way, truly, wisdom conquers malice; in this way you have heaped coals of fire upon the stubborn man\u2019s head, to kindle him to repentance. You have conquered, then, Lord; you have conquered the rebel. Here I offer my hands for your bonds and bow my neck beneath your yoke. Only deign to let me serve you; allow me to labour for you. Receive me as your servant for ever, though a useless one, unless even now your grace is with me and labours with me, always both going before and following after. May it go before us, first showing examples of humility and patience; may it follow us, helping us to imitate what has been shown. How happy we shall be, my brothers, if in this we heed the Apostle\u2019s counsel: \u201cHave this mind in you,\u201d he says, \u201cwhich was also in Christ Jesus\u201d (Philipp. ii, 5), as you know it was in him before you. That is, let no one exalt himself above himself, but rather humble himself beneath himself; let the greater serve the others; if anyone is injured, let him be the first to make amends; let each obey in the common life unto death. In these footsteps, brothers, we shall follow Christ in the form of God, in which he lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The passage about the Jewish priests preserves the sermon\u2019s historical anti-Jewish polemic. It is source rhetoric, not an editorial endorsement. The sacrifice, flesh, blood and continuing life imagery is retained in the translation."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Psalm 23:6 for the running giant. The image is in Psalm 18:6 in historical Vulgate numbering (Psalm 19 in modern numbering). The following Luke 12:47\u201348 reference is also retained; the saying about many blows occurs in verse 47."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon has three numbered sections; the final prayer continues within the third."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "\u201cGoing before and following after\u201d describes grace that initiates and assists our imitation of Christ. Guerric asks the injured person\u2014not the person causing injury\u2014to be first to make amends."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon I: Christ Becomes All Things for Us", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "Passion", "humility", "service", "obedience", "Eucharist", "grace", "repentance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De crucis scientia et virtute., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 130-134.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "1 Corinthians 2:2", "1 Corinthians 1:30", "John 14:30", "Galatians 6:14", "Job 40:18", "Song of Songs 7:8", "4 Kings 2:23 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Psalm 106:2 (historical numbering)", "Ezekiel 9:4\u20137", "Galatians 5:24", "2 Corinthians 7:1", "Psalm 118:120 (historical numbering)", "Numbers 25:4\u20139", "Joshua 10:26", "Numbers 23:10"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric preaches Christ crucified in the days of the Passion. He presents the cross as hidden strength that overcame the devil, and Christ's ascent of the cross as free, triumphant and fruitful for the redeemed. He warns those who bear the sign of the cross falsely and urges the brothers to crucify their vices through the fear of God and to persevere on the cross until evening. His argument includes polemic against Jews. The sermon appears under the Palm Sunday heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De crucis scientia et virtute."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the knowledge and power of the cross.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Paulus, doctor noster in fide et veritate, si hodie veniret ad nos, puto quia non judicaret se aliquid scire inter nos, nisi Jesum Christum, et hunc crucifixum (I Cor. II, 2). In his siquidem diebus in quibus anniversaria dominicae passionis et crucis memoria solemniter celebratur, nihil, ut arbitror, convenientius praedicatur, quam Jesus Christus, et hic crucifixus. Nam et aliis quibuslibet diebus quid unquam potest praedicari fidelius? quid audiri salubrius? quid cogitari fructuosius? Quid enim tam pium fidelium affectibus, tam medicinale moribus; quid sic interficit peccata, crucifigit vitia, virtutes nutrit et roborat, sicut crucifixi memoria? Loquatur ergo Paulus inter perfectos sapientiam in mysterio absconditam; mihi, cujus imperfectum vident etiam oculi hominum, loquatur Christum crucifixum, his quidem qui pereunt stultitiam, mihi autem et his qui salvi fiunt, plane Dei virtutem Deique sapientiam; mihi prorsus altissimam atque nobilissimam philosophiam, per quam infatuatam irrideo tam mundi quam carnis sapientiam. Quam perfectum me putarem, quam provectum in sapientia, si Crucifixi vel idoneus invenirer auditor, qui factus est nobis a Deo non solum sapientia, sed et justitia, et sanctificatio, et redemptio! (Id. I, 30.) Omnino si Christo confixus es cruci, sapiens es, justus es, sanctus es, liber es. An non sapiens, qui, cum Christo exaltatus a terra, sapit et quaerit quae sursum sunt? an non justus, in quo destructum est corpus peccati, ut ultra non serviat peccato? an non sanctus, qui semetipsum exhibuit hostiam vivam, sanctam, placentem Deo? an non vere liber, quem Filius liberavit, qui de libertate conscientiae illam liberam vocem Filii sibi assumere confidit: Venit princeps mundi hujus, et in me non habet quidquam? (Joan. XIV, 30.) Vere apud Crucifixum misericordia, et copiosa apud eum redemptio, qui sic redimit Israel ex omnibus iniquitatibus ejus, ut principis hujus mundi calumnias liber evadere mereatur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Paul, our teacher in faith and truth, if he came to us today, I think he would judge that he knew nothing among us except Jesus Christ, and him crucified (I Cor. II, 2). Indeed, in these days in which the yearly memory of the Lord's passion and cross is solemnly celebrated, nothing, I think, is more fittingly preached than Jesus Christ, and him crucified. For on any other days as well, what can ever be preached more faithfully? What heard more wholesomely? What thought upon more fruitfully? For what is so pious for the affections of the faithful, so medicinal for morals; what so kills sins, crucifies vices, nourishes and strengthens virtues, as the memory of the Crucified One? Let Paul, then, speak wisdom hidden in mystery among the perfect; to me, whose imperfection even the eyes of men can see, let him speak of Christ crucified \u2014 to those indeed who perish, foolishness, but to me and to those who are being saved, plainly the power of God and the wisdom of God; to me indeed the loftiest and most noble philosophy, by which, having made foolish the wisdom of both world and flesh, I laugh it to scorn. How perfect I would think myself, how advanced in wisdom, if I were found a fit hearer of the Crucified, who was made for us by God not only wisdom, but also righteousness, and sanctification, and redemption! (Id. I, 30.) Altogether, if you have been nailed with Christ to the cross, you are wise, you are righteous, you are holy, you are free. Is he not wise who, lifted up from the earth with Christ, savors and seeks the things that are above? Is he not righteous in whom the body of sin has been destroyed, so that he no longer serves sin? Is he not holy who has presented himself a living sacrifice, holy, pleasing to God? Is he not truly free whom the Son has set free, who from the freedom of conscience dares to take up for himself that free utterance of the Son: The prince of this world is coming, and he has nothing in me? (Joan. XIV, 30.) Truly with the Crucified is mercy, and with him abundant redemption, who so redeems Israel from all his iniquities that he deserves to escape free from the slanders of the prince of this world.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Sciat tamen, sciat quicunque est ille beatus et verus Israel, non esse hoc suae meritum perfectionis, sed divinae gratiam redemptionis; non scilicet quia peccatum non fecerit, nec inventus sit dolus in ore ejus, sed quia ille cujus haec laus propria est, Christus scilicet, purgavit peccata illius, qui per sanguinem crucis suae purgationem peccatorum faciens, ibi maxime triumphavit principatus et potestates, ubi abscondita erat fortitudo ejus. Erat abscondita, sed non perdita; quia crucifixus ex infirmitate, vivebat ex virtute Dei. Erat occulta, sed non erat otiosa; quia crucifixus veterem hominem in omnibus electis crucifigebat. Crucifigebat mundum Paulo, et Paulum mundo (Galat. VI, 14). Crucifigebat denique tyrannum hujus mundi, omnesque suae antiquae ministros tyrannidis. Hamum plane sub esca recondebat, qui sub infirmitate fortitudinem abscondebat. Ac perinde ille homicida ab initio sitiens humanum sanguinem, dum irruit in infirmitatem, incidit in virtutem: morsus dum momordit, confixus dum Crucifixum appetivit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Yet let him know, let whoever is that blessed and true Israel know, that this is not the merit of his own perfection, but the grace of divine redemption; not indeed because he has committed no sin, nor has guile been found in his mouth, but because he to whom this praise properly belongs, namely Christ, has purged his sins \u2014 he who, by making purgation of sins through the blood of his cross, triumphed over principalities and powers precisely there where his strength was hidden. It was hidden, but not lost; for, crucified out of weakness, he lived by the power of God. It was concealed, but not idle; for the Crucified One was crucifying the old man in all the elect. He was crucifying the world to Paul, and Paul to the world (Galat. VI, 14). He was crucifying, in short, the tyrant of this world, and all the ministers of his ancient tyranny. He was plainly hiding the hook beneath the bait, he who was hiding strength beneath weakness. And accordingly that murderer from the beginning, thirsting for human blood, while he rushed upon weakness, fell into power: biting, he was bitten; and while he sought to seize the Crucified, he was himself pierced.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Gratias cruci et clavis tuis, Domine Jesu: video perforatas fauces illius draconis, ut transeant liberati, qui etiam fuerant absorpti. Et qui fiduciam habebat quod influeret Jordanis in os ejus (Job. XL, 18), fluvium, quem absorbuerat, se non minima ex parte perdidisse frendens irascitur. De illis quippe faucibus nobis isti venerunt qui hodie nobiscum nobilem ac magnificum crucis decantant triumphum. Plane de ore leonis liberati sunt, imo de ventre inferi regressi sunt. Irascatur ergo, fremat et tabescat de cujus dentibus praeda erepta est: nam Christus gratulabitur quod non gratis crucifixus est. Lugeat infernus et mors; ille morsus, ista mortua: nam coeli laetantur, et exsultat Ecclesia, quod Christus infernum spoliat, mortemque triumphat. In conversione nempe istorum triumphum suae renovavit passionis, crucisque miracula suscitavit. In istis crux refloruit, hunc fructum pretiosum nunc etiam arbor vitae tulit. Quomodo namque maneret sterilis, quae non modo rigata, sed etiam vivifica facta est sanguine Salvatoris? jam non poenitebit eum quod in palmam ascendit, cum tantum tamque pretiosum de ea fructum collegerit. Hunc quippe fructum inter alios praevidebat, cum voluntarius ad crucem properabat. Dixi, inquit, ascendam in palmam, et apprehendam fructus ejus (Cantic. VII, 8). Paucis absolvit quod voluntate passus sit, quod passione exaltatus sit, quod non sine fructu nostro passus sit. In dicto siquidem proponentis, arbitrii libertas; in ascensu, triumphi sublimitas; in apprehensione fructuum, redemptionis monstratur utilitas. Vos, insensati Judaei, clamabatis quidem, Ascende, calve; ascende, calve (IV Reg. II, 23); sed ordinationi ejus liberrimae, qui sponte decreverat ascendere, non potuit furor vester nisi servire. Ascendit crucem voluntate, triumphavit in ea potestate, fructum ex ea collegit pietate. Uno igitur opere et Judaeum irrisit, et diabolum peremit, atque christianum redemit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Thanks be to your cross and your nails, Lord Jesus: I see the jaws of that dragon pierced through, so that they who had even been swallowed up might pass through, now set free. And he who had confidence that the Jordan would flow into his mouth (Job. XL, 18) now, gnashing his teeth, is enraged that he has lost no small part of the river he had swallowed. For from those jaws came to us these who today with us sing the noble and magnificent triumph of the cross. Plainly they have been freed from the mouth of the lion, indeed they have returned from the belly of hell. Let him then be enraged, let him gnash and waste away, from whose teeth the prey has been snatched away: for Christ will rejoice that he was not crucified in vain. Let hell and death mourn; the one bitten, the other dead: for the heavens rejoice, and the Church exults, that Christ despoils hell and triumphs over death. In the conversion of these, indeed, he has renewed the triumph of his passion, and raised up again the miracles of the cross. In these the cross has flowered again; this precious fruit the tree of life has borne even now. For how could it remain barren, which has been not only watered, but even made life-giving by the blood of the Savior? Now he will not regret that he climbed up into the palm tree, since he has gathered from it so great and so precious a fruit. For indeed he foresaw this fruit among others when, of his own will, he hastened to the cross. I said, he says, I will climb up into the palm tree, and I will take hold of its fruits (Cantic. VII, 8). In a few words he sums up that he suffered willingly, that he was exalted by his passion, that he did not suffer without fruit for us. For in the saying of one proposing, the freedom of will is shown; in the climbing, the sublimity of triumph; in the taking hold of the fruits, the usefulness of redemption is displayed. You, senseless Jews, cried out indeed, Go up, you baldhead; go up, you baldhead (IV Reg. II, 23); but your rage could do nothing but serve the most free ordinance of him who of his own accord had decreed to go up. He climbed the cross by his will, he triumphed on it by his power, he gathered fruit from it by his loving-kindness. By one and the same work, then, he mocked the Jew, destroyed the devil, and redeemed the Christian.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Dicant igitur qui redempti sunt a Domino, quos redemit de manu inimici, et de regionibus congregavit eos (Psal. CVI, 2); dicant, inquam, voce et mente magistri sui: Mihi autem absit gloriari, nisi in cruce Domini nostri Jesu Cristi (Galat. VI, 14)! in qua sapientia Dei nequam infatuavit consilium, justitia destruxit eum qui habebat mortis imperium, misericordia liberavit captivum. Merito prorsus, o sapiens gloriator, gloriaberis in cruce Domini tui, cujus liberatus es triumpho, vivificatus mysterio, justificatus exemplo, munitus signo. Sic enim justae rationis consequentia postulare videtur, ut exemplum Crucifixi ad justificationem suam imprimant moribus, qui signum crucis ad munimen sui imprimunt frontibus, et illius vivant lege, cujus armantur fide. Alioquin fallaciter miles characterem regis gestat, cujus praescriptum non observat; nec recte se tuetur illius signo, cujus non paret imperio. Vide autem quanta perversitas et abusio sit, ut inimici crucis Christi tueri se velint signo crucis Christi: dum securi sunt, luxuriantes contra pietatem crucis; cum in periculo deprehensi sunt, volentes esse defensi virtute crucis. Plane inimici crucis Christi sunt, amici ventris sui; quorum deus venter est, quorum idolum nummus est. Sciat tamen, sciat quicunque nunc fallaciter sibi Crucifixi signum usurpat; nequaquam se illo tueri poterit in illa necessitate suprema, quando jam non arbitrio hominum, sed judicio et ministerio Angelorum signabitur Thau super frontes virorum gementium et dolentium, ut discernantur salvandi a multitudine perditorum (Ezech. IX, 4-7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let those therefore say who have been redeemed by the Lord, whom he redeemed from the hand of the enemy, and gathered out of the regions (Psal. CVI, 2); let them say, I say, with the voice and mind of their teacher: But far be it from me to glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ (Galat. VI, 14)! In which the wisdom of God made foolish the counsel of the wicked one, justice destroyed him who had the empire of death, mercy freed the captive. Most deservedly indeed, O wise boaster, will you glory in the cross of your Lord, by whose triumph you were set free, by whose mystery you were given life, by whose example you were justified, by whose sign you were fortified. For thus the sequence of right reason seems to require: that those who imprint the sign of the cross on their foreheads for their protection should also imprint the example of the Crucified on their character for their justification, and should live by the law of him by whose faith they are armed. Otherwise a soldier falsely bears the mark of a king whose decree he does not observe; nor does he rightly protect himself by the sign of him whose command he does not obey. But see what great perversity and abuse it is, that enemies of the cross of Christ should wish to protect themselves by the sign of the cross of Christ: while they are secure, living wantonly against the piety of the cross; when they are caught in danger, wishing to be defended by the power of the cross. Plainly they are enemies of the cross of Christ, friends of their own belly; whose god is the belly, whose idol is money. Let him know, however, let whoever now falsely usurps to himself the sign of the Crucified know this: he will by no means be able to protect himself by it in that supreme necessity, when no longer at the discretion of men, but by the judgment and ministry of Angels, the Thau will be marked upon the foreheads of men who groan and grieve, so that those to be saved may be distinguished from the multitude of the lost (Ezech. IX, 4-7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed et Paulus, dux ille strenuus militiae christianae, fidelis signifer, qui stigmata Crucifixi in corpore suo portabat, nunc quoque in hac verorum ac falsorum militum confusione, signo utique notabili istos ab illis discern[e]bat, cum dicebat: Qui autem sunt Christi, carnem crucifixerunt cum vitiis et concupiscentiis (Galat. V, 24). Cauta prorsus et circumspecta definitio, ac velut formam referens de ipsius veritatis exemplari impresso. Nimirum quod impressum retinet vita, signantius exprimit lingua. Qui ergo Christo confixus erat cruci, de propriae conscientiae exemplari formam istam protulit: Qui Christi sunt, carnem crucifixerunt cum vitiis et concupiscentiis. Noverat vir multae scientiae et experientiae, multos esse aut fore qui crucifigerent concupiscentias carnis, et sinerent regnare vitia cordis; multos e contrario qui, fiducia pacati cordis, negligerent mortificationem corporis. Sed sicut nonnunquam divina justitia spiritum sibi non subditum, contumeliis carnis licet afflictae flagellat, sic plerumque corpus incrassatum recalcitrat, et nova bella spiritui jam quieto resuscitat. Eapropter vult Apostolus ut, crucifixis tam vitiis interioribus quam concupiscentiis exterioribus, mundemus nos ab omni inquinamento carnis et spiritus, perficientes sanctimoniam in timore Dei (II Cor. VII, 1). Timor nempe Dei, velut quidam clavi in altum defixi, cruci nos configit, et velut affixus justitiae continere nos facit, ut non exhibeamus membra nostra arma iniquitatis, sed justitiae; et, licet sit, non tamen regnet peccatum in nostro mortali corpore. Sane quod timor Dei comparetur configentibus clavis, sententia est David, qui ait: Confige, quasi a clavis, timore tuo carnes meas; a judiciis enim tuis timui (Psal. CXVIII, 120). Si ergo nondum praevales ut vitia exstinguas, in hoc vult te esse sollicitum Apostolus, ut crucifigas. Non ait: Qui Christi sunt, exstinxerunt vitia, quae virtus paucorum est; sed, crucifixerunt, sine quo salus nulla est, sicut, praeter crucem Christi, nulla redemptio est. Ideo namque Redemptor ad operandam et informandam salutem nostram hunc passionis modum elegit, ut redemptionis sacramentum, justificationis fieret exemplum; ut quomodo videlicet ipse similitudinem carnis peccati crucifixit, de peccato damnans peccatum, sic nos, imo multo magis nos, carnem peccatricem cruciemus, crucifigentes, etsi nondum exstinguentes in ea peccatum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But Paul too, that vigorous leader of the Christian army, that faithful standard-bearer, who bore the marks of the Crucified in his own body, now also, in this confusion of true and false soldiers, distinguished the one from the other by a quite notable sign, when he said: But those who are Christ's have crucified the flesh with its vices and desires (Galat. V, 24). A truly cautious and circumspect definition, and one that, as it were, reproduces the form impressed from the very model of truth itself. For indeed, what life retains as impressed, the tongue expresses the more pointedly. He, then, who had been fastened with Christ to the cross, brought forth this form from the model of his own conscience: Those who are Christ's have crucified the flesh with its vices and desires. This man of great knowledge and experience knew that there are, or will be, many who crucify the desires of the flesh and yet allow the vices of the heart to reign; and many others, on the contrary, who, trusting in a heart at peace, neglect the mortification of the body. But just as divine justice sometimes scourges a spirit not subject to itself with the reproaches of the flesh, even though the flesh be already afflicted, so too the body, once fattened, often grows restive, and stirs up new wars against a spirit already at rest. For this reason the Apostle wishes that, with both the inner vices and the outer desires crucified, we should cleanse ourselves from all defilement of flesh and spirit, perfecting holiness in the fear of God (II Cor. VII, 1). For indeed the fear of God, like certain nails driven deep, fastens us to the cross, as though fixed to righteousness, makes us hold ourselves in check, so that we do not present our members as weapons of iniquity, but of righteousness; and, though sin be present, it does not reign in our mortal body. Indeed, that the fear of God should be compared to nails that fasten us is the saying of David, who says: Pierce my flesh, as with nails, with your fear; for I have feared your judgments (Psal. CXVIII, 120). If, then, you are not yet able to extinguish your vices, the Apostle wishes you to be anxious about this at least: that you crucify them. He does not say: Those who are Christ's have extinguished their vices \u2014 which is the virtue of few \u2014 but, have crucified them, without which there is no salvation, just as, apart from the cross of Christ, there is no redemption. For this is why the Redeemer chose this manner of suffering for working out and shaping our salvation: that the sacrament of redemption might become the pattern of justification; so that, just as he himself crucified the likeness of sinful flesh, for sin condemning sin, so we \u2014 indeed, all the more we \u2014 should torment our sinful flesh, crucifying it, even if we do not yet extinguish sin within it.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Hic recordari potes quia Moyses ad placandam iram Domini crucifixit principes Israelitarum (Num. XXV, 4-9); Josue autem, qui et Jesus, quinque reges Amorrhaeorum (Josue X, 26). Si enim iram Domini, quam meruimus, placare volumus, necesse est ut per continentiam nos cruciemus. Jesus autem noster, qui nos in terram promissionis introducturus est, vitia quinque sensuum in nobis crucifiget, imo et exstinguet; si tamen, sicut est justum, pendeamus in patibulis usque ad vesperum. Hujus enim perseverantiae in cruce Salvator de se ipso tibi curavit exemplum praebere, nolens consummari nisi in cruce, aut deponi de cruce ante vesperum tam diei ipsius quam vitae suae. Balaam dicebat, Moriatur anima mea morte justorum (Num. XXIII, 10); tu vero dic, Moriatur anima mea morte Domini mei Jesu Christi, et fiant novissima mea illius similia; ut videlicet in voluntaria cruce poenitentiae pendere merear usque ad terminum vitae. Quanta fiducia de cruce Filii, in manus Patris spiritum commendabis! imo quanta clementia Pater suscipiet quem Filius commendabit (8)! Filius namque qui causam animae tuae semel in cruce suscepit agendam, nunquam eam agere desinit, apud Patrem semper interpellans. Vade securus, vade gratulabundus, ubi judex tuus est advocatus tuus; tantummodo signum crucis, mortificationem Jesu, quam circumfers in corpore tuo, secum deferat spiritus tuus. Dominus autem gloriae, qui pro vobis, fratres, passus, in vobis est glorificatus, dignetur vos habere socios passionis et gloriae, et gloriantes in cruce glorificet in ea claritate quam habuit apud Patrem ante initium saeculorum, et habiturus est per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Here you may recall that Moses, to appease the wrath of the Lord, crucified the princes of the Israelites (Num. XXV, 4-9); and Joshua\u2014who is also called Jesus\u2014crucified the five kings of the Amorites (Josue X, 26). For if we wish to appease the wrath of the Lord, which we have deserved, it is necessary that we crucify ourselves through self-restraint. But our Jesus, who is to lead us into the land of promise, will crucify in us the vices of the five senses\u2014indeed, will even extinguish them\u2014provided only that, as is right, we hang upon the gibbets until evening. For of this perseverance on the cross the Savior took care to offer you an example in his own person, unwilling to be consummated except on the cross, or to be taken down from the cross before the evening both of that very day and of his life. Balaam said, 'Let my soul die the death of the righteous' (Num. XXIII, 10); but you should say, 'Let my soul die the death of my Lord Jesus Christ, and let my last end be like his'\u2014that is, that I may merit to hang upon the voluntary cross of penance even to the end of life. With what confidence, from the cross of the Son, will you commend your spirit into the hands of the Father! Indeed, with what tenderness will the Father receive him whom the Son commends! (8) For the Son, who once on the cross undertook to plead the cause of your soul, never ceases to plead it, interceding always before the Father. Go in security, go rejoicing, where your judge is your advocate; only let your spirit carry with it the sign of the cross, the mortification of Jesus, which you bear about in your body. And may the Lord of glory, who suffered for you, brothers, and was glorified in you, deign to count you companions of his passion and his glory, and, as you glory in the cross, glorify you in that splendor which he had with the Father before the beginning of the ages, and which he will have through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-n008", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(8) Vide similia sup. serm. 1 de Adventu, circ. fin., item apud S. Bernard., serm. 3 pasch., n. 3 et 8."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-n008", "label": "English", "text": "(8) See similar passages above in Sermon 1 for Advent, near the end; likewise in St. Bernard, Sermon 3 for Easter, nn. 3 and 8.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The supplied e in discern[e]bat represents damaged print."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon II", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "Passion", "cross", "redemption", "devil", "fear of God", "mortification", "perseverance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De duplici specie qua Christus proponitur spectandus., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 134-137.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "Psalm 68:21 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 52:14", "Matthew 21:10", "Psalm 87:16 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 68:8 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 50:7", "Psalm 30:2 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 17:18", "Matthew 21:9", "Matthew 26:66", "James 5:11", "Job 16:18", "Matthew 27:46", "Luke 16:25", "Matthew 24:30", "John 6:15", "Matthew 21:12\u201313", "Sirach 27:12", "Ecclesiastes 8:1", "Proverbs 16:15", "Genesis 1:27", "Luke 22:61\u201362", "Job 25:3", "2 Corinthians 6:14", "Psalm 118:74 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric sets the procession and the Passion side by side: Christ honored as king and Christ condemned as a thief. He hears the Son's cry of abandonment and answers it with the future glory that the procession prefigured. Christ's meekness when honored and his zeal in cleansing the temple form a pattern for those in authority; his unchanged mind amid changed fortunes is set before the brothers. His argument includes polemic against Christ's Jewish opponents. The sermon appears under the Palm Sunday heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De duplici specie qua Christus proponitur spectandus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the twofold appearance under which Christ is set before us to be beheld.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Desiderium animae nostrae, speciosum forma prae filiis hominum, hodierna dies alia et alia specie repraesentat filiis hominum: utraque quidem specie spectabilem, in utraque desiderabilem et amabilem, quia in utraque salvatorem hominum; licet in una sublimem, in altera humilem; in ista gloriosum, in illa aerumnosum; in ista venerabilem, in illa miserabilem: si tamen dici decet miserabilis, qui miseriam suscepit dignatione miserationis, ut de miseria sua misericordiam praestaret miseris; non ut qui sibi beatitudo est, misericordiam a miseris flagitaret. Ubi ergo miserandus videri voluit, ibi et amplius venerandus fuit. Sed sustinui, inquit, qui simul contristaretur, et non fuit; qui consolaretur, et non inveni (Psal. LXVIII, 21). Ita qui miseratione omnium miser ultro fieri voluit, apud nullum fere miserationem invenit. Sed ubi, inquies, spectatur hodie sublimis et gloriosus, ubi humilis et aerumnosus? Vide processionem; audi passionem. In his evidenter agnoscere poteris quod Isaias ait: Sicut obstupuerunt super eum multi, sic inglorius erit inter viros vultus ejus, et forma ejus inter filios hominum (Isai. LII, 14). Obstupuerunt multi de gloria quasi victoris triumphantis, cum Jerusalem ingrederetur; sed nihilominus paulo post vultus ejus inglorius et despectus, cum pateretur. Cum intrasset Jerosolymam, ait Matthaeus, commota est universa civitas, dicens: Quis est hic? (Matth. XXI, 10). Cum pateretur, confusio faciem ejus operuit, ut vere confiteatur: Exaltatus, humiliatus sum et confusus (Psal. LXXXVII, 16). Sane quod hic se confusum dicit, intelligendum est secundum hoc quod alibi dicit de facie corporis: Operuit confusio faciem meam (Psal. LXVIII, 8); quia non pepercerunt in faciem meam spuere, velare, percutere et illudere. Nam facies mentis quae semper et immobiliter habitabat cum vultu Dei, nec turbari poterat nec confundi. Dominus Deus, inquit, auxiliator meus; ideo non sum confusus; ideo posui faciem meam ut petram durissimam, et scio quoniam non confundar (Isai. L, 7). In te enim, Domine, speravi, non confundar in aeternum (Psal. XXX, 2). Confundantur potius illi, et non confundar ego; paveant, et non paveam ego (Jerem. XVII, 18)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. The desire of our soul, beautiful in form beyond the sons of men, today's feast presents to the sons of men under one appearance and another: in each appearance indeed worthy of beholding, in each desirable and lovable, because in each he is the Savior of men; although in one sublime, in the other humble; in this one glorious, in that one full of hardship; in this one venerable, in that one pitiable\u2014if indeed it is fitting to call pitiable him who took on wretchedness by the condescension of his compassion, so that out of his own misery he might show mercy to the wretched; not as one who, being blessedness itself, should beg for mercy from the wretched. Therefore where he wished to appear pitiable, there he was all the more to be venerated. But I waited, he says, for one who would grieve with me, and there was none; for one who would console me, and I found none (Psal. LXVIII, 21). Thus he who willingly chose to become wretched out of compassion for all found compassion from almost no one. But where, you will ask, is he beheld today as sublime and glorious, and where as humble and full of hardship? Look at the procession; listen to the Passion. In these you will be able clearly to recognize what Isaiah says: As many were astonished at him, so his countenance shall be without glory among men, and his appearance among the sons of men (Isai. LII, 14). Many were astonished at the glory, as of a triumphant victor, when he entered Jerusalem; but nonetheless, a little afterward his countenance was without glory and despised, when he suffered. When he had entered Jerusalem, says Matthew, the whole city was stirred, saying: Who is this? (Matth. XXI, 10). When he suffered, confusion covered his face, so that he might truly confess: I was exalted, I have been humbled and confounded (Psal. LXXXVII, 16). Indeed, when he here says he was confounded, it must be understood according to what he says elsewhere about the face of his body: Confusion has covered my face (Psal. LXVIII, 8); because they did not spare to spit upon my face, to cover it, to strike it, and to mock it. For the face of his mind, which dwelt always and unchangeably with the countenance of God, could neither be disturbed nor confounded. The Lord God, he says, is my helper; therefore I am not confounded; therefore I have set my face like the hardest rock, and I know that I shall not be confounded (Isai. L, 7). For in thee, O Lord, have I hoped; let me never be confounded (Psal. XXX, 2). Let them rather be confounded, and let me not be confounded; let them fear, and let me not fear (Jerem. XVII, 18).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si igitur, ut dicere coeperam, hodierna processio simul et passio considerentur, ibi plane videtur Jesus sublimis et gloriosus, hic humilis et aerumnosus. In processione namque cogitatur in honore regis; in passione spectatur in poena latronis. Ibi circumdant eum gloria et honor; hic non est ei species neque decor. Ibi gaudium hominum et gloria plebis; hic opprobrium hominum et abjectio plebis. Ibi acclamatur ei: Hosanna filio David, benedictus qui venit rex Israel (Matth. XXI, 9); hic inclamatur, reus mortis (Id. XXVI, 66); subsannaturque quod se fecerit regem Israel. Ibi cum ramis palmarum ei occurritur; hic alapis palmarum in faciem caeditur, et arundine percutitur caput. Ibi praeconiis sublimatur; hic saturatur opprobriis. Ibi certatim ei via sternitur vestimentis alienis; hic exuitur propriis. Ibi tanquam rex justus et salvator suscipitur in Jerusalem; hic tanquam reus et seductor damnatus ejicitur de Jerusalem. Ibi sedet in asino stipatus obsequiis; hic pendet in ligno crucis caesus flagellis, confossus plagis, et desertus a suis. Ecce plus quam Job hic, cui tam repente, tam vehementer omnia semel in contrarium Deus vertit. Sufferentiam Job audistis, finem Domini vidistis (Jacobi V, 11), ait Jacobus apostolus. Ac si diceret: Sufferentia Job fuit usque ad reditum substantiae; sufferentia Domini usque ad exitum vitae. Job quidem patienter damna sustinuit, sed mox in terra sua duplicia suscepit; Christus ut erat repletus miseriis, et inebriatus amaritudinibus, e mundo migravit. Itaque plus quam Job hic, qui de summa, ut putabatur, repente semelque dejectus felicitate, in extrema atque gravissima finem sortitus est calamitate. Et haec, inquit, passus sum absque iniquitate manus meae, cum haberem mundas ad Deum preces (Job XVI, 18), etiam pro crucifixoribus, ut eis ignosceret."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If, then, as I began to say, today's procession and Passion are considered together, there Jesus is plainly seen as sublime and glorious, here as humble and full of hardship. For in the procession he is regarded with the honor of a king; in the Passion he is beheld with the punishment of a thief. There glory and honor surround him; here there is in him no comeliness nor beauty. There is the joy of men and the glory of the people; here the reproach of men and the outcast of the people. There he is acclaimed: Hosanna to the son of David, blessed is he who comes, the king of Israel (Matth. XXI, 9); here it is cried out against him that he is guilty of death (Id. XXVI, 66); and he is mocked for having made himself king of Israel. There he is met with branches of palms; here he is struck in the face with blows of the palm of the hand, and his head is beaten with a reed. There he is exalted with acclamations; here he is filled with reproaches. There the way is strewn before him eagerly with others' garments; here he is stripped of his own. There he is received into Jerusalem as a just king and savior; here he is cast out of Jerusalem as a condemned criminal and deceiver. There he sits upon an ass surrounded by homage; here he hangs on the wood of the cross, scourged with lashes, pierced through with wounds, and abandoned by his own. Behold, here is one greater than Job, for whom God so suddenly, so violently, turned all things at once into their opposite. You have heard of the patience of Job, you have seen the end of the Lord (Jacobi V, 11), says the apostle James. As if he said: The patience of Job lasted until the return of his possessions; the patience of the Lord lasted until the end of his life. Job indeed patiently bore his losses, but soon received double in his own land; Christ, filled as he was with miseries and made drunk with bitterness, departed from the world. Therefore here is one greater than Job, who, cast down suddenly and once for all, as it seemed, from the summit of happiness, met his end in the utmost and gravest calamity. And these things, he says, I have suffered without iniquity of my hand, since I had pure prayers to offer to God (Job XVI, 18), even on behalf of those who crucified me, that he might forgive them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. An non igitur recte videbitur velut turbatus tanta tamque subita rerum mutatione Filius Patrem interpellare illa voce lacrymabili: Deus, Deus meus, utquid dereliquisti me? (Matth. XXVII, 46). Certe manus tuae fecerunt me! in voluntate tua deduxisti me, et paulo ante cum gloria suscepisti me; et sic repente praecipitas me? Elevans allisisti me, quasi super ventum ponens me, et elidens valde. Exaltatus, humiliatus sum et confusus; et eo confusus amplius, quo tunc exaltatus sublimius, et nunc humiliatus profundius; quantoque tunc altius elevatus, tanto nunc gravius allisus. Justum quidem est, o Pater, ut qui se exaltat humilietur, et elatio indigni digna dejectione puniatur; sed nunquid similiter justum erat ut, quem tu exaltaveras, ita confusibiliter humiliaretur, et gloriam quam humilitas meruerat, tanta sequeretur contumelia? Nunquid mihi, Pater, iratus fuisti, quod vel ad horam hic me passus sum honorari; et illud etiam perexiguum quod susceperam de bonis hujus mundi, exsolvendum erat ante mortem in poenis et contumeliis, ne postea mihi posset objici: Fili, suscepisti bona in vita tua? (Luc. XVI, 25). At qui tuus erat honor, o Pater, honor erat Filii; quia qui non honorificat Filium, non honorificat Patrem qui misit illum. Ecce exprobrant inimici tui, Domine, exprobrant commutationem Christi tui; ecce gloriantur in contumelia mea et tormento, qui paulo ante cruciabantur gloria et honore meo. Nunquid non respondebo exprobrantibus mihi verbum, ut sciant aliquando qua dispensatione commutaveris Christum tuum, quo consilio sic exaltaveris mox humiliandum, vel humiliaveris exaltatum?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Will it not, then, rightly seem that the Son, as though troubled by so great and so sudden a change of circumstances, cries out to the Father with that tearful voice: \"God, my God, why have you forsaken me?\" (Matth. XXVII, 46). Surely your hands made me! By your will you led me, and but a little while ago you received me with glory; and do you now so suddenly cast me down? Lifting me up, you have dashed me down, setting me as it were upon the wind, and shattering me exceedingly. Exalted, I have been humbled and confounded; and the more confounded, the more sublimely I was then exalted, and the more deeply I am now humbled; and the higher I was then lifted up, the more grievously am I now dashed down. It is indeed just, O Father, that whoever exalts himself should be humbled, and that the pride of the unworthy should be punished with a fitting downfall; but was it likewise just that he whom you had exalted should be so shamefully humbled, and that such great contempt should follow the glory which humility had merited? Were you, Father, angry with me, because I allowed myself to be honored here even for an hour; and was even that very small portion which I had received of the goods of this world to be paid back before death in sufferings and insults, lest afterward it might be objected to me: \"Son, you received good things in your life\" (Luc. XVI, 25)? But the honor that was yours, O Father, was the honor of the Son; for he who does not honor the Son does not honor the Father who sent him. Behold, your enemies, Lord, reproach\u2014they reproach the changed state of your Christ; behold, they glory in my contempt and torment, who but a little while ago were tormented by my glory and honor. Shall I not then answer those who reproach me with a word, that they may know at last by what dispensation you have changed your Christ, by what counsel you have thus exalted one soon to be humbled, or humbled one who was exalted?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Respondebis, o Domine Jesu, respondebis exprobrantibus tibi verbum, et verbum asperum, quando scilicet respondebit eis in facie arrogantia eorum, et arguet eos malitia eorum, videntes in quem transfixerunt. Cum enim videbunt Filium hominis venientem in nubibus cum potestate magna et majestate (Matth. XXIV, 30), tunc scient quod modo nolunt credere, quia illius posterioris gloriae, prior hodiernae processionis gloria, mysterium fuerit et figura; sicut passionis contumelia, meritum et causa. Tunc intelligent quia Christus nunc honoratus, nunc ludibrio habitus, his qui pereunt positus est in offensionem et ruinam, sicut his qui salvi fiunt, in resurrectionem et doctrinam; quia quod sacramentum est redemptionis, simul est etiam documentum aedificationis. Sic enim postulabant merita superborum, ut eorum superbiae et honor Christi fieret scandalum, et mors praecipitium; atque invidiam juste pereuntium gloria triumphantis excitaret, morientis poena damnaret. Caeterum his qui salvi fiunt, id est nobis, omnino erat necessarium ut Christus, transiens viam hujus mundi, sequentibus se viam sterneret, sicut per adversa, sic etiam per prospera; atque prius exaltatus, post humiliatus, suo traderet exemplo qualiter modestia servaretur in honore, patientia teneretur in contumelia vel dolore. Ipse siquidem honorari potuit, inflari non potuit; voluit despici, sed nescivit aut pusillanimitate dejici, aut ira morderi. Sicut enim alias cum vellent eum rapere et facere regem, ipse refugiens, et in solitudinem ad orandum aufugiens (Joan. VI, 15), docuit exemplo quod dixit et verbo, ne scilicet velimus in sublime tolli; sic modo quadam alia dispensatione sese ad horam honorari patiens, et nihilominus solitam, quia inolitam, mansuetudinem in honore retinens, formam dabat his qui in potestate sunt constituti. Qui tamen ut discerent ea discretione per humilitatem esse mansueti, ut, cum res postularet, per zelum essent erecti, Dominus statim in templum regressus, flagello facto de funiculis vindicat injurias Patris, magisque eligit furorem sacerdotum in necem suam provocare, quam templi profanationem dissimulare (Matth. XXI, 12, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. You will answer, O Lord Jesus, you will answer those who reproach you with a word\u2014and a harsh word\u2014namely, when their own arrogance shall answer them to their face, and their own malice shall accuse them, as they see him whom they pierced. For when they shall see the Son of man coming in the clouds with great power and majesty (Matth. XXIV, 30), then they will know what they now refuse to believe: that the earlier glory of today's procession was the mystery and figure of that later glory, just as the contempt of the Passion was its merit and cause. Then they will understand that Christ, now honored, now held up to mockery, has been set, for those who perish, as a stumbling block and a ruin, just as for those who are being saved he has been set for resurrection and instruction; because that which is a sacrament of redemption is at the same time also a lesson for edification. For thus the deserts of the proud demanded: that for their pride even the honor of Christ should become a scandal, and his death a stumbling-block to ruin; and that the glory of the triumphant one should arouse the envy of those who are justly perishing, while the punishment of the dying one should condemn that envy. But for those who are being saved, that is, for us, it was altogether necessary that Christ, passing along the way of this world, should pave the way for those who follow him, as much through adversity as through prosperity; and that, first exalted and afterward humbled, he should hand down by his own example how modesty is to be kept in honor, and patience held fast in contempt or sorrow. For he indeed could be honored, but could not be puffed up; he chose to be despised, but did not know how to be cast down by faintheartedness, or stung by anger. For just as on another occasion, when they wished to seize him and make him king, he fled, withdrawing into solitude to pray (Joan. VI, 15), teaching by example what he had also said in word\u2014namely, that we should not desire to be raised to the heights\u2014so now, by a certain other dispensation, allowing himself to be honored for an hour, and nevertheless retaining in that honor his accustomed meekness (accustomed, because ingrained), he gave a pattern to those who are set in positions of power. Yet, that they might learn to be meek through humility with such discretion that, when the matter demanded it, they should be roused through zeal, the Lord, returning at once into the temple, with a whip made of cords avenges the injuries done to his Father, and chooses rather to provoke the fury of the priests to his own death than to overlook the profanation of the temple (Matth. XXI, 12, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ut igitur, fratres, inoffenso pede tam per prospera, quam per adversa sequamur ducem nostrum, in processione consideremus eum in honore positum, in passione contumeliis et doloribus subditum; nunquam tamen in tanta mutatione rerum mente mutatum, etsi vultum immutaverit coram Abimelech, id est regno [al. rege] Judaeorum, quam commutationem Christi caecitas exprobrat perfidorum. De immutabili mentis illius statu dicit Scriptura: Homo sanctus in sapientia permanet sicut sol; nam stultus ut luna mutatur (Eccli. XXVII, 12). De mutatione faciei illius dicit alia Scriptura: Sapientia hominis lucet in vultu ejus, et potentissimus faciem illius immutabit (Eccle. VIII, 1). Prorsus in vultu tuo, Domine Jesu, qualitercunque videatur immutatus, sive gloriosus sive inglorius appareat, lucet sapientia, de vultu tuo candor lucis aeternae radiat; utinam super nos lumen vultus tui, Domine, effulgeat! Vultus tuus sicut in tristibus, sic in laetis sibi modestus, serenus, ac totus arcano lumine cordis floridus; justis hilaris et jucundus, poenitentibus clemens et pius. Intendite, fratres, in vultum serenissimi Regis. In hilaritate vultus regis vita, dicit Scriptura, et clementia ejus quasi imber serotinus (Prov. XVI, 15). Aspexit in Protoplastum, et mox animatus spiravit in vitam (Gen. I, 27); respexit Petrum, et mox compunctus respiravit ad veniam. Mox enim ut Dominus respexit Petrum, Petrus de clementia piissimi vultus suscepit imbrem serotinum, lacrymas post peccatum (Luc. XXII, 61, 62). Lux vultus tui, o lumen aeternum, teste Job, non cadit in terram (Job. XXV, 3). Quae enim communicatio luci ad tenebras (II Cor. VI, 14)? Animae potius fidelium radios illius excipiant, aspiretque bene consciis laetitiam, sauciis medicinam. Plane vultus triumphantis Jesu, qualis spectandus est in processione, laetitia et jubilus; vultus morientis, qualis cogitandus est in passione, medela et salus. Qui timent te, videbunt me, inquit, et laetabuntur (Psal. CXVIII, 74); qui dolent se, videbunt me, et sanabuntur, sicut aspicientes in serpentem ligno suspensum post venena serpentium. Te igitur, gaudium et salus omnium, seu videant sedentem in asino, seu pendentem in ligno, vota benedicant omnium; ut, cum viderint regnantem in throno, laudent in saecula saeculorum, cui laus et honor per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Therefore, brothers, so that we may follow our leader with unstumbling foot both through prosperity and through adversity, let us consider him in the procession set in honor, and in the passion subjected to insults and sorrows; yet never changed in mind amid so great a change of circumstances, even though he changed his countenance before Abimelech, that is, before the kingdom [or, in another reading, the king] of the Jews\u2014a change in Christ which the blindness of the faithless throws back at him as a reproach. Concerning the unchangeable state of his mind, Scripture says: The holy man abides in wisdom like the sun; but the fool is changed like the moon (Eccli. XXVII, 12). Concerning the change of his countenance, another Scripture says: The wisdom of a man shines in his face, and the most powerful will change his countenance (Eccle. VIII, 1). Indeed, in your countenance, Lord Jesus, however it may seem changed, whether it appears glorious or inglorious, wisdom shines; from your countenance the brightness of eternal light radiates. Would that the light of your countenance might shine upon us, Lord! Your countenance, whether in sorrow or in joy, is modest in itself, serene, and altogether flourishing with the hidden light of the heart; to the just, cheerful and pleasant; to the penitent, merciful and kind. Attend, brothers, to the countenance of the most serene King. In the cheerfulness of the king's countenance is life, says Scripture, and his clemency is like the late rain (Prov. XVI, 15). He looked upon the first-formed man, and at once the man, given life, breathed into life (Gen. I, 27); he looked back upon Peter, and at once, struck with compunction, Peter breathed again unto pardon. For as soon as the Lord looked back upon Peter, Peter received from the clemency of that most merciful countenance the late rain, tears after sin (Luc. XXII, 61, 62). The light of your countenance, O eternal light, as Job bears witness, does not fall to the ground (Job. XXV, 3). For what fellowship has light with darkness (II Cor. VI, 14)? Rather let the souls of the faithful receive its rays, and let it breathe joy upon those of good conscience, and healing upon the wounded. Clearly the countenance of Jesus triumphant, such as is to be beheld in the procession, is joy and jubilation; the countenance of the dying one, such as is to be contemplated in the passion, is remedy and salvation. Those who fear you, he says, will see me and will rejoice (Psal. CXVIII, 74); those who grieve over themselves will see me and will be healed, just as those who looked upon the serpent raised up on wood were healed after the poison of serpents. You, therefore, joy and salvation of all\u2014whether they see you seated upon the ass, or hanging upon the wood\u2014let the prayers of all bless you; so that when they shall see you reigning upon the throne, they may praise you forever and ever, to whom be praise and honor through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon III", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "Passion", "humility", "glory", "meekness", "zeal", "constancy", "repentance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:palm", "calendar_label": "Palm Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-palms-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO IV., Qualiter Christus benedicendus., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 137-142.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Palm Sunday", "Palm Sunday", "Matthew 21:9", "Psalm 12:1\u20132 (printed locator; quotation from Psalm 112:1\u20132, historical numbering)", "Job 1:21", "Psalm 8:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 7:17 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 108:2 (historical numbering)", "Zechariah 9:9", "Psalm 88:16 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 15:10\u201311", "John 8:48, 52\u201353", "Psalm 108:28 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 15:10", "Proverbs 19:17", "2 Timothy 1:12", "Luke 10:35", "Luke 19:39\u201340", "Matthew 27:51\u201352", "Hosea 4:5", "Isaiah 42:11", "Isaiah 2:10", "Psalm 21:17 (historical numbering)", "Colossians 3:3", "Matthew 21:5"], "occasion": "Dominica in ramis palmarum", "occasion_detail": "Palm Sunday", "occasion_label": "Palm Sunday", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric hears the children's Hosanna as praise prompted by the Holy Spirit. The poverty of the king riding on an ass is the sign by which the humble recognize Christ, and he warns against a lukewarm response to grace. He contrasts the Church that received Christ with the Synagogue that rejected him, then invites the brothers to nest like doves in the clefts of the rock by meditating on Christ's wounds. His argument includes polemic against the Synagogue. The sermon appears under the Palm Sunday heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Qualiter Christus benedicendus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How Christ Is to Be Blessed.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Hosanna filio David (Matth. XXI, 9). Vox exsultationis et salutis, vox gaudii et pietatis, vox fidei et amoris, adgratulans adventui Redemptoris, atque prophetico protestans gaudio desideratae laetitiam redemptionis. Hosanna filio David, ait haec familia David; salus est illi qui factus est ex semine David, ut salvet eos qui sunt ex fide David. Laudate, laudate, pueri, Dominum, laudate nomen Domini (Psal. XII, 1, 2); dicite: Sit nomen Domini benedictum (Job. I, 21), sit benedictus, qui venit in nomine Domini. Ex ore istorum perfecisti, o Pater, laudem Filii tui, ut incorrupto simplicis innocentiae testimonio destruas inimicum et ultorem (Psal. VIII, 3), pharisaeum et pontificem; non ultorem divinae legis, ut mentita est iniquitas sibi, sed invidiae propriae et furoris. Sed convertetur dolor ejus in caput ejus, et in verticem ipsius iniquitas ejus descendet (Psal. VII, 17). Deus laudem meam ne tacueris, ait Filius Patri; quia os peccatoris et os dolosi super me apertum est (Psal. CVIII, 2). Nihil potest negare Pater Filio; non tacuit laudem Filii vox paterna saepius audita de coelo, non tacuerunt creaturae, in tot signis et prodigiis confitentes eum auctorem naturae. Angeli testati sunt, daemones confessi sunt, ordo propheticus voce consona sibi invicem respondentes cecinerunt. Sed ista demum perfecta laus est, quam aetas illa non tacet, quae nescit adulari, et quod Spiritus suggerit, dissimulare non potest. Quid enim manifestius quam quod illa aetas a semetipsa, tam nova, tam insolita nec dicebat nec faciebat; sed Spiritus sanctus more suo per ora simplicium loquens, testimonium Filio perhibebat?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Hosanna to the Son of David (Matth. XXI, 9). A voice of exultation and salvation, a voice of joy and devotion, a voice of faith and love, rejoicing at the coming of the Redeemer, and with prophetic gladness proclaiming the joy of the long-desired redemption. Hosanna to the Son of David, says this household of David; salvation belongs to him who was made of the seed of David, that he might save those who are of the faith of David. Praise, praise, O children, the Lord; praise the name of the Lord (Psal. XII, 1, 2); say: Blessed be the name of the Lord (Job. I, 21); blessed be he who comes in the name of the Lord. Out of the mouths of these, O Father, you have perfected the praise of your Son, that by the incorrupt testimony of simple innocence you might destroy the enemy and the avenger (Psal. VIII, 3) \u2014 the Pharisee and the high priest; not an avenger of the divine law, as iniquity falsely claims of itself, but an avenger of his own envy and fury. But his sorrow shall be turned back upon his own head, and his iniquity shall come down upon his own crown (Psal. VII, 17). O God, do not be silent concerning my praise, says the Son to the Father; for the mouth of the sinner and the mouth of the deceitful has been opened against me (Psal. CVIII, 2). The Father can deny nothing to the Son; the Father's voice, heard many times from heaven, did not keep silent the praise of the Son; the creatures did not keep silent, confessing him, in so many signs and wonders, to be the author of nature. The angels bore witness, the demons confessed, the order of the prophets sang in harmonious voice, answering one another. But this, after all, is the perfect praise, which that age does not keep silent \u2014 an age that does not know how to flatter, and cannot hide what the Spirit suggests. For what could be plainer than that that age, of itself, neither said nor did things so new, so unheard of; but the Holy Spirit, speaking in his own manner through the mouths of the simple, was bearing witness to the Son?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Plane Spiritus sanctus conscius operum Christi, quid ille scilicet Christi pareret adventus, quid gaudii, quid salutis humano generi passio parturiret illius, praesaga in cordibus innocentium gaudia suscitabat, atque ad prophetandam mundo redemptionis laetitiam, simplicium sibi ministeria sensuum assumebat. Istos nempe Spiritus alloquebatur, vocans ea quae non sunt, tanquam ea quae sunt, cum per prophetam suum dicebat quae hodie per ipsum facta sunt, mandabat tunc, et nunc creata sunt. Exsulta, inquit, satis, filia Sion; jubila, filia Jerusalem: ecce Rex tuus veniet tibi justus et Salvator, ipse pauper et ascendens super asinum (Zach. IX, 9 et Matth. XXI, 5). Jam, inquit, exsulta satis, quae hactenus in tristitia fuisti; jam te satia, si tamen satiari potes, gaudio ineffabili, quod sic desiderium satiat ut amplius et felicius esurire faciat. Repleatur gaudio os tuum, et lingua tua exsultatione; cui si nec os nec lingua possunt sufficere, supereffundat jubilus quod non capit affectus. Jubila, inquit, filia Jerusalem. Beatus enim populus qui scit jubilationem (Psal. LXXXVIII, 16). Plane beatus populus qui scit et intelligit hodie sibi ineffabiliter gaudendum, quando ei Salvator advenit promissus et exspectatus ab initio saeculorum. Beatus populus qui tota alacritate devotionis hodie ei occurrit, corde simul et voce acclamans ei: Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini (Matth. XXI, 9). In hoc siquidem benedictus est a Patre Filius, ut qui benedixerit ei, benedictionibus repleatur, non una scilicet, sed pluribus; quia nimirum benedictio quam Domino quia feneratur, cum multiplici fenore redit in caput ejus. Vae genti peccatrici, semini nequam, filiis sceleratis! ad quos spectat illa Domini querela terribilis: Non feneravi, nec feneravit mihi quisquam; omnes maledicunt mihi, dicit Dominus (Jerem. XV, 10, 11). Judaicus populus hic erat, qui in ratione dati et accepti communicare contempsit Domino, nolens benedicere Benedictum Dei Patris, ut benediceretur ab eo; sed Samaritanum, daemoniacum vocans, insanis atque blasphemis Deum insectabatur maledictis (Joan. VIII, 48, 52, 53). Maledicent illi, inquit, et tu benedices (Psal. CVIII, 28); nam ab eis benedictio quam noluerunt, elongabitur ad gentes. Benedicite, gentes, Deum nostrum, quoniam qui prior feneravit vobis, praeveniens vos in benedictionibus dulcedinis, fenerantibus sibi multiplices restituet usuras in benedictionibus beatitudinis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Clearly the Holy Spirit, aware of the works of Christ \u2014 of what the coming of Christ would bring forth, what joy, what salvation his passion would bring forth for the human race \u2014 was stirring up prophetic joys in the hearts of the innocent, and was taking to himself the service of simple senses in order to prophesy to the world the gladness of redemption. Indeed the Spirit was speaking to these, calling the things that are not as though they were, when through his prophet he spoke of what is today being accomplished through him; he commanded it then, and now it has been brought into being. Rejoice greatly, he says, O daughter of Sion; shout for joy, O daughter of Jerusalem: behold, your King shall come to you, just and a Savior, himself poor and riding upon an ass (Zach. IX, 9 et Matth. XXI, 5). Now, he says, rejoice greatly, you who until now have been in sorrow; now sate yourself \u2014 if indeed you can be sated \u2014 with ineffable joy, which so satisfies desire that it makes it hunger more abundantly and more happily. Let your mouth be filled with joy, and your tongue with exultation; and if neither mouth nor tongue can suffice for this, let the shout of joy overflow with what feeling cannot contain. Shout for joy, he says, O daughter of Jerusalem. For blessed is the people that knows jubilation (Psal. LXXXVIII, 16). Truly blessed is the people that knows and understands that today it must rejoice ineffably, since the Savior has come to it, promised and awaited from the beginning of the ages. Blessed is the people that today, with the whole eagerness of devotion, goes out to meet him, acclaiming him with heart and voice together: Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord (Matth. XXI, 9). For in this the Son is blessed by the Father, so that whoever blesses him may be filled with blessings \u2014 not one only, but many; because indeed the blessing that is lent to the Lord returns upon the lender's own head with manifold interest. Woe to the sinful nation, the wicked seed, the children of wickedness! It is to them that the Lord's terrible complaint applies: I have not lent, nor has anyone lent to me; all curse me, says the Lord (Jerem. XV, 10, 11). This was the Jewish people, who scorned to share with the Lord in the account of giving and receiving, refusing to bless the Blessed One of God the Father, so that they might be blessed by him; but instead, calling him a Samaritan and possessed by a demon, they pursued God with mad and blasphemous curses (Joan. VIII, 48, 52, 53). They will curse, he says, but you will bless (Psal. CVIII, 28); for from them the blessing which they refused will be carried far off to the nations. Bless, O nations, our God, for he who first lent to you, going before you in blessings of sweetness, will restore to those who lend to him manifold interest in blessings of beatitude.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Ego tamen vereor, fratres mei, ne forte haec tempora teporis et infidelitatis tangat illa querela Domini: Non feneravi, nec feneravit mihi quisquam (Jerem. XV, 10); quia videlicet gratia offertur, nec suscipitur; promittitur merces operi, et vix aliquis spe illius operatur. Dominus siquidem fenerat, cum talenta servis partiens, scientiam verbi vel gratiam cujuslibet muneris ad lucrum praerogat: Domino autem fenerat jucundus homo qui miseretur et commodat, dicente Scriptura: Feneratur Domino, qui misericordiam facit proximo (Prov. XIX, 17). Imo Domino feneratur quicunque spe divinae retributionis aliquid operatur, ut dicere possit: Scio cui credidi (II Tim. I, 12), scio qui dixit: Si quid erogaveris, cum rediero reddam tibi (Luc. X, 35). Nos autem aut nihil aut ita timide gelideque feneramus ei, quasi debitor infidelis sit, aut non habeat unde reddere possit. Magnae prorsus fidei illa plebs Hebraea, quae, cum eum pauperem sedentem super asellum, et ipsum non suum, cerneret, omni tamen securitate ac devotione fenerabat ei, non solum vestimenta sua in via sternens, sed et se totam, quantum poterat, honori ejus impendens. Sed nimirum intelligebat super egenum et pauperem; quippe cui propheta signum dederat intelligendi Salvatorem, ipsam qua superbis viluit, paupertatem. Ipse, inquit, pauper, et ascendens super asinum (Zach. IX, 9). Hoc, inquit, signo poteris venientem agnoscere Regem tuum, cujus regnum non est de hoc mundo, quia scilicet, ad debellandam superbiam quae regnat in mundo, paupertatem seu humilitatem evangelizabit sicut verbo sic etiam exemplo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Yet I fear, my brothers, lest perhaps these times of lukewarmness and unbelief be touched by that complaint of the Lord: \"I have not lent, nor has anyone lent to me\" (Jerem. XV, 10); because, that is, grace is offered and not received, a reward is promised for the work, and scarcely anyone works in hope of it. For the Lord lends when, distributing talents to his servants, he grants beforehand knowledge of the word or the grace of any gift, for profit; but a man who is kind, who shows mercy and lends, lends to the Lord, as Scripture says: \"He who shows mercy to his neighbor lends to the Lord\" (Prov. XIX, 17). Indeed, whoever does anything in hope of divine repayment lends to the Lord, so that he can say: \"I know whom I have believed\" (II Tim. I, 12), \"I know him who said: If you spend anything, when I return I will repay you\" (Luc. X, 35). But we either lend nothing to him, or lend so timidly and coldly, as if he were an unfaithful debtor, or had nothing with which to repay. Truly great was the faith of that Hebrew people, who, although they saw him sitting poor upon a young ass, and not even his own, nevertheless lent to him with complete confidence and devotion, not only spreading their garments in the way, but spending their whole selves, as much as they could, in his honor. For they understood, no doubt, concerning the needy and the poor one; since the prophet had given them a sign by which to recognize the Savior\u2014namely, that very poverty for which the proud held him cheap. \"He himself,\" it says, \"poor, and riding upon an ass\" (Zach. IX, 9). By this sign, it says, you will be able to recognize your King as he comes, whose kingdom is not of this world, because, to overthrow the pride that reigns in the world, he would preach poverty or humility as much by example as by word.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Beata itaque filia Sion, quae humilitatem tanquam armaturam coelestem, tanquam insigne regium didicit venerari. Misera mater ejus infidelis illa Sion, quae cum humilem vidit, fastidivit, cum honoratum vidit, invidit. Beata, inquam, Ecclesia primitivorum, quae tam fideliter agnovit, tamque gratanter suscepit venientem in nomine Domini; infelix Synagoga perfidorum, quae, venturum in suo nomine parata suscipere, illius qui Patris gloriam quaerebat cruciabatur honore. Increpa, inquiunt, discipulos tuos; quasi scilicet aut illorum simplicitas posset adulari, aut ipsius puritas vanis posset laudibus delectari. Dico, inquit, vobis, quia si hi tacuerint, lapides clamabunt (Luc. XIX, 39, 40); quia Deus laudem meam non tacebit. Prorsus ita est: si tacuerint, lapides clamabunt; quia tempore passionis isti tacuerunt, sed lapides clamaverunt, dum in testimonium et laudem morientis, petrae scissae sunt, et monumenta aperta sunt (Matth. XXVII, 51, 52). Prorsus ita est, quia modo tacente Synagoga, juxta illud: Nocte tacere feci matrem tuam (Ose. IV, 5), clamat vivis ex lapidibus Gentium Ecclesia, clamant plane lapides, de quibus suscitavit filios Abrahae qui potens est. Laudate, inquit propheta, habitatores petrae, de vertice montium clamabunt (Isai. XLII, 11). Ecce hodie in foraminibus petrae, in cavernis maceriae vox columbae resonat, clamantis et dicentis: Hosanna filio David; benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini (Matth. XXI, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Blessed, then, is the daughter of Sion, who has learned to venerate humility as a heavenly armor, as a royal insignia. Wretched is her unbelieving mother, that Sion, who, when she saw him humble, disdained him, and when she saw him honored, envied him. Blessed, I say, is the Church of the firstborn, which so faithfully recognized and so gratefully received him who came in the name of the Lord; unhappy is the Synagogue of the faithless, which, prepared to receive one coming in his own name, was tormented by the honor paid to him who sought the glory of the Father. \"Rebuke,\" they say, \"your disciples\"\u2014as though either their simplicity could flatter, or his purity could be delighted by empty praises. \"I tell you,\" he says, \"that if these are silent, the stones will cry out\" (Luc. XIX, 39, 40); because God will not be silent about my praise. And indeed it is so: if they are silent, the stones will cry out; for at the time of the Passion these men were silent, but the stones cried out, when, in testimony and praise of the dying one, the rocks were split and the tombs were opened (Matth. XXVII, 51, 52). And indeed it is so, because now, while the Synagogue is silent\u2014according to that word: \"I have made your mother be silent at night\" (Ose. IV, 5)\u2014the Church of the Gentiles cries out from living stones; truly the stones cry out, from which he who is able raised up sons of Abraham. \"Praise,\" says the prophet, \"you inhabitants of the rock, they will cry out from the top of the mountains\" (Isai. XLII, 11). Behold, today in the clefts of the rock, in the hollows of the wall, the voice of the dove resounds, crying out and saying: \"Hosanna to the Son of David; blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord\" (Matth. XXI, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Benedictus qui, ut nidificare possem in foraminibus petrae, manus, pedes et latus perforari sibi tulit, et se mihi totum aperuit, ut ingrediar in locum tabernaculi admirabilis, et protegar in abscondito tabernaculi sui. Opportunum quidem petra refugium erinaciis, sed et habitatio grata columbis, cujus foramina tot vulneribus toto fere corpore patentia, et veniam offerunt reis, et gratiam conferunt justis. Imo vero tuta habitatio, fratres mei, turrisque fortitudinis a facie inimici, pia ac sedula meditatione Christi Domini vulneribus immorari, fideque et amore Crucifixi, animam ab aestu carnis, a turbine saeculi, ab impetu diaboli contutari. Super omnem gloriam mundi protectio hujus tabernaculi, videlicet in umbraculum diei ab aestu, in securitatem et absconsionem a turbine et a pluvia, ut per diem sol non urat te prosperitate, nec turbo moveat in tempestate. Ingredere igitur in petram, o homo, abscondere in fossa humo (Isai. II, 10), pone tibi latibulum in Crucifixo. Ipse petra, ipse humus, quia Deus et homo; ipse petra forata, humus fossa, quia foderunt manus meas, inquit, et pedes meos (Psal. XXI, 17). Abscondere, inquit, in fossa humo a facie timoris Domini (Isai. II, 10): hoc est, ab ipso fuge ad ipsum, a judice ad redemptorem, a tribunali ad crucem, a justo ad misericordem; ab eo qui percutiet terram virga oris sui, ad eum qui inebriat terram stillicidiis cruoris sui; ab eo qui spiritu labiorum suorum interficiet impium, ad eum qui sanguine vulnerum suorum vivificat exstinctum. Quinimo non ad ipsum tantum, sed in ipsum fuge, in foramina petrae ingredere, in fossa humo abscondere, in ipsis manibus foratis, in fosso latere te ipsum reconde. Vulnus enim in latere Christi, quid nisi ostium in latere Arcae salvandis a facie diluvii? Illud tamen figura, istud autem veritas, ubi non modo servatur vita mortalis, sed recuperatur immortalis. Ideo quippe latus suum pius et misericors aperuit, ut cruor te vulneris vivificet, calor corporis refocillet, spiritus cordis quasi patenti et libero meatu aspiret. Ibi tuto latebis, donec traneat iniquitas; ibi nequaquam algebis, eo quod in visceribus Christi non frigescat charitas; ibi deliciis afflues, ibi gaudiis superefflues; vel tunc demum cum a vita capitis tua et omnium membrorum corporis ejus absorpta fuerit mortalitas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Blessed is he who, so that I might build a nest in the clefts of the rock, allowed his hands, feet, and side to be pierced, and opened himself wholly to me, that I might enter into the place of the wonderful tabernacle, and be sheltered in the hidden place of his tabernacle. The rock is indeed a fitting refuge for hedgehogs, but it is also a pleasing dwelling for doves, and its openings\u2014laid open by so many wounds over almost the whole body\u2014both offer pardon to the guilty and bestow grace upon the just. Indeed, my brothers, it is a safe dwelling, a tower of strength against the face of the enemy, to dwell with pious and diligent meditation upon the wounds of the Lord Christ, and by faith and love of the Crucified, to keep the soul safe from the heat of the flesh, from the whirlwind of the world, from the assault of the devil. Above all the glory of the world is the protection of this tabernacle, namely, a shelter by day from the heat, a security and hiding place from whirlwind and rain, so that the sun of prosperity may not burn you by day, nor the whirlwind disturb you in time of storm. Enter, therefore, into the rock, O man; hide yourself in the dug earth (Isai. II, 10); make for yourself a hiding place in the Crucified One. He himself is the rock, he himself the earth, because he is God and man; he himself is the pierced rock, the dug earth, for he says, \"they have dug through my hands and my feet\" (Psal. XXI, 17). \"Hide yourself,\" he says, \"in the dug earth from before the fear of the Lord\" (Isai. II, 10): that is, flee from him to him, from the judge to the redeemer, from the tribunal to the cross, from the just one to the merciful one; from him who shall strike the earth with the rod of his mouth, to him who makes the earth drunk with the drippings of his blood; from him who shall slay the wicked with the breath of his lips, to him who gives life to the dead by the blood of his wounds. Nay, flee not only to him, but into him; enter into the clefts of the rock, hide yourself in the dug earth; conceal yourself in his very pierced hands, in his opened side. For what is the wound in the side of Christ but the door in the side of the Ark for those to be saved from the face of the flood? That, however, was a figure, but this is the truth, where not only is mortal life preserved, but immortal life is recovered. For this reason indeed the pious and merciful one opened his side, so that the blood of the wound might give you life, the warmth of the body might refresh you, and the breath of the heart might breathe upon you, as it were, through an open and free passage. There you will lie hidden in safety until iniquity passes away; there you will never grow cold, because love does not grow cold in the heart of Christ; there you will abound in delights, there you will overflow with joys\u2014or rather, then at last, when your mortality, and that of all the members of his body, shall have been swallowed up by the life of the Head.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Merito itaque columba Christi, formosa Christi, cui ipsius vulnera tam tuta quam grata ad nidificandum paravere foramina, laudes ipsius utique hodie laetabunda decantat, ac de passionis memoria vel imitatione, de vulnerum meditatione, tanquam de foraminibus petrae vox suavis in auribus sponsi resonat. Caeterum vobis, fratres mei, qui tanto interius nidificastis in foraminibus petrae, quanto secretius vivitis in Christo, et vita vestra abscondita est cum Christo in Deo (Coloss. III, 3); vobis incumbit omnino ut quorum est conversatio quietior et tutior, sit etiam devotio dulcior; praesertim hodie, cum et recursus temporis et repraesentatio operis velut interesse nos facit illi gaudio solemni quo susceptus est in Jerusalem. Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini; ipsi benedictio, regnum et imperium, qui est super omnia Deus benedictus in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Rightly, then, does the dove of Christ, the fair one of Christ\u2014for whom his very wounds have prepared clefts as safe as they are pleasant for nesting\u2014today joyfully sing forth his praises; and from the memory or imitation of the passion, from meditation on the wounds, as it were from the clefts of the rock, a sweet voice resounds in the ears of the Bridegroom. But as for you, my brothers, who have nested the more inwardly in the clefts of the rock the more secretly you live in Christ, and whose life is hidden with Christ in God (Coloss. III, 3): it falls upon you entirely that, since your way of life is quieter and safer, your devotion should also be sweeter\u2014especially today, when both the return of the season and the re-enactment of the deed make us, as it were, present at that solemn joy with which he was received into Jerusalem. Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord; to him be blessing, kingdom, and dominion, who is over all things, God blessed for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-palms-04-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quoted call to praise the name of the Lord comes from Psalm 112:1\u20132 in Vulgate numbering (113:1\u20132 in modern numbering); the edition prints Psal. XII, 1, 2."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Palm Sunday Sermon IV", "topics": ["Palm Sunday", "praise", "Holy Spirit", "poverty", "humility", "Church", "wounds of Christ", "contemplation"], "url": "sermons/guerric-palms-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:spiritus-domini", "calendar_label": "Pentecost Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-pentecost-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo Pentecostes, sermo I, De Dei charitate et hominum ingratitudine, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 157-160.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Pentecost", "Feast of Pentecost", "Romans 8:32", "Luke 5 (printed locator; Luke 15 narrative, see note)", "Romans 5:15, 20", "John 1:9", "Matthew 5:45", "Psalm 100:1 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 30:16", "Ecclesiastes 2:2", "Proverbs 14:13", "Acts 2:2", "Isaiah 66:12 (printed punctuation retained; see note)", "Acts 6:10", "John 7:37", "Isaiah 55:1", "Hebrews 6:5 (printed locator; quotation includes 6:4, see note)", "Psalm 101:5 (historical numbering; printed punctuation retained, see note)", "1 Corinthians 10:20\u201321 (printed locator; see note)"], "occasion": "In festo Pentecostes", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Pentecost", "occasion_label": "Pentecost", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny presents the Father's gift of the Son for redemption and of the Holy Spirit for adoption as signs of divine generosity. He contrasts that generosity with human ingratitude, urging his hearers to hope for others' repentance while avoiding both despair about others and presumption about themselves. Fear made chaste by love guards spiritual joy. The Spirit's outpouring at Pentecost becomes an invitation to thirst for God's gifts; Guerric then warns against spiritual loss of appetite, slander and the passions that conflict with sharing Christ's table. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The historical heading names the feast of Pentecost, and Guerric recalls the Spirit given to the Apostles on this day."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN FESTO PENTECOSTES."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE FEAST OF PENTECOST", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De Dei charitate et hominum ingratitudine."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On God's Charity and Human Ingratitude", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Ineffabilis Deus, ineffabilis misericordia ejus. Secundum nomen ejus, ita et opus ejus. Plane ineffabilis divinae circa nos dignatio charitatis. Parum erat Patri, tradidisse Filium, ut redimeret servum; nisi daret et Spiritum sanctum, quo servum adoptaret in Filium. Dedit filium in pretium redemptionis; dedit Spiritum in privilegium adoptationis: se denique totum servat haereditatem adoptatis. O Deum, si fas est dici, prodigum sui, prae desiderio hominis! An non prodigum, qui non solum sua, sed et se ipsum impendit, ut hominem recuperaret, non tam sibi, quam homini ipsi? an non prodigum, qui, sicut proprio Filio suo non pepercit, sed pro nobis omnibus tradidit illum (Rom. viii, 32), sic nec Spiritui sancto pepercit, ut ita loquar, sed nova et mira largitate super omnem carnem effudit illum? Multum quidem effusus sui prodigus ille filius, qui tam patrimonium, quam se ipsum donavit meretricibus; sed multo effusior pater in recuperationem perditi filii, quam ille in perditionem sui (Luc. v); si tamen ulla debet esse comparatio gratiae et pecuniae, spiritus et carnis, Dei et hominis: Vide enim quanta largitate toto orbe gratia Spiritus effusa sit, non solum justis confirmandis, sed et peccatoribus justificandis: quomodo ubique gentium, Spiritu creante novum genus hominum, renovata sit facies terrae: imo quanta quotidie mutatio fiat per dexteram Excelsi, ut repente perditissimi hominum publicani et meretrices, justos quam plurimos praecedant in regno Dei, et novissimi fiant primi. Prorsus non sicut delictum, ita et donum: quia ubi abundavit delictum, ibi superabundans gratia (Rom. v, 15, 20): non solum donat delicta, sed etiam virtutum accumulat merita; et sublimius restituit lapsos redemptio, quam constituerat prima creatio. Sane in his omnibus quanto mirabilior Dei gratia praedicatur, tanto damnabilior hominis duritia convincitur, si gratiam vel oblatam recuset, vel acceptam non servet. Cui enim non offertur? super quem non effulget lumen illius? quis se abscondit a calore ejus? Neque enim reliquit se Deus sine testimonio apud conscientias hominum, illustrans eos splendore veritatis, et fovens calore bonitatis; cum et lux vera illuminet omnem hominem venientem in hunc mundum (Joan. i, 9), et Deus faciat oriri solem suum super bonos et malos, et pluat super justos et injustos (Matth. v, 45). Sed vae illis qui rebelles sunt lumini, et resistunt Spiritui sancto, nec acquiescunt veritati quam intelligunt; et sicut lutum ex calore solis, ita ex bonitate et beneficiis Dei duriores fiunt, audacter etiam provocantes eum, cum ipse dederit omnia in manibus eorum!"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. God is beyond all telling; his mercy is beyond all telling. As is his name, so also is his work. Beyond all telling indeed is the condescension of divine charity towards us. It was too little for the Father to have handed over the Son to redeem the servant, unless he also gave the Holy Spirit, through whom he might adopt the servant as a son. He gave the Son as the price of redemption; he gave the Spirit as the privilege of adoption; finally, he keeps his whole self as the inheritance of those adopted. O God, prodigal of himself, if one may say so, in his longing for man! Is he not prodigal, who spends not only what is his but his very self, to recover man, not so much for himself as for man's own sake? Is he not prodigal, who, just as he did not spare his own Son but handed him over for us all (Rom. viii, 32), likewise did not spare the Holy Spirit, so to speak, but poured him out upon all flesh with a new and wondrous generosity? That prodigal son indeed poured himself out lavishly, giving both his patrimony and himself to harlots; but the father was far more lavish in recovering his lost son than the son had been in losing himself (Luc. v), if indeed grace and money, spirit and flesh, God and man ought to be compared at all. For see how generously the grace of the Spirit has been poured out throughout the world, not only to strengthen the just but also to justify sinners; how everywhere among the nations the face of the earth has been renewed, as the Spirit creates a new race of men; indeed, how great a change is wrought daily by the right hand of the Most High, so that suddenly the most abandoned of men, publicans and harlots, go before very many of the just into the kingdom of God, and the last become first. Truly, the gift is not like the trespass, for where the trespass abounded, grace is superabundant (Rom. v, 15, 20): it not only forgives trespasses but heaps up the merits of virtues; and redemption restores the fallen to a higher state than the first creation had established. In all this, surely, the more wondrous God's grace is proclaimed to be, the more deserving of condemnation man's hardness is proved to be, if he either refuses grace when offered or fails to keep it once received. For to whom is it not offered? Upon whom does its light not shine? Who hides himself from its warmth? For God has not left himself without witness in men's consciences, enlightening them with the splendour of truth and warming them with the heat of goodness, since the true light enlightens every man coming into this world (Joan. i, 9), and God makes his sun rise upon the good and the evil, and rains upon the just and the unjust (Matth. v, 45). But woe to those who rebel against the light and resist the Holy Spirit, who will not assent to the truth they understand; and who, like clay under the sun's heat, grow harder through God's goodness and benefits, even boldly provoking him, although he himself has put all things into their hands!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Verum quid ad nos de his qui foris sunt? Ad vos potius nobis est sermo, qui spiritum adoptionis filiorum accepistis, habentes eumdem spiritum in signum adoptionis et pignus haereditatis; qui velut quidam character insignis, vasa misericordiae a vasis irae discernit. Quanquam de salute nostra, vel magis de spe salutis, ea nobis modestia gaudendum sit, ut quod in illis dolemus, adhuc de nobis pro nostra mutabilitate timeamus. Sicut enim de illis desperare impium, ita de nobis praesumere est temerarium, cum tam de nobis quam de illis, qualiscunque sit scientia praesentium, nulla possit esse praescientia futurorum; nec levis sit injuria summae potestati, penes quam est arbitrium vitae et mortis, si vel de illis tam crudeliter, vel de nobis tam audacter praejudicetur ejus judiciis. Denique de massa eorum sumus assumpti, et qui paulo ante eramus, sicut et illi, natura et vita filii irae, repente facti sumus filii gratiae. Nos ergo simus illis exemplum spei ad poenitentiam; ipsi nobis, timoris ad perseverantiam. In nobis se misericordia commendat, ut ametur; in illis se judicium exserit ut timeatur. Misericordiam et judicium cantabo tibi, Domine (Psal. c, 1): pro ista gratias agens, pro illo preces fundens, pro ista gratus et laetus, pro illo timoratus et cautus: de utroque eruditus exsultabo tibi cum tremore, quia tunc ingrediar in veritate tua, si ita laetetur cor meum ut timeat nomen tuum. Neque enim timor iste, quem amor castum facit, gaudium tollit, sed custodit; non destruit, sed instruit; non inamaricat, sed condit: ut tanto sit durabilius, quanto modestius; tanto verius, quanto severius; tanto dulcius, quanto sanctius. O castum et fidele gaudium! quam fidelis est illa de te sententia Sapientis: Non est census super salutem corporis, et non est oblectatio super gaudium cordis! (Eccli. xxx, 16) Hoc utique gaudio non est gaudere impiis, quorum fatuos risus et frivola gaudia Sapiens detestatur et increpat. Risum, inquit, reputavi errorem, et gaudio dixi: Quid frustra deciperis? (Eccle. ii, 2) Risus dolore miscebitur, et extrema gaudii luctus occupat (Prov. xiv, 13)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. But what have we to do with those who are outside? Our discourse is addressed rather to you, who have received the spirit of adoption as sons, possessing that same spirit as the sign of adoption and pledge of the inheritance; like a distinctive seal, he distinguishes vessels of mercy from vessels of wrath. Yet we must rejoice in our salvation, or rather in the hope of salvation, with such restraint that, because of our own changeableness, we still fear for ourselves what we lament in them. For just as it is impious to despair of them, so it is rash to presume concerning ourselves, since, whatever knowledge there may be of present things, there can be no foreknowledge of things to come, either concerning ourselves or concerning them; nor is it a slight affront to the supreme power, in whose hands lies the determination of life and death, if his judgments are prejudged so cruelly concerning them or so boldly concerning ourselves. After all, we have been taken from their same mass, and we who a little while ago, like them, were children of wrath by nature and manner of life have suddenly become children of grace. Let us therefore be for them an example of hope leading to repentance; let them be for us an example of fear leading to perseverance. In us mercy commends itself, that it may be loved; in them judgment shows itself, that it may be feared. I will sing to you of mercy and judgment, O Lord (Psal. c, 1): giving thanks for the one, pouring out prayers concerning the other; grateful and glad for the one, fearful and watchful concerning the other. Instructed by both, I will exult before you with trembling, for I shall walk in your truth when my heart rejoices in such a way as to fear your name. For this fear, which love makes chaste, does not take joy away but guards it; does not destroy it but builds it up; does not embitter it but seasons it, so that it is the more lasting for being more restrained, the truer for being more serious, the sweeter for being holier. O chaste and faithful joy! How true is the Wise Man's saying about you: There is no wealth above bodily health, and no delight above joy of heart! (Eccli. xxx, 16) It is not for the impious to rejoice with this joy: the Wise Man detests and rebukes their foolish laughter and frivolous joys. Laughter, he says, I reckoned an error, and to joy I said: Why are you vainly deceived? (Eccle. ii, 2) Laughter will be mingled with sorrow, and mourning takes possession of the end of joy (Prov. xiv, 13).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O bone Jesu, quam dissimile est illud gaudium tuum, quo interim consolaris renuntiantes illi falso et fallaci gaudio! quam melior est misericordia tua super vitas! quam melior est dies una in atriis tuis super millia! quanto beatiores facis pauperes tuos ipsa paupertate tua, quam mundus facere possit sua quantalibet affluentia, ubi quidquid affluit, defluit, sibique inhaerentem pereffluere facit! Aliis affluebat deliciis illa pauper familia Christi, quam fluminis impetus inundabat laetificans civitatem Dei; cum scilicet hodierna die Spiritus, tanquam torrens, replevit totam domum ubi erant sedentes (Act. ii, 2.) Apostoli. Exhibebat quippe divina Veritas, quod per prophetam promiserat: Ecce ego declino in vos, ut flumen pacis, et ut torrens inundans gloriae gentium (Isai. lxvi, 12. Quantum illis affluebat, quibus tantus influebat! quantumque de illis profluebat, de quorum ventre fluebant flumina aquae vivae! Nec solum de corde benevolentia charitatis, sed etiam ex ore vehementia profluebat torrentis eloquii, cui non poterant resistere et contradicere omnes eorum adversarii: sicut de Stephano dicitur, quia non poterant resistere sapientiae et Spiritui qui loquebatur (Act. vi, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O good Jesus, how different is that joy of yours, with which you meanwhile console those who renounce that false and deceitful joy! How much better is your mercy than lives! How much better is one day in your courts than thousands! How much happier you make your poor through your very poverty than the world could make them with any abundance of its own, where whatever flows in flows away, and makes whoever clings to it flow away with it! That poor household of Christ abounded in other delights, flooded by the rushing river that gladdens the city of God, when on this very day the Spirit, like a torrent, filled the whole house where the Apostles were sitting (Act. ii, 2.). For divine Truth was fulfilling what he had promised through the prophet: Behold, I turn towards you as a river of peace, and as an overflowing torrent of the glory of the nations (Isai. lxvi, 12. How abundantly he flowed upon them, into whom so great a One flowed! And how much flowed forth from those out of whose innermost being flowed rivers of living water! Not only did the benevolence of charity flow from their hearts, but from their mouths also flowed the force of a torrent of speech, which none of their adversaries could resist or contradict, as it is said of Stephen that they could not resist the wisdom and the Spirit who spoke (Act. vi, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Ad hujusmodi gaudia vos interim, fratres, consolator vester invitat. Hoc torrente voluptatis suae sitibundas amantium mentes potare desiderat. Si quis, inquit, sitit, veniat et bibat (Joan. vii, 37). O affluens liberalitas Dei! o indeficiens largitas divinae bonitatis! Spiritum, cujus hodie primitias dedit Apostolis, offert universis. Thesaurum suum, fontem aquae vivae aperit tam hominibus quam jumentis; tanquam et ipse omnibus sapientibus et insipientibus debitor sit. Omnes, inquit, sitientes, venite ad aquas (Isai. lv, 1). Ecce personas non accipit, conditiones non discernit, merita non requirit: tantum sitire quis noverit, venire velit. Gratia quippe non admittit fastidientes; sed sicut esurientes implet bonis, sic divites dimittit inanes. O fastidium tinea cordium, rubigo mentium languor pessimus animarum, quod abominari facis bonum Dei verbum, contemnere coeleste donum, fastidire manna propter ollas carnium! Quae enim lues tam pestifera, quis morbus tam lethalis, qui sic faciat hominem immemorem suae salutis, ridentem ac male securum appropinquare usque ad portas mortis? Sed unde, rogo tam late lues ista Christi ovilia infecit, greges invasit, ut quam pluribus de grege Domini videatur etiam locus horroris et vastae solitudinis, locus pascuae ubi sunt collocati, et in pascuis uberrimis, in herbis virentibus misere pereant fastidio languidi? An non, quaeso gustaverunt donum coeleste, et participes facti sunt Spiritus sancti; gustaverunt nihilominus bonum Dei verbum, et virtutes saeculi venturi? (Hebr. vi, 5.) Si enim non gustaverunt bonum Dei verbum, unde toties eructavit cor eorum verbum bonum, cum scilicet de memoria abundantiae suavitatis ejus eructabant labia eorum hymnum? Nunc autem divinis intersunt laudibus, et dormitant; aut otiosa vel etiam perniciosa mente pertractant: sedent ad librum, et oscitant: verbum exhortationis audiunt, et ipso auditu laborant: de pascuis mutantur in pascua, et tam ista fastidiunt quam illa: jugiter versantur inter fercula vitae, et moriuntur fame. Unde ergo post illam felicem experientiam, gustumque suavem supernae dulcedinis, tanta haec oblivio, et incuria boni, languorque mentium subintravit? Sola relicta est eis vox illa plangentis, si tamen plangere velint: Percussus sum ut fenum, et aruit cor meum, quia oblitus sum comedere panem meum (Psal. ci, 5. Currebant utique bene: quis eos fascinavit retrorsum converti? Spiritu coeperunt: quomodo nunc carne consummantur? Voluptuose vescebantur: quomodo nunc intereunt in viis?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Meanwhile, brothers, your Comforter invites you to joys of this kind. With this torrent of his delight he longs to give drink to the thirsty minds of those who love him. If anyone thirsts, he says, let him come and drink (Joan. vii, 37). O overflowing generosity of God! O unfailing bounty of divine goodness! The Spirit whose firstfruits he gave to the Apostles today he offers to everyone. He opens his treasury, the fountain of living water, to men and beasts alike, as though he too were debtor to all, wise and unwise. All you who thirst, he says, come to the waters (Isai. lv, 1). See, he shows no partiality, distinguishes no ranks, requires no merits: let a man only know how to thirst and be willing to come. For grace does not admit those who have lost their appetite; but as it fills the hungry with good things, so it sends the rich away empty. O loss of appetite, moth of hearts, rust of minds, most grievous sickness of souls, you who make them loathe the good word of God, despise the heavenly gift, and tire of manna for the sake of the flesh pots! For what plague is so deadly, what sickness so lethal, as to make a man so forgetful of his salvation that he approaches the very gates of death laughing and falsely secure? But how, I ask, has this plague infected Christ's sheepfolds so widely and invaded his flocks, that to so many of the Lord's flock even the pasture where they have been placed seems a place of horror and vast solitude, and in the richest pastures, amid green grass, they miserably perish, sick with loss of appetite? Have they not, I ask, tasted the heavenly gift and become sharers in the Holy Spirit; have they not also tasted the good word of God and the powers of the age to come? (Hebr. vi, 5.) For if they have not tasted the good word of God, how did their hearts so often pour forth a good word, when their lips poured forth a hymn from the remembrance of his abundant sweetness? But now they attend the divine praises and doze; or turn over idle or even harmful thoughts in their minds. They sit at their books and yawn; they hear a word of exhortation and find even listening a burden; they move from pasture to pasture and tire of the one as much as the other; they are continually surrounded by dishes of life, and die of hunger. How then, after that happy experience and sweet taste of heavenly sweetness, have such forgetfulness, such neglect of the good and such sickness of mind crept in? All that remains to them is that cry of lament, if indeed they are willing to lament: I have been struck down like grass, and my heart has withered, because I have forgotten to eat my bread (Psal. ci, 5. They were indeed running well: who has bewitched them into turning back? They began in the Spirit: how are they now being brought to completion in the flesh? They used to feed in delight: how are they now perishing on the roads?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Videant, quaeso, ipsi, ne forte inimicus homo, qui super bonum semen patrisfamilias lolium suum superseminare consuevit, post illam suavem escam Christi, felle suo fauces eorum tinxerit, ac per hoc non modo desiderium, sed et memoriam prioris gustus aboleverit. Enimvero statim denuntiat Apostolus his qui ejusmodi sunt: Non potestis calicem Domini bibere, et calicem daemoniorum. Non potestis mensae Domini participes esse, et mensae daemoniorum (I Cor. x, 20, 21). An non tibi videtur non modo potatus sed et inebriatus calice daemoniorum, qui libidinis, irae, impatientiae, aut similium raptatur furore passionum? Ego, ut loquar de his quae jam magis in usu quam in crimine sunt, puto quod et ille mensae daemoniorum particeps sit, et pascens eos pascatur ab eis, cujus os abundat malitia, lingua concinnat dolos, sedens adversus fratrem suum loquitur et adversus filium matris suae ponit scandalum: qui etiamsi non detrahit, detrahentem libenter audit; qui scurrilitate stultiloquii cachinnantes dissolvit. Videat ergo qui ejusmodi est, an dignum sit ut, volutatus velut in cruore immolatio et sordibus daemonum, mox admittatur ad mensam Christi et Angelorum. Absit autem, fratres mei, ut, haec dicens, innocentiam vestram, qua plurimum delector, inscribam sceleri; sed haec dico ut salvi sitis, et aliorum exemplo cautiores facti, manusque lavantes in sanguine peccatoris, gratiam Spiritus sancti quam alii negligentia perdunt, vos omni vigilantia servare curetis; ad laudem et gloriam ipsius largitoris Jesu Christi Domini nostri, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Let them themselves consider, I beg, whether perhaps the enemy, who is accustomed to sow his tares over the householder's good seed, has, after that sweet food of Christ, stained their throats with his gall, and thereby blotted out not only the desire but even the memory of their former taste. Indeed, the Apostle at once warns those who are like this: You cannot drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of demons. You cannot share in the table of the Lord and the table of demons (I Cor. x, 20, 21). Does he not seem to you to have drunk from the cup of demons and even to be drunk with it, who is swept away by the fury of lust, anger, impatience or similar passions? To speak of things which are now more often treated as customary than as sinful, I think that he too shares in the table of demons, feeding them and being fed by them, whose mouth abounds in malice, whose tongue contrives deceit, who sits and speaks against his brother and sets a stumbling block against his mother's son; who, even if he does not slander, gladly listens to a slanderer; who with the buffoonery of foolish talk makes his hearers dissolve in loud laughter. Let such a man consider, then, whether it is fitting that, after wallowing as though in sacrificial blood and the filth of demons, he should at once be admitted to the table of Christ and the Angels. But far be it from me, my brothers, in saying this to charge with wickedness your innocence, in which I take very great delight. I say these things so that you may be saved and, made more watchful by the example of others and washing your hands in the blood of the sinner, may take care with all vigilance to preserve the grace of the Holy Spirit which others lose through negligence, to the praise and glory of its giver himself, Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon contains five numbered sections. The titles follow the printed edition."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites Luke 5 for the prodigal son; the narrative is in Luke 15. The printed reference is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition leaves the parenthesis after Isaiah 66:12 unclosed. The quotation is complete; the words that follow begin Guerric\u2019s comment."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Latin prints cruore immolatio, an irregular blood-and-sacrifice phrase. \u201cSacrificial blood\u201d conveys its meaning in this warning about the table of demons; the uncertain Latin wording is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-01-e005", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Hebrews quotation includes Hebrews 6:4 as well as the printed 6:5. The edition also cites 1 Corinthians 10:20\u201321 and leaves the parenthesis after Psalm 101:5 unclosed. The final image of washing hands in a sinner\u2019s blood is Guerric\u2019s biblical language."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Pentecost Sermon I: God's Charity and Human Ingratitude", "topics": ["Pentecost", "Holy Spirit", "charity", "grace", "hope", "presumption", "despair", "Eucharist"], "url": "sermons/guerric-pentecost-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:spiritus-domini", "calendar_label": "Pentecost Sunday", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-pentecost-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De linguarum mysterio et dono lacrymarum., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 160-164.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Pentecost", "Feast of Pentecost", "Acts 2:11", "Psalm 62:6 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:12 (historical numbering)", "John 6:45", "Psalm 93:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 18:8 (historical numbering)", "Lamentations 1:13", "Sirach 51:30", "Isaiah 50:4", "Acts 11:9", "Job 6:7", "Luke 15:7", "Psalm 118:103 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:24 (historical numbering)", "Amos 5:10", "Romans 12:9", "Psalm 49:16 (historical numbering)", "Job 16:7", "Proverbs 16:26", "Psalm 6:7 (historical numbering)", "Jeremiah 31:19", "Matthew 5:5", "John 16:7", "Proverbs 31:6\u20137", "2 Corinthians 6:10", "2 Corinthians 5:16", "Psalm 68:30 (historical numbering)", "Sirach 7:3"], "occasion": "In festo Pentecostes", "occasion_detail": "Feast of Pentecost", "occasion_label": "Pentecost", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the fiery tongues of the apostles at Pentecost. He wishes for a tongue to praise God, or at least one to heal the wounds of those who confess, and calls blessed those who draw grace from licking the sores of sinners. He asks for the unutterable groanings of the Spirit in place of the manual labor his office excuses, and urges the brothers to seek the gift of tears, through which the Spirit consoles those who mourn. The sermon appears under the Pentecost heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De linguarum mysterio et dono lacrymarum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the mystery of tongues and the gift of tears.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Loquebantur variis linguis Apostoli magnalia Dei (Act. II, 11.). Nimirum ex abundantia cordis linguae loquebantur in eis. Exsultationes Dei in gutture eorum, quia charitas Dei diffusa erat in cordibus eorum. O Domine Deus meus, et ego utique similiter laudarem, si similiter potatus essem. Quia vero arida est anima mea, ideo est et torpida lingua. Sed sicut adipe et pinguedine repleatur anima mea, et labiis exsultationis laudabit os meum (Psal. LXII, 6). Eructabunt labia mea hymnum; sed cum docueris me justificationes tuas: id est cum dederis gustare quam suavis es, ut discam te diligere toto corde, tota anima, tota virtute. Bonus es tu, et in bonitate tua doce me justificationes tuas (Psal. CXVIII, 12). Bonitas siquidem tua, unctio tua est, qua doces eos de quibus praedictum est: Erunt omnes docibiles Dei (Joan. VI, 45). Beatus homo quem tu erudieris, Domine, et de lege tua docueris eum (Psal. XCIII, 12). Lex Domini immaculata convertens animas (Psal. XVIII, 8), charitas est: lex utique ignea, quae est in dextera ejus, quae cum digito Dei scribitur super latitudinem cordis, et ipsum cor amoris incendio, et eos ignito fervere facit eloquio. De excelso, inquit, misit ignem in ossibus meis, et erudivit me (Thren. I, 13). O quanta facilitate et celeritate, quanta copia et facultate, ignis ille quem Dominus Jesus misit in terram, non solum erudivit imperitos, sed etiam expedivit impeditos! Prorsus igneae linguae, quas ex se dispertivit ignis iste, quae sic ignescere fecerunt non solum mentes, sed et linguas Apostolorum, ut etiam nunc pius auditor ad sermones ignescat eorum. Prorsus ignea lingua Petri, ignea lingua Pauli, in quorum verbis nunc quoque vivit ignis perpetuus, qui et super corda nostra scintillat, si accedamus, si aurum vel animum ab eorum sermonibus non avertamus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"The Apostles spoke in various tongues the great works of God\" (Act. II, 11.). Surely it was out of the abundance of the heart that their tongues spoke in them. The exultations of God were in their throat, because the charity of God had been poured out in their hearts. O Lord my God, I too would indeed praise you in like manner, if I had been given to drink in like manner. But because my soul is parched, my tongue too is sluggish. But as my soul shall be filled with marrow and fatness, my mouth shall praise you with lips of exultation (Psal. LXII, 6). My lips shall pour forth a hymn; but only when you have taught me your justifications \u2014 that is, when you have given me to taste how sweet you are, so that I may learn to love you with my whole heart, my whole soul, my whole strength. You are good, and in your goodness teach me your justifications (Psal. CXVIII, 12). For your goodness is your anointing, by which you teach those of whom it was foretold: \"They shall all be taught of God\" (Joan. VI, 45). Blessed is the man whom you, O Lord, shall instruct, and whom you shall teach out of your law (Psal. XCIII, 12). The law of the Lord, unspotted, converting souls (Psal. XVIII, 8), is charity \u2014 indeed, a fiery law, which is in his right hand, which is written with the finger of God upon the breadth of the heart, and sets the heart itself ablaze with the fire of love, and makes them burn with fiery speech. \"From on high,\" he says, \"he sent fire into my bones, and taught me\" (Thren. I, 13). O with what ease and swiftness, with what abundance and power, did that fire which the Lord Jesus sent upon the earth not only instruct the untaught, but also set free the hindered! Truly fiery were the tongues which this fire distributed from itself, which so set ablaze not only the minds but also the tongues of the Apostles, that even now the devout hearer catches fire at their sermons. Truly fiery is the tongue of Peter, fiery the tongue of Paul, in whose words even now a perpetual fire lives, which also sparks upon our hearts, if we draw near, if we do not turn our ear or our mind away from their sermons.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Si meruissem aliquam accipere de linguis hujusmodi, dicerem utique et ego, Dominus dedit mihi linguam mercedem meam, et in ipsa laudabo eum (Eccli. LI, 30); sicut de illis scriptum est, Loquebantur variis linguis Apostoli magnalia Dei. Dicerem et illud: Dominus dedit mihi linguam eruditam ut sciam sustentare eum qui lapsus est, verbo (Isai. L, 4). Apostoli et eorum similes linguis sibi datis magnalia Dei praedicant, tyrannos verberant, daemonia flagellant, terram compluunt, coelos aperiunt; quia linguae eorum claves coeli factae sunt, quippe quibus de ipsis coelis linguae missae sunt. Mihi autem utinam vel lingua canis data sit, qua possim primum quidem propria, deinde et aliena ulcera lingere, si qui forte sunt qui ad hoc ipsum sibi me dignentur admittere. Beati quidem, quibus amor et dilectio divinae laudis cor replet gaudio et os jubilo. Sed et illos utique beatos dixerim, qui, cum de vulneribus animarum virus ablingunt et purulentiam, trahunt in se, quo anima eorum saginetur, spiritum et gratiam. Esuriunt siquidem et sitiunt justitiam, et famem patiuntur ut canes: ideoque nihil fastidiunt quod in corpus suum possint trajicere; neminem peccatorem abominantur, quem ad justitiam possint convertere. Quod Deus mundavit, tu ne immundum dixeris (Act. XI, 9), dictum est Principi Apostolorum, et in ipso aliis: et ob hoc mactat et manducat omne genus reptilium et volatilium, et ait, Quae prius tangere nolebat anima mea, nunc prae angustia impatientis desiderii cibi mei sunt (Job. VI, 7). Quinimo mirum in modum quanto quisque peccator ante conversionem amarior, tanto postmodum conversio ejus dulcior; quantoque desperatior, tanto gratior nobis ejus salus: quia et salvantis gratiam plus miramur, qui ovem perditam reportans in humeris, gaudium facit Angelis super uno peccatore poenitentiam agente, quam super nonaginta novem justis (Luc. XV, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. If I had merited to receive any of such tongues, I too would certainly say: \"The Lord has given me a tongue as my reward, and with it I will praise him\" (Eccli. LI, 30); just as it is written of them, \"The Apostles spoke in various tongues the great works of God.\" I would also say this: \"The Lord has given me a learned tongue, that I may know how to sustain with a word him who has fallen\" (Isai. L, 4). The Apostles, and those like them, with the tongues given them, preach the great works of God, scourge tyrants, flog demons, rain down upon the earth, open the heavens; for their tongues have become the keys of heaven, since it was indeed from heaven itself that tongues were sent to them. But as for me \u2014 would that at least the tongue of a dog were given to me, with which I might lick, first my own wounds, and then those of others, if perhaps there are any who would deign to admit me to this very task on their behalf. Blessed indeed are those whose heart the love and affection of divine praise fills with joy, and whose mouth with jubilation. But I would also surely call blessed those who, in licking away the venom and pus from the wounds of souls, draw into themselves the spirit and grace by which their own soul is fattened. For they hunger and thirst after justice, and suffer hunger like dogs; and therefore they disdain nothing that they can take into their own body; they abominate no sinner whom they might be able to convert to justice. \"What God has cleansed, do not call unclean\" (Act. XI, 9), was said to the Prince of the Apostles, and in him to others; and for this reason he slaughters and eats every kind of creeping thing and bird, and says: \"The things my soul formerly refused to touch are now, through the anguish of impatient desire, my food\" (Job. VI, 7). Indeed, in a wondrous way, the more bitter any sinner was before his conversion, the sweeter afterward is his conversion; and the more he was despaired of, the more welcome to us is his salvation \u2014 because we marvel all the more at the grace of him who saves, who, carrying the lost sheep back on his shoulders, causes more joy to the Angels over one sinner doing penance, than over ninety-nine just men (Luc. XV, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Dicant igitur alii quibus datum est: Quam dulcia faucibus meis eloquia tua Domine (Psal. CXVIII, 103)! anima autem mea esuriens et famem patiens ut canis, etiam amarum sumet pro dulci, et abominabile pro desiderabili. Delectentur alii lambere mella Scripturarum; ego vero delectabor ulcera lingere peccatorum, mei scilicet mihique similium. Foedum quidem ulcus peccati, visuque horribile; sed tamen ipsum lingere, cujus sit saporis et gratiae, non sapit, non capit, nisi qui salutem periclitantium esurit, et famem patitur ut canis: de qualibus dictum est, Lingua canum tuorum ex inimicis (Psal. CXVIII, 24). Sed vae miseris qui tanto studio pereunt, tantoque desiderio mortem sibi consciscunt, ut vulnera sua tegant, curamque canum refugiant, et asperitatem linguae medicinalis arbitrentur morsum odii lethalis! Oderunt corripientem in porta, et perfecte loquentem abominati sunt (Amos. V, 10). Perfecte loquentem dico, non tam de perfectione disserentem, quam perfecta dilectione corripientem. Sed dicam, Perfecta dilectione, an perfecto odio? Imo utrumque, et perfecta dilectione, et perfecto odio: quia perfectum odium nihil aliud est, quam perfecta dilectio; et una est utriusque perfectio, quam Apostolus determinat, dicens, Odientes malum, adhaerentes autem bono (Rom. XII, 9)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Let others, then, to whom it has been given, say: \"How sweet are your words to my palate, O Lord!\" (Psal. CXVIII, 103). But my soul, hungering and suffering famine like a dog, will take even the bitter for sweet, and the abominable for the desirable. Let others delight to lick the honey of the Scriptures; I, however, will delight to lick the sores of sinners\u2014namely, my own, and those of men like me. Foul indeed is the sore of sin, and horrible to see; yet what savor and grace there is in licking it, no one tastes, no one grasps, except him who hungers for the salvation of those in peril, and suffers famine like a dog: of such it is said, \"The tongue of your dogs shall be red from the enemies\" (Psal. CXVIII, 24). But woe to the wretched who perish with such zeal, and bring death upon themselves with such desire, that they cover their wounds and flee the care of dogs, judging the roughness of the healing tongue to be the bite of deadly hatred! \"They hated him that reproved in the gate, and abhorred him that spoke perfectly\" (Amos. V, 10). I say 'spoke perfectly'\u2014not so much one discoursing about perfection, as one reproving with perfect love. But shall I say 'with perfect love,' or 'with perfect hatred'? Rather both: with perfect love and with perfect hatred; for perfect hatred is nothing other than perfect love, and the perfection of both is one, which the Apostle defines, saying, \"Hating that which is evil, cleaving to that which is good\" (Rom. XII, 9).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Sed revertar ad id unde digressus eram. Optabam mihi linguam qua laudarem Deum, vel certe linguam qua confitentium ulcera curarem: in altero quidem mihi quaerens divinae devotionis fructum; in altero, fraternae salutis lucrum: vobis quoque in altero dulcis, in altero fieri desiderans utilis. Nam et de saecularium arte et officio poetarum, ita sumptum est apud quemdam eorum: \nAut prodesse volunt, aut delectare poetae.\nOmne tulit punctum qui miscuit utile dulci.\n(HORAT., Art. poet.)\n Optabam, inquam, mihi gratiam oris, qua Deo et vobis officiosus deservirem; ut aliquatenus compensarem verbo, quod minus prosum exemplo. Quanquam satis suspecta mihi sit haec consolatio, si dicam et non faciam; si suppetant suffragia linguae, cum desint merita vitae; imo magis fortasse timendum sit, ne peccanti dicat Deus, Quare tu enarras justitias meas, et assumis testamentum meum per os tuum? (Psal. XLIX, 16). Sed quid agam? Si locutus fuero, non quiescet dolor meus; et si tacuero, non recedet a me (Job. XVI, 7). Pavor est utrobique molestus, et coarctor e duobus; quia locutionem et officii locus exigit, et vita contradicit. Sed recordor verbi quod apud Sapientem inveni: Anima laborantis laborat sibi, quia compulit eum os suum (Prov. XVI, 26). Loquar igitur, non prout locus expetit, sed prout sensus suppetit, imo prout Dominus dederit, in cujus manu sumus et nos, et sermones nostri. Loquar, inquam, et me ipsum lingua obligabo propria, ut vel prae confusione aliquando laborare compellar: et si corpus a labore manuum excusaverit, certe anima laborantis laboret sibi, ut dicat cum David, Laboravi in gemitu meo (Psal. VI, 7). O si mihi dentur illi gemitus inenarrabiles, quibus Spiritus postulat pro sanctis, ut laborem in eis! Procul dubio labor hujusmodi gemituum satis digne pensaret mihi quotidianum laborem manuum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. But I will return to the point from which I digressed. I was wishing for myself a tongue with which to praise God, or at least a tongue with which to heal the sores of those who confess: seeking in the one case for myself the fruit of divine devotion, in the other the gain of my brothers' salvation; desiring to become, for you also, sweet in the one case, useful in the other. For concerning the art and office of the secular poets, this is taken from one of them: 'Poets wish either to profit or to please. He has carried every point who has mixed the useful with the sweet.' (Horace, Art of Poetry). I was wishing, I say, for the grace of speech, by which I might dutifully serve both God and you, so that I might in some measure make up by word for my being of less use by example. Though this consolation is quite suspect to me, if I should speak and not do; if the tongue's resources should be at hand while the merits of life are lacking\u2014indeed it is rather to be feared that God might say to the sinner, \"Why do you declare my justices, and take my covenant into your mouth?\" (Psal. XLIX, 16). But what shall I do? \"If I speak, my sorrow will not rest; and if I am silent, it will not depart from me\" (Job. XVI, 7). The dread is troublesome on both sides, and I am constrained between the two; for the place of duty demands speech, and my life contradicts it. But I recall a saying I found in the Wise Man: \"The soul of him who labors, labors for himself, because his own mouth has compelled him\" (Prov. XVI, 26). I will speak, then, not as the occasion demands, but as understanding supplies\u2014rather, as the Lord shall grant, in whose hand are both we ourselves and our words. I will speak, I say, and I will bind myself by my own tongue, so that I may at least be compelled by shame to labor at some point: and if the body be excused from the labor of the hands, let the soul of him who labors certainly labor for itself, that it may say with David, \"I have labored in my groaning\" (Psal. VI, 7). O, if only those unutterable groanings might be given to me, with which the Spirit intercedes for the saints, that I might labor in them! Without doubt, labor of such groanings would quite worthily make up for my daily labor of hands.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed et vos ipsi, fratres, si aemulari meliora charismata didicistis, optate gemitus hujusmodi a Spiritu vobis infundi. Nescio si sit in donis Spiritus aliud convenientius aut commodius his qui sunt infirmitate et miseria circumdati: nescio si Spiritui sancto qui in columba apparuit, alia vox familiarior gemitu, aut gratior sit. Hoc autem scio, quia in nullo opere tanta tamque parata nobis suppetat materia, sicut in gemitu et planctu nostri: nisi miseriam suam nostra dissimulet superbia, aut sensus obduretur stupore vel amentia. Hoc autem primum circa infirmos, quorum curam suscipit, operatur medicina sancti Spiritus, qui est illuminatio nostra et salus nostra, ut videlicet se ipsum phreneticus sentiat et sciat, et conversus ad cor dicat Domino cum propheta: Postquam convertisti me, egi poenitentiam; et postquam ostendisti mihi, percussi femur meum (Jerem. XXXI, 19). Nisi enim quis apponat scientiam, non apponet dolorem: nisi doluerit, non meretur consolationem. Beati enim qui lugent, quoniam ipsi consolabuntur (Matth. V, 5). Puto quia nec in Apostolis hodie locum invenisset Paracleti consolatio, nisi quia lugebant se desolatos, quia non poterant non lugere filii sponsi, cum sponsus ablatus esset ab eis. Inde erat quod eis dicebat: Nisi ego abiero, Paracletus non veniet ad vos (Joan. XVI, 7). Nisi desolati fueritis praesentia mea corporali, non vos consolabitur visitatio spiritualis. Date, inquit, siceram moerentibus, et vinum his qui amaro animo sunt, non scilicet illis qui ebrii sunt gaudio et luxu saeculi. Quae enim participatio justitiae cum iniquitate? nunquid bibere possunt calicem Domini, et calicem daemoniorum? Bibant, inquit, potius pauperes Apostoli, et obliviscantur egestatis suae (Prov. XXXI, 6, 7), ut dicant: Sicut egentes multos autem, locupletantes (II Cor. VI. 10). Bibant moerentes de absentia sponsi, et dolorum non recordentur amplius, sed dicant: Etsi cognovimus Christum secundum carnem, sed nunc jam non novimus (Id. V, 16). Nam et te ipsum, si pio affectu Psalmistae dicere potueris, Ego sum pauper et dolens (Psal. LXVIII, 30), sic ditabit, sic, laetificabit sobria ebrietas hujus praeclari calicis, ut, etsi pauper sis, paupertas animum non urat; et si dolebas quia peccasti, jam peccati reatus conscientiam non mordeat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But you yourselves also, brothers, if you have learned to be zealous for the better gifts, desire that such groanings be poured into you by the Spirit. I do not know whether there is among the gifts of the Spirit anything more fitting or more suitable for those who are surrounded by weakness and misery; I do not know whether to the Holy Spirit, who appeared in the form of a dove, any voice is more familiar or more pleasing than a groan. But this I know: that in no work is there so great and so ready a store of material for us as in groaning and lamenting over ourselves\u2014unless our pride disguises our own misery, or our perception is hardened by stupor or madness. Now this is the first thing that the medicine of the Holy Spirit, who is our illumination and our salvation, works upon the sick whose care it undertakes: that the one who is deranged should feel and know himself to be so, and, turning to his heart, say to the Lord with the prophet: 'After you converted me, I did penance; and after you showed me, I struck my thigh' (Jerem. XXXI, 19). For unless one adds knowledge, he will not add sorrow; unless he has grieved, he does not merit consolation. For blessed are they that mourn, for they shall be comforted (Matth. V, 5). I think that the consolation of the Paraclete would not have found a place among the Apostles today, had they not been mourning themselves as desolate\u2014for the sons of the bridegroom could not but mourn when the bridegroom had been taken from them. Hence it was that he said to them: 'Unless I go away, the Paraclete will not come to you' (Joan. XVI, 7). Unless you have been left desolate of my bodily presence, a spiritual visitation will not console you. 'Give,' he says, 'strong drink to those that mourn, and wine to those who are of bitter mind'\u2014not, that is, to those who are drunk with the joy and excess of this world. For what fellowship has justice with iniquity? Can they drink the cup of the Lord and the cup of devils? Let the poor Apostles rather drink,' he says, 'and forget their poverty' (Prov. XXXI, 6, 7), so that they may say: 'As needy, yet enriching many' (II Cor. VI. 10). Let those who mourn over the absence of the bridegroom drink, and let them remember their sorrows no more, but say: 'And if we have known Christ according to the flesh, yet now we know him so no longer' (Id. V, 16). For you yourself too, if with the pious feeling of the Psalmist you can say, 'I am poor and sorrowful' (Psal. LXVIII, 30), the sober inebriation of this glorious cup will so enrich you, will so gladden you, that although you are poor, poverty will not burn your soul; and if you were sorrowful because you had sinned, the guilt of sin will no longer gnaw at your conscience.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Vide autem, si non et ipse Spiritus sanctus in judicium venit in hunc mundum, ut qui dolent non doleant, et qui rident, aeterno et inconsolabili se luctui tradant. Melius est igitur ire ad domum luctus quam ad domum convivii (Eccli. VII, 3). Et revera sapiens, etsi aliquando consolationem meretur, ut dolorum non recordetur amplius, eorum scilicet de quibus consolationem accipit, semper tamen, ut locum paret consolationibus novis, novas exquirit de se ipso causas doloris; non statim blandiens sibi, quasi jam per omnia justus sit; sed eo subtilior, quo jam illuminari coepit; eo districtior, quo justificari coepit, fit accusator et judex sui. Ei ergo qui ejusmodi est, crebro, nisi fallor, Spiritus consolator advenit, quoniam et adventum jam ipse suum antevenit; adveniens scilicet ut consolationem tribuat, sed anteveniens, ut lugere doceat. Luctus siquidem pius atque religiosus, in doctrina Spiritus et ordine primus, et utilitate invenitur praecipuus; quippe cum sit summa sapientia sanctorum, custodia justorum, sobrietas modestorum, prima incipientium virtus, proficientium stimulus, perfectorum cumulus, pereuntium salus, periclitantium portus, promissionem denique habens consolationum quae nunc sunt, et gaudiorum quae futura sunt; ad quae nos perducat qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. But consider whether the Holy Spirit himself does not come into this world for judgment, so that those who grieve may grieve no more, and those who laugh may give themselves over to eternal and inconsolable mourning. It is better, therefore, to go to the house of mourning than to the house of feasting (Eccli. VII, 3). And indeed the wise man, even though he sometimes merits consolation so that he no longer remembers those sorrows for which he receives comfort, nevertheless always seeks out new causes of sorrow from himself, so as to make room for new consolations; not at once flattering himself as though he were already righteous in every respect, but becoming his own accuser and judge\u2014the more discerning, the more he has begun to be enlightened; the more strict, the more he has begun to be justified. To one, then, who is of this kind, the comforting Spirit, unless I am mistaken, comes often, since he himself anticipates his own coming\u2014coming, that is, to grant consolation, but anticipating it in order to teach mourning. For pious and devout mourning is found, in the teaching of the Spirit, to be first in order and chief in usefulness; since it is the highest wisdom of the saints, the guardianship of the just, the sobriety of the modest, the first virtue of beginners, the spur of those advancing, the crown of the perfect, the salvation of those perishing, the harbor of those in peril\u2014having, finally, the promise both of the consolations that now are, and of the joys that are to come; to which may he bring us who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-pentecost-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints aurum (gold), where aurem (ear) is expected alongside animum (mind)."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Pentecost Sermon II", "topics": ["Pentecost", "Holy Spirit", "tongues", "preaching", "confession", "conversion", "tears", "compunction"], "url": "sermons/guerric-pentecost-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:peter-paul", "calendar_label": "Sts. Peter and Paul, Apostles", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-peter-paul-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN NATALI APOSTOLORUM PETRI ET PAULI, SERMO I., De duabus olivis ad dexteram candelabri., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 177-180.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saints Peter and Paul", "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "Zechariah 4:4, 11, 14", "John 8:12", "Zechariah 4:14", "Psalm 127:3 (historical numbering)", "Habakkuk 3:17", "Jeremiah 11:16", "Lamentations 1:13", "Psalm 108:24 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:66 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 122:2 (historical numbering)", "4 Kings 5:16 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)"], "occasion": "In natali Apostolorum Petri et Pauli", "occasion_detail": "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "occasion_label": "Saints Peter and Paul", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads Zechariah's vision of two olive trees beside the golden lampstand as Peter and Paul, sons of the oil of splendor who stand before the Lord. Christ is the lampstand, golden by substance, while believers are gilded by grace. Guerric reminds the brothers, who share one property and one house, to keep the fervor of their first profession, and reads the oil of splendor as the Holy Spirit, who anoints and enlightens. The sermon appears under the heading for the Apostles Peter and Paul in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN NATALI APOSTOLORUM PETRI ET PAULI"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE BIRTHDAY (FEAST) OF THE APOSTLES PETER AND PAUL", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De duabus olivis ad dexteram candelabri."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the two olive trees at the right hand of the lampstand.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Et respondi, inquit Zacharias, et dixi ad angelum qui loquebatur in me: Quid sunt duae olivae istae ad dexteram candelabri et ad sinistram ejus? Isti sunt, inquit, duo filii olei splendoris, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae (Zach. IV, 4, 11, 14). De candelabro quod viderat totum aureum, et septem lucernas ejus super illud intellexerat Propheta, sed nec nobis dubitare jam licet, cum in lumine ejus videamus lumen. Hoc lumen cum alia demonstret, seipsum ignorari non patitur, sed testimonium perhibens de seipso loquitur: Ego sum lux mundi (Joan. VIII, 12). Lux ex divinitate, candelabrum ex humanitate. Et ipsum totum aureum, non auratum; quia sive divinitas, sive sapientia, sive charitas auro significetur, haec omnia Christus non per participationem, sed per substantiam. Nos cum simus natura plumbei, vel magis lutei, gratia et participatione ipsius, non aurum vel aurei, sed ut multum aurati. Septem lucernae super ipsum, septem spiritus, septem oculi Domini, qui discurrunt in universam terram. De his ergo Propheta certus, de duabus olivis quidnam sint percunctatur, auditque ab angelo qui in eo loquebatur: Isti sunt duo filii olei splendoris, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae (Zach. IV, 14). Interpretentur alii ut volunt aut possunt, unusquisque pro sensu suo: sive Enoch et Eliam, qui apud Deum in secreto vivunt; sive Moysen et Eliam, qui cum eo in monte visi sunt; sive duos ordines praedicatorum, veteris scilicet ac novi Testamenti: ut qui Veteris, ad sinistram sint propter promissa temporalia; qui Novi, ad dexteram, propter aeterna: ego quidquid a regula fidei non discrepat, aut a contextu lectionis non nimie dissonat, suscipio, veneror, amplector."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"And I answered,\" says Zechariah, \"and I said to the angel who spoke in me: What are these two olive trees at the right hand of the lampstand, and at the left thereof? These,\" he says, \"are the two sons of the oil of splendor, who stand before the Ruler of the whole earth\" (Zach. IV, 4, 11, 14). The Prophet had understood the lampstand which he had seen, all of gold, and its seven lamps upon it; but we are no longer permitted to doubt, since in his light we see light. This light, though it shows other things, does not allow itself to go unknown, but bears witness of itself and says: \"I am the light of the world\" (Joan. VIII, 12). Light from his divinity, lampstand from his humanity. And it itself is wholly gold, not gilded; because whether divinity, or wisdom, or charity is signified by gold, Christ is all these, not by participation, but by substance. We, since by nature we are leaden, or rather of clay, by grace and participation in him are not gold or golden, but at most gilded. The seven lamps upon him are the seven spirits, the seven eyes of the Lord, which run through the whole earth. Concerning these, then, the Prophet, being certain, inquires what the two olive trees might be, and hears from the angel who was speaking in him: \"These are the two sons of the oil of splendor, who stand before the Ruler of the whole earth\" (Zach. IV, 14). Let others interpret as they will or can, each according to his own understanding: whether Enoch and Elijah, who live with God in secret; or Moses and Elijah, who were seen with him on the mountain; or the two orders of preachers, namely of the Old and the New Testament\u2014so that those of the Old are on the left on account of temporal promises, and those of the New are on the right on account of eternal ones: I accept, venerate, and embrace whatever does not disagree with the rule of faith, nor conflict excessively with the context of the reading.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Puto tamen, sine praejudicio melioris intellectus, Petrum et Paulum non absurde posse intelligi: imo absurdum videri, qui hodie aliud interpretari velit, cum hodie nihil aliud cantet aut tractet Ecclesia ubique terrarum, quam praeconia Patrum doctorumque suorum. Nam etsi aliis possit aptari, puto quia istis specialius congruit, seu illud quod tanquam duae olivae circa candelabrum a Propheta videntur, seu quod duo filii esse olei splendoris ab angelo memorantur. Et alterum quidem altero exponitur, visio scilicet prophetica interpretatione angelica: ut inde intelligantur olivae, quia filii olei splendoris; inde circa candelabrum, quia Domino assistunt. Quia enim ex oleo Spiritus sancti sunt regenerati, hodieque florent et fructificant sicut oliva fructifera in domo Domini, etiam oleastro gentili, qui in ipsos insertus est, impertientes pinguedinis suae succum ac dulcedinis, gratias illis agat sterilis dudum et amara gentilitas; et si non ab ipsis, tamen per ipsos est, quod nunc tam copioso, tam pretioso fructu per orbem abundat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. I think, however, without prejudice to a better understanding, that Peter and Paul can not unreasonably be understood here; indeed it would seem unreasonable for anyone today to wish to interpret it otherwise, since today the Church throughout the whole world sings or treats of nothing else than the praises of her Fathers and teachers. For even though it could be fitted to others, I think that it applies more specially to these two, whether as regards what is seen by the Prophet as two olive trees around the lampstand, or what is called by the angel the two sons of the oil of splendor. And indeed the one is explained by the other\u2014the prophetic vision, that is, by the angelic interpretation\u2014so that from it we understand them to be olive trees because they are sons of the oil of splendor, and around the lampstand, because they stand before the Lord. For since they were regenerated from the oil of the Holy Spirit, and today they flourish and bear fruit like a fruitful olive tree in the house of the Lord, even the wild olive of the Gentiles, which has been grafted into them, receiving from them the sap of their richness and sweetness\u2014let the Gentile world, once barren and bitter, give thanks to them; and even if it is not from them, yet it is through them that there now abounds throughout the world so abundant, so precious a fruit.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vobis tamen, fratres, quibus sicut substantia et domus una, ita cor unum et anima una: vobis, inquam, specialius in ipsis censeo gloriandum, qui sicut novella olivarum de eorum radice traduxistis non solum fidei succum, sed et formam vitae atque ordinis exemplum. Quod si novella olivarum vos, utique et filii olei vos, filii uncti, qui est Christus Dei; diceturque et de vobis, cum hodie ad altare accedetis: Filii tui sicut novellae olivarum, in circuitu mensae tuae (Psal. CXXVII, 3). O novella olivarum nobilium! o generosum germen tam pinguium, tamque feracium arborum! semper, obsecro, recordare de qua pullulaveris stirpe, de qua succreveris radice, ne unquam sterilitas operum, aut amaritudo morum te degenerem arguat, aut, quod absit, inutilem ignibus addicat. Cave, quaeso, notam infelicium temporum, de quibus meministi prophetatum: Mentietur opus olivae, et arva non afferent cibum (Habac. III, 17). Duo tamen praecipue tibi existimo cavenda: ne aut per teporem fias sicut oliva projiciens florem suum, sicut legis in Propheta, ut videlicet fructum quem in initiis novitii fervoris promittebas non facias; aut cum feceris, ne per superbiam perdas, et dicat tibi Jeremias: Olivam uberem, pulchram, fructiferam, speciosam vocavit Dominus nomen tuum: sed heu! ad vocem loquelae grandis exarsit ignis in ea, et combusta sunt omnia fruteta ejus (Jerem. XI, 16). Confidimus autem de vobis meliora, dilectissimi, tametsi ita loquamur. Confidimus quia oleum splendoris, vel magis olei splendor, cujus estis filii, sicut et patres nostri Apostoli, unctione sua docebit vos de omnibus: nec solum deterget torporem, quia oleum est; sed et discutiet errorem, quia splendor est."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Yet to you, brothers, for whom, just as there is one property and one house, so there is one heart and one soul: to you, I say, I judge it belongs more especially to glory in them, you who, like young shoots of the olive, have derived from their root not only the sap of faith, but also the pattern of life and the example of your order. But if you are young shoots of the olive trees, then surely you too are sons of the oil, sons of the Anointed, who is the Christ of God; and it shall be said of you also, when today you approach the altar: \"Your children are like young olive plants round about your table\" (Psal. CXXVII, 3). O noble young shoots of olive trees! O generous offspring of trees so rich and so fruitful! Always, I beg you, remember from what stock you have sprung, from what root you have grown, lest ever the barrenness of works, or the bitterness of conduct, convict you of degeneracy, or\u2014God forbid\u2014condemn you as useless to the fires. Beware, I beg, of the mark of unhappy times, of which you remember it was prophesied: \"The labor of the olive shall fail, and the fields shall yield no food\" (Habac. III, 17). Yet I think there are two things especially you must guard against: either that through lukewarmness you become like an olive tree casting off its flower, as you read in the Prophet\u2014that is, that you fail to produce the fruit which in the beginnings of your novice fervor you promised; or, having produced it, that through pride you lose it, and Jeremiah say to you: \"The Lord called your name a fruitful olive tree, fair, fruitful, beautiful: but alas! at the voice of a great utterance fire has blazed up in it, and its branches are all burned\" (Jerem. XI, 16). We trust, however, better things of you, most beloved, even though we speak thus. We trust that the oil of splendor\u2014or rather, the splendor of the oil\u2014whose sons you are, as were our fathers the Apostles, will by its anointing teach you all things: not only will it wipe away sluggishness, because it is oil, but it will also dispel error, because it is splendor.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Scio posse intelligi olei splendoris, id est, quod de splendore Filio procedit, vel quod splendorem illuminationis diffundit: sed puto quia non inconvenienter etiam per intransitionem accipi potest oleum, quod splendor est. Utrumque siquidem Spiritus est in natura sui, et unctio scilicet spiritualis, et splendor invisibilis: utrumque in nobis operatur munere sui. Ungit affectus ex eo quod oleum est: illuminat intellectus ex eo quod splendor est. Qui tamen non ex alio atque alio, sed ex uno ipso et oleum, et splendor est; quia nonnisi unum ipsum est. Ungit affectus, quia charitas est: illuminat intellectus, quia veritas est. Ungit affectus, cum devotionem donat: illuminat intellectus, cum mysteria revelat. Cum bonitatem docet, ut simus simplices sicut columbae, ungit affectus: cum scientiam docet, ut simus prudentes sicut serpentes, illuminat intellectus. Illuminat, ut habeamus in nobis salem: ungit, ut habeamus inter nos pacem. In hoc igitur te nobis probabis filium olei splendoris, si suavem et unctum per benevolentiam, discretum et maturum per sapientiam te nobis exhibueris; ut suavitas veritate sit condita, et aequitate charitas ordinata. Puto autem quod hanc germinam virtutem sui, vel affectum muneris sui, propter quam splendor voluit dici, etiam in illo igne Spiritus sanctus significaverit, in quo ipse invisibilis invisibiliter Apostolis apparuit. Qui enim splendor fuit in aspectu oculorum, oleum, nisi fallor, fuit in sensu corporum: ut veluti in quodam lavacro olei viderentur sibi baptizati, cum super eos effunderetur ignis iste coelestis. Verumtamen qualiscunque fuerit in eis corporaliter sensus illius ignis (parum enim interest qualis sentiatur, sed qualiter operetur), illud, ut opinor, et probabilius dicitur, et religiosius creditur, quod idem ignis qui inflammavit et illuminavit corda, refrigeravit corpora: ut per charitatem mentes arderent, et per castitatem membra frigerent, et utrumque congrueret singulis, sive illud Jeremiae, De excelso misit ignem in ossibus meis (Thren. I, 13); sive illud Psalmistae, Caro mea immutata est propter oleum (Psal. CVIII, 24). Nisi forte aliquid de lege membrorum contra legem mentis relinquere voluit Spiritus, ut virtus in infirmitate perficeretur."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. I know that 'oil of splendor' can be understood as meaning that which proceeds from Splendor, the Son, or that which pours forth the splendor of illumination. But I think that, not unfittingly, it can also be taken appositionally, as 'oil, which is splendor.' For indeed the Spirit is both of these in his very nature\u2014spiritual anointing, and invisible splendor\u2014and he works both of these in us by the gift of himself. He anoints the affections insofar as he is oil; he illuminates the intellect insofar as he is splendor. Yet he is not oil from one source and splendor from another, but from one and the same self he is both oil and splendor, because he is nothing other than one and the same. He anoints the affections, because he is charity; he illuminates the intellect, because he is truth. He anoints the affections when he grants devotion; he illuminates the intellect when he reveals mysteries. When he teaches goodness, that we may be simple as doves, he anoints the affections; when he teaches knowledge, that we may be prudent as serpents, he illuminates the intellect. He illuminates, that we may have salt within us; he anoints, that we may have peace among ourselves. In this, then, you will prove yourself to us a son of the oil of splendor, if you show yourself to us sweet and anointed through benevolence, and discreet and mature through wisdom; so that sweetness may be seasoned with truth, and charity ordered by equity. I think, moreover, that the Holy Spirit signified this twofold power of his\u2014or this affective working of his gift\u2014on account of which he wished to be called splendor, even in that fire in which he, invisible, appeared invisibly to the Apostles. For what was splendor to the sight of their eyes was, unless I am mistaken, oil to the sense of their bodies: so that they seemed to themselves baptized as in a kind of bath of oil, when that heavenly fire was poured out upon them. Yet whatever the bodily sensation of that fire may have been in them (for it matters little how it was felt, but how it worked), this, I think, is both the more probable and the more devout belief: that the same fire which kindled and illumined their hearts also cooled their bodies, so that through charity their minds burned, and through chastity their members grew cool; and each saying fit the case\u2014whether that of Jeremiah, 'He sent fire from on high into my bones' (Thren. I, 13), or that of the Psalmist, 'My flesh is changed because of oil' (Psal. CVIII, 24). Unless perhaps the Spirit wished to leave something of the law of the members at war with the law of the mind, so that power might be perfected in weakness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Sed prosequamur quod proposuimus. Isti sunt, inquit, duo filii olei splendoris, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae (Zach. IV, 14). Ecce habes bonitatem in oleo, scientiam in splendore: accipe disciplinam in eo quod ait, qui assistunt Dominatori universae terrae; ut agnoscas eos vere discipulos illius fuisse cui cantamus, Bonitatem et disciplinam et scientiam doce me (Psal. CXVIII, 66). Haec est namque perfectio disciplinae, tam in mente, quam in corpore; ut proponentes Dominum in conspectu nostro semper, semper et ubique majestati aeternae, quae nos inspicit et judicat sine intermissione, cum metu et reverentia assistamus, pervigili fide ac jugi devotione. Sicut oculi, inquit, servorum in manibus dominorum suorum, ita oculi nostri ad Dominum Deum nostrum (Psal. CXXII, 2), intentissime et indeclinabiliter observantes nutus ejus, quid velit, quid jubeat, ut quam celerrime devotio nostra obediat. Sic Moyses invisibilem tanquam videns sustinebat, nec unquam imminentis metum judicis dissimulare poterat. Sic Elias et alii Prophetae qui dicebant: Vivit Dominus, in cujus conspectu sto (IV Reg. V, 16). Inde nimirum et gratiam, et nomen assecuti sunt Videntium; quoniam quae semper intuebantur per fidem non fictam, merebantur saepius intueri per speciem, licet factam. Haec fides vigil, non ficta, non dissimulatrix, ipsa est, ut arbitror, quae sola vel maxime facili compendio ad perfectionem perducit; quae gravitatem disciplinae, sobrietatemque modestiae, tam interiori quam exteriori nostro tribuit, et velut in disciplina famulantium Domino semper assistere facit. Hac virtute meruerunt Apostoli dici filii olei splendoris: ut sicut Domino astiterunt in terris contemplando per fidem, ita nunc ei assistant in coelis contemplando per speciem; Regemque in decore suo videant oculi eorum, cujus timorem hic meditabatur cor eorum. Hac via, fratres, sequimini Patres fidei vestrae, et auctores Ordinis, quatenus eorum provocati exemplis et adjuti suffragiis, quo ipsi hodie praecesserunt, pervenire valeatis, adjuvante Domino nostro Jesu Christo, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But let us continue what we have proposed. 'These,' he says, 'are the two sons of the oil of splendor, who stand before the Lord of all the earth' (Zach. IV, 14). Behold, you have goodness in the oil, knowledge in the splendor: receive discipline in what he says, 'who stand before the Lord of all the earth'; that you may recognize that they were truly disciples of him to whom we sing, 'Teach me goodness, and discipline, and knowledge' (Psal. CXVIII, 66). For this is the perfection of discipline, both in mind and in body: that, setting the Lord ever before our sight, we should always and everywhere stand before the eternal majesty\u2014which beholds and judges us without ceasing\u2014with fear and reverence, with ever-watchful faith and unfailing devotion. 'As the eyes of servants are on the hands of their masters, so are our eyes toward the Lord our God' (Psal. CXXII, 2), watching most intently and unswervingly for his least sign\u2014what he wills, what he commands\u2014so that our devotion may obey as swiftly as possible. Thus Moses endured, as one who saw him who is invisible, and could never put out of mind the fear of the Judge who was at hand. Thus Elijah and the other Prophets, who said: 'The Lord lives, in whose sight I stand' (IV Reg. V, 16). From this, surely, they obtained both the grace and the name of 'Seers'; because that which they always beheld through unfeigned faith, they often merited to behold through a vision\u2014though a created one. This watchful faith, not feigned, not dissembling, is itself, I think, that which alone, or at least most readily and by the easiest path, leads to perfection; which bestows the gravity of discipline and the sobriety of modesty on both our inward and our outward self, and causes us, as it were in the discipline of servants, to stand always before the Lord. By this virtue the Apostles deserved to be called sons of the oil of splendor: so that just as they stood before the Lord on earth, contemplating through faith, so now they stand before him in heaven, contemplating through vision; and their eyes behold the King in his beauty, whose fear their heart meditated here below. By this way, brothers, follow the Fathers of your faith and the founders of your Order, so that, roused by their examples and helped by their intercessions, you may be able to arrive where they themselves have gone before this day, with the help of our Lord Jesus Christ, who lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saints Peter and Paul Sermon I", "topics": ["Saints Peter and Paul", "Holy Spirit", "grace", "Christ", "monastic life", "perseverance"], "url": "sermons/guerric-peter-paul-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:peter-paul", "calendar_label": "Sts. Peter and Paul, Apostles", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-peter-paul-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., Quomodo fidelium animos alant apostoli., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 180-183.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saints Peter and Paul", "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "Song of Songs 4:5\u20136", "Isaiah 49:23", "Isaiah 60:16", "1 Peter 2:2", "1 Corinthians 3:2", "1 Thessalonians 2:7", "Song of Songs 8:8", "Song of Songs 1:1", "John 21:15\u201316", "Song of Songs 8:10", "Song of Songs 4:5", "Song of Songs 2:16\u201317", "Song of Songs 7:2", "Acts 20:34", "1 Corinthians 4:12", "1 Thessalonians 2:9", "2 Thessalonians 3:8", "1 Thessalonians 3:8", "1 Thessalonians 2:19", "Philippians 4:1", "2 Corinthians 12:3", "Matthew 16:2\u20136 (printed locator; passage from Matthew 16:17)", "Acts 10:10\u201316"], "occasion": "In natali Apostolorum Petri et Pauli", "occasion_detail": "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "occasion_label": "Saints Peter and Paul", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the bride's two breasts in the Song of Songs as Peter and Paul, who nourish the Church with the milk of doctrine. The heavenly Church grieved that its little sister had no breasts until the Spirit filled the apostles at Pentecost. Paul fed others with his words and was fed by their works; Peter received the revelation of the Son and the keys of heaven. The sermon appears under the heading for the Apostles Peter and Paul in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo fidelium animos alant apostoli."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How the apostles nourish the souls of the faithful.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Duo ubera tua sicut duo hinnuli capreae gemelli, qui pascuntur in liliis, donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae (Cant. IV, 5, 6). Videte, fratres, ne degeneretis. Ut taceam de nobilitate patris vestri, nobilis est mater quae vos genuit, generosa sunt ubera quibus lactati estis. Mater vestra est sponsa cui haec dicuntur, cujus ubera voce sponsi laudantur. Quae nimirum duo ubera Ecclesiae Petrum et Paulum esse, non solum interpretatur nobis dies ista, in qua potamur ab uberibus consolationis eorum satis jucunde et abunde; sed etiam evidentia operis ipsorum approbant argumenta ac monumenta doctrinae. Quibus enim aliis uberibus nutriti sunt filii Ecclesiae, sive ex praeputio, sive ex circumcisione? Istos utique ego reor esse, de quibus olim parvulae et pauperculae consolatio divina promittebat Ecclesiae: Reges nutritii tui erunt (Isai. XLIX, 23), et mammilla regum lactaberis (Isa. LX, 16). Nisi enim tam generoso lacte nutrita esset, nunquam ad hoc virtutis ac gloriae culmen excrevisset. Ad ubera parvulos Petrus invitabat, quibus dicebat: Sicut modo geniti infantes, lac concupiscite (I Petr. II, 2). Ubera Paulus praebuerat quibus dicebat: Lac vobis potum dedi (I Cor. III, 2); et illud; Factus sum in medio vestri sicut nutrix sedens et nutriens filios suos (I Thess. II, 7). Quam plenus autem lacte redundaverit spirituali, pulchre significatum est unda illius lactis visibilis, quod de corpore ipsius fertur exisse loco sanguinis, cum decollatus hodierna die pro illis, quibus dabat mammillam, dedit et animam. Plane nihil in eo sanguineum, sed totum lacteum; qui nihil carnale, nihil suum cogitabat, sed tantum quod aliis utile erat. Nec tam habebat ubera, quam ipse totus uber erat; qui ita misericordia uberi affluebat, ut non modo spiritum suum desideraret totum filiis immulgere, sed et corpus impendere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Your two breasts are like two twin fawns of a roe, which feed among the lilies, until the day break and the shadows retire (Cant. IV, 5, 6). Take care, brothers, that you do not degenerate. To say nothing of the nobility of your father, noble is the mother who bore you, and generous are the breasts at which you were nursed. Your mother is the bride to whom these words are spoken, whose breasts are praised by the voice of the bridegroom. That these two breasts of the Church are, without doubt, Peter and Paul, is interpreted for us not only by this present day, on which we drink from the breasts of their consolation most pleasantly and abundantly, but also the clear evidence of their work and the monuments of their teaching confirm it. For by what other breasts have the children of the Church been nourished, whether from the uncircumcised or from the circumcision? These, surely, I judge to be they of whom long ago the divine consolation promised to the little and poor Church: Kings shall be your nursing fathers (Isai. XLIX, 23), and you shall be suckled with the breast of kings (Isa. LX, 16). For unless she had been nourished with milk so generous, she would never have grown to this summit of virtue and glory. Peter invited little children to the breasts, to whom he said: As newborn babes, desire the milk (I Petr. II, 2). Paul had offered breasts to those to whom he said: I gave you milk to drink (I Cor. III, 2); and also this: I became in the midst of you as a nurse sitting and nourishing her children (I Thess. II, 7). How full he overflowed with spiritual milk is beautifully signified by that wave of visible milk which is reported to have flowed from his body in place of blood, when, beheaded on this very day for those to whom he gave the breast, he gave also his soul. Clearly nothing bloody was in him, but all was milky; for he thought nothing carnal, nothing of his own, but only what was useful to others. Nor did he so much have breasts, as he himself was wholly a breast; for he so abounded in overflowing mercy that he desired not only to pour out his whole spirit for his children, but also to spend his very body.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Antequam haec duo ubera, Petrum et Paulum, accepisset primitiva Ecclesia sanctorum in terris, conquerebatur et dicebat Ecclesia beatorum spirituum in coelis: Soror nostra parva est, et ubera non habet (Cantic. VIII, 8). Cum enim Christus rediens in coelum reliquisset pusillum gregem discipulorum, ac nondum misisset Spiritum suum, quo erant fetanda viscera, et implenda ubera sanctorum: erat, ut opinor, illa Ecclesia in coelis sollicita pro filiolis sponsi, sive jam natis, sive nascituris, cui videlicet committerentur alendi; eo quod Ecclesiam istam viderent parvam numero, virtute et auctoritate, et sine uberibus doctrinae. Nam et ipse Sponsus in diebus carnis suae genuerat aliquos verbo veritatis, et quandiu fuerat cum eis, lactaverat eos uberibus aedificationis et consolationis. Et ipse siquidem Sponsus habet ubera meliora vino (Cant. I, 1), id est, doctrina legali, vel gaudio saeculari. Habet, inquam, Sponsus ubera, ne ullum de omnibus officiis et nominibus pietatis eum praetereat: ut qui pater est creatione naturae, vel regeneratione gratiae, seu etiam auctoritate disciplinae, sit etiam mater affectu clementiae, sit et nutrix sedulitate officii curae. Erant igitur parvuli, quos alebat initium aliquod creaturae ejus, sed nonnisi initium: multumque supererat curae et laboris, donec adducerentur ad perfectum, et formaretur Christus in eis. Cum autem et ipse reliquisset eos; superni spiritus, etsi laeti de reditu Unigeniti, de nova tamen adoptionis progenie solliciti, quodammodo affectu suo videbantur apud eum conqueri: Quis illos nutriet? Lactasti eos, sed ante tempus ablactasti. Non enutristi juvenes, nec ad incrementum perduxisti virgines: quis illos nutriet? Soror nostra parva est, et ubera non habet. Dixisti Petro: Pasce agnos meos (Joan. XXI, 15, 16): sed nec ipse adhuc sufficientiam lactis in uberibus habet. Cito pietas ejus aresceret, qui plus adhuc cuti suae quam animabus parvulorum timet. Facile relinqueret agnos in tentatione, qui Pastorem tam suum quam illorum negavit in interrogatione. Ecce autem repente Spiritu misso de coelo, tanquam lacte de propriis Christi uberibus effuso, Petrus impletus est lacte plurimo: nec multo post de Saulo fit Paulus, praedicator de persecutore, mater de tortore, nutrix de carnifice; ut plane intelligeres totum ejus sanguinem mutatum in lactis dulcedinem, saevitiam in pietatem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Before the primitive Church of the saints on earth had received these two breasts, Peter and Paul, the Church of the blessed spirits in heaven was complaining and saying: Our sister is little, and has no breasts (Cantic. VIII, 8). For when Christ, returning to heaven, had left behind a tiny flock of disciples, and had not yet sent his Spirit, by which the wombs of the saints were to conceive and their breasts to be filled, that Church in heaven was, I suppose, anxious for the little children of the bridegroom, whether already born or yet to be born, to whom they were to be entrusted for nourishing\u2014because they saw this Church to be small in number, in power and in authority, and without the breasts of doctrine. For the Bridegroom himself, in the days of his flesh, had begotten some by the word of truth, and as long as he was with them had suckled them with the breasts of edification and consolation. For indeed the Bridegroom himself has breasts better than wine (Cant. I, 1), that is, better than the teaching of the law, or than worldly joy. The Bridegroom has breasts, I say, lest any of all the offices and titles of piety should pass him by: so that he who is father by the creation of nature, or by the regeneration of grace, or even by the authority of discipline, might also be mother by the affection of his clemency, and nurse by the diligence of his caring office. There were, then, little children, whom he was nourishing, a kind of beginning of his creation, but only a beginning: and much of care and labor still remained, until they should be brought to perfection, and Christ be formed in them. But when he too had left them, the spirits above, though rejoicing at the return of the Only-begotten, yet anxious about the new offspring of adoption, seemed in a way, by their own affection, to complain before him: Who will nourish them? You have nursed them, but you have weaned them before their time. You have not nourished the young men, nor brought virgins to full growth: who will nourish them? Our sister is little, and has no breasts. You said to Peter: Feed my lambs (Joan. XXI, 15, 16); but even he does not yet have sufficiency of milk in his breasts. Quickly would his devotion wither, who still fears more for his own skin than for the souls of the little ones. Easily would he abandon the lambs in temptation, who denied the Shepherd, both his own and theirs, under questioning. But behold, suddenly, the Spirit having been sent from heaven, as if milk had been poured out from Christ's own breasts, Peter was filled with abundant milk: and not long after, Saul became Paul\u2014a preacher from a persecutor, a mother from a torturer, a nurse from an executioner; so that you might clearly understand that all his blood had been changed into the sweetness of milk, and his savagery into tenderness.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. His igitur uberibus geminis pectori suo affixis gloriatur Ecclesia, non modo matrem se esse fecundam, sed et civitatem munitam. Ego, inquit, murus, et ubera mea turris (Cantic. VIII, 10). Mille clypei pendent jam de turribus istis, omnis armatura fortium. Ipse quoque sponsus de eisdem uberibus in laudem sponsae in carmine amoris, mystico sermone sic ait: Duo ubera tua sicut duo hinnuli capreae gemelli (Cant. IV, 5). Capream hoc loco accipimus Ecclesiam; acutam visu ad penetranda mysteria Christi, agilem saltu ad transilienda spinosa hujus saeculi, potentem ad exsuperanda venena serpentis antiqui. Cujus duo gemelli non inconvenienter, ut arbitror, intelliguntur hi duo Apostoli; germani fide, consimiles devotione, pares merito et virtute, unanimes charitate, conjuncti in ipsa etiam passione et morte. Quomodo enim in vita sua dilexerunt se, ita et in morte non sunt separati. Quia vero desideria summorum pascuntur in profectibus minorum, donec scilicet aspirante gratia tollant se ad contemplandum sublimia, bene de his hinnulis subditur: Qui pascuntur in liliis, donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae (Cant. II, 16, 17). Animalia cum declinant aestum diei, captant umbrosa pascua convallium, ubi et liliorum solet esse proventus uberior, donec spirante tepescentis aura vesperi, egrediantur ad aperta camporum, vel ardua montium. Sic et hinnuli nostri quandiu non aspirat eis ad contemplandum clementior aura diei aeterni, pascuntur in liliis convallium; id est, delectantur in virtutibus humilium, aut etiam occupantur in actionibus eorum. Cum autem aspirat dies ille mentibus eorum, egrediuntur et saliunt ad uberiora et feliciora pascua montium aeternorum. Per me, inquit Pastor et ostium, si quis introierit salvabitur, et ingredietur Ecclesiam, et egredietur saepius contemplando, semel migrando ad coelestem patriam; et pascua inveniet, et hic, et ibi. Hic, inter lilia agri; ibi, inter ligna paradisi: hic, in floribus; ibi, in fructibus: hic, in virtutibus sanctorum; ibi, in gaudiis Angelorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. With these two breasts, then, fastened to her bosom, the Church glories not only that she is a fruitful mother, but also a fortified city. 'I,' she says, 'am a wall, and my breasts are a tower' (Cantic. VIII, 10). A thousand shields now hang from these towers, the whole armory of the mighty. The Bridegroom himself, too, speaking of these same breasts in praise of the Bride, in the song of love, says in mystical language: 'Your two breasts are like two twin fawns of a roe' (Cant. IV, 5). By 'roe' here we understand the Church: sharp of sight to penetrate the mysteries of Christ, nimble in leaping to spring over the thorns of this world, strong to overcome the poisons of the ancient serpent. Her two twin fawns, I think, are not unfittingly understood to be these two Apostles: brothers in faith, alike in devotion, equal in merit and virtue, of one mind in charity, joined even in their very passion and death. For as they loved one another in life, so in death they were not separated. Now because the desires of the highest are fed on the progress of the lesser, until, that is, with grace breathing upon them they lift themselves up to contemplate things sublime, it is rightly added of these fawns: 'Who feed among the lilies till the day break, and the shadows retire' (Cant. II, 16, 17). Animals, when they avoid the heat of the day, seek out the shaded pastures of the valleys, where too the growth of lilies is usually more abundant, until, as the cooling breeze of evening breathes, they go out to the open fields or the steep mountains. So too our fawns, so long as the gentler breeze of the eternal day does not breathe upon them for contemplation, feed among the lilies of the valleys; that is, they delight in the virtues of the humble, or are even occupied in their actions. But when that day breathes upon their minds, they go out and leap to the richer and happier pastures of the eternal mountains. 'By me,' says the Shepherd and the door, 'if any man enter in, he shall be saved'; and he shall enter the Church, and shall go out\u2014often by contemplating, once by passing over to the heavenly homeland\u2014and he shall find pastures, both here and there. Here, among the lilies of the field; there, among the trees of paradise: here, in flowers; there, in fruits: here, in the virtues of the saints; there, in the joys of the Angels.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Prorsus in loco pascuae ibi me Dominus collocavit, cum ecclesiae sanctorum associavit, cujus venter sicut acervus tritici vallatus liliis (Cant. VII, 2), ut pascat simul et tritici gustu, et liliorum aspectu. Quid enim acervus tritici, nisi copia divini sermonis in tot libris undique congestis? Quid lilia, nisi justi qui germinant sicut lilium, et florent immarcessibiliter ante Dominum; quorum tantus candor in sanctimonia corporis et cordis puritate, tanta fragrantia in opinione, tanta medicinalis virtus in opere et sermone? Non mediocre pabulum istud fideli animae, videre circa se tot lilia tanta venustate et gratia florentia, ubi omnium virtutum ex eis, licet diversarum ex diversis, capere possit exempla. Iste fundatior humilitate, ille amplior charitate. Alius robustior ad patientiam, alius velocior ad obedientiam. Iste frugalitate parcior, ille labore utilior: iste devotior in oratione, ille in lectione studiosior: iste in administratione prudentior, ille in quiete sanctior. Sed licet aliquam insignius florentem in unoquoque gratiam mireris, non una tamen, sed multae virtutes in singulis, sicut flores in liliis. Quot enim justi, tot lilia: quot virtutes eorum, tot flores liliorum. Qui igitur haec videns gaudet in illis, aut etiam proficit ex illis, quid, nisi pascitur in liliis?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Indeed, it was there, in that place of pasture, that the Lord set me, when he joined me to the church of the saints, whose belly is like a heap of wheat set about with lilies (Cant. VII, 2), so that it may feed at once by the taste of the wheat and the sight of the lilies. For what is the heap of wheat but the abundance of the divine word, gathered together from so many books on every side? And what are the lilies but the just, who spring up like the lily and flower unfadingly before the Lord; whose whiteness is so great in the holiness of body and purity of heart, whose fragrance so great in reputation, whose healing power so great in deed and word? It is no small pasture for a faithful soul to see around it so many lilies flowering with such beauty and grace, where it may gather examples of all the virtues from them, though these be diverse virtues from diverse persons. This one is more firmly grounded in humility, that one more abundant in charity. One is stronger for patience, another swifter for obedience. This one is more sparing in frugality, that one more useful in labor; this one more devout in prayer, that one more studious in reading; this one more prudent in administration, that one more holy in quiet. But although in each you may admire some one grace flowering more conspicuously, yet there is not one virtue alone, but many, in each, as there are many flowers in lilies. For as many as the just, so many the lilies; as many as their virtues, so many the flowers of the lilies. He, then, who, seeing these things, rejoices in them, or even profits by them\u2014what is he doing, but feeding among the lilies?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Pascebat Paulus: sed nihilominus in illis quos pascebat. Pascebat eos sermonibus, pascebatur eorum operibus. Ventrem quidem suum, imo et alienum pascebat manibus propriis (Act. XX, 34; I Cor. IV, 12; I Thess. II, 9, et II Thess. III, 8): mentem vero non tam suis pascebat bonis quam alienis, qui non quaerebat quod sibi utile, sed quod aliis. Nunc vivimus, inquit, si vos statis in Domino (I Thess. III, 8). Quae enim gloria mea, aut gaudium, aut corona? Nonne vos ante Deum? (I Thess. II, 19; Philipp. IV, 1.) Ideo saepius ei spirabat dies aeterni luminis, atque afflabat eum lenior aura Spiritus sancti, cujus vi rapiebatur ad interna pascua, aliquando paradisi, aliquando tertii coeli, sive in corpore, sive extra corpus, nescio, inquit, Deus scit (II Cor. XII, 3). Dignum quippe erat, ut qui fideliter pascebat, fideliter pasceretur: et qui foris gaudebat in bonis dominicae familiae, in gaudium Domini sui juberetur intrare, atque repleretur in bonis domus Dei, non solum istius visibilis, pro qua sudabat, sed etiam illius invisibilis, ad quam spirabat, unde et dies ei aspirabat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Paul fed others, but nonetheless was himself fed in those whom he fed. He fed them with his words, and was fed by their works. His own belly indeed\u2014nay, even that of others\u2014he fed with his own hands (Act. XX, 34; I Cor. IV, 12; I Thess. II, 9, et II Thess. III, 8); but his mind he fed not so much with his own goods as with those of others, for he sought not what was profitable to himself, but what was profitable to others. 'Now we live,' he says, 'if you stand fast in the Lord' (I Thess. III, 8). 'For what is my glory, or my joy, or my crown? Are you not it, before God?' (I Thess. II, 19; Philipp. IV, 1.). Therefore the day of eternal light breathed upon him the more often, and the gentler breeze of the Holy Spirit blew upon him, by whose power he was caught up to the inward pastures, now of paradise, now of the third heaven\u2014'whether in the body, or out of the body, I know not,' he says, 'God knows' (II Cor. XII, 3). It was indeed fitting that he who fed others faithfully should himself be faithfully fed, and that he who outwardly rejoiced in the goods of the Lord's household should be bidden to enter into the joy of his Lord, and be filled with the good things of the house of God\u2014not only of that visible house, for which he toiled, but also of that invisible one, toward which he breathed, whence also the day breathed upon him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Nec minora de beato Petro sentienda puto, cum maxima sit gloria Pauli, illius coaequari meritis, quem judicium Veritatis potestate et primatu praetulit universis. Cum enim Pater qui est in coelis veritatem Unigeniti Petro revelabat (Matth. XVI, 2-6), quid aliud quam dies ei aeternus aspirabat, et dies diei verbum eructabat? Cui autem magis pervium fuisse coelum credendum est, quam ipsi Janitori, cujus lingua clavis coeli facta est? Qui potestatem habebat claudere coelum nubibus, et aperire portas ejus, quomodo ipse non saepius intrasse credetur? Esuriit aliquando Petrus, et coelum ei apertum est, cum adhuc in terra consisteret; tantaque ei inde ciborum copia missa est, ut adhuc multa supersint, licet plurima mactaverit, et manducaverit, immunda et virulenta animalia in sancta et florulenta convertens lilia (Act. X, 10-16): ut videlicet deliciose pasceretur in liliis, donec aspirante die, gloriose in ipsis pasceretur coelis. Sed ecce dum commendamus vobis duo haec ubera sponsae, hora jam praeteriit, qua de ipsis uberibus lac vobis cupiebamus immulgere: quanquam et aliis diebus hoc ipsum facere possimus et soleamus. De uberibus siquidem Apostolorum lac vobis trahimus, quoties de verbis eorum ad aedificationem vestram tractamus. Nunc igitur pro tempore satis sit admonere, si tamen moneri indigetis, ut sicut ubera ista diligitis, ita lac eorum semper concupiscatis; ut in eo crescatis in salutem, donec formatum in vobis exhibeatis Salvatorem Jesum Christum Dominum nostrum, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. I think we should judge no less highly of blessed Peter, since it is Paul's greatest glory to be made equal in merits to him whom the judgment of Truth preferred above all others in power and primacy. For when the Father who is in heaven revealed to Peter the truth concerning the Only-Begotten (Matth. XVI, 2-6), what else was this but that the eternal day breathed upon him, and day uttered speech unto day? And to whom must heaven be believed to have been more open than to that very Doorkeeper, whose tongue was made the key of heaven? He who had the power to shut heaven with clouds, and to open its gates \u2014 how shall he not be believed to have entered it the more often himself? Once Peter hungered, and heaven was opened to him while he still stood on earth; and so great an abundance of food was sent to him from there, that much still remained over, although he had already slaughtered and eaten many unclean and venomous creatures, turning them into holy and flowering lilies (Act. X, 10-16): so that, to be sure, he might be fed delightfully among lilies, until, when the day breaks,, he might be fed gloriously in the very heavens themselves. But behold, while we commend to you these two breasts of the Bride, the hour has already passed in which we desired to draw out for you the milk from those very breasts \u2014 although on other days we are able, and are accustomed, to do this very thing. For indeed we draw milk for you from the breasts of the Apostles, as often as we treat of their words for your edification. For now, then, let it suffice for the time to admonish you, if indeed you need admonishing, that just as you love these breasts, so you may always long for their milk; that in it you may grow unto salvation, until you show forth, formed in you, the Saviour Jesus Christ our Lord, who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Father's revelation to Peter is Matthew 16:17. The edition prints Matth. xvi, 2-6."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saints Peter and Paul Sermon II", "topics": ["Saints Peter and Paul", "Church", "preaching", "Pentecost", "Song of Songs", "spiritual nourishment"], "url": "sermons/guerric-peter-paul-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:peter-paul", "calendar_label": "Sts. Peter and Paul, Apostles", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-peter-paul-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De eo quod scriptum est: \u00ab Donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae. \u00bb (Cant. II, 17)., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 183-188.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Saints Peter and Paul", "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "Song of Songs 2:17", "1 Corinthians 13:12", "Exodus 19:20", "Psalm 58:6 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "Isaiah 18:11 (printed locator; passage from Isaiah 58:11)", "Psalm 101:12 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 83:11 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 58:11", "James 1:17", "Isaiah 27:8", "Matthew 11:28", "Job 7:2", "Song of Songs 2:3", "Hosea 14:8", "Lamentations 4:20", "Job 40:17, 20"], "occasion": "In natali Apostolorum Petri et Pauli", "occasion_detail": "Saints Peter and Paul, Apostles", "occasion_label": "Saints Peter and Paul", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Pressed by the brothers, Guerric finishes the verse he began the day before: \"Until the day breathe, and the shadows be inclined.\" He distinguishes shadows that chill and darken the soul from those that cool and enlighten it, and holds that even the clearest vision of this life is a bright shadow in a mirror. He invites the laboring brothers to rest in the shade of Christ, and warns against the heavy shade of worldly cares in which Leviathan sleeps. The sermon appears under the heading for the Apostles Peter and Paul in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De eo quod scriptum est: \u00ab Donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae. \u00bb (Cant. II, 17)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On what is written: \"Until the day breathe, and the shadows be inclined.\" (Cant. II, 17).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Erratis, fratres, in me; sed amore magis, ut arbitror, aut humilitate, quam temeritate. Putatis me Scripturarum scientiam habere, qui vix unquam attigi vel primum limen scientiae. Displicet enim vobis, ut mihi videtur, quod capitulum Scripturae, de quo heri sum locutus, non sum ad finem usque persecutus: quasi illa mihi sit facultas, ut valeam Scripturas explanare, aut etiam explanationes aliorum digne et congrue ad memoriam revocare. Sed neque hoc mihi solet propositum esse, ut Scripturam, unde sermonem ordior, exponam; sed ut de ipsa, et secundum ipsam sermonem debitum suo diei persolvam. Huc accedit quod magister noster (10) ille interpres Spiritus sancti, de toto illo carmine nuptiali loqui instituit, spemque nobis dedit ex eis quae jam edidit, quia si pervenerit ad locum de quo quaeritis, donec aspiret dies, et inclinentur umbrae, umbras ipsas ponat in lucem intelligentiae: quod dictum est vel erit in tenebris, nobis dicet in lumine. Vos tamen dicitis, et recte dicitis, quia vetera novis supervenientibus projicietis; quantoque magis incondita seu rancida fuerint vetera nostra, tanto nova illius sapidiora erunt et gratiora. Nam et ipse Jesus servat vivum bonum usque ad extremum, sciens utique quomodo nostro sit fastidio medendum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. You are mistaken about me, brothers; but out of love, I think, or out of humility, rather than out of rashness. You suppose me to have knowledge of the Scriptures\u2014I who have scarcely ever touched even the first threshold of knowledge. For it displeases you, as it seems to me, that I did not pursue to its end the chapter of Scripture of which I spoke yesterday: as though I had the capacity to explain the Scriptures, or even to recall to memory, worthily and fittingly, the explanations of others. But it has never been my custom to set out to expound the Scripture from which I begin my sermon; rather, my aim is to pay to each day the sermon owed to it, drawn from and in accordance with that Scripture. To this is added the fact that our master (10), that interpreter of the Holy Spirit, has undertaken to speak on that whole nuptial song, and has given us hope, from what he has already set forth, that if he should come to the passage you are asking about\u2014\"until the day breathe, and the shadows be inclined\"\u2014he will set those very shadows into the light of understanding: what has been said, or will be said, in darkness, he will tell us in the light. Yet you say, and you say rightly, that you will cast aside the old things when the new ones come; and the more unrefined or stale our old things have been, the more savory and pleasing the new ones from him will be. For Jesus himself keeps the living good even to the last, knowing well how our distaste is to be remedied.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Quia igitur cogitis me (nam impatientes dilationis vos video), nec spes incerta futuri praesenti satisfacit desiderio; ecce vobis morem gero, finemque versiculi, de quo heri loquebamur, suo, prout potero, reddam principio. Illud enim quaerere videmini, quid sit aspirante die spirituali umbras inclinari; cum magis consequens videretur, ut non crescere, sed minui tunc et deficere, dicerentur. Nam si sequamur proprietatem verbi, hoc est umbris crescere, quod inclinari: cum umbrarum ea natura sit, ut quanto inclinantur, tanto procumbant longiores; quantoque surgunt, tanto stent minores. Unde et poeta vesperum significans ait: Majoresque cadunt altis de montibus umbrae. (VIRG. Buc. eclog. 1.) Sed sciendum, quia cum haec proprietas umbrarum sit corporalium, longe alia est spiritualium, de quibus non dubitamus hic loqui Spiritum sanctum. Nam sicut corporalibus umbris inclinari est crescere, sic spiritualibus hoc est inclinari, quod deponi, minui, vel deficere. Umbrae autem spirituales sunt umbrosi spiritus et tetri, seu daemonum, seu hominum: umbrae sunt tenebrae errorum: umbrae sunt obscurae significationes veterum sacramentorum. Et umbrae quidem significationum jam inclinatae sunt ad occasum et finem: umbrae autem errorum de die in diem inclinantur ad diminutionem: umbrae vero tenebrosorum spirituum tandem inclinabuntur in infernum et mortem. Primum quidem factum est, cum dies aeternus aspiravit, apparens in carne: secundum quotidie fit, cum magis ac magis aspirat illustrans veritate: tertium novissime fiet, cum aspirabit rutilans in majestate."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Since, then, you compel me (for your impatience at delay is plain to me), and an uncertain hope of the future does not satisfy a present desire; behold, I will indulge you, and will render, as best I can, the end of the little verse we were discussing yesterday, together with its own beginning. For you seem to be asking what it means for the shadows to be inclined when the spiritual day breathes forth; whereas it would seem more fitting to say that then they are not increasing but diminishing and failing. For if we follow the proper sense of the word, for shadows \"to be inclined\" is the same as \"to grow\": since it is the nature of shadows that the more they are inclined, the longer they stretch out; and the more they rise, the shorter they stand. Whence the poet, signifying evening, says: \"And longer shadows fall from the high mountains.\" But it must be understood that, while this is the property of bodily shadows, the case is far different with spiritual shadows, of which we do not doubt the Holy Spirit is speaking here. For just as for bodily shadows to be inclined is to grow, so for spiritual shadows to be inclined is to be laid down, diminished, or to fail. Now the spiritual shadows are the shadowy and gloomy spirits, whether of demons or of men: shadows are the darknesses of errors: shadows are the obscure significations of the old sacraments. And indeed the shadows of significations have already been inclined toward their setting and their end: but the shadows of errors are inclined, day by day, toward diminishment: while the shadows of the dark spirits will at last be inclined into hell and death. The first indeed came to pass when the eternal Day breathed forth, appearing in the flesh: the second happens daily, as he breathes forth more and more, enlightening with truth: the third will happen at the very last, when he will breathe forth glowing in majesty.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Videre autem mihi videor et alias umbras intra nos, quae velut in quodam speculo de rebus aliis nascuntur in sensu nostro, et obumbrant nobis, vel obumbrant nos. Obumbrant nos quae refrigidant et obscurant nos: obumbrant nobis quae refrigerant et illuminant nos. Istae quidem noxiae et graves, illae delectabiles et salubres, istae namque de inferioribus sunt et mundanis; illae, de superioribus et divinis. Anima nempe humana in quodam medio condita et posita est, ut sub ipsa esset mundus, supra ipsam Deus. Supra ipsam, a quo et ad quem et propter quem facta est: sub ipsa, quod propter ipsam factum est. Nam sicut propter animam corpus, sic propter corpus domus ejus, id est, mundus. Cum igitur incurvatur ad corporalia vel mundana, ascendunt in eam umbrae de inferioribus: cum erigitur ad divina, inclinantur super eam umbrae de superioribus. Illius enim rei quam cogitat, umbram sibi format. Quanquam de divinis satis dubia umbra est, quantumcunque cogitationis evacuatur acies: aut certe non umbra ipsius rei, sed aliud pro illa est, nisi cum aspirat dies ille. Nam tunc quoque non videtur, ut arbitror, nisi umbra, quae etsi luminosa, etsi gloriosa est, tamen umbra est; sicut cum in facie speculi tersa et lucida, splendidissimae rei splendens imago resultat. Caeterum videre faciem veritatis ipsius, vel veritatem faciei ejus, sicut nunquam erit hujus corporis: ita nec modo est hujus temporis, nec aliquando erit, donec corpus dissolvet mortalitas, aut absolvet immortalitas, et tempus absorbebit aeternitas, et spiritum qui nunc gravatur corpore et mutatur tempore, sublevabit ad se, et firmabit in se immortalis et immutabilis divinitas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But it seems to me that I can see other shadows within us as well, which, as it were in a kind of mirror, arise in our senses from other things, and either cast shade for us, or overshadow us. Those overshadow us which chill and darken us: those cast shade for us which cool and illuminate us. The former indeed are harmful and heavy, the latter delightful and wholesome; for the former come from lower, worldly things, the latter from higher, divine things. For the human soul has been established and placed in a kind of middle position, so that the world should be beneath it, and God above it. Above it is he from whom, and to whom, and for whose sake it was made; beneath it is what was made for its sake. For just as the body exists for the sake of the soul, so the soul's house\u2014that is, the world\u2014exists for the sake of the body. When, therefore, the soul bends down toward bodily or worldly things, shadows from the lower things rise up into it; but when it is lifted up toward divine things, shadows from the higher things are inclined over it. For of whatever thing it thinks upon, it forms for itself a shadow. Although, concerning divine things, the shadow is quite uncertain, however much the keenness of thought is emptied out: or rather, it is not the shadow of the thing itself, but something else in its place, except when that day breathes forth. For then too, I think, nothing is seen except a shadow\u2014which, even though luminous, even though glorious, is nevertheless a shadow; just as when, on the face of a mirror polished and bright, the shining image of a most splendid thing is reflected back. But to see the face of truth itself, or the truth of its face, will never belong to this body\u2014just as it is not now the lot of this present time, nor ever will be, until mortality dissolves the body, or immortality releases it, and eternity swallows up time, and the immortal and unchangeable divinity lifts up to itself, and establishes firmly in itself, the spirit which is now weighed down by the body and changed by time.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Initium hujus gratiae, et perfectionem illius gloriae Paulus satis proprie distinguebat, cum dicebat: Videmus nunc per speculum in aenigmate; tunc autem facie ad faciem (I Cor. XIII, 12). Ut autem et hoc ipsum sit, scilicet ut videamus per speculum et in aenigmate, opus est non solum ut facies speculi nostri ab omni phantasmate et umbra corporalium tersa sit: sed etiam ut ille sublimis, habitans lucem inaccessibilem, dignetur ad nos inclinari, et vel per umbram suae imaginis manifestari. Quantumcunque enim contendamus a visibilibus ad invisibilia, nihil nostra apprehendet humilitas, nisi condescendat illa majestas. Moyses ascendit in montem, et Dominus inclinavit coelos et descendit (Exod. XIX, 20). Et alius: Exsurge inquit in occursum meum, et vide (Psal. LVIII, 6). Ideo nec umbra videtur, nisi inclinetur; nec inclinari dicitur, nisi aspirante die: quia neque per speculum et in aenigmate possumus Deum videre, nisi inclinante se majestate, et aspirante favore gratiae. Sane hoc ipsum quod umbra vocatur comparatione manifestae veritatis, ineffabilis plerumque gloriae est et splendoris, in illis scilicet quorum est perlucidum speculum mentis. Cujus felicis experientiae Paulus sibi conscius, de se sibique similibus loquebatur: Nos vero omnes gloriam Domini speculantes, in eamdem imaginem transformamur a claritate in claritatem, tanquam a Domini Spiritu (II Cor. III, 18). Sic enim in promissione dictum est: Implebit splendoribus animam tuam (Isai. XVIII, 11.). Ut autem claritates istas umbras esse noveris, audi David: Dies mei, inquit, sicut umbra declinaverunt. Transibat quidem et ipse David de claritate in claritatem, tanquam de die in diem, Sed heu! tam breves et obscuros dies, tam longas et laboriosas noctes habet hiems ista, ut et propheta laborans in gemitu suo, lavansque per singulas noctes lectum lacrymis, merito plangat: Dies mei sicut umbra declinaverunt! (Psal. CI, 12.) Prorsus melior est dies una in atriis tuis super millia (Psal. LXXXIII, 11) dierum meorum; quia dies mei sicut umbra quamvis lucida: dies illa una, lux vera et mera, sicut sine umbra, sic sine vespera; cum dies meos faciat plurimos et brevissimos assidua longarum noctium interpolatio."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Paul distinguished quite properly the beginning of this grace and the perfection of that glory, when he said: \"We see now through a glass in a dark manner; but then face to face\" (I Cor. XIII, 12). But in order for this very thing to come about\u2014namely, that we see through a glass and in a dark manner\u2014it is necessary not only that the face of our mirror be wiped clean of every phantasm and shadow of corporeal things, but also that he who is exalted on high, dwelling in inaccessible light, deign to incline himself toward us, and be made manifest to us at least through the shadow of his image. For however much we strive to pass from the visible to the invisible, our lowliness will apprehend nothing unless that majesty condescends. Moses went up into the mountain, and the Lord bowed down the heavens and came down (Exod. XIX, 20). And another says: \"Arise to meet me, and behold\" (Psal. LVIII, 6). Therefore the shadow is not seen unless it is inclined; nor is it said to incline except when the day breathes forth: because we cannot see God through a glass and in a dark manner unless majesty inclines itself and the favor of grace breathes upon us. Indeed, this very thing which is called a shadow, in comparison with manifest truth, is for the most part of ineffable glory and splendor\u2014namely, in those whose mirror of the mind is perfectly clear. Conscious of this happy experience, Paul spoke of himself and those like him: \"But we all, beholding the glory of the Lord, are transformed into the same image from glory to glory, as by the Spirit of the Lord\" (II Cor. III, 18). For thus it was said in the promise: \"He will fill your soul with splendors\" (Isai. XVIII, 11.). But that you may know that these very splendors are shadows, listen to David: \"My days,\" he says, \"have declined like a shadow.\" David too was indeed passing from glory to glory, as it were from day to day. But alas! this winter has days so short and dark, and nights so long and laborious, that the prophet himself, laboring in his groaning and washing his bed every night with tears, justly laments: \"My days have declined like a shadow!\" (Psal. CI, 12.). Truly \"better is one day in your courts\" (Psal. LXXXIII, 11) than a thousand of my days; because my days are like a shadow, however bright; but that one day is true and pure light\u2014as it is without shadow, so it is without evening\u2014whereas my days are made very many and very brief by the constant interruption of long nights.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Caeterum, si quaeratur cur umbrae pluraliter dictae sint, cum res ipsa, cujus umbrae sunt, una sit: ideo nimirum, quia nunc minus, nunc amplius nobis Altissimi virtus obumbrat; nunc obscurius, nunc manifestius veritas sese nobis innotescens adumbrat: et sicut non aequaliter semper spirat, ita nec similiter semper nobis imagines format. Hoc et Apostolus innuit, qui transire se confitetur a claritate in claritatem (II Cor. III, 18), a minore scilicet in majorem: et propheta, qui non splendore sed splendoribus implendam promittit fidelem animam (Isai. LVIII, 11). Sic Jacobus Deum memorat patrem luminum (Jacobi I, 17), cum lumen, cujus pater est, et unum sit et unicum. Quanquam sic possit intelligi pater luminum, quomodo pater misericordiarum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Furthermore, if it be asked why the shadows are spoken of in the plural, when the thing itself, of which they are shadows, is one: it is doubtless because now the power of the Most High overshadows us less, now more; now the truth, making itself known to us, casts its shadow more obscurely, now more manifestly: and just as he does not always breathe upon us equally, so neither does he always form images for us in like manner. The Apostle hints at this too, confessing that he passes \"from glory to glory\" (II Cor. III, 18), that is, from a lesser to a greater glory; and the prophet, who promises that the faithful soul will be filled not with a splendor but with splendors (Isai. LVIII, 11). Thus James calls God \"the Father of lights\" (Jacobi I, 17), though the light of which he is Father is one and unique. Although it can be understood that he is called \"Father of lights\" in the same way he is called \"Father of mercies.\"", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Jam vero vos fratres, qui laboratis portantes pondus diei et aestus, vel secundum Isaiam, in spiritu vestro duro meditamini per diem aestus (Isa. XXVII, 8), ad umbras istas placet invitare quas ille dies serenus aspirans rorem misericordiae cupit aestuantibus inclinare. Audite ipsum diem, ad umbrarum refrigeria clementer vos invitantem: Venite ad me, omnes qui laboratis et onerati estis, et ego reficiam vos (Matth. XI, 28). Servus enim sub sole laborans, ut ait Job, desiderat umbram (Job VII, 2), in qua scilicet a labore recreetur ad laborem, vel per horam dimidiam, qua silentium fit in coelo: quam cum obtinuerit indulgentia Prioris sui, gloriatur et dicit, Sub umbra illius, quem desiderabam, sedi. In qua nimirum umbra, quia non sola sessio est ad quietem, sed etiam refectio ad satietatem, congrue subdit: Et fructus ejus dulcis gutturi meo (Cantic. II, 3). Sic enim dictum est per prophetam: Convertentur sedentes in umbra ejus, vivent tritico et germinabunt sicut vitis (Osee XIV, 8). Servus seu mercenarius, quem saepius haec umbra recreaverit, longanimiter praestolabitur finem operis sui. Sic enim habes: Servus desiderat umbram, et mercenarius praestolatur finem operis sui (Job VII, 2). In umbra quidem non finis, sed pausatio. Finis enim erit operis remuneratio, cum evacuatis omnibus umbris, videbimus eum sicuti est: cui modo dicimus, In umbra tua vivemus inter gentes (Thren. IV, 20); inter angelos nempe, non in umbra, sed in luce manifesta."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Now, brothers, you who labor, bearing the burden of the day and the heat, or who, according to Isaiah, meditate in your hard spirit through the day of heat (Isa. XXVII, 8), it is fitting to invite you to these shadows which that serene day, breathing forth the dew of mercy, desires to incline toward those who burn with heat. Hear the Day himself, mercifully inviting you to the refreshments of the shadows: \"Come to me, all you who labor and are burdened, and I will refresh you\" (Matth. XI, 28). For the servant laboring under the sun, as Job says, desires the shadow (Job VII, 2), namely, that in it he may be refreshed from labor for labor, or at least for the half hour in which silence is made in heaven; and when he has obtained this by the indulgence of his Prior, he glories and says, \"Under the shadow of him whom I desired, I sat.\" And because in this shadow there is not only sitting for rest, but also feasting to satisfaction, he fittingly adds: \"And his fruit was sweet to my throat\" (Cantic. II, 3). For so it is said through the prophet: \"They that dwell under his shadow shall be turned, they shall live by the wheat and shall blossom as the vine\" (Osee XIV, 8). The servant or hireling, whom this shadow has often refreshed, will patiently await the end of his work. For thus you have it: \"The servant desires the shadow, and the hireling awaits the end of his work\" (Job VII, 2). In the shadow, indeed, there is not the end, but a pause. For the end will be the reward of the work, when, all shadows having been emptied away, we shall see him as he is\u2014to whom we now say, \"Under your shadow we shall live among the nations\" (Thren. IV, 20)\u2014that is, among the angels, not in shadow, but in manifest light.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Hoc autem notandum, quod cum umbrae illae de superioribus inclinantur supra nos, umbrae istae de inferioribus inclinantur infra nos. Cum enim divinis propinquamus, mundana superamus: et quatenus vel ad umbram lucis accedimus, eatenus mortis umbram evadimus. Lux enim et vita in coelo, mors in inferno: umbra autem mortis in hoc terreno et tenebroso loco. Cum igitur spiritus altius nos elevat inter terram et coelum, non tangimur umbra terrenorum: et licet nondum plena luce fruamur coelorum, tamen umbris illuminamur montium aeternorum, vel certe montis illius umbrosi et condensi, de quo venit Sanctus sanctorum. Mox tamen pondere nostro in regionem umbrae mortis revolvimur, ubi et diutissime sedere cogimur, donec visitatione orientis ex alto consolemur. Cogimur, inquam, et utinam cogamur, et non delectemur! utinam quod patimur, necessitatis tantum sit, non etiam voluntatis aut voluptatis! Sed heu! tam facile, tam libenter projicimus nos, et obdormiscimus sub gravi umbra juniperi spinosae et infructuosae arboris, tam negligentes et securi torpescimus et immorimur curis et desideriis hujus mundi! Propterea Leviathan, qui sub umbra dormit, cubile sibi, nisi praecaveamus, ponit in nobis; quia videlicet amicas sibi umbras hujus saeculi invenit in sensibus et affectibus nostris. Istae siquidem umbrae, teste Job, protegunt umbra ejus (Job. XL, 20, 17); quia nimirum curae et desideria saecularia, quae sunt in nobis, umbram nobis abscondunt imminentis damnationis, ut eam non praevideamus, donec in ipsam impingamus. Aspira, o dies dierum, obumbra supra caput nostrum in die belli, inclina super nos umbras salutis et refrigerii, et inclinentur subtus nos umbrae torporis et taedii, imo caecitatis et mortis. Et licet contingant nos umbrae de cogitatione terrenorum, non nos contegant tenebrae, id est, umbrae crassiores de amore eorum; sed semper respiremus in luminosum amorem tuum, o Pater luminum, qui vivis et regnas per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now this must be noted: that while those shadows from the higher things incline over us, these shadows from the lower things incline beneath us. For when we draw near to things divine, we rise above worldly things; and to the extent that we approach even the shadow of the light, to that extent we escape the shadow of death. For light and life are in heaven, death is in hell; but the shadow of death is in this earthly and darksome place. When, therefore, the spirit lifts us higher, between earth and heaven, we are not touched by the shadow of earthly things; and although we do not yet enjoy the full light of the heavens, nevertheless we are illumined by the shadows of the eternal mountains \u2014 or at least of that shadowy and dense mountain from which the Holy One of holy ones came. Yet soon, by our own weight, we are rolled back down into the region of the shadow of death, where we are forced to sit for a very long time, until we are consoled by the visitation of the Dayspring from on high. We are forced, I say \u2014 and would that we were only forced, and did not take delight in it! Would that what we suffer were a matter of necessity alone, and not also of will or of pleasure! But alas, how easily, how willingly we fling ourselves down and fall asleep under the heavy shadow of the juniper, a thorny and fruitless tree; so negligent and so unconcerned do we grow sluggish and lose ourselves in the cares and desires of this world! Therefore Leviathan, who sleeps beneath the shadow, makes his lair in us, unless we take care \u2014 because, indeed, he finds shadows of this age that are friendly to him in our senses and affections. For these shadows, as Job testifies, protect his shadow (Job. XL, 20, 17); because, truly, the worldly cares and desires that are in us hide from us the shadow of the damnation that hangs over us, so that we do not foresee it until we strike against it. Breathe upon us, O Day of days; overshadow our heads in the day of battle; incline over us the shadows of salvation and refreshment, and let the shadows of torpor and weariness \u2014 nay, of blindness and death \u2014 be inclined beneath us. And though the shadows that come from the thought of earthly things may touch us, let not the darkness \u2014 that is, the thicker shadows that come from the love of them \u2014 cover us over; but let us always breathe toward your luminous love, O Father of lights, who live and reign through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-n010", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(10) S. Bernardum intelligit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-n010", "label": "English", "text": "(10) He means St. Bernard.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The quoted promise of rest and brightness comes from Isaiah 58:11; the edition prints Isai. XVIII, 11 here and LVIII, 11 later."}, {"anchor": "guerric-peter-paul-03-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints vivum bonum; the allusion is to the good wine kept until last in John 2:10."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Saints Peter and Paul Sermon III", "topics": ["Saints Peter and Paul", "Song of Songs", "contemplation", "vision of God", "rest", "worldly cares"], "url": "sermons/guerric-peter-paul-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo I, De caeremonia luminum hoc festo usitata, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier freres, 1879), cols. 63-67.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Candlemas", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "Luke 22:35 (printed locator; quotation identified as Luke 12:35, see note)", "Job 14:4", "Zechariah 13:1", "James 4:17", "Luke 12:47", "Deuteronomy 4:24", "III Regum 1:17 (printed locator; Abishag episode identified as modern 1 Kings 1:1\u20134, see note)", "Song of Songs 1:12", "Luke 12:35 (lamps in the hands)", "Proverbs 31:18", "Song of Songs 8:6, 7", "Luke 12:35 (good works as a lamp)", "Matthew 5:16", "Psalm 118:105 (historical numbering; word as a lamp)", "2 Peter 1:19", "Psalm 118:130 (historical numbering)", "1 Peter 4:11", "Psalm 118:105 (historical numbering; repeated in counsel on speech)", "Proverbs 20:27", "Job 29:2, 3", "Zephaniah 1:12 (divine examination contrasted with illuminating grace)", "Matthew 6:22 and Luke 11:34 (single printed locator group)", "Zephaniah 1:12 (Jerusalem searched with lamps)", "Proverbs 20:20", "Job 11:17 and Isaiah 58:10 (single printed locator group)"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "occasion_label": "Candlemas", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny reads the Purification's ceremony of lights through Simeon receiving Christ in the temple. Mary's obedience prompts a call to humility, bodily chastity and purity of heart; carrying a candle should express devotion and good deeds. Simeon cherishes the Word in flesh like light in wax, and Guerric invites his hearers to become lamps themselves. He develops the image through faith, charitable works, speech that builds up others, prayer and a sincere intention before God. The sermon closes by contrasting divine examination with the enduring light promised beyond death. The individual sermon is undated; its collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The source names the Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary, traditionally known as Candlemas."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN FESTO PURIFICATIONIS BEATAE MARIAE VIRGINIS."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE FEAST OF THE PURIFICATION OF THE BLESSED VIRGIN MARY.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De caeremonia luminum hoc festo usitata."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On the ceremony of lights customary at this feast.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Sint lumbi vestri praecincti, et lucernae ardentes in manibus vestris (Luc. xxii, 35). Sint lumbi nostri praecincti, ut Purificationem Mariae aemulemur; sint lucernae ardentes in manibus, ut gaudium Simeonis, lumen in manibus portantis, etiam visibili signo in nobis repraesentemus. Simus videlicet casti corpore, et mundi corde; et Purificationem Mariae expressimus: simus ardentes devotione, et lucentes opere; et cum Simeone Christum portamus in manibus. Maria tamen non tam purificata fuit, quam mysterium Purificationis commendavit, implendo legalem, significando spiritualem. Quid enim in ea purificandum esse potuit, quae virgo concepit, virgo peperit, virgoque permansit, imo quae ipso conceptu plane purificata fuit, si quid forte minus ante puritatis habuisset [alias habuit)? Quid, inquam, mundandum habuit conceptus ille, qui solus potest facere mundum de immundo conceptum semine (Job. xiv, 4), qui fontem quo immundus purificetur edidit, fontem domus David, qui hodieque patet indefesseque scatet in ablutionem peccatoris, et menstruatae? (Zach.. xiii, 1.) Suscepit tamen Mater totius puritatis, purgationis legitimae speciem, ut simul et obedientissimae humilitatis virtutem, et evangelicae purificationis insinuaret veritatem. Ubi nunc ille, qui tam fallaciter quam contumaciter praesumens de sanctitate, detrectat purgatoria poenitentium remedia subire? Esto quod sanctus sit; nunquid sicut sanctarum Sanctissima, quae Sanctum sanctorum peperit, Maria? Utinam, fratres mei, utinam nos illam haberemus humilitatem in peccatis nostris quam vere habuerunt sancti in virtutibus suis. Sed omittimus nunc istud, ut quod coepimus prosequamur. Ac primo quidem de illa spirituali Purificatione, quam Maria, offerens pro se et pro Filio par turturum aut duos pullos columbarum, nobis commendavit, prosequeremur, nisi quod patres nostri, sicut vobis notissimum est, omnia ad liquidum prosecuti sunt; et quomodo turtur ad castitatem carnis, columba ad simplicitatem mentis pertineat, diligenter discurrerunt; nec illud quidem praetereuntes, quid sit sacrificium de uno turture vel pullo columbino, quid holocaustum de altero. Hoc unum tamen adjicimus, quod quanto notiora nobis sunt mysteria, tanto districtius et justius eorum a nobis exiguntur opera; quia scienti bonum et non facienti, peccatum est illi (Jac. iv, 17); et servus sciens voluntatem domini sui, et non faciens digna, plagis vapulabit multis (Luc. xii, 47)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Let your loins be girded, and lamps burning in your hands (Luc. xxii, 35). Let our loins be girded, so that we may imitate Mary's Purification; let lamps be burning in our hands, so that by a visible sign we too may represent the joy of Simeon carrying the light in his hands. Let us be chaste in body and clean in heart, and we have expressed Mary's Purification; let us burn with devotion and shine in our deeds, and with Simeon we carry Christ in our hands. Yet Mary did not so much undergo purification as commend the mystery of Purification, by fulfilling the legal purification and signifying the spiritual one. For what could there have been in her needing purification, when she conceived as a virgin, gave birth as a virgin, and remained a virgin; indeed, when in the very act of conceiving she was fully purified, if perhaps she had previously possessed any lesser degree of purity [variant: possessed)? What, I ask, did that conceived child have that needed cleansing, he who alone can make clean one conceived of unclean seed (Job. xiv, 4), who brought forth the fountain by which the unclean may be purified, the fountain of the house of David, which even today stands open and flows unceasingly for the washing of the sinner and the menstruating woman? (Zach.. xiii, 1.) Nevertheless, the Mother of all purity accepted the outward form of purification prescribed by the law, to convey both the virtue of most obedient humility and the reality of evangelical purification. Where now is the man who, presuming on his holiness as deceitfully as obstinately, refuses to undergo the cleansing remedies of penitents? Granted that he is holy: is he as holy as Mary, the Holiest of holy women, who bore the Holy of holies? Would that, my brothers, would that we had in our sins the humility that the saints truly had in their virtues! But we leave this aside now, to continue what we began. First, indeed, we would pursue that spiritual Purification which Mary commended to us by offering for herself and her Son a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons, were it not that our fathers, as you well know, have treated everything with complete clarity. They have carefully explained how the turtledove pertains to chastity of the flesh and the dove to simplicity of mind, without passing over what is meant by the sacrifice of one turtledove or young pigeon and the whole burnt offering of the other. Yet we add this one point: the better known the mysteries are to us, the more strictly and justly are the deeds they require demanded of us; for to him who knows the good and does not do it, it is sin (Jac. iv, 17); and the servant who knows his master's will and does not act worthily will be beaten with many blows (Luc. xii, 47).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Relinquentes igitur quod tractatum est sufficienter, illud potius tractemus, si placet, quid haec pulcherrima Ecclesiae consuetudo luminarium hodie gestandorum, aut factum repraesentet, aut faciendum demonstret. Quanquam ne id puto quidem vobis incognitum, etiamsi nunquam sit auditum. Quis enim hodie, cereum ardentem gestans in manibus, non statim illius senis recordetur, qui, hodie Jesum accipiens in ulnas suas, Verbum in carne tanquam lumen in cera, ipsum esse lumen ad illuminationem gentium perhibebat? Erat profecto et ipse lucerna ardens et lucens, testimonium perhibens de lumine, qui ad hoc ipsum in Spiritu quo plenus erat, venit in templum, ut, suscipiens misericordiam tuam, Deus, in medio templi tui praedicaret ipsam esse misericordiam, et lumen populi tui. Vere tu, senex serene, lumen portabas non modo manibus, sed et sensibus; qui sic eras illuminatus, ut, cum tanto ante futuram illuminationem gentium tam clare videres, etiam nunc inter tenebras Judaicae infidelitatis, hodierna claritas fidei nostrae tibi coruscaret. Laetare jam, senior juste, vide jam quod praevidebas: discussae sunt mundi tenebrae, ambulant gentes in lumine tuo; plena est omnis terra gloria luminis illius quod occultum in sinu fovebas, imo quo sensus tuos refovebas. O fomes amoris, ipse amor suavis et dulcis ad refocillandam animam! Prorsus suavis et dulcis, etiam cum crucias et affligis; cruciatus feliciter delectans, sicut ignis salubriter refrigerans. Ignis iste, fratres, si in sinu absconditus fuerit, vestimenta non comburit; imo illum ignem exstinguit, qui, in sinu celatus, non modo vestimentum adurit, sed et quod vestitum est, depascit, id est, corpus et animam pariter, consumit. Deus, inquit, noster ignis consumens est (Deut. iv, 24), sed ignem consumentem; nam animam refovet, demulcet mentem, spiritum recreat, reparatque pereuntem. Hujus ignis vim Simeon non ignorabat, qui in sinu suo eum portare gaudebat, pectusque senile fotu illius calefaciebat. Quanto salubrius et suavius ignis iste senem nostrum calefecit, quam regem David Abisag Sunamitis? (III Reg. i, 17) nisi forte Abisag hic ignis idem fuit, sapientia scilicet, cujus amplexu non solum recalescunt frigidi, sed etiam reviviscunt mortui."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. Leaving aside, then, what has been sufficiently treated, let us consider instead, if you please, what past event this most beautiful custom of the Church, carrying lights today, represents, or what action it shows us we should perform. Yet I do not think even this is unknown to you, though you may never have heard it explained. For who today, carrying a burning candle in his hands, would not immediately remember that old man who today took Jesus in his arms, the Word in flesh like light in wax, and testified that he was the light to enlighten the nations? He himself, indeed, was also a burning and shining lamp, bearing witness to the light. In the Spirit who filled him he came into the temple for this very purpose: receiving your mercy, O God, in the midst of your temple, to proclaim it to be the mercy and light of your people. Truly, serene old man, you carried the light in your understanding as well as in your hands. You were so enlightened that, while seeing so clearly so far in advance the future enlightenment of the nations, even then, amid the darkness of Jewish unbelief, the present brightness of our faith flashed before you. Rejoice now, righteous elder; see now what you foresaw: the world's darkness has been scattered, the nations walk in your light; the whole earth is full of the glory of that light which you cherished hidden in your bosom, or rather, by which you warmed your understanding. O kindling of love, love itself, pleasant and sweet for reviving the soul! Truly pleasant and sweet, even when you torment and afflict: a torment that brings happy delight, like a fire that gives wholesome coolness. This fire, brothers, if it is hidden in the bosom, does not burn the garments; rather, it extinguishes that fire which, concealed in the bosom, not only scorches the garment but devours what is clothed, that is, consumes body and soul together. Our God, it says, is a consuming fire (Deut. iv, 24), but one that consumes the consuming fire; for it warms the soul, soothes the mind, refreshes the spirit, and restores the one who is perishing. Simeon knew the power of this fire: he rejoiced to carry it in his bosom and warmed his aged breast with its heat. How much more wholesomely and pleasantly this fire warmed our old man than Abishag the Shunammite warmed King David! (III Reg. i, 17) Unless perhaps Abishag was this very same fire, namely wisdom, in whose embrace not only do the cold grow warm again, but even the dead return to life.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Amplectere igitur, o beate senex, sapientiam Dei, et recalescant atque recanescant quasi de novo sensus tui; astringe uberibus tuis misericordiam Dei, et erit senectus tua in misericordia uberi. Inter ubera, inquit, mea commorabitur (Cantic. i, 12), etiam cum reddam illum Matri suae, manebit apud me. Et cum inter materna ubera continebitur, nihilominus inter ubera mea commorabitur, et ubera quoque mea inebriabit misericordia uberi; etsi non quantum ubera Matris. Illa quippe sicut singulariter mater est summae misericordiae, sic excellenter habet ubera uberis misericordiae. Gratulor et gratificor tibi, gratia plena, quae misericordiam genuisti quam suscepi; cereum praeparasti, quem accepi. Tu ceram ministrasti suscepto lumini, Virgo virginum Virgini; dum incorrupta mater incorruptibile Verbum, incorrupta carne vestisti. Eia, fratres mei, ecce ardet cereus in manibus Simeonis, accendite et vos cereos vestros de illius mutuatione luminis, lucernas dico quas Dominus jubet esse in manibus vestris (Luc. xii, 35). Accedite ad eum, et illuminamini, ut non tam lucernas feratis quam ipsi lucernae sitis, lucentes intus et foris, vobis et proximis. Sit ergo lucerna in corde, sit in manu, sit in ore. Lucerna in corde luceat vobis: lucerna in manu vel ore luceat proximis Lucerna in corde, est pietas fidei; lucerna in manu, exemplum operis; lucerna in ore, sermo aedificationis. Non solum autem coram hominibus lucere nos necesse est per operationem et sermonem, sed et coram angelis, per orationem, coram Deo per intentionem. Lucerna nostra coram angelis est pura devotio, cum in conspectu angelorum psallimus sapienter vel oramus ardenter; lucerna coram Deo, simplex intentio, ut ei soli placeamus, cui nos probavimus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "4. Embrace, then, blessed old man, the wisdom of God, and let your faculties grow warm and gray again, as though anew; clasp God's mercy to your breasts, and your old age will abound in mercy. Between my breasts, he says, he will dwell (Cantic. i, 12); even when I give him back to his Mother, he will remain with me. And when he is held between his Mother's breasts, he will nevertheless dwell between my breasts, and will also fill my breasts to overflowing with abundant mercy, though not as much as his Mother's breasts. For just as she is uniquely the mother of supreme mercy, so her breasts possess abundant mercy in an outstanding way. I rejoice with you and give you thanks, full of grace, who bore the mercy I have received; you prepared the candle I have taken. You supplied wax for the light that was received, O Virgin of virgins, for the Virgin, when as an uncorrupted mother you clothed the incorruptible Word in uncorrupted flesh. Come, my brothers: see, the candle burns in Simeon's hands. Light your own candles too by borrowing from his light, I mean the lamps the Lord commands to be in your hands (Luc. xii, 35). Come to him and be enlightened, so that you may not so much carry lamps as be lamps yourselves, shining within and without, for yourselves and your neighbors. Let there be a lamp, then, in the heart, in the hand, and in the mouth. Let the lamp in the heart shine for you; let the lamp in the hand or mouth shine for your neighbors. The lamp in the heart is the devotion of faith; the lamp in the hand, the example of deeds; the lamp in the mouth, speech that builds up. We must shine not only before men through deeds and speech, but also before angels through prayer, and before God through intention. Our lamp before angels is pure devotion, when in the sight of angels we sing psalms with understanding or pray fervently; the lamp before God is a single intention, to please him alone, to whom we have approved ourselves.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Et ne putemur, in his quae dicimus, sensus nostri somnia secuti fuisse; probemus nos potius in omnibus adhaesisse testimoniis Scripturae. Quod enim fides lucerna sit, testatur Salomon loquens de muliere forti, Non exstinguetur in nocte lucerna ejus (Prov. xxxi, 18); quod utique intelligimus, Non deficiet in tentatione fides ejus. Nam et de charitate, quae nihil aliud est quam fides operans per dilectionem, apud eumdem legimus Salomonem: Lampades ejus, lampades ignis atque flammarum. Aquae multae non poterunt exstinguere charitatem, nec flumina obruent illam (Cantic. viii, 6, 7). Quod opus bonum lucerna sit, lux ipsa veritatis indicat, cum ait, Sint lumbi vestri praecincti, et lucernae ardentes in manibus vestris (Luc. xii, 35): et, Luceat lux vestra coram hominibus, ut videant opera vestra bona (Matth. v, 16). Quod sermo aedificationis lucerna sit, David dicit: Lucerna pedibus meis verbum tuum, et lumen semitis meis (Psal. cxviii, 105). Et Petrus apostolus de sermone prophetico, cui benefacitis, inquit, intendentes, tanquam lucernae lucenti in obscuro loco (II Petr. i, 19). Declaratio quoque sermonum Dei illuminat, et intellectum dat parvulis (Psal. cxviii, 130), sive cum sermones evangelici declarant umbram legis, sive cum ipsi sermones tam Evangelii quam legis declarantur expositionibus dilucidis, qui non intelligentibus parvulis videbantur obscuri; et in lumine dicimus quod dictum erat nobis in tenebris. Ideo Philippus in figura cujuslibet ecclesiastici doctoris dictus est os lampadis, eo quod de ore ipsius ad illuminationem audientium coruscaret ignitum eloquium Domini. Sermonem autem Dei intelligo, fratres mei, quidquid spiritus ejus dignatione sua loquitur in vobis; omnem utique sermonem qui bonus est ad aedificationem fidei, in provocationem charitatis. Si quis ergo vestrum loquitur, quasi sermones Dei (I Petr. iv, 11); ut etiam in privatis colloquiis omnis sermo malus non procedat de ore vestro, sed si quis est ad aedificationem fidei, ut gratiam det audienti; ut qui audierit, dicat gratias agens tibi, Benedictus sermo oris tui, quia lucerna pedibus meis verbum tuum, et lumen semitis meis (Psal. cxviii, 105). De oratione autem quod et ipsa lucerna sit, maxime si divinitus sit illuminata, ex eo colligimus quod Salomon ait: Lucerna Domini spiraculum hominis, quae investigat omnia secreta ventris (Prov xx, 27). Lumen namque quod nobis orantibus vel psallentibus desuper aperitur, spiraculum vitae est, in quod suaviter respiramus. Hujus lucernae videtur Job recordari, cum in moerore tentationis recolens cum suspirio laetitiam praeteritae consolationis, ait: Quis mihi tribuat ut sim juxta menses pristinos, secundum dies quibus Deus custodiebat me, quando splendebat lucerna ejus super caput meum, et ad lumen ejus ambulabam in tenebris? (Job, xxix, 2, 3.) Caeterum quod lucerna haec dicitur investigare omnia secreta ventris, id est mentis; non illud esse putandum est, quod Dominus comminatur se scrutaturum in lucernis (Sophon. i, 12): cum istud gratiae sit illuminantis, illud judicii exquirentis. Nam et aliter investigat secreta ventris potio medici; aliter gladius carnificis. Jam vero de intentione, quod et ipsa dicatur lucerna, nemo est qui ambigat, si tamen evangelicum illud intelligat: Lucerna corporis tui est oculus tuus (Matth. vi, 22, et Luc. xi, 34)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. And lest we be thought, in what we say, to have followed the dreams of our own understanding, let us show instead that in everything we have kept to the testimonies of Scripture. For Solomon testifies that faith is a lamp when he says of the valiant woman, Her lamp will not be extinguished in the night (Prov. xxxi, 18); which we understand to mean, Her faith will not fail in temptation. Concerning charity too, which is nothing other than faith working through love, we read in the same Solomon: Its lamps are lamps of fire and flame. Many waters will not be able to extinguish charity, nor will rivers overwhelm it (Cantic. viii, 6, 7). That a good deed is a lamp is shown by the light of truth itself when it says, Let your loins be girded, and lamps burning in your hands (Luc. xii, 35); and, Let your light shine before men, that they may see your good deeds (Matth. v, 16). That speech which builds up is a lamp, David declares: Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my paths (Psal. cxviii, 105). And the Apostle Peter says of the prophetic word, to which you do well to attend as to a lamp shining in a dark place (II Petr. i, 19). The unfolding of God's words also gives light and understanding to little ones (Psal. cxviii, 130), whether the words of the Gospel make plain the shadow of the law, or the words of both Gospel and law themselves are made plain by clear explanations, having seemed obscure to little ones who did not understand; and we speak in the light what was said to us in darkness. Thus Philip, as a figure of every teacher in the Church, was called the mouth of a lamp, because the Lord's fiery utterance flashed from his mouth to enlighten his hearers. By God's word, my brothers, I mean whatever his Spirit graciously speaks in you: every word, indeed, that is good for building up faith and stirring up charity. If any of you speaks, let him speak as the words of God (I Petr. iv, 11), so that even in private conversations no evil word may proceed from your mouth, but only such as serves to build up faith and give grace to the hearer; so that whoever hears may thank you and say, Blessed is the word of your mouth, for your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my paths (Psal. cxviii, 105). That prayer too is a lamp, especially when divinely illuminated, we gather from Solomon's saying: The breath of man is the Lord's lamp, which searches all the hidden places of the belly (Prov xx, 27). For the light opened to us from above as we pray or sing psalms is the breath of life, into which we breathe with delight. Job seems to remember this lamp when, in the sorrow of trial, recalling with a sigh the joy of past consolation, he says: Who will grant me to be as in former months, in the days when God guarded me, when his lamp shone above my head and by its light I walked in darkness? (Job, xxix, 2, 3.) Yet when this lamp is said to search all the hidden places of the belly, that is, of the mind, we must not suppose it to be the same as the Lord's threat to search with lamps (Sophon. i, 12): this belongs to illuminating grace, that to searching judgment. For a physician's medicine searches the hidden places of the belly in one way, an executioner's sword in another. As for intention, no one doubts that it too is called a lamp, provided he understands the Gospel saying: The lamp of your body is your eye (Matth. vi, 22, et Luc. xi, 34).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ut igitur tot lucernas vobis, fratres, illuminetis, accedite ad fontem luminis, et illuminamini, Jesum dico, qui lucet in manibus Simeonis, ut scilicet fidem illuminet, opus illustret, sermonem homini subministret, orationem inflammet, intentionem purget; ut sive opere, sive sermone vel oratione quaeratis placere coram eo in lumine viventium, qui scrutari habet Jerusalem in lucernis suis (Sophon. i, 12), et examinare etiam lumen nostrum. His omnibus accensis, o fili lucis, jam non ambulabis in tenebris, nec timenda tibi erit sententia maledicti, Qui maledixerit patri, vel matri, exstinguetur lucerna ejus in tenebris (Prov. xx, 20), id est, deficiet consolatio hujus lucis, irruentibus undique hinc exterioribus, inde interioribus tenebris. Tibi enim, cui tot interius ardent lucernae, cum exstincta fuerit lucerna hujus vitae, orietur lux inexstinguibilis vitae, et quasi fulgor meridianus consurget tibi ad vesperum: et cum te consumptum putaveris, orieris ut lucifer, et tenebrae tuae erunt sicut meridies (Job. xi, 17, et Isai. lviii, 10). Non tamen erit tibi sol amplius ad lucendum per diem, aut splendor lunae illuminabit te; sed Dominus erit tibi in lucem sempiternam; quia Agnus lucerna est novae Jerusalem cui benedictio et claritas in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Therefore, brothers, to light so many lamps for yourselves, come to the fountain of light and be enlightened: I mean Jesus, who shines in Simeon's hands, so that he may enlighten faith, make deeds radiant, supply man with speech, inflame prayer, and purify intention. Thus, whether by deed, speech, or prayer, you may seek to please him in the light of the living, before him who is to search Jerusalem with his lamps (Sophon. i, 12) and examine even our light. With all these kindled, O child of light, you will no longer walk in darkness, nor will you have to fear the sentence of the curse: Whoever curses father or mother, his lamp will be extinguished in darkness (Prov. xx, 20); that is, the consolation of this light will fail, as darkness rushes in from every side, outward darkness on one side and inward darkness on the other. For you, within whom so many lamps burn, when the lamp of this life is extinguished, the inextinguishable light of life will arise, and at evening a brightness like noonday will rise for you. When you think yourself consumed, you will rise like the morning star, and your darkness will be as midday (Job. xi, 17, et Isai. lviii, 10). Yet the sun will no longer serve to give you light by day, nor will the brightness of the moon illuminate you; rather, the Lord will be your everlasting light, for the Lamb is the lamp of the new Jerusalem, to whom be blessing and glory for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The source numbers the sections 1, 2, 4, 4, 5; the repeated 4 is retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition includes an alternative Latin form after habuisset: [alias habuit). The English translates alias as \u201cvariant\u201d and retains the mismatched brackets. Guerric\u2019s conditional wording about Mary\u2019s purity, and his distinction between Mary and the child conceived, are retained. The language about the menstruating woman comes from the sermon\u2019s biblical quotation."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The opening quotation about lamps is printed with Luke 22:35, but comes from Luke 12:35. The reference to Abishag warming David is printed as III Regum 1:17; the episode is in modern 1 Kings 1:1\u20134. Both source references are retained."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The divine fire consumes the destructive fire while renewing the person. \u201cGrow warm and gray again\u201d preserves Guerric\u2019s wordplay about age and warmth. He applies \u201cVirgin\u201d to Christ as well as to Mary, and develops the imagery of breasts, wax and light. His explanation of Philip as \u201cmouth of a lamp\u201d is his own interpretation. The contrast between a physician\u2019s medicine and an executioner\u2019s sword belongs to the sermon."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon I: The Ceremony of Lights", "topics": ["Purification", "Virgin Mary", "Simeon", "light", "humility", "faith", "charity", "prayer", "good works"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo II, Quanto sit affectu occurrendum Jesu advenienti, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 67-71.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Psalm 117:26\u201328 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:38", "Psalm 117:27 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:27", "Lamentations 1:4", "Malachi 3:1", "Isaiah 21:12", "Daniel 4:14", "Proverbs 25:5", "Psalm 88:15 (historical numbering)", "John 7:34\u201336", "1 John 5:6", "Song of Songs 1:2", "Isaiah 25:1", "Luke 2:32", "Psalm 118:82 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 117:28 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 42:4 (historical numbering)", "John 4:23", "Acts 7:48", "Isaiah 69:16 (printed locator; no such chapter)", "Isaiah 66:10"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Simeon and Anna receive Christ with longing, joy and praise. Guerric describes the encounter as an anointing of the heart, then reads the four figures in the temple as examples of prayer, devotion, humility and charity. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quanto sit affectu occurrendum Jesu advenienti."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "With What Love We Should Go Out to Meet the Coming Jesus", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Sicut ad diem nativitatis, id est adventus Dominici, pertinuit illa gratiarum actio et vox laudis, Benedictus qui venit in nomine Domini; et ad diem apparitionis, id est, nostrae illuminationis, illud quod sequitur: Deus Dominus et illuxit nobis, sic etiam quod subsequitur, Constituite diem solemnem in confrequentationibus [Vulg. in condensis] usque ad cornu altaris; non absurde, ut arbitror, pertinere dicetur ad hunc diem, nobis utique celebrem, frequentia solemni, diem scilicet Dominicae Praesentationis, quando a Matre solemniter ad altare praesentatus est Patri. Nam et in eo quod adjungitur, Deus meus es tu, et confitebor tibi; Deus meus es tu, et exaltabo te; confitebor tibi quoniam exaudisti me, et factus es mihi in salutem (Psal. CXVII, 26-28), quid tam convenienter accipimus quam fidem et confessionem Simeonis et Annae, quae et ipsa hora superveniens confitebatur Domino, et loquebatur de illo omnibus qui exspectabant consolationem Israel. (Luc. II, 38)? Bene ergo citharista noster monebat laetari Jerusalem et conventum facere in die tam solemni: Constituite, inquiens, diem solemnem in confrequentationibus usque ad cornu altaris (Psal. CXVII, 27). Non mediocris aut parum solemnis illa frequentia fuit, cum inde venit Jesus et Maria cum turba parentum inducentium puerum, ut facerent secundum consuetudinem legis pro eo (Luc. II, 27); inde occurrit Simeon et Anna cum turba exspectantium consolationem Israel, quibus testificati sunt de eo."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Just as that thanksgiving and voice of praise, \"Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord,\" belonged to the day of the Nativity, that is, of the Lord's Advent; and just as what follows, \"The Lord is God, and he has shone upon us,\" belonged to the day of the Appearing, that is, of our illumination; so too, I think, it may not unfittingly be said that what comes next, \"Appoint a solemn day in the gatherings [Vulg. amid the thick boughs] even to the horn of the altar,\" belongs to this day \u2014 a day certainly made festive for us by a solemn gathering \u2014 namely, the day of the Lord's Presentation, when he was solemnly presented by his Mother to the Father at the altar. For in what follows, \"You are my God, and I will give thanks to you; you are my God, and I will exalt you; I will give thanks to you, for you have heard me, and you have become my salvation\" (Psal. CXVII, 26-28), what do we more fittingly understand than the faith and confession of Simeon and Anna, who, coming in at that very hour, gave thanks to the Lord and spoke of him to all who were awaiting the consolation of Israel (Luc. II, 38)? Well, then, did our harpist urge Jerusalem to rejoice and to hold assembly on so solemn a day, saying: \"Appoint a solemn day in the gatherings, even to the horn of the altar\" (Psal. CXVII, 27). That gathering was no mean or unimpressive one, when from one side came Jesus and Mary with the crowd of relatives bringing in the child, that they might do for him according to the custom of the law (Luc. II, 27); and from the other side Simeon and Anna came to meet them with the crowd of those awaiting the consolation of Israel, to whom they bore witness concerning him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Non crediderim ego de hac solemnitate quae tam pium, tam laetum conventum habuit, illam fuisse prophetae querelam lacrymabilem, Viae Sion lugent, quia non est qui veniat ad solemnitatem (Thren. I, 4): nisi forte arguat paucos venisse de tanto populo qui erat in omni Judaea et Jerusalem. Ad comparationem siquidem tantae multitudinis pauci venerunt, et de ipsis paucis paucissimi, ut arbitror, eum receperunt, cum praesentem viderent diu exspectatum, sicut voce Patris par Malachiam fuerat repromissum: Ecce, inquit, veniet ad templum suum sanctum Dominator quem vos quaeritis, et Angelus testamenti quem vos vultis (Malach. III, 1). O Judaei, adest, ecce, Dominator quem vos quaeritis? cur offerentem se vobis non recipitis? Quaeritis et non quaeritis. Sed si quaeritis; quaerite: convertimini et venite (Isai. XXI, 12). Quaeritis qui redimat de manu hominum, non quaeritis qui redimat de manu daemonum. Quaeritis qui eruat de servitute Romanorum, non quaeritis qui eruat de captivitate vitiorum. Si vere utique liberatorem quaeritis, quaerite liberatorem animarum vestrarum, qui salvum faciat populum suum a peccatis eorum. Si quaeritis sublimem, quaerite prius humilem, quoniam, ut ait Daniel: Humillimum hominem constituet Deus super omne regnum (Dan. IV, 14). Si quaeritis dominatorem potentiae, quaerite prius doctorem justitiae, quia justitia firmabitur thronus ejus (Prov. XXV, 5), et justitia et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Quia enim perfecte non quaeritis, ideo nec praesentem invenitis, sicut de vobis ipse praedixit: Quaerent me mali, et non invenient me, quia oderunt me (Joan. VII, 34-36). Quaerent absentem, nec invenient etiam praesentem, quia oderunt lucem mala eorum opera arguentem, cum sapientia non nisi per dilectionem possit quaeri, nedum inveniri. Simeon namque, quia pio et fideli quaerebat desiderio, quaesitum invenit, et inventum agnovit sine alterius indicio, sine humano scilicet testimonio. Nam Spiritus est qui testificatur quoniam Christus est veritas (I Joan. V, 6); virtus utique quae de ipso procedit, unctio docens de omnibus, quae de illo, singulariter secundum nomen suum uncto, multipliciter profluit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. I would not believe that of this solemnity, which had so devout and so joyful a gathering, there held true that tearful complaint of the prophet: \"The ways of Sion mourn, because there is none that comes to the solemnity\" (Thren. I, 4) \u2014 unless perhaps it be charging that few came, out of so great a people as was then in all Judea and Jerusalem. For in comparison with so great a multitude, few came; and of those few, I think, very few indeed received him, though they saw present before them him who had long been awaited, just as had been promised by the voice of the Father through Malachi: \"Behold,\" he says, \"the Ruler whom you seek shall come to his holy temple, and the Angel of the covenant whom you desire\" (Malach. III, 1). O Jews, behold \u2014 is the Ruler whom you seek present here? Why do you not receive him who offers himself to you? You seek, and you do not seek. But if you seek, then seek: turn and come (Isai. XXI, 12). You seek one who may redeem you from the hand of men; you do not seek one who may redeem you from the hand of demons. You seek one who may rescue you from servitude to the Romans; you do not seek one who may rescue you from captivity to vices. If indeed you truly seek a liberator, seek the liberator of your souls, who will save his people from their sins. If you seek one who is exalted, seek first one who is humble, since, as Daniel says: \"God will set the most humble of men over every kingdom\" (Dan. IV, 14). If you seek a ruler of power, seek first a teacher of justice, for \"his throne shall be established by justice\" (Prov. XXV, 5), and \"justice and judgment are the preparation of his seat\" (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). For because you do not seek perfectly, therefore you do not find him even when he is present, just as he himself foretold concerning you: \"The wicked will seek me, and will not find me, because they have hated me\" (Joan. VII, 34-36). They will seek him absent, and will not find him even when present, because they hated the light that exposes their evil works, since wisdom cannot be sought \u2014 much less found \u2014 except through love. For Simeon, because he sought with pious and faithful desire, found what he sought, and recognized what he had found without any other indication, that is, without human testimony. For it is the Spirit who testifies that Christ is the truth (I Joan. V, 6) \u2014 namely, the power that proceeds from him, the anointing that teaches concerning all things, which flows forth in many ways from him who, uniquely according to his own name, is the Anointed One.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Quid autem putamus, fratres mei, unctus iste, qui ungit etiam quos non tangit, quomodo candidum et sanctum pectus senis nostri perunxerit, cum suscipiens eum in ulnas suas, astringeret eum uberibus suis, et gestiret includere, si posset, in intimis cordis sui? Quid, inquam, putamus ille suavis et mitis, qualem insinuabat seipsum castissimo sinui pii senis, qualis illabebatur medullis, quam delectabilem et salubrem, imo quam omnino ineffabilem se implicabat ossibus, inspirabat sensibus? Prorsus liquescebat unctus in unctionem, quasi ad ignem resolutus ad amplectentis amorem; ut nimirum doceret unctio quod dicit lectio, sed non discit nisi dilectio. Quomodo enim legis? Unguentum, inquit, exinanitum [Vulg. effusum] nomen tuum (Cantic. I, 2). Hoc siquidem unguentum exinanitur, cum a quadam duritia sui resolvitur, non ut pereffluat substantia illius, sed ut fluant aromata illius, ut quasi nihil habens solidi sentiri possit, teneri non possit. Aut certe magis anima senis liquescebat in amplexu hujus uncti, vel unguenti, ut diceret: Anima mea liquefacta est, ut dilectus meus infusus est mihi. Infusio itaque unctionis, testimonium est uncti. Haec seni testabatur de puero, Simeoni de Christo, tanquam spiritus veritatis et charitatis, veritate docens, charitate ungens et accendens, et omnia interiora gelidi senis ponens, quasi cum unguenta bulliunt. Motus bullientium unguentorum, aestus desideriorum: sonus eorum, jubilus affectuum pariter dicentium (si tamen aliquid exprimere valentium), Deus meus es tu, et confitebor tibi; Deus meus es tu, et exaltabo te (Isa. XXV, 1). Lumen ad revelationem gentium, et gloriam plebis tuae Israel (Luc. II, 32), annuntians te. Confitebor, inquam, tibi, quoniam exaudisti me, quotidie cum lacrymis dicentem, Quando consolaberis me? (Psal. CXVIII, 82.) Exaudisti me, et nunc demum Simeonem, id est, exauditum fecisti me; quia factus es mihi in salutem (Psal. CXVII, 28). Jesus mihi factus ex semine David secundum carnem, qui David et omnia fecisti secundum deitatem, omnibus quidem generaliter factus es in Salvatorem; sed mihi specialiter factus es in salutem, pectus morbidum perungens, vitalemque resuscitans calorem, animam refocillans, et oculos illuminans. Defecerat enim anima mea in salutare tuum, o Domine, Pater Domini mei Jesu Christi, defecerunt oculi mei in eloquium tuum, dicentes: Quando consolaberis me? (Psal. CXVIII, 82.) Factus eram sicut uter in pruina, frigidus corpore, aridus corde, spiritu marcidus, et totus desiderio languidus. Sed quia in verbum tuae promissionis supersperavi, non visurum scilicet me mortem, nisi viderem Christum tuum, quod speravi jam video, quod desideravi teneo, quod concupivi complector. Video Deum Salvatorem meum in carne mea, et salva facta est anima mea. Viderunt oculi mei salutare Dei, et illuminati sunt interiores oculi mei, qui languebant prae inopia. Omnino ad tactum hujus pueri, hujus hominis novi, renovata est ut aquilae juventus mea, sicut paulo ante mihi loquens pollicebar. Introibo ad altare Dei, ubi Maria Jesum offert Patri, ad Deum qui laetificat senectutem meam, imo renovabit juventutem meam (Psal. XLII, 4)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But what do we think, my brothers, as to how this Anointed One\u2014who anoints even those he does not touch\u2014anointed the white and holy breast of our old man, when, taking him up into his arms, he pressed him to his breast and longed, if he could, to enclose him in the innermost depths of his heart? What, I say, do we think\u2014how sweet and gentle he was, in the way he insinuated himself into the most chaste bosom of the devout old man, how he glided into his inmost marrow, how delightful and health-giving\u2014nay, how utterly ineffable\u2014was the way he entwined himself in his bones and breathed himself into his senses? Truly the Anointed was melting into anointing, as though dissolved by fire into the love of the one embracing him; so that the anointing itself might teach what the reading tells, but which only love can learn. For how do you read? 'Your name,' it says, 'is an ointment emptied out [Vulg. poured out]' (Cantic. I, 2). This ointment indeed is emptied out when it is dissolved from a certain hardness of itself, not so that its substance flows entirely away, but so that its fragrant spices may flow forth, so that, having as it were nothing solid, it may be perceptible but not graspable. Or rather, the old man's soul was melting in the embrace of this Anointed One, or of this ointment, so that he might say: 'My soul has melted, as my beloved has been poured into me.' The pouring-in of the anointing, then, is the testimony of the Anointed One. This it testified to the old man concerning the child, to Simeon concerning Christ, as the Spirit of truth and of love, teaching by truth, anointing and kindling by love, and setting all the inward parts of the chilled old man aglow, as it were, when ointments come to a boil. The stirring of boiling ointments is the ardor of desires; their sound is the jubilation of affections speaking together (if indeed anything can be expressed), 'You are my God, and I will give thanks to you; you are my God, and I will exalt you' (Isa. XXV, 1). 'A light for the revelation of the Gentiles, and the glory of your people Israel' (Luc. II, 32), announcing you. I will give thanks to you, I say, because you have heard me, day after day saying with tears, 'When will you console me?' (Psal. CXVIII, 82.). You have heard me, and now at last you have made Simeon\u2014that is, you have made me\u2014one who is heard; because you have become my salvation (Psal. CXVII, 28). Jesus, made for me of the seed of David according to the flesh, you who made David and all things according to your Godhead, have become Savior of all generally; but to me specially you have become salvation, anointing my sick breast, reviving my vital warmth, refreshing my soul, and enlightening my eyes. For my soul had fainted for your salvation, O Lord, Father of my Lord Jesus Christ, my eyes had failed for your word, saying, 'When will you console me?' (Psal. CXVIII, 82.). I had become like a wineskin in the frost, cold in body, dry in heart, withered in spirit, and altogether languishing with desire. But because I had hoped beyond hope in the word of your promise, namely that I should not see death unless I had seen your Christ, what I hoped for I now see, what I desired I now hold, what I longed for I now embrace. I see God my Savior in my flesh, and my soul has been saved. My eyes have seen the salvation of God, and my inward eyes, which were failing for want, have been enlightened. Altogether, at the touch of this child, this new man, my youth has been renewed like the eagle's, just as a little while ago I was promising myself, speaking to myself. 'I will go in to the altar of God, where Mary offers Jesus to the Father, to God who gives joy to my old age\u2014nay, who will renew my youth' (Psal. XLII, 4).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Nec minore fortassis gaudio exsultabat cor Annae in Domino; quae, ut arbitror tanto majoris fuit meriti, quanto altioris propositi et sanctioris desiderii, quam illa Anna mater Samuelis; cum illa cogitaret quae mundi sunt, quomodo placeret viro; haec quomodo Deo. illa de thoro viri quaereret sobolem; haec de sinu Patris peteret Salvatorem. Haec igitur, quodam presagio gratiae excellentis quae in vita ejus effloruit, qua etiam gratiam Redemptoris agnoscere et praedicare meruit, gratiae nomen accepit; exemplar verae viduitatis, speculum sanctitatis, et vivum in emortuo corpore simulacrum totius virtutis. Haec finem Legis et initium Evangelii sentiens propinquare, ritus Judaicos christiana jam coeperat pietate mutare, jejuniis potius et orationibus serviens nocte ac die, quam cibis et potibus et variis baptismatibus, justitiis carnis, non animae. Inde erat quod benedictione filiorum, quae in Lege magni habebatur, contempta, nomen melius a filiis et filiabus elegerat, et jam per annos octoginta quatuor, lignum esse aridum lignoque insidere arido, tanquam turtur amissa semel conjuge, gaudebat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Nor was the heart of Anna perhaps exulting in the Lord with any less joy; who, I think, was of so much the greater merit as she was of a higher purpose and a holier desire than that other Anna, the mother of Samuel; since that Anna thought on the things of the world, how she might please her husband, but this one thought how she might please God; that Anna sought offspring from her husband's bed, but this one sought the Savior from the bosom of the Father. This woman, therefore, by a kind of presaging of the excellent grace which flowered in her life\u2014by which she also deserved to recognize and proclaim the grace of the Redeemer\u2014received the name of grace; an example of true widowhood, a mirror of holiness, and a living image, in a body already as good as dead, of every virtue. Sensing that the end of the Law and the beginning of the Gospel were drawing near, she had already, with Christian devotion, begun to exchange the Jewish rites, serving night and day rather in fastings and prayers than in foods and drinks and various washings\u2014righteousnesses of the flesh, not of the soul. Hence it was that, despising the blessing of children, which was held in great esteem under the Law, she had chosen a better name than sons and daughters, and now, for eighty-four years, rejoiced to be a dry stick and to sit upon a dry stick, like a turtledove that has lost its mate once for all.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Ecce habes par turturum, senem justum, et viduam anum, utrumque castum, utrumque desiderio Redemptoris gemebundum; qui utique semetipsos hostiam vivam, sanctam, Deo placentem, Domino et pro Domino obtulerunt. Par turturum sane dico, non conjugum, sed conjunctorum, non copula matrimonii, sed sacratioris mysterii; pares nimirum fide, aequales castitate, similes devotione, consortes praedicatione gratiae, ambos provectae aetatis, ambos perfectae sanctitatis, qui etiam ante Evangelium, evangelicae puritatis ac pietatis in utroque sexu primitias dedicaverunt. Hi duo vario decore virtutem adornaverunt thalamum tuum, Sion, ad suscipiendum Christum Regem; non parietes templi tui, sed penetralia cordis sui secreta cubiculi, ubi adorare Patrem in spiritu et veritate jubemur (Joan. IV, 23), ubi et suscipitur qui in manufactis se nequaquam habitare testatur (Act. VII, 48). In istis itaque suscepit Christum fidelis Sion, in istis gaudens et laetabunda occurrit Deo suo. Alias vix habet ubi reclinet caput suum, et secundum Isaiam, Aporiatus est, et non est qui occurrat (Isa. LXIX, 16). Sufficit tamen istorum occursus ad omnem solemnitatem et laetitiam, eo quod consummatio abbreviata inundet justitiam, et consummata atque abundans justitia paucorum facile compenset infidelitatem multorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Behold, you have a pair of turtledoves: the righteous old man, and the aged widow, both chaste, both groaning with desire for the Redeemer; who indeed offered themselves as a living sacrifice, holy and pleasing to God, to the Lord and for the Lord. I call them, indeed, a pair of turtledoves \u2014 not of spouses, but of those joined together; not by the bond of matrimony, but by a more sacred mystery; equal, surely, in faith, equal in chastity, alike in devotion, companions in the preaching of grace, both of advanced age, both of perfect holiness, who, even before the Gospel, dedicated in both sexes the firstfruits of evangelical purity and piety. These two, with varied beauty, adorned your bridal chamber, O Zion, with virtue, for the receiving of Christ the King \u2014 not the walls of your temple, but the inner recesses of their hearts, the secret chamber, where we are bidden to worship the Father in spirit and in truth (Joan. IV, 23), where also he is received who testifies that he dwells in no way in things made by hands (Act. VII, 48). In these, then, faithful Zion received Christ; in these, rejoicing and glad, she went out to meet her God. Apart from these, he scarcely has anywhere to lay his head, and, according to Isaiah, 'He was dismayed, and there was none to meet him' (Isa. LXIX, 16). Yet the meeting of these two suffices for all solemnity and gladness, because a shortened consummation overflows with righteousness, and the consummated and abundant righteousness of a few easily compensates for the unfaithfulness of many.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Mihi omnino celebris est iste conventus, mihi solemnis ista processio, omnique festiva gaudio, ubi hinc advenit puer et puerpera, Jesus et Maria; inde occurrit senex et vidua, Simeon et Anna; hinc Dominus et Domina, inde servus et ancilla; hinc Mediator et Mediatrix, inde tam fideles tamque devoti ipsorum testes et ministri. In hoc denique conventu misericordia et veritas obviaverunt sibi, misericordia scilicet redemptionis in Jesu, veritas confessionis in senibus. In hoc occursu, justitia et pax osculatae sunt; dum justitia piorum senum, et pax reconciliantis mundum invicem in osculo affectuum et gaudio Spiritus consociatae sunt. Recte ergo quod hac nocte cantavimus, ad laetitiam hujus diei pertinere credimus, salvo duntaxat alio profundiore intellectu: Laetare, Jerusalem; et diem festum agite, omnes qui diligitis eam (Isai. LXVI, 10). Ac si diceretur: Laetata est Nazareth de annuntiatione, Bethlehem de nativitate: laetare et tu, Jerusalem, de purificatione, quoniam qui in Nazareth conceptus, et in Bethlehem natus est, in Jerusalem est susceptus et praedicatus. Et diem, inquit, festum agite, sive conventum facite, omnes qui diligitis eam, scilicet Jerusalem, id est, Festiva devotione convenite in templum, ad suscipiendum Redemptorem, omnes qui exspectatis redemptionem Israel."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p006", "label": "English", "text": "7. To me this gathering is altogether festive, to me this procession is solemn and full of festive joy, where on this side comes the child and the woman who bore him, Jesus and Mary; on that side comes to meet them the old man and the widow, Simeon and Anna; here the Lord and the Lady, there the servant and the handmaid; here the Mediator and the Mediatrix, there such faithful and devoted witnesses and ministers of them. In this gathering, finally, mercy and truth have met one another \u2014 namely, the mercy of redemption in Jesus, the truth of confession in the elders. In this meeting, righteousness and peace have kissed, while the righteousness of the devout elders and the peace of him who reconciles the world have been joined together in the kiss of affections and the joy of the Spirit. Rightly, then, do we believe that what we sang this night pertains to the joy of this day \u2014 without prejudice to another, deeper interpretation: 'Rejoice, Jerusalem, and keep festival, all you who love her' (Isai. LXVI, 10). As if it were said: Nazareth rejoiced at the annunciation, Bethlehem at the nativity; rejoice also, you, Jerusalem, at the purification, since he who was conceived in Nazareth and born in Bethlehem is received and proclaimed in Jerusalem. 'And keep festival,' it says, or 'make assembly, all you who love her' \u2014 namely, Jerusalem \u2014 that is: Gather with festive devotion into the temple, to receive the Redeemer, all you who await the redemption of Israel.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Jam vero, fratres, si moraliter, sicut mos vester est, aedificari vestra quaerit Sanctitas, quatuor illas illustres in hac processione consideret personas; quarum vita, non solum ecclesias illustrat, sed et coelos adornat: Jesum loquor et Mariam, Simeonem et Annam. Et, ut ab inferioribus ad superiora progrediatur sermo, in Anna, quae jejuniis et obsecrationibus serviebat nocte ac die, jejunium nobis commendatur et oratio; in Simeone, qui tanto Jesum amplectitur gaudio, pietas et devotio; in Maria, quae cum nihil deberet legi, in hac scilicet parte purificationis legem nihilominus implevit, humilitas et obedientia; in Domino autem Jesu, qui factus ex muliere, factus est etiam sub lege, ut eos qui sub lege erant redimeret, charitas et misericordia. Nam castitatis sanctimonia, cujus laus, ut comperi, vestram semper puritatem delectat, in omnibus effulsit communiter, licet satis dissimiliter; cum in senibus fuerit labore continentiae; in puella, munere gratiae; in puero, jure et proprietate naturae, non pueritiae recens natae, sed puritatis innatae. In ipso non solum hujus virtutis, sed etiam omnium origo et perfectio; ab ipso singularum distributio; per ipsum earum conservatio; apud ipsum remuneratio. Apud ipsum igitur ambiamus, tam virginis Mariae, quam Simeonis et Annae meritis suffragantibus, ut quod minus habemus virtutis largiatur, quod largitus fuerit tueatur; ut depositum ipsius, per tutelam ipsius integrum ei reconsignantes, remunerationem consequamur ipsius, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula seculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Now indeed, brothers, if your Holiness seeks to be built up morally, as is your custom, let it consider those four illustrious persons in this procession, whose life not only illumines churches but also adorns the heavens: I mean Jesus and Mary, Simeon and Anna. And, that the sermon may proceed from lower things to higher, in Anna, who served night and day in fastings and prayers, fasting and prayer are commended to us; in Simeon, who embraces Jesus with such great joy, piety and devotion; in Mary, who, though she owed nothing to the Law, nevertheless in this matter of the purification fulfilled the Law all the same, humility and obedience; and in the Lord Jesus, who, made of a woman, was also made under the Law, that he might redeem those who were under the Law, charity and mercy. For the holiness of chastity \u2014 the praise of which, as I have learned, always delights your own purity \u2014 shone forth in all of them alike, though in quite dissimilar ways: in the elders it was through the labor of continence; in the maiden, through the gift of grace; in the child, by the right and property of nature \u2014 not of a childhood recently born, but of purity that is innate. In him is the origin and perfection not only of this virtue but of all virtues; from him comes the distribution of each; through him their preservation; with him their reward. Let us therefore petition him, with the merits of the Virgin Mary, and of Simeon and Anna, interceding for us, that he may grant what virtue we lack, and may guard what he has granted; so that, handing back to him intact, under his own protection, his deposit entrusted to us, we may obtain his reward \u2014 who lives and reigns through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints par Malachiam, evidently a misprint for per Malachiam. The printed form is kept."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition numbers two consecutive sections 7 (Mihi omnino and Jam vero) and prints no section 6. The printed numbers are kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon II: With What Love We Should Go Out to Meet the Coming Jesus", "topics": ["Purification", "Simeon and Anna", "meeting Christ", "spiritual anointing", "joy"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo III, Quomodo Simeon imitandus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 71-75.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Isaiah 65:20", "Ecclesiastes 11:8", "Wisdom 4:8\u20139", "Proverbs 16:31", "Psalm 5:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 47:10 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 75:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 88:15 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:25", "Psalm 124:1\u20132 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:29", "Psalm 4:9 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 57:2", "1 Corinthians 4:5", "1 Corinthians 11:31\u201332", "1 Corinthians 4:4", "Sirach 1:28", "Malachi 3:1", "Isaiah 52:1", "Psalm 47:10\u201311 (historical numbering)"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Simeon carries the child who is the Ancient of Days. Guerric considers the virtues of old age, the reception of the Word in hearing and prayer, and the fear of God that accompanies justice. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Quomodo Simeon imitandus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "How Simeon Is to Be Imitated", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Senex puerum portabat, senex plenus dierum, puerum antiquum dierum. Longitudine dierum adimpleverat Deus senem istum, ut ostenderet ei salutare suum in vultu nostro; sed adhuc aliorum longitudine dierum adimpleturus erat, ut ostenderet ei salutare suum in vultu suo. Plenus ergo dierum temporalium portabat antiquum dierum antiquorum, atque spei aeternae pignus fidelissimum tenebat in sinu repositum; quod videlicet dies suos super dies regis adjiceret; puer ille, juventutemque ejus renovaret ut aquilae. Quanquam senem quoque istum, sicut Abraham, cum dicitur plenus dierum, libentius accipiam plenum virtutum quam temporum: cum tempora statum nullum habeant, suoque accessu decedentia, hominem potius exhauriant quam adimpleant. Sed neque magnum est, imo magis malum est, multis vixisse diebus, si non senueris virtutibus; cum puer centum annorum moriturus sit, et peccator centum annorum maledicendus (Isai. LXV, 20). Ideo, Si annis multis vixerit homo, ut ait Salomon, et in omnibus laetatus fuerit, meminisse debet tenebrosi temporis et dierum multorum, qui cum venerint, vanitatis arguentur praeterita (Eccle. XI, 8). Caeterum e contrario, Senectus venerabilis est, non diuturna, neque numero annorum computata. Cani sunt enim sensus hominis, et aetas senectutis vita immaculata (Sap. IV, 8, 9). Si hac utique senectute Simeon non consenuisset, nunquam coronam senum, sapientiam Dei Christum portare meruisset. Sic namque scriptum est: Corona dignitatis senectus, quae in viis justitiae reperitur (Prov. XVI, 31). Et quae est illa senectus, quae est in viis justitiae reperitur, quae reperienti in coronam aptatur, nisi sapientia Dei Christus? ipse namque merito justorum operum tribuit canos sensus sapientiae, quam postmodum formabit justis in coronam gloriae. Sanctus itaque senex Simeon, ut sanctius senesceret adhuc, senectutem sapientium, sive sapientiam senum factam puerum, in viis justitiae, cum scilicet in spiritu venit in templum, reperit, et comperit in veritate; quia senectus erat in lactente, sapientia in infante, virtus in infirmitate, Verbum in carne. O infantia, o senectus! quam bene vobis pariter convenit in hujus pueri sensibus et moribus; quo utique nihil innocentius, sed nihil sapientius, nihil jucundius, sed nihil maturius, nihil mitius, sed nihil justius!"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. An old man was carrying a boy\u2014an old man full of days, carrying the Boy who was ancient of days. God had fulfilled this old man with length of days, that He might show him His salvation in our face; but He was still going to fulfill him with the length of other days, that He might show him His salvation in His own face. Full, then, of temporal days, he was carrying the Ancient of ancient days, and he held, laid up in his bosom, the most faithful pledge of eternal hope: namely, that that Boy would add His days above the days of the king, and renew his youth like the eagle's. Although, when it is said of this old man too, as of Abraham, that he was \"full of days,\" I would rather understand it as full of virtues than of time; since times have no permanence, but by their very passing rather drain a man than fill him up. Nor indeed is it a great thing\u2014rather it is a worse thing\u2014to have lived many days, if you have not grown old in virtues; since the boy of a hundred years shall die, and the sinner of a hundred years shall be accursed (Isai. LXV, 20). Therefore, \"If a man has lived many years,\" as Solomon says, \"and has rejoiced in them all, he ought to remember the dark time and the many days, which when they come, the past will be proved vain\" (Eccle. XI, 8). But on the other hand, \"Venerable old age is not that of long duration, nor counted by the number of years. For the understanding of a man is gray hairs, and a spotless life is the age of old age\" (Sap. IV, 8, 9). If Simeon had not grown old with this very old age, he would never have deserved to carry the crown of old men, the Wisdom of God, Christ. For thus it is written: \"Old age is a crown of dignity, when it is found in the ways of justice\" (Prov. XVI, 31). And what is that old age which is found in the ways of justice, which fits the one who finds it as a crown, except the Wisdom of God, Christ? For He Himself, in reward for the deeds of the just, bestows the gray hairs of wisdom, which afterward He will fashion for the just into a crown of glory. And so holy old Simeon, that he might grow old yet more holily, found and truly discovered, in the ways of justice\u2014that is, when he came in the Spirit into the temple\u2014the old age of the wise, or the wisdom of old men become a child; because old age was in the nursing infant, wisdom in the babe, strength in weakness, the Word in flesh. O infancy, O old age! how well you agree together in the senses and manners of this Boy, than whom indeed nothing is more innocent, yet nothing more wise; nothing more delightful, yet nothing more mature; nothing more gentle, yet nothing more just!", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Vobis, o filii hominum, vobis jam tunc ipso suo silentio loquebatur Dei Sermo, ut videlicet malitia sitis parvuli, sensibus autem perfecti. Alii siquidem ex vobis columba seducta non habens cor, simplicitatem non salientes prudentia, relinquuntur fatui; alii sapientes sunt ut faciant mala, bene autem facere nesciunt, et, prudentiam non dulcorantes bonitate, efficiuntur acerbi. Prorsus congruum et gratum utrisque temperamentum atque condimentum puer iste antiquus dierum, in pueritia praeferens ingenitam sibi innocentiam, et de antiquitate dierum referens primogenitam omnium sapientiam. Si quis se imperfectum sensibus, aut inconditum intelligit moribus, veniat in templum cum Simeone, et puerum quem mater affert Maria, accipiat in manibus, id est Verbum Dei quod mater offert Ecclesia, complectatur affectibus Ipsum utique repositum in sinu sensus aedificabit, mores dulcorabit, totum statum mentis et ordinem vitae grata et salubri suavitate temperabit. Non solum autem mater Ecclesia in audiendo, sed multo magis mater gratia in orando, puerum tibi dabit amplectendum, si modo frequens et devotus orator venias in templum, ut dicas quotidie Deo: Adorabo ad templum sanctum tuum in timore tuo (Psal. V, 8). Quem enim Ecclesia praedicans offert auribus, gratia illuminans infert cordibus, eo utique praesentiorem et suaviorem, quo nudam veritatem puris tradidit sensibus. Veritatem quippe, quae est Christus, Maria vestitam carne, Ecclesia vestitam sermone, gratia nudam tradit amplectendam Spiritus infusione: quanquam hoc ipsum diverso modo fiat, pro captu scilicet suscipientis animae, sive pro arbitratu dispensantis misericordiae. Licet enim ipsam summae veritatis faciem non facile videamus, magnusque habeatur, cui vel per speculum et in aenigmate eam intueri datur: quiddam tamen ipsius, cum Spiritus nobis illabitur, veluti nudum sentimus, sensusque noster in amorem ipsius veluti ad nudum confricatur. Tunc prorsus ex sententia dicere possumus, et cantare laetabundi: Suscepimus, Deus, misericordiam tuam, in medio templi tui (Psal. XLVII, 10). Beatus qui ut dignius et crebrius eam suscipiat, congruum ei locum in cordis penetralibus parat. Beata Sion illa, quae thalamum suum diligenter ac decenter adornat, ut cum digno ac placito Christum regem honore suscipiat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. To you, O sons of men, even then by His very silence the Word of God was speaking, namely, that you should be children in malice, but mature in understanding. For some of you, like a dove that has been deceived, having no heart, not tempering simplicity with prudence, are left foolish; others are wise to do evil, but know not how to do good, and, not sweetening prudence with goodness, become harsh. A thoroughly fitting and pleasing blend and seasoning for both is this Boy who is ancient of days, who in His childhood displays the innocence native to Him, and from the antiquity of His days brings forth the wisdom that is firstborn of all things. If anyone perceives himself imperfect in understanding, or unformed in manners, let him come into the temple with Simeon, and let him receive into his hands the Boy whom His mother Mary brings\u2014that is, the Word of God which the Church, His mother, offers\u2014and let him embrace Him in his affections. He, once laid up in the bosom of the mind, will build up the understanding, sweeten the manners, and temper the whole condition of the soul and order of life with a pleasing and wholesome sweetness. But not only will Mother Church give you the Boy to embrace in hearing, but much more will Mother Grace give Him to you in prayer, if only you come as a frequent and devout worshiper into the temple, so as to say daily to God: \"I will worship toward thy holy temple in fear of thee\" (Psal. V, 8). For the One whom the Church, preaching, offers to the ears, grace, illuminating, brings into the hearts\u2014all the more present and sweet, the more it has delivered the naked truth to pure senses. For the Truth, which is Christ, Mary delivers clothed in flesh, the Church delivers clothed in speech, but grace delivers Him naked to be embraced, by the infusion of the Spirit: although this itself happens in different ways, according to the capacity of the soul that receives, or according to the judgment of the mercy that dispenses it. For although we do not easily see the very face of the highest Truth, and he is accounted great to whom it is given even to behold it through a mirror and in an enigma; yet something of it, when the Spirit flows into us, we feel as it were bare, and our spiritual sense is, as it were, rubbed bare so as to love Him. Then indeed we can say with full conviction, and sing joyfully: \"We have received thy mercy, O God, in the midst of thy temple\" (Psal. XLVII, 10). Blessed is he who, that he may receive it more worthily and more often, prepares a fitting place for it in the inner chambers of his heart. Blessed is that Sion which diligently and fittingly adorns her bridal chamber, that she may receive Christ the King with due and pleasing honor.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. O sancta Sion, anima speculans aeterna, considera quae sit illa majestas suscipienda; cogita qua cura ac diligentia te illi praeparare locum oporteat. Si quis dixerit: Et ad haec quis idoneus? quis esse potest magnificentiae et gloriae tanti Regis congruus ornatus? Frustra, inquit David, paupertatem quispiam causetur: In pace factus est locus ejus (Psal. LXXV, 3); et: Justitia et judicium praeparatio sedis ejus (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Simeon utique quia hujusmodi palliis thalamum adornavit, ideo Christum cum multa suscipere gratia dignus fuit. Vis videre quia in pace invenerat locum Domino, quia sedem praeparaverat ei in justitia et judicio? Homo, inquit, erat in Jerusalem, cui nomen Simeon; et homo iste justus et timoratus (Luc. II, 25). In pace erat domus Simeonis, qui habitabat in Jerusalem, id est, in visione et amore pacis; de qua scilicet Jerusalem audierat David promittentem: Non commovebitur in aeternum qui habitat in Jerusalem (Psal. CXXIV, 1, 2). Et, ut noveris quia pacis habitator et amator erat, pacemque imperturbabilem illius stabilis et aeternae Jerusalem prae omnibus requirebat; cum pacem dei et hominum Jesum, qui pacem super pacem dare veniebat, sinu recepit, haec prima vox de pectore senis jamdudum pacem meditantis erupit: Nunc dimittis servum tuum, Domine, secundum verbum tuum, in pace (Luc. II, 29). Postquam viderunt oculi mei salutare tuum, qui est pax nostra faciens utraque unum, non solum Judaeum et Gentilem, sed etiam Deum et hominem, et in homine spiritum et carnem: jam hoc solum superest desideriis servi tui, ut in pace in idipsum dormiam et requiescam (Psal. IV, 9); et pax Dei quae exsuperat omnem sensum, me totum in illius summae et simplicis unitatis absorbeat consensum. Venit pax quam sustinui: requiescat in pace qui pacem quaesivit, et quae pacis sunt sectari semper studuit. Sic quippe Isaias ait: Veniat pax, requiescat in cubili suo, quia ambulavit in directione sua (Isai. LVII, 2). In pace utique Simeon straverat cubile suum, qui omnibus aliis curis ejectis solum in corde meditabatur Jesum, ubi et reclinavit susceptum. In directione quoque sua ambulaverat, qui justus et timoratus erat, exspectans consolationem Israel, adventanti quotidie desideriis occurrebat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. O holy Zion, soul that gazes on things eternal, consider what majesty it is you must receive; think with what care and diligence you must prepare a place for him. If someone should say: \"And who is fit for this? What adornment can be worthy of the magnificence and glory of so great a King?\" \u2014 in vain, says David, would anyone plead poverty as an excuse: \"His place is made in peace\" (Psal. LXXV, 3); and: \"Justice and judgment are the preparation of his throne\" (Psal. LXXXVIII, 15). Simeon indeed, because he adorned his chamber with coverings of this kind, was therefore worthy to receive Christ with great grace. Do you wish to see that he had found a place for the Lord in peace, that he had prepared a seat for him in justice and judgment? \"There was,\" it says, \"a man in Jerusalem whose name was Simeon; and this man was just and God-fearing\" (Luc. II, 25). Simeon's house was in peace, for he dwelt in Jerusalem, that is, in the vision and love of peace; concerning which Jerusalem he had heard David promising: \"He who dwells in Jerusalem shall never be moved\" (Psal. CXXIV, 1, 2). And, that you may know he was a dweller in and lover of peace, and sought above all things the unshakeable peace of that stable and eternal Jerusalem: when he received into his bosom Jesus, the peace of God and of men, who came to give peace upon peace, this first word burst forth from the breast of the old man who had long been meditating on peace: \"Now, Lord, you dismiss your servant, according to your word, in peace\" (Luc. II, 29). After my eyes have seen your salvation, who is our peace, making both one \u2014 not only Jew and Gentile, but also God and man, and in man spirit and flesh \u2014 now this alone remains for the desires of your servant, that \"in peace I may sleep and rest in the very same\" (Psal. IV, 9); and that the peace of God which surpasses all understanding may absorb me wholly into agreement with that highest and simple unity. The peace I have awaited has come: let him rest in peace who sought peace, and who always strove to pursue what belongs to peace. For thus indeed Isaiah says: \"Let peace come, let him rest in his bed, because he walked in his straight way\" (Isai. LVII, 2). Simeon indeed had made his bed in peace, since, casting out all other cares, he meditated on Jesus alone in his heart, and there also he laid him down once received. He had likewise walked in his straight way, he who was just and God-fearing, awaiting the consolation of Israel, meeting its coming each day with his desires.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. In eo igitur quod justus dicitur et timoratus, justitiam et judicium intellige, quo sedes Dei praeparatur. Et justitia quidem suo nomine demonstratur: judicium vero timoris nomine, vel timor nomine judicii ideo recte accipi posse putatur, quia scilicet timor non solum nascitur de futuri consideratione judicii, sed etiam operatur nunc in nobis quamdam illius formam et exsecutionem judicii. Qui enim prohibemur judicare ante tempus de proximis (I Cor. IV, 5), jubemur de nobis ut, dum nosmetipsos dijudicamus, a Domino non judicemur (I Cor. XI, 31, 32). Porro timor iste sanctior est, cum justitiam sequitur, quam cum praecedit: quia qui praecedens ad justitiam initiat, sequens eadem consummat et conservat; dum scilicet de justitia praesumere non sinit, sed omnia opera sua hominem vereri facit, ut post judicium sui adhuc de subtiliore judicio suspectus sit, dicatque sibi: Qui judicat me Dominus est (I Cor. IV, 4), qui cum acceperit tempus, etiam justitias judicabit. Qui sine timore isto est, non poterit justificari (Eccli. I, 28), ut Scriptura testatur; quia quantumcunque se justis exercitet operibus, alieni comedunt robur ejus, dum suam volens justitiam constituere, justitiae Dei non est subjectus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. In the fact, then, that he is called just and God-fearing, understand justice and judgment, by which the seat of God is prepared. Justice indeed is shown by its own name; but judgment, it is thought, may rightly be understood under the name of fear, or fear under the name of judgment, because fear not only arises from the consideration of the judgment to come, but also now works in us a certain form and execution of that judgment. For we who are forbidden to judge our neighbors before the time (I Cor. IV, 5) are commanded, as regards ourselves, that while we judge ourselves we may not be judged by the Lord (I Cor. XI, 31, 32). Moreover, this fear is holier when it follows justice than when it precedes it: for the fear that precedes initiates one into justice, while the fear that follows completes and preserves it \u2014 namely, by not allowing a man to presume upon his justice, but making him regard all his own works with fear, so that even after judging himself he remains apprehensive of a more searching judgment, and says to himself: \"He who judges me is the Lord\" (I Cor. IV, 4), who, when he has taken the appointed time, will judge even acts of justice. He who is without this fear cannot be justified (Eccli. I, 28), as Scripture testifies; for however much he exercises himself in just works, strangers devour his strength, since, wishing to establish his own justice, he is not subject to the justice of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Vigilanter itaque Evangelium perfectam commendans Simeonis justitiam, non ait: Timoratus et justus, ne timorem, qui est initium justitiae, intelligeremus: sed ait, justus et timoratus, ut timorem, qui justitiae per omnes gradus comes individuus ipsam promovet et tuetur, ipsius exemplo disceremus. Nam neque illud putaverim otiosum, quod non timentem dixit, sed timoratum: non novum, non horarium, aut perfunctorium volens intelligi timorem illum; sed qui utique versus esset in habitum, qui altius hominis imbibisset affectum, possideret sensum, modestia et gravitate sermonem ornaret et vultum, circumspectione moderaretur actum, totum denique interioris et exterioris hominis componeret statum. Neque enim vanis animum consolationibus resolvebat, qui consolationem Israel exspectabat. Quod et ipsum ad suae cumulum adjecerat justitiae, ut sollicitus non pro sua tantum, sed etiam pro populi consolatione, longa suspensus maneret exspectatione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Watchfully, then, the Gospel, commending Simeon's perfect justice, does not say \"fearing and just,\" lest we should understand thereby the fear that is the beginning of justice; but says \"just and fearing,\" so that by his example we might learn of that fear which, as an inseparable companion of justice through all its degrees, advances and guards it. Nor would I think it without purpose that it said not \"fearing\" but \"God-fearing\": not wishing that fear to be understood as something new, momentary, or superficial; but one that had truly become a settled habit, that had penetrated more deeply into the man's affections, possessed his mind, adorned his speech and countenance with modesty and gravity, moderated his action with circumspection, and finally composed the whole state of the inner and outer man. For he who awaited the consolation of Israel did not relax his mind with vain consolations. And this very thing he had added to the sum of his justice: that, anxious not only for his own consolation but also for that of the people, he remained in suspense through a long expectation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Denique, ut manifestius audias quam pretioso ac vero nitore virtutum, non fuco colorum adornaverat thalamum suum illa Sion, id est Simeon, Spiritus sanctus, inquit, erat in eo. Quid huic ornatui Sion illa terrena comparare poterat, in omni illa multifaria thalami, id est templi sui, ambitione et gloria? Si faciem templi coronis ornasset aureis, quid aurum nisi lutum etiam sine comparatione virtutis? Puto quia nec Salomon in omni gloria sua ornatus est sicut Simeon: cum ille sapientiam, qua florebat, in senectute perdiderit, iste susceperit. Te tamen, o infidelis Sion, thalamus tuus arguit, quod cum satis tibi Praedictum esset: Ecce veniet ad templum suum Dominator quem vos quaeritis (Malach. III, 1); adorna thalamum tuum, Sion, induere vestimentis gloriae tuae, civitas Sancti (Isai. LII, 1): tu tamen nihil prorsus ad quotidianum cultum templi in adventu ipsius addidisti, non vela, non coronas, non lucernas plures, aut victimas, non alios psalmos, aut cantica; omnino inhonoratum, insalutatum remittis, qui te salvare venerat. Ideo utique tuus iste thalamus, domus gloriae tuae deserta tibi relinquetur; imo non relinquetur lapis super lapidem, qui non destruatur. Tibi enim, Domine, templum augustius, amplius et perfectius, scilicet Ecclesiam magnam a solis ortu usque ad occasum fides aedificabit gentium, et secundum nomen tuum, Deus, ita et laus tua in fines terrae; unde etiam hodie laudes audivimus, gloriam justi, voces scilicet laudantium et dicentium: Suscepimus, Deus, misericordiam tuam in medio templi tui (Psal. XLVII, 11, 10), id est, in communione sancti populi tui. O fratres, suscepistis misericordiam Dei et vos, imo abundantius vos: videte ne in vacuum susceperitis, ne gratiam ingratitudine vacuetis. Gaudeat ergo vestra devotio: quem Simeon hodie portavit in sinu suo, vos omni die glorificate et portate in corpore vestro Jesum Christum Dominum nostrum, cui honor et gloria in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-03-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Finally, that you may hear more clearly with how precious and true a splendor of virtues\u2014not with the paint of colors\u2014that Sion, that is, Simeon, had adorned his bridal chamber, the Gospel says: The Holy Spirit was in him. What could that earthly Sion compare to this adornment, in all the manifold pomp and glory of her bridal chamber, that is, of her temple? If she had crowned the face of her temple with golden crowns, what is gold but mud, even without comparison to virtue? I think that not even Solomon in all his glory was adorned as Simeon was: since Solomon lost in old age the wisdom in which he had flourished, while Simeon received it. Yet your own bridal chamber convicts you, O unfaithful Sion, since it had been foretold to you plainly enough: \"Behold, the Lord whom you seek shall come to his temple\" (Malach. III, 1); \"Adorn your bridal chamber, O Sion; put on the garments of your glory, O city of the Holy One\" (Isai. LII, 1): yet you added absolutely nothing to the daily service of the temple at his coming\u2014no veils, no crowns, no additional lamps or victims, no other psalms or songs; you sent away wholly unhonored and unsaluted the one who had come to save you. Therefore that very bridal chamber of yours, the house of your glory, will be left deserted to you; indeed, not one stone upon another will be left that will not be thrown down. For you, O Lord, a more august, more ample, and more perfect temple\u2014namely, the great Church\u2014the faith of the nations will build, from the rising of the sun to its setting, and according to your name, O God, so also your praise unto the ends of the earth; whence also today we have heard praises, the glory of the just one, namely, the voices of those praising and saying: \"We have received, O God, your mercy in the midst of your temple\" (Psal. XLVII, 11, 10), that is, in the communion of your holy people. O brothers, you too have received the mercy of God, indeed more abundantly; see that you have not received it in vain, lest you empty grace through ingratitude. Let your devotion therefore rejoice: him whom Simeon today carried in his bosom, do you every day glorify and carry in your body\u2014Jesus Christ our Lord, to whom be honor and glory for ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon III: How Simeon Is to Be Imitated", "topics": ["Purification", "Simeon", "justice and fear of God", "peace", "wisdom of the aged"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-04", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo IV, De quatuor purificationis nostrae generibus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 75-79.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Luke 2:21", "Matthew 3:15", "Malachi 3:4\u20135", "Psalm 17:19 (historical numbering)", "Isaiah 6:5\u20137", "Luke 12:49", "Luke 1:35", "Psalm 6:2 (historical numbering)", "1 John 5:6, 8", "Psalm 65:12 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 15:27", "Daniel 4:24", "Luke 11:41", "Acts 15:9", "Mark 10:52", "Proverbs 16:6", "Proverbs 20:6"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Mary's purification introduces an account of contrition, bodily penance, works of mercy and faith, and patience in tribulation. Guerric closes by asking how faithfulness and mercy govern the use of goods shared with the poor. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO IV."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON IV.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De quatuor purificationis nostrae generibus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the Four Kinds of Our Purification", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Postquam impleti sunt dies purgationis Mariae (Luc. II, 21). Scriptura redemptionis nostrae narrans mysteria, sic refert quae pro nobis historialiter sunt gesta, ut significet quae moraliter a nobis sunt gerenda. Cum enim hodie beatae Purificatio Mariae recensetur, manifeste de nostra ipsorum purificatione admonemur. Quem vero tanti non moveat auctoritas exempli, cum videamus scilicet, quia illa sanctarum sanctissima, quae nihil purgandum habuit, purgationis tamen legalis implere mandatum non renuit? O mater immaculata, mater intacta, nonne tuae tibi conscia es puritatis; quia scilicet integritatem tuam nec conceptus, nec partus violavit, sed sacravit? Quare ergo, tanquam muliebre aliquid in concipiendo vel pariendo sis passa, sic mundationis quae muliebri provisa est infirmitati, quaeris remedia? Sic, inquit, decet nos implere omnem justitiam (Matth. III, 15), ut quae summae mater electa sum justitiae, speculum quoque sim totius et exemplar justitiae. Novi ego superbiam filiorum Evae, quae promptior est ad excusandum, quam ad expurgandum commissa. Necessarium arbitror, ut vitiis originis antiquae statim in initiis omnia novae generationis occurrant exempla. Mater praevaricationis peccavit et excusavit procaciter: mater redemptionis non peccet, et satisfaciat humiliter; ut filii hominum qui de matre vetustatis traducunt necessitatem peccandi, de matre saltem novitatis trahant humilitatem purgandi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"After the days of Mary's purification were fulfilled\" (Luc. II, 21). Scripture, in narrating the mysteries of our redemption, relates the things that were historically done for us in such a way as to signify what must morally be done by us. For when today the Purification of the Blessed Mary is commemorated, we are plainly admonished concerning our own purification. For who would not be moved by the authority of so great an example, seeing that she, the holiest of holy women, who had nothing needing to be purged, nevertheless did not refuse to fulfill the mandate of the legal purification? O immaculate Mother, O inviolate Mother, are you not conscious of your own purity\u2014since neither conception nor childbirth violated your integrity, but rather consecrated it? Why then do you seek the remedies of that cleansing which was provided for feminine infirmity, as though you had suffered something womanly in conceiving or giving birth? \"Thus,\" she says, \"it becomes us to fulfill all justice\" (Matth. III, 15), so that I, who have been chosen as the mother of the highest Justice, may also be the mirror and exemplar of all justice. I know the pride of the children of Eve, which is readier to make excuses than to purge away what has been committed. I judge it necessary that, right from the beginning, all the examples of the new generation should at once confront the vices of the ancient origin. The mother of transgression sinned and insolently made excuses: let the mother of redemption not sin, and let her humbly make satisfaction; so that the sons of men, who inherit from the mother of the old order the necessity of sinning, may at least draw from the mother of the new order the humility of being purged.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. O filii hominum, tempus purgationis advenit, quando Mater summae puritatis, de cujus purgatione dies festus est nobis, pariter et fontem edidit, et exemplum dedit nobis quod debeamus purgari. Satius est, o fratres, et suavius fonte purgari quam igne. Prorsus qui fonte non fuerint modo purgati, igne habent purgari, si tamen purgari meruerint: quando scilicet Judex ipse, quasi ignis conflans, sedebit conflans et emundans argentum, et purgabit filios Levi (Malach. III, 4, 5). Nunc Christus aqua diluens; tunc ignis consumens. Modo fons patens in ablutionem peccatoris et menstruatae; tunc flamma saeviens et ignis vorans usque ad medullas animae. De ardore siquidem irati fontis ille purgatorius ignis exardescet in conspectu ejus, et inflammabit in circuitu inimicos ejus. Ascendit, inquit, fumus in ira ejus, et ignis a facie ejus exarsit (Psal. XVII, 19). Inde cadent super eos carbones poenaliter purgatorii, quibus modo non adhibentur carbones salubriter desolatorii: qualem illum fuisse reor carbonem sublatum de altari, qui prophetae labia purgavit (Isai. VI, 5-7). An vero et illi carbones hujusmodi queant intelligi, de quibus cuidam dicitur: Ecce dati sunt tibi carbones, et sedebis super eos; hi erunt tibi in adjutorium: vobis relinquo dijudicandum. Illud autem non dubius assero, quia ignis ille, quem Dominus Jesus misit in terram (Luc. XII, 49), si vehementer (sicut est voluntas mittentis) in nobis arserit, ignis ille purgatorius qui in judicio purgabit filios Levi, non ligna, non fenum, non stipulam consumenda reperiet in nobis. Uterque quidem ignis purgatorius, sed satis differenti modo. Iste siquidem purificat ungendo, ille urendo. Hic refrigerium roris, ibi spiritus judicii et spiritus ardoris, quo abluet Dominus sordes filiarum Sion, et sanguinem Jerusalem lavabit de medio ejus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. O sons of men, the time of purification has come, when the Mother of the highest purity\u2014whose purification is for us a feast day\u2014has both brought forth the fountain and given us the example that we ought to be purified. It is better, brothers, and sweeter, to be purified by the fountain than by fire. Indeed, those who are not purified now by the fountain will have to be purified by fire, if indeed they are found worthy to be purified at all: namely when the Judge himself, like a refining fire, shall sit refining and cleansing silver, and shall purge the sons of Levi (Malach. III, 4, 5). Now Christ washes with water; then he will consume with fire. Now the fountain stands open for the washing of the sinner and the menstruous woman; then the raging flame and the devouring fire will reach to the very marrow of the soul. For indeed, from the heat of his wrathful fountain that purgatorial fire will blaze forth in his sight, and will inflame his enemies round about. \"Smoke went up,\" he says, \"in his wrath, and fire was kindled from his face\" (Psal. XVII, 19). From it the coals of punitive purgation shall fall upon those to whom the healthfully desolating coals are not now applied\u2014such a coal, I think, as that one taken from the altar, which purged the lips of the prophet (Isai. VI, 5-7). Or indeed, might those coals also be understood to be of this kind, of which it is said to a certain one: \"Behold, coals have been given to you, and you shall sit upon them; these shall be for your help\"\u2014I leave it to you to judge. But this I assert without doubt: that the fire which the Lord Jesus sent upon the earth (Luc. XII, 49), if it burns fiercely in us (as is the will of the one who sends it), that purgatorial fire which in judgment will purge the sons of Levi will find in us no wood, no hay, no stubble to be consumed. Both fires are indeed purgatorial, but in a quite different manner. For the one purifies by anointing, the other by burning. In the one there is the refreshment of dew; in the other, the spirit of judgment and the spirit of burning, by which the Lord will wash away the filth of the daughters of Sion, and shall wash the blood of Jerusalem from the midst of her.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Est quidem et in hoc ipso multa Domini clementia erga filias Sion: sed longe clementius et mitius purificata est illa, quam beatam praedicant filiae Sion, cui scilicet dictum est: Spiritus sanctus superveniet in te, et virtus Altissimi obumbrabit tibi. Haec nempe supernae virtutis obumbratio, vera fuit purificatio Mariae; non ista, quae quadam mystica dispensatione tantum specie tenus hodie celebrata est. Haec plane fuit tota ac vera sanctificatio Matris et Filii, sicut et angelus exposuit ei. Ideo, inquit, et quod nascetur ex te Sanctum, vocabitur Filius Dei (Luc. I, 35). Antea namque purificanda erat natura mortalis, ut Deum conciperet, non posteaquam concepisset: cum concepisse Sanctum sanctorum summa sanctificatio sit, et ea quae Mater ipsius sanctitatis effecta sit, nihil sanctius esse possit. Cum ergo filiae Sion quae mollius et dissolutius vivunt, et ardorem forsitan experiuntur carnis, merito purificandae sint spiritu judicii et spiritu ardoris; Virgo perpetua ac singularis, quae sola Spiritus obumbratione Deum concepit, solo purificata est spiritu gratiae et refrigerio roris. Omnium sane purgationum, quibus conditio reparatur mortalis, haec beatae purificatio Virginis mitissima atque felicissima fuit: nam illa filiarum Sion quae purgatorio fiet igne judicii, sicut novissima, sic et severissima omnium erit. Nec ista itaque potest esse nobis nisi admirationi, nec illa nisi formidini: quippe qui nec ad istam possimus aspirare, et in illam, si sapimus, semper formidamus incidere. Omnino si sapimus, semper clamabimus, plus emendatione morum, quam strepitu verborum: Domine, ne in furore tuo arguas me, neque in ira tua corripias me (Psal. VI, 2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. There is indeed in this very thing much clemency of the Lord toward the daughters of Sion: but far more clement and gentle was the purification of her whom the daughters of Sion proclaim blessed, to whom it was said: \"The Holy Spirit shall come upon you, and the power of the Most High shall overshadow you.\" This overshadowing of supernal power was truly the purification of Mary\u2014not that one which, by a certain mystical dispensation, was today celebrated only in outward appearance. That overshadowing was plainly the whole and true sanctification of both Mother and Son, as the angel also explained to her. \"Therefore,\" he says, \"that which shall be born of you, holy, shall be called the Son of God\" (Luc. I, 35). For mortal nature had to be purified beforehand, so that it might conceive God\u2014not afterward, once it had conceived; since to have conceived the Holy of Holies is the highest sanctification, and nothing can be holier than she who was made the Mother of that very holiness. Therefore, since the daughters of Sion, who live more softly and dissolutely, and perhaps experience the burning of the flesh, are rightly to be purified by the spirit of judgment and the spirit of burning; the perpetual and singular Virgin, who alone conceived God by the overshadowing of the Spirit, was purified by the spirit of grace alone and by the refreshment of dew. Of all the purifications by which mortal condition is restored, this purification of the Blessed Virgin was the gentlest and most blessed; for that purification of the daughters of Sion, which will be accomplished by the purgatorial fire of judgment, will be, as the last of all, so also the severest of all. Neither, therefore, can the one be anything to us but a matter for admiration, nor the other anything but a matter for dread: since we can neither aspire to that former one, and, if we are wise, we must always fear falling into that latter one. Altogether, if we are wise, we shall always cry out\u2014more by the amendment of our conduct than by the noise of words\u2014\"Lord, rebuke me not in your fury, nor chastise me in your wrath\" (Psal. VI, 2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Necessarie igitur nobis et misericorditer inter utrumque genus purgationis, illud summum ad quod non attingimus, et illud ultimum quod metuimus, alia purgationum genera pietas providit divina, sicut tempus et locum poenitentiae, sic plurima salutis indulgens remedia. Quae licet multa ac varia sint, sub quatuor tamen generibus videntur omnia fere posse comprehendi; ne scilicet indigesta multitudine rerum memoriam confundamus auditoris. Haec igitur quatuor esse arbitror quibus opportune et commode his diebus purgationis nostrae purgari possumus: scilicet contritionem cordis, afflictionem corporis, opera pietatis et fidei, patientiam tribulationis. Ad hunc siquidem modum quatuor invenies, quibus mundum generaliter purificat Christus, purgationem peccatorum faciens: aquam scilicet et sanguinem, spiritum et ignem. Hic est, inquit Joannes, qui venit per aquam et sanguinem. Hic est qui baptizat in Spiritu sancto et igni (I Joan. V, 6, 8). Aqua baptizavit, pedesque discipulorum lavit, dedicans baptismum quoque lacrymarum, quae de contritione profluunt cordis. Sanguinem fudit; ut et nos mortificatione corporis participantes ejus passioni, lavemus stolas nostras in sanguine Agni. Spiritum dedit; ut charitas Dei ac proximi diffusa in cordibus nostris per Spiritum, multitudinem in nobis operiat peccatorum. Quae, si minus perfecta est, ut tot et tanta non sufficiat operire, admovet ignem conflator ille, qui purgat filios Levi; et quidquid residuum est rubiginis, ignis excoquit, seu praesentis, seu futurae tribulationis, ut tandem decantare possint: Transivimus per ignem et aquam, et eduxisti nos in refrigerium (Psal. LXV, 12). Sic nempe et mundus iste prius baptizatus aqua diluvii, postea purgatus igne judicii, in novum transibit statum incorruptionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Necessarily, then, and mercifully, between these two kinds of purgation\u2014the highest, which we do not attain, and the last, which we fear\u2014divine kindness has provided for us other kinds of purgation, granting, as it grants a time and place for penance, many remedies of salvation as well. Though these are many and varied, they can nevertheless almost all be comprehended under four kinds, lest we confuse the hearer's memory with an undigested multitude of things. These, then, I judge to be the four by which we can be fittingly and conveniently purified in these days of our purification: namely, contrition of heart, affliction of the body, works of piety and faith, and patience in tribulation. In this manner you will find four things by which Christ generally purifies the world, effecting a purgation of sins: namely, water and blood, spirit and fire. 'This is he,' says John, 'who came by water and blood. This is he who baptizes in the Holy Spirit and in fire' (I Joan. V, 6, 8). He baptized with water, and washed the feet of the disciples, consecrating also the baptism of tears, which flow from contrition of heart. He shed blood, that we too, sharing in his passion through mortification of the body, might wash our robes in the blood of the Lamb. He gave the Spirit, that the love of God and of neighbor, poured into our hearts through the Spirit, might cover a multitude of sins in us. If this love is too imperfect to suffice to cover so many and so great sins, that refiner applies the fire, who purges the sons of Levi; and whatever rust remains, the fire\u2014whether of present or future tribulation\u2014smelts away, so that at last they may be able to sing: 'We have passed through fire and water, and you have brought us out into a place of refreshment' (Psal. LXV, 12). Thus, indeed, this world too, first baptized with the water of the flood, and afterward purged by the fire of judgment, will pass into a new state of incorruption.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Denique, ut teneris atque delicatis geramus aliquatenus morem, si lacrymas non habent, si labores horrent, si nesciunt ferre tribulationem: nunquid et illud excusabunt, quod Salomon commendat: Per misericordiam, inquiens, et fidem purgantur peccata? (Prov. XV, 27; XVI, 6.) Quid misericordia suavius? quid fide jucundius? Haec oleum est membris, illa lumen oculis. Haec ungit affectus, illa sensus illuminat, dirigitque gressus: ut ad lumen ejus ambules in tenebris, ut invisibilia contempleris, et futuram tuam beatitudinem jam animo praesumptam mediteris. Bene autem dicitur, per misericordiam et fidem purgari peccata: quia misericordia de proprio impendens, redimit debita; fides autem etiam gratis sine operibus indulgentiam impetrat. De misericordia nempe dicit propheta: Iniquitates tuas redime misericordiis pauperum (Dan. IV, 24); et Dominus prophetarum: Date eleemosynam, et omnia munda sunt vobis (Luc. XI, 41). De fide autem ait Apostolus: Fide mundans corda eorum (Act. XV, 9); et Dominus apostolorum: Vade, inquit, fides tua te salvum fecit (Marc. X, 52). Quanquam in hac Salomonis sententia, possit intelligi non fides qua credimus, sed qua credibiles nos exhibemus, dum fidem Deo et hominibus servamus; ut quod hic dicitur: Per misericordiam et fidem purgantur peccata (Prov. XV, 27), et paulo post: Misericordia et veritate redimitur iniquitas (Prov. XVI, 6), eadem sit in diversitate verborum repetita sententia. Recte igitur misericordia et veritas seu fides sociata sunt: cum in omnibus viis nostris nisi misericordia et veritas obvient sibi, plus augeri quam purgari peccata sit verendum. Ut enim de aliis actionibus interim taceam, et de misericordiis pauperum loquar, quas abundantius nunc fieri temporis hujus postulat necessitas: quid in ipsis etiam, quibus peccata purganda sunt, peccati committitur, si misericordia fide, vel fides misericordia destituatur?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Finally, that we may to some extent make allowance for the tender and delicate\u2014if they have no tears, if they shrink from labors, if they do not know how to bear tribulation\u2014will they also excuse themselves from what Solomon commends, saying: 'By mercy and faith sins are purged'? (Prov. XV, 27; XVI, 6.). What is sweeter than mercy? What more delightful than faith? The one is oil for the limbs, the other light for the eyes. The one anoints the affections, the other illumines the senses and directs the steps, so that by its light you may walk in darkness, that you may contemplate invisible things, and meditate upon your future blessedness already anticipated in the soul. And it is well said that sins are purged by mercy and faith: because mercy, spending from one's own goods, redeems debts, while faith, even freely, without works, obtains pardon. Of mercy indeed the prophet says: 'Redeem your iniquities by mercies shown to the poor' (Dan. IV, 24); and the Lord of the prophets: 'Give alms, and all things are clean to you' (Luc. XI, 41). Of faith the Apostle says: 'cleansing their hearts by faith' (Act. XV, 9); and the Lord of the apostles says: 'Go, your faith has made you whole' (Marc. X, 52). Although in this saying of Solomon one might understand not the faith by which we believe, but that by which we show ourselves trustworthy, in that we keep faith with God and with men; so that what is said here, 'By mercy and faith sins are purged' (Prov. XV, 27), and a little afterward, 'By mercy and truth iniquity is redeemed' (Prov. XVI, 6), is the same statement repeated in different words. Rightly, then, are mercy and truth, or faith, joined together: since unless mercy and truth meet one another in all our ways, it is to be feared that sins are increased rather than purged. For, to say nothing for now of other actions, and to speak of mercies shown to the poor, which the necessity of this present time demands be done more abundantly: what sin is committed even in those very acts by which sins are to be purged, if mercy is deprived of faith, or faith of mercy?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Audiant ergo quibus dispensatio credita est; audiant quibus credita non est: isti scilicet, ne sic velint esse fideles, ut misericordiam deserant; illi, ne sic velint esse misericordes, ut fidem laedant. Sunt enim aliqui qui plus volunt esse fideles quam oportet; et sunt aliqui qui plus volunt esse misericordes quam eis liceat. Qui ergo contra fidem indebitam usurpant misericordiam, audiant Salomonem dicentem: Multi homines misericordes vocantur; fidelem autem virum quis inveniet? (Proverb. XX, 6.) Qui autem velut propter fidem negligunt debitam misericordiam, audiant plus quam Salomonem dicentem: Quod superest date eleemosynam (Luc. XI, 41); ut saltem abundantia vestra aliorum suppleat inopiam. Multum prorsus pepercit infirmae et pusillae nostrae fidei, qui bona communia non ex aequo fratribus partiri, sed ex eo quod superest propriae necessitati, jussit eleemosynam eis impertiri. Ut enim taceamus de jure naturae, quae quidquid parens omnium terra generat, omnibus commune terrigenis adjudicat: illis saltem quibus indifferens est germanitas adoptionis, et communis sors haereditatis quomodo communis non erit usus panis quem Pater administrat coelestis? Esto tamen, sit istud jus ordinatae charitatis, quod moderatio Evangelii suas cuique necessitates primum excepit: quis ille est qui recte metiatur necessitates proprias qui inter necessarium et superfluum aequo judicio discernat? Attendamus nobis, fratres, ne de mortibus pauperum fratrum nostrorum incipiamus judicari, si superflue retinemus aut insumimus nobis unde vita eorum valeat sustentari. Et quoniam hodierna purificatio purissimae ac pauperrimae Virginis de nostra nos purificatione loqui commonuit, in hoc maxime nostram constare puritatem noverimus, si quidquid superfluum est nobis amputemus: ut non solum sanctitate castitatis, sed etiam simplicitate paupertatis aliquatenus aemulemur pauperem matrem pauperis Christi, cui regnum et imperium, et nunc et per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-04-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Let them hear, then, to whom the stewardship has been entrusted; and let them hear to whom it has not been entrusted: those without the stewardship, lest they wish to be so faithful that they abandon mercy; those entrusted with it, lest they wish to be so merciful that they injure faith. For there are some who wish to be more faithful than they ought, and there are some who wish to be more merciful than is lawful for them. Those, therefore, who contrary to faith, arrogate to themselves an undue mercy, let them hear Solomon saying: \"Many men are called merciful; but who shall find a faithful man?\" (Proverb. XX, 6.) But those who, as it were for the sake of faith, neglect due mercy, let them hear one greater than Solomon saying: \"That which remains over, give as alms\" (Luc. XI, 41), that at least your abundance may supply the want of others. He has indeed greatly spared our weak and feeble faith, who did not command that common goods be divided equally among the brethren, but that alms be given to them out of what remains over and above one's own necessity. For to say nothing of the law of nature, which assigns whatever the earth, the parent of all, brings forth, as common to all her children\u2014for those at least to whom the brotherhood of adoption is undivided, and the lot of inheritance common, how shall the use of the bread which the heavenly Father dispenses not also be common? Yet granted that this is the right of ordered charity, that the moderation of the Gospel has first reserved to each his own necessities: who is he that rightly measures his own necessities, who with fair judgment distinguishes between the necessary and the superfluous? Let us take heed, brothers, lest we begin to be judged for the deaths of our poor brothers, if we superfluously keep back or spend upon ourselves that by which their life might be sustained. And since today's purification of the most pure and most poor Virgin has reminded us to speak of our own purification, let us know that our purity consists chiefly in this: that we cut away whatever is superfluous to us; so that not only in holiness of chastity, but also in simplicity of poverty, we may in some measure imitate the poor mother of the poor Christ, to whom belong kingdom and dominion, both now and for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon IV: On the Four Kinds of Our Purification", "topics": ["Purification", "purgation", "contrition and penance", "mercy and faith", "almsgiving"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-04.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-05", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo V, De mundanda et adornanda Christo anima, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 79-89.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Sirach 10:9", "Isaiah 40:6\u20137", "Sirach 14:18", "Galatians 6:14", "2 Corinthians 1:12", "1 Corinthians 1:31", "Jeremiah 9:24", "2 Corinthians 10:17", "Jeremiah 17:6", "Matthew 25:11\u201312", "Wisdom 2:24\u201325", "Job 5:2", "Matthew 5:22", "Psalm 6:8 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 4:5 (historical numbering)", "Ephesians 4:26", "Ecclesiastes 7:4", "Psalm 119:5\u20136 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 6:10", "John 16:20", "Colossians 3:5", "Psalm 118:36 (historical numbering)", "Exodus 16:3", "Numbers 11:5\u20136", "Isaiah 22:13", "2 Corinthians 15:32", "1 Corinthians 6:13", "Exodus 32:6", "1 Corinthians 10:7", "Matthew 12:45", "Hosea 7:4", "Psalm 20:10 (historical numbering)", "Luke 13:27", "Psalm 6:9 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 7:23", "Proverbs 28:14", "Psalm 2:11 (historical numbering)", "Luke 1:48", "Matthew 5:3", "Luke 6:20", "Sirach 30:24", "Isaiah 66:2", "Matthew 5:4", "Psalm 36:11 (historical numbering)", "Song of Songs 1:7", "Mark 9:22", "Matthew 5:6", "Isaiah 61:2", "Psalm 119:2 (historical numbering)", "Matthew 5:4, 7", "Exodus 3:5", "Exodus 33:20", "Matthew 5:8", "Philippians 1:21, 23\u201324", "Matthew 5:9", "Leviticus 12:2, 4, 6, 8", "Luke 2:29\u201330, 32", "Isaiah 9:2", "Romans 11:25\u201326", "Genesis 22:18", "Psalm 131:11 (historical numbering)", "1 Corinthians 11:29", "Deuteronomy 4:24", "Deuteronomy 23:10", "1 Corinthians 10:16"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Cleansing the bridal chamber of the soul begins with expelling vices and cultivating virtues. The sermon then turns to Mary and Simeon in the temple and to reverence for the Eucharist. The 1879 edition includes it under Guerric, but its footnote records Horstius's doubt about that attribution and its earlier printing among Bernard's works. The individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO V (2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON V (2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De mundanda et adornanda Christo anima."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Cleansing and Adorning the Soul for Christ", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. \u00ab Adorna thalamum tuum, Sion, et suscipe regem Christum, quem virgo concepit, virgo peperit, virgo post partum quem genuit adoravit (3). \u00bb Ad nos, fratres, ad nos loquitur Spiritus sanctus, qui sumus Sion, qui sumus speculantes, qui die ac nocte levamus oculos nostros ad Dominum Deum nostrum, donec misereatur nostri. Adornemus igitur thalamum nostrum, et suscipiamus regem Christum. Ecce, fratres, Christus in propria veniens, et a suis repulsus, non habet ubi caput reclinet. Prae foribus nostris inter materna brachia vagiens, per Spiritum sanctum quaerit hospitium, dicens: \u00ab Adorna thalamum tuum, Sion. \u00bb Emundemus igitur thalamum nostrum, prius expellendo quae oculos Christi intrando offendere possunt; et sic demum adornemus ea inferendo quibus delectatur et gaudet. Quae sunt, ait forsitan aliquis vestrum, quae oculos Christi offendere possunt? Cui dicimus: Nihil ita oculos ejus offendit, sicut superbia, quae angelum de coelo, et hominem expulit de paradiso: quae est initium omnis peccati: quam hortatur nos Spiritus sanctus vitare, dicens: Quid superbis, terra et cinis? (Eccli. X, 9); et alibi: Omnis caro fenum; exsiccatum est fenum, et flos ejus decidit (Isai. XL, 6, 7; Eccli. XIV, 18). Videamus nunc quid superbia fecit. Angelum de coelo, et hominem expulit de paradiso. Merito haec oculos Christi offendit quae pulcherrimum opus ejus in coelo et in paradiso foedavit; quae fecit de angelo diabolum, et de homine nuper creato justo perfidum. Quae sicut est peccatorum primum et maximum, sic est omnium regina et mater vitiorum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Adorn your bridal chamber, Sion, and welcome Christ the king, whom a virgin conceived, a virgin bore, and after bearing him a virgin adored him (3).\" It is to us, brothers, to us that the Holy Spirit speaks, we who are Sion, we who are watchmen, who day and night lift up our eyes to the Lord our God until he have mercy on us. Let us, then, adorn our bridal chamber and welcome Christ the king. Behold, brothers, Christ coming into his own, and rejected by his own, has nowhere to lay his head. Crying before our doors amid his mother's arms, he seeks lodging through the Holy Spirit, saying: \"Adorn your bridal chamber, Sion.\" Let us therefore cleanse our bridal chamber, first by expelling whatever might offend the eyes of Christ upon his entering; and only then let us adorn it by bringing in those things in which he delights and rejoices. What things, someone among you may perhaps ask, can offend the eyes of Christ? To him we say: Nothing offends his eyes so much as pride, which cast an angel out of heaven and man out of paradise; which is the beginning of all sin; which the Holy Spirit urges us to avoid, saying: Why are you proud, earth and ashes? (Eccli. X, 9); and elsewhere: All flesh is grass; the grass withers and its flower falls (Isai. XL, 6, 7; Eccli. XIV, 18). Let us now see what pride has done. It cast an angel out of heaven and man out of paradise. Rightly does this offend the eyes of Christ, which defiled his most beautiful work in heaven and in paradise; which made of an angel a devil, and of man, newly created righteous, a faithless man. As it is the first and greatest of sins, so it is the queen and mother of all vices.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Videndum est igitur et sollicite considerandum, ut eam mens nostra cum tota prole sua excludat, ut thalamus noster emundatus et ornatus Christum regem qui foris [al. fores] pulsat, suscipiat. Cum tota, inquam, prole sua, septem scilicet principalibus vitiis. Quae sunt haec? Inanis gloria, invidia, ira, tristitia, avaritia, ventris ingluvies, luxuria. Hae sunt filiae superbiae, quae matrem suam sequuntur: quae hospitium quodcunque intraverint, fetidum reddunt. Sed quae nobis cum istis communio? Quis consensus templo Dei cum idolis? Nulla nobis (Deo agimus gratias) cum eis societas, quas, exuentes veterem hominem cum actibus suis, gladio verae confessionis et poenitentiae enecavimus. Nihil igitur in nobis ultra possit superbia, quae de archangelo diabolum, et de primo homine fecit impium et Creatori suo rebellem. Nihil habeat in nobis vana gloria, qui a die conversionis nostrae, in cruce Domini nostri Jesu Christi coepimus gloriari cum Apostolo dicentes: Mihi autem absit gloriari, nisi in cruce Domini nostri Jesu Christi, per quem mihi mundus crucifixus est, et ego mundo! (Galat. VI, 14). Gloria itaque nostra sit crux Christi quam portamus, et testimonium conscientiae per crucem Christi purgatae, de qua Apostolus ait: Gloria nostra haec est, testimonium conscientiae nostrae (II Cor. I, 12). In qua conscientia gloriari, de donis Dei est Deo gratias agere. Ad quod nos hortatur Apostolus, dicens: Qui gloriatur, in Domino glorietur (I Cor. I, 31; Jer. IX, 24; II Cor. X, 17): id est, in Domino de puritate conscientiae suae glorietur. Non enim qui se ipsum commendat, etc. Caeterum qui in se gloriatur, et qui sibi placet, stulto homini placet. Qui in alio homine gloriatur, aut laudem in mente ab ore alterius exspectat, mercedem operum recipit, ac oleo peccatoris ungit caput suum, auditque prophetam sibi dicentem: Maledictus homo qui ponit spem suam in homine (Jerem. XVII, 6). Et in die judicii cum dixerint: Domine, Domine, aperi nobis, ait: Amen dico vobis, nescio vos (Matth. XXV, 11, 12). Haec vana gloria filia est superbiae; quae in quocunque fuerit, inobedientem facit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. We must therefore watch and carefully consider that our mind exclude pride together with its whole offspring, so that our chamber, cleansed and adorned, may welcome Christ the king who knocks outside [or: at the doors]. With its whole offspring, I say, namely the seven principal vices. What are these? Vainglory, envy, anger, sadness, avarice, gluttony of the belly, lust. These are the daughters of pride, who follow their mother: whatever lodging they enter, they make foul. But what fellowship have we with these? What agreement has the temple of God with idols? We have no partnership with them (thanks be to God), for, stripping off the old man with his deeds, we have slain them with the sword of true confession and penance. Let pride, then, have no further power in us \u2014 pride which made of an archangel a devil, and of the first man a wicked man rebellious against his Creator. Let vainglory have nothing in us, we who from the day of our conversion have begun to glory in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, saying with the Apostle: But God forbid that I should glory, save in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom the world is crucified to me, and I to the world! (Galat. VI, 14). Let our glory, then, be the cross of Christ which we bear, and the testimony of a conscience purged through the cross of Christ, of which the Apostle says: Our glory is this, the testimony of our conscience (II Cor. I, 12). To glory in such a conscience is to give thanks to God for God's gifts. To this the Apostle exhorts us, saying: He who glories, let him glory in the Lord (I Cor. I, 31; Jer. IX, 24; II Cor. X, 17): that is, let him glory in the Lord concerning the purity of his conscience. For it is not he who commends himself, etc. But he who glories in himself, and who pleases himself, pleases a foolish man. He who glories in another man, or expects praise in his mind from another's mouth, receives the reward of his works, and anoints his own head with the oil of the sinner, and hears the prophet saying to him: Cursed is the man who puts his hope in man (Jerem. XVII, 6). And on the day of judgment, when they shall say: Lord, Lord, open to us, he says: Amen I say to you, I know you not (Matth. XXV, 11, 12). This vainglory is a daughter of pride; whoever harbors it, it makes disobedient.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Rursum, post conversionem, nihil ultra in nobis habeat invidia, per quam mors intravit in orbem terrarum. Diabolus enim invidit homini ascensum unde corruerat, et propter hoc tentavit, et in anima occidit. Haec est illa, quae non magnos, sed parvos semper torquet (Sap. II, 24, 25), dicente sancto Job: Parvulum occidit invidia (Job V, 2). In eo enim quod aliquis alicui invidet, minorem se ostendit ei cui invidet. Sic diabolus invidit primo homini, sic Esau Jacob, et Cain Abel. Haec invidia parvulum occidit; quia nisi inferior esset, de bono alterius non doleret. Hac peste nullus moritur, nisi qui terrena haec appetit, quae tota non veniunt pluribus. Unde si qua desiderat, illi invidet qui penitus ea accepit, vel illi partem habendo quantitatem ei restringit. Hunc parvulum occidit invidia, quia dum non potest adipisci quod alii invidet, in odium, invidiae filiam, cadit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Again, after conversion, let envy have no further place in us, through which death entered into the world. For the devil envied man the ascent from which he himself had fallen, and for this reason tempted him, and slew him in the soul. This is that vice which torments not the great, but always the small (Sap. II, 24, 25), as holy Job says: Envy slays the little one (Job V, 2). For in that someone envies another, he shows himself lesser than the one he envies. Thus the devil envied the first man, thus Esau envied Jacob, and Cain envied Abel. This envy slays the little one; because unless he were inferior, he would not grieve over another's good. No one dies of this plague except one who craves these earthly things, which do not come whole to many. Hence if anyone desires such things, he envies the one who has received them entirely, or the one who, by having a part, restricts their quantity for him. Envy slays this little one, because, since he cannot obtain what he envies in another, he falls into hatred, the daughter of envy.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram ira non habeat in nobis locum, quia, ut ait Dominus in Evangelio, gradatim ducit ad gehennae puteum, dicens: Qui irascitur fratri suo, reus erit judicio. Qui dixerit, Racha, hoc est, vocem indignationis per iram emiserit, reus erit concilio, in quo tractatur qua poena sit mulctandus. Qui dixerit, Fatue, hoc est, tale quid fecerit aut verbo aut verbere, ut inter fratres despiciatur, reus erit gehennae ignis (Matth. v, 22), nisi resipuerit. Haec est ira, quae stultorum oculos exstinguit, sapientium oculos nonnunquam turbat, dicente Psalmista: Turbatus est prae ira oculus meus (Psal. vi, 8). Et de ira stulti Job ait: Virum stultum interficit iracundia (Job. v, 2). Ecce duas iras videmus; quarum altera nonnunquam turbat oculum, altera semper exstinguit. Altera venit ex impatientia; altera, ex zelo rectitudinis. Altera venit ex vitio; altera, ex virtute. De altera quae venit ex virtute Psalmista ait: Irascimini, et nolite peccare (Psal. iv, 5; Ephes. iv, 26). Quasi dicat: Crudeles estote in vitium, mites in fratrem, considerantes vosmetipsos, ne et vos tentemini. De altera, quae venit ex vitio, ait Apostolus: Sol non occidat super iracundiam vestram (Ephes. iv, 26): id est, Christus, qui est sol justitiae, mentem vestram irascentem non deserat, qui cum ira nunquam habitat. Ira, quae venit ex zelo rectitudinis, debemus irasci prius nostris erratibus; et post, erratibus proximorum: quae melior est risu, quia per tristitiam vultus corrigitur proximus (Eccle. vii, 4). Quae debet tamen subesse rationi, ne sit immoderata, sed in ultione peccati tempus et modum consideret. Haec sapientes interdum turbat: ira per vitium stultos occidit, dum in rixas et in contumeliam linguam relaxat."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Again, brothers, after our conversion let anger have no place in us, because, as the Lord says in the Gospel, it leads step by step down to the pit of hell, saying: Whoever is angry with his brother shall be liable to judgment. Whoever says, Raca\u2014that is, utters a cry of indignation through anger\u2014shall be liable to the council, in which it is determined with what penalty he is to be punished. Whoever says, Fool\u2014that is, does something, either by word or by blow, such that his brother is held in contempt among the brothers\u2014shall be liable to the fire of hell (Matth. v, 22), unless he comes to his senses. This is the anger that puts out the eyes of fools, and sometimes troubles the eyes of the wise, as the Psalmist says: My eye is troubled because of anger (Psal. vi, 8). And of the fool's anger Job says: Anger kills the foolish man (Job. v, 2). Behold, we see two angers; of which the one sometimes troubles the eye, the other always puts it out. The one comes from impatience; the other, from zeal for righteousness. The one comes from vice; the other, from virtue. Of the one that comes from virtue the Psalmist says: Be angry, and do not sin (Psal. iv, 5; Ephes. iv, 26). As if he were saying: Be fierce against vice, gentle toward your brother, considering yourselves, lest you too be tempted. Of the other, which comes from vice, the Apostle says: Let not the sun go down upon your anger (Ephes. iv, 26): that is, let Christ, who is the sun of righteousness, not abandon your angry mind, for he never dwells together with anger. As for the anger that comes from zeal for righteousness, we ought to be angry first at our own faults, and afterward at the faults of our neighbors; and this is better than laughter, because through the sadness of the countenance the neighbor is corrected (Eccle. vii, 4). Yet it must remain subject to reason, lest it be immoderate, but should consider the time and measure in punishing sin. This anger sometimes troubles the wise; but anger through vice kills fools, as it lets the tongue loose into quarrels and insult.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram muniendus est thalamus cordis nostri, ne tristitia rediens introeat: illam dico quae mortem operatur, quae de amissione rerum temporalium venit. Haec est illa quae pravas cogitationes generat, murmurosum monachum reddit, quae fastidium parit, et tenentem aratrum Dei cum uxore Loth retro respicere compellit. Sine dolore namque non potest amittere, quod cum inordinato amore possedit. Hanc itaque tristitiam quae mortem operatur excludamus: et intromittamus eam quae venit de memoria peccatorum, et de peregrinatione nostra, dicamusque cum Psalmista: Heu mihi, quia incolatus meus prolongatus est! habitavi cum habitantibus Cedar, multum incola fuit anima mea (Psal. cxix, 5, 6). Haec est tristitia, quae vitam operatur, de qua ait Apostolus: Quasi tristes, semper autem gaudentes (II Cor. vi, 10). Haec est illa de qua Dominus ad discipulos dicit: Tristitia vestra vertetur in gaudium (Joan. xvi, 20). Altera parit mortem, parit animi rancorem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Again, brothers, after our conversion the bedchamber of our heart must be fortified, lest sadness return and enter in: I mean that sadness which works death, which comes from the loss of temporal things. This is the sadness that breeds wicked thoughts, makes the monk a grumbler, gives rise to disgust, and compels the one holding the plow of God to look back, like the wife of Lot. For one cannot lose without sorrow what one possessed with disordered love. Let us therefore shut out this sadness which works death, and let us admit that sadness which comes from the memory of our sins and from our condition as pilgrims, and let us say with the Psalmist: Woe is me, for my sojourning is prolonged! I have dwelt with the inhabitants of Kedar, my soul has been long a sojourner (Psal. cxix, 5, 6). This is the sadness that works life, of which the Apostle says: As sorrowful, yet always rejoicing (II Cor. vi, 10). This is that of which the Lord says to his disciples: Your sorrow shall be turned into joy (Joan. xvi, 20). The other sadness brings forth death, brings forth rancor of soul.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem muniamus thalamum nostrum, ne avaritia, quae est radix omnium malorum, rediens introeat, nosque ab unius Dei cultura abstrahat, idolorum cultores faciat. De qua inter caetera sic ait Apostolus ad discipulos suos: Mortificate membra vestra quae sunt super terram: fornicationem, immunditiam, libidinem, concupiscentiam malam, et avaritiam, quae est idolorum servitus (Coloss. iii, 5). Ecce avaritiam idololatriae comparat, et merito: quia sicut uni Deo idololatra nititur auferre gloriam, ne solus habeat nomen Deitatis; ita avarus in Dei res se extendit, ut solus usurpet quae Deus omnipotens in usum plurimorum fecit. Utraque Deo inimica, quia negat Deo quae sunt ejus. Est et avaritia non solum amor congregandae pecuniae aut conservandae, sed ambitio praelationis, cum nititur homo homini praeesse. Haec, ut verum dicam, etiam pauperes (qui semel pro Christo reliquerunt sua) in tantum vexat, ut contra jus et fas, et propositum suum, non tantum velint, sed etiam studeant fratribus et conservis praeesse. Quod si abbas aut prior eorum voto non consenserit, irascuntur, obloquuntur, eosque aut invidos, aut sibi malevolos (qui se non provehunt) clamant. Haec est avaritia quae memoriam mandatorum Dei turbat, dicente Psalmista Domino: Inclina cor meum in testimonia tua, et non in avaritiam (Psal. cxviii, 36): quae proditionis est mater, quae Judam Dei discipulum primum ad mandatorum Dei oblivionem, de oblivione ad proditionem, de proditione traxit ad laqueum."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Again, brothers, after our conversion let us fortify our bedchamber, lest avarice, which is the root of all evils, return and enter in, and draw us away from the worship of the one God, making us worshippers of idols. Concerning this, among other things, the Apostle says thus to his disciples: Put to death your members which are upon the earth: fornication, uncleanness, lust, evil desire, and avarice, which is the service of idols (Coloss. iii, 5). See how he compares avarice to idolatry, and rightly so: for just as the idolater strives to take glory away from the one God, so that he alone may not bear the name of Deity, so the avaricious man extends himself into the things of God, so that he alone may seize for himself what almighty God made for the use of many. Both are enemies of God, because each denies to God what belongs to him. And avarice is not only the love of gathering or keeping money, but also the ambition for precedence, when a man strives to set himself over another man. This, to speak truly, torments even the poor (who have once left their own goods for Christ's sake) to such a degree that, against right and justice and against their own purpose, they not only desire but even strive to be set over their brothers and fellow-servants. And if the abbot or prior does not consent to their wish, they grow angry, they murmur against him, and cry out that those who do not advance them are either envious or ill-disposed toward them. This is the avarice that disturbs the memory of God's commandments, as the Psalmist says to the Lord: Incline my heart to your testimonies, and not to avarice (Psal. cxviii, 36): which is the mother of betrayal, which drew Judas, God's disciple, first to forgetfulness of God's commandments, then from forgetfulness into betrayal, and from betrayal to the noose.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p007", "label": "Latin original", "text": "7. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram muniamus thalamum cordis, ne gula rediens introeat, quae et Adam innocentiae veste, et Esau privavit primogeniti dignitate. Haec est illa pestis quae patres nostros in deserto ingratos beneficiis Dei fecit, quae compulit eos murmurare contra Deum, et dicere: Utinam mortui essemus in Aegypto, quando sedebamus super ollas carnium, et comedebamus allia, cepe, et pepones (Exod. xvi, 3; Num. xi, 5, 6; xiv, 3.) Haec est illa funesta pestis quae eos ad blasphemiam duxit, quasi non esset alia vita post istam, cum dicebant: Comedamus et bibamus; cras enim moriemur (Isa. xxii, 13; II Cor. xv, 32), id est, in futuro. Quasi dicerent: Hoc solum habemus quod comedimus; nihil in futuro post hanc vitam nos habituros speramus. Hanc pestem cum homine perituram pronuntiat Apostolus, dicens: Esca ventri, et venter escis. Deus autem et hunc et hanc destruet (I Cor. vi, 13). Haec pestis tripartita est: quia aut ante horam canonicam, aut aliud quam licet, aut plus quam necesse est facit comedere, et carnis curam in concupiscentiis nititur facere. De qua nascitur inepta laetitia: sicut de populo Israel dicitur: Sedit populus manducare et bibere, et surrexerunt ludere (Exod. XXXII, 6; I Cor. X, 7)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p007", "label": "English", "text": "7. Again, brothers, after our conversion let us fortify the chamber of our heart, lest gluttony, returning, enter in \u2014 the vice which deprived Adam of the garment of innocence, and Esau of the dignity of the firstborn. This is that plague which made our fathers in the desert ungrateful for the benefits of God, which drove them to murmur against God and say: \"Would that we had died in Egypt, when we sat by the pots of flesh and ate garlic, onions, and melons\" (Exod. xvi, 3; Num. xi, 5, 6; xiv, 3.). This is that deadly plague which led them to blasphemy, as though there were no other life after this one, when they said: \"Let us eat and drink, for tomorrow we shall die\" (Isa. xxii, 13; II Cor. xv, 32) \u2014 that is, in the life to come. As if they said: We have only what we eat; we hope to have nothing in the future after this life. The Apostle declares that this plague will perish along with man, saying: \"Food for the belly, and the belly for food; but God will destroy both the one and the other\" (I Cor. vi, 13). This plague is threefold: for it makes a man eat either before the canonical hour, or something other than is permitted, or more than is necessary, and it strives to make provision for the flesh in its lusts. From it is born foolish merriment, just as it is said of the people of Israel: \"The people sat down to eat and drink, and rose up to play\" (Exod. XXXII, 6; I Cor. X, 7).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p008", "label": "Latin original", "text": "8. Rursum, fratres, post conversionem nostram muniamus thalamum cordis nostri, ne luxuria rediens introitum inveniat: quae pestilentia rediens est pejor quam ante fuerat. Nunquam enim sola redit; sed sicut Dominus ait in Evangelio: Assumit septem spiritus nequiores se, et ingressi habitant ibi: et fiunt novissima hominis illius pejora prioribus (Matth. XII, 45). Haec est pestis illa quae corpus Christi dimembrat, quae de membris Christi facit membra diaboli. Nam qui adhaeret alicui personae causa libidinis explendae, unum corpus fit cum illa persona, et fit membrum diaboli: cujus et illa membrum est per conjunctionem malorum operum. Haec est illa pestis quae ignem infernalem in corpus humanum accendit, ut vegetum sit et robustum ad libidinem explendam: et quae igni illi inexstinguibili materiam semper apponit, et vires ministrat, ut de suo semper habeat unde sine fine ardeat. De his duobus ignibus luxuriae et gehennae habemus auctoritatem Scripturae. De igne luxuriae ait: Omnes adulterantes quasi clibanus succensus a coquente (Osee VII, 4). De igne gehennae ait: Pones eos ut clibanum ignis in tempore vultus tui, hoc est, judicii, ut intus ardeant (sicut clibanus accensus) in conscientia. Dominus in ira sua conturbabit eos (Psal. XX, 10), dicens: Discedite a me, operarii iniquitatis (Luc. XIII, 27; Psal. VI, 9; Matth. VII, 23). Et devorabit eos ignis extremus infernalis, quem accenderunt propter luxuriae foeda opera. Sit ergo, fratres, summa cura et summum studium his qui in talibus sunt, de talibus evadere et fugere."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p008", "label": "English", "text": "8. Again, brothers, after our conversion let us fortify the chamber of our heart, lest lust, returning, find an entrance \u2014 a plague which, returning, is worse than it was before. For it never returns alone; but, as the Lord says in the Gospel: \"It takes with it seven spirits more wicked than itself, and they enter in and dwell there; and the last state of that man becomes worse than the first\" (Matth. XII, 45). This is that plague which dismembers the body of Christ, which turns members of Christ into members of the devil. For whoever clings to another person for the sake of satisfying lust becomes one body with that person, and becomes a member of the devil \u2014 of whom that other person is likewise a member, through the joining together of their evil deeds. This is that plague which kindles the infernal fire within the human body, so that it may be lively and vigorous for the satisfying of lust; and which continually supplies fuel to that unquenchable fire, and furnishes it strength, so that it may always have from its own store that by which it burns without end. Concerning these two fires, that of lust and that of hell, we have the authority of Scripture. Of the fire of lust it says: \"They are all adulterers, like an oven heated by the baker\" (Osee VII, 4). Of the fire of hell it says: \"Thou shalt make them as an oven of fire in the time of thy anger\" (that is, of judgment), that they may burn within (as in a heated oven) in their conscience. \"The Lord in his wrath shall trouble them\" (Psal. XX, 10), saying: \"Depart from me, you workers of iniquity\" (Luc. XIII, 27; Psal. VI, 9; Matth. VII, 23). And the fire of hell at the last shall devour them, which they kindled on account of the foul works of lust. Let it be, therefore, brothers, the greatest care and the greatest zeal of those who are caught in such things, to escape and flee from such things.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p009", "label": "Latin original", "text": "9. Nobis vero qui per gratiam Dei evasimus, et veterem exuimus hominem, qui sumus Sion, sit summum studium thalamum nostrum munire et adornare, et Christum regem suscipere, sicut haec scriptura praecipit quam recitavimus: \u201cAdorna thalamum tuum, Sion.\u201d Quid est adornare thalamum tuum? Virtutes congregare, quibus et pavimentum et parietes quasi cortinis, et floribus tota domus adornetur. Primus ornatus thalami nostri, qui sumus Sion, sit timor, qui est initium sapientiae, id est, primus gradus per quem ascenditur ad sapientiam. De quo timore ait Scriptura: Beatus homo qui semper pavidus (Prov. XXVIII, 14). Et Psalmista ait: Servite Domino in timore, et exsultate ei cum tremore (Psal. II, 11): ut timor auferat securitatem noxiam, et exultatio mortiferam tristitiam. Hic timor generat humilitatem, quae est pretiosissimus ornatus thalami Sion, quae magis placuit Christo cum in thalamum virgineum ingrederetur, dicente beata Maria: Quia respexit humilitatem ancillae suae, ecce enim ex hoc beatam me dicent omnes generationes (Luc. I, 48). Haec, inquam, humilitas est ornatus Sion, id est, pauperum Christi, de quibus ipse dicit: Beati pauperes spiritu, quoniam ipsorum est regnum coelorum (Matth. V, 3; Luc. VI, 20). Ecce quomodo timor Domini primus gradus ascensionis, et primus ornatus thalami Sion, alios etiam ornatus generat, humilitatem scilicet, et voluntariam paupertatem. Adorna ergo thalamum tuum, Sion, timore Domini."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p009", "label": "English", "text": "9. But as for us, who by the grace of God have escaped and have put off the old man, who are Sion, let it be our greatest zeal to fortify and adorn our chamber, and to receive Christ the King, just as this Scripture which we have recited commands: \"Adorn thy chamber, O Sion.\" What does it mean to adorn your chamber? To gather together virtues, by which the whole house \u2014 both floor and walls \u2014 may be adorned as though with hangings and flowers. Let the first adornment of our chamber, we who are Sion, be fear, which is the beginning of wisdom, that is, the first step by which one ascends to wisdom. Of this fear Scripture says: \"Blessed is the man that is always fearful\" (Prov. XXVIII, 14). And the Psalmist says: \"Serve the Lord with fear, and rejoice unto him with trembling\" (Psal. II, 11): so that fear may remove harmful security, and rejoicing remove deadly sorrow. This fear begets humility, which is the most precious adornment of the chamber of Sion, which pleased Christ most when he entered the virginal chamber, as blessed Mary says: \"Because he has regarded the humility of his handmaid; for behold, from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed\" (Luc. I, 48). This humility, I say, is the adornment of Sion, that is, of the poor of Christ, of whom he himself says: \"Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven\" (Matth. V, 3; Luc. VI, 20). Behold how the fear of the Lord, the first step of ascent and the first adornment of the chamber of Sion, also begets other adornments, namely humility and voluntary poverty. Adorn, therefore, your chamber, O Sion, with the fear of the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p010", "label": "Latin original", "text": "10. Secundus ornatus thalami Sion, et secundus gradus ad sapientiam, est pietas, de qua dicit Scriptura: Miserere animae tuae placens Deo (Eccli. XXX, 24); et alibi: Super quem requiescet spiritus meus, nisi super humilem et quietum? (Isa. LXVI, 2.) Haec miseretur in se peccantibus, fraterna onera portat; magis eligit quaelibet temporalia bona perdere, quam cum fratre lite contendere. Quae profecto pietas aliud nihil est nisi Deum colere, et per cultum Dei Deo placere. Haec in nobis generat mansuetudinem, optimum ornatum thalami Sion, quae filios Dei nos facit, dicente Domino: Beati mites, quoniam filii Dei vocabuntur (Matth. V, 4; Psal. XXXVI, 11). Ecce quomodo pietas secundus ornatus thalami Sion et secundus gradus ascensionis, alium ornatum de se generat mansuetudinem, quae filios Dei efficit. Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, pietate."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p010", "label": "English", "text": "10. The second adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion, and the second step toward wisdom, is piety, of which Scripture says: \"Have mercy on your own soul, pleasing God\" (Eccli. XXX, 24); and elsewhere: \"Upon whom shall my spirit rest, but upon the humble and quiet?\" (Isa. LXVI, 2.). This piety has mercy on those who sin against it, bears the burdens of brothers; it chooses rather to lose any temporal goods whatsoever than to contend with a brother in a lawsuit. This piety, indeed, is nothing other than to worship God, and through the worship of God to please God. This generates in us meekness, the best adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion, which makes us sons of God, the Lord saying: \"Blessed are the meek, for they shall be called sons of God\" (Matth. V, 4; Psal. XXXVI, 11). Behold how piety, the second adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion and the second step of ascent, generates from itself another adornment, meekness, which makes us sons of God. Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with piety.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p011", "label": "Latin original", "text": "11. Tertius ornatus thalami Sion et tertius gradus ascensionis est scientia, quae homini sui cognitionem praebet, ut scilicet homo, neque minus neque plus quam debet de se praesumat. Quorum alterum, scilicet de se nimium praesumere, in mala facit praecipitem; alterum, scilicet se non cognoscere, facit ad quaelibet bona torpentem. Ad hanc scientiam Spiritus sanctus invitat Ecclesiam in Canticis: Si ignoras te, o pulchra inter mulieres, abi post vestigia gregum tuorum, scilicet haereticorum: et pasce haedos tuos (Cantic. I, 7), hoc est, carnales concupiscentias. Haec scientia praebet nobis fiduciam vincendi omnes tentationes per veram fidem, dicente Domino in Evangelio: Si potes credere, omnia possibilia sunt credenti (Marc. IX, 22), per Dei scilicet gratiam, et liberum arbitrium. Videmus jam quomodo scientia tertius ornatus thalami Sion, et tertius gradus ascensionis, alium ornatum in nobis generat, religiosum videlicet luctum, qui consolationem a Domino in futuro meretur. Haec scientia parat in nobis dolorem et luctum peregrinationis, ut videndo mala exsilii quae patimur, cum luctu desideremus patriam a qua peregrinamur, audiamusque a Domino nobis dici: Beati qui lugent, quoniam ipsi consolabuntur (Matth. V, 6; Isa. LXI, 2)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p011", "label": "English", "text": "11. The third adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion and the third step of ascent is knowledge, which grants a man knowledge of himself, so that a man presumes of himself neither less nor more than he ought. Of these two faults, the one\u2014presuming too much of oneself\u2014makes one headlong into evils; the other\u2014not knowing oneself\u2014makes one sluggish toward whatever is good. To this knowledge the Holy Spirit invites the Church in the Canticle: \"If you know not yourself, O fairest among women, go forth after the footsteps of your flocks\"\u2014that is, of the heretics\u2014\"and feed your kids\" (Cantic. I, 7), that is, carnal desires. This knowledge affords us confidence to overcome all temptations through true faith, the Lord saying in the Gospel: \"If you can believe, all things are possible to him that believes\" (Marc. IX, 22), namely through the grace of God and free will. We see now how knowledge, the third adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion and the third step of ascent, generates in us another adornment, namely religious mourning, which merits consolation from the Lord in the future. This knowledge prepares in us the sorrow and grief of pilgrimage, so that, seeing the evils of exile which we suffer, we may with grief desire the homeland from which we are pilgrims, and may hear it said to us by the Lord: \"Blessed are they that mourn, for they shall be comforted\" (Matth. V, 6; Isa. LXI, 2).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p012", "label": "Latin original", "text": "12. Quartus ornatus thalami Sion, est fortitudo, quae convenit laborantibus pro veris bonis acquirendis. Haec est quae munit thalamum Sion, ut non extollatur prosperis, non frangatur adversis: quae linguam increpantem et blandientem his verbis confundit, dicens: Domine, libera animam meam a labiis iniquis, et a lingua dolosa (Psal. CXIX, 2). Haec est illa quae semper de die in diem robustior, esuriem et sitim justitiae in thalamo Sion accendit, facitque eam audire a Domino: Beati qui esuriunt et sitiunt justitiam, quoniam ipsi saturabuntur. Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, fortitudine. Quintus ornatus thalami Sion est consilium, quo homo infirmitates suas requirit, suum posse et suum non posse investigat: et ex sua infirmitate alios considerat, aliis parcit et miseretur, sicut et vult Deum sibi misereri; aliis dimittit debita, sicut sibi vult dimitti a Deo: mereturque audire a Domino: Beati misericordes, quoniam ipsi misericordiam consequentur (Matth. V. 4, 7). Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, consilio. Sextus ornatus thalami Sion, est intellectus; qui mentem hominis super haec infirma rapit, omnesque corporeas substantias intellectuali intuitu transiens, non corporeis substantiis figitur, volensque ultra progredi, et propius accedere audit a Domino: Ne appropinques huc (Exod. III, 5): non enim videbit me homo, et vivet (Exod. XXXIII, 20). Hic intellectus convenit iis qui a Deo purgatum oculum habent, de quibus dicitur: Beati mundo corde, quoniam ipsi Deum videbunt (Matth. V, 8). Adorna igitur thalamum tuum, Sion, intellectu."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p012", "label": "English", "text": "12. The fourth adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is fortitude, which belongs to those laboring to acquire true goods. This is what fortifies the bridal chamber of Zion, so that it is not lifted up by prosperity, nor broken by adversity; which confounds the tongue that rebukes and the tongue that flatters with these words, saying: \"Lord, deliver my soul from wicked lips, and from a deceitful tongue\" (Psal. CXIX, 2). This is that virtue which, growing ever stronger from day to day, kindles in the bridal chamber of Zion a hunger and thirst for justice, and makes it hear from the Lord: \"Blessed are they that hunger and thirst after justice, for they shall be filled.\" Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with fortitude. The fifth adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is counsel, by which a man examines his own weaknesses, searches out what he can and cannot do; and from his own weakness considers others, spares and shows mercy to others, just as he wishes God to show him mercy; forgives others their debts, as he wishes to be forgiven by God; and merits to hear from the Lord: \"Blessed are the merciful, for they shall obtain mercy\" (Matth. V. 4, 7). Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with counsel. The sixth adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is understanding, which snatches the mind of man above these weak things, and, passing by intellectual gaze beyond all bodily substances, is not fixed upon bodily substances; and wishing to go further and draw nearer, hears from the Lord: \"Come not near hither\" (Exod. III, 5): \"for man shall not see me, and live\" (Exod. XXXIII, 20). This understanding belongs to those who have had their eye purged by God, of whom it is said: \"Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God\" (Matth. V, 8). Adorn, therefore, your bridal chamber, O Zion, with understanding.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p013", "label": "Latin original", "text": "13. Septimus ornatus thalami Sion, est sapientia, quae convenit iis qui intellectualibus oculis aeterna contemplantur; quorum conversatio in coelis est, qui cupiunt dissolvi, et esse cum Christo: in quorum persona dicebat Apostolus: Mihi vivere Christus est, et mori lucrum. Quasi diceret: Ut Christum magnificem, Christo vivere volo tantum, et Christus est praemium mortis. Et post pauca: Coarctor autem e duobus, desiderium habens dissolvi et esse cum Christo, multo melius: manere autem in carne, necessarium propter vos (Philipp. I, 21, 23, 24). Ecce quid facit vera sapientia, quae omnes rebelles motus corporis ita subjugat, ut nullam ultra pugnam in carne sentiat: pro infirmis fratribus (ut eis Christum nuntiet) exsilium libenter patitur; probra non audit, damna non sentit, auferenti tunicam dimittit pallium, percutienti unam maxillam praebet et alteram, auditque a Domino: Beati pacifici, quoniam filii Dei vocabuntur (Matth. V, 9). Hi ornatus thalami Sion: istis nos adornemus qui sumus Sion, et regem Christum suscipiamus, quem Virgo concepit, Virgo peperit, Virgo post partum quadragesima, hoc est hodierna die, in templo Dei praesentavit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p013", "label": "English", "text": "13. The seventh adornment of the bridal chamber of Zion is wisdom, which belongs to those who with intellectual eyes contemplate eternal things; whose conversation is in heaven, who desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ: in whose person the Apostle said: \"To me to live is Christ, and to die is gain.\" As if he said: \"That I may magnify Christ, I wish only to live for Christ, and Christ is the reward of death.\" And shortly after: \"But I am straitened between the two, having a desire to be dissolved and to be with Christ, a thing by far the better: yet to abide in the flesh is necessary for your sake\" (Philipp. I, 21, 23, 24). Behold what true wisdom does, which so subjugates all the rebellious movements of the body that it no longer feels any battle in the flesh: for the sake of weak brothers (that it may announce Christ to them) it willingly endures exile; it does not hear reproaches, it does not feel losses, to him that takes away its coat it gives up its cloak also, to him that strikes one cheek it offers the other also, and hears from the Lord: \"Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called the children of God\" (Matth. V, 9). These are the adornments of the bridal chamber of Zion: with these let us, who are Zion, adorn ourselves, and let us receive Christ the king, whom the Virgin conceived, the Virgin bore, and the Virgin, on the fortieth day after the birth\u2014that is, on this very day\u2014presented in the temple of God.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p014", "label": "Latin original", "text": "14. In lege namque scriptum erat, ut mulier quae suscepto semine peperisset masculum, quadragesimo die purgata templum cum filio intraret, deferens hostias secum pro peccato, agnum anniculum immaculatum. Si autem non haberet agnum, par turturum, sive duos pullos columbae offerret (Levit. XII, 2, 4, 6, 8). Haec omnia beata Virgo non ex necessitate, sed ex humilitate implevit. Abstinuit ab introitu templi quadraginta diebus, non necesse habens purgari, quae sine omni delectatione, salvo virginitatis sigillo, de Spiritu sancto concepit. Obtulit hostiam non pro peccato, sicut aliae mulieres, sed pro humilitate: ostendens etiam in hoc quod Filius Dei Jesus Christus non solvere venerat legem, sed adimplere. Et ecce Simeon grandaevus, qui acceperat responsum non visurum se mortem nisi videret Christum Domini, et eum susciperet quem exspectabat, accepit eum in ulnas suas, hoc est, inter brachia sua amplexatus est; illum qui appendit tribus digitis molem terrae, qui omnia implet, omnia complectitur, inter brachia portat, dicens: Nunc dimittis, servum tuum, Domine, secundum verbum tuum, in pace. Cupio dissolvi, et ejus adventum cum patriarchis et prophetis in inferno exspectare, qui inter te et humanum genus facturus est pacem. Quia viderunt oculi mei salutare tuum, Filium tuum, quem desideravi, de quo responsum a Spiritu sancto accepi. Lumen ad revelationem gentium, et gloriam plebis tuae Israel (Luc. II, 29, 30, 32). De quo ait Isaias: Populus qui ambulabat in tenebris, vidit lucem magnam (Isa. IX, 2): quia, cum plenitudo Gentium intraverit, tunc omnis Israel salvus fiet (Rom. XI, 25, 26)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p014", "label": "English", "text": "14. For it was written in the Law that a woman who, having conceived, had borne a male child, should on the fortieth day, once purified, enter the temple with her son, bringing with her sacrifices for sin, a lamb of one year without blemish. But if she had no lamb, she was to offer a pair of turtledoves, or two young pigeons (Levit. XII, 2, 4, 6, 8). All these things the Blessed Virgin fulfilled not out of necessity, but out of humility. She abstained from entering the temple for forty days, though she had no need of purification, she who, without any delight, with the seal of her virginity unbroken, had conceived of the Holy Spirit. She offered the sacrifice not for sin, as other women did, but out of humility, showing also by this that the Son of God, Jesus Christ, had not come to abolish the Law, but to fulfill it. And behold, Simeon, now advanced in years, who had received the answer that he would not see death until he had seen the Christ of the Lord, and had received him whom he awaited, took him into his arms\u2014that is, embraced him between his arms\u2014him who weighs the mass of the earth with three fingers, who fills all things and embraces all things. Simeon carries him in his arms, saying: Now, Lord, you dismiss your servant, according to your word, in peace. I desire to be dissolved, and to await with the patriarchs and prophets in hell his coming, who is to make peace between you and the human race. For my eyes have seen your salvation, your Son, whom I desired, concerning whom I received an answer from the Holy Spirit. A light for the revelation of the Gentiles, and the glory of your people Israel (Luc. II, 29, 30, 32). Of whom Isaiah says: The people who walked in darkness have seen a great light (Isa. IX, 2): because, when the fullness of the Gentiles has entered in, then all Israel shall be saved (Rom. XI, 25, 26).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p015", "label": "Latin original", "text": "15. Audite, fratres, audite quid offert Maria, quid Simeon suscipit: Unigenitum Dei, Deo Patri consubstantialem et coaeterum, Verbum in principio. Et, ut utilitatem et consolationem nostram audiamus, illum offert Virgo, quem Deus Abrahae promisit, dicens: In semine tuo, hoc est, in Christo, benedicentur omnes gentes (Gen. XXII, 18; XII, 3, et XVIII, 18); et quem David promisit, dicens: De fructu ventris tui ponam super sedem tuam (Psal. CXXXI, 11). Hic est quem Virgo offert, patriarcharum gaudium, desiderium prophetarum, Gentium exspectatio, Judaeorum qui eum receperunt salvatio, ruinae angelorum reparatio. Talis est quem Virgo offert Deo, imo multo major quam lingua, aut hominum, aut angelorum possit dicere. Et cum sit tantus ac talis, qualem aut quantum ipse se novit, non sufficit Virgini ipsum solum offerre; sed hostiam, quam lex praeceperat, pro eo obtulit: ob humilitatis scilicet exemplum, ut nos doceret quomodo et nos eum debeamus offerre. Duos turtures, aut duos pullos columbae pro eo obtulit. Columba, fratres, simplex est animal, felle caret, rostro non laedit, in cavernis petrarum nidificat, alienos pullos nutrit, juxta fluenta manet, ut visa umbra accipitris fugiat, meliora grana eligit, gemitum pro cantu reddit, gregatim volat, alis se defendit, visum recuperat. Et nos, fratres, cum ante altare steterimus, ut Dei Filium Deo offeramus, cum eo et pro eo par turturum, castitatem corporis et animae offeramus: et duos pullos columbarum, pro cantu gementes, compunctionem geminam pro desiderio patriae coelestis, et de consideratione nostrae miseriae habentes. Columba felle caret: et nos ira careamus, quae peccare nos facit. Rostro non laedamus, sed percussi in unam maxillam, alium praebeamus. In cavernis petrarum nidificemus; in fide plagarum Christi refugium habeamus. Alienos pullos nutriamus; de filiis diaboli facientes verbo praedicationis filios Dei. Juxta flumina maneamus: id est, juxta hunc mortalitatis fluxum sedentes, umbram accipitris, hoc est, peccata quae sunt umbra diaboli, malorum ruinas considerantes, ab ejus ungulis fugiamus. Meliora grana eligamus id est, meliores sententias in Scripturis sanctis. Gregatim volemus. unum consensum in bono in congregatione sub pastore habentes. Alis Novi et Veteris Testamenti contra inimicos Dei nos defendamus; vel duobus praeceptis charitatis, Dei videlicet amore et proximi. Visum recuperemus, de bono actionis ad bonum semper intendentes contemplationis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p015", "label": "English", "text": "15. Hear, brothers, hear what Mary offers, what Simeon receives: the Only-begotten of God, consubstantial and coeternal with God the Father, the Word in the beginning. And, that we may hear of our profit and consolation, the Virgin offers him whom God promised to Abraham, saying: In your seed, that is, in Christ, all nations shall be blessed (Gen. XXII, 18; XII, 3, et XVIII, 18); and whom David promised, saying: Of the fruit of your womb I will set upon your throne (Psal. CXXXI, 11). This is he whom the Virgin offers: the joy of the patriarchs, the desire of the prophets, the expectation of the Gentiles, the salvation of the Jews who received him, the restoration of the ruin of the angels. Such is he whom the Virgin offers to God\u2014indeed far greater than any tongue, whether of men or of angels, can tell. And although he is so great and of such a kind as he alone knows himself to be, it was not enough for the Virgin to offer him alone; but she offered for him the sacrifice which the Law had commanded\u2014namely, as an example of humility, that she might teach us how we too ought to offer him. She offered for him two turtledoves, or two young pigeons. The dove, brothers, is a simple creature; it has no gall, does not wound with its beak, builds its nest in the clefts of the rocks, nourishes the young of others, dwells beside the streams so that, seeing the shadow of the hawk, it may flee; it chooses the better grains, utters a moan instead of a song, flies in flocks, defends itself with its wings, and recovers its sight. And we, brothers, when we stand before the altar to offer the Son of God to God, let us offer with him and for him a pair of turtledoves\u2014the chastity of body and soul; and two young pigeons, groaning instead of singing, having a twofold compunction: one from desire for our heavenly homeland, and one from the consideration of our own misery. The dove has no gall: let us likewise be without anger, which makes us sin. Let us not wound with our beak, but, struck on one cheek, let us offer the other. Let us nest in the clefts of the rocks; let us have our refuge in the faith of the wounds of Christ. Let us nourish the young of others, making, by the word of preaching, sons of God out of the children of the devil. Let us dwell beside the rivers: that is, sitting beside this flow of mortality, considering the shadow of the hawk\u2014that is, sins, which are the shadow of the devil\u2014and the ruin of the wicked, let us flee from his claws. Let us choose the better grains, that is, the better teachings in the Holy Scriptures. Let us fly in flocks, having one accord in good within the congregation under the shepherd. Let us defend ourselves against the enemies of God with the wings of the New and Old Testament; or with the two precepts of charity, namely, the love of God and of neighbor. Let us recover our sight, always directing ourselves from the good of action toward the good of contemplation.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p016", "label": "Latin original", "text": "16. Fratres charissimi, tales oportet nos esse cum corpus Christi consecramus, cum consecratum manducantes sacrificamus, cum vobis idem corpus in salutem corporis et animae porrigimus. Tales etiam vos oportet esse, cum sacrum sacramentum de manibus nostris accipitis, scientes, quod qui corpus Christi indigne accipit, et sanguinem ejus indigne bibit, judicium sibi manducat et bibit (I Cor. XI, 29). Verum, quia raro invenitur talis qui in se haec omnia habeat, diligat illum qui bonum habet, quod in se non habet: et in illo habeat, quod in se non videt. Neque enim credere debemus quod soli sacerdoti supradictae virtutes sint necessariae, quasi solus consecret et sacrificet corpus Christi. Non solus sacrificat, non solus consecrat, sed totus conventus fidelium qui astat, cum illo consecrat, cum illo sacrificat (4). Nec solus ligni faber facit domum, sed alius virgas, alius ligna, alius trabes, etc., comportat. Debent itaque astantes habere de suo, sicut et sacerdos, fidem firmam, orationem puram, devotionem piam. Sciendumque quod sine firma fide nemo astantium esse debet. Caeteras vero virtutes, quas supra diximus, etsi omnes non habent, diligant illum qui habet quod in se non inveniunt, et in illo habeant quod in se nondum vident. Sicut Petrus in Joanne virginitatis habet meritum: sic Joannes in Petro martyrii habet praemium."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p016", "label": "English", "text": "16. Dearest brothers, such as these ought we to be when we consecrate the body of Christ, when, by eating what has been consecrated, we offer sacrifice, when we hold out that same body to you for the salvation of body and soul. Such also ought you to be when you receive the sacred sacrament from our hands, knowing that whoever receives the body of Christ unworthily and drinks his blood unworthily eats and drinks judgment to himself (I Cor. XI, 29). But because one is rarely found who has all these things in himself, let him love the one who has the good which he himself does not have, and let him possess in that other what he does not see in himself. For we ought not to believe that the aforesaid virtues are necessary to the priest alone, as though he alone consecrates and sacrifices the body of Christ. He does not sacrifice alone, he does not consecrate alone, but the whole assembly of the faithful who stand present consecrates with him, sacrifices with him (4). Nor does the carpenter alone build the house, but one brings rods, another timber, another beams, and so on. Therefore those standing present ought to have of their own, just as the priest does, firm faith, pure prayer, pious devotion. And it should be known that no one of those standing present ought to be without firm faith. As for the other virtues we have mentioned above, even if not all possess them, let them love the one who has what they themselves do not find in themselves, and let them possess in him what they do not yet see in themselves. Just as Peter has in John the merit of virginity, so John has in Peter the reward of martyrdom.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p017", "label": "Latin original", "text": "17. Sciendum quoque, quod corpori Christi, quem Maria hodie praesentavit in templo, quem Simeon in ulnis suis suscepit, tria debemus: dignitatem, reverentiam et fructum. Dignitatem, ut cum Dei Filium, Deum de Deo, omnipotentem, creatorem omnium, judicem vivorum et mortuorum credamus. Dignitatem, ut credamus nos statim postquam de corpore exierimus, ab eo, sicut a judice, percepturos sicut gessimus, sive bonum, sive malum. Ille namque quem Maria in templo praesentavit, quem Simeon suscepit, quem in manibus consecrantes ante Deum levamus, benedicimus, manducamus, vobis porrigimus manducandum; ille familiaris noster, judex erit noster. Caveamus ergo ab eo separari per judicium post mortem, cui ita sumus familiares in vita. Dicamus itaque ei in corde, cum eum tractamus; dicamus cum eum consecramus: Domine, ad te veniemus post mortem, ante tuum tribunal judicandi astabimus. Qui nobis ita familiaris esse dignaris in vita, quod a nobis tractaris, consecraris, manducaris, noli nobis alienus esse in morte, sed parce nobis in die mala. Confundantur daemones qui nos tunc persequentur, destrue fortitudinem illorum in virtute tua; quia non est qui pugnet pro nobis, nisi tu Deus et familiaris noster. Haec est dignitas quam ei debemus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p017", "label": "English", "text": "17. We should also know that to the body of Christ, which Mary presented today in the temple, which Simeon received into his arms, we owe three things: dignity, reverence, and fruit. Dignity, that we believe him to be the Son of God, God of God, omnipotent, creator of all things, judge of the living and the dead. Dignity, that we believe that immediately after we leave the body, we shall receive from him, as from a judge, according as we have done, whether good or evil. For he whom Mary presented in the temple, whom Simeon received, whom we, consecrating, lift up in our hands before God, whom we bless, whom we eat, whom we hold out to you to be eaten \u2014 he who is so familiar to us will be our judge. Let us take care, then, not to be separated from him at the judgment after death, from him with whom we are so intimate in this life. Let us therefore say to him in our heart, when we handle him; let us say when we consecrate him: Lord, we shall come to you after death; we shall stand before your tribunal to be judged. You who deign to be so familiar with us in this life, that you are handled by us, consecrated by us, eaten by us \u2014 do not be a stranger to us in death, but spare us on the evil day. Let the demons who will then pursue us be confounded; destroy their strength by your power; for there is no one to fight for us except you, God and our familiar one. This is the dignity we owe him.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p018", "label": "Latin original", "text": "18. Debemus ei et reverentiam, investigare scilicet nosmetipsos: et si invenimus cor pollutum foedis cogitationibus aut mala voluntate, ab ejus contactu, ut ab igne, et nos et alios arceamus. Ignis enim consumens est (Deut. IV, 24.), ad quem indignus non debet accedere, ne interius consumatur, et in reprobum sensum tradatur. Exeat ergo extra castra, sicut Moyses praecepit, qui nocturno pollutus est somno (Deut. XXIII, 10), hoc est, qui per negligentiam istius obscurae vitae (quae tota ut somnus pertransit) pervenit usque ad pollutas cogitationes. Exeat extra castra, hoc est, dum in illo statu est, judicet se indignum societate fidelium communicantium hoc sacro corpore, lavetque vestimenta sua per sanctam confessionem; et post solis occasum, hoc est, post tentationis calorem et ardorem jam mortuum, laetus redeat, et cum aliis fidelibus communicet. Haec est reverentia quam debemus corpori Christi, quem virgo Maria hodierna die in templo praesentavit, quem Simeon laetus inter brachia suscepit."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p018", "label": "English", "text": "18. We also owe him reverence, namely, to search ourselves, and if we find a heart defiled by foul thoughts or evil will, to keep both ourselves and others from contact with him, as from fire. For he is a consuming fire (Deut. IV, 24.), whom an unworthy person ought not approach, lest he be consumed within and be given over to a depraved mind. Let him therefore go outside the camp, as Moses commanded, who has been defiled by nocturnal sleep (Deut. XXIII, 10) \u2014 that is, let him who through the negligence of this dark life (which passes away wholly like sleep) has come even to defiled thoughts. Let him go outside the camp, that is, while he is in that state, let him judge himself unworthy of the fellowship of the faithful who partake of this sacred body, and let him wash his garments through holy confession; and after the setting of the sun \u2014 that is, after the heat and burning of temptation has now died down \u2014 let him return joyfully, and commune with the other faithful. This is the reverence we owe to the body of Christ, whom the Virgin Mary presented in the temple on this day, whom Simeon joyfully received into his arms.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p019", "label": "Latin original", "text": "19. Debemus etiam fructum scire, intelligere scilicet quem fructum accipimus, si eum digne comedimus. Audite, fratres, non me, sed Apostolum dicentem: Calix benedictionis cui benedicimus, nonne communicatio sanguinis Christi est? Calix, id est communicatio calicis, facit nos habere quamdam communionem cum Christo. Et panis, quem frangimus, nonne participatio corporis est Domini? (I Cor. X, 16.) Ac si dicat: Panis, quem frangimus, facit nos unum corpus sub Christo capite: quoniam, sicut unus panis ex multis granis conficitur, qui postea in corpus Christi et per fidem, et per sancta verba, quae Christus suos docuit, convertitur: sic diversi participantes hoc corpore in unitate fidei, spei et charitatis, unum corpus cum Christo sunt, ad Christum statim post mortem venturi, et cum Christo sicut cum capite suo sine fine regnaturi: quem Virgo concepit, Virgo peperit. Virgo in templo hodierna die praesentavit, qui cum Patre et Spiritu sancto, vivit et regnat Deus, per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-p019", "label": "English", "text": "19. We ought also to know the fruit, that is, to understand what fruit we receive if we eat him worthily. Hear, brothers, not me, but the Apostle saying: The cup of blessing which we bless, is it not a communion of the blood of Christ? The cup, that is, the sharing of the cup, makes us have a certain communion with Christ. And the bread, which we break, is it not a participation in the body of the Lord? (I Cor. X, 16.). As if he were saying: The bread which we break makes us one body under Christ as head: for just as one bread is made from many grains, which is afterward converted into the body of Christ both by faith and by the holy words which Christ taught his own, so the various people who partake of this body in the unity of faith, hope, and charity are one body with Christ, destined to come to Christ immediately after death, and to reign without end with Christ as with their head \u2014 whom the Virgin conceived, the Virgin bore. The Virgin presented him in the temple on this day \u2014 he who with the Father and the Holy Spirit lives and reigns as God, through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(2) De hoc sermone dubitat Horstius an sit Guerrici: tum quod desideretur in manuscripto Coloniensi, qui ipsius sermones continet. tum quod Guerrici stylus pressior et nervosior videatur. Porro licet hactenus Bernardi operibus insertus sit, minus tanto dignus est auctore."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n002", "label": "English", "text": "(2) Horstius doubts whether this sermon is Guerric's: both because it is missing from the Cologne manuscript, which contains his sermons, and because Guerric's style seems more compressed and vigorous. Moreover, although it has hitherto been inserted among the works of Bernard, it is less worthy of so great an author.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(3) Ita canit Ecclesia in officio hujus diei."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n003", "label": "English", "text": "(3) So the Church sings in the Office of this day.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(4) Solum sacerdotem esse proprium et sufficientem ministrum Eucharistiae consecrandae de fide est, ut patet ex can. 4 conc. Nic. ubi Patres dum diaconos reprehendunt, quod quibusdam in locis administrent Eucharistiam presbyteris, idque de caetero fieri prohibent, hanc ejusce decreti reddunt rationem: Hoc inquiunt, neque regula neque consuetudo tradidit ut qui offerendi sacrificii non habent potestatem, his qui offerunt corpus Christi porrigant. Idem statuit conc. Lateran. sub Innoc. III, et refertur cap. Firmiter extra de sum. Trin. Nam veritate sacramenti explicata, ita subjungit: Et hoc utique sacramentum nemo potest conficere nisi sacerdos, qui rite fuerit ordinatus, secundum claves Ecclesiae, quas concessit apostolis eorumque successoribus Jesus Christus. Ita fere Isambertus, in 3 q. 82, art. 1. Quanquam dici potest, astantes etiam suo modo sacrificium offerre et conficere per sacerdotem et cum sacerdote qui populi mediator et minister est. Unde in canone missae olim ita legebatur: Et omnium circumstantium, qui tibi offerunt hoc sacrificium laudis, quibus verbis haec inserta sunt: Pro quibus tibi offerimus, vel qui tibi offerunt. etc."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-n004", "label": "English", "text": "(4) That the priest alone is the proper and sufficient minister for consecrating the Eucharist is a matter of faith, as is clear from canon 4 of the Council of Nicaea, where the Fathers, in rebuking the deacons because in certain places they were administering the Eucharist to the presbyters, and forbidding that this be done henceforth, give this reason for the decree: \"Neither rule nor custom,\" they say, \"has handed down that those who have no power of offering the sacrifice should give the Body of Christ to those who offer it.\" The Lateran Council under Innocent III established the same thing, and it is referred to in the chapter \"Firmiter\" in the Decretals, On the Most High Trinity; for having explained the truth of the sacrament, it adds: \"And indeed no one can consecrate this sacrament except a priest who has been duly ordained, according to the keys of the Church which Jesus Christ granted to the apostles and their successors.\" So, more or less, Isambert, in part 3, question 82, article 1. Although it may be said that those standing by also, in their own way, offer and accomplish the sacrifice through the priest and with the priest, who is the mediator and minister of the people. Whence in the canon of the Mass it used to be read: \"And of all those standing round, who offer to Thee this sacrifice of praise,\" words into which these were inserted: \"For whom we offer to Thee,\" or \"who offer to Thee,\" etc.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "Authorship uncertain. The edition presents this sermon as Guerric's, but its footnote (2) reports that the earlier editor Horstius doubted it: the sermon is missing from a Cologne manuscript of Guerric's sermons, and its style is less terse and vigorous than his. It had also been printed among the works of Bernard of Clairvaux."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints alium, where aliam (agreeing with maxillam) is expected. The printed form is kept."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-05-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints cum, evidently a misprint for eum (him). The printed form is kept."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon V: On Cleansing and Adorning the Soul for Christ", "topics": ["Purification", "adorning the soul", "vices and virtues", "Eucharist", "Mary and Simeon"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-05.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:candlemas", "calendar_label": "The Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary (Candlemas)", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138-1157", "id": "guerric-purification-06", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis, sermo VI, De perficienda nostri purificatione, ut Domino sisti possimus, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 89-92.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Purification", "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "Luke 2:22", "Proverbs 20:9", "Sirach 7:34", "Psalm 54:19 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 6:8\u201311", "Leviticus 12:4", "Isaiah 1:15", "Ezekiel 16:6", "1 Corinthians 15:50", "Jeremiah 48:10", "Psalm 50:16 (historical numbering)", "Luke 2:34", "Job 9:30\u201331", "Isaiah 9:5", "Luke 2:23", "Psalm 83:4 (historical numbering)", "2 Corinthians 3:18"], "occasion": "In festo purificationis beatae Mariae Virginis", "occasion_detail": "Purification of the Blessed Virgin Mary", "occasion_label": "Purification", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Simeon's completed purification prompts an examination of negligence and the use of time. Guerric considers purification through prayer, watchful faith and the final vision of God. The sermon appears in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO VI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON VI.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De perficienda nostri purificatione, ut Domino sisti possimus."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On Perfecting Our Purification So That We May Be Presented to the Lord", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Postquam impleti sunt dies purgationis, tulerunt Jesum in Jerusalem. O quam felix est, de quo dici potest: Impleti sunt dies purgationis ejus: ut jam nihil aliud ei supersit, nisi ut ferant eum in Jerusalem supernam, et sistant eum Domino (Luc. II, 22). Talis nimirum erat Simeon ille noster senex, tam desiderabilis quam plenus desideriis, cujus cum jam olim, ut arbitror, essent impleti dies purgationis, hodie impleti sunt etiam dies exspectationis: ut jam, secundum verbum Domini, nihil aliud ei restaret, postquam vidisset Christum Domini, Christum pacem Dei et hominum, nisi ut dimitteretur in pace, et dormiret in pace in idipsum; hoc est, ut ferrent eum in Jerusalem pacis aeternae, et sisterent eum ad contemplandam pacem quae exsuperat omnem sensum. O Simeon vir desideriorum, impletum est in bonis desiderium tuum. O beate senex, renovata est ut aquilae juventus tua. Introisti jam ad altare Dei illud supernum, aeternum, totum aureum, ad Deum qui laetificat juventutem tuam aeterna visione sui, cujus laetificavit senectutem hodierna visione Christi sui. Ad altare illud invisibile jam ipse praesentatus es Patri; cui ad altare hoc visibile Filium hodie praesentasti. Ibi Filium ipsum, quem hodie tuo sinu gestasti, aeterno jam et indissolubili tibi conseris amplexu."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"After the days of purification were fulfilled, they carried Jesus up to Jerusalem.\" O how happy is he of whom it can be said: \"The days of his purgation were fulfilled,\" so that nothing else remains for him but that they carry him up to the heavenly Jerusalem and present him to the Lord (Luc. II, 22). Such, surely, was our Simeon of old, that elder as desirable as he was full of desires, whose days of purgation had, I think, long since been fulfilled; and today his days of expectation also were fulfilled, so that, according to the word of the Lord, nothing else remained for him, once he had seen the Christ of the Lord, the Christ who is the peace of God and of men, but to be dismissed in peace and to sleep in peace in that very peace \u2014 that is, that they should carry him into the Jerusalem of eternal peace, and present him to behold the peace which surpasses all understanding. O Simeon, man of desires, your desire has been fulfilled with good things! O blessed elder, your youth has been renewed like the eagle's! You have already come to that heavenly altar, eternal, wholly golden, to God who gladdens your youth with the eternal vision of himself, who gladdened your old age today with the vision of his own Christ. At that invisible altar you yourself have already been presented to the Father, to whom at this visible altar you presented the Son today. There you now embrace in an eternal and indissoluble embrace that very Son whom today you bore in your own arms.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Repletum est igitur in bonis beati senis desiderium, cujus tota erat exspectatio et desiderium, Exspectatio gentium et Desideratus earum. Renovata est ut aquilae juventus ejus, quoniam in senectute bona impleti sunt dies purgationis ejus. Alioquin nequaquam illius capax novitatis esse posset, nisi sedulo vetustatem his diebus expurgasset. Fratres, expurgate vetus fermentum, dum tempus habetis ad expurgandum; ut cum impleti fuerint dies vestrae purgationis, inveniamini et vos capaces illius gaudii, quo plena est jam anima Simeonis. Et quidem dies qui ad purgandum nobis dati sunt, velimus, nolimus, implemus: sed vae nobis si dies implentur, et purgatio minime impletur; ut postea necesse sit illo nos igne repurgari, quo nihil poenalius, nihil acrius, aut vehementius in hac vita excogitari potest. Sed quis ille tam perfectus, tam sanctus, qui cum hinc exierit, nihil illi debeat igni: qui sic ad purum omnem in se scoriam excoxerit peccati, ut gloriari possit castum se habere cor, ut dicere possit: Mundum est cor meum, purus sum a peccato? (Prov. XX, 9.) Pauci quidem sunt electi; sed inter illos paucos paucissimi sunt, ut arbitror, ita perfecti, ut purgationem illam impleverint, de qua Sapiens dicit: De negligentia purga te cum paucis (Eccli. VII, 34). Omnino, si de negligentia nos purgaremus, de paucis essemus. Nunc autem quoniam non modo minima, sed et maxima negligimus, longe a paucis de terra divisi sumus, et de nobis dicit ille, qui paucitatem sanctorum multitudini praefert negligentium: Inter multos erant mecum (Psal. LIV, 19)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. The desire of the blessed old man, therefore, was fulfilled with good things: his whole expectation and desire was the Expectation of the nations and their Desired One. His youth was renewed like the eagle's, because in a good old age the days of his purgation were fulfilled. Otherwise he could by no means have been capable of that newness, had he not diligently purged away his oldness in those days. Brethren, purge out the old leaven while you have time to purge it, so that when the days of your purgation have been fulfilled, you too may be found capable of that joy with which the soul of Simeon is now filled. And indeed the days given us for purging, whether we will or not, we do fulfill; but woe to us if the days are fulfilled and the purgation is by no means fulfilled, so that afterward we must needs be purged again by that fire than which nothing more penal, nothing more bitter or more violent can be conceived in this life. But who is so perfect, so holy, that when he departs from here he owes nothing to that fire \u2014 who has so thoroughly burned out every dross of sin in himself that he can boast of having a chaste heart, that he can say: \"My heart is clean, I am pure from sin\"? (Prov. XX, 9.) Few indeed are the elect; but among those few, fewest of all, I think, are so perfect as to have fulfilled that purgation of which the Wise Man says: \"Purge yourself from negligence with the few\" (Eccli. VII, 34). Certainly, if we purged ourselves of negligence, we would be among the few. But now, since we neglect not only the smallest things but even the greatest, we are far removed from the few on earth, and of us he says, who prefers the fewness of the saints to the multitude of the negligent: \"They were with me among the many\" (Psal. LIV, 19).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Et quid hodie tam proprium simul et commune his quos religionis fovet umbra, sicut negligentia? et, ut de meipso loquar, quoties derident hostes Sabbata mea? quoties dormitat anima mea prae taedio, diemque fere totum, ac si tempus revocabile esset, inertia consumo? Et utinam sic dormitaret anima mea, ut apertis et vigilantibus oculis, ipse mihi misero non fingerem somnia vana et illusoria! At nunc in perversum mutata sententia, ego vigilo, et cor meum dormit, somnoque tam gravi, ut vix aliquando valeat expergisci ad tonitruum supernae illius increpationis: Usquequo, piger, dormis? quando consurges e somno tuo? Paululum dormies, paululum dormitabis, paululum conseres manus ut dormias; et veniet tibi quasi viator egestas, et pauperies quasi vir armatus (Prov. VI, 8-11; XXIV, 33, 34). Illud sane miserrimum est, quod hodie non putatur jactura, sed lucrum, si dies qui nobis ad purgationem dati sunt, elabantur nobis dormiendo, praetereant et pereant negligendo. Gentilis, aiunt, cum sederet ad mensam, venissetque in mentem nihil strenuum, aut memorabile se egisse die illo; cum gemitu gravi et dolore mensam repulit, exclamans: \u00ab O amici, diem perdidi (5) \u00bb Nos hodie dicere videmur, diem lucrifeci, si sine opere totus per inertiam possit elabi. Sic nullus fere est qui tempori pretium ponat, qui cogitet apud se quantum valeat ad comparandam aeternitatem dies una. Putasne est aliquis in nobis, qui quotidianam dierum suorum a semetipso rationem exigat; qui cujusque diei dispendia vel emolumenta diligenter sibi describat: ut si deprehenderit se hodie negligenter egisse, vindicet in seipsum, ac de caetero cautius agat? Beatus ille servus, quem, cum venerit dies extremus, invenerit ita facientem."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. And what today is at once so proper and so common to those whom the shadow of religion shelters, as negligence? And, to speak of myself, how often do enemies mock my Sabbaths? How often does my soul drowse from weariness, and I consume almost the whole day in idleness, as though time could be recalled! And would that my soul drowsed in such a way that, with eyes open and watchful, I did not fashion for myself, wretched man, vain and illusory dreams! But now, in a perverse reversal, I am awake, and my heart sleeps \u2014 and sleeps so heavily that it can scarcely ever be roused by the thunder of that heavenly rebuke: \"How long will you sleep, O sluggard? When will you rise from your sleep? You will sleep a little, you will slumber a little, you will fold your hands a little to sleep; and your want shall come upon you like a traveler, and your poverty like an armed man\" (Prov. VI, 8-11; XXIV, 33, 34). This indeed is most wretched, that today it is not thought a loss but a gain, if the days given us for purgation slip away from us in sleep, and pass by and perish through negligence. The pagan, they say, sitting at table, when it came to his mind that he had done nothing vigorous or memorable that day, pushed back the table with heavy groaning and grief, exclaiming: \"O friends, I have lost a day (5)!\" We today seem to say, \"I have gained a day,\" if it can slip away entirely through idleness, without any work. Thus there is almost no one who sets a price on time, who considers within himself how much a single day is worth toward securing eternity. Do you think there is anyone among us who demands of himself a daily account of his days, who diligently sets down for himself the losses or gains of each day, so that if he finds he has acted negligently today, he punishes himself for it, and acts more cautiously thereafter? Blessed is that servant whom, when the last day comes, he shall find so doing.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hoc nempe esset de negligentia cum paucis purgari, si recte et utiliter implerentur dies purgationis, ut aliquando posset accessus ad sancta salubriter obtineri. Alioquin, quandiu manserit quis in sanguine purificationis suae, sancivit aeterna justitia (sicut scriptum est in lege Moysi) ut omne sanctum non tangat, nec ingrediatur sanctuarium, donec impleantur dies purgationis ejus (Levit. XII, 4). Viderint ergo qua fronte ingrediantur sanctuarium, qua conscientia non solum tangant sancta, sed etiam tractent et sumant Sanctum sanctorum; qui scilicet nondum liberati de sanguinibus, nullum tamen suae purificationis studium gerunt. Annon audiunt protestantem et dicentem: Cum extenderitis manus vestras, avertam oculos meos; manus enim vestrae sanguine plenae sunt? (Isai. I, 15.) Quid enim, si mundus sum a sanguine alieno, et conculcari me Dominus videt in sanguine proprio (Ezech. XVI, 6), sicut de Jerusalem loquitur per Ezechielem? Quid, inquam, mihi proderit, si veloces non sunt pedes mei ad effundendum sanguinem proximorum; et veloces sunt affectus mei ad acquiescendum carni et sanguini, de quibus dicit Apostolus: Quia caro et sanguis regnum Dei non possidebunt? (I Cor. XV, 50.) Maledictus, inquit Jeremias, qui prohibet gladium suum a sanguine (Jerem. XLVIII, 10): id est, qui vitia carnis et sanguinis sibi parcit abscindere. Libera me de sanguinibus, Deus, Deus salutis meae (Psal. L, 16), ne cum sanguine purificationis meae sanctuarium ingrediens, aut sancta contingens, reus inveniar corporis et sanguinis tui, indigne manducans et bibens. Verumtamen gratias tibi, Deus, Deus salutis meae, qui in hoc ipsum pretiosum tuum fudisti sanguinem, ut immunditiam nostri purificares sanguinis, tactumque peccatricis sanguinem fluentis, sed salutem desiderantis non abhorreres: sciens nimirum quod nullus potest facere mundum de immundo conceptum semine, nisi tu, qui solus es; nec sanis, sed male habentibus, melius tamen habere cupientibus, medicus opus est. Tangere itaque Sanctum sanctorum, sicut illis quos delectat in sanguine suo versari, judicium est mortis: sic illis qui desiderant purificari, remedium est salutis, juxta prophetiam Simeonis, Quia hic positus est in ruinam et in resurrectionem multorum (Luc II, 34)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. This indeed would be to be purified from negligence along with the few, if the days of purification were rightly and profitably fulfilled, so that access to holy things might at some point be beneficially obtained. Otherwise, as long as anyone remains in the blood of his purification, eternal justice has decreed (as it is written in the law of Moses) that he shall not touch anything holy, nor enter the sanctuary, until the days of his purification are fulfilled (Levit. XII, 4). Let them consider, then, with what effrontery they enter the sanctuary, with what conscience they not only touch holy things but even handle and consume the Holy of Holies \u2014 those, namely, who are not yet freed from blood, and yet make no effort at all toward their own purification. Do they not hear him protesting and saying: 'When you stretch out your hands, I will turn away my eyes; for your hands are full of blood'? (Isai. I, 15.). For what does it profit me if I am clean of another's blood, while the Lord sees me trampled underfoot in my own blood (Ezech. XVI, 6), as he speaks of Jerusalem through Ezekiel? What, I say, will it profit me if my feet are not swift to shed the blood of my neighbors, while my affections are swift to acquiesce to flesh and blood, of which the Apostle says: 'For flesh and blood shall not possess the kingdom of God' (I Cor. XV, 50.)? 'Cursed,' says Jeremiah, 'is he who withholds his sword from blood' (Jerem. XLVIII, 10): that is, he who spares himself from cutting away the vices of flesh and blood. Deliver me from blood, O God, God of my salvation (Psal. L, 16), lest, entering the sanctuary with the blood of my purification, or touching holy things, I be found guilty of your body and blood, eating and drinking unworthily. Yet thanks be to you, God, God of my salvation, who for this very purpose poured out your precious blood, that you might purify the uncleanness of our blood, and did not abhor the touch of a sinful woman flowing with blood, yet desiring salvation: knowing well that no one can make clean what was conceived of unclean seed, except you, who alone can; nor is the physician needed for the healthy, but for those who are ill, yet desire to be better. Thus to touch the Holy of Holies is, for those who delight in wallowing in their own blood, a sentence of death; but for those who desire to be purified, it is a remedy of salvation, according to the prophecy of Simeon: 'For this one is set for the fall and for the resurrection of many' (Luc II, 34).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Fratres, et vos quidem in sanguine fuistis, sed abluti estis, sed purificati estis: nisi quia, sicut justus confitetur: Si loti fueritis velut aquis nivis, sordibus vos statim intingit (Job IX, 30, 31), non solum conditio corrupta, sed etiam voluntas infirma; ut semper novis sordibus opus sit purificatione nova. Illa vero mirabilis et miserabilis est in hominibus sui negligentia, ut, cum nemo sit qui se purificatione non indigere praesumat, vix tamen est aliquis qui tempus ad hoc indultum, quasi purgatissimus sit, non negligat. Fratres, breves dies hominis sunt (ibid., 25), sed tamen valde pretiosi sunt; imo quo breviores, eo pretiosiores; quia dies sunt purificationis, cum statim postmodum futuri sint dies retributionis. Beati qui in diebus istis lavant stolas suas, et candidas eas faciunt in sanguine Agni; ne postea vestimentum eorum, si mistum fuerit sanguine, sit in combustionem et cibus ignis (Isai. IX, 5). Nec solum timor urget supplicii, sed etiam spes invitat praemii, ut omni cura et instantia nostrae modo studeamus purificationi; ut quod hodie figurare voluit in seipso Filius Dei quadam dispensatoria specie, completum quandoque gaudeamus in nobis ipsa rerum veritate. Quid illud? Postquam, inquit, impleti sunt dies purgationis, tulerunt Jesum in Jerusalem, ut sisterent eum Domino (Luc. II, 23). Et nos utique, si tempora purgationis legitime fuerint a nobis impleta, tollent in supernam Jerusalem ministeria angelorum, ut illic assistentes vultui Dei, oblatio ejus simus accepta et immaculata. Ibi demum purificabimur ex toto; sicut a peccato, sic etiam a poena peccati. Ibi nostrae purificationis ipsa erit consummatio, quae et remuneratio; cum scilicet divinus ignis totos nos absumet in holocaustum Domino."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Brothers, you too were indeed in blood, but you have been washed, you have been purified \u2014 except that, as the just man confesses: 'If you have been washed as it were with waters of snow, it immediately plunges you into filth' (Job IX, 30, 31), it is not only our corrupted condition but also our weak will that does this, so that there is always need of new purification for new filth. But this is a marvelous and wretched thing in men \u2014 this negligence of themselves \u2014 that, though there is no one who presumes himself not to need purification, there is scarcely anyone who does not neglect the time granted for this, as though he were already most thoroughly purified. Brothers, the days of man are few (ibid., 25), yet they are very precious; indeed, the shorter they are, the more precious \u2014 because they are days of purification, since immediately afterward will come the days of retribution. Blessed are those who in these days wash their robes, and make them white in the blood of the Lamb; lest afterward their garment, if it be mingled with blood, be for burning and fuel for fire (Isai. IX, 5). Not only does the fear of punishment press us, but the hope of reward also invites us, so that we should now strive toward our purification with all care and urgency; so that what the Son of God today willed to prefigure in himself by a certain outward dispensation, we may one day rejoice to see fulfilled in us in the very truth of the reality. What is that? 'After,' it says, 'the days of purification were fulfilled, they brought Jesus to Jerusalem, to present him to the Lord' (Luc. II, 23). And we too, surely, if the times of our purification have been rightly fulfilled by us, the ministries of angels will carry us up into the heavenly Jerusalem, so that, standing there before the face of God, we may be his acceptable and unblemished offering. There at last we shall be purified wholly \u2014 as from sin, so also from the punishment of sin. There the very consummation of our purification will take place, which will also be our reward; when, that is, the divine fire shall consume us wholly as a holocaust to the Lord.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Porro hunc ipsum beatissimae purificationis inexplicabilem modum nec modo quidem cessat aemulari devotio sanctorum, quantum tamen corruptela corporum, et sollicitudo terrenae inhabitationis permittunt: dum scilicet mente excedentes in illam Jerusalem, verum orationis locum, illic in conspectu Domini quasi turturem et columbam pro seipsis ac de seipsis offerunt; corde videlicet et carne exsultantes in Deum vivum, quia scilicet columba invenit sibi domum, et turtur nidum, altaria tua, Domine virtutum (Psal. LXXXIII, 4). Ego tamen arbitror quod ad meritum non sit inferius, et fortassis ad purgationem efficacius, si illud quod per speculum et in aenigmate raro et raris experiri datur, id est, ut in Jerusalem sistantur Domino, nos per fidem jugiter actitemus: proponentes scilicet Dominum in conspectu nostro semper, et oculos ejus et judicia super nos pervigili fide, irremissoque semper timore cogitantes. Fides ista, fratres, sit in vobis, et eritis de paucis: timor iste sit in vobis, et de negligentia purgabitis vos cum paucis; quia timor iste nihil facile negligit. Denique ab initio hujus pertransibitis, sicut sponsus sponsae pollicetur, euntes de virtute in virtutem, a claritate in claritatem, tanquam a Domini Spiritu (II Cor. III, 18): proficientes de visione quae est per fidem, ad illam quae est per speculum et imaginem, ac postremo, de illa, quae est in imagine speciei, ad eam quae erit in ipsa veritate faciei, seu facie veritatis. Si enim jugiter praesentiam Domini, licet velatam, intendatis per fidem; aliquando etiam revelata facie gloriam ejus speculabimini, licet per speculum et imaginem. Cum autem impletis diebus purgationis venerit quod perfectum est, assistetis Domino in Jerusalem, habitantes cum vultu ejus, ac sine fine contuentes eum facie ad faciem, cui benedictio, et claritas in saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Moreover, the devotion of the saints does not even now cease to imitate this inexpressible manner of most blessed purification, so far as the corruption of our bodies and the anxious care of our earthly dwelling permit: that is, when, rising above in mind into that Jerusalem which is the true place of prayer, they there offer in the sight of the Lord, as it were, a turtledove and a dove for themselves and of themselves; rejoicing, namely, in heart and flesh unto the living God, because indeed the dove has found herself a house, and the turtledove a nest, even your altars, O Lord of hosts (Psal. LXXXIII, 4). Yet I reckon that it is no less meritorious, and perhaps more effective for purgation, if that which is granted to be experienced rarely and by the few through a mirror and in a riddle \u2014 namely, to be presented to the Lord in Jerusalem \u2014 we continually practice through faith: setting the Lord always before our sight, and pondering, with ever-watchful faith and unrelaxing fear, his eyes and his judgments upon us. Let this faith, brothers, be in you, and you will be among the few; let this fear be in you, and you will purify yourselves from negligence along with the few, since this fear neglects nothing easily. Finally, from this beginning you will pass on, as the Bridegroom promises to his bride, going from strength to strength, from glory to glory, as from the Spirit of the Lord (II Cor. III, 18): advancing from the vision that comes through faith, to that which comes through a mirror and image, and at last, from that which is in the image of a likeness, to that which will be in the very truth of the face, or the face of truth. For if you continually fix your attention, through faith, upon the presence of the Lord, veiled though it be, you will one day also behold his glory with face unveiled, though still as through a mirror and image. But when, the days of purification being fulfilled, that which is perfect shall come, you will stand before the Lord in Jerusalem, dwelling with his countenance, and gazing upon him face to face without end \u2014 to whom be blessing and glory for ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-n005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "(5) Titus imp., apud Sueton. Iisdem pene verbis queritur Seneca, epist. 1."}, {"anchor": "guerric-purification-06-n005", "label": "English", "text": "(5) The Emperor Titus, in Suetonius. Seneca complains in almost the same words, Epistle 1.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Purification Sermon VI: On Perfecting Our Purification So That We May Be Presented to the Lord", "topics": ["Purification", "negligence", "spiritual progress", "purgatorial fire", "Simeon"], "url": "sermons/guerric-purification-06.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:resurrexi", "calendar_label": "Easter Sunday of the Resurrection", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-resurrection-01", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny, Sermones per annum, De ressurrectione Domini, sermo I, Pulchre verba Jacob de Joseph applicantur ad resurrectionem Christi, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 141\u2013144.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Easter", "Resurrection of the Lord", "Genesis 45:26, 28", "Matthew 5:6", "Proverbs 9:9", "Genesis 39:6", "Genesis 41:45", "Hosea 12:10", "Genesis 45:26", "Mark 16:10\u201313 (historical wording; see note)", "Luke 24:9, 11", "Genesis 45:27\u201328", "Luke 24:37", "John 20:22", "John 6:40; 11:26 (printed locators; see note)", "Matthew 28:18", "Matthew 11:28 (adapted quotation; see note)", "Matthew 25:34 (adapted quotation; see note)"], "occasion": "De resurrectione Domini", "occasion_detail": "Resurrection of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Easter", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric of Igny reads the news that Joseph is alive as a figure of the announcement that Jesus has risen. Jacob\u2019s grief and renewed spirit become a pattern for the apostles\u2019 passage from doubt to joy, and for the hearers\u2019 growth in love. The Holy Spirit\u2019s witness, love that seeks Christ\u2019s life rather than its own advantage, and Christ\u2019s flesh as provision for the journey shape the concluding exhortation. The individual sermon is not dated; the collection is associated approximately with Guerric\u2019s abbacy in 1138\u20131157 and is read here from the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. Its historical heading names the Resurrection of the Lord, and the sermon explicitly celebrates Easter."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "DE RESSURRECTIONE DOMINI."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h001", "label": "English", "text": "THE RESURRECTION OF THE LORD", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO I."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON I", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "Pulchre verba Jacob de Joseph applicantur ad resurrectionem Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-h003", "label": "English", "text": "Jacob\u2019s Words about Joseph Are Aptly Applied to Christ\u2019s Resurrection", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Nuntiaverunt Jacob, dicentes: Joseph vivit. Quo audito, revixit spiritus ejus, et ait: Sufficit mihi, si Joseph filius meus vivit. Vadam et videbo eum antequam moriar. (Gen. xlv, 26, 28). Dicetis forsitan, mihi, Bene; sed quid ad rem? quid de Joseph ad gaudium hujus diei, ad gloriam resurrectionis Christi? Pascha est, et tu iterum apponis quadragesimalia? Agnum paschalem esurit anima nostra, cui se per tam longa praeparavit jejunia. Ardens est cor nostrum in nobis de Jesu, Jesum desideramus; sed nondum meremur ipsum videre vel de ipso audire. Jesum, non Joseph esurimus; Salvatorem, non somniatorem; dominatorem coeli, non Aegypti; non qui pavit ventres, sed qui pascit mentes, sed esurientes. In hoc saltem nos adjuvet sermo tuus, ut, quem esurimus, amplius esuriamus. Legimus enim: Beati qui esuriunt, quoniam ipsi saturabuntur. (Matth. v, 6). Cum audimus, magis esurimus. Nam et qui epulas commendat, famem irritat. Si de Jesu audierimus, auditui nostro dabitur gaudium et laetitia, et exsultabunt ossa humiliata. Humiliata sunt ossa nostra quadragesimali afflictione et luctu, magis autem dolore passionis ejus; sed exsultabunt nuntio resurrectionis ejus. Cur ergo tuum Joseph nobis ingeris, cum praeter Jesum, quidquid loquaris non sapiat nobis: praesertim hodie, cum paschalis agnus comeditur, cum Pascha nostrum immolatus est Christus?"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. They brought news to Jacob, saying: Joseph is alive. On hearing this, his spirit revived, and he said: It is enough for me if Joseph my son is alive. I will go and see him before I die. (Gen. xlv, 26, 28). Perhaps you will say to me: Very well; but what has this to do with the matter? What has Joseph to do with the joy of this day, with the glory of Christ\u2019s resurrection? It is Easter, and you are serving Lenten fare again? Our soul hungers for the Paschal Lamb, for whom it has prepared itself through such long fasts. Our heart burns within us for Jesus; we long for Jesus, but we do not yet deserve to see him or hear about him. We hunger for Jesus, not Joseph; for the Saviour, not the dreamer; for the ruler of heaven, not Egypt; for him who feeds minds, and hungry minds at that, rather than him who fed bellies. At least let your sermon help us in this: to hunger still more for him for whom we hunger. For we read: Blessed are those who hunger, for they shall be filled. (Matth. v, 6). As we hear, we hunger more. For one who praises a feast also whets the appetite. If we hear about Jesus, joy and gladness will be granted to our hearing, and the bones brought low will exult. Our bones have been brought low by Lenten affliction and mourning, and still more by grief for his Passion; but they will exult at the news of his resurrection. Why, then, do you thrust your Joseph upon us, when whatever you say apart from Jesus has no savour for us, especially today, when the Paschal Lamb is eaten, when Christ our Passover has been sacrificed?", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Ovum sive nucem apposui vobis, fratres; frangite testam, et invenietis escam. Joseph discutiatur, et Jesus invenietur agnus paschalis, quem esuritis; qui tanto dulcius comeditur, quanto latens abstrusius, et studiosius quaeritur, et difficilius invenitur Dicitis mihi: Quid Joseph ad Christum? quid historia quam proposui ad diem istum? Multum per omnem modum. Historiam recordamini, et ultro sese vobis revelabit pietas mysterii, si modo Jesum interpretem habeatis, qui, hodieque resurgens, de occidente littera loquitur suis in via, et aperit Scripturas. Quis enim in omnibus Patriarchis et Prophetis manifestius aut signantius quam Joseph figuram exprimit Salvatoris? Et, ut breviter summam perstringam, secundum illud: Da occasionem sapienti, et addetur ei sapientia (Prov. ix, 9), si cum fide ac pietate cogitemus interpretationem nominis ejus, deinde quod prae caeteris fratribus pulchra facie et decorus aspectu (Gen. xxxix, 6), quod innocens actu, prudens intellectu, quod venditus a suis, suos a morte redemit, quod prius humiliatus usque ad ergastulum, et sic exaltatus usque ad solium, quod denique pro merito operis sui novo nomine appellatus est inter gentes Salvator mundi (Gen. xli, 45); si haec omnia, inquam, pie ac fideliter cogitemus, nonne incunctanter agnoscimus quam vere sit a Domino dictum: In manibus Prophetarum assimilatus sum? (Ose. xii, 10)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. I have served you an egg or a nut, brothers; break the shell, and you will find food. Let Joseph be examined, and Jesus will be found, the Paschal Lamb for whom you hunger. He is eaten all the more sweetly when, hidden more deeply, he is sought more earnestly and found with greater difficulty. You say to me: What has Joseph to do with Christ? What has the story I have set before you to do with this day? Much in every way. Recall the story, and the loving mercy of the mystery will reveal itself to you of its own accord, provided you have Jesus as your interpreter, who, rising even today from the letter that kills, speaks to his own on the road and opens the Scriptures. For who among all the patriarchs and prophets expresses the figure of the Saviour more clearly or distinctly than Joseph? And, to touch briefly on the main points, according to the saying, Give the wise man an opportunity, and wisdom will be added to him (Prov. ix, 9): if we consider with faith and devotion the meaning of his name; then how, beyond his other brothers, he was beautiful in face and comely in appearance (Gen. xxxix, 6); how he was innocent in deed and prudent in understanding; how, sold by his own, he redeemed his own from death; how he was first humbled as far as the prison and then raised as high as the throne; and finally how, in recognition of his work, he was given a new name among the nations, Saviour of the world (Gen. xli, 45)\u2014if, I say, we consider all these things devoutly and faithfully, do we not acknowledge without hesitation how truly the Lord said: Through the hands of the prophets I have been represented in likeness? (Ose. xii, 10).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Jam vero si veniamus ad verba illa quae de historia proposui, puto quia non tam expositione indigebunt, quam admirationem et gaudium movebunt; quod videlicet tam evidenter resurrectio Christi testificata sit a Lege et Prophetis, et historia vetus tam proprie nova sacramenta loquatur, ut, cum prophetia legitur, pene Evangelium mutatis duntaxat nominibus audiri videatur. Nuntiaverunt, inquit, Jacob, dicentes: Joseph vivit. Quid aliud hic intelligere possum, nisi, Nuntiaverunt Apostolis dicentes: Jesus vivit? Jacob namque nihil aliud quam Apostolorum chorum intelligo; nec absurde, ut arbitror: non solum quia de Jacob orti sunt, non solum quia de Jacob Israel facti sunt, cum de lucta activae ad visionem et requiem contemplativae transierunt; sed etiam quia Patres sunt multitudinis credentium, id est verorum Israelitarum, sicut ille carnalium. Sicut ille, sic isti, suum Joseph cum putarent periisse, inconsolabiliter doluerunt; cum audissent vivere, difficulter et tarde crediderunt; cum cognovissent, ineffabiliter gavisi sunt. Nuntiaverunt, inquit, Jacob, dicentes: Joseph vivit. Quo audito Jacob, quasi de gravi somno evigilans, tamen non credebat eis (Gen. xlv, 26). Quasi aliis verbis mihi videtur dictum, quod in Evangelio legitur scriptum: Illa vadens, haud dubium quin Maria Magdalene, nuntiavit his qui cum ea fuerant lugentibus et flentibus; et illi audientes quia viveret et visus esset ab ea, non crediderunt. Post haec ostensus est duobus in via, et illi exeuntes nuntiaverunt caeteris, nec illis crediderunt (Marc. xvi, 10-13). Item apud Lucam. Et regressae a monumento, nuntiaverunt haec omnia illis undecim, et caeteris omnibus; et visa sunt ante illos sicut deliramentum verba ista, et non credebant illis (Luc. xxiv, 9, 11); nimirum quia evigilabant de gravi somno taedii et desperationis. Cum autem, inquit, vidisset Jacob universa quae Joseph miserat ei, revixit spiritus ejus, et ait: Sufficit mihi si Joseph filius meus vivit. Vadam et videbo eum, antequam moriar. (Gen. xlv, 27, 28). Sic utique in Apostolis parum profecerunt verba, donec acceperunt munera. Nam et ipse Jesus cum se eis praesentem exhibuit, non tam persuasit ostensione corporis, quam aspiratione muneris."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Now, if we turn to those words from the story which I set before you, I think they will need explanation less than they will stir wonder and joy. For Christ\u2019s resurrection is so plainly attested by the Law and the Prophets, and the ancient story speaks so aptly of the new mysteries, that when the prophecy is read, it seems almost as though we were hearing the Gospel with only the names changed. They brought news to Jacob, it says, saying: Joseph is alive. What else can I understand here but: They brought news to the apostles, saying: Jesus is alive? By Jacob I understand none other than the company of the apostles, and not unreasonably, I think. This is not only because they descended from Jacob, nor only because from Jacob they became Israel when they passed from the struggle of the active life to the vision and rest of the contemplative life, but also because they are fathers of the multitude of believers, that is, of the true Israelites, just as he was of the Israelites according to the flesh. Like him, when they thought their Joseph had perished, they grieved inconsolably; when they heard he was alive, they believed with difficulty and slowly; when they knew it, they rejoiced beyond words. They brought news to Jacob, it says, saying: Joseph is alive. On hearing this, Jacob, as though waking from a deep sleep, nevertheless did not believe them (Gen. xlv, 26). It seems to me to say in other words what we read written in the Gospel: She went\u2014undoubtedly Mary Magdalene\u2014and brought the news to those who had been with her, mourning and weeping; and when they heard that he was alive and had been seen by her, they did not believe. After this he appeared to two on the road, and they went out and brought the news to the others, but they did not believe them either (Marc. xvi, 10-13). Again, in Luke: Returning from the tomb, they told all these things to the eleven and to all the others; and these words seemed to them like nonsense, and they did not believe them (Luc. xxiv, 9, 11). For they were indeed waking from the deep sleep of weariness and despair. But when Jacob saw all that Joseph had sent him, it says, his spirit revived, and he said: It is enough for me if Joseph my son is alive. I will go and see him before I die. (Gen. xlv, 27, 28). So too, words made little headway with the apostles until they received gifts. For when Jesus himself showed himself present to them, he persuaded them less by displaying his body than by breathing his gift upon them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Scitis quia cum venit ad eos januis clausis, et stetit in medio eorum, illi conturbati et conterriti existimabant se spiritum videre (Luc. xxiv, 37). Cum autem insufflavit in eos, dicens: Accipite Spiritum sanctum (Joan. xx, 22), vel cum coelitus misit eumdem Spiritum, sed aliud donum, haec utique dona, resurrectionis et vitae indubitata testimonia fuerunt et argumenta. Spiritus enim est qui testificatur apud sanctorum corda et per eorum ora, quoniam Christus est veritas, vera resurrectio et vita. Ideo et Apostoli, qui prius dubitabant etiam post intuitum corporis viventis, post gustum spiritus vivificantis, virtute magna reddebant testimonium resurrectionis. Adeo plus est corde Jesum concipere, quam oculis videre, vel auribus de ipso audire; tantoque potentior est operatio Spiritus apud sensus hominis interioris, quam corporalium apud sensus exterioris. Quis enim dubietati relinquatur locus, ubi qui testificatur, et cui testificatur, unus fuerit Spiritus? Si unus Spiritus et unus sensus, parque consensus. Tunc ergo vere, sicut de Jacob legitur, revixit eorum spiritus, qui jam propemodum luctu erat exstinctus, imo desperatione sepultus. Tunc, nisi fallor, quisque eorum dicebat sibi: Sufficit mihi quod Joseph meus vivit; quia vivere mihi Christus est, et mori lucrum. Vadam igitur in Galilaeam, in montem ubi constituit nobis Jesus, et videbo eum, et adorabo antequam moriar, ut postea nunquam moriar; quia omnis qui videt Filium et credit in eum, habet vitam aeternam, ut, etiamsi mortuus fuerit, vivat (Joan. vi, 40, et xi, 26)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. You know that when he came to them with the doors closed and stood in their midst, they were troubled and terrified and thought they were seeing a spirit (Luc. xxiv, 37). But when he breathed upon them, saying: Receive the Holy Spirit (Joan. xx, 22), or when he sent from heaven the same Spirit but a different gift, these gifts were indeed unquestionable testimonies and proofs of resurrection and life. For it is the Spirit who bears witness in the hearts of the saints and through their mouths that Christ is the truth, the true resurrection and life. Thus the apostles, who had doubted even after looking upon his living body, after tasting the life-giving Spirit bore witness to the resurrection with great power. So much greater is it to receive Jesus in the heart than to see him with the eyes or hear about him with the ears; and so much more powerful is the Spirit\u2019s action upon the senses of the inward man than that of bodily things upon the senses of the outward man. For what room can remain for doubt when the one who bears witness and the one to whom witness is borne are one Spirit? If there is one Spirit, there is also one perception and equal agreement. Then truly, as we read of Jacob, their spirit revived, which had already been almost extinguished by grief, indeed buried by despair. Then, unless I am mistaken, each of them said to himself: It is enough for me that my Joseph is alive, for to me to live is Christ and to die is gain. I will therefore go to Galilee, to the mountain where Jesus has appointed to meet us, and I will see him and worship before I die, so that thereafter I may never die; for everyone who sees the Son and believes in him has eternal life, so that even if he has died, he may live (Joan. vi, 40, et xi, 26).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Nunc ergo, fratres mei, quid vobis testatur gaudium cordis vestri de amore Christi? Puto equidem (an recte, vos videritis) quoniam si unquam amastis Jesum, seu vivum, seu mortuum, sive redivivum, hodie, cum tam crebro in Ecclesia personant et consonant nuntii Resurrectionis, cor vestrum in vobis gloriatur sibi, et dicit: Nuntiaverunt mihi, dicentes: Jesus Deus meus vivit. Quo audito revixit spiritus meus, qui dormitabat prae taedio, vel languebat tepore, aut nimia deficiebat pusillanimitate. Nam et de morte suscitat criminosos jucunda vox hujus felicis nuntii. Alioquin certe desperandus est et oblivione sepeliendus, quem Jesus, regrediens ab inferis, in intimo reliquit inferni. In hoc sane noveris quod spiritus tuus plene in Christo revixerit. si quod sequitur ex sententia dixerit: Sufficit mihi, si Jesus vivit. O vocem fidelem et plane dignam amicis Jesu! o castissimum affectum, qui sic loquitur: Sufficit mihi si Jesus vivit! Si vivit, vivo, cum de ipso pendeat anima mea, imo ipse sit vita mea, ipse sufficientia mea. Quid enim mihi deesse poterit, si Jesus vivit? Imo desint omnia alia, nihil interest mea, dummodo Jesus vivat. Ipse ergo, si placet ei, desit mihi; sufficit mihi, dummodo vivat ipse vel sibi. Cum sic amor Christi totum absorbuerit affectum hominis, ut, negligens et immemor sui nonnisi Jesum Christum et ea quae sunt Jesu Christi sentiat, tunc demum, ut arbitror, perfecta est in eo charitas. Huic utique qui sic affectus est, non est onerosa paupertas: iste non sentit injurias, ridet opprobria, contemnit damna, mortem lucrum deputat; imo nec mori se putat, cum magis de morte ad vitam transire se sciat, et fidenter dicat; Vadam et videbo eum, antequam moriar."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Now then, my brothers, what does the joy of your heart testify to you about love for Christ? I think indeed\u2014you must judge whether rightly\u2014that if you have ever loved Jesus, whether living, dead, or restored to life, today, when the messengers of the Resurrection sound out so often and in harmony in the Church, your heart within you takes its delight and says: They brought me news, saying: Jesus my God is alive. On hearing this, my spirit revived, which had been dozing from weariness, languishing in lukewarmness, or failing through excessive faintheartedness. For the joyful voice of this happy news raises even sinners from death. Otherwise, surely, that man must be given up for lost and buried in oblivion whom Jesus, returning from the underworld, has left in its deepest depths. By this you may know for certain that your spirit has fully come to life again in Christ: if it sincerely says what follows, It is enough for me if Jesus is alive. O faithful utterance, entirely worthy of Jesus\u2019 friends! O most chaste love, which speaks in this way: It is enough for me if Jesus is alive! If he lives, I live, since my soul depends upon him; indeed, he himself is my life, he himself all that suffices me. For what could I lack if Jesus is alive? Indeed, let everything else be lacking: it matters nothing to me, provided Jesus lives. Let even he himself, then, be absent from me, if that pleases him; it is enough for me, provided he himself lives, even for himself alone. When love for Christ has so absorbed all a person\u2019s affection that, neglectful and forgetful of self, that person is mindful only of Jesus Christ and the things of Jesus Christ, then at last, I think, charity is perfected in that person. For one so disposed, poverty is no burden; he does not feel injuries, laughs at reproaches, despises losses, and counts death as gain. Indeed, he does not even think he is dying, knowing rather that he is passing from death to life, and confidently saying: I will go and see him before I die.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p006", "label": "Latin original", "text": "6. Nos tamen, fratres mei, licet non simus tantae nobis puritatis conscii, eamus nihilominus, eamus videre Jesum in montem Galilaeae coelestis, ubi constituit nobis. Eundo crescet affectus, et saltem perveniendo perficietur. Eundo, via prius arcta et difficilis dilatatur, et virtus infirmis augetur. Ut enim nec Jacob, nec aliquis de domo Jacob se excusaret a via praeter alia munera, missa sunt pauperi seni sumptus et vehicula, ne quis videlicet aut paupertatem causaretur, aut infirmitatem. Caro Christi est viaticum, spiritus vehiculum. Ipse est cibus, ipse currus Israel et auriga ejus. Cum perveneris, tua sunt optima quaeque, non Aegypti, sed coeli: in optimo loco regni tuus Joseph tibi requiem providit. Qui prius misit Angelos, mulieres, et Apostolos resurrectionis suae testes et nuntios, nunc ipse clamat e coelo: Ecce ego quem mortuum lugebatis hoc triduo, mortuus quidem fui propter vos, sed ecce vivo, et data est mihi omnis potestas in coelo et in terra (Matth. xxviii, 18). Venite ad me, qui laboratis fame: et ego reficiam vos. (Id. xi, 28), Venite, benedicti Patris mei, percipite regnum quod vobis paravi (Id, xxv, 34). Qui vos eo vocat, ipse perducat, ubi cum Patre et Spiritu sancto vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-p006", "label": "English", "text": "6. Yet, my brothers, although we are not conscious of such purity in ourselves, let us go nonetheless; let us go to see Jesus on the mountain of heavenly Galilee, where he has appointed to meet us. As we go, love will grow, and at least on arriving it will be perfected. As we go, the road, at first narrow and difficult, grows wider, and strength increases for the weak. For, so that neither Jacob nor anyone of Jacob\u2019s household might excuse himself from the journey, besides the other gifts, provisions and vehicles were sent to the poor old man, so that no one might plead either poverty or weakness. Christ\u2019s flesh is provision for the journey; the spirit is the conveyance. He himself is the food, he himself the chariot of Israel and its driver. When you arrive, all the best things are yours, of heaven rather than Egypt: in the best place in the kingdom your Joseph has provided rest for you. He who first sent angels, women, and apostles as witnesses and messengers of his resurrection now himself cries from heaven: Behold, I whom you mourned as dead these three days was indeed dead for your sake, but behold, I live, and all power in heaven and on earth has been given to me (Matth. xxviii, 18). Come to me, you who labour with hunger, and I will refresh you. (Id. xi, 28), Come, blessed of my Father, receive the kingdom which I have prepared for you (Id, xxv, 34). May he who calls you there himself lead you to where, with the Father and the Holy Spirit, he lives and reigns for ever and ever. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints the Latin title RESSURRECTIONE. The opening quotation from Genesis belongs to the first paragraph."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The Mark quotation reads \u201cthose who had been with her,\u201d rather than the familiar Gospel wording \u201cwith him.\u201d The Latin and English follow the sermon. Guerric\u2019s comparison between Jacob and Israel and the Apostles is his own typological interpretation."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e003", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition cites John 6:40 and 11:26. Its combined quotation also draws on John 11:25 (\u201ceven if he has died\u201d)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-01-e004", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The sermon adapts Christ\u2019s invitations: \u201cyou who labour with hunger,\u201d \u201cI will refresh you,\u201d and \u201cthe kingdom which I have prepared for you.\u201d The abbreviation Id. refers back to Matthew."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Resurrection Sermon I: Joseph Lives, Jesus Lives", "topics": ["Resurrection", "Easter", "hope", "Holy Spirit", "charity", "Eucharist", "Scripture"], "url": "sermons/guerric-resurrection-01.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:resurrexi", "calendar_label": "Easter Sunday of the Resurrection", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-resurrection-02", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO II., De duplici nostra resurrectione., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 144-148.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Easter", "Resurrection of the Lord", "Revelation 20:5 (printed locator; quotation from Revelation 20:6)", "John 11:25", "Philippians 3:21", "Romans 8:11", "Revelation 20:6", "Romans 5:14", "Romans 6:9", "Hosea 13:14", "1 Corinthians 15:55\u201356", "Romans 5:12", "Romans 4:25", "Philippians 3:7\u201311", "Philippians 1:21", "Isaiah 55:2", "Sirach 10:10", "Acts 20:24", "Isaiah 55:1", "Song of Songs 5:1", "Hebrews 3:14", "Job 20:15, 21, 23", "Hebrews 6:7\u20138"], "occasion": "De Resurrectione Domini", "occasion_detail": "Resurrection of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Easter", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric reads the blessing on those who share in the first resurrection. Christ is both resurrections: by the sacrament of his rising he works the first, of souls, and by its example he will work the second, of bodies. Death reigned from Adam until Christ, free of sin and of the debt of death, died and rose for us. Guerric calls the poor to buy and eat the paschal bread, and warns those who squander in idle talk the sweetness received in devotion. The sermon appears under the Resurrection heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO II."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON II.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De duplici nostra resurrectione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On our twofold resurrection.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beatus et sanctus qui habet partem in resurrectione prima (Apoc. XX, 5). Ego sum, inquit Jesus, resurrectio et vita (Joan. XI, 25). Ipse utique resurrectio prima, ipse et resurrectio secunda. Resurgens quippe a mortuis primiti\u00e6 dormientium Christus, et sacramento su\u00e6 resurrectionis operatus est nobis resurrectionem primam, et exemplo ejusdem su\u00e6 resurrectionis operabitur nobis secundam. Prima enim est animarum, cum eas sibi conresuscitat in novitatem vitae: secunda erit corporum cum reformabit corpus humilitatis nostrae, configuratum corpori claritatis suae (Philipp. iii, 21). Recte igitur resurrectionem et vitam Christus se profitetur, quandoquidem per ipsum et in ipsum resurgimus, ut secundum ipsum et apud ipsum vivamus: nunc quidem secundum ipsum, in sanctitate et justitia; postmodum apud ipsum, in beatitudine et gloria. Porro sicut resurrectio prima capitis nostri Domini Jesu Christi causa est et argumentum secundae resurrectionis, quae erit totius ipsius corporis, sic unicuique nostrum prima resurrectio animae, qua reviviscit a morte peccati, argumentum est et causa secundae suae resurrectionis, qua corpus liberabitur non modo a corruptione mortis, sed etiam ab omni corruptibilitate mortalitatis. Quod enim haec illius argumentum et causa sit, Apostolus evidenter ostendit, ubi ait: Si spiritus Christi habitat in vobis, qui suscitavit Christum a mortuis vivificabit et mortalia corpora vestra, propter inhabitantem spiritum ejus in vobis (Rom. viii, 11)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed and holy is he who has a part in the first resurrection (Apoc. XX, 5). I am, says Jesus, the resurrection and the life (Joan. XI, 25). He himself is indeed the first resurrection, and he himself the second resurrection. For Christ, rising from the dead, the firstfruits of those who sleep, has by the sacrament of his own resurrection wrought for us the first resurrection, and by the example of that same resurrection of his will accomplish for us the second. For the first is of souls, when he raises them together with himself into newness of life; the second will be of bodies, when he will refashion the body of our lowliness, conformed to the body of his glory (Philipp. iii, 21). Rightly, therefore, does Christ declare himself to be resurrection and life, since it is through him and in him that we rise, that according to him and with him we may live: now indeed according to him, in holiness and justice; afterward with him, in blessedness and glory. Moreover, just as the first resurrection of our Head, the Lord Jesus Christ, is the cause and proof of the second resurrection, which will be that of his whole body, so for each one of us the first resurrection of the soul, by which it revives from the death of sin, is the proof and cause of his second resurrection, by which the body will be freed not only from the corruption of death but also from all corruptibility of mortality. That the one is indeed the proof and cause of the other, the Apostle clearly shows where he says: If the Spirit of Christ dwells in you, he who raised up Christ from the dead will also give life to your mortal bodies, because of his Spirit dwelling in you (Rom. viii, 11).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3 Bene igitur dicitur. Beatus et sanctus qui habet partem in prima resurrectione (Apoc. xx, 6). Sanctus videlicet propter primam, quam jam adeptus est per innovationem animae; beatus propter secundam, quam feliciter exspectat in corporis restitutione. Denique unde beatus sit, eadem aperit Scriptura, cum subdit: In his scilicet qui partem habent in prima resurrectione, mors secunda non habet potestatem, etiamsi prima mors visa est ad horam in eis exercuisse dominationem. Regnavit quippe mors ab Adam usque ad Moysen, etiam in eos qui peccaverunt in similitudinem praevaricationis Adae (Rom. v, 14). Sed sicut Christus, sic et christianus resurgens a mortuis, jam non moritur, mors illi ultra non dominabitur (Id. vi, 9). Itaque in beatis illic nec mors secunda potestatem habebit, nec prima potestatem quam ad tempus habuit, retinebit; quoniam mors una Christi utramque nostram triumphavit, ab ista liberans jam captos, ab illa, capiendos; ne videlicet in illam incidamus, nec in ista remaneamus. Quam vera, quam pia simul et magnifica illa morientis comminatio: Ero mors tua, o mors (Osee. xiii, 14)! Quam pulchre et mirifice triumphavit, qui, mortem pro omnibus gustans, tam suam quam omnium omnimodam mortem absorbuit! Prorsus absorpta est mors in victoria. Securus insultet ei quicunque beatus ille est, qui partem habet in resurrectione prima: Ubi est, mors, victoria tua? ubi est, mors, stimulus tuus? Victa es, quae omnia vincebas; sed et arma perdidisti, in quibus confidebas. Ubi enim est stimulus tuus? Stimulus mortis peccatum (I Cor. xv, 55, 56), quod semel pungens humani radicem generis, in totam propaginem immedicabile mortis venenum diffudit, dicente Apostolo quia per unum hominem peccatum, et per peccatum mors, et ita in omnes homines pertransivit (Rom. v, 12). Victrix ergo regnabat mors ab Adam primo etiam usque ad secundum; quoniam sicut originali lege tenebatur universum genus humanum vinculo peccati, sic etiam debito moriendi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p002", "label": "English", "text": "3. Rightly, then, is it said: Blessed and holy is he who has a part in the first resurrection (Apoc. xx, 6). He is holy, namely, on account of the first resurrection, which he has already attained through the renewal of the soul; he is blessed on account of the second, which he happily awaits in the restoration of the body. Finally, Scripture itself reveals the reason for this blessedness when it adds: namely, that in those who have a part in the first resurrection, the second death has no power, even though the first death seemed for a time to have exercised dominion over them. For death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over those who sinned after the likeness of Adam's transgression (Rom. v, 14). But just as Christ, so too the Christian, rising from the dead, no longer dies; death will no longer have dominion over him (Id. vi, 9). And so among the blessed there, neither will the second death have power, nor will the first retain the power it held for a time; since the one death of Christ triumphed over both our deaths, freeing from the one those already captive, and from the other those who were to be captured\u2014namely, that we might not fall into that second death, nor remain in this first one. How true, how pious and at the same time how magnificent is that threat of the dying Christ: I will be your death, O death! (Osee. xiii, 14). How beautifully and wonderfully he triumphed, who, tasting death for all, swallowed up death utterly, both his own and that of all! Death is indeed swallowed up in victory. Let whoever is that blessed one who has a part in the first resurrection boldly taunt death: Where, O death, is your victory? Where, O death, is your sting? You who conquered all things are conquered; and you have lost also the weapons in which you trusted. For where is your sting? The sting of death is sin (I Cor. xv, 55, 56), which, once piercing the root of the human race, spread an incurable poison of death through the whole lineage, as the Apostle says, that through one man sin, and through sin death, entered, and so passed into all men (Rom. v, 12). Death, then, reigned as victor from the first Adam even to the second, since, just as the whole human race was held bound by the original law in the bond of sin, so also it was held under the debt of dying.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Deo autem gratias, qui dedit nobis victoriam tam peccati quam mortis, utique per Dominum Jesum Christum, qui prorsus a peccato immunis, ac per hoc liber a debito mortis, hanc tamen ultro pro nobis exsolvit moriens, et peccato nos absolvit resurgens. Christus enim, ait Apostolus, mortuus est propter delicta nostra, et resurrexit propter justificationem nostram (Rom. iv, 25) Moriens namque delictorum nostrorum pendit poenam, et resurgens justificationis perpetuae nobis condidit formam et causam, ut, quomodo videlicet Christus resurgens a mortuis jam non moritur, mors illi ultra non dominabitur (Rom. vi, 9), sic christianus conresurgens Christo jam non peccet ad mortem, nec ei peccatum ultra dominetur. Hic est ille beatus et sanctus, qui partem habet in resurrectione prima, in quem nec potestatem habebit mors secunda, sed absorbebitur in victoriam resurrectionis Christi etiam mors prima. Hic est qui non solum agnovit, sed etiam apprehendit virtutem resurrectionis Christi, et societatem passionis illius; configuratus morti ejus, ut occurrat ad resurrectionem ex mortuis. Non errabat Apostolus, qui, ob lucrum illud, omnia quae ei lucra fuerant non solum detrimenta reputabat, sed etiam velut stercora arbitrabatur, tantum ut Christo inveniretur, configuratus sicut morti, sic et resurrectioni ipsius (Philipp. iii, 7-11). Lucrosum omnino commercium, contemnere ea quae te deterunt et polluunt, ut lucrifacias Christum; imo, si opus sit, non solum tua, sed et te ipsum superimpendere, ut cum tanto fenore immortalitatis et gloriae te ipsum merearis recipere! Quis enim ambigat lucrosum esse commercium, seminare corpus mortale, animale, ignobile, ut surgat immortale, spirituale, gloriosum; mori mundo, ut possis dicere, Mihi vivere Christus est, et mori lucrum? (Id. i, 21.) O cupidi, quid immoramini studio lucrandi? cur non discitis artem lucrandi? cur non vilia, imo detrimenta et stercora contemnitis, ut Christum lucrifaciatis? Quare appenditis argentum non in panibus, et laborem vestrum non in saturitate (Isai. lv, 2)? Ut video, vilior est vobis argento vestro panis ille qui de coelo descendit, et dat vitam mundo. Sed non potest scire quanti [ alias quantus. ] sit, qui non vult gustare qualis sit. Utinam avarus vel ipse sibi pretiosior esset pecunia sua, nec amore illius venalem exponeret animam suam, projiceretque in vita sua intima sua (Eccli. x, 10). Prudens ille negotiator dignusque rerum aestimator, Paulum nimirum loquor, qui nec animam suam, id est animalem sensualemque vitam, pretiosiorem fecit se ipso (Act. xx, 24), id est spiritu suo, quo videlicet connexus inhaerebat Christo, paratus animam perdere, ut in vitam aeternam posset eam custodire."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Thanks be to God, who gave us victory over both sin and death, namely through our Lord Jesus Christ, who, being entirely free from sin, and therefore free from the debt of death, nevertheless willingly paid that death for us by dying, and absolved us from sin by rising again. For Christ, says the Apostle, died for our sins, and rose again for our justification (Rom. iv, 25). For by dying he paid the penalty of our sins, and by rising he established for us the pattern and cause of perpetual justification, so that just as Christ, rising from the dead, dies no more, and death shall no longer have dominion over him (Rom. vi, 9), so the Christian, rising together with Christ, may no longer sin unto death, nor may sin have dominion over him any longer. This is that blessed and holy one who has a part in the first resurrection, over whom the second death shall have no power, but even the first death shall be swallowed up in the victory of Christ's resurrection. This is he who has not only recognized but also laid hold of the power of Christ's resurrection, and the fellowship of his passion; being conformed to his death, so that he may attain to the resurrection from the dead. The Apostle was not mistaken, who, for the sake of that gain, counted all the things that had been gains to him not only as losses, but even reckoned them as dung, provided only that he might be found in Christ, conformed both to his death and to his resurrection (Philipp. iii, 7-11). It is altogether a profitable exchange to despise the things that wear you down and defile you, so that you may gain Christ; indeed, if need be, to spend not only your goods but even yourself, so that with such great interest of immortality and glory you may deserve to receive yourself back! For who could doubt that it is a profitable exchange, to sow a mortal, natural, ignoble body, that it may rise immortal, spiritual, glorious; to die to the world, so that you may say, For to me to live is Christ, and to die is gain? (Id. i, 21.) O you who are greedy, why do you linger in your zeal for gaining? Why do you not learn the art of gaining? Why do you not despise cheap things \u2014 indeed losses and dung \u2014 so that you may gain Christ? Why do you weigh out your silver for that which is not bread, and your labor for that which does not satisfy (Isai. lv, 2)? As I see it, that bread which came down from heaven and gives life to the world is of less worth to you than your silver. But one who refuses to taste what it is like cannot know how great it is. Would that the miser were at least more precious to himself than his money, and would not, for love of it, offer his own soul up for sale, and cast away his inmost self while yet alive (Eccli. x, 10). That prudent merchant and worthy appraiser of things \u2014 I mean, of course, Paul \u2014 who did not reckon his own soul, that is, his natural and sensual life, more precious than himself (Act. xx, 24), that is, than his spirit, by which, closely joined, he clung to Christ, ready to lose his soul so that he might keep it unto eternal life.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Quia igitur qui divitias habent, difficile intrant in regnum coelorum; et qui thesaurizant argentum, citius appendunt illud in inanibus, quam in panibus, in azymis dico sinceritatis et veritatis, cum quibus hodie comedendus est Agnus paschalis; vos beati pauperes, filii pauperis Crucifixi, vos inquam, qui non habetis argentum, properate, emite et comedite. Promptius omnino et facilius emunt bonum illud nihil habentes, quam multa possidentes. Cum deest facultas, sufficit ad emendum bona voluntas, qua plerumque sunt ditiores, qui rebus sunt pauperiores. Hos nimirum recte invitat Scriptura: Venite, emite absque argento, et absque ulla commutatione, vinum et lac (Isai. LV, 1). Vides, o felix pauper, solam a te voluntatem bonam exigi, in illa sola rationem tanti constare commercii? Ne recuses ingratus quod tam gratis offertur, imo ne perdas ingrata voluntate, quod jam merueras beata paupertate. Agnosce quanti lucri sit, non habere partem in ruina mundi, ut partem habeas in resurrectione Christi. Intellige quantae felicitatis sit, non inebriari luxu et furore saeculi, ut cum Christo novum bibas vinum in regno Patris sui. Invitat ipse Agnus paschalis amicos ad delicias corporis et sanguinis sui: Comedite, inquiens, amici, bibite, et inebriamini, charissimi (Cantic. V, 1). Cibus iste et potus, vitae est mysterium, immortalitatis medicamentum, causa resurrectionis primae, pignusque secundae, quia divinae plane in nobis initium substantiae. Participes, inquit Apostolus, Christi effecti sumus, si tamen initium substantiae ejus usque ad finem firmum retineamus (Hebr. III, 14). Qui enim post acceptam gratiam ad vomitum suum revertitur, Divitias quas devoravit evomet, et de ventre illius extrahet eas Deus. Vel certe panis ejus in utero illius vertetur in fel aspidum intrinsecus: quia nimirum gratia percepta in poenam conscientiae vertitur, cum sanguis Testamenti, quo sanctificatus quis fuerat, pollutus ducitur, et Spiritui gratiae contumelia irrogatur. Hoc contemptu et nausea divitiae devoratae evomuntur, ita ut de eo qui ejusmodi est, dicatur: Non remansit quidquam de cibo ejus, et propterea nihil permanebit de bonis illius."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Therefore, since those who have riches enter the kingdom of heaven with difficulty, and those who hoard up silver sooner weigh it out for empty things than for bread \u2014 I mean the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth, with which the paschal Lamb is to be eaten today \u2014 you blessed poor, children of the poor Crucified One, you, I say, who have no silver, hasten, buy and eat. Those who have nothing buy that good thing far more readily and easily than those who possess much. When means are lacking, good will suffices for the purchase \u2014 and those who are poorer in goods are often the richer in that very will. It is indeed such as these whom Scripture rightly invites: Come, buy wine and milk without silver, and without any price (Isai. LV, 1). Do you see, O happy poor man, that only good will is demanded of you, and that the whole worth of so great a transaction consists in that alone? Do not ungratefully refuse what is offered so freely; rather, do not lose, by an ungrateful will, what you had already merited by blessed poverty. Recognize how great a gain it is to have no part in the ruin of the world, so that you may have a part in the resurrection of Christ. Understand how great a happiness it is not to be made drunk with the luxury and fury of this age, so that you may drink new wine with Christ in the kingdom of his Father. The paschal Lamb himself invites his friends to the delights of his body and blood: Eat, he says, O friends, drink, and be inebriated, O dearly beloved (Cantic. V, 1). This food and drink is the mystery of life, the medicine of immortality, the cause of the first resurrection, and the pledge of the second, because it is plainly in us the beginning of divine substance. We have been made partakers, says the Apostle, of Christ, if indeed we hold the beginning of his substance firm unto the end (Hebr. III, 14). For he who, after receiving grace, returns to his own vomit, shall vomit up the riches he had devoured, and God shall draw them out of his belly. Or certainly, within him, his bread shall be turned to the gall of asps in his belly \u2014 because indeed the grace received is turned into punishment of conscience, when the blood of the Testament, by which one had been sanctified, is accounted polluted, and insult is done to the Spirit of grace. By this contempt and loathing, the riches that had been devoured are vomited up, so that of such a one it is said: Nothing remained of his food, and therefore nothing of his goods shall remain.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Videat autem ne forte et ipsum terribilis ista perstringat sententia, qui, cum per gratiam devotionis repletus fuerit in bonis domus Dei, nihil prorsus ex illis in memoria retinet, ut memoriam abundantiae suavitatis divinae nobis eructet; non saporem in sermonibus, tanquam gustum in faucibus; non virtutem in moribus, tanquam succum in visceribus; sed dum statim totum per inania vel scurrilia revomit, gratiam sibi in iram convertit. Prorsus in iram, et iram terribilem, si in ipsum cadit quod Scriptura contexuit dicens: Cum satiatus fuerit, arctabitur, et irruet in eum omnis dolor. Utinam impleatur venter ejus, ut immittat in eum iram furoris sui, et pluat super eum bellum suum (Job XX, 15, 21, 23). Justum quippe sentiebat atque consentiebat Propheta ut pluat super peccatores bellum, qui ad pluviam voluntariam, fructum pacis non retulerunt; sitque ignis, sulphur et spiritus procellarum pars calicis eorum, qui calicem Domini indigne biberunt. Terra enim saepe venientem bibens imbrem, et generans herbam opportunam, accipit benedictionem; proferens autem spinas et tribulos, reproba est et maledictio proxima; cujus consummatio in combustionem (Hebr. VI, 7, 8). Confidimus autem de vobis, fratres, meliora et viciniora saluti. Tantum gratiae Dei grati sitis, et sicut in novam creaturam translati estis paschalibus mysteriis, sic in novitate vitae semper ambuletis. Qui ergo participes Christi effecti estis societate fidei, participatione sacramenti, communione Spiritus sancti, initium substantiae ejus non solum usque ad finem firmum retinere, sed etiam sedulo augere nitimini: ut, qui per tot munerum praerogativas partem habere coepistis in resurrectione prima, tanti fiducia pignoris in die agnitionis consignati, jus vobis perpetuum vindicetis in resurrectione secunda, praestante Domino nostro Jesu Christo, resurrectione et vita nostra, qui pro nobis mortuus per triduum, jam vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. But let him take care lest this terrible sentence strike him as well who, when he has been filled through the grace of devotion with the good things of the house of God, retains nothing at all of them in memory from which to belch forth\u2014no savor in his speech, as a taste lingering in the mouth; no virtue in his conduct, as sap in the inward parts\u2014but who, straightway vomiting back the whole through empty or scurrilous things, turns grace into wrath for himself. Into wrath indeed, and terrible wrath, if upon him falls what Scripture has woven together, saying: 'When he shall be filled, he shall be straitened, and every sorrow shall rush upon him. Would that his belly might be filled, that God may send upon him the wrath of His fury, and rain down His war upon him' (Job XX, 15, 21, 23). For the Prophet rightly judged, and rightly consented, that war should rain down upon sinners who, in return for the willing rain, did not bring forth the fruit of peace; and that fire, brimstone, and a spirit of storms should be the portion of the cup of those who drank the cup of the Lord unworthily. For the earth, which often drinks in the rain that comes upon it, and brings forth herbage fit for use, receives a blessing; but that which brings forth thorns and briers is worthless and near to a curse, whose end is to be burned (Hebr. VI, 7, 8). But we are confident of better things concerning you, brothers, and things nearer to salvation. Only be grateful for the grace of God, and just as you have been transformed into a new creature by the paschal mysteries, so may you always walk in newness of life. You, then, who have been made partakers of Christ through fellowship of faith, participation in the sacrament, and communion of the Holy Spirit, strive not only to hold firm the beginning of his substance even to the end, but also diligently to increase it: so that you, who through so many prerogatives of gifts have begun to have part in the first resurrection, sealed by the assurance of so great a pledge on the day of recognition, may claim for yourselves a perpetual right in the second resurrection, through the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, our resurrection and our life, having been dead for us for three days, now lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition numbers the second section 3, so two sections carry that number. The reading keeps the printed numeral."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-02-e002", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The blessing on those who share in the first resurrection is Revelation 20:6. The edition prints Apoc. XX, 5 here and Apoc. xx, 6 when the verse returns in the second section."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Resurrection Sermon II", "topics": ["Resurrection", "Easter", "first resurrection", "sin", "death", "Eucharist", "poverty", "devotion"], "url": "sermons/guerric-resurrection-02.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "dayKey:resurrexi", "calendar_label": "Easter Sunday of the Resurrection", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-resurrection-03", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, SERMO III., De vigilantia necessaria ut partem habeamus in resurrectione Christi., in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 148-152.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Easter", "Resurrection of the Lord", "Revelation 20:6", "Psalm 83:3 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 3:6 (historical numbering)", "Ephesians 5:14", "Psalm 117:24 (historical numbering)", "John 8:56", "Matthew 27:61", "Luke 24:1\u20138", "Wisdom 6:13\u201315", "Proverbs 8:17", "Isaiah 26:9", "Psalm 62:2 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 89:14 (historical numbering)", "Romans 13:11\u201312", "Psalm 117:24, 27 (historical numbering)", "Proverbs 6:9\u201310", "Wisdom 5:6", "Malachi 4:2", "Isaiah 33:15, 17", "Wisdom 6:17", "Luke 24:3\u20134, 13\u201327", "4 Kings 4:34\u201335 (Vulgate numbering; 2 Kings)", "Psalm 38:4 (historical numbering)", "Psalm 118:131 (historical numbering)", "Philippians 3:11"], "occasion": "De Resurrectione Domini", "occasion_detail": "Resurrection of the Lord", "occasion_label": "Easter", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric calls the brothers to watchfulness, so that they may share in the resurrection of Christ, the morning of a day without evening. Like Abraham and the women at the tomb, those who keep vigil at the doors of Wisdom see the risen Christ, who meets those who pray and those who work. Elisha's raising of the Shunammite's son marks the stages of the soul's return to life: a warmed heart, sighs that enlarge desire, and eyes opened to contemplation. The sermon appears under the Resurrection heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO III."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h001", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON III.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De vigilantia necessaria ut partem habeamus in resurrectione Christi."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-h002", "label": "English", "text": "On the vigilance necessary that we may have a part in the resurrection of Christ.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Beatus et sanctus qui habet partem in resurrectione prima (Apoc. XX, 6). Primitiae dormientium Christus, primogenitus ex mortuis, quia sua resurrectione, quae prima est omnium, et primam nobis resurrectionem animarum, et secundam corporum dedicavit; dum in corpore suo, quod suscitavit a mortuis, et animabus sacramentum, et corporibus exemplum resurgendi nuntiavit. Sed et animabus ipsis duplicem gratiam resurrectionis simplex Christi resurrectio praeparavit, dum et quotidie reviviscunt a morte peccati per operationem mysterii, et hodie maxime resurgunt a somno torporis per devotionem gaudii. Quis enim ille tam piger aut tepidus, qui hodie audiens vocem illam omni gaudio plenam, Resurrexit Dominus, non totus in exsultationem suscitetur, non totus reviviscat et recalescat spiritu? Imo et cor meum et caro mea exsultaverunt, inquit, in Deum vivum (Psal. LXXXIII, 3), qui totus concideram in moerorem ac desperationem, Jesum aspiciens mortuum. Non mediocri lucro fidei, nec parvo fenore gaudii, Jesus mihi de sepulchro redit, dum et Deus vivus agnoscitur, qui paulo ante ut homo mortuus lugebatur, et quem occisum cor meum dolebat, jam in eum vivum non solum cor meum, sed et caro mea exsultat, de sua scilicet per eum resurrectione et immortalitate secura. O anima mea, Ego dormivi et exsurrexi (Psal. III, 6), ait Christus; surge et tu, quae dormis, et exsurge a mortuis, et illuminabit te Christus (Ephes. V, 14). An non, fratres, mortuo similis, qui adhuc stetit orto jam sole, qui adhuc negligentia et socordia premitur, quasi desperato sepultus torpore, jam ubique gratia resurrectionis rutilante? Ferit oculos Sol novus emergens ab inferis, his qui de mane vigilant ad eum, diem reserans aeternitatis. Dies iste vesperam nescit, quia non occidet ultra sol ejus, qui semel occidens, semel ascendit super occasum, mortem sibi subjiciens."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. Blessed and holy is he who has a part in the first resurrection (Apoc. XX, 6). Christ is the firstfruits of those who sleep, the firstborn from the dead, because by his resurrection, which is first of all, he consecrated for us both the first resurrection, that of souls, and the second, that of bodies; since in his body, which he raised from the dead, he announced to souls a sacrament, and to bodies an example, of rising again. But Christ's single resurrection also prepared a twofold grace of resurrection for souls themselves, since they daily come back to life from the death of sin through the working of the mystery, and today especially they rise from the sleep of sluggishness through the devotion of joy. For who is so sluggish or lukewarm that, hearing today that word so full of all joy, 'The Lord has risen,' is not wholly roused to exultation, does not wholly come back to life and grow warm again in spirit? Indeed, 'my heart and my flesh have exulted,' he says, 'in the living God' (Psal. LXXXIII, 3) \u2014 I who had wholly collapsed into grief and despair, beholding Jesus dead. With no small gain of faith, nor with slight interest of joy, Jesus returns to me from the tomb, since he who a little before, as man, was mourned as dead, is now recognized as the living God; and he whom, slain, my heart grieved over, now \u2014 living \u2014 not only my heart but my flesh as well exults in him, being made secure, through him, of its own resurrection and immortality. O my soul, 'I have slept and risen up' (Psal. III, 6), says Christ; rise up, you too who sleep, and arise from the dead, and Christ will enlighten you (Ephes. V, 14). Is he not, brothers, like one dead, who still stands motionless when the sun has already risen, who is still weighed down by negligence and sloth, as if buried in hopeless torpor, now that the grace of the resurrection glows red everywhere? The new Sun, emerging from the world below, strikes the eyes of those who watch for him early in the morning, unlocking for them the day of eternity. This day knows no evening, because its sun will set no more \u2014 he who, setting once, rose once above his setting, subjecting death to himself.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. O fratres, haec dies quam fecit Dominus, exsultemus et laetemur in ea (Psal. CXVII, 24). Exsultemus in spe ejus, ut videamus et gaudeamus in luce ejus. Exsultavit Abraham ut videret diem Christi, et hoc merito vidit, et gavisus est (Joan. VIII, 56). Nam et tu quoque si vigiles quotidie ad fores Sapientiae, et observes ad postes ejus, pervigilque excubes cum illa Magdalena ad ostium monumenti ejus (Matth. XXVII. 61), probabis, ni fallor, et ipse cum eadem Maria (Luc. XXIV, 1-8), quam verum sit quod de ipsa Sapientia, quae Christus est, legitur: Facile videtur ab his qui diligunt eam, et invenitur ab his qui quaerunt illam. Praeoccupat qui se concupiscunt, ut illis se prior ostendat. Qui de luce vigilaverit ad illam, non laborabit assidentem enim illam foribus suis inveniet (Sap. VI, 13-15). Sic enim et ipse promisit dicens: Ego diligentes me diligo, et qui mane vigilaverint ad me, invenient me (Prov. VIII, 17). Invenit quidem Maria corporaliter Jesum ad quem vigilabat, ad cujus monumentum, cum adhuc essent tenebrae, excubare venerat. Tu vero qui jam non debes nosse Jesum secundum carnem, sed secundum spiritum, spiritualiter nimirum eum invenire poteris, si simili eum desiderio quaesieris, si te similiter pervigilem in oratione adverterit. Dic ergo Domino Jesu desiderio et affectu Mariae: Anima mea desideravit te in nocte, sed et spiritus meus in praecordiis meis; de mane vigilabo ad te (Isai. XXVI, 9). Dic voce et mente Psalmistae: Deus, Deus meus, ad te de luce vigilo: sitivit in te anima mea (Psal. LXII, 2). Et vide si non cum eis tibi cantare congruerit: Repleti sumus mane misericordia tua et exsultavimus, et delectati sumus (Psal. LXXXIX, 14)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. O brothers, 'this is the day which the Lord made; let us exult and rejoice in it' (Psal. CXVII, 24). Let us exult in hope of it, that we may see and rejoice in its light. Abraham exulted that he might see the day of Christ, and this he deservedly saw, and rejoiced (Joan. VIII, 56). For you too, if you watch daily at the doors of Wisdom, and keep watch at her posts, and keep vigil, like that Magdalene, at the door of his tomb (Matth. XXVII. 61), will prove, unless I am mistaken, together with that same Mary (Luc. XXIV, 1-8), how true is what is read of Wisdom herself, who is Christ: 'She is easily seen by those who love her, and is found by those who seek her. She anticipates those who desire her, that she may show herself to them first. He who watches for her from early light shall not labor, for he shall find her sitting at his doors' (Sap. VI, 13-15). For he himself promised thus, saying: 'I love those who love me, and those who watch for me early shall find me' (Prov. VIII, 17). Mary indeed found, bodily, the Jesus for whom she watched, having come to keep vigil at his tomb while it was still dark. But you, who ought now to know Jesus no longer according to the flesh but according to the spirit, will surely be able to find him spiritually, if you seek him with a like desire, if he finds you likewise keeping vigil in prayer. Say therefore to the Lord Jesus, with the desire and affection of Mary: 'My soul has desired you in the night, and my spirit also within me; from the morning I will watch for you' (Isai. XXVI, 9). Say with the voice and mind of the Psalmist: 'O God, my God, from early light I watch for you; my soul has thirsted for you' (Psal. LXII, 2). And see whether it will not be fitting for you to sing with them: 'We have been filled in the morning with your mercy, and we have exulted and been delighted' (Psal. LXXXIX, 14).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vigilate ergo, fratres, in orationibus intenti, vigilate in actionibus circumspecti; praesertim quia jam mane illius diei indeclinabilis inclaruit, postquam videlicet lux aeterna serenior nobis et gratior ab inferis rediit, solemque novum susceptio matutina reparavit. Prorsus hora est jam nos de somno surgere, postquam nox praecessit, dies autem appropinquavit (Rom. XIII, 11. 12). Vigilate, inquam, ut oriatur vobis matutina lux, Christus scilicet, cujus quasi diluculum praeparatus est egressus, paratus utique vigilantibus ad se, mysterium suae matutinae resurrectionis saepius innovare. Tunc utique cantabis in jubilo cordis, Deus Dominus illuxit nobis. Haec dies quam fecit Dominus, exsultemus et laetemur in ea (Psal. CXVII, 27. 24), cum videlicet lucem quam in manibus abscondit interlucere tibi permiserit, annuntians de ea amico quod possessio ejus sit, et ad eam possit ascendere. Usquequo, piger, dormis? usquequo dormitas? Paululum dormies, paululum dormitabis, paululum conseres manus ut dormias (Prov. VI, 9, 10, et XXIV, 33); et cum te dormiente, te nesciente Christus de sepulcro surrexerit, et gloria ejus transeunte, nec posteriora ejus videre merueris; tunc sera poenitentia planges, et dices cum impiis, Ergo erravimus a via veritatis, et justitiae lumen non luxit nobis, et sol intelligentiae non est ortus nobis (Sap. V, 6). Vobis autem, inquit, timentibus nomen meum, orietur sol justitiae (Malach. IV, 2); et, qui ambulat in justitiis, Regem in decore suo videbunt oculi ejus (Isai. XXXIII, 15, 17). Et id quidem beatitudo est vitae sequentis, sed juxta quemdam modum indulgetur etiam ad consolationem praesentis, sicut evidenter probat resurrectio Christi. In multis siquidem argumentis per dies quadraginta Sapientia nobis probavit quoniam dignos se ipsa circuit quaerens, et in viis suis ostendit se illis hilariter, et in omni prudentia occurrit illis (Sap. VI, 17.) Ut enim Jesus se illam esse sapientiam, de qua haec scripta sunt, ostenderet; et, quod quotidie spiritualiter actitare non desinit, id est, in viis justitiae se ostendere hilariter, hodie corporalis etiam operis specie figuraret, in via hodie occurrit mulieribus a monumento redeuntibus, in via se ostendit duobus euntibus in Emmaus (Luc. XXIV, 3, 4, 13-27)."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. Watch, then, brothers, intent on your prayers; watch, circumspect in your actions\u2014especially since now the morning of that unending day has shone forth, since indeed the eternal light, more serene and more welcome to us, has returned from the underworld, and the morning's rising has restored to us a new sun. Truly it is now the hour for us to rise from sleep, since the night is past and the day is at hand (Rom. XIII, 11. 12). Watch, I say, that the morning light may rise for you\u2014namely Christ, whose going forth is prepared like the dawn, ready indeed, for those who watch for him, to renew again and again the mystery of his morning resurrection. Then truly you will sing with the jubilation of your heart: 'The Lord God has shone upon us. This is the day which the Lord has made; let us exult and be glad in it' (Psal. CXVII, 27. 24)\u2014when, that is, he allows the light which he hides in his hands to shine through to you, declaring of it to his friend that it is his possession, and that he can ascend to it. How long, O sluggard, will you sleep? How long will you slumber? A little you will sleep, a little you will slumber, a little you will fold your hands to sleep (Prov. VI, 9, 10, et XXIV, 33); and when, while you are sleeping, while you know nothing of it, Christ has risen from the tomb, and his glory has passed by, and you have not merited even to see his back as he goes; then with late repentance you will weep, and say with the wicked: 'So we have strayed from the way of truth, and the light of righteousness has not shined on us, and the sun of understanding has not risen for us' (Sap. V, 6). But to you, he says, who fear my name, the sun of righteousness shall rise (Malach. IV, 2); and he who walks in righteousness\u2014his eyes shall see the King in his beauty (Isai. XXXIII, 15, 17). And this indeed is the blessedness of the life to come, but in a certain measure it is granted even for the consolation of the present life, as the resurrection of Christ plainly proves. For by many proofs, over forty days, Wisdom showed us that she herself goes about seeking out those worthy of her, and shows herself to them cheerfully in their ways, and meets them with all prudence (Sap. VI, 17.). For in order that Jesus might show himself to be that very Wisdom of whom these things are written, and might today figure, even under the appearance of a bodily deed, what he never ceases to do daily in a spiritual way\u2014that is, to show himself cheerfully in the ways of righteousness\u2014today he met the women returning from the tomb on the road, and on the road he showed himself to the two going to Emmaus (Luc. XXIV, 3, 4, 13-27).", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Audiant et gaudeant qui in viis justitiae ambulant; audiant, inquam, quia non solum inhaerentes studio contemplationis, sed etiam ambulantes juste ac pie vias actionis, Jesus dignatur et occursu et manifestatione sui. Agnoscit, ni fallor, experientia quorumdam vestrum, quia saepe Jesus quem quaesierunt velut ad monumentum, ad memorias altarium, nec invenerunt, insperatus occurrit eis in viis laborum. Tunc nimirum accesserunt, et tenuerunt pedes ejus, quorum scilicet pedes non tenuerat pigritia, prae desiderio ejus. Noli ergo tu, frater, nimis parcere pedibus tuis a viis obedientiae et discursibus operum, quandoquidem Jesus pedibus suis non perpercit propter te etiam a dolore clavorum; et adhuc eorumdem amplexu et osculo pedum non gravatur laborem remunerare aut relevare pedum tuorum. Nam et illud quantae consolationis erit, si se tibi viae comitem adjunxerit, miraque delectatione suae sermocinationis, etiam sensum tibi laboris ademerit, aperiens tibi sensum, ut intelligas Scripturas, quas fortasse domi sedens legebas et non intelligebas? Obsecro vos, fratres mei, quibus hoc aliquando dedit experiri dignatio divina, nonne cor vestrum ardens erat in vobis de Jesu, cum loqueretur vobis in via, et aperiret Scripturas? Recordentur igitur illud experti, et cantent in viis Domini, quoniam magna est gloria Domini; credant, et experiri satagant inexperti, ut aliquando et ipsis cantabiles fiant justificationes Dei, in loco peregrinationis suae et afflictionis."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let those who walk in the ways of righteousness hear and rejoice; let them hear, I say, that Jesus deigns to grant both his meeting and the manifestation of himself not only to those who cling to the pursuit of contemplation, but also to those who walk justly and devoutly in the ways of action. The experience of some of you recognizes, unless I am mistaken, that often Jesus, whom they sought as it were at the tomb, at the memorials of the altars, and did not find, meets them unexpectedly in the ways of their labors. Then indeed they approached and held his feet\u2014those, namely, whose feet laziness had not held back, because of their desire for him. Do not, then, brother, spare your feet too much from the ways of obedience and the busy comings and goings of works, since Jesus did not spare his own feet for your sake, even from the pain of the nails; and even now he is not burdened, by the embrace and kiss of those same feet, to reward or relieve the labor of your feet. For how great a consolation it will be, if he joins himself to you as a companion on the road, and by the wondrous delight of his conversation even takes from you the very feeling of your labor, opening your understanding, that you may understand the Scriptures which perhaps, sitting at home, you were reading and not understanding? I beseech you, my brothers, to whom the divine condescension has at some time granted to experience this\u2014was not your heart burning within you concerning Jesus, when he spoke to you on the road and opened the Scriptures? Let those, then, who have experienced this remember it, and sing in the ways of the Lord, for great is the glory of the Lord; and let those who have not experienced it believe, and strive earnestly to experience it, that at some time the justifications of God may become songs even for them, in the place of their pilgrimage and affliction.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p005", "label": "Latin original", "text": "5. Resurgat itaque ac reviviscat spiritus omnium nostrum, sive ad vigilantiam orandi, sive ad instantiam operandi, ut quadam rediviva ac vivida alacritate probet se de novo portionem accepisse in resurrectione Christi. Et primum quidem vitae in hominem redeuntis indicium est, si strenuus et impiger sit ad actionem; nam perfecta resurrectio ejus est in hoc duntaxat moribundo corpore, si oculos aperiat ad contemplationem. Quod tamen non ante promeretur intellectus, quam crebris suspiriis et vehementibus desideriis dilatetur affectus, ut tantae nimirum capax majestatis reddatur. Quod videlicet velut per quosdam gradus incrementum resurgendi signanter figuratum arbitror in mortuo quem Elisaeus suscitavit, qui cum ad vitam redire inciperet, primo dicitur quia calefacta est caro pueri; deinde, quia septies oscitavit, et sic demum oculos aperuit (IV Reg. IV, 34, 35). Caro pueri, cor carneum est in Christo parvuli, cui prima spes vitae est, si dicere possit: Concaluit cor meum intra me, et in meditatione mea exardescet ignis (Psal. XXXVIII, 4). Quanquam et vestimenta ejus calida sint, cum terra ejus perflata austro, Spiritu utique sancto, quem verus Elisaeus praeveniendo aspirat redivivo. Amplius autem et evidentius ad resurrectionem proficit, cum desiderio et esurie quadam justitiae oscitare frequentius incipit, illo scilicet modo quo oscitabat qui dicebat: Os meum aperui et attraxi spiritum, quia mandata tua desiderabam (Psal. CXVIII, 131). Oscitatio quippe ista, distensio est affectus, ut capacior fiat spiritus vitae, quatenus post alia charismata septiformis gratiae, infusus etiam spiritus intellectus et sapientiae, oculos tandem ad contemplandum Deum faciat aperire. Primus ergo calor vitae redeuntis est, cum bonus exercetur actus: secundus resurrectionis profectus, cum per orationem dilatatur affectus: perfectio autem, cum ad contemplandum illuminatur intellectus. His gradibus virtutum, his sanctioris vitae incrementis, fratres mei, resurgere magis ac magis enitimini, si quo modo occurratis, ut Apostolus ait, ad resurrectionem Christi ex mortuis (Philipp. III, 11), qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-resurrection-03-p005", "label": "English", "text": "5. Let the spirit of us all, therefore, rise again and come back to life, whether for watchfulness in prayer or for eagerness in working, so that by a certain revived and lively readiness it may prove that it has newly received a share in the resurrection of Christ. And the first sign indeed of life returning into a man is if he be vigorous and unwearied for action; for his resurrection is perfect in this mortal body only to this extent, that he open his eyes to contemplation. Yet the intellect does not merit this before the affection is first enlarged by frequent sighs and ardent desires, so that it may be made capable of so great a majesty. This increase of rising again I think is signified, as it were by certain steps, in the dead man whom Elisha raised to life; who, when he began to return to life, is said first to have had the flesh of the boy grow warm; then, that he yawned seven times, and only then at last opened his eyes (IV Reg. IV, 34, 35). The flesh of the boy is the heart of flesh in a little one in Christ, for whom the first hope of life is if he can say: My heart grew warm within me, and in my meditation a fire shall flame out (Psal. XXXVIII, 4). And indeed his garments too are warm, when his earth is fanned by the south wind \u2014 that is, by the Holy Spirit, whom the true Elisha, going before, breathes into the one being revived. But he progresses further and more plainly toward resurrection when he begins to yawn more frequently with a certain desire and hunger for righteousness, in that manner in which he yawned who said: I opened my mouth and drew in breath, because I longed for your commandments (Psal. CXVIII, 131). For that yawning is a stretching of the affection, so that the affection may become more capable of receiving the spirit of life, until, after the other gifts of the sevenfold grace, the spirit of understanding and wisdom also being poured in, it at last makes the eyes open to the contemplation of God. The first warmth, then, of returning life is when a good act is exercised; the second advance of resurrection, when the affection is enlarged through prayer; but the perfection, when the understanding is illumined for contemplation. By these steps of virtues, by these increases of a holier life, my brothers, strive to rise more and more, that you may somehow attain, as the Apostle says, to the resurrection of Christ from the dead (Philipp. III, 11), who lives and reigns through all ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Resurrection Sermon III", "topics": ["Resurrection", "Easter", "vigilance", "prayer", "contemplation", "action", "spiritual growth"], "url": "sermons/guerric-resurrection-03.html"}, {"author": "Guerric of Igny", "calendar": "unassigned", "calendar_label": "Unassigned", "centuries": [12], "composition": "Approximately 1138\u20131157", "id": "guerric-rogations", "language": "la", "language_label": "Latin", "metadata": ["Guerric of Igny (collection attribution), Sermones per annum, IN DIEBUS ROGATIONUM., SERMO, De pane verbi petendo et apponendo, in J.-P. Migne, Patrologiae cursus completus, Series Latina, vol. 185, pars prior (Paris: Garnier fr\u00e8res, 1879), cols. 151-154.", "Internet Archive, 2011; PIMS, University of Toronto; University of Toronto funding.", "Rogation Days", "Rogation Days", "Isaiah 3:7", "Sirach 32:1", "Psalm 80:11 (historical numbering)", "Romans 10:12", "Psalm 144:16 (historical numbering)", "James 1:5", "Lamentations 4:5", "Sirach 3:21", "Hebrews 5:12", "1 Corinthians 14:3", "Luke 11:5"], "occasion": "In diebus Rogationum", "occasion_detail": "Rogation Days", "occasion_label": "Rogation Days", "passages": [{"anchor": "editorial-context", "label": "Editorial introduction", "text": "Guerric takes the friend who asks for three loaves at midnight as the figure of one who must feed his brothers with the word. He confesses that he presides by office without bread of his own, asks God to make him humble and useful, and pleads the merit of those to be fed. He leaves the loaves of speculation on the Trinity to the angels, since God is to be believed rather than discussed, and sets out other threefold loaves from Scripture. The sermon appears under the Rogation Days heading in the 1879 edition of Patrologia Latina. The collection is associated approximately with Guerric's abbacy in 1138\u20131157; the individual sermon is undated."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "IN DIEBUS ROGATIONUM."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h001", "label": "English", "text": "ON THE DAYS OF ROGATION.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "SERMO"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h002", "label": "English", "text": "SERMON", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "De pane verbi petendo et apponendo"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-h003", "label": "English", "text": "On asking for and setting forth the bread of the word", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p001", "label": "Latin original", "text": "1. Commoda mihi tres panes Luc. XI, 5). Amici venerunt ad nos de via, sed non habeo quod ponam ante illos. Non sum medicus, et in domo mea non est panis: ideo dicebam ab initio, Nolite me constituere principem (Isai. III, 7). Non enim debet praeesse, qui non potest prodesse. Quomodo autem prodesse potest, qui nec medicus est, nec panem in domo habet, id est, nec artem novit qua sciat curare, nec doctrinam habet qua sufficiat pascere? Haec quidem dicebam; sed heu! non audistis me: principem enim constituistis me. Supererat igitur ut, quia non potui effugere periculum, confugerem ad remedium, et audirem super hoc illud Sapientis consilium: Principem te constituerunt; esto inter illos quasi unus ex ipsis (Eccli. XXXII, 1). Sed vae mihi! neque hoc ipsum relictum est mihi. Sicut enim imperitia prohibet esse super alios, sic imbecillitas non patitur inter alios esse; et sicut spiritu non sufficio ad ministrandum verbum, sic corpore deficio ad praebendum exemplum. Qui igitur idoneus non sum nec praeesse, nec coesse: ubi potero esse, nisi ultimum et tutissimum eligam locum, scilicet omnibus subesse? Et hoc quidem valeo, humilia scilicet, imo vera de me sentiendo; nihilque me prohibet, imo plurimum veritas ipsa monet, subesse cunctis animo, licet praeesse cogar officio."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p001", "label": "English", "text": "1. \"Lend me three loaves\" Luc. XI, 5). Friends have come to us from the road, but I have nothing to set before them. I am not a physician, and in my house there is no bread: therefore I said from the beginning, \"Do not make me a ruler\" (Isai. III, 7). For he who cannot be of use ought not to be set over others. But how can he be of use who is neither a physician nor has bread in his house\u2014that is, who neither knows the skill by which he might know how to heal, nor has the teaching by which he might suffice to feed? This indeed I said; but alas! you did not listen to me: for you made me a ruler. It remained, then, since I could not escape the danger, that I should take refuge in the remedy, and hear on this matter that counsel of the Wise Man: \"They have made you a ruler; be among them as one of themselves\" (Eccli. XXXII, 1). But woe is me! not even this was left to me. For just as inexperience forbids being over others, so weakness does not allow one to be among others; and just as in spirit I am not sufficient to minister the word, so in body I fail to provide example. Since, then, I am fit neither to preside nor to stand as a companion among others, where can I be, unless I choose the last and safest place\u2014namely, to be beneath all? And this indeed I am able to do, that is, by thinking humbly\u2014or rather truly\u2014of myself; nor does anything prevent me, indeed the truth itself most strongly urges me, to be beneath all in spirit, though I am compelled by office to preside.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p002", "label": "Latin original", "text": "2. Tu, Domine Deus, ipse es qui et subesse mones, et nihilominus praeesse jubes; a te expeto, a te exspecto ut humilem simul et utilem in injuncto me facias ministerio: humilem, vera de me sentiendo; utilem, recta de te loquendo. Illud inspira cordi, hoc ministra ori. Da sermonem rectum et bene sonantem in apertione oris mei, qui dixisti, Aperi os tuum et implebo illud (Psal. LXXX, 11), ut omnis familia tua benedictionibus impleatur. Ecce venerunt amici, mei quidem amici, sed amplius tui. Non habeo quod ponam ante illos, nisi commodatum fuerit ab alio. Et quis alius aeque dives aut liberalis ad praestandum, sicut Dominus omnium, dives in omnes qui invocant illum (Rom. X, 12); qui aperit manum suam, et implet omne animal benedictione (Psal. CXLIV, 16), qui dat omnibus affluenter, et non improperat (Jacobi I, 5), nisi forte illi qui vel pigre petit, vel ingrate detinet gratiam quam accepit? Quanti mercenarii in domo Patrisfamilias istius abundant panibus, qui, quoniam Christum annuntiant, licet non sincere, propter alios tamen eis doctrinae gratia non negatur? Et ubi abundant mercenarii, nunquid egebunt filii? Tu ergo, Domine (amicum enim dicere vereor, sed Dominum confiteor), commoda mihi tres panes, unde reficiantur amici, ne, si dimisero eos jejunos, deficiant in via, et tunc vocer in causam pro eis, et dicatur mihi: Parvuli petierunt panem, et non fuit qui frangeret eis (Thren. IV, 5). Commoda Domine, quod in lucrum tuum proficiat, recepturus utique cum tibi placuerit, quod tuum est, cum usura. Commoda, inquam, tres panes, si placet; aut certe quodcunque placet, quantulumque fuerit, etiamsi buccella panis sufficiet in quantalibet millia, si tantum benedicas. Scio equidem quia vis nos esse tibi importunos, ut, licet dissimules, licet excuses, eo quod jam in coelum receptus sis, et Apostoli tui tecum sint in cubili, nos tamen nihilominus perseveremus in petendo, quaerendo, pulsando, sciente quia doctrinam non sola vitae meretur innocentia, qua amici efficimur, sine assiduitate studii et instantia precis, qua importuni videamur. Mihi tamen neutrum suffragatur: solum eorum qui pascendi sunt meritum obtendo; illi merentur quod ego non mereor."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p002", "label": "English", "text": "2. You, Lord God, are the very one who both counsel me to be beneath, and nonetheless command me to preside; from you I ask, from you I expect, that you may make me at once humble and useful in the ministry laid upon me: humble, by thinking truly of myself; useful, by speaking rightly of you. Breathe the one into my heart; supply the other to my mouth. Give a right word, well-sounding, at the opening of my mouth, you who said, \"Open your mouth, and I will fill it\" (Psal. LXXX, 11), that all your household may be filled with blessings. Behold, friends have come\u2014my friends indeed, but yours even more. I have nothing to set before them unless it be lent me by another. And who else is equally rich or generous in granting, as the Lord of all, rich toward all who call upon him (Rom. X, 12); who opens his hand and fills every living thing with blessing (Psal. CXLIV, 16); who gives to all abundantly and does not reproach (Jacobi I, 5)\u2014unless perhaps to him who either asks sluggishly, or ungratefully withholds the grace he has received? How many hired men abound with loaves in the house of this Father of the family, who, because they proclaim Christ\u2014though not sincerely\u2014yet for the sake of others the grace of teaching is not denied them? And where hired men abound, shall the sons be in want? You, therefore, Lord\u2014for I fear to call you friend, yet I confess you Lord\u2014lend me three loaves, from which my friends may be refreshed, lest, if I send them away hungry, they faint along the way, and I then be called to account for them, and it be said to me: \"The little ones asked for bread, and there was none to break it for them\" (Thren. IV, 5). Lend, Lord, what will turn to your profit, since you will certainly receive back, when it pleases you, what is yours, with interest. Lend, I say, three loaves, if it please you; or indeed whatever pleases you, however small it be\u2014even if a single morsel of bread will suffice for however many thousands, if only you bless it. I know well that you wish us to be importunate with you, so that, although you pretend otherwise, although you make excuse on the ground that you have already been received into heaven, and your Apostles are with you in your chamber, we should nevertheless persevere in asking, seeking, knocking\u2014knowing that teaching is not merited by innocence of life alone, by which we become friends, without diligence of study and the insistence of prayer, by which we may seem importunate. Yet neither of these avails for me: I plead only the merit of those who are to be fed; they deserve what I do not deserve.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p003", "label": "Latin original", "text": "3. Vos autem, fratres mei, usibus quorum, et pro meritis quorum peto panes istos, putatis, sufficimus, sive ego ad frangendum, sive vos ad edendum? Timeo ne dicatur mihi: Altiora te ne quaesieris, et fortiora te ne scrutatus fueris (Eccli. III, 21). Timeo ne dicatur et vobis: Facti estis quibus opus est lacte, non pane (Hebr. V, 12). Scio revera panes esse apud Patrem familias, quos si nostra praesumat infantia, citius frangentur dentes, quam impleantur ventres, id est aedificentur mentes. Quis enim capiat, quis explicare aut cogitare digne sufficiat illud Trinitatis mysterium ineffabile: quomodo Pater a se, Filius a Patre, Spiritus sanctus ab utroque, et tres personae in unitate substantiae? Mulier insipiens, audax haereticorum vanitas, prurientes auribus ad talia discutienda sollicitat, cum Deus credendus sit, non discutiendus. Panes, inquit, occultos libenter attingite. Quasi vero tu insipiens attigeris, quod sublimius est Angelis. Et ad quid mihi panes occultos attingere, quos non licet sine periculo frangere vel edere? Sufficit mihi scire quia sunt, quia tres panes sunt; non dico Trinitatem personarum, sed trinitatem sermonum, vel magis intellectuum, qui de personis habendi sunt: tres, inquam, panes unius magnitudinis et ponderis, unius formae et saporis. Quanta enim et qualia de Patre, tanta et talia de Filio seu de Spiritu sancto sentienda sunt, excepto duntaxat, quod proprietatem discretio numerum facit personarum, sicut in Trinitate, sic intelligentiarum in earum distinctione."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p003", "label": "English", "text": "3. But you, my brothers, for whose use, and for whose merits, I ask these loaves \u2014 do you think we are sufficient, either I for the breaking, or you for the eating? I fear lest it be said to me: Seek not the things that are too high for thee, and search not into things above thy ability (Eccli. III, 21). I fear lest it also be said to you: You are become such as have need of milk, not of bread (Hebr. V, 12). I know indeed that there are loaves with the Father of the household which, if our infancy should presume upon them, our teeth would be broken sooner than our stomachs filled \u2014 that is, our minds built up. For who could grasp, who could suffice to explain or worthily consider that ineffable mystery of the Trinity: how the Father is from himself, the Son from the Father, the Holy Spirit from both, and yet three persons in unity of substance? A foolish woman, the bold vanity of heretics, stirs up those whose ears itch to discuss such things, though God is to be believed, not discussed. 'Gladly touch hidden loaves,' she says. As though you, foolish one, had touched what is loftier than the Angels! And to what purpose should I touch hidden loaves, which it is not lawful to break or eat without danger? It is enough for me to know that they exist, that there are three loaves \u2014 I do not say a Trinity of persons, but a trinity of words, or rather of understandings, which are to be held concerning the persons: three loaves, I say, of one magnitude and weight, of one form and savor. For whatever and however great things are to be thought of the Father, the same and equally great things are to be thought of the Son and of the Holy Spirit, except only that the distinction of properties makes a number of persons, just as in the Trinity, so in the distinction of our understandings concerning them.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p004", "label": "Latin original", "text": "4. Hos ergo panes relinquamus frangendos angelicae sublimitati, donec ad eorum crescentes aequalitatem, mensae eorum simus idonei. Multifariae siquidem aliorum trinitates panum de Scriptura nobis possunt proponi, qui nostrae magis congruant infirmitati, verbi gratia, ut nondum longius ab illa summa Trinitate recedamus, quod ex ipso, et per ipsum, et in ipso sunt omnia; quod a Patre creati, per Filium redempti, in Spiritu sancto sumus sanctificati. In hunc modum tam multa dici possunt, ut quamlibet famelicus venerit amicus de via, si vel dimidium ei apponas, non minus fortasse incipiat periclitari fastidio, quam prius inedia: ut copia obruat, quem inopia prius angebat. Non solum enim ipsum qui fecit nos, et ea quae facta sunt propter nos, sed etiam quae scripta sunt propter nos, describere tripliciter poteris, ut copiosa refectio sit in tribus panibus, historiae, allegoriae, et moralitatis. Universa quoque Scripturae continentia in tres partes divisa, velut in tres panes digesta est, dum aut de naturali justitia disserit, aut de litterali, aut de spirituali; id est, ante legem, sub lege, et post legem, quod est sub gratia. Natura rectum dedit intellectum; lex, etiam actum; gratia autem, affectum. Sed et Doctor et pastor Gentium in fide et veritate, pascendam docet Ecclesiam quadam panum trinitate; ut qui Ecclesiam aedificat, loquatur ad aedificationem, ad exhortationem, ad consolationem (I Cor. XIV, 3). Ad aedificationem, ut quid agere debeas, noris; ad exhortationem, ut quod noris, velis; ad consolationem, ut etiam in adversis possis quod noveris et volueris. Denique non solum in materia rerum, sensibus et partibus Scripturarum, generibus et modis locutionum, sed etiam in omni fine earum, quamdam panum invenies trinitatem, et ipsam quidem sapidam satis et salubrem: fidem scilicet, spem, et charitatem. Eo quippe omnis Scripturarum, vel sermonum intentio spectare videtur, ut credamus, speremus, diligamus. Nam et charitas sola finis praecepti definita est, quae et ipsa quodammodo triplex est, dum ex toto corde, tota anima, tota virtute habenda est. Sed quoniam vitio ducitur longior et varia epulatio, nos finem dabimus lectioni: vestrum erit fragmenta quae superaverunt, id est subtiliora, quae manus nostras effugerunt, colligere; simul autem et vestrum et nostrum, ei qui pascit nos cantare, Benedictus Deus in donis suis, qui vivit et regnat per omnia saecula saeculorum. Amen."}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-p004", "label": "English", "text": "4. Let us therefore leave these loaves to be broken by the sublimity of angels, until, growing to equality with them, we become fit for their table. For indeed many other trinities of loaves can be set before us from Scripture, which are better suited to our weakness \u2014 for example, that we may not yet withdraw too far from that highest Trinity: that of him, and through him, and in him are all things; that we were created by the Father, redeemed through the Son, sanctified in the Holy Spirit. In this way so many things can be said that, however hungry a friend may come from the road, if you set before him even half of it, by surfeit \u2014 so that abundance may overwhelm him whom want formerly distressed. For not only him who made us, and the things that were made for our sake, but also the things that were written for our sake, you may describe in a threefold way, so that there be an abundant refreshment in three loaves: of history, of allegory, and of moral teaching. The whole content of Scripture too, divided into three parts, is as it were arranged into three loaves, in that it treats either of natural justice, or of the literal, or of the spiritual \u2014 that is, before the law, under the law, and after the law, which is under grace. Nature gave a right understanding; the law gave also right action; but grace gave right affection. Moreover the Teacher and Pastor of the Gentiles in faith and truth teaches that the Church is to be fed with a certain trinity of loaves: that he who builds up the Church should speak unto edification, unto exhortation, unto consolation (I Cor. XIV, 3). Unto edification, that you may know what you ought to do; unto exhortation, that you may will what you know; unto consolation, that even in adversities you may be able to do what you have known and willed. Finally, not only in the matter of things, in the senses and parts of the Scriptures, in the kinds and modes of speech, but also in their whole end and purpose, you will find a certain trinity of loaves, and one indeed quite savory and wholesome: namely, faith, hope, and charity. For to this end the whole intention of the Scriptures, or of discourses, seems to look: that we may believe, hope, and love. For charity alone has been defined as the end of the commandment, and it too is in a certain way threefold, since it must be held with the whole heart, the whole soul, and the whole strength. But since a feast too long and too varied is accounted a fault, we will put an end to our reading: it will be yours to gather up the fragments that remain \u2014 that is, the finer points that have escaped our hands \u2014 and it will be both yours and ours together to sing to him who feeds us: Blessed be God in his gifts, who lives and reigns through all the ages of ages. Amen.", "translation_id": "ai"}, {"anchor": "guerric-rogations-e001", "label": "Editorial note", "text": "The edition prints Luc. xi, 5) without an opening parenthesis. The request for three loaves is Luke 11:5."}], "status": "English translation", "text_kind": "translated", "title": "Rogations Sermon", "topics": ["Rogation Days", "preaching", "prayer", "humility", "Scripture", "Trinity", "faith"], "url": "sermons/guerric-rogations.html"}];
